// AAMC full-length tests: flashcards built from the concepts tested in each test's sections.
// One scenario card per tested concept, checked against AAMC's answers and explanations.
// Original wording only: no AAMC passages, questions, figures or explanations are reproduced.
// src = test and question number the concept came from (e.g. pe5-cp:28 = Practice Exam 5, Chem/Phys, Q28).
// Format: {s: tag, disc: scoring discipline, aamc: content category, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src}
const QB_AAMC_FL = [
  // ── Practice Exam 5 · Chem/Phys (built 2026-11-11) ──
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"An assay calls for 100.0 mL of a 60 μM solution of an inhibitor with a molar mass of 484 g/mol. The solvent has a density of 1.00 g/mL. Approximately what mass of inhibitor should be weighed out?",idStem:"To make 100.0 mL of a 60 μM solution of a drug (molar mass 484 g/mol), about how much drug do you need?",choices:["29 mg","2.9 mg","0.29 mg","2.9 g"],ans:1,exp:"**moles = M × V(L)** = 60 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L × 0.1000 L = 6.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol; × 484 g/mol = 2.9 × 10⁻³ g = **2.9 mg**.",src:"pe5-cp:1",how:{"ask":"Turn a concentration and a volume into moles, then turn moles into a mass.","know":"**Molarity (M) = moles of solute ÷ liters of solution.** So moles = M × V, with V in liters. mass = moles × molar mass (g/mol). Prefixes: μ (micro) = 10⁻⁶, m (milli) = 10⁻³. The solvent density is not needed.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: you need the moles of inhibitor in the final volume, then the mass. Molarity counts moles per liter of solution.","hl":"100.0 mL of a 60 μM solution"},{"text":"Convert both units before multiplying.","calc":["60 μM = 60 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M**","100.0 mL ÷ 1000 = **0.1000 L**"]},{"text":"Moles = molarity × volume in liters.","calc":["n = M × V","n = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L** × **0.1000 L**","n = **6.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol**"]},{"text":"Mass = moles × molar mass.","hl":"molar mass of 484 g/mol","calc":["m = n × M","m = 6.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol × **484 g/mol**","m = **2.9 × 10⁻³ g** = **2.9 mg**"]},{"text":"Eliminate the unit slips: 29 mg uses 1 L instead of 0.1000 L, 0.29 mg drops another factor of 10, and 2.9 g reads μM as mM.","strike":["29 mg","0.29 mg","2.9 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.9 mg. The density is a distractor; molarity already gives moles per liter of solution.","hl":"The solvent has a density of 1.00 g/mL"}],"trap":"29 mg comes from using 1 L instead of 0.1000 L (skipping the mL → L step). 2.9 g comes from reading μM as mM."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives you and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"100.0 mL","note":"volume of solution"},{"t":"60 μM","note":"concentration"},{"t":"484 g/mol","note":"mass of 1 mole"}]},{"text":"**Molarity (M)** = how many moles of stuff are dissolved in **1 liter**. The tiny **μ** (micro) means one-millionth.","marks":[{"t":"60 μM","note":"60 millionths mol per L"}],"calc":["M = moles ÷ liters","μ = micro = 10⁻⁶","60 μM = 60 × 10⁻⁶ = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L**"]},{"text":"**Molar mass** = how many grams **one mole** weighs. It turns moles into grams at the end.","marks":[{"t":"molar mass of 484 g/mol","note":"1 mol weighs 484 g"}]},{"text":"The solvent's density tells you about the water, not the inhibitor. **You don't need it.** Exams add extra numbers on purpose.","marks":[{"t":"density of 1.00 g/mL","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"Molarity is **per liter**, but the volume is in **mL**. Convert first.","marks":[{"t":"100.0 mL","note":"= 0.1000 L"}],"calc":["1 L = 1000 mL","100.0 mL ÷ 1000 = **0.1000 L**"]},{"text":"**Moles = molarity × liters.** Multiply the two marked numbers.","marks":[{"t":"60 μM","note":"6.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L"},{"t":"100.0 mL","note":"× 0.1000 L"}],"calc":["n = M × V","n = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L × 0.1000 L","n = **6.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol**"]},{"text":"**Grams = moles × molar mass.** Then turn grams into milligrams.","marks":[{"t":"484 g/mol","note":"× this"}],"calc":["m = 6.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol × 484 g/mol","m = **2.9 × 10⁻³ g**","1 mg = 10⁻³ g → m = **2.9 mg**"]},{"text":"**Tempting slips:** using 1 L instead of 0.1 L gives 29 mg; reading μ (10⁻⁶) as m (10⁻³) gives 1000× too much, 2.9 g.","marks":[{"t":"100.0 mL","note":"0.1 L, not 1 L"},{"t":"60 μM","note":"micro, not milli"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass to weigh = molarity (mol/L) × volume (L) × molar mass (g/mol). Convert μ, m and mL to plain units first; ignore the solvent's density."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"In a binding study, increasing amounts of an inhibitor I are added to a fixed amount of enzyme E at constant temperature. The fraction of enzyme present as the EI complex rises with each addition, while the measured dissociation constant Kd stays the same. Which statement is most consistent with these results?",idStem:"Adding more inhibitor increases the amount of enzyme–inhibitor complex. Which principle explains this?",choices:["The system shifts toward free E, since added I lowers the stability of EI","The system shifts toward EI, since Kd decreases as [I] increases","The system shifts toward EI, since added I is a stress the system offsets","The system does not shift, since Kd is constant at fixed temperature"],ans:2,exp:"E + I ⇌ EI is an equilibrium. **Adding a reactant is a stress, and the equilibrium shifts to consume it (Le Châtelier)**, so more EI forms while Kd itself stays constant.",src:"pe5-cp:2",how:{"ask":"What happens to an equilibrium mixture when you add more of one reactant, and what stays fixed?","know":"A reversible reaction reaches **equilibrium**, where forward and reverse rates are equal. The ratio Kd = [E][I]/[EI] is a constant at a given temperature. **Le Châtelier's principle:** if you disturb an equilibrium (add or remove a species, change T or P), the system shifts to partly undo the disturbance.","steps":[{"text":"Two observations: more enzyme ends up as EI, and Kd does not change. The right answer must fit both.","hl":"The fraction of enzyme present as the EI complex rises with each addition"},{"text":"Write the equilibrium and compare Q with Kd right after I is added.","calc":["E + I ⇌ EI","Kd = [E][I] / [EI]","add I: Q = [E][**I↑**] / [EI] > Kd","to restore Kd: E + I → EI, a **shift toward EI**"]},{"text":"The curve shows the fraction of EI climbing with [I] while the Kd line stays flat: the composition changes, the constant does not.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Binding of E + I ⇌ EI as I is added (enzyme fixed)","x":{"label":"[I] added ÷ Kd","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fraction of E as EI","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.5,0.33],[1,0.5],[2,0.67],[3,0.75],[4,0.8],[5,0.83]]},{"name":"Kd ÷ starting Kd","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[4,1],[5,1]]}]},"graph":{"focus":["Fraction of E as EI","Kd ÷ starting Kd"],"read":[{"series":"Fraction of E as EI","x":3},{"series":"Kd ÷ starting Kd","x":3}],"labels":[{"series":"Fraction of E as EI","x":1,"text":"0.50 when [I] = Kd","at":"right"},{"series":"Fraction of E as EI","x":5,"text":"0.83: more EI","at":"below"},{"series":"Kd ÷ starting Kd","x":2,"text":"Kd stays 1 (flat)","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":0.5,"to":0.83,"text":"EI rises by 0.33","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Eliminate choices that contradict the data: Kd does not decrease, and the system does shift because EI rises.","hl":"the measured dissociation constant Kd stays the same","strike":["The system shifts toward EI, since Kd decreases as [I] increases","The system does not shift, since Kd is constant at fixed temperature"]},{"text":"Answer: the system shifts toward EI because added I is a stress it partly uses up (Le Châtelier). A shift toward free E is the wrong direction.","strike":["The system shifts toward free E, since added I lowers the stability of EI"]}],"trap":"\"Kd decreases as [I] increases\" has the right direction but the wrong reason. The data say Kd stayed the same; the constant is fixed, the composition shifts."},teach:[{"text":"First, **read what was done** and what was measured.","marks":[{"t":"increasing amounts of an inhibitor I","note":"added more and more I"},{"t":"fixed amount of enzyme E","note":"E kept the same"},{"t":"constant temperature","note":"T never changes"}]},{"text":"**EI complex** = enzyme and inhibitor stuck together. They stick and come apart all the time, both ways at once: an **equilibrium**.","marks":[{"t":"EI complex","note":"E and I stuck together"}],"calc":["E + I ⇌ EI","⇌ = goes both ways at once"]},{"text":"**Kd** is a fixed ratio for this equilibrium. A constant like this changes **only with temperature**, not with how much you add.","marks":[{"t":"dissociation constant Kd","note":"fixed ratio at this T"}],"calc":["Kd = [E][I] / [EI]","[ ] = concentration","only temperature can change **Kd**"]},{"text":"**Two results** to explain: more of the enzyme ends up as EI, and Kd does not move.","marks":[{"t":"rises with each addition","note":"more EI each time"},{"t":"stays the same","note":"Kd unchanged"}]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** if you push on an equilibrium, it shifts to partly undo the push. Adding I is the push; making EI uses some I up.","marks":[{"t":"increasing amounts of an inhibitor I","note":"the push (stress)"}],"calc":["add I → [E][**I↑**] / [EI] is now > Kd","to get back to Kd: E + I → **more EI**","= **shift toward EI**"]},{"text":"**See it:** the fraction of enzyme as EI climbs as I is added, while Kd stays flat. The mix changes; the constant does not.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"E + I ⇌ EI as I is added (enzyme fixed)","x":{"label":"[I] added ÷ Kd","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fraction of E as EI","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.5,0.33],[1,0.5],[2,0.67],[3,0.75],[4,0.8],[5,0.83]]},{"name":"Kd ÷ starting Kd","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[4,1],[5,1]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Fraction of E as EI","x":1,"text":"0.50 when [I] = Kd","at":"right"},{"series":"Fraction of E as EI","x":5,"text":"0.83: more EI","at":"below"},{"series":"Kd ÷ starting Kd","x":2,"text":"Kd stays the same","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":0.5,"to":0.83,"text":"EI rises","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Wrong ideas:** Kd did not decrease (the data say so), the system did shift (EI rose), and a shift toward free E is backwards.","marks":[{"t":"stays the same","note":"so Kd did NOT drop"},{"t":"rises with each addition","note":"so it DID shift to EI"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a reactant shifts an equilibrium toward products (Le Châtelier), but K itself stays put unless the temperature changes."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"A lead compound, shown, has the potency a drug team wants but dissolves poorly in water. Chemists prepare four analogs, each with one change to the lead. Which analog is most likely to be more soluble in water than the lead?",idStem:"Which change would make a drug molecule MORE water-soluble?",fig:"2-phenylethanol",choiceFigs:["pyridylethanol","phenethyl-methyl-ether","4-chlorophenylethanol","4-methylphenylethanol"],choices:["Pyridine analog","Methyl ether analog","Chloro analog","Methyl analog"],ans:0,exp:"Water solubility rises with **groups that hydrogen bond with water**. Swapping a ring C–H for N adds a lone pair that **accepts an H bond** from water, so the pyridine analog is more soluble. Capping the O–H as O–CH₃ removes the lead's only H-bond donor, and a ring Cl or CH₃ only adds nonpolar surface.",src:"pe5-cp:3",how:{"ask":"Compare each analog with the lead. Which change adds a site that can hydrogen bond with water, rather than removing one or adding nonpolar bulk?","know":"• **Hydrogen bond:** an H on N, O or F (the donor) attracted to a lone pair on N, O or F (the acceptor). Water both donates and accepts.\n• A molecule dissolves in water better the more donor and acceptor sites it has and the less nonpolar surface (C–H, C–C, C–Cl) it carries.\n• The ring N of pyridine has a lone pair pointing out of the ring: an acceptor. A ring C–H is neither.\n• Turning O–H into O–CH₃ keeps an acceptor but loses the donor H, so solubility falls.\n• On structure choices, circle what changed from the lead, then ask: did the change add or remove an N/O lone pair or an N–H/O–H?","steps":[{"text":"Every analog is compared with the lead, so first find what changed in each drawing.","hl":"each with one change to the lead","fig":"2-phenylethanol"},{"text":"Score each change by the H-bond sites it adds or removes.","calc":["pyridine analog: ring C–H → N: **+1 acceptor**","methyl ether: O–H → O–CH₃: **−1 donor**","chloro analog: C–H → C–Cl: **0**, more nonpolar surface","methyl analog: C–H → C–CH₃: **0**, more nonpolar surface"]},{"text":"A ring Cl or CH₃ adds nonpolar surface and no H-bond sites, so both lower solubility.","strike":["Chloro analog","Methyl analog"]},{"text":"Capping the O–H removes the only donor the lead had.","strike":["Methyl ether analog"]},{"text":"The ring N, highlighted, has a lone pair in the plane of the ring that accepts an H bond from water.","fig":"pyridylethanol-n"},{"text":"Answer: the pyridine analog. Rule: more N/O lone pairs and N–H/O–H groups mean more water solubility; capping an O–H or adding carbon lowers it."}],"trap":"The methyl ether analog is tempting because its oxygen can still accept an H bond, but capping the O–H removes the lead's only donor and adds a CH₃, so it is less soluble than the lead."},teach:[{"text":"**Every corner or line end is a carbon**. Hydrogens on carbons are hidden. Letters are other atoms; OH is oxygen bonded to hydrogen.","fig":"t5-pe-read"},{"text":"**In water, O pulls the shared electrons toward itself, so O is slightly negative and the H's slightly positive**. Lopsided like this = polar.","fig":"t5-water"},{"text":"**A lone pair is a pair of electrons an atom keeps to itself**. An H on O or N is drawn to a lone pair on another O or N: a hydrogen bond.","fig":"t5-water-lp"},{"text":"**The OH can give its H to water and take water's H on its lone pairs**. The ring and chain are only C and H: nonpolar (oily), so water ignores them.","fig":"t5-pe-oh"},{"text":"**Swap a ring C–H for N: the N's lone pair points outward and accepts an H bond from water**. The OH is still there too.","fig":"t5-pyr-n"},{"text":"**Capping OH as O–CH₃ removes the only H on O, so it can no longer donate an H bond, and adds an oily carbon**.","fig":"t5-ether-o"},{"text":"**Cl has no H to give and grabs water poorly**. It mainly adds oily surface.","fig":"t5-chloro-cl"},{"text":"**CH₃ is only carbon and hydrogen: more oily surface, nothing new for water to hold**.","fig":"t5-methyl-c"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: more O–H, N–H or lone-pair N/O = more soluble; more C, H or Cl = less. In each drawing ask: did the change add a spot for water to H-bond?","fig":"t5-pe-rule"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"A competitive inhibitor with Ki = 60 μM is tested at [S] = 2Km. The inhibitor concentration is then raised from 60 μM to 180 μM, with temperature and pH unchanged. Compared with the 60 μM trial, which statement describes the 180 μM trial?",idStem:"An inhibitor's concentration is raised from 60 μM to 180 μM. What happens to its Ki?",choices:["Ki triples, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited increases","Ki falls to one-third, and the fraction inhibited increases","Ki is unchanged, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited increases","Ki is unchanged, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited is unchanged"],ans:2,exp:"**Ki is an equilibrium (dissociation) constant** for EI ⇌ E + I, fixed by the enzyme, inhibitor, temperature and pH. Raising [I] binds more enzyme, so inhibition increases, but Ki does not change.",src:"pe5-cp:4",how:{"ask":"Is Ki a property of the inhibitor–enzyme pair or of the amount of inhibitor added? And what does more inhibitor do?","know":"**Ki = [E][I]/[EI]** is a dissociation constant: a small Ki means tight binding. Like any equilibrium constant, it changes only with temperature (or conditions like pH), not with concentrations. The **fraction of enzyme bound** depends on [I] relative to Ki.","steps":[{"text":"Ki is an equilibrium constant. Like every K, it changes with temperature (and here pH), not with concentration.","hl":"with temperature and pH unchanged","calc":["EI ⇌ E + I","Ki = [E][I] / [EI] = **60 μM** at this T and pH","T and pH unchanged → Ki still **60 μM**"]},{"text":"Eliminate both choices in which Ki changes.","strike":["Ki triples, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited increases","Ki falls to one-third, and the fraction inhibited increases"]},{"text":"Now find how much inhibition each trial gives with the competitive-inhibition rate law.","calc":["v_I / v₀ = (Km + [S]) / (Km(1 + [I]/Ki) + [S])","[S] = 2Km → v_I / v₀ = 3 / (3 + [I]/Ki)","[I] = 60 μM: 3 / (3 + **1**) = 0.75 → **25%** inhibited","[I] = 180 μM: 3 / (3 + **3**) = 0.50 → **50%** inhibited"]},{"text":"Read the curve: same Ki, but tripling [I] moves the enzyme from 25% to 50% inhibited.","hl":"raised from 60 μM to 180 μM","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Competitive inhibitor, Ki = 60 μM, [S] = 2Km","x":{"label":"[I] (μM)","min":0,"max":360,"step":60},"y":{"label":"% of enzyme activity inhibited","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Ki = 60 μM","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[60,25],[120,40],[180,50],[240,57],[300,63],[360,67]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":60},{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":180}],"spans":[{"x":60,"from":0,"to":25,"text":"25%","side":"right"},{"x":180,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"50%","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":60,"text":"[I] = 60 μM","at":"above"},{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":180,"text":"[I] = 180 μM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Answer: Ki is unchanged, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited increases. Unchanged inhibition would require [I] to stay the same.","strike":["Ki is unchanged, and the fraction of enzyme inhibited is unchanged"]}],"trap":"\"Ki triples\" assumes Ki tracks [I]. It doesn't: Ki is a constant, and only the degree of inhibition changes."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it stands for.","marks":[{"t":"Ki = 60 μM","note":"inhibitor binding constant"},{"t":"[S] = 2Km","note":"substrate amount"},{"t":"from 60 μM to 180 μM","note":"inhibitor tripled"}]},{"text":"A **competitive inhibitor** sits in the enzyme's active site, the same spot the substrate needs, so it blocks the substrate.","marks":[{"t":"competitive inhibitor","note":"fights substrate for site"}]},{"text":"**Ki** = an equilibrium constant for the inhibitor letting go of the enzyme. Small Ki = sticks tightly.","marks":[{"t":"Ki = 60 μM","note":"dissociation constant"}],"calc":["EI ⇌ E + I","Ki = [E][I] / [EI]"]},{"text":"Like every equilibrium constant, **Ki changes only with temperature (or pH)**, never with how much inhibitor you add.","marks":[{"t":"temperature and pH unchanged","note":"so Ki stays 60 μM"},{"t":"from 60 μM to 180 μM","note":"[I] changed, not Ki"}]},{"text":"More inhibitor means more enzyme gets blocked. Work out **how much activity is lost** in each trial.","marks":[{"t":"[S] = 2Km","note":"S = 2 × Km"},{"t":"from 60 μM to 180 μM","note":"[I]/Ki: 1 then 3"}],"calc":["v with I ÷ v without = 3 / (3 + [I]/Ki)","60 μM: 3 / (3 + 1) = 0.75 → **25% inhibited**","180 μM: 3 / (3 + 3) = 0.50 → **50% inhibited**"]},{"text":"**See it:** same enzyme, same Ki, one curve. Tripling [I] moves you up the curve from 25% to 50% inhibited.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Competitive inhibitor, Ki = 60 μM, [S] = 2Km","x":{"label":"[I] (μM)","min":0,"max":360,"step":60},"y":{"label":"% of activity inhibited","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Ki = 60 μM","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[60,25],[120,40],[180,50],[240,57],[300,63],[360,67]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":60},{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":180}],"labels":[{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":60,"text":"60 μM: 25%","at":"above"},{"series":"Ki = 60 μM","x":180,"text":"180 μM: 50%","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":180,"from":25,"to":50,"text":"+25%","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Wrong ideas:** \"Ki triples\" mixes up [I] with Ki; \"Ki falls\" has no cause; \"inhibition unchanged\" ignores the extra inhibitor.","marks":[{"t":"Ki = 60 μM","note":"constant: does not move"},{"t":"180 μM trial","note":"more I → more blocked"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ki, Km and Kd are constants: changing a concentration never changes them (only T or pH can). Changing [I] changes how much enzyme is bound."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"An enzyme reduces acetophenone, shown, using NADPH; overall the substrate gains two electrons and two hydrogen ions, and its carbon skeleton is unchanged. Which structure is the product?",idStem:"An enzyme adds two electrons and two hydrogens to a ketone. What forms?",fig:"acetophenone",choiceFigs:["1-phenylethanol","2-phenylethanol","phenylacetic-acid","ethylbenzene"],choices:["Secondary alcohol","Primary alcohol","Carboxylic acid","Alkane"],ans:0,exp:"Adding 2 e⁻ and 2 H⁺ adds H₂ across the C=O: the carbonyl carbon keeps its two carbon neighbors and gains an H, giving **a secondary alcohol, 1-phenylethanol**. Its oxidation state falls from **+2 to 0**. The alkane would need 4 e⁻ (carbon at −2), the acid is an oxidation (+3), and the primary alcohol moves the oxygen to a different carbon.",src:"pe5-cp:5",how:{"ask":"Two electrons plus two H⁺ add one H to each end of the C=O. Which drawing keeps the oxygen on the same carbon, now as C–OH?","know":"• **Reduction** = gain of electrons. In organic molecules it shows up as gaining C–H bonds or losing C–O bonds.\n• **Oxidation state of a carbon:** count +1 for each bond to O or N, −1 for each bond to H, 0 for each bond to C.\n• NADPH delivers a hydride, H⁻ (2 e⁻ + 1 H⁺); a second H⁺ comes from the solvent. Net: H₂ added across C=O.\n• A ketone carbon has two carbon neighbors, so the alcohol it gives is **secondary**: the C–OH carbon is bonded to two carbons.\n• On product drawings, check that every atom stays on the same carbon unless a step moves it.","steps":[{"text":"Two electrons plus two H⁺ amount to H₂: one H goes on the carbonyl carbon and one on the oxygen.","hl":"gains two electrons and two hydrogen ions"},{"text":"Find the carbonyl carbon, highlighted, and assign its oxidation state.","fig":"acetophenone-hl","calc":["C=O counts **+2**; two C–C bonds count **0**","carbonyl carbon in acetophenone: **+2**"]},{"text":"Add H to each end of the C=O. The same carbon now carries OH and H and is still bonded to two carbons: a secondary alcohol.","fig":"1-phenylethanol-hl","calc":["C–O **+1**, C–H **−1**, two C–C **0**","alcohol carbon: **0**","change: +2 → 0 = **gain of 2 e⁻**"]},{"text":"The alkane carbon would be −2, a gain of 4 e⁻: too many. The acid carbon is +3: that is an oxidation.","strike":["Alkane","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"The primary alcohol puts the OH on the end carbon, which never held the oxygen.","strike":["Primary alcohol"]},{"text":"Answer: the secondary alcohol. Rule: reducing a ketone gives a secondary alcohol on the same carbon; reducing an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol."}],"trap":"The primary alcohol has the same formula, C₈H₁₀O, but its OH sits on the end carbon. Reduction adds H across the existing C=O; it does not move the oxygen."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners and line ends are carbons; hidden H fill each carbon to 4 bonds**. A double line is two bonds between the same two atoms.","fig":"t5-ap-read"},{"text":"**A carbon double-bonded to O is a carbonyl**. Its carbon touches 2 other carbons: the CH₃ and a ring carbon. The reaction happens here.","fig":"t5-ap-co"},{"text":"**H⁺ is a hydrogen without its electron**. 2 electrons + 2 H⁺ = 2 whole H atoms. Gaining electrons is called reduction.","fig":"t5-ap-2h"},{"text":"**One bond of the C=O opens: an H joins the carbon, an H joins the oxygen**. The carbon keeps the same CH₃ and ring.","fig":["t5-ap-2h","t5-pe1-oh"]},{"text":"**An OH on a carbon with only single bonds is an alcohol**. Two carbon neighbors = secondary alcohol; one would be primary.","fig":"t5-pe1-2nd"},{"text":"**Here the O sits on the far carbon**. Adding H atoms never moves an O to a different carbon.","fig":"t5-pe2-moved"},{"text":"**Removing the O entirely needs 4 electrons and 4 H⁺, with the O leaving as water: twice what the question gives**.","fig":"t5-eb-noo"},{"text":"**This carbon has 3 bonds to O**. More bonds to O means electrons were lost: oxidation, the opposite of reduction.","fig":"t5-paa-ox"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on a C=O gives H–C–OH on the same carbon. In each drawing ask: is the OH on the carbon that held the C=O, same neighbors?","fig":"t5-ap-rule"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Flavin mononucleotide, FMN, is shown with four atoms labeled. FMN accepts two electrons and two protons to become neutral FMNH₂, and each added proton ends up bonded to a ring nitrogen that bore no hydrogen in FMN. Which labeled atom gains a hydrogen?",idStem:"FAD and FMN share which redox-active component?",fig:"fmn-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"The redox work is done by the **isoalloxazine ring**, the three fused rings. In neutral FMNH₂ the two new H atoms sit on **N5 and N1**, the ring nitrogens that make only two bonds and have a free lone pair. Site II is N5. N3 (I) already has its H, N10 (III) already makes three bonds, and the phosphate (IV) lies outside the ring system; in FAD it is where the AMP unit attaches.",src:"pe5-cp:6",how:{"ask":"Count the bonds on each labeled atom. Which ring nitrogen has room for one more bond to H and stays neutral?","know":"• **Flavins** (FMN, FAD) carry 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on the isoalloxazine ring: the three flat fused rings of riboflavin. The ribityl chain and phosphate only hold the cofactor in the enzyme.\n• A neutral N makes **three bonds** and keeps one lone pair. A ring N drawn with only two bonds can take an H and stay neutral; an N already making three bonds would become N⁺ if it took another.\n• The two N that gain H in FMNH₂ are N5 and N1, the two unsubstituted nitrogens of the central ring.\n• On a cofactor drawing, look for the conjugated ring system (alternating single and double bonds): that is where electrons can be added.","steps":[{"text":"The question gives the rule: each H goes on a ring nitrogen that had no H.","hl":"each added proton ends up bonded to a ring nitrogen that bore no hydrogen in FMN"},{"text":"Count the bonds to each labeled atom.","fig":"fmn-sites","calc":["I, N3: already N–H: **has an H**","II, N5: **2 bonds**, free lone pair","III, N10: **3 bonds**, two ring + ribityl","IV: phosphorus of the phosphate, **not a ring N**"]},{"text":"I already carries an H, and IV is outside the ring system: in FAD this phosphate links to AMP, which is not the redox part.","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"N10 has no room: a fourth bond would make it N⁺, but FMNH₂ is neutral.","strike":["III"]},{"text":"In FMNH₂ the two new H atoms, highlighted, sit on N5 and N1 of the central ring.","fig":"fmnh2"},{"text":"Answer: II, N5. Rule: in flavins, N5 and N1 of the isoalloxazine ring take the two hydrogens; the sugar chain and phosphates are only handles."}],"trap":"III, N10, also has no H, but it already makes three bonds: two in the ring and one to the ribityl chain. Adding H would give a positive N, not neutral FMNH₂."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. Letters: N nitrogen, O oxygen, P phosphorus. Rings sharing an edge are fused. I–IV point at four atoms.","fig":"fmn-sites"},{"text":"**A proton is H⁺**. Each new H lands on a ring nitrogen that had no H. The ring nitrogens are highlighted.","fig":"t5-fmn-task"},{"text":"**Site IV is a phosphorus in the phosphate tail, outside the rings**. It is a handle, not where H lands.","fig":"t5-fmn-iv"},{"text":"**Site I is written NH: it already carries an H, so it is not a nitrogen that bore none**.","fig":"t5-fmn-i"},{"text":"**A neutral N makes 3 bonds and keeps one lone pair (its own electron pair)**. Site III already has 3 single bonds: full, no room for H.","fig":"t5-fmn-iii"},{"text":"**Site II touches only 2 atoms, one by a double line**. Added electrons open that double line, freeing a bond on N for an H.","fig":"t5-fmn-ii"},{"text":"**In FMNH₂ the new H atoms sit on site II's N and on the other two-neighbor N**. The double lines in the rings shift to make room.","fig":"t5-fmnh2-new"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: a ring N takes a new H only if it has no H and touches just two atoms. At each label ask: is it N? has it an H? how many atoms does it touch?","fig":"t5-fmn-rule"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A crystallization drop contains 150 mM NaCl, 20 mM Tris buffer and 0.75 mM enzyme. What is the approximate molar ratio of Na⁺ ions to enzyme molecules in the drop?",idStem:"A solution has 150 mM Na⁺ and 0.75 mM enzyme. What is the cation-to-enzyme ratio?",choices:["20:1","1:200","200:1","2000:1"],ans:2,exp:"**Divide concentrations in the same units:** 150 mM ÷ 0.75 mM = 200, so Na⁺ : enzyme = **200:1**. Each NaCl gives one Na⁺; Tris is not needed.",src:"pe5-cp:7",how:{"ask":"Compare two concentrations in the same volume, keeping track of which goes on top.","know":"In one solution, the **ratio of moles = ratio of concentrations** (same volume). Make sure both are in the same units (here both mM). NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻, so [Na⁺] = [NaCl].","steps":[{"text":"NaCl dissociates 1:1, so [Na⁺] = 150 mM. Tris does not contain Na⁺; it is a distractor.","hl":"150 mM NaCl"},{"text":"Line up the concentrations. All are in the same drop, so the concentration ratio equals the mole ratio.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Species in the crystallization drop","x":{"label":"Species"},"y":{"label":"Concentration (mM)","min":0,"max":160,"step":40},"categories":["Na⁺","Cl⁻","Tris","Enzyme"],"series":[{"name":"Concentration","values":[150,150,20,0.75]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na⁺","Concentration"],["Enzyme","Concentration"]]}},{"text":"Divide in the same units: the mM cancels.","hl":"0.75 mM enzyme","calc":["Na⁺ : enzyme = 150 mM ÷ 0.75 mM","= **200**","→ **200 : 1**"]},{"text":"Order matters: the question names Na⁺ first, so Na⁺ goes on top. 1:200 is the flipped ratio.","strike":["1:200"]},{"text":"20:1 and 2000:1 are decimal slips (150 ÷ 7.5 and 150 ÷ 0.075). Answer: 200:1.","strike":["20:1","2000:1"]}],"trap":"1:200 is the same numbers flipped. The question asks Na⁺ to enzyme, so Na⁺ goes first. 20:1 is a decimal slip (150 ÷ 7.5)."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number**: three things dissolved in one drop.","marks":[{"t":"150 mM NaCl","note":"salt concentration"},{"t":"20 mM Tris buffer","note":"buffer concentration"},{"t":"0.75 mM enzyme","note":"enzyme concentration"}]},{"text":"**mM** = millimoles per liter: how crowded each thing is. All three share **one drop**, so comparing concentrations compares counts.","marks":[{"t":"molar ratio","note":"how many of one per other"}],"calc":["same volume for all","→ ratio of mM = **ratio of particles**"]},{"text":"**NaCl** is table salt. In water it splits into one Na⁺ and one Cl⁻, so there is **one Na⁺ per NaCl**.","marks":[{"t":"150 mM NaCl","note":"→ 150 mM Na⁺"},{"t":"Na⁺ ions","note":"come from NaCl"}],"calc":["NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻","[Na⁺] = **150 mM**"]},{"text":"Tris has no sodium in it. **It is not needed.** Exams add extra numbers on purpose.","marks":[{"t":"20 mM Tris buffer","note":"not needed: no Na⁺"}]},{"text":"**See it:** Na⁺ towers over the enzyme. The question asks how many times taller.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Species in the crystallization drop","x":{"label":"Species"},"y":{"label":"Concentration (mM)","min":0,"max":160,"step":40},"categories":["Na⁺","Cl⁻","Tris","Enzyme"],"series":[{"name":"Concentration","values":[150,150,20,0.75]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na⁺","Concentration"],["Enzyme","Concentration"]]}},{"text":"**Divide** Na⁺ by enzyme. Both are in mM, so the units cancel.","marks":[{"t":"150 mM NaCl","note":"top: 150"},{"t":"0.75 mM enzyme","note":"bottom: 0.75"}],"calc":["150 mM ÷ 0.75 mM","= 15000 ÷ 75 = **200**","Na⁺ : enzyme = **200 : 1**"]},{"text":"**Order matters:** Na⁺ is named first, so it goes first. 1:200 is flipped. 20:1 and 2000:1 are decimal-point slips.","marks":[{"t":"Na⁺ ions to enzyme molecules","note":"Na⁺ first, enzyme second"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in one solution, the particle ratio = the concentration ratio (same units). Count ions per formula unit (NaCl gives 1 Na⁺) and skip species that don't contain the ion."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"In an inner mitochondrial membrane complex, electrons pass from NADH (E°′ = −0.32 V) to ubiquinone (E°′ = +0.05 V), a step with ΔG°′ ≈ −70 kJ/mol. Calorimetry shows far less heat released in intact membranes than when the same electron transfer occurs in free solution. Which explanation is most consistent?",idStem:"An exergonic redox reaction releases little heat inside a cell. What best explains this?",choices:["Part of the free energy is used to move H⁺ against its gradient","Splitting the transfer into several steps lowers the overall ΔH","The enzyme makes ΔG°′ less negative by lowering the activation energy","The enzyme lowers the reaction's ΔH by providing an alternate pathway"],ans:0,exp:"ΔH and ΔG are **state functions**; catalysts and multistep paths can't change them (Hess's law). The energy that isn't released as heat is **coupled to work**: pumping H⁺ up its gradient.",src:"pe5-cp:8",how:{"ask":"If the overall reaction is the same, where else could the released energy go besides heat?","know":"**State function:** depends only on start and end, not the path (ΔH, ΔG). **Hess's law:** the ΔH of a reaction is the same whether it happens in one step or many. A **catalyst** lowers Ea only. Cells **couple** favorable reactions to unfavorable work, like moving ions against a concentration gradient.","steps":[{"text":"Check the free energy from the potentials first.","hl":"ΔG°′ ≈ −70 kJ/mol","calc":["ΔE°′ = E°′(acceptor) − E°′(donor)","ΔE°′ = 0.05 − (−0.32) = **0.37 V**","ΔG°′ = −nFΔE°′ = −2 × 96,485 × 0.37","ΔG°′ ≈ **−71 kJ/mol** ✓"]},{"text":"Same reaction, same start and end states, yet less heat appears in membranes. The energy must be going somewhere other than heat.","hl":"far less heat released in intact membranes"},{"text":"ΔH is a state function: splitting the path into steps or providing an alternate pathway cannot change it (Hess's law).","strike":["Splitting the transfer into several steps lowers the overall ΔH","The enzyme lowers the reaction's ΔH by providing an alternate pathway"]},{"text":"Lowering the activation energy speeds the reaction but never changes ΔG°′.","strike":["The enzyme makes ΔG°′ less negative by lowering the activation energy"]},{"text":"Complex I couples the electron transfer to pumping H⁺ out of the matrix. Energy stored as an H⁺ gradient is work, so it is not released as heat. Answer: part of the free energy moves H⁺ against its gradient.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Fate of the ≈71 kJ/mol, illustrative split","x":{"label":"Setting"},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"categories":["Free solution","Intact membrane"],"series":[{"name":"Released as heat","values":[71,30]},{"name":"Stored as H⁺ gradient","values":[0,41]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Intact membrane","Stored as H⁺ gradient"]]}}],"trap":"\"Splitting into steps lowers ΔH\" sounds like how cells work, but Hess's law says the total ΔH is the same for any path."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number**: two molecules trading electrons, and the energy of that step.","marks":[{"t":"NADH (E°′ = −0.32 V)","note":"electron giver"},{"t":"ubiquinone (E°′ = +0.05 V)","note":"electron taker"},{"t":"ΔG°′ ≈ −70 kJ/mol","note":"energy released"}]},{"text":"**E°′** = how hard a molecule pulls on electrons. Electrons run downhill from the lower (more negative) one to the higher one.","marks":[{"t":"−0.32 V","note":"weak pull: gives e⁻"},{"t":"+0.05 V","note":"stronger pull: takes e⁻"}],"calc":["ΔE°′ = taker − giver","ΔE°′ = 0.05 − (−0.32) = **0.37 V**"]},{"text":"**ΔG** = free energy the step can give off. Negative = energy released. The numbers agree.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG°′ ≈ −70 kJ/mol","note":"checks out ✓"}],"calc":["ΔG°′ = −nFΔE°′ (n = 2 electrons)","= −2 × 96,485 × 0.37","≈ **−71 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Calorimetry** measures heat. Same reaction, but the membrane gives off **far less heat** than plain solution.","marks":[{"t":"Calorimetry","note":"measures heat"},{"t":"far less heat released in intact membranes","note":"less heat here"},{"t":"free solution","note":"all comes out as heat"}]},{"text":"**State function:** ΔG and ΔH depend only on start and end, not the path. One big step or many small ones end at the same place.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same start and end, two paths","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-80,"max":20,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"One step (solution)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,15],[4,-70]]},{"name":"Many steps (membrane)","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,-15],[2,-30],[3,-50],[4,-70]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"One step (solution)","x":0,"text":"same start","at":"above"},{"series":"Many steps (membrane)","x":4,"text":"same end","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":-70,"text":"ΔG = −70 either way","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"So splitting into steps or using an enzyme **can't change ΔH or ΔG**. An enzyme only lowers the hill (activation energy) to go faster.","marks":[{"t":"same electron transfer","note":"same start + end"}]},{"text":"Energy is either heat or **work**. In the membrane, part of it pumps **H⁺ uphill** across the membrane, storing it instead of heating.","marks":[{"t":"inner mitochondrial membrane complex","note":"pumps H⁺ across"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Where the ≈71 kJ/mol goes (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Setting"},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"categories":["Free solution","Intact membrane"],"series":[{"name":"Released as heat","values":[71,30]},{"name":"Stored as H⁺ gradient","values":[0,41]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Intact membrane","Stored as H⁺ gradient"]]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG and ΔH are fixed by start and end (Hess's law); catalysts and extra steps never change them. Less heat than expected → energy was coupled to work, like pumping ions."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"Two open cylindrical tanks hold water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³). Tank A has a diameter of 2.0 m and holds 3000 L; tank B has a diameter of 0.50 m and holds 200 L. How does the absolute pressure 10 cm below the surface in tank A compare with that in tank B?",idStem:"Two open tanks hold different volumes of the same liquid. How do the pressures compare 10 cm below each surface?",choices:["P_A > P_B, since more water lies above the point in A","P_A = P_B, since pressure depends on ρ, g and depth alone","P_A < P_B, since the water's weight acts on a smaller area in B","P_A = P_B, since both tanks are open to the atmosphere"],ans:1,exp:"**P = P_atm + ρgh.** Same liquid, same depth, same open surface → same pressure. Volume and tank width don't appear in the formula.",src:"pe5-cp:9",how:{"ask":"Which quantities set the pressure at a point under a liquid surface?","know":"**Hydrostatic pressure:** P = P_atm + ρgh, where ρ = density (kg/m³), g = 9.8 m/s², h = depth below the surface (m). It doesn't depend on the container's shape, width or total volume.","steps":[{"text":"In a fluid at rest, pressure depends on how deep the point is below the open surface, not on how much fluid is in the container.","hl":"10 cm below the surface"},{"text":"Compute the pressure at 10 cm in either tank.","calc":["P = P_atm + ρgh","ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × **0.10 m**","ρgh ≈ **980 Pa**","P ≈ 101,325 + 980 ≈ **102.3 kPa** in both"]},{"text":"Both tanks fall on the same pressure–depth line, whatever their width or volume.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absolute pressure vs depth in each tank","x":{"label":"Depth below surface (m)","min":0,"max":0.5,"step":0.1},"y":{"label":"P (kPa)","min":101,"max":107,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,101.3],[0.1,102.3],[0.2,103.3],[0.3,104.2],[0.4,105.2],[0.5,106.2]]},{"name":"Tank B, d = 0.50 m","marker":"square-open","line":"none","points":[[0,101.3],[0.1,102.3],[0.2,103.3],[0.3,104.2],[0.4,105.2],[0.5,106.2]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","x":0.1},{"series":"Tank B, d = 0.50 m","x":0.1}],"labels":[{"series":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","x":0,"text":"surface: P_atm = 101.3 kPa","at":"right"},{"series":"Tank B, d = 0.50 m","x":0.1,"text":"10 cm: 102.3 kPa in both","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":0.1,"from":101.3,"to":102.3,"text":"ρgh","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Volume and width don't appear in ρgh. A wider tank has more water, but its weight spreads over a proportionally larger area.","strike":["P_A > P_B, since more water lies above the point in A","P_A < P_B, since the water's weight acts on a smaller area in B"]},{"text":"Being open to the air is needed but not enough: open tanks at different depths still differ. Answer: P_A = P_B, since pressure depends on ρ, g and depth alone.","strike":["P_A = P_B, since both tanks are open to the atmosphere"]}],"trap":"\"Both are open to the atmosphere\" has the right claim but an incomplete reason: open tanks at different depths would differ. Equality needs the same ρ and the same depth."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it describes.","marks":[{"t":"ρ = 1000 kg/m³","note":"density of water"},{"t":"diameter of 2.0 m and holds 3000 L","note":"A: wide, lots of water"},{"t":"diameter of 0.50 m and holds 200 L","note":"B: narrow, little water"}]},{"text":"**Pressure** = push per area. **Absolute** pressure counts everything pushing down: the air on top plus the water above the point.","marks":[{"t":"Two open cylindrical tanks","note":"air presses on top"},{"t":"absolute pressure","note":"air + water above"}],"calc":["P = P_atm + ρgh"]},{"text":"Each letter in the formula is in the question. **h is depth**: how far below the surface, not the tank's size.","marks":[{"t":"ρ = 1000 kg/m³","note":"ρ (rho)"},{"t":"10 cm below the surface","note":"h = depth"}],"calc":["ρ = density, g = 9.8 m/s² (gravity)","h = depth below the surface"]},{"text":"Plug in. **10 cm must be meters** first. Both tanks get the same numbers.","marks":[{"t":"10 cm below the surface","note":"h = 0.10 m"}],"calc":["ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × 0.10 = **980 Pa**","P = 101,325 + 980 ≈ **102.3 kPa**","same ρ, g, h → **same P in both tanks**"]},{"text":"**See it:** pressure grows in a straight line with depth, and both tanks sit on the **same line**.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absolute pressure vs depth in each tank","x":{"label":"Depth below surface (m)","min":0,"max":0.5,"step":0.1},"y":{"label":"P (kPa)","min":101,"max":107,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,101.3],[0.1,102.3],[0.2,103.3],[0.3,104.2],[0.4,105.2],[0.5,106.2]]},{"name":"Tank B, d = 0.50 m","marker":"square-open","line":"none","points":[[0,101.3],[0.1,102.3],[0.2,103.3],[0.3,104.2],[0.4,105.2],[0.5,106.2]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","x":0.1}],"labels":[{"series":"Tank A, d = 2.0 m","x":0,"text":"surface: 101.3 kPa","at":"right"},{"series":"Tank B, d = 0.50 m","x":0.1,"text":"10 cm: 102.3 kPa in both","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":0.1,"from":101.3,"to":102.3,"text":"ρgh","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Width and volume **are not in P = P_atm + ρgh**. They are extra numbers you don't need.","marks":[{"t":"3000 L","note":"not needed"},{"t":"200 L","note":"not needed"},{"t":"diameter of 0.50 m","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Why more water doesn't matter:** the wide tank's extra water also spreads over a wider floor, so push per area stays the same.","calc":["P = weight ÷ area","A: more weight, more area","B: less weight, less area → **same ratio**"],"marks":[{"t":"Tank A has a diameter of 2.0 m","note":"more water, more area"}]},{"text":"Being open to the air is true, but that alone isn't the reason; two open tanks at different depths would differ. **Depth** is what matters.","marks":[{"t":"Two open cylindrical tanks","note":"true, but not the reason"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pressure in a still liquid depends only on ρ, g and depth h (P = P_atm + ρgh). Tank shape, width and total volume never change it."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"A sample contains nuclei that each have a mass number of 14 and contain 8 neutrons. A student identifies them as nitrogen, because the most abundant isotope of nitrogen also has a mass number of 14. Which evaluation of the student's claim is correct?",idStem:"An atom has 6 protons and 8 neutrons. What element is it?",choices:["Correct, since mass number identifies the element","Incorrect; they are oxygen, since they have 8 neutrons","Incorrect; they are carbon, since they have 6 protons","Incorrect; they are silicon, since they have 14 nucleons"],ans:2,exp:"**The number of protons Z defines the element.** Z = A − N = 14 − 8 = 6, which is carbon, so these are ¹⁴C nuclei. Nitrogen-14 has the same A but 7 protons.",src:"pe5-cp:10",how:{"ask":"Work out the proton count from the mass number and the neutron count, then name the element that count defines.","know":"**Atomic number Z = number of protons** → the element. **Mass number A = protons + neutrons.** Isotopes of one element have the same Z but different A. Periodic table: Z = 6 C, 7 N, 8 O, 14 Si.","steps":[{"text":"Mass number A counts nucleons: protons plus neutrons. The stem gives A and N, not Z.","hl":"each have a mass number of 14 and contain 8 neutrons"},{"text":"Solve for the proton count.","calc":["A = Z + N","Z = A − N = 14 − **8**","Z = **6**"]},{"text":"The proton count Z, not A, names the element. Z = 6 is carbon, so the student's reasoning fails.","strike":["Correct, since mass number identifies the element"]},{"text":"Oxygen has 8 protons and silicon has 14 protons. Each wrong choice uses the wrong number as Z.","strike":["Incorrect; they are oxygen, since they have 8 neutrons","Incorrect; they are silicon, since they have 14 nucleons"]},{"text":"Compare with the nucleus the student had in mind. Answer: incorrect; they are carbon, since they have 6 protons.","calc":["¹⁴C: Z = 6, N = 8","¹⁴N: Z = **7**, N = **7**","same A, different Z → **different elements**"]}],"trap":"\"Correct, since mass number identifies the element\" repeats the student's error: ¹⁴C and ¹⁴N share A = 14 but have 6 and 7 protons, so they are different elements."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find the two numbers** given for these nuclei.","marks":[{"t":"each have a mass number of 14","note":"A = 14"},{"t":"8 neutrons","note":"N = 8"}]},{"text":"A **nucleus** holds protons (+ charge) and neutrons (no charge). Together they are called **nucleons**.","marks":[{"t":"each have a mass number of 14","note":"protons + neutrons = 14"}],"calc":["mass number A = protons + neutrons","A = Z + N"]},{"text":"**Atomic number Z = the number of protons.** It alone decides which element it is. Nitrogen always has 7 protons.","marks":[{"t":"identifies them as nitrogen","note":"needs 7 protons to be N"}]},{"text":"Find the protons by subtracting neutrons from the mass number.","marks":[{"t":"each have a mass number of 14","note":"A = 14"},{"t":"8 neutrons","note":"− 8"}],"calc":["Z = A − N","Z = 14 − 8 = **6**","6 protons = **carbon**"]},{"text":"**See it:** same total of 14, but split differently. Different proton counts = **different elements**.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Inside each nucleus","x":{"label":"Nucleus"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["This sample (¹⁴C)","Nitrogen-14"],"series":[{"name":"Protons","values":[6,7]},{"name":"Neutrons","values":[8,7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["This sample (¹⁴C)","Protons"],["Nitrogen-14","Protons"]]}},{"text":"The student matched the **mass number**, but many elements share a mass number. **Isotopes** = same protons, different neutrons.","marks":[{"t":"also has a mass number of 14","note":"same A ≠ same element"}]},{"text":"**Wrong ideas:** oxygen would need 8 **protons** (not neutrons); silicon would need 14 **protons** (not 14 nucleons).","marks":[{"t":"8 neutrons","note":"neutrons ≠ element"},{"t":"each have a mass number of 14","note":"nucleons ≠ element"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the element is set by protons (Z) only. Z = A − N. Equal mass numbers do not mean the same element."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Four molecules are drawn as the answer choices, with lone pairs omitted. Which molecule has a nonzero dipole moment?",idStem:"Which molecule is polar?",choiceFigs:["silicon-tetrafluoride","carbon-disulfide","boron-trichloride","phosphorus-trichloride"],choices:["Silicon tetrafluoride","Carbon disulfide","Boron trichloride","Phosphorus trichloride"],ans:3,exp:"**PCl₃** has 4 electron domains on P: three bonds and **one lone pair**, so it is trigonal pyramidal and its three P–Cl dipoles do not cancel. SiF₄ is tetrahedral, CS₂ linear and BCl₃ trigonal planar, each with no lone pair on the central atom, so their bond dipoles cancel.",src:"pe5-cp:11",how:{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonded atoms and lone pairs. Which shape leaves the bond dipoles unbalanced?","know":"• **Dipole moment:** a molecule has one when its bond dipoles (arrows from δ+ to δ−) do not add to zero.\n• **Electron domains** on the central atom = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double bond counts as one domain).\n• Lone pairs on the center = (valence electrons − electrons it uses in bonds) ÷ 2.\n• Identical outer atoms arranged around a center with **no lone pairs** (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) cancel. A lone pair bends the shape (pyramidal, bent) and leaves a net dipole.\n• Drawings often omit lone pairs, so count them from the group number.","steps":[{"text":"A net dipole needs polar bonds that do not cancel. All four have polar bonds, so the shape decides.","hl":"nonzero dipole moment"},{"text":"The drawings show bonds only, so count the central atom's lone pairs from its valence electrons.","fig":["silicon-tetrafluoride","carbon-disulfide","boron-trichloride","phosphorus-trichloride"],"calc":["Si: 4 valence e⁻, 4 bonds → **0** lone pairs","C in CS₂: 4 e⁻, two double bonds → **0**","B: 3 e⁻, 3 bonds → **0**","P: 5 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 left = **1** lone pair"]},{"text":"Turn domains into shapes.","calc":["SiF₄: 4 domains, 0 lone → tetrahedral, cancels","CS₂: 2 domains → linear, cancels","BCl₃: 3 domains → trigonal planar, cancels","PCl₃: 4 domains, 1 lone → **pyramidal**, does not cancel"]},{"text":"Symmetric shapes with no lone pair on the center cancel their bond dipoles.","strike":["Silicon tetrafluoride","Carbon disulfide","Boron trichloride"]},{"text":"Answer: PCl₃. Rule: identical outer atoms plus a lone pair on the center make a polar molecule.","fig":"phosphorus-trichloride"}],"trap":"Every molecule here has polar bonds, so a polar-bond argument does not separate them. Boron trichloride is tempting because B has an incomplete octet, but with no lone pair its three B–Cl dipoles cancel at 120°."},teach:[{"text":"**Each letter is an atom and each line a bond**. Lone pairs are NOT drawn here, so you must count them yourself.","fig":"t5-pcl3-read"},{"text":"**The atom with higher electronegativity (pulling power) takes more of the shared electrons**. Each lopsided bond is a bond dipole.","fig":"t5-pcl3-pull"},{"text":"**The dipole moment is the sum of all the pulls**. In straight-line S=C=S the two pulls point opposite ways and cancel: zero.","fig":"t5-cs2-cancel"},{"text":"**Bonds and lone pairs (unshared electron pairs) on the central atom spread apart**. No lone pair = balanced shape; a lone pair pushes bonds aside.","fig":"t5-pcl3-lp"},{"text":"**To find lone pairs: central atom's outer electrons (B 3, C and Si 4, P 5) minus one per bond (two per double bond)**. Two left = one lone pair."},{"text":"**C has 4 outer electrons; two double bonds use all 4**. No lone pair: a straight line, so the pulls cancel.","fig":"t5-cs2-c"},{"text":"**B has 3; three bonds use all 3**. No lone pair: a flat triangle, 120° apart, so the pulls cancel.","fig":"t5-bcl3-b"},{"text":"**Si has 4; four bonds use all 4**. No lone pair: a tetrahedron (four equal faces), so the pulls cancel.","fig":"t5-sif4-si"},{"text":"**P has 5; three bonds use 3, leaving one lone pair**. It pushes the three Cl into a pyramid, so their pulls add up instead of cancelling.","fig":"t5-pcl3-p"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: lopsided bonds + an unbalanced shape (usually a lone pair on the central atom) = a dipole. In each drawing, count outer electrons minus bonds: anything left?","fig":"t5-pcl3-p"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A sample of an ideal gas in a cylinder with a movable piston is at 27 °C and 1.0 atm and contains 0.50 mol. Which single change, with the other variables held constant, would NOT double the gas's volume?",idStem:"All else held constant, which change would NOT double a gas's volume?",choices:["Raising the temperature from 27 °C to 327 °C","Raising the pressure from 1.0 atm to 2.0 atm","Lowering the pressure from 1.0 atm to 0.50 atm","Adding 0.50 mol of the same gas"],ans:1,exp:"From PV = nRT, **V = nRT/P**. Doubling P halves V. Doubling n, halving P, or doubling the kelvin temperature (300 K → 600 K) each doubles V.",src:"pe5-cp:12",how:{"ask":"How does V depend on n, T and P, and which temperatures are really doubled?","know":"**Ideal gas law: PV = nRT** → V = nRT/P. V rises with n and with T (in **kelvin**), and falls with P. K = °C + 273.","steps":[{"text":"Solve the ideal gas law for V and note how it depends on each variable, holding the others fixed.","hl":"with the other variables held constant","calc":["PV = nRT → V = nRT/P","V ∝ n, V ∝ T in kelvin, V ∝ 1/P"]},{"text":"Temperature must be in kelvin.","calc":["27 °C + 273 = **300 K**","327 °C + 273 = **600 K**","T × 2 → V × 2"],"strike":["Raising the temperature from 27 °C to 327 °C"]},{"text":"Halving P or doubling n each doubles V.","calc":["P: 1.0 → 0.50 atm → V × **2**","n: 0.50 → 1.00 mol → V × **2**"],"strike":["Lowering the pressure from 1.0 atm to 0.50 atm","Adding 0.50 mol of the same gas"]},{"text":"Doubling P does the opposite: V is halved. Answer: raising the pressure to 2.0 atm does not double V.","hl":"would NOT double","calc":["P: 1.0 → 2.0 atm → V × **½**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Volume after each change","x":{"label":"Change"},"y":{"label":"V ÷ starting V","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Start","327 °C","0.50 atm","+0.50 mol","2.0 atm"],"series":[{"name":"V ÷ V₀","values":[1,2,2,2,0.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["2.0 atm","V ÷ V₀"]]}}],"trap":"\"27 °C to 327 °C\" looks like a 12× change, so it seems wrong, but in kelvin it is 300 K → 600 K, exactly double."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number**: the gas's starting conditions.","marks":[{"t":"27 °C","note":"temperature"},{"t":"1.0 atm","note":"pressure"},{"t":"0.50 mol","note":"amount of gas"}]},{"text":"A **movable piston** is a lid that slides, so the gas's volume can change. The **ideal gas law** ties all four quantities together.","marks":[{"t":"movable piston","note":"volume can change"}],"calc":["PV = nRT","P pressure, V volume, n moles, T temperature"]},{"text":"Change **one thing at a time**. Solve for V to see how each one moves it.","marks":[{"t":"with the other variables held constant","note":"change one at a time"}],"calc":["V = nRT / P","n up → V up; T up → V up","P up → **V down**"]},{"text":"**Temperature must be in kelvin** (K = °C + 273). 327 °C is double 27 °C only in kelvin, and that doubles V.","marks":[{"t":"27 °C","note":"= 300 K"}],"calc":["27 + 273 = **300 K**","327 + 273 = **600 K**","T × 2 → **V × 2**"]},{"text":"Adding 0.50 mol doubles the gas. More gas → twice the volume.","marks":[{"t":"0.50 mol","note":"+0.50 → 1.00 mol"}],"calc":["n: 0.50 → 1.00 mol → **V × 2**"]},{"text":"**Pressure works backwards.** Squeeze harder, the gas shrinks. Half the pressure → double V; double the pressure → half V.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 atm","note":"V ∝ 1/P"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"V vs P for this gas (0.50 mol, 300 K)","x":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Volume (L)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"V = nRT/P","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,24.6],[1,12.3],[1.5,8.2],[2,6.2],[2.5,4.9]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":1,"text":"start: 12.3 L","at":"right"},{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":0.5,"text":"0.50 atm: 24.6 L (×2)","at":"right"},{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":2,"text":"2.0 atm: 6.2 L (×½)","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Compare all four changes.** Three double V; one halves it.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Volume after each change","x":{"label":"Change"},"y":{"label":"V ÷ starting V","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Start","327 °C","0.50 atm","+0.50 mol","2.0 atm"],"series":[{"name":"V ÷ V₀","values":[1,2,2,2,0.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["2.0 atm","V ÷ V₀"]]}},{"text":"The question asks which would **NOT** double V: the odd one out is raising the pressure to 2.0 atm.","marks":[{"t":"would NOT double","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = nRT/P. V follows n and kelvin T up, and goes opposite to P. Always convert °C to K before comparing temperatures."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Krypton has atomic number 36 and an average atomic mass of 83.80 u. Natural krypton is about 57% ⁸⁴Kr and 17% ⁸⁶Kr. Compared with a ⁸⁴Kr nucleus, how many additional neutrons does a ⁸⁶Kr nucleus have, and how many neutrons does ⁸⁶Kr contain in total?",idStem:"How many neutrons are in krypton-86 (atomic number 36)?",choices:["2 additional; 48 neutrons","0 additional; 50 neutrons","2 additional; 50 neutrons","2 additional; 86 neutrons"],ans:2,exp:"Isotopes share Z, so the 2 extra nucleons in ⁸⁶Kr are neutrons. **N = A − Z** = 86 − 36 = **50**. The average atomic mass describes the natural mixture, not one isotope.",src:"pe5-cp:13",how:{"ask":"Use the fact that isotopes of one element share a proton count, then subtract the atomic number from this isotope's own mass number.","know":"**Mass number A** (the superscript) = protons + neutrons. **Atomic number Z** = protons. So **N = A − Z**. The periodic table's average atomic mass is a weighted average over all isotopes.","steps":[{"text":"Every krypton nucleus has 36 protons. Isotopes of one element differ only in neutrons.","hl":"atomic number 36"},{"text":"The two isotopes differ by 2 nucleons, and none of them can be protons.","calc":["ΔA = 86 − 84 = **2**","ΔZ = 0 (both krypton)","→ **2 additional neutrons**"],"strike":["0 additional; 50 neutrons"]},{"text":"Neutrons = mass number − atomic number, using this isotope's own A.","calc":["N = A − Z","N(⁸⁶Kr) = **86** − 36 = **50**","check: N(⁸⁴Kr) = 84 − 36 = 48; 50 − 48 = **2** ✓"]},{"text":"83.80 u is a weighted average over all isotopes. 83.80 − 36 ≈ 48 is not ⁸⁶Kr's neutron count.","hl":"average atomic mass of 83.80 u","strike":["2 additional; 48 neutrons"]},{"text":"86 counts all nucleons, protons included. Answer: 2 additional; 50 neutrons.","strike":["2 additional; 86 neutrons"]}],"trap":"\"2 additional; 48 neutrons\" uses the average atomic mass: 83.80 − 36 ≈ 48 describes the mixture (and happens to be ⁸⁴Kr's count), not ⁸⁶Kr. Use the isotope's own mass number, 86."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what each number is**.","marks":[{"t":"atomic number 36","note":"protons in every Kr"},{"t":"average atomic mass of 83.80 u","note":"average of all isotopes"},{"t":"17% ⁸⁶Kr","note":"86 = mass number"}]},{"text":"The small number on top-left is the **mass number A** = protons + neutrons. The % is just how common that version is.","marks":[{"t":"57% ⁸⁴Kr","note":"A = 84"},{"t":"17% ⁸⁶Kr","note":"A = 86"}],"calc":["A = protons + neutrons"]},{"text":"**Isotopes** = the same element with different neutron counts. Every krypton has **36 protons**, no matter which isotope.","marks":[{"t":"atomic number 36","note":"Z = 36 for all Kr"}]},{"text":"Compare the two nuclei. The extra 2 nucleons can't be protons, so they are **neutrons**.","marks":[{"t":"⁸⁴Kr nucleus","note":"84 nucleons"},{"t":"⁸⁶Kr nucleus","note":"86 nucleons"}],"calc":["86 − 84 = **2** more nucleons","protons: 36 = 36 (no change)","→ **2 additional neutrons**"]},{"text":"Neutrons = mass number − protons, using **this isotope's own A**.","marks":[{"t":"17% ⁸⁶Kr","note":"A = 86"},{"t":"atomic number 36","note":"− 36"}],"calc":["N = A − Z","N(⁸⁶Kr) = 86 − 36 = **50**","check: ⁸⁴Kr = 84 − 36 = 48; 50 − 48 = **2** ✓"]},{"text":"**See it:** same protons, only the neutron bar grows.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Inside two krypton nuclei","x":{"label":"Isotope"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"categories":["⁸⁴Kr","⁸⁶Kr"],"series":[{"name":"Protons","values":[36,36]},{"name":"Neutrons","values":[48,50]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["⁸⁶Kr","Neutrons"]]}},{"text":"**Not needed:** 83.80 u is a mix average (83.80 − 36 ≈ 48 is a trap), and the percentages don't change any nucleus. 86 neutrons forgets the protons.","marks":[{"t":"83.80 u","note":"mixture average: not needed"},{"t":"57%","note":"not needed"},{"t":"17%","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** neutrons N = A − Z, with A from the isotope's own superscript and Z from the element. Isotopes differ only in neutrons; the average atomic mass describes the mix."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"The aromatic heterocycle shown is a building block of several anti-inflammatory drugs. What are its molecular formula and molar mass? Use C = 12, H = 1, N = 14 g/mol.",idStem:"Pyrrole is a five-membered aromatic ring containing one nitrogen. What is its formula?",fig:"pyrazole",choices:["C₃H₄N₂, 68 g/mol","C₃H₃N₂, 67 g/mol","C₃H₈N₂, 72 g/mol","C₄H₄N₂, 80 g/mol"],ans:0,exp:"Each unlabeled corner of a skeletal ring is a **carbon**, filled out to four bonds with H. The ring has **3 C**, each with one H, and **2 N**, one of them N–H: **C₃H₄N₂**, 3(12) + 4(1) + 2(14) = **68 g/mol**.",src:"pe5-cp:14",how:{"ask":"Count the corners: which are carbon, which are nitrogen, and how many hydrogens are hidden on each?","know":"• In a **skeletal (line) drawing**, every unlabeled corner or line end is a carbon. Hydrogens on carbon are not drawn: add enough to give each C four bonds.\n• Heteroatoms (N, O, S) are written as letters, and an H on them is drawn, e.g. NH.\n• In an aromatic five-membered ring each C has two ring bonds plus one double bond, so it carries **one H**.\n• Molar mass = sum of (number of atoms × atomic mass).","steps":[{"text":"Aromatic means the ring keeps its double bonds, so no extra hydrogens are added across them.","hl":"The aromatic heterocycle shown"},{"text":"The highlighted corners carry no letter, so they are carbons. The two N are written out.","fig":"pyrazole-c","calc":["ring atoms: **5** = **3 C** + **2 N**"]},{"text":"Fill each carbon to four bonds with hidden H, then add the H drawn on N.","calc":["each ring C: 2 ring bonds + 1 double bond → **1 H**","3 C × 1 H = **3 H**","N–H drawn on one N: **+1 H**","formula: **C₃H₄N₂**"]},{"text":"Drop the slips: missing the N–H, saturating the ring, or counting six ring atoms.","strike":["C₃H₃N₂, 67 g/mol","C₃H₈N₂, 72 g/mol","C₄H₄N₂, 80 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: C₃H₄N₂, 68 g/mol. Rule: corner = C, fill each C to four bonds with H, then add H drawn on heteroatoms.","calc":["3 × 12 = **36**","4 × 1 = **4**","2 × 14 = **28**","36 + 4 + 28 = **68 g/mol**"]}],"trap":"C₃H₃N₂ forgets the H drawn on nitrogen. C₃H₈N₂ adds hydrogens as if the ring had no double bonds, and C₄H₄N₂ treats the five-membered ring as a six-membered one."},teach:[{"text":"**Unlabeled corners are carbons; hydrogens on carbon are hidden**. An H on N is written (NH). A double line is two bonds.","fig":"t5-pz-read"},{"text":"**Three unlabeled corners = 3 carbons**.","fig":"t5-pz-c"},{"text":"**Carbon always makes 4 bonds**. Each ring carbon shows one single and one double line = 3, so each hides 1 H: 3 H in all.","fig":"t5-pz-h"},{"text":"**There are 2 nitrogens**. One is written NH: 1 more H. The other already has 3 bonds and no H.","fig":"t5-pz-n"},{"text":"**Total: C 3, H 3 + 1 = 4, N 2**. A formula writes each count after its element: C₃H₄N₂.","fig":"t5-pz-sum"},{"text":"**Molar mass adds the atoms' masses: 3 × 12 + 4 × 1 + 2 × 14 = 36 + 4 + 28 = 68 g/mol**.","fig":"t5-pz-mass"},{"text":"**Traps: missing the H on N gives H₃; ignoring double lines and stuffing carbons with H gives too many; calling an N a C adds a carbon**.","fig":"t5-pz-trap"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: unlabeled corners are C, every C gets 4 bonds (missing = hidden H), add H written on N or O. In each answer, check both formula and mass.","fig":"pyrazole-c"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A gas-collection tube with an internal volume of 11 cm³ is filled with N₂ at 0 °C and 1.0 atm. Given R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K), approximately how many moles of N₂ does the tube contain?",idStem:"About how many moles of gas fill an 11 cm³ tube at 0 °C and 1 atm?",choices:["0.5 mol","5 × 10⁻⁴ mol","4 × 10⁻⁵ mol","2 × 10⁻⁶ mol"],ans:1,exp:"At 0 °C and 1 atm (STP), **1 mol of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L**. 11 cm³ = 0.011 L, so n = 0.011 ÷ 22.4 ≈ **5 × 10⁻⁴ mol**.",src:"pe5-cp:15",how:{"ask":"Convert the volume to liters, then find moles at standard temperature and pressure.","know":"**PV = nRT** → n = PV/RT. At **STP** (273 K, 1 atm), RT/P = 0.0821 × 273 ≈ 22.4 L/mol, the **molar volume**. **1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.**","steps":[{"text":"0 °C and 1 atm is STP, where 1 mol of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L. R is given if you prefer PV = nRT; both give the same answer.","hl":"0 °C and 1.0 atm"},{"text":"Convert the volume to liters.","hl":"11 cm³","calc":["11 cm³ = 11 mL","11 mL ÷ 1000 = **0.011 L**"]},{"text":"Solve for n.","calc":["n = PV / RT","n = (1.0 atm × **0.011 L**) / (0.0821 × **273 K**)","n = 0.011 / 22.4","n ≈ **4.9 × 10⁻⁴ mol** ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁴ mol"]},{"text":"0.5 mol skips the cm³ → L step; 4 × 10⁻⁵ and 2 × 10⁻⁶ mol are off by powers of ten. Answer: 5 × 10⁻⁴ mol.","strike":["0.5 mol","4 × 10⁻⁵ mol","2 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]}],"trap":"0.5 mol comes from 11 ÷ 22.4 without converting cm³ to liters."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"11 cm³","note":"volume"},{"t":"0 °C and 1.0 atm","note":"temperature, pressure"},{"t":"R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K)","note":"gas constant"}]},{"text":"**Ideal gas law:** PV = nRT. You want n (moles), so rearrange.","marks":[{"t":"how many moles","note":"n = ?"}],"calc":["PV = nRT","n = PV / RT"]},{"text":"Look at R's units: it uses **liters** and **kelvin**. Your numbers must match those units.","marks":[{"t":"R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K)","note":"needs L and K"}]},{"text":"Convert. **1 cm³ = 1 mL**, and 1000 mL = 1 L. Kelvin = °C + 273.","marks":[{"t":"11 cm³","note":"= 11 mL = 0.011 L"},{"t":"0 °C","note":"= 273 K"}],"calc":["11 cm³ = 11 mL","11 ÷ 1000 = **0.011 L**","0 + 273 = **273 K**"]},{"text":"**Shortcut:** 0 °C and 1 atm is **STP**, where 1 mol of any ideal gas fills **22.4 L**. R × T ÷ P gives that number.","marks":[{"t":"0 °C and 1.0 atm","note":"STP"}],"calc":["RT/P = 0.0821 × 273 ÷ 1.0","= **22.4 L per mol**"]},{"text":"Now divide the volume by 22.4 L per mol.","marks":[{"t":"11 cm³","note":"0.011 L"},{"t":"1.0 atm","note":"P = 1.0"}],"calc":["n = (1.0 × 0.011) / (0.0821 × 273)","n = 0.011 / 22.4","n ≈ **4.9 × 10⁻⁴ mol** ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁴ mol"]},{"text":"**See it:** at STP, moles grow in a straight line with volume. A tiny tube holds a tiny amount.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Moles of gas vs volume at STP","x":{"label":"Volume (mL)","min":0,"max":44,"step":11},"y":{"label":"n (× 10⁻⁴ mol)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"n = V / 22.4 L","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[11,4.9],[22,9.8],[33,14.7],[44,19.6]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"n = V / 22.4 L","x":11}],"labels":[{"series":"n = V / 22.4 L","x":11,"text":"11 mL → 4.9 × 10⁻⁴ mol","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** forgetting cm³ → L uses 11 L and gives ≈ 0.5 mol, 1000× too big. Other choices are powers-of-ten slips.","marks":[{"t":"11 cm³","note":"not 11 L!"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = PV/RT with V in liters and T in kelvin. At STP (0 °C, 1 atm), 1 mol = 22.4 L, so n = V(L) ÷ 22.4."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A fluorescent dye emits at 450 nm when excited at 350 nm. When a student shines 605 nm light on the same dye instead, no 450 nm emission is detected. Given h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s and c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, which statement best explains this?",idStem:"Why can 605 nm light NOT make a sample fluoresce at 450 nm?",choices:["E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀, and the excess energy is lost as heat before emission","E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀, and longer wavelengths are absorbed by the solvent first","E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀, and an emitted photon can't carry more energy than the absorbed one","E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀, and the molecule is ionized rather than excited"],ans:2,exp:"**E = hc/λ**, so longer λ means less energy: E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀. In fluorescence, some energy is lost before emission, so the emitted photon is always **lower in energy** than the absorbed one.",src:"pe5-cp:16",how:{"ask":"Which photon has more energy, and can a molecule give out more energy than it took in?","know":"**Photon energy: E = hc/λ** (h = Planck's constant, c = speed of light, λ = wavelength). Longer λ → lower E. **Fluorescence:** a molecule absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a photon of **lower energy (longer λ)**.","steps":[{"text":"Fluorescence: the dye absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a lower-energy, longer-wavelength photon. 350 nm in, 450 nm out fits.","hl":"emits at 450 nm when excited at 350 nm"},{"text":"Compute both photon energies with E = hc/λ.","calc":["hc = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸ = **1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m**","E₄₅₀ = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ / 450 × 10⁻⁹ = **4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**","E₆₀₅ = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ / 605 × 10⁻⁹ = **3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"On the curve, longer wavelength means lower energy: E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy E = hc/λ","x":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":300,"max":700,"step":100},"y":{"label":"E (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Photon energy","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[300,6.6],[350,5.66],[400,4.95],[450,4.4],[500,3.96],[550,3.6],[605,3.27],[650,3.05],[700,2.83]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Photon energy","x":450},{"series":"Photon energy","x":605}],"labels":[{"series":"Photon energy","x":450,"text":"E₄₅₀ = 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","at":"above"},{"series":"Photon energy","x":605,"text":"E₆₀₅ = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":605,"from":3.27,"to":4.4,"text":"too little energy to make a 450 nm photon","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Both \"E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀\" choices reverse E = hc/λ.","strike":["E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀, and the excess energy is lost as heat before emission","E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀, and the molecule is ionized rather than excited"]},{"text":"Nothing in the stem suggests the solvent absorbs. The real reason is energy conservation: a 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J photon can't produce a 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J photon. Answer: E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀, and an emitted photon can't carry more energy than the absorbed one.","hl":"no 450 nm emission is detected","strike":["E₆₀₅ < E₄₅₀, and longer wavelengths are absorbed by the solvent first"]}],"trap":"\"E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀\" reverses E = hc/λ: the bigger wavelength carries less energy, not more."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find each wavelength** and what it does.","marks":[{"t":"emits at 450 nm","note":"light given out"},{"t":"excited at 350 nm","note":"light taken in"},{"t":"shines 605 nm light","note":"new light in"}]},{"text":"**Fluorescence:** a molecule soaks up light, loses a little energy as heat, then glows. The glow has **less energy** than what went in.","marks":[{"t":"fluorescent dye","note":"absorbs, then glows"}],"calc":["in: 350 nm → out: 450 nm","out has **less** energy than in"]},{"text":"Light comes in packets (**photons**). λ (wavelength) is the length of one wave. The formula links λ to energy.","marks":[{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"h: Planck's constant"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"c: speed of light"}],"calc":["E = hc / λ","λ is on the bottom → longer λ = **less energy**"]},{"text":"Compute both energies. Turn nm into meters (× 10⁻⁹).","marks":[{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"× c"},{"t":"emits at 450 nm","note":"450 × 10⁻⁹ m"},{"t":"shines 605 nm light","note":"605 × 10⁻⁹ m"}],"calc":["hc = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸ = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵","E₄₅₀ = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ / 450 × 10⁻⁹ = **4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**","E₆₀₅ = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ / 605 × 10⁻⁹ = **3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"**See it:** energy falls as wavelength grows. 605 nm sits lower than 450 nm.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy E = hc/λ","x":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":300,"max":700,"step":100},"y":{"label":"E (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Photon energy","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[300,6.6],[350,5.66],[400,4.95],[450,4.4],[500,3.96],[550,3.6],[605,3.27],[650,3.05],[700,2.83]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Photon energy","x":450},{"series":"Photon energy","x":605}],"labels":[{"series":"Photon energy","x":350,"text":"350 nm in: 5.7","at":"right"},{"series":"Photon energy","x":450,"text":"450 nm out: 4.4","at":"above"},{"series":"Photon energy","x":605,"text":"605 nm in: 3.3","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":605,"from":3.27,"to":4.4,"text":"too little","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"A photon can't give out more energy than it brought in. 3.3 in can't make 4.4 out, so **no 450 nm glow**.","marks":[{"t":"no 450 nm emission is detected","note":"can't make a bigger photon"}],"calc":["E₆₀₅ = 3.3 < E₄₅₀ = 4.4","energy out ≤ energy in"]},{"text":"**Wrong ideas:** \"E₆₀₅ > E₄₅₀\" flips E = hc/λ. The solvent is never mentioned, so nothing suggests it absorbs the light.","marks":[{"t":"the same dye","note":"no solvent mentioned"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hc/λ, so longer wavelength = lower energy. Fluorescence always emits lower energy (longer λ) than it absorbs, so the input photon must carry at least the output energy."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"A bacterial racemase converts L-serine, shown, into D-serine. The enzyme removes one hydrogen and puts it back on the opposite face of the same atom; no other bond is broken. Which labeled atom changes configuration?",idStem:"Circular dichroism of a protein mainly detects the chirality of which atom?",fig:"serine-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"Only an atom with **four different groups** has a configuration whose inversion gives a different, mirror-image molecule. In serine that is the **α carbon (II)**: H, NH₂, COOH and CH₂OH. The β carbon (IV) carries two identical H atoms, the carboxyl carbon (III) is trigonal planar, and the amine N (I) flips freely.",src:"pe5-cp:17",how:{"ask":"Which labeled atom has four different groups attached, so that moving its H to the other face gives the mirror-image molecule?","know":"• **Chiral center (stereocenter):** an sp³ atom bonded to four different groups. Swapping two of its groups gives the enantiomer.\n• **L and D** name the configuration at the α carbon of an amino acid. Proteins use L; every amino acid except glycine has a chiral α carbon.\n• A carbon with two identical groups (CH₂, CH₃) cannot be a stereocenter, and a trigonal planar carbon (C=O) has only three groups.\n• An amine N with a lone pair inverts rapidly, so it is not a lasting stereocenter.\n• On a drawing, check each labeled atom: count its groups and ask whether any two are the same.","steps":[{"text":"Moving an H to the other face changes the configuration only at a stereocenter.","hl":"puts it back on the opposite face of the same atom"},{"text":"Count the different groups on each labeled atom.","fig":"serine-sites","calc":["I, N: lone pair, flips freely: **not a stereocenter**","II, Cα: H, NH₂, COOH, CH₂OH: **4 different**","III, C=O carbon: **3 groups**, planar","IV, CH₂: **two identical H**"]},{"text":"The nitrogen and the planar carboxyl carbon cannot hold a fixed configuration.","strike":["I","III"]},{"text":"The β carbon has two H atoms; swapping one for the other gives the same molecule.","strike":["IV"]},{"text":"Answer: II, the α carbon. Rule: L vs D, like R vs S, is decided at the carbon bearing four different groups.","fig":"serine-alpha"}],"trap":"IV, the β carbon, also bears hydrogens an enzyme could remove, but it carries two identical H atoms, so putting one back on the other face gives the same molecule."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. A solid wedge points toward you; a dashed wedge points away. I–IV mark four atoms.","fig":"serine-sites"},{"text":"**An atom with 4 groups in 3D comes in two mirror-image versions, like left and right hands**. Moving its H to the opposite face swaps versions.","fig":"t5-ser-config"},{"text":"**The swap makes a new molecule only if all 4 groups differ (a stereocenter)**. If two groups match, you get the same molecule back.","fig":"t5-ser-config"},{"text":"**Site I, the N: it holds two identical H, and an N like this flips inside-out by itself constantly**. No lasting configuration.","fig":"t5-ser-i"},{"text":"**Site III bonds to only 3 atoms (the C=O counts once)**. It is flat and has no H to move.","fig":"t5-ser-iii"},{"text":"**Site IV holds H, H, OH and C**. Two identical H, so moving one to the other face gives the same molecule.","fig":"t5-ser-iv"},{"text":"**Site II holds H, NH₂, COOH and CH₂OH: four different groups, plus the H the enzyme can remove**. It is a stereocenter.","fig":"t5-ser-ii"},{"text":"**Moving that H to the other face turns L-serine (left) into its mirror image, D-serine (right)**. Nothing else changes.","fig":["t5-l-ser","t5-d-serine"]},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: configuration only changes meaningfully at an atom with 4 different groups. For each label, list its groups (count hidden H): all different, with an H?","fig":"serine-alpha"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A mutation replaces a phenylalanine with a tyrosine in a surface loop of a protein at pH 7.4. Both amino acids are shown; the tyrosine side-chain O–H has a pKa of about 10.1. Which property of the side chain is unchanged by the mutation?",idStem:"A mutation swaps leucine for serine. Which property of that side chain does NOT change?",fig:["phenylalanine-plain","tyrosine-plain"],choices:["Hydrophobicity, since both contain a benzene ring","Hydrogen-bonding ability, since both are uncharged","Mass, since both have the same carbon skeleton","Net charge, since neither ionizes at pH 7.4"],ans:3,exp:"The only change is an **O–H** added to the ring. Tyrosine's phenol has pKa ≈ 10.1, so at pH 7.4 it stays **protonated and neutral**, like phenylalanine's ring: **net charge 0 in both**. The O–H makes tyrosine more polar, adds an H-bond donor and acceptor, and adds 16 Da.",src:"pe5-cp:18",how:{"ask":"Spot the one group that differs between the two drawings. Which properties would that group change, and which would it leave the same at pH 7.4?","know":"• Compare side chains by the atoms that differ. Here: H on the ring (Phe) → O–H (Tyr).\n• An O–H makes a group more polar and lets it **donate and accept** H bonds, so hydrophobicity falls.\n• An acidic group loses H⁺ when pH > pKa. Phenol pKa ≈ 10: at pH 7.4, only about 1 in 500 is deprotonated, so it counts as neutral.\n• Mass: one added O adds 16 Da (C₉H₁₁NO₂ → C₉H₁₁NO₃).","steps":[{"text":"Put the two side chains side by side: the only change is an O–H on tyrosine's ring.","fig":["phenylalanine-sc","tyrosine-sc"]},{"text":"Ask whether the new O–H ionizes at this pH.","hl":"the tyrosine side-chain O–H has a pKa of about 10.1","calc":["pH 7.4 < pKa 10.1","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(7.4 − 10.1) = 10^**−2.7** ≈ **1/500**","→ O–H stays **protonated, neutral**"]},{"text":"The O–H adds polarity and an H-bond donor and acceptor, so hydrophobicity and H bonding both change.","strike":["Hydrophobicity, since both contain a benzene ring","Hydrogen-bonding ability, since both are uncharged"]},{"text":"Same carbon skeleton, but one extra oxygen: the mass changes.","strike":["Mass, since both have the same carbon skeleton"],"calc":["Phe, C₉H₁₁NO₂: **165** g/mol","Tyr, C₉H₁₁NO₃: **181** g/mol","difference: **+16**, one O"]},{"text":"Answer: net charge. Rule: compare only the atoms that differ, then check each new group's pKa against the pH."}],"trap":"Hydrogen-bonding ability is tempting because neither side chain is charged, but charge is not what makes an H bond: tyrosine's O–H can donate and accept H bonds, and phenylalanine's ring cannot."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. Both share a backbone (NH₂, a middle C, COOH); the side chain is what differs.","fig":["t5-phe-read","t5-tyr-read"]},{"text":"**The only change is an O–H on the end of the ring**. For each property, ask: does one O–H change it?","fig":["t5-phe-ring","t5-tyr-oh"]},{"text":"**A group turns + by gaining H⁺ or − by losing it**. pKa = the pH where half have lost their H⁺. Lower pH = more H⁺ around.","fig":"t5-tyr-pka"},{"text":"**pH 7.4 is below 10.1, so the O–H keeps its H: neutral**. Phenylalanine's ring has nothing to gain or lose: also neutral.","fig":["t5-phe-neutral","t5-tyr-neutral"]},{"text":"**Hydrophobic means water-avoiding (oily)**. The polar O–H makes tyrosine less hydrophobic, even with the same ring.","fig":["t5-phe-oily","t5-tyr-polar"]},{"text":"**A hydrogen bond pulls an H on O or N toward a lone pair (unshared electron pair) on another O or N**. Phe's ring can't; Tyr's O–H can, uncharged or not.","fig":"t5-tyr-hb"},{"text":"**Tyrosine has one extra O atom, 16 units heavier**. Same carbon skeleton, different mass.","fig":"t5-tyr-mass"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: charge changes only by gaining or losing H⁺, and a group keeps H⁺ while pH < pKa. In each answer ask: is the reason true for both side chains?","fig":"t5-tyr-neutral"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5C",q:"A 66-kDa protein is incubated with a protease that cuts it at two sites. Aliquots removed at 0, 5, 15 and 60 min are boiled in SDS sample buffer and run in adjacent lanes of an SDS-PAGE gel. Which pattern is expected as time increases?",idStem:"A protein is digested over time, and samples are run on SDS-PAGE. What pattern appears?",choices:["The 66-kDa band fades, and bands below 66 kDa appear","The 66-kDa band fades, and bands above 66 kDa appear","All bands stay the same, since boiling in SDS stops the protease","All bands run farther, since the fragments carry more charge"],ans:0,exp:"Proteolysis cuts the chain, so **the intact band shrinks and smaller fragments appear lower on the gel**. Boiling stops digestion only at the moment each aliquot is taken.",src:"pe5-cp:19",how:{"ask":"What does cutting a protein do to its size, and where do smaller pieces run on an SDS gel?","know":"**SDS-PAGE** coats proteins with negative SDS, giving roughly the same charge per mass, so they separate by **size**: smaller → runs farther (lower). A **protease** hydrolyzes peptide bonds, making smaller pieces.","steps":[{"text":"Two cuts in one chain give up to three fragments, each smaller than 66 kDa.","hl":"cuts it at two sites"},{"text":"SDS-PAGE sorts by mass alone: smaller runs farther. Boiling in SDS denatures the protease and stops cutting in that aliquot only.","hl":"boiled in SDS sample buffer"},{"text":"Later aliquots had more time with the protease, so the intact band fades and the fragment bands grow.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Expected band intensity over time, illustrative","x":{"label":"Time of aliquot (min)","min":0,"max":60,"step":15},"y":{"label":"Band intensity (% of total)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"66-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,100],[5,70],[15,40],[60,5]]},{"name":"Fragments below 66 kDa","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[5,30],[15,60],[60,95]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"66-kDa band","x":0},{"series":"66-kDa band","x":60}],"focus":["66-kDa band","Fragments below 66 kDa"],"labels":[{"series":"66-kDa band","x":0,"text":"0 min: 100% intact","at":"right"},{"series":"66-kDa band","x":60,"text":"60 min: 5%","at":"above"},{"series":"Fragments below 66 kDa","x":60,"text":"fragments: 95%","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Cutting can't make anything bigger than 66 kDa, and SDS gives every chain about the same charge per mass, so extra charge doesn't explain band movement.","strike":["The 66-kDa band fades, and bands above 66 kDa appear","All bands run farther, since the fragments carry more charge"]},{"text":"Boiling freezes each aliquot at its own time point, so the lanes still differ. Answer: the 66-kDa band fades and bands below 66 kDa appear.","strike":["All bands stay the same, since boiling in SDS stops the protease"]}],"trap":"\"Boiling in SDS stops the protease\" is true for each aliquot, but each one was taken at a later time, so the lanes still differ."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what is given**: a protein, a cutter, and samples over time.","marks":[{"t":"66-kDa protein","note":"size of whole protein"},{"t":"cuts it at two sites","note":"2 cuts"},{"t":"0, 5, 15 and 60 min","note":"sampling times"}]},{"text":"**kDa** is a unit of mass for big molecules. A **protease** is an enzyme that cuts protein chains. Two cuts → three smaller pieces.","marks":[{"t":"protease","note":"protein-cutting enzyme"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Two cuts make three smaller pieces (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Piece"},"y":{"label":"Mass (kDa)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"categories":["Intact","Piece 1","Piece 2","Piece 3"],"series":[{"name":"Mass","values":[66,30,20,16]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Intact","Mass"]]}},{"text":"**SDS-PAGE** pulls proteins through a gel. SDS coats each one with the same charge per mass, so only **size** matters: small pieces run farther.","marks":[{"t":"SDS-PAGE gel","note":"sorts by size"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"How far a protein runs in the gel","x":{"label":"Protein mass (kDa)","min":10,"max":70,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Distance run (cm)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Distance","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[10,7],[20,4.9],[30,3.6],[40,2.7],[50,2],[66,1.2],[70,1]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Distance","x":66,"text":"66 kDa: near the top","at":"above"},{"series":"Distance","x":20,"text":"small piece: runs far","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Boiling in SDS** kills the protease. Each sample is frozen in time at the moment it was taken.","marks":[{"t":"boiled in SDS sample buffer","note":"stops cutting then"},{"t":"adjacent lanes","note":"one lane per time"}]},{"text":"**See it:** the longer the protease works, the less intact protein and the more small pieces.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength over time (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Time of sample (min)","min":0,"max":60,"step":15},"y":{"label":"Band strength (% of total)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"66-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,100],[5,70],[15,40],[60,5]]},{"name":"Pieces below 66 kDa","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[5,30],[15,60],[60,95]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"66-kDa band","x":0,"text":"0 min: all intact","at":"right"},{"series":"66-kDa band","x":60,"text":"60 min: fading","at":"above"},{"series":"Pieces below 66 kDa","x":60,"text":"pieces: growing","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Cutting only shrinks.** Nothing above 66 kDa can appear. SDS gives every piece the same charge per mass, so \"more charge\" is wrong.","marks":[{"t":"cuts it at two sites","note":"cuts only make smaller"}]},{"text":"\"All bands the same\" is wrong: boiling stops each sample **when it is taken**, so later lanes had more cutting time.","marks":[{"t":"Aliquots removed at 0, 5, 15 and 60 min","note":"each = a snapshot"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SDS-PAGE separates by size only (smaller runs farther). Digestion over time → the full-size band fades and lower bands appear."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"A methyltransferase moves a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine, SAM (398 g/mol), to an –OH of a substrate (151 g/mol), forming S-adenosylhomocysteine, SAH (384 g/mol), and the methylated product. What is the molar mass of the methylated product, and what is the net change in combined mass of substrate plus cofactor?",idStem:"A methyltransferase moves a CH₃ group from a cofactor to a substrate. What is the net mass change of substrate plus cofactor together?",choices:["166 g/mol; +15 g/mol","137 g/mol; 0 g/mol","165 g/mol; 0 g/mol","165 g/mol; −14 g/mol"],ans:2,exp:"SAM → SAH loses 14 g/mol (CH₃ out, H back in effect); the substrate's O–H becomes O–CH₃, a gain of **+14 → 165 g/mol**. A **transferase only moves atoms**, so total mass is **conserved: net 0**.",src:"pe5-cp:20",how:{"ask":"Track what the cofactor loses and what the substrate gains, then add them.","know":"A **transferase** moves a group from a donor to an acceptor; no atoms are created or destroyed, so **mass is conserved**. Replacing an H with CH₃ adds CH₃ − H = 15 − 1 = **14 g/mol**.","steps":[{"text":"A transferase only moves a group from one molecule to another, so total mass is conserved.","hl":"moves a methyl group"},{"text":"Cofactor side: SAM loses the methyl.","calc":["SAH − SAM = 384 − 398","= **−14 g/mol**"]},{"text":"Substrate side: O–H becomes O–CH₃, so CH₃ is gained and H is lost.","calc":["+CH₃ (15) − H (1) = **+14**","151 + 14 = **165 g/mol**"],"strike":["166 g/mol; +15 g/mol","137 g/mol; 0 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add both sides. Answer: 165 g/mol; net change 0 g/mol.","calc":["before: 151 + 398 = **549**","after: 165 + 384 = **549**","net = **0 g/mol**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Mass change in the methyl transfer","x":{"label":"Molecule"},"y":{"label":"Δ molar mass (g/mol)","min":-20,"max":20,"step":10},"categories":["SAM → SAH","Substrate → product","Net"],"series":[{"name":"Δ mass","values":[-14,14,0]}]},"strike":["165 g/mol; −14 g/mol"],"graph":{"bars":[["SAM → SAH","Δ mass"],["Substrate → product","Δ mass"],["Net","Δ mass"]]}}],"trap":"\"166; +15\" adds a whole CH₃ without removing the H it replaces, and counts only the substrate's side of the transfer."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every molecule and its mass**.","marks":[{"t":"SAM (398 g/mol)","note":"methyl giver"},{"t":"substrate (151 g/mol)","note":"methyl receiver"},{"t":"SAH (384 g/mol)","note":"giver after"}]},{"text":"A **methyl group** is CH₃. A **transferase** is an enzyme that only **moves** a group from one molecule to another; no atoms appear or vanish.","marks":[{"t":"methyltransferase","note":"moves CH₃"},{"t":"methyl group","note":"CH₃ = 12 + 3 = 15"}]},{"text":"**Giver side:** SAM turns into SAH. How much lighter did it get?","marks":[{"t":"SAM (398 g/mol)","note":"before"},{"t":"SAH (384 g/mol)","note":"after"}],"calc":["384 − 398 = **−14 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**Receiver side:** the O–H becomes O–CH₃. The H is **swapped** for CH₃, so it gains CH₃ but loses H.","marks":[{"t":"an –OH","note":"H → CH₃"},{"t":"substrate (151 g/mol)","note":"start: 151"}],"calc":["+ CH₃ (15) − H (1) = **+14**","151 + 14 = **165 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**Add up both sides.** The total is the same before and after: mass is conserved.","marks":[{"t":"the methylated product","note":"165 g/mol"}],"calc":["before: 151 + 398 = **549**","after: 165 + 384 = **549**","net change = **0 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**See it:** one side loses 14, the other gains 14, and the net is zero.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Mass change in the methyl transfer","x":{"label":"Molecule"},"y":{"label":"Δ molar mass (g/mol)","min":-20,"max":20,"step":10},"categories":["SAM → SAH","Substrate → product","Net"],"series":[{"name":"Δ mass","values":[-14,14,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["SAM → SAH","Δ mass"],["Substrate → product","Δ mass"],["Net","Δ mass"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 166 adds the whole CH₃ (forgets the lost H); 137 subtracts instead of adds; a net −14 counts only the giver side.","marks":[{"t":"an –OH","note":"H is lost, so +14 not +15"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** swapping H for CH₃ adds 14 g/mol. A transferase only moves atoms, so the total mass of all molecules is unchanged."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Testosterone, shown, is synthesized in the testes from a lipid that already contains its four fused rings; the side chain of that precursor is cut off during synthesis. Which structure is the precursor?",idStem:"Progesterone and other steroid hormones are made from which molecule?",fig:"testosterone",choiceFigs:["cholesterol","palmitic-acid","sphingosine","triacylglycerol"],choices:["Cholesterol","Palmitic acid","Sphingosine","Triacylglycerol"],ans:0,exp:"All steroid hormones are made from **cholesterol**: the same **four fused rings** (three six-membered, one five-membered), with its 8-carbon side chain cut off. Palmitic acid, sphingosine and triacylglycerol are lipids too, but each is built from long open chains with no rings.",src:"pe5-cp:21",how:{"ask":"Match the ring pattern in testosterone. Which drawing already has three six-membered rings fused to a five-membered ring?","know":"• **Steroids** share one core: four fused rings, three of six carbons and one of five.\n• **Cholesterol** (27 C) is the starting point for every steroid hormone: cortisol, aldosterone, progesterone, testosterone, estradiol. Cutting off its side chain is the first step.\n• Other lipids look different: **fatty acids** are long chains ending in COOH; **triacylglycerols** are three fatty acids on glycerol; **sphingosine** is a long chain with an amino-alcohol head.\n• When asked for a precursor, look for the drawing that already contains the product's skeleton.","steps":[{"text":"The clue is structural: find the drawing that already has testosterone's ring system.","hl":"already contains its four fused rings"},{"text":"Read the core of the product.","fig":"testosterone","calc":["rings: **3** six-membered + **1** five-membered, all fused","carbons: **19**"]},{"text":"These are open chains with no rings: a fatty acid, an amino alcohol and a fatty-acid triester.","strike":["Palmitic acid","Sphingosine","Triacylglycerol"]},{"text":"Cholesterol has the identical four rings plus a side chain that is trimmed off.","fig":"cholesterol","calc":["cholesterol: **27** C = same 4 rings + **8**-carbon side chain","27 − 8 = **19** C, as in testosterone"]},{"text":"Answer: cholesterol. Rule: every steroid hormone is cholesterol with its side chain trimmed."}],"trap":"All four choices are lipids, so 'it is a lipid' does not decide it. Only one drawing contains rings, and they match the product's four-ring core."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. Rings that share an edge are fused. Solid wedges point toward you; dashed ones point away.","fig":"testosterone"},{"text":"**Four fused rings: three six-membered, one five-membered**. This four-ring frame is called the steroid skeleton.","fig":"t5-testo-rings"},{"text":"**The precursor is a lipid (an oily molecule of mostly C and H) that already has these four rings, plus a side chain that gets cut off**.","fig":"t5-testo-rings"},{"text":"**Cholesterol has the same four fused rings plus an 8-carbon side chain**. Cut the chain off and the frame matches testosterone.","fig":"t5-chol-chain"},{"text":"**Palmitic acid: one straight 16-carbon chain ending in COOH**. No rings.","fig":"t5-palm-norings"},{"text":"**Sphingosine: a long carbon chain carrying OH and NH₂**. No rings.","fig":"t5-sph-norings"},{"text":"**Triacylglycerol (a fat): a glycerol holding three long chains**. No rings.","fig":"t5-tag-norings"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: all steroid hormones (testosterone, estrogen, cortisol) are made from cholesterol. In each drawing, count rings: four fused rings already there?","fig":["cholesterol","testosterone"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5C",q:"A protein carrying six consecutive histidines at its C-terminus is loaded onto a resin bearing immobilized Ni²⁺ ions. Other proteins wash through, and the target is then eluted with 250 mM imidazole. This purification step is best categorized as:",idStem:"A His-tagged protein sticks to a nickel column and is released with imidazole. What type of chromatography is this?",choices:["cation exchange, since His is positively charged at pH 8","affinity, since imidazole competes for the Ni²⁺ sites","anion exchange, since the Ni²⁺ resin is positively charged","size exclusion, since the tag makes the protein larger"],ans:1,exp:"The His tag binds Ni²⁺ **specifically**, and free imidazole, the same ring as His's side chain, competes it off. **Specific binding + competitive elution = affinity chromatography.**",src:"pe5-cp:22",how:{"ask":"What makes the tagged protein stick, and why would adding imidazole release it?","know":"**Affinity chromatography:** the column holds a partner that binds one target specifically; you elute with a competitor. **Ion exchange** separates by charge, eluting with salt or pH. **Size exclusion** separates by size with no binding. Histidine's side chain is an **imidazole** ring that coordinates metal ions.","steps":[{"text":"The His side chain is an imidazole ring. Its ring N lone pairs coordinate transition-metal ions such as Ni²⁺.","hl":"six consecutive histidines","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"Free imidazole is the same ring. At high concentration it outcompetes the tag for the Ni²⁺ sites and releases the protein.","hl":"eluted with 250 mM imidazole","fig":"imidazole"},{"text":"Binding is to the metal, not by charge: at pH 8, His is mostly neutral.","calc":["pH − pKa = 8 − 6 = 2","[His] : [HisH⁺] = 10² = **100 : 1**","→ mostly **neutral**"],"strike":["cation exchange, since His is positively charged at pH 8","anion exchange, since the Ni²⁺ resin is positively charged"]},{"text":"Size exclusion uses no binding, and six His add only about 0.8 kDa. Answer: affinity, since imidazole competes for the Ni²⁺ sites.","strike":["size exclusion, since the tag makes the protein larger"]}],"trap":"His's pKa is ≈ 6, so at pH 8 it is mostly neutral; binding is to the metal, not by charge. That rules out cation exchange."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find the three key players**.","marks":[{"t":"six consecutive histidines","note":"His tag on the protein"},{"t":"immobilized Ni²⁺ ions","note":"metal stuck to beads"},{"t":"250 mM imidazole","note":"chemical used to release"}]},{"text":"**Chromatography** = pouring a mix over a column of beads (resin). How proteins stick or pass decides the type.","marks":[{"t":"loaded onto a resin","note":"column of beads"}],"calc":["**affinity**: binds one partner, released by a competitor","**ion exchange**: binds by charge, released by salt","**size exclusion**: no binding, sorts by size"]},{"text":"Histidine's side chain is an **imidazole ring**. Its nitrogen grabs metal ions like Ni²⁺. Six in a row grip very tightly.","marks":[{"t":"six consecutive histidines","note":"each has an imidazole ring"},{"t":"Ni²⁺","note":"grabbed by His"}]},{"text":"Only the tagged protein sticks. Everything else runs straight through: **one specific partner**.","marks":[{"t":"Other proteins wash through","note":"no tag → no grip"}]},{"text":"**Free imidazole** is the same ring as His. Flood the column with it and it takes over the Ni²⁺ spots, pushing the protein off.","marks":[{"t":"eluted with 250 mM imidazole","note":"competitor pushes it off"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Tagged protein released vs imidazole (illustrative)","x":{"label":"[Imidazole] (mM)","min":0,"max":250,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Protein released (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Released","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[50,10],[100,35],[150,70],[200,90],[250,98]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Released","x":0,"text":"no imidazole: stuck","at":"right"},{"series":"Released","x":250,"text":"250 mM: released","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Not ion exchange:** binding is to the metal, not by charge. At pH 8 His is mostly uncharged anyway.","marks":[{"t":"immobilized Ni²⁺ ions","note":"binds metal, not charge"}],"calc":["His side chain pKa ≈ 6","pH 8 − 6 = 2 → 100 neutral : 1 charged"]},{"text":"**Not size exclusion:** that method has no binding at all, and six His add only ≈0.8 kDa.","marks":[{"t":"the target is then eluted","note":"it was bound first"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** specific binding to a partner + release by a competitor = affinity chromatography. Release by salt = ion exchange; no binding = size exclusion."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A lab needs to remove an integral membrane protein from the lipid bilayer and keep it dissolved in water. Which of the additives shown would be most effective?",idStem:"A detergent is used to free proteins from a membrane. Which feature makes it work?",choiceFigs:["octyl-glucoside","beta-glucopyranose","n-octane","triacylglycerol"],choices:["Octyl glucoside","Glucose","Octane","Triacylglycerol"],ans:0,exp:"A membrane protein has a **nonpolar belt** where it touched the lipid tails. A **detergent**, one molecule with a **nonpolar tail and a polar head**, coats that belt with its tails and faces the water with its heads. Octyl glucoside has an 8-carbon tail on a sugar head. Glucose is only polar; octane and the triacylglycerol are essentially only nonpolar.",src:"pe5-cp:23",how:{"ask":"Which drawing has two distinct ends on one molecule: a long nonpolar chain and a polar head with several O–H groups?","know":"• **Amphipathic:** one molecule with a hydrophobic part (C–C and C–H chains) and a hydrophilic part (O–H groups, charged groups).\n• **Detergents** are amphipathic; they form micelles and wrap the hydrophobic surface of a membrane protein so it stays soluble in water.\n• Ionic detergents (SDS) have a charged head; nonionic ones (octyl glucoside, Triton X-100) have a neutral polar head such as a sugar.\n• On drawings, find the zigzag chain (nonpolar) and the cluster of O or N (polar). A useful detergent needs both on one molecule.","steps":[{"text":"Two jobs: reach into the nonpolar bilayer and stay in water. That needs a molecule with both kinds of region.","hl":"remove an integral membrane protein from the lipid bilayer and keep it dissolved in water"},{"text":"Sort each drawing into nonpolar tail and polar head.","calc":["octyl glucoside: tail **C₈**, head **4 O–H**","glucose: tail **none**, head **5 O–H**","octane: tail **C₈**, head **none**","triacylglycerol: **3** tails, head **no O–H**"]},{"text":"Glucose is all head: it dissolves in water but cannot cover the protein's nonpolar surface.","strike":["Glucose"]},{"text":"Octane and the triacylglycerol are all tail: they dissolve in the bilayer, not in water.","strike":["Octane","Triacylglycerol"]},{"text":"Answer: octyl glucoside. The highlighted chain binds the protein's lipid-facing surface; the sugar head faces water. Rule: detergent = long nonpolar tail + polar head on one molecule.","fig":"octyl-glucoside-tail"}],"trap":"Glucose is very water soluble, but with no nonpolar tail it cannot cover the protein's lipid-facing surface. The additive must bridge the two environments, not just dissolve."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H**. A zigzag is a carbon chain. A six-sided ring containing an O is a sugar ring.","fig":"t5-og-read"},{"text":"**Groups with O–H are polar: water clings to them**. Chains of only C and H are nonpolar (oily): water pushes them away.","fig":["t5-oct-oily","t5-glc-polar"]},{"text":"**A cell membrane is a lipid bilayer: two layers of lipids like this one, polar heads facing water, oily tails pointing inward**.","fig":"t5-pc-parts"},{"text":"**An integral membrane protein crosses the tail layer, so it has an oily belt**. In plain water that belt makes proteins clump and fall out.","fig":"t5-pc-belt"},{"text":"**A detergent is one molecule with an oily tail and a polar head**. Tails coat the protein's oily belt; heads face the water.","fig":"t5-og-both"},{"text":"**Octyl glucoside: an 8-carbon tail on a glucose head covered in OH groups**. Tail and head in one molecule.","fig":"t5-og-tail"},{"text":"**Glucose alone is only polar: nothing oily to grab the protein's belt**.","fig":"t5-glc-notail"},{"text":"**Octane is only oily: it doesn't dissolve in water, so it can't carry the protein there**.","fig":"t5-oct-nohead"},{"text":"**Triacylglycerol is three long oily chains with a small polar middle**. It forms oil drops instead of dissolving.","fig":"t5-tag-oily"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: to keep an oily surface dissolved in water you need an oily tail and a polar head in one molecule. In each drawing ask: does it have both?","fig":"t5-og-both"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A technician prepares 500 mL of a 300 mM NaCl solution (NaCl = 58.4 g/mol). How many moles of NaCl are needed, and about what mass?",idStem:"How many moles of NaCl are in 500 mL of 300 mM NaCl?",choices:["0.15 mol; 8.8 g","0.015 mol; 0.88 g","1.5 mol; 88 g","0.60 mol; 35 g"],ans:0,exp:"**n = M × V(L)** = 0.300 mol/L × 0.500 L = **0.15 mol**; × 58.4 g/mol ≈ **8.8 g**.",src:"pe5-cp:24",how:{"ask":"Turn a millimolar concentration and a milliliter volume into moles, then into grams.","know":"**Molarity M = mol/L**, so **n = M × V** with V in liters. 1 mM = 10⁻³ M; 1 mL = 10⁻³ L. **mass = n × molar mass.**","steps":[{"text":"Convert to mol/L and liters first.","hl":"500 mL of a 300 mM NaCl solution","calc":["300 mM = **0.300 M**","500 mL = **0.500 L**"]},{"text":"Moles = molarity × volume.","calc":["n = M × V","n = 0.300 mol/L × 0.500 L","n = **0.15 mol**"]},{"text":"Mass = moles × molar mass.","hl":"NaCl = 58.4 g/mol","calc":["m = 0.15 mol × 58.4 g/mol","m ≈ **8.8 g**"]},{"text":"0.015 and 1.5 mol are factor-of-10 slips; 0.60 mol divides M by V instead of multiplying. Answer: 0.15 mol; 8.8 g.","strike":["0.015 mol; 0.88 g","1.5 mol; 88 g","0.60 mol; 35 g"]}],"trap":"0.60 mol comes from dividing (0.300 ÷ 0.500) instead of multiplying. Moles = M × V."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"500 mL","note":"volume to make"},{"t":"300 mM","note":"concentration"},{"t":"NaCl = 58.4 g/mol","note":"1 mol weighs 58.4 g"}]},{"text":"**Molarity** = moles per liter. **mM** = millimolar = one-thousandth of a mole per liter.","marks":[{"t":"300 mM","note":"= 0.300 mol/L"}],"calc":["m = milli = 10⁻³","300 mM = **0.300 M**"]},{"text":"Molarity is **per liter**, so turn mL into L.","marks":[{"t":"500 mL","note":"= 0.500 L"}],"calc":["500 mL ÷ 1000 = **0.500 L**"]},{"text":"**Moles = molarity × liters.**","marks":[{"t":"300 mM","note":"0.300 mol/L"},{"t":"500 mL","note":"× 0.500 L"}],"calc":["n = M × V","n = 0.300 × 0.500","n = **0.15 mol**"]},{"text":"**See it:** at 0.300 M, moles grow in a straight line with volume. Half a liter → 0.15 mol.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Moles of NaCl in a 0.300 M solution","x":{"label":"Volume (L)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Moles NaCl","min":0,"max":0.3,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"n = 0.300 × V","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.075],[0.5,0.15],[0.75,0.225],[1,0.3]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"n = 0.300 × V","x":0.5}],"labels":[{"series":"n = 0.300 × V","x":0.5,"text":"0.500 L → 0.15 mol","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Grams = moles × molar mass.**","marks":[{"t":"NaCl = 58.4 g/mol","note":"× this"}],"calc":["m = 0.15 mol × 58.4 g/mol","m = **8.76 g ≈ 8.8 g**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 0.015 or 1.5 mol are decimal slips; 0.60 mol divides 0.300 by 0.500 instead of multiplying.","marks":[{"t":"300 mM","note":"M × V, not M ÷ V"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles = molarity (mol/L) × volume (L); grams = moles × molar mass. Convert mM → M and mL → L first."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A recombinant receptor is purified by Ni²⁺ affinity and then size-exclusion chromatography. Data: SDS-PAGE shows one band at 52 kDa, the expected mass; the His tag is still attached; ligand binding gives Kd = 4 nM, the same as receptor isolated from tissue. Which observation most strongly supports that the purified receptor is in its native fold?",idStem:"What best shows that a purified protein is still in its native, folded state?",choices:["The ligand Kd matches that of the tissue receptor","The SDS-PAGE band matches the expected mass","The His tag stayed attached through purification","The protein ran as one peak on both columns"],ans:0,exp:"**Only function depends on the folded 3-D structure.** Matching ligand affinity shows the binding site is properly formed. Mass, the tag and purity are the same for unfolded protein.",src:"pe5-cp:25",how:{"ask":"Which of these results would change if the protein were unfolded?","know":"A protein's **native fold** (tertiary structure) creates its binding sites. **Denatured** protein keeps the same sequence, mass and tags. SDS-PAGE denatures proteins anyway, so it can't report folding. **Kd** = ligand concentration at half-saturation; a matching Kd means a native-like site.","steps":[{"text":"Native fold means the correct 3-D structure. Look for an observation that would fail if the protein were unfolded.","hl":"most strongly supports that the purified receptor is in its native fold"},{"text":"SDS unfolds every protein before it runs, so folded and unfolded receptor give the same band.","hl":"SDS-PAGE shows one band at 52 kDa","strike":["The SDS-PAGE band matches the expected mass"]},{"text":"The tag stays on and the protein elutes as one peak whether or not it is folded correctly. These report identity and purity, not structure.","strike":["The His tag stayed attached through purification","The protein ran as one peak on both columns"]},{"text":"Ligand binding needs a correctly formed binding site. The two curves overlap and give the same half-saturation point. Answer: the ligand Kd matches the tissue receptor.","hl":"Kd = 4 nM, the same as receptor isolated from tissue","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ligand binding, Kd = 4 nM","x":{"label":"[Ligand] (nM)","min":0,"max":32,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fraction of receptor bound","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Purified receptor","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,0.33],[4,0.5],[8,0.67],[16,0.8],[32,0.89]]},{"name":"Tissue receptor","marker":"square-open","line":"none","points":[[0,0],[2,0.33],[4,0.5],[8,0.67],[16,0.8],[32,0.89]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Purified receptor","x":4},{"series":"Tissue receptor","x":4}],"yLine":0.5,"labels":[{"series":"Purified receptor","x":4,"text":"half bound at 4 nM = Kd (both curves)","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":0.5,"text":"0.5 bound","side":"left"}]}}],"trap":"The SDS-PAGE mass match is tempting, but SDS unfolds every protein before it runs, so it can't tell folded from unfolded."},teach:[{"text":"First, **sort the data**: three checks were done on the purified receptor.","marks":[{"t":"SDS-PAGE shows one band at 52 kDa","note":"size check"},{"t":"the His tag is still attached","note":"tag check"},{"t":"Kd = 4 nM","note":"binding check"}]},{"text":"**Native fold** = the protein's correct 3-D shape. An unfolded protein has the same chain and the same mass, just the wrong shape.","marks":[{"t":"native fold","note":"correct 3-D shape"}]},{"text":"So look for the one result that would **change if the protein were unfolded**.","marks":[{"t":"most strongly supports","note":"find a shape-only test"}]},{"text":"**SDS-PAGE unfolds every protein** before running it. Folded or not, the band is 52 kDa: it can't tell shape.","marks":[{"t":"SDS-PAGE shows one band at 52 kDa","note":"same if unfolded"}]},{"text":"The tag stays on and the columns give one peak **whether or not** the shape is right. These show identity and purity, not shape.","marks":[{"t":"the His tag is still attached","note":"same if unfolded"},{"t":"Ni²⁺ affinity","note":"purity, not shape"},{"t":"size-exclusion chromatography","note":"purity, not shape"}]},{"text":"**Kd** = the ligand concentration that fills half the receptors. Small Kd = tight binding. Binding needs a correctly shaped pocket.","marks":[{"t":"Kd = 4 nM","note":"half bound at 4 nM"},{"t":"ligand binding","note":"needs the right shape"}]},{"text":"**See it:** the purified and tissue receptors give the same curve; both are half bound at 4 nM.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ligand binding, Kd = 4 nM","x":{"label":"[Ligand] (nM)","min":0,"max":32,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fraction bound","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Purified receptor","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,0.33],[4,0.5],[8,0.67],[16,0.8],[32,0.89]]},{"name":"Tissue receptor","marker":"square-open","line":"none","points":[[0,0],[2,0.33],[4,0.5],[8,0.67],[16,0.8],[32,0.89]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Purified receptor","x":4}],"labels":[{"series":"Purified receptor","x":4,"text":"half bound at 4 nM","at":"right"},{"series":"Tissue receptor","x":32,"text":"curves overlap","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":0.5,"text":"0.5","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Matching the natural receptor's Kd means the binding pocket is built right, which **only a folded protein** can do.","marks":[{"t":"the same as receptor isolated from tissue","note":"pocket works like native"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to prove a protein is folded, use a test of function (binding, activity). Mass, tags and purity look the same for unfolded protein."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"In a star's core, two deuterium nuclei must approach to within about 10⁻¹⁵ m before they can fuse. Which statement best explains why this process requires temperatures near 10⁷ K?",idStem:"Why does nuclear fusion need extremely high temperatures?",choices:["High temperature weakens the strong force that holds protons apart","Nuclei need large kinetic energies to overcome their Coulomb repulsion","High temperature strips the nuclei of charge so they no longer repel","High temperature lowers the fuel's density so collisions are gentler"],ans:1,exp:"Both nuclei are positive and repel. **Temperature measures average kinetic energy**; only very fast nuclei get close enough (≈10⁻¹⁵ m) for the attractive **strong nuclear force** to take over.",src:"pe5-cp:26",how:{"ask":"What force keeps two positive nuclei apart, and what does high temperature give them?","know":"**Coulomb's law:** like charges repel, F ∝ q₁q₂/r². The **strong nuclear force** attracts nucleons but acts only over ~10⁻¹⁵ m. **Temperature** ∝ average kinetic energy of particles. Stripping electrons leaves the nuclei still positively charged.","steps":[{"text":"Each deuterium nucleus is one proton plus one neutron, so each carries +1e and the two repel.","hl":"two deuterium nuclei"},{"text":"Compute the repulsive energy at the fusion distance.","hl":"within about 10⁻¹⁵ m","calc":["U = kq₁q₂ / r","U = (9.0 × 10⁹)(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)² / **10⁻¹⁵ m**","U ≈ **2.3 × 10⁻¹³ J** ≈ 1.4 MeV"]},{"text":"The repulsive energy climbs steeply as r shrinks, so the nuclei must arrive with enough kinetic energy to climb this hill.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Coulomb repulsion between two deuterium nuclei","x":{"label":"Separation r (fm, 10⁻¹⁵ m)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Repulsive energy (MeV)","min":0,"max":1.6,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"U = ke²/r","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,1.44],[2,0.72],[3,0.48],[4,0.36],[6,0.24],[8,0.18],[10,0.144]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"U = ke²/r","x":1}],"labels":[{"series":"U = ke²/r","x":1,"text":"1 fm: fusion distance","at":"right"},{"series":"U = ke²/r","x":10,"text":"far apart: low","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":1.44,"text":"barrier ≈ 1.4 MeV","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"Temperature sets the kinetic energy. Even at 10⁷ K the average is below the barrier, so only the fastest nuclei, helped by quantum tunneling, fuse. That is why T must be so high.","hl":"temperatures near 10⁷ K","calc":["KE_avg = (3/2)kT","= 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × **10⁷ K**","≈ **2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J**"]},{"text":"The strong force attracts nucleons rather than holding them apart; heat removes electrons, not nuclear charge; lower density means fewer collisions. Answer: nuclei need large kinetic energies to overcome their Coulomb repulsion.","strike":["High temperature weakens the strong force that holds protons apart","High temperature strips the nuclei of charge so they no longer repel","High temperature lowers the fuel's density so collisions are gentler"]}],"trap":"\"Strips the nuclei of charge\" confuses removing electrons with removing charge. Ionization frees the nuclei, but each still has its proton's + charge."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what is given**.","marks":[{"t":"two deuterium nuclei","note":"2 heavy-hydrogen nuclei"},{"t":"10⁻¹⁵ m","note":"how close they must get"},{"t":"10⁷ K","note":"10 million kelvin"}]},{"text":"Deuterium's nucleus = 1 proton + 1 neutron, so it has **+1 charge**. Two + charges **repel** (push apart).","marks":[{"t":"two deuterium nuclei","note":"each +1: they repel"}],"calc":["deuterium nucleus = p⁺ + n","charge = **+1e** each","like charges → **repel**"]},{"text":"To **fuse** (merge), they must get so close that the **strong nuclear force** takes over. It attracts, but only reaches about 10⁻¹⁵ m.","marks":[{"t":"approach to within about 10⁻¹⁵ m","note":"strong-force range"},{"t":"fuse","note":"merge into one nucleus"}]},{"text":"How much energy does the push-apart cost at that distance?","marks":[{"t":"10⁻¹⁵ m","note":"r"}],"calc":["U = kq₁q₂ / r","U = (9.0 × 10⁹)(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)² / 10⁻¹⁵","U ≈ **2.3 × 10⁻¹³ J** ≈ 1.4 MeV"]},{"text":"**See it:** the repulsion energy climbs steeply as they get closer. They need lots of speed to climb this hill.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Coulomb repulsion between two deuterium nuclei","x":{"label":"Separation r (× 10⁻¹⁵ m)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Repulsion energy (MeV)","min":0,"max":1.6,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"U = ke²/r","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,1.44],[2,0.72],[3,0.48],[4,0.36],[6,0.24],[8,0.18],[10,0.144]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"U = ke²/r","x":1,"text":"fusion distance","at":"right"},{"series":"U = ke²/r","x":10,"text":"far apart: low","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":1.44,"text":"barrier ≈ 1.4 MeV","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Temperature measures how fast particles move** (their kinetic energy). Hotter → faster → closer collisions.","marks":[{"t":"temperatures near 10⁷ K","note":"very fast nuclei"}],"calc":["average KE = (3/2)kT","= 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 10⁷","≈ **2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J**: only the fastest few fuse"]},{"text":"**Wrong ideas:** the strong force attracts (it doesn't hold protons apart); heat strips electrons, not the nucleus's charge; lower density means fewer collisions.","marks":[{"t":"In a star's core","note":"hot, dense plasma"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fusion needs extreme temperature because positive nuclei repel (Coulomb); only very high kinetic energy gets them close enough for the strong force to bind them."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"The two forms of limonene shown, R and S, smell of orange and of turpentine, respectively. Data: both boil at 176 °C, both have density 0.84 g/mL, and their specific rotations are equal in size and opposite in sign. Which conclusion is most consistent with these data?",idStem:"Two enantiomers can differ in which property?",fig:["r-limonene","s-limonene"],choices:["They are diastereomers, since their odors are different","They are enantiomers, and the odor receptors are chiral","They are enantiomers, so their boiling points must differ","They are constitutional isomers, since their rotations differ"],ans:1,exp:"The drawings have the same connectivity and differ only at **one stereocenter**, so they are mirror images: **enantiomers**. Enantiomers share all physical properties in an achiral setting (boiling point, density) and rotate light by equal and opposite amounts. They smell different because **odor receptors are proteins built from L-amino acids, so they are chiral** and bind the two forms differently.",src:"pe5-cp:27",how:{"ask":"Compare the drawings atom by atom. Where do they differ, and how many stereocenters does each have?","know":"• **Constitutional isomers:** same formula, different connectivity. **Stereoisomers:** same connectivity, different 3D arrangement.\n• **Enantiomers:** non-superimposable mirror images; every stereocenter is inverted. **Diastereomers:** stereoisomers that are not mirror images (only some centers inverted, so they need at least two centers, or a C=C with E/Z).\n• Enantiomers have identical boiling points, densities, solubilities and IR spectra; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in how they interact with other chiral things, such as receptors and enzymes.\n• On drawings: wedge vs dash at the same atom, with nothing else changed, means one center is flipped.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two drawings: same ring, same groups, same connections. Only the wedge at the highlighted carbon differs.","fig":["r-limonene","s-limonene"]},{"text":"Classify from the drawings.","calc":["connectivity: **same** → not constitutional isomers","stereocenters: **1**, inverted","one center inverted → mirror images: **enantiomers**"],"strike":["They are diastereomers, since their odors are different","They are constitutional isomers, since their rotations differ"]},{"text":"The data agree: identical physical properties, as enantiomers must have.","hl":"both boil at 176 °C, both have density 0.84 g/mL","strike":["They are enantiomers, so their boiling points must differ"]},{"text":"Odor is detected by receptor proteins made of L-amino acids. A chiral receptor binds two enantiomers differently, as a right glove fits only a right hand.","hl":"smell of orange and of turpentine"},{"text":"Answer: enantiomers, and the receptors are chiral. Rule: one stereocenter flipped = enantiomers; same physical properties, different behavior only toward chiral partners."}],"trap":"Different odors pull you toward diastereomers, but with only one stereocenter, two stereoisomers must be enantiomers. The odor difference comes from the chiral receptor, not from different physical properties."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. Solid wedge = toward you; dashed = away. The two drawings differ only at the arrowed carbon.","fig":["t5-rl-read","t5-sl-read"]},{"text":"**Trace the atoms: the same atoms, joined in the same order in both**. Only the 3D arrangement at one carbon differs.","fig":["t5-rl-same","t5-sl-same"]},{"text":"**That carbon holds 4 different groups: H, the C(=CH₂)CH₃ group, and two different paths around the ring**. It is a stereocenter.","fig":"t5-rl-4"},{"text":"**Same connections, different 3D = stereoisomers**. Mirror images (like hands) = enantiomers; others = diastereomers. One center flipped = enantiomers.","fig":["t5-rl-hand","t5-sl-hand"]},{"text":"**Enantiomers have identical boiling point and density**. They twist polarized light (light vibrating in one plane) equally, in opposite directions.","fig":["t5-rl-bp","t5-sl-bp"]},{"text":"**Odor receptors are proteins built from L-amino acids, so they are chiral (one-handed)**. Like a glove, they fit each enantiomer differently.","fig":["t5-rl-odor","t5-sl-odor"]},{"text":"**A different smell does not make diastereomers; enantiomers never differ in boiling point; opposite rotation does not mean different connections**."},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: same connections + one stereocenter flipped = enantiomers: same bp and density, opposite rotation, different only with chiral things. Check each label and reason.","fig":["r-limonene","s-limonene"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"Blood (ρ = 1060 kg/m³) flows at 30 cm/s through a vessel with an inner diameter of 1.6 cm. At a narrowing, the inner diameter is 0.8 cm. Assuming steady flow of an incompressible fluid, the speed of blood in the narrowing is:",idStem:"Blood flows at 30 cm/s in a tube 1.6 cm wide. How fast does it flow where the tube narrows to 0.8 cm wide?",choices:["60 cm/s","120 cm/s","15 cm/s","7.5 cm/s"],ans:1,exp:"**Continuity: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂**, with A = πd²/4. Halving d makes A one-fourth, so v becomes 4 × 30 = **120 cm/s**. Density is not needed.",src:"pe5-cp:28",how:{"ask":"What stays the same along the tube, and how does the cross-sectional area depend on the diameter?","know":"**Continuity equation:** for an incompressible fluid, the volume flow rate Q = Av is the same everywhere in the tube. **Area of a circle A = πr² = πd²/4**, so A ∝ d².","steps":[{"text":"Steady, incompressible flow means the volume flow rate Q = Av is the same everywhere (continuity). Density cancels.","hl":"Assuming steady flow of an incompressible fluid"},{"text":"Area scales with diameter squared.","calc":["A = πd²/4 → A ∝ d²","d₂/d₁ = 0.8 / 1.6 = **½**","A₂/A₁ = (½)² = **¼**"]},{"text":"Apply continuity.","calc":["A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ → v₂ = v₁ × A₁/A₂","v₂ = 30 cm/s × **4**","v₂ = **120 cm/s**"]},{"text":"One-fourth the area means four times the speed. 60 cm/s scales with d instead of d², and 15 or 7.5 cm/s would slow down in a narrowing. Answer: 120 cm/s.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Area and speed at the two sections","x":{"label":"Section"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["Wide, d = 1.6 cm","Narrow, d = 0.8 cm"],"series":[{"name":"Area","values":[4,1]},{"name":"Speed ÷ 30 cm/s","values":[1,4]}]},"strike":["60 cm/s","15 cm/s","7.5 cm/s"],"graph":{"bars":[["Narrow, d = 0.8 cm","Area"],["Narrow, d = 0.8 cm","Speed ÷ 30 cm/s"]]}}],"trap":"60 cm/s assumes the area scales with diameter, not diameter squared."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it describes.","marks":[{"t":"30 cm/s","note":"speed in wide part"},{"t":"inner diameter of 1.6 cm","note":"wide part"},{"t":"inner diameter is 0.8 cm","note":"narrow part"}]},{"text":"**Incompressible** = can't be squeezed. **Steady** = not changing over time. So the same volume of blood passes every spot each second.","marks":[{"t":"steady flow of an incompressible fluid","note":"same flow everywhere"}],"calc":["flow Q = area × speed","A₁v₁ = A₂v₂"]},{"text":"Area of a circle grows with **diameter squared**. Half the diameter → one-fourth the area.","marks":[{"t":"inner diameter of 1.6 cm","note":"d₁ = 1.6"},{"t":"inner diameter is 0.8 cm","note":"d₂ = 0.8 = half"}],"calc":["A = πd²/4 → A ∝ d²","d₂/d₁ = 0.8 / 1.6 = **½**","A₂/A₁ = (½)² = **¼**"]},{"text":"Same flow through ¼ the area → blood must go **4 times faster**.","marks":[{"t":"30 cm/s","note":"× 4"}],"calc":["v₂ = v₁ × A₁/A₂","v₂ = 30 cm/s × 4","v₂ = **120 cm/s**"]},{"text":"**See it:** area shrinks to ¼, speed grows ×4. Their product stays the same.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Area and speed at the two sections","x":{"label":"Section"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["Wide, d = 1.6 cm","Narrow, d = 0.8 cm"],"series":[{"name":"Area","values":[4,1]},{"name":"Speed ÷ 30 cm/s","values":[1,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Narrow, d = 0.8 cm","Area"],["Narrow, d = 0.8 cm","Speed ÷ 30 cm/s"]]}},{"text":"Density doesn't appear in A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. **Not needed.**","marks":[{"t":"ρ = 1060 kg/m³","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 60 cm/s scales with d instead of d². 15 or 7.5 cm/s would mean blood slows in a narrowing, which is backwards.","marks":[{"t":"At a narrowing","note":"narrower → faster"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** continuity A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, and area ∝ diameter². Halve the diameter → ¼ the area → 4× the speed."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Consider the data (atomic number, electrons): Ca²⁺: 20, 18; Cu²⁺: 29, 27; Fe²⁺: 26, 24; Zn⁺: 30, 29. Which ion has the ground-state electron configuration of a noble gas?",idStem:"Which ion has a noble-gas electron configuration?",choices:["Ca²⁺, since losing both 4s electrons leaves [Ar]","Fe²⁺, since its 3d subshell is exactly half filled","Cu²⁺, since its 3d subshell is nearly completely filled","Zn⁺, since its 3d subshell is completely filled"],ans:0,exp:"Ca: [Ar]4s² → Ca²⁺: **[Ar]**, 18 electrons like argon. Transition-metal ions lose 4s electrons but **keep 3d electrons**, so they are not noble-gas-like.",src:"pe5-cp:29",how:{"ask":"Write each ion's configuration and see which matches a noble gas exactly.","know":"**Noble-gas configuration** = completely filled shells like He, Ne, Ar (18 e⁻), Kr. For transition-metal ions, remove **4s electrons before 3d**. Fe²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁶, Cu²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁹, Zn⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹.","steps":[{"text":"A noble-gas configuration means the ion's electrons exactly fill the shells of a noble gas, with no extra d or s electrons.","hl":"(atomic number, electrons)"},{"text":"Calcium loses both 4s electrons.","calc":["Ca: [Ar]4s² (20 e⁻)","Ca²⁺: remove **2 × 4s** → 18 e⁻","Ca²⁺ = **[Ar]** ✓"]},{"text":"Transition metals lose 4s electrons first and keep their 3d electrons. Fe²⁺ (d⁶) is not half filled, which would be d⁵, and Cu²⁺ (d⁹) is not noble.","calc":["Fe²⁺: [Ar]**3d⁶**","Cu²⁺: [Ar]**3d⁹**","Zn⁺: [Ar]3d¹⁰**4s¹**"],"strike":["Fe²⁺, since its 3d subshell is exactly half filled","Cu²⁺, since its 3d subshell is nearly completely filled"]},{"text":"Zn⁺ keeps a 4s electron, and a full d subshell is not a noble-gas configuration. Answer: Ca²⁺, since losing both 4s electrons leaves [Ar].","strike":["Zn⁺, since its 3d subshell is completely filled"]}],"trap":"Zn⁺ does have a full 3d subshell, but it still has one 4s electron, and full d does not equal a noble-gas core."},teach:[{"text":"First, **read the data format**: each ion lists its protons, then its electrons.","marks":[{"t":"(atomic number, electrons)","note":"protons, then electrons"},{"t":"Ca²⁺: 20, 18","note":"20 p, 18 e⁻"},{"t":"Zn⁺: 30, 29","note":"30 p, 29 e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Noble gases** (He, Ne, Ar, Kr) have perfectly filled shells. Argon has **18 electrons**: [Ar]. **Ground state** = electrons in their lowest spots.","marks":[{"t":"noble gas","note":"full shells, e.g. Ar = 18"},{"t":"ground-state","note":"lowest-energy arrangement"}]},{"text":"**See it:** only one ion has exactly argon's 18 electrons.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Electrons in each ion vs argon","x":{"label":"Species"},"y":{"label":"Electrons","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"categories":["Ca²⁺","Fe²⁺","Cu²⁺","Zn⁺","Ar"],"series":[{"name":"Electrons","values":[18,24,27,29,18]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Ca²⁺","Electrons"],["Ar","Electrons"]]}},{"text":"**Calcium** loses its two outer 4s electrons and is left with argon's exact arrangement.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺: 20, 18","note":"18 e⁻ = argon"}],"calc":["Ca: [Ar]4s² (20 e⁻)","remove 2 × 4s → 18 e⁻","Ca²⁺ = **[Ar]** ✓"]},{"text":"**Transition metals** lose 4s electrons first but **keep their 3d electrons**, so extra d electrons remain beyond [Ar].","marks":[{"t":"Fe²⁺: 26, 24","note":"[Ar]3d⁶"},{"t":"Cu²⁺: 29, 27","note":"[Ar]3d⁹"},{"t":"Zn⁺: 30, 29","note":"[Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** half-filled d would be d⁵, not Fe²⁺'s d⁶. A full or nearly full d subshell is stable but is **not** a noble-gas configuration.","marks":[{"t":"Fe²⁺: 26, 24","note":"d⁶, not half (d⁵)"},{"t":"Cu²⁺: 29, 27","note":"d⁹: not noble"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an ion has a noble-gas configuration only if its electron count equals a noble gas (2, 10, 18, 36…) with nothing left in d. Main-group ions (Ca²⁺, Na⁺, Cl⁻) do this; transition-metal ions keep d electrons."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A modified phenylalanine carries a –CH₂NH₂ group on its ring, as shown; the –CH₂NH₃⁺ form of this group has a pKa of about 9.3. At pH 7.4, the side chain of this residue is best categorized as:",idStem:"A tryptophan side chain gains a –COOH group. How is it now classified at pH 7?",fig:"aminomethyl-phe",choices:["basic, since the –CH₂NH₂ is mostly protonated","acidic, since the –CH₂NH₃⁺ can donate a proton","hydrophobic, since the benzene ring is unchanged","polar neutral, since the –CH₂NH₂ is mostly unprotonated"],ans:0,exp:"pH 7.4 is below the pKa of 9.3, so the amine is **mostly protonated**: [R–NH₂]/[R–NH₃⁺] = 10^(7.4 − 9.3) ≈ 1/80. The side chain carries a **+1 charge**, as lysine's does, so it is **basic**.",src:"pe5-cp:31",how:{"ask":"Compare the pH with the pKa of the new group. Is the group mostly protonated or not, and what charge does that give the side chain?","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]). If pH < pKa the protonated form dominates; if pH > pKa the deprotonated form dominates.\n• For an amine, the protonated form R–NH₃⁺ is charged (+1) and the deprotonated form R–NH₂ is neutral.\n• Side chains are categorized by their charge at physiological pH: **basic** (+: Lys, Arg), **acidic** (−: Asp, Glu), **polar neutral**, **nonpolar**.\n• Drawings show one form; the pH and the pKa decide which form actually dominates.","steps":[{"text":"Find the group that differs from phenylalanine: the highlighted –CH₂NH₂ on the ring, an amine.","fig":"aminomethyl-phe-hl"},{"text":"Compare the pH with the pKa.","hl":"has a pKa of about 9.3","calc":["pH − pKa = 7.4 − 9.3 = **−1.9**","[–CH₂NH₂]/[–CH₂NH₃⁺] = 10^−1.9 ≈ **1/80**","→ about **99%** protonated, charge **+1**"]},{"text":"Mostly protonated means charged, so neither neutral category fits.","strike":["polar neutral, since the –CH₂NH₂ is mostly unprotonated","hydrophobic, since the benzene ring is unchanged"]},{"text":"Acidic side chains are negative at pH 7.4. Losing H⁺ from –CH₂NH₃⁺ would give a neutral amine, not an anion, and at this pH the group mostly keeps its H⁺.","strike":["acidic, since the –CH₂NH₃⁺ can donate a proton"]},{"text":"Answer: basic. Rule: pH below an amine's pKa means NH₃⁺ and a basic side chain; pH above a carboxylic acid's pKa means COO⁻ and an acidic one."}],"trap":"The drawing shows –CH₂NH₂ with no charge, which suggests polar neutral. The drawing is just one form; at pH 7.4, two units below the pKa, the group is almost all –CH₂NH₃⁺."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. Backbone: NH₂, a middle C, COOH. The side chain here is a benzene ring carrying –CH₂NH₂.","fig":"t5-amp-read"},{"text":"**This N's lone pair (its own electron pair) can grab an H⁺ (a hydrogen without its electron), becoming –NH₃⁺ with a +1 charge**.","fig":"t5-amp-lp"},{"text":"**pH tells how much H⁺ is around (lower pH = more)**. pKa is the pH at which half the groups hold the extra H⁺.","fig":"t5-amp-pka"},{"text":"**pH below pKa: the group holds its H⁺**. 7.4 is 1.9 below 9.3; each unit is ×10, so about 80 of every 81 are –NH₃⁺.","fig":"t5-amp-nh3"},{"text":"**Basic = side chain + at pH 7.4 (like lysine's NH₃⁺)**. Acidic = −. Polar neutral = polar, no charge. Hydrophobic = oily C and H.","fig":["t5-amp-nh3","t5-lys-plus"]},{"text":"**Acidic side chains turn negative by losing H⁺**. –NH₃⁺ losing H⁺ only becomes neutral, and at pH 7.4 it mostly keeps it.","fig":"t5-amp-notacid"},{"text":"**Polar neutral and hydrophobic both need an uncharged side chain**. The ring is oily, but the + charge dominates.","fig":"t5-amp-charge"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: pH below pKa → protonated (amine = NH₃⁺, +1); above → not. Mostly + at pH 7.4 = basic. In each answer, check the category and its reason.","fig":"t5-amp-nh3"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"To identify the catalytic base of an enzyme, its activity is measured across a range of pH, as shown in the figure. The residue must be deprotonated to act, and replacing it with alanine abolishes activity. Which residue is it most likely to be?",idStem:"Which amino acid side chain contains an imidazole ring?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the enzyme versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":4,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Relative activity (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[4,1],[4.5,3],[5,9],[5.5,24],[6,50],[6.5,76],[7,91],[7.5,97],[8,99],[9,100]]}]},choices:["Aspartate, since its side chain has a pKa near 4","Histidine, since its side chain has a pKa near 6","Tyrosine, since its side chain has a pKa near 10","Lysine, since its side chain has a pKa near 10.5"],ans:1,exp:"Activity tracks the fraction of the residue that is deprotonated, so the **midpoint of the curve is the residue's pKa**. Half activity falls at **pH ≈ 6.0**, which matches the **imidazole side chain of histidine (pKa ≈ 6.0)**.",src:"pe5-cp:32",how:{"ask":"Where does the curve reach half of its maximum activity, and which side-chain pKa matches that pH?","know":"• For a group that must be deprotonated to work, activity ∝ fraction deprotonated = 1 / (1 + 10^(pKa − pH)).\n• At **pH = pKa** the group is half deprotonated, so activity is **50%** of maximum: read the pKa where the curve crosses half height.\n• Side-chain pKa values: Asp ≈ 3.9, Glu ≈ 4.1, **His ≈ 6.0**, Cys ≈ 8.3, Tyr ≈ 10.1, Lys ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5.\n• Histidine is the classic catalytic acid/base because its pKa sits near physiological pH.","steps":[{"text":"Activity rises as the residue loses its proton, so the curve is a titration curve of that one group.","hl":"The residue must be deprotonated to act","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":4,"x2":5,"text":"protonated: off"},{"x1":7.5,"x2":9,"text":"deprotonated: on"}]}},{"text":"Find half of the maximum on the y-axis, go across to the curve, then down to the pH axis.","graph":{"yLine":50,"xLine":6,"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":6}],"spans":[{"x":6,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"half = 50%","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"pKa ≈ 6.0","at":"right"}]},"calc":["maximum activity ≈ **100%** → half = **50%**","curve crosses 50% at pH **6.0**","→ pKa ≈ **6.0**"]},{"text":"Check: one pH unit on either side should give about 9% and 91% for a single group with pKa 6.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":5},{"series":"Activity","x":7}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":5,"text":"pH 5: 9%","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":7,"text":"pH 7: 91%","at":"right"}]},"calc":["pH 5: 1 / (1 + 10¹) ≈ **9%**","pH 7: 1 / (1 + 10⁻¹) ≈ **91%**"]},{"text":"A pKa near 4 would center the curve at pH 4; a pKa near 10 would keep the enzyme nearly inactive until above pH 9.","strike":["Aspartate, since its side chain has a pKa near 4","Tyrosine, since its side chain has a pKa near 10","Lysine, since its side chain has a pKa near 10.5"]},{"text":"Answer: histidine, whose imidazole side chain is highlighted. Rule: the pH at half-maximal activity reads out the pKa of the catalytic group.","fig":"histidine-sc"}],"trap":"Aspartate is tempting because carboxylates often act as bases, but a pKa near 4 would put the curve's midpoint at pH 4, where this enzyme is almost inactive."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = how active the enzyme is**, as a % of its best. 100% = fully working; 0% = switched off.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"its activity is measured","note":"the y axis"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = pH going up.** Low pH = lots of H⁺ (acidic); high pH = few H⁺, so groups **lose** their H⁺.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"a range of pH","note":"the x axis"}]},{"text":"A **catalytic base** grabs an H⁺ during the reaction, so it must first be **deprotonated** (have no H⁺ of its own).","marks":[{"t":"catalytic base","note":"grabs an H⁺"},{"t":"must be deprotonated to act","note":"works only without H⁺"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":4,"x2":5,"text":"has H⁺: off"},{"x1":7.5,"x2":9,"text":"lost H⁺: on"}]}},{"text":"**pKa** = the pH where **half** of a group has lost its H⁺. Here that is where activity is **half** of max.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":4.5,"text":"nearly off","at":"above"},{"series":"Activity","x":9,"text":"max = 100%","at":"below"}]},"calc":["half deprotonated → half activity","pH at 50% activity = **pKa**"]},{"text":"**Read it:** go to 50% on the y axis, across to the curve, then down to the pH axis.","graph":{"yLine":50,"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":6}],"spans":[{"x":6,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"50%","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"pKa ≈ 6.0","at":"right"}]},"calc":["half of 100% = **50%**","curve hits 50% at pH **6.0** → pKa ≈ 6.0"]},{"text":"**Check:** one pH unit either side of pKa should give about 9% and 91% for one group. The curve agrees.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":5},{"series":"Activity","x":7}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":5,"text":"pH 5: 9%","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":7,"text":"pH 7: 91%","at":"right"}]},"calc":["pH 5: 1 / (1 + 10¹) ≈ **9%**","pH 7: 1 / (1 + 10⁻¹) ≈ **91%**"]},{"text":"Alanine's side chain is just CH₃: it can't take an H⁺. Losing all activity proves **this residue** does the job.","marks":[{"t":"replacing it with alanine abolishes activity","note":"proves it's the key residue"}]},{"text":"**Match the pKa** to a side chain. Only histidine sits near 6.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain pKa values","x":{"label":"Residue"},"y":{"label":"Side-chain pKa","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Asp","His","Tyr","Lys"],"series":[{"name":"pKa","values":[3.9,6,10.1,10.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["His","pKa"]]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a pH–activity curve, the pH at half-maximal activity is the pKa of the catalytic group. pKa ≈ 6 → histidine; ≈ 4 → Asp/Glu; ≈ 10 → Tyr/Lys."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A cysteine buried in a protein's core does not react with a thiol-labeling reagent at 25 °C. After the protein is heated to 75 °C in buffer containing no reducing agent, the cysteine is labeled, and mass spectrometry shows the polypeptide's mass is unchanged. Which explanation is most consistent?",idStem:"Heating a protein to 75 °C makes a buried residue react with a chemical. Why?",choices:["Heating hydrolyzed peptide bonds, releasing the cysteine","Heating reduced a disulfide bond containing the cysteine","Heating let the reagent pass through the folded core","Heating unfolded the protein, exposing the cysteine"],ans:3,exp:"Heat **denatures** a protein: noncovalent interactions break and it unfolds, but covalent (primary) structure is unchanged. The buried cysteine becomes solvent-exposed and reachable.",src:"pe5-cp:33",how:{"ask":"Which explanation fits all three facts: heat, no reducing agent, and unchanged mass?","know":"**Denaturation** = loss of 2°, 3° and 4° structure by breaking H bonds and hydrophobic contacts; the **primary structure** (peptide bonds) stays. Breaking peptide bonds (hydrolysis) changes the mass. Reducing a disulfide needs a reducing agent.","steps":[{"text":"No reaction at 25 °C means the thiol is hidden from the reagent, not missing.","hl":"buried in a protein's core does not react"},{"text":"Hydrolysis adds water and breaks the chain, which would change the masses seen.","hl":"mass spectrometry shows the polypeptide's mass is unchanged","calc":["each hydrolyzed peptide bond: + H₂O = **+18 Da**","observed Δmass = **0**"],"strike":["Heating hydrolyzed peptide bonds, releasing the cysteine"]},{"text":"Reducing a disulfide needs an electron donor such as DTT, and none was present.","hl":"containing no reducing agent","calc":["R–S–S–R + 2e⁻ + 2H⁺ → 2 R–SH"],"strike":["Heating reduced a disulfide bond containing the cysteine"]},{"text":"Heat breaks noncovalent interactions, so the chain unfolds and the buried thiol becomes exposed; covalent structure is untouched. A folded core has no pores for the reagent to pass through. Answer: heating unfolded the protein, exposing the cysteine.","fig":"cysteine-sc","strike":["Heating let the reagent pass through the folded core"]}],"trap":"Disulfide reduction would expose a cysteine, but the buffer had no reducing agent, so nothing could donate the electrons."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what changed** between the two tests.","marks":[{"t":"buried in a protein's core","note":"hidden inside"},{"t":"at 25 °C","note":"not labeled"},{"t":"heated to 75 °C","note":"labeled"}]},{"text":"**Cysteine** is an amino acid with an –SH group (a **thiol**). The labeling reagent sticks to –SH, but only if it can reach it.","marks":[{"t":"A cysteine","note":"amino acid with –SH"},{"t":"thiol-labeling reagent","note":"tags –SH groups"}]},{"text":"A folded protein is held by many **weak** contacts (H bonds, oily contacts). The **core** is packed tight; the reagent can't get in.","marks":[{"t":"does not react","note":"can't reach it when folded"}]},{"text":"**Heat breaks the weak contacts** and the chain unfolds (**denatures**). The hidden –SH ends up on the outside.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Unfolding as the protein is heated (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":25,"max":75,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Fraction unfolded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Unfolded","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[25,0],[35,0.01],[45,0.05],[55,0.3],[60,0.5],[65,0.7],[75,0.98]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Unfolded","x":25,"text":"folded: Cys hidden","at":"above"},{"series":"Unfolded","x":75,"text":"unfolded: Cys exposed","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Mass unchanged** → no covalent bond was broken. Cutting a peptide bond adds water and would change the mass.","marks":[{"t":"mass is unchanged","note":"no bonds broken"}],"calc":["each peptide bond cut: + H₂O = **+18 Da**","observed change = **0**"]},{"text":"Breaking an S–S bridge needs a **reducing agent** (electron donor). None was present.","marks":[{"t":"no reducing agent","note":"nothing to break S–S"}],"calc":["R–S–S–R + 2e⁻ + 2H⁺ → 2 R–SH"]},{"text":"Heating doesn't open holes in a folded core for the reagent to slip through; it opens the **whole fold**.","marks":[{"t":"heated to 75 °C","note":"unfolds it"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heat denatures by breaking noncovalent bonds; covalent structure and mass stay the same. Buried groups become reachable once the protein unfolds."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A volatile buffer for mass spectrometry is made by neutralizing acetic acid, CH₃COOH, with ammonia. Which are the formula and molar mass of the salt formed? Use H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16 g/mol.",idStem:"Which formula is ammonium acetate?",choices:["NH₄CH₃CO₂, 77 g/mol","NH₄HCO₂, 63 g/mol","NH₄HCO₃, 79 g/mol","NH₄NH₂CO₂, 78 g/mol"],ans:0,exp:"NH₃ + CH₃COOH → NH₄⁺ + CH₃CO₂⁻: **ammonium acetate, NH₄CH₃CO₂** = 18 + 59 = **77 g/mol**.",src:"pe5-cp:34",how:{"ask":"Transfer the acid's proton to ammonia, then combine the two ions and add up the masses.","know":"Acid + base: the acid gives H⁺ to the base. **NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺ (ammonium).** **CH₃COOH − H⁺ → CH₃CO₂⁻ (acetate).** Formate = HCO₂⁻ (1 carbon); bicarbonate = HCO₃⁻; carbamate = NH₂CO₂⁻.","steps":[{"text":"Neutralization transfers H⁺ from the acid to ammonia.","hl":"neutralizing acetic acid, CH₃COOH, with ammonia","fig":"acetic-acid","calc":["CH₃COOH + NH₃ → **NH₄⁺** + **CH₃CO₂⁻**"]},{"text":"The salt is cation + anion: NH₄CH₃CO₂, ammonium acetate.","fig":"ammonium-acetate"},{"text":"Add up the molar mass.","calc":["N: 1 × 14 = 14","H: 7 × 1 = **7**","C: 2 × 12 = **24**","O: 2 × 16 = 32","total = **77 g/mol**"]},{"text":"NH₄HCO₂ is ammonium formate, with one carbon; NH₄HCO₃ is ammonium bicarbonate; NH₄NH₂CO₂ is ammonium carbamate. Answer: NH₄CH₃CO₂, 77 g/mol.","strike":["NH₄HCO₂, 63 g/mol","NH₄HCO₃, 79 g/mol","NH₄NH₂CO₂, 78 g/mol"]}],"trap":"NH₄HCO₂ is ammonium formate, from formic acid, which has only one carbon. Acetic acid has two."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find the two reactants** and the atom masses you'll need.","marks":[{"t":"acetic acid, CH₃COOH","note":"the acid"},{"t":"ammonia","note":"the base (NH₃)"},{"t":"H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16 g/mol","note":"atom masses"}]},{"text":"**Neutralizing** = an acid hands an H⁺ to a base. What's left pairs up into a **salt**.","marks":[{"t":"neutralizing","note":"acid gives H⁺ to base"}],"calc":["acid → gives H⁺","base → takes H⁺"]},{"text":"**Acetic acid** gives away the H on its –COOH. What remains is **acetate**, CH₃CO₂⁻.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃COOH","note":"loses this last H"}],"calc":["CH₃COOH → **CH₃CO₂⁻** + H⁺"]},{"text":"**Ammonia** (NH₃) takes that H⁺ and becomes **ammonium**, NH₄⁺.","marks":[{"t":"ammonia","note":"NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺"}],"calc":["NH₃ + H⁺ → **NH₄⁺**"]},{"text":"The **salt** = the + ion and the − ion together: **ammonium acetate**.","marks":[{"t":"the salt formed","note":"NH₄⁺ + CH₃CO₂⁻"}],"calc":["NH₄⁺ + CH₃CO₂⁻ → **NH₄CH₃CO₂**"]},{"text":"**Count the atoms** in NH₄CH₃CO₂ and add their masses.","marks":[{"t":"C = 12","note":"× 2 carbons"},{"t":"N = 14","note":"× 1 nitrogen"},{"t":"O = 16","note":"× 2 oxygens"}],"calc":["N: 1 × 14 = 14","H: 4 + 3 = 7 × 1 = 7","C: 2 × 12 = 24","O: 2 × 16 = 32","total = **77 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**See it:** each element's share of the 77 g/mol.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Mass in ammonium acetate, NH₄CH₃CO₂","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Mass (g/mol)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"categories":["N","H","C","O"],"series":[{"name":"Mass","values":[14,7,24,32]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C","Mass"],["O","Mass"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** NH₄HCO₂ is ammonium formate (1 carbon); NH₄HCO₃ is bicarbonate; NH₄NH₂CO₂ is carbamate. Acetic acid has **2 carbons**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃COOH","note":"2 C: keep the CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid + base → the acid loses H⁺ (its anion) and the base gains H⁺ (its cation); the salt is the two ions together. Then add atom masses carefully."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5C",q:"At pH 8.0, the phosphorylated form of an enzyme has a net charge of −6, and the unphosphorylated form has a net charge of −4; their masses differ by less than 1%. Which method separates them, and which form elutes later as the salt concentration is increased?",idStem:"Two forms of a protein are both negatively charged, but one is more negative. Which technique separates them?",choices:["Cation exchange; the phosphorylated form","Anion exchange; the unphosphorylated form","Anion exchange; the phosphorylated form","Size exclusion; the unphosphorylated form"],ans:2,exp:"Both forms are negative, so they bind a **positively charged anion-exchange resin**. The **more negative (phosphorylated) form binds tighter** and needs more salt to elute.",src:"pe5-cp:35",how:{"ask":"Which resin will hold negatively charged proteins, and which form holds on more strongly?","know":"**Ion-exchange chromatography:** an **anion exchanger** has + groups and binds negative molecules; a **cation exchanger** has − groups and binds positive ones. Bound proteins are released by rising salt; **more charge → tighter binding → elutes later**. Size exclusion separates by size.","steps":[{"text":"Both forms are negative at pH 8, so they stick to a positively charged anion-exchange resin. A cation exchanger would bind neither.","hl":"net charge of −6","strike":["Cation exchange; the phosphorylated form"]},{"text":"Size exclusion separates by size, and a mass difference under 1% can't be resolved.","hl":"their masses differ by less than 1%","strike":["Size exclusion; the unphosphorylated form"]},{"text":"The more charge, the tighter the binding.","calc":["binding strength ∝ |net charge|","|−6| > |−4|","phosphorylated binds **tighter** → needs **more salt**"]},{"text":"Salt anions compete for the resin's + sites: the −4 form comes off first, the −6 form later. Answer: anion exchange; the phosphorylated form.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Expected anion-exchange elution, illustrative","x":{"label":"[NaCl] (mM)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"y":{"label":"A₂₈₀","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Unphosphorylated, −4","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[100,0],[150,0.3],[200,1],[250,0.3],[300,0],[500,0]]},{"name":"Phosphorylated, −6","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[250,0],[300,0.3],[350,1],[400,0.3],[450,0],[500,0]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unphosphorylated, −4","x":200},{"series":"Phosphorylated, −6","x":350}],"labels":[{"series":"Unphosphorylated, −4","x":200,"text":"−4 comes off first (200 mM)","at":"left"},{"series":"Phosphorylated, −6","x":350,"text":"−6 comes off later (350 mM)","at":"right"}]},"strike":["Anion exchange; the unphosphorylated form"]}],"trap":"\"Anion exchange; the unphosphorylated form\" picks the right column but reverses the elution order. More charge holds on longer."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** Two forms of one enzyme, at pH 8.0.","marks":[{"t":"net charge of −6","note":"phosphorylated form: −6"},{"t":"net charge of −4","note":"unphosphorylated form: −4"},{"t":"less than 1%","note":"almost the same size"}]},{"text":"**Chromatography** = pouring a mixture through a column of beads (the **resin**). Molecules that stick to the beads come off (**elute**) later.","marks":[{"t":"elutes later","note":"comes off the column last"}]},{"text":"**Both forms are negative.** The bars show each form's charge at pH 8.0.","marks":[{"t":"At pH 8.0","note":"both forms counted here"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Net charge at pH 8.0","x":{"label":"Form"},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-8,"max":0,"step":2},"categories":["Phosphorylated","Unphosphorylated"],"series":[{"name":"Net charge","values":[-6,-4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Phosphorylated","Net charge"],["Unphosphorylated","Net charge"]]}},{"text":"**Ion exchange** = beads with a charge. **Anion exchange**: + beads grab − molecules. **Cation exchange**: − beads grab + molecules. Opposites attract.","marks":[{"t":"net charge of −6","note":"sticks to + beads"},{"t":"net charge of −4","note":"also sticks to + beads"}]},{"text":"**Cation exchange fails:** its − beads repel both negative forms, so neither sticks and they wash through together.","marks":[{"t":"net charge of −6","note":"repelled by − beads"},{"t":"net charge of −4","note":"repelled by − beads"}]},{"text":"**Size exclusion fails:** it sorts by size, and these two weigh almost the same.","marks":[{"t":"their masses differ by less than 1%","note":"size can't tell them apart"}]},{"text":"**Salt releases them.** Salt's own − ions (Cl⁻) crowd onto the + beads and push the proteins off. More salt = stronger push.","marks":[{"t":"as the salt concentration is increased","note":"salt pushes proteins off"}]},{"text":"**More charge = held tighter = needs more salt.** The −6 form comes off last.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Anion-exchange column: protein coming off vs salt","x":{"label":"Salt (mM)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"y":{"label":"Protein coming off","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Unphosphorylated (−4)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[100,0],[150,3],[200,8],[250,3],[300,0]]},{"name":"Phosphorylated (−6)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[200,0],[250,3],[300,8],[350,3],[400,0]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Unphosphorylated (−4)","x":200,"text":"−4: comes off first","at":"left"},{"series":"Phosphorylated (−6)","x":300,"text":"−6: comes off later","at":"right"}]},"calc":["|−6| > |−4|","phosphorylated binds **tighter** → elutes **later**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** negative protein → anion exchange (+ beads); positive → cation exchange. The more charge, the tighter it binds and the later it elutes as salt rises."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"An unknown with formula C₅H₁₀O gives an IR spectrum with a strong, sharp band at 1715 cm⁻¹, no broad band at 3200–3550 cm⁻¹, and no pair of weak bands near 2720 and 2820 cm⁻¹. Which structure is the unknown?",idStem:"A C=O sits inside a ring, with ring carbons on both sides of it. What functional group is it?",choiceFigs:["3-pentanone","pentanal","cyclopentanol","tetrahydropyran"],choices:["Ketone","Aldehyde","Cyclic alcohol","Cyclic ether"],ans:0,exp:"A strong band near **1715 cm⁻¹ is a C=O stretch**, which rules out the alcohol and the ether. Aldehydes also show the **C–H stretch of the CHO group as two weak bands near 2720 and 2820 cm⁻¹**; these are absent. A C=O with no aldehyde C–H and no O–H is a **ketone: 3-pentanone**.",src:"pe5-cp:36",how:{"ask":"Use each IR clue to rule structures in or out: which groups absorb near 1715, which give a broad band near 3300, and which give the pair near 2720 and 2820?","know":"• **IR spectroscopy** detects bond vibrations; each bond type absorbs in a typical range of wavenumbers (cm⁻¹).\n• **C=O** stretch: strong and sharp, 1700–1750 cm⁻¹ (ketone ≈ 1715, aldehyde ≈ 1725, ester ≈ 1740).\n• **O–H** of an alcohol: broad, 3200–3550 cm⁻¹. Carboxylic acid O–H: very broad, 2500–3300.\n• **Aldehyde C–H** (the H on the C=O carbon): two weak bands near 2720 and 2820 cm⁻¹.\n• An ether shows only a C–O stretch at 1050–1150 cm⁻¹, with no C=O and no O–H.\n• Use absent bands as actively as present ones.","steps":[{"text":"A strong, sharp band near 1715 is a C=O stretch. Only two of the drawings contain C=O.","hl":"a strong, sharp band at 1715 cm⁻¹"},{"text":"The alcohol and the ether have no C=O, and the alcohol's O–H band is also missing.","strike":["Cyclic alcohol","Cyclic ether"],"calc":["alcohol: O–H broad at **3200–3550**, no C=O","ether: C–O at **1050–1150** only, no C=O"]},{"text":"An aldehyde's H on the C=O carbon gives that pair of weak bands. It is missing, so the C=O carbon bears no H.","hl":"no pair of weak bands near 2720 and 2820 cm⁻¹","strike":["Aldehyde"]},{"text":"The highlighted carbonyl carbon is bonded to two carbons: a ketone, 3-pentanone, C₅H₁₀O.","fig":"3-pentanone-hl"},{"text":"Answer: the ketone. Rule: 1715 = C=O; add 2720/2820 for an aldehyde, a very broad 2500–3300 for an acid, nothing extra for a ketone."}],"trap":"The aldehyde also has a C=O near 1715–1725 cm⁻¹, so that band alone does not decide. The missing 2720/2820 pair is what rules the aldehyde out."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H**. All four choices are C₅H₁₀O, so the formula can't decide; the spectrum must.","fig":["t5-pal-read","t5-pon-read"]},{"text":"**IR light makes bonds stretch like springs**. Each bond absorbs at its own number, in cm⁻¹ (a frequency label: stiffer bond = bigger number).","fig":"t5-pon-spring"},{"text":"**A strong, sharp band near 1715 cm⁻¹ means a C=O (a carbonyl)**. The unknown has one.","fig":"t5-pentanone-co"},{"text":"**The cyclic alcohol has an O–H, not a C=O**. O–H gives a broad band at 3200–3550; the unknown shows neither.","fig":"t5-cyclopentanol-oh"},{"text":"**The cyclic ether's O sits between two carbons**. No C=O, so no 1715 band.","fig":"t5-thp-o"},{"text":"**In an aldehyde the C=O ends the chain, so its carbon also holds an H**. That C–H gives two weak bands near 2720 and 2820, which are missing here.","fig":"t5-pentanal-cho"},{"text":"**In a ketone the C=O is mid-chain: its carbon has two carbon neighbors and no H, so there is no 2720/2820 pair**.","fig":"t5-pon-ketone"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: 1715 strong = C=O; broad 3200–3550 = O–H; 2720 + 2820 = aldehyde C–H. In each drawing ask: a C=O? an O–H? an H on the C=O carbon?","fig":["t5-pal-read","t5-pon-read"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A rigid flask held at 500 K initially contains N₂ and H₂ in a 1:3 mole ratio at a total pressure of 1.0 atm. If the reaction N₂(g) + 3 H₂(g) → 2 NH₃(g) goes to completion, what is the final pressure in the flask?",idStem:"In a sealed flask at constant temperature, 4 mol of gas react to form 2 mol of gas. If the starting pressure is 1.0 atm, what is the final pressure?",choices:["1.0 atm","2.0 atm","0.50 atm","0.25 atm"],ans:2,exp:"At constant V and T, **P ∝ n(gas)**. 4 mol of gas → 2 mol of gas halves the moles, so P = **0.50 atm**.",src:"pe5-cp:37",how:{"ask":"With volume and temperature fixed, what does pressure track, and how does the gas count change?","know":"**PV = nRT** → at fixed V and T, **P is proportional to the total moles of gas**. Count gas moles on each side of the balanced equation.","steps":[{"text":"Rigid flask: V fixed. Held at 500 K: T fixed. Then P = nRT/V, so P is proportional to the moles of gas.","hl":"A rigid flask held at 500 K"},{"text":"Count moles of gas before and after. The 1:3 ratio matches the equation, so nothing is left over.","hl":"in a 1:3 mole ratio","calc":["start: 1 N₂ + 3 H₂ = **4 mol gas**","end: 2 NH₃ = **2 mol gas**"]},{"text":"The gas count halves.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles of gas per mole of N₂","x":{"label":"Stage"},"y":{"label":"mol of gas","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["Start","End"],"series":[{"name":"Moles of gas","values":[4,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["End","Moles of gas"]]}},{"text":"Scale the pressure. 2.0 atm flips the ratio; 1.0 atm ignores the change in moles. Answer: 0.50 atm.","calc":["P₂ = P₁ × n₂/n₁","P₂ = 1.0 atm × **2/4**","P₂ = **0.50 atm**"],"strike":["1.0 atm","2.0 atm","0.25 atm"]}],"trap":"2.0 atm flips the ratio (4/2 instead of 2/4). Fewer gas molecules means lower pressure."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"rigid flask","note":"volume can't change"},{"t":"500 K","note":"temperature fixed"},{"t":"1.0 atm","note":"starting pressure"}]},{"text":"**Pressure** = gas molecules hitting the walls. With volume and temperature fixed, more molecules = more hits = more pressure.","marks":[{"t":"total pressure","note":"from gas hitting walls"}],"calc":["PV = nRT → P = n × (RT/V)","V and T fixed → **P ∝ n** (moles of gas)"]},{"text":"**Read the equation.** The big number in front (coefficient) = how many moles. (g) = it is a gas.","marks":[{"t":"N₂(g)","note":"1 mol gas"},{"t":"3 H₂(g)","note":"3 mol gas"},{"t":"2 NH₃(g)","note":"2 mol gas"}]},{"text":"**The arrow means \"turns into\".** Left side is used up; right side is made. \"Completion\" means the left side is used up entirely.","marks":[{"t":"→","note":"turns into"},{"t":"goes to completion","note":"all reactants used up"}]},{"text":"**The 1:3 ratio is the exact recipe**, so nothing is left over at the end.","marks":[{"t":"1:3 mole ratio","note":"matches 1 N₂ : 3 H₂"}]},{"text":"**Count gas moles before and after.** 4 moles of gas become 2.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles of gas per 1 mol of N₂","x":{"label":"Stage"},"y":{"label":"Moles of gas","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["Start (1 N₂ + 3 H₂)","End (2 NH₃)"],"series":[{"name":"Gas moles","values":[4,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Start (1 N₂ + 3 H₂)","Gas moles"],["End (2 NH₃)","Gas moles"]]},"calc":["start: 1 + 3 = **4 mol gas**","end: **2 mol gas**"]},{"text":"**Half the gas → half the pressure.**","marks":[{"t":"1.0 atm","note":"× 2/4"}],"calc":["P_final = 1.0 atm × (2 / 4)","P_final = **0.50 atm**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1.0 atm ignores that gas disappears; 2.0 atm flips the ratio (4/2); 0.25 atm halves the pressure twice.","marks":[{"t":"final pressure","note":"fewer gas moles → lower"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fixed volume and temperature → pressure is proportional to moles of gas. Count gas coefficients on each side and scale the pressure by after ÷ before."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"A neutral compound with log P (octanol/water) = 3.5 and no ionizable groups is shaken with equal volumes of water and diethyl ether, and the layers are allowed to separate. Where will most of the compound be found?",idStem:"A lipophilic compound is shaken with water and an ether. Where does it end up?",choices:["In the water layer, since log P > 0 favors water","In the ether layer, since the compound is lipophilic","In the water layer, since ether is less dense than water","Split evenly, since the two volumes are equal"],ans:1,exp:"**log P > 0 means the compound prefers the organic phase** (P ≈ 10³·⁵ ≈ 3000 in favor of octanol). Like dissolves like: it goes into the nonpolar ether layer.",src:"pe5-cp:38",how:{"ask":"What does a positive log P tell you about where the compound prefers to be?","know":"**Partition coefficient P = [compound]organic / [compound]water.** log P > 0 → more in the organic layer (lipophilic); log P < 0 → more in water. **Like dissolves like.** Density only decides which layer is on top.","steps":[{"text":"P is the ratio of concentrations, organic over water.","hl":"log P (octanol/water) = 3.5","calc":["P = [compound]organic / [compound]water","log P = 3.5 → P = 10³·⁵ ≈ **3 × 10³**"]},{"text":"log P > 0 means the organic phase wins. \"Favors water\" flips the definition.","strike":["In the water layer, since log P > 0 favors water"]},{"text":"Neutral with nothing to ionize, the compound can't be pulled into water by pH. Ether, like octanol, is a nonpolar organic solvent.","hl":"no ionizable groups"},{"text":"With equal volumes, almost all of it goes to ether. Density only decides which layer is on top, and equal volumes don't mean equal amounts. Answer: the ether layer, since the compound is lipophilic.","calc":["fraction in ether ≈ P / (P + 1)","≈ 3000 / 3001 ≈ **99.97%**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Where the compound ends up, equal volumes","x":{"label":"Layer"},"y":{"label":"% of compound","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Ether layer","Water layer"],"series":[{"name":"% of compound","values":[99.97,0.03]}]},"strike":["In the water layer, since ether is less dense than water","Split evenly, since the two volumes are equal"],"graph":{"bars":[["Ether layer","% of compound"]]}}],"trap":"\"log P > 0 favors water\" flips the definition: P has the organic concentration on top."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"log P (octanol/water) = 3.5","note":"oil-vs-water preference"},{"t":"no ionizable groups","note":"never gets a charge"},{"t":"equal volumes","note":"same-size layers"}]},{"text":"**P (partition coefficient)** = how much dissolves in the oily layer divided by how much in water, after shaking.","marks":[{"t":"octanol/water","note":"oil layer ÷ water layer"}],"calc":["P = [compound]oil / [compound]water"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.** log P = 3.5 means P = 10 to the 3.5 power.","marks":[{"t":"= 3.5","note":"power of 10"}],"calc":["P = 10³·⁵ = 10³ × 10⁰·⁵","P ≈ 1000 × 3.2 ≈ **3000**","3000 parts in oil for every 1 in water"]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water is polar; octanol and **diethyl ether** are both oily, nonpolar solvents. A compound that loves octanol also loves ether.","marks":[{"t":"equal volumes of water","note":"polar"},{"t":"diethyl ether","note":"oily, like octanol"}]},{"text":"**Almost all of it ends up in ether.**","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Where the compound ends up (P ≈ 3000)","x":{"label":"Layer"},"y":{"label":"% of compound","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Ether layer","Water layer"],"series":[{"name":"% of compound","values":[99.97,0.03]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Ether layer","% of compound"]]},"calc":["3000 / 3001 ≈ **99.97%** in ether"]},{"text":"**No charge, ever.** Charged molecules like water; this one can't be charged at any pH, so nothing pulls it into water.","marks":[{"t":"A neutral compound","note":"no charge"},{"t":"no ionizable groups","note":"pH can't change that"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** density only decides which layer sits on top, not where the compound goes. Equal volumes don't mean a 50/50 split.","marks":[{"t":"the layers are allowed to separate","note":"density = which is on top"},{"t":"equal volumes","note":"still not 50/50"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** log P > 0 → prefers the oily (organic) layer; log P < 0 → prefers water. Each +1 in log P = 10× more in the oily layer."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Enzyme activity was measured at several inhibitor concentrations and plotted against the logarithm of the inhibitor concentration, as shown. Under these assay conditions, the authors take the inhibition constant to equal the concentration that gives half-maximal activity. What is it?",idStem:"On a semi-log plot of enzyme activity against inhibitor concentration, where do you read Ki?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Enzyme activity versus inhibitor concentration","x":{"label":"log₁₀([I] / μM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Activity (% of uninhibited)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,95],[0.5,86],[1,67],[1.3,50],[1.5,39],[2,17],[2.5,6],[3,2]]}]},choices:["About 1.3 μM","About 10 μM","About 20 μM","About 100 μM"],ans:2,exp:"Half-maximal activity, **50%**, falls at **log[I] = 1.3** on the x-axis. The axis is a logarithm, so [I] = 10^1.3 μM ≈ **20 μM**.",src:"pe5-cp:39",how:{"ask":"Where does the curve cross 50% activity, and what concentration does that x-axis value stand for?","know":"• Dose-response curves plot activity against **log[I]**, which makes the S-shape symmetric and spreads data over many powers of ten.\n• Each x-value is an exponent: log[I] = 1 means [I] = 10¹ = 10 μM; log[I] = 2 means 100 μM.\n• The concentration at half-maximal activity is the **IC₅₀**. Read it by going across from 50% to the curve, then down to the x-axis, then taking the antilog.\n• Useful: 10^0.3 ≈ 2, so 10^1.3 ≈ 20.","steps":[{"text":"The x-axis is a logarithm, so every number on it is an exponent.","hl":"plotted against the logarithm of the inhibitor concentration","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"Go across from 50% to the curve, then straight down.","graph":{"yLine":50,"xLine":1.3,"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3,"text":"log[I] = 1.3","at":"right"}]},"calc":["maximum ≈ 100% → half = **50%**","curve crosses 50% at log[I] = **1.3**"]},{"text":"Undo the logarithm.","calc":["[I] = 10^1.3 μM","10^1.3 = 10¹ × 10^0.3 = 10 × **2.0**","[I] ≈ **20 μM**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3,"text":"[I] = 10^1.3 ≈ 20 μM","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Check the neighbors: at 10 μM (log 1) activity is still 67%, and at 100 μM (log 2) it is down to 17%. And 1.3 μM is the log value misread as a concentration.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":1},{"series":"Activity","x":2}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1,"text":"10 μM: 67%","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":2,"text":"100 μM: 17%","at":"right"}]},"strike":["About 1.3 μM","About 10 μM","About 100 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: about 20 μM. Rule: on a log axis, read the x-value at 50%, then raise 10 to that power."}],"trap":"1.3 μM is what you get by reading the x-axis value as the concentration itself. That number is a logarithm and must be converted: 10^1.3 ≈ 20 μM."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = how much the enzyme still works**, as a % of the enzyme with no inhibitor. 100% = untouched; 0% = fully blocked.","marks":[{"t":"Enzyme activity","note":"y axis: % still working"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**An inhibitor** is a molecule that slows an enzyme down. **Left to right = more inhibitor**, but on a log scale.","marks":[{"t":"logarithm of the inhibitor concentration","note":"x axis: a power of 10"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**On a log axis every number is an exponent:** 0 → 1 μM, 1 → 10 μM, 2 → 100 μM, 3 → 1000 μM.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1,"text":"x = 1 → 10 μM","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":2,"text":"x = 2 → 100 μM","at":"right"}]},"calc":["[I] = 10^x μM","x = 1 → 10¹ = **10 μM**","x = 2 → 10² = **100 μM**"]},{"text":"**One falling curve:** little inhibitor, the enzyme works almost fully; lots of inhibitor, it is almost shut down.","graph":{"focus":["Activity"],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":0,"text":"little inhibitor: 95%","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":3,"text":"lots: 2%","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**The target:** the concentration that leaves **half** the activity. The maximum is 100%, so half-max = 50%.","marks":[{"t":"inhibition constant","note":"the number we want"},{"t":"half-maximal activity","note":"= 50% on the y axis"}],"graph":{"yLine":50,"spans":[{"x":0.2,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"half-max = 50%","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Go across from 50% to the curve, then straight down** to the x axis.","graph":{"yLine":50,"xLine":1.3,"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1.3,"text":"50% at x = 1.3","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Undo the log** to turn x = 1.3 into a concentration.","marks":[{"t":"the concentration that gives half-maximal activity","note":"= 10^1.3 μM"}],"calc":["[I] = 10^1.3 μM","= 10¹ × 10^0.3","= 10 × **2.0**","[I] ≈ **20 μM**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1.3 μM reads the exponent as a concentration. 10 μM and 100 μM leave 67% and 17%, not 50%.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":1,"text":"10 μM: 67% left","at":"left"},{"series":"Activity","x":2,"text":"100 μM: 17% left","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":1.3,"text":"1.3 is a power, not μM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a log x axis, read the x value, then raise 10 to it (10^x). Half-maximal = 50% of the top plateau; read across, then down."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Ligand binding was measured for a tetrameric protein T and for a monomeric protein M, as shown; both are half-saturated at the same ligand concentration. Binding to T is best categorized as:",idStem:"A Hill coefficient of 2.5 means binding is:",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation versus ligand concentration","x":{"label":"[L] (μM)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"M, monomer","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,0.167],[5,0.333],[10,0.5],[15,0.6],[20,0.667],[30,0.75],[40,0.8],[50,0.833]]},{"name":"T, tetramer","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,0.018],[5,0.15],[10,0.5],[15,0.734],[20,0.85],[30,0.94],[40,0.97],[50,0.982]]}]},choices:["negatively cooperative, since T binds less ligand at low [L]","noncooperative, since both proteins reach full saturation","positively cooperative, since its curve is sigmoidal","positively cooperative, since T binds more tightly at every [L]"],ans:2,exp:"T's curve is **sigmoidal**: flat at low [L], then steep around half saturation. That shape means binding of the first ligand **raises the affinity** of the remaining sites: **positive cooperativity**. M's hyperbola is the noncooperative reference. Both are half-saturated at 10 μM, but T climbs from about 0.02 at 2 μM to 0.85 at 20 μM, far more steeply than M.",src:"pe5-cp:40",how:{"ask":"Compare the two curves' shapes. Which one is S-shaped, and what does a slow start followed by a steep rise say about how binding at one site affects the others?","know":"• **Noncooperative** binding (one site, or independent sites) gives a **hyperbola**: Y = [L]/(Kd + [L]), steepest at the start.\n• **Positive cooperativity:** binding at one site makes the next site bind more tightly. The curve is **sigmoidal** (S-shaped): low binding at first, then a steep rise. Hill coefficient n > 1. Example: hemoglobin.\n• **Negative cooperativity:** later sites bind more weakly; the curve is flatter than a hyperbola, n < 1.\n• Every curve reaches full saturation at high [L], so that says nothing about cooperativity. The shape in the middle does.","steps":[{"text":"Several sites make cooperativity possible. Compare T's curve with the monomer's.","hl":"a tetrameric protein T","graph":{"focus":["M, monomer","T, tetramer"],"labels":[{"series":"M, monomer","x":5,"text":"M: one site","at":"above"},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":15,"text":"T: four sites","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"M rises steeply at first and then levels off: a hyperbola, the noncooperative shape.","graph":{"focus":["M, monomer"],"read":[{"series":"M, monomer","x":2},{"series":"M, monomer","x":20}],"labels":[{"series":"M, monomer","x":2,"text":"0.17 already","at":"above"},{"series":"M, monomer","x":20,"text":"0.67, leveling off","at":"above"}]},"calc":["M at 2 μM: **0.17**","M at 10 μM: **0.50**","M at 20 μM: **0.67**"]},{"text":"T starts flat and then climbs steeply around 10 μM: an S-shaped curve.","graph":{"focus":["T, tetramer"],"read":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":2},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":20}],"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":2,"text":"0.02: flat start","at":"below"},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":20,"text":"0.85 after steep climb","at":"right"}]},"calc":["T at 2 μM: **0.02**","T at 10 μM: **0.50**","T at 20 μM: **0.85**"]},{"text":"Every curve saturates eventually, so that says nothing. And T binds less than M below 10 μM, so it is not tighter everywhere.","strike":["noncooperative, since both proteins reach full saturation","positively cooperative, since T binds more tightly at every [L]"],"graph":{"focus":["M, monomer","T, tetramer"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":10,"text":"T below M here"}]}},{"text":"A weak start followed by a steep rise means later ligands bind more tightly: the opposite of negative cooperativity.","strike":["negatively cooperative, since T binds less ligand at low [L]"],"graph":{"focus":["T, tetramer"],"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":5,"text":"weak start","at":"right"},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":15,"text":"steep rise","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: positively cooperative. Rule: hyperbola = independent sites; sigmoid = positive cooperativity."}],"trap":"At low [L], T binds less than M, which looks like weaker, negative cooperativity. That slow start is part of the S shape: the first ligand binds weakly, and later ones bind more tightly."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = fraction of binding sites filled.** 0 = nothing bound; 1 = every site full.","marks":[{"t":"Ligand binding","note":"y axis: sites filled"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**A ligand** is a small molecule that sticks to a protein (like O₂ on hemoglobin). **Left to right = more ligand** in the solution.","marks":[{"t":"ligand concentration","note":"x axis: how much ligand"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two curves, two proteins.** A monomer is one chain with one site; a tetramer is four chains, so four sites.","marks":[{"t":"tetrameric protein T","note":"4 chains = 4 sites"},{"t":"monomeric protein M","note":"1 chain = 1 site"}],"graph":{"focus":["M, monomer","T, tetramer"],"labels":[{"series":"M, monomer","x":5,"text":"M: 1 site","at":"above"},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":20,"text":"T: 4 sites","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Half-saturated = half the sites full (0.5).** Both reach 0.5 at 10 μM, so the middle can't tell them apart. The **shape** can.","marks":[{"t":"half-saturated at the same ligand concentration","note":"both 0.5 at 10 μM"}],"graph":{"yLine":0.5,"read":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":10,"text":"both 0.5 here","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**M rises fastest at the very start, then bends over.** That shape is a **hyperbola**: each site binds on its own (noncooperative).","graph":{"focus":["M, monomer"],"labels":[{"series":"M, monomer","x":2,"text":"fast start","at":"left"},{"series":"M, monomer","x":40,"text":"bends over","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**T starts flat, then shoots up, then levels off:** an **S-shape (sigmoid)**.","graph":{"focus":["T, tetramer"],"shade":[{"x1":5,"x2":15,"text":"T climbs steeply"}],"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":2,"text":"flat start: 0.02","at":"below"},{"series":"T, tetramer","x":20,"text":"0.85 already","at":"above"}]},"calc":["T: 0.15 at 5 μM → 0.73 at 15 μM","M: 0.33 at 5 μM → 0.60 at 15 μM"]},{"text":"**Cooperativity:** once one site binds ligand, the other sites grab ligand **more easily**. That is **positive** cooperativity, and it makes the S-shape.","marks":[{"t":"Binding to T","note":"S-shape → cooperative"}],"graph":{"focus":["T, tetramer"],"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":15,"text":"one bound → next easier","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** T binding less at low [L] is just the flat start of an S, not negative cooperativity. And T is NOT tighter at every [L]: below 10 μM it is lower than M.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"T, tetramer","x":5,"text":"T below M here","at":"below"},{"series":"M, monomer","x":5,"text":"M higher at low [L]","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** hyperbola = noncooperative; sigmoid (S-shaped) = positive cooperativity. Reaching full saturation says nothing about cooperativity; the shape does."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"In a plant, enzyme E1 converts A to B, enzyme E2 converts B to C, and enzyme E3 converts C to D. Compound C is the desired flavor, and D is an irritant. To accumulate C while eliminating D, which enzyme should be knocked out?",idStem:"A pathway makes a flavor compound, then converts it into an irritant. To keep the flavor but stop the irritant, which step should be blocked?",choices:["E1, since it catalyzes the committed step","E1 and E3, since both control flux to D","E3, since it acts downstream of C","E2, since it produces C"],ans:2,exp:"To keep a product but block what it becomes, **block the step after it**. Knocking out E3 stops C → D, so C builds up.",src:"pe5-cp:41",how:{"ask":"Where in the pathway must the block sit so that C is still made but not consumed?","know":"In a linear pathway A → B → C → D, blocking an enzyme causes the compound **before** the block to build up and everything **after** it to disappear.","steps":[{"text":"Write the pathway and the goal.","hl":"enzyme E1 converts A to B, enzyme E2 converts B to C, and enzyme E3 converts C to D","calc":["A —E1→ B —E2→ **C** —E3→ D","goal: keep **C**, lose **D**"]},{"text":"Knocking out E1 stops everything after A: no B, C or D. Losing D is good, but losing C fails the goal.","strike":["E1, since it catalyzes the committed step","E1 and E3, since both control flux to D"]},{"text":"Knocking out E2 stops C itself from being made.","strike":["E2, since it produces C"]},{"text":"Block the step right after the desired product. C is still made and has nowhere to go, so it builds up while D disappears. Answer: E3.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Expected levels after each knockout, illustrative","x":{"label":"Knockout"},"y":{"label":"Level ÷ normal","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["None","E1","E2","E3"],"series":[{"name":"C","values":[1,0,0,3]},{"name":"D","values":[1,0,0,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["E3","C"],["E3","D"]]}}],"trap":"Blocking E1 does remove D, but it removes C too, because everything downstream of A stops."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the pathway in the question.** Each enzyme does one step, like stations on an assembly line.","marks":[{"t":"E1 converts A to B","note":"station 1: A → B"},{"t":"E2 converts B to C","note":"station 2: B → C"},{"t":"E3 converts C to D","note":"station 3: C → D"}]},{"text":"**Enzyme** = a protein that does one chemical step. **Knocked out** = its gene deleted, so that step stops.","marks":[{"t":"knocked out","note":"enzyme gone: step stops"}],"calc":["A —E1→ B —E2→ C —E3→ D"]},{"text":"**The goal:** keep C, get rid of D.","marks":[{"t":"desired flavor","note":"C: keep it"},{"t":"irritant","note":"D: remove it"},{"t":"accumulate C","note":"C should pile up"}]},{"text":"**Blocking rule:** a block is a dam. The compound right **before** the dam piles up; everything **after** it disappears.","calc":["block a step → the compound before it **builds up**","everything after it → **gone**"]},{"text":"**Try knocking out E1:** no B is made, so no C and no D. D is gone, but so is C. Fails.","marks":[{"t":"enzyme E1","note":"knock out: no B, C or D"}]},{"text":"**Try knocking out E2:** C itself is never made. Fails.","marks":[{"t":"enzyme E2","note":"knock out: no C made"}]},{"text":"**Knock out E3:** C is still made but can't turn into D, so C piles up and D vanishes.","marks":[{"t":"enzyme E3","note":"knock out: C piles up"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Amounts in the plant (illustration)","x":{"label":"Compound"},"y":{"label":"Amount (relative)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["A","B","C","D"],"series":[{"name":"Normal","values":[2,2,2,6]},{"name":"E3 knocked out","values":[2,2,8,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C","E3 knocked out"],["D","E3 knocked out"]]}},{"text":"**Trap:** knocking out E1 too adds nothing useful: it would also wipe out C.","marks":[{"t":"eliminating D","note":"only E3 needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to build up a compound in a chain, block the enzyme right after it. To remove a compound, block any step before it."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A crystal structure shows a ligand's phosphate group (charge −2 at pH 7) within 3 Å of one residue's side chain, forming a salt bridge, and the ligand's aromatic ring stacked parallel at 3.5 Å to a second residue's side chain. Which pair of residues is most consistent with these contacts?",idStem:"A negatively charged phosphate is held by a salt bridge, and its aromatic ring by π-stacking. Which pair of residues fits?",choices:["Arg and Tyr","Lys and Ser","Glu and Phe","Asp and Tyr"],ans:0,exp:"A salt bridge to a **negative** phosphate needs a **positive** side chain (Arg, Lys). π-stacking needs an **aromatic** ring (Phe, Tyr, Trp). **Arg + Tyr** does both.",src:"pe5-cp:42",how:{"ask":"What charge must the first residue have, and what kind of ring must the second have?","know":"**Salt bridge** = attraction between opposite charges. At pH 7: Asp, Glu are −; Lys, Arg are +. **π-stacking** = parallel stacking of aromatic rings (Phe, Tyr, Trp; His sometimes). Ser is polar but neither charged nor aromatic.","steps":[{"text":"A salt bridge pairs opposite charges. A −2 phosphate needs a positive side chain: Lys or Arg.","hl":"phosphate group (charge −2 at pH 7)"},{"text":"Glu and Asp are negative at pH 7, so they would repel the phosphate.","fig":"aspartate-sc","strike":["Glu and Phe","Asp and Tyr"]},{"text":"Parallel stacking needs an aromatic ring: Phe, Tyr or Trp. Ser has none.","hl":"stacked parallel at 3.5 Å","strike":["Lys and Ser"]},{"text":"Arg's guanidinium (+1) bridges the phosphate and Tyr's phenol ring stacks with the ligand. Answer: Arg and Tyr.","fig":["arginine-sc","tyrosine-sc"]}],"trap":"\"Asp and Tyr\" has the right aromatic partner, but Asp is negative and would repel the phosphate."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the two contacts the question describes.** The ligand (a small molecule) touches two amino acids in the protein.","marks":[{"t":"phosphate group (charge −2 at pH 7)","note":"negative part"},{"t":"aromatic ring","note":"flat ring part"},{"t":"Which pair of residues","note":"one amino acid each"}]},{"text":"**Residue** = one amino acid in the protein chain. **Side chain** = the part that sticks out and differs between amino acids.","marks":[{"t":"one residue's side chain","note":"amino acid #1"},{"t":"second residue's side chain","note":"amino acid #2"}]},{"text":"**Salt bridge** = a + charge and a − charge attracting each other. The phosphate is −2, so partner #1 must be **positive**.","marks":[{"t":"forming a salt bridge","note":"+ and − attract"},{"t":"(charge −2 at pH 7)","note":"needs a + partner"}]},{"text":"**Side-chain charges at pH 7:** Arg and Lys are +; Asp and Glu are −; the rest are neutral.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain charge at pH 7","x":{"label":"Amino acid"},"y":{"label":"Charge","min":-1,"max":1,"step":0.5},"categories":["Arg","Lys","Asp","Glu","Ser","Tyr","Phe"],"series":[{"name":"Charge","values":[1,1,-1,-1,0,0,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Arg","Charge"],["Lys","Charge"]]}},{"text":"**Glu and Asp are negative**, so they would push the −2 phosphate away. Pairs starting with them fail.","marks":[{"t":"(charge −2 at pH 7)","note":"Asp/Glu −: repel"}]},{"text":"**π-stacking** = two flat rings stacked like coins about 3.5 Å apart. Partner #2 needs its own flat (aromatic) ring.","marks":[{"t":"stacked parallel at 3.5 Å","note":"rings stacked like coins"},{"t":"aromatic ring","note":"needs a ring partner"}]},{"text":"**Ring side chains:** Phe, Tyr, Trp. Ser has only an –OH, no ring, so it can't stack.","marks":[{"t":"second residue's side chain","note":"Phe, Tyr or Trp"}],"calc":["aromatic: Phe, **Tyr**, Trp","not aromatic: Ser, Lys, Arg"]},{"text":"**Put both tests together.**","marks":[{"t":"salt bridge","note":"Arg (+) ✓"},{"t":"stacked parallel","note":"Tyr (ring) ✓"}],"calc":["Arg: + charge → salt bridge ✓","Tyr: aromatic ring → stacking ✓","Lys + Ser: Ser has no ring ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** salt bridge to a negative group → Lys or Arg; to a positive group → Asp or Glu. Parallel ring stacking → Phe, Tyr or Trp."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"On the free-energy diagram shown, the left end of the curve is the reactants, the right end is the products, and the peak is the transition state. Reactions with activation energies below about 40 kJ/mol proceed quickly at 298 K. The forward reaction is best described as:",idStem:"A reaction is described as both fast and spontaneous. Which pair fits?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Free energy along the reaction coordinate","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":80,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Reaction","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,33],[3,42],[4,54],[5,60],[6,56],[7,50],[8,46],[9,45],[10,45]]}]},choices:["slow and spontaneous, since Ea is high and ΔG < 0","fast and spontaneous, since Ea is low and ΔG < 0","fast and nonspontaneous, since Ea is low and ΔG > 0","slow and nonspontaneous, since Ea is high and ΔG > 0"],ans:2,exp:"Read three heights: reactants **30**, peak **60**, products **45 kJ/mol**. **Ea = 60 − 30 = 30 kJ/mol**, below 40, so the reaction is fast. **ΔG = 45 − 30 = +15 kJ/mol**, so it is nonspontaneous: the products lie above the reactants.",src:"pe5-cp:44",how:{"ask":"Read the heights of the start, the peak and the end. Which differences give Ea and ΔG, and what are their signs?","know":"• On a reaction-coordinate diagram, **Ea (forward) = transition state − reactants**. It sets the rate: smaller Ea, faster reaction.\n• **ΔG = products − reactants**. It sets spontaneity: ΔG < 0 spontaneous (downhill); ΔG > 0 nonspontaneous (uphill).\n• Rate and spontaneity are independent: a reaction can be fast and nonspontaneous (it quickly reaches an equilibrium with little product) or slow and spontaneous.\n• Always subtract from the reactants' level, never from zero.","steps":[{"text":"Read the three key heights: the start, the peak and the end.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0},{"series":"Reaction","x":5},{"series":"Reaction","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0,"text":"30","at":"below"},{"series":"Reaction","x":5,"text":"60","at":"above"},{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"45","at":"below"}]},"calc":["reactants: **30 kJ/mol**","transition state: **60 kJ/mol**","products: **45 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Ea is the climb from the reactants to the peak. Compare it with the cutoff.","hl":"below about 40 kJ/mol proceed quickly","graph":{"yLine":30,"spans":[{"x":5,"from":30,"to":60,"text":"Ea = 30","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea = 60 − 30 = **30 kJ/mol**","30 < 40 → **fast**"]},{"text":"Ea is low, so drop both slow choices.","strike":["slow and spontaneous, since Ea is high and ΔG < 0","slow and nonspontaneous, since Ea is high and ΔG > 0"]},{"text":"The products end above the reactants: uphill overall.","calc":["ΔG = 45 − 30 = **+15 kJ/mol**","ΔG > 0 → **nonspontaneous**"],"strike":["fast and spontaneous, since Ea is low and ΔG < 0"],"graph":{"yLine":30,"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":30,"to":45,"text":"ΔG = +15","side":"right"}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products"}]}},{"text":"Answer: fast and nonspontaneous. Rule: Ea = peak − reactants decides speed; ΔG = products − reactants decides direction."}],"trap":"Reading Ea as the height of the peak itself, 60 kJ/mol, makes the reaction look slow. Ea is measured from the reactants' level, not from zero."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = free energy** of the molecules. Higher = more energy at that moment.","marks":[{"t":"free-energy diagram","note":"y axis: energy"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = the reaction happening.** Left edge = before (reactants); right edge = after (products).","marks":[{"t":"the left end of the curve is the reactants","note":"start"},{"t":"the right end is the products","note":"finish"}],"graph":{"axis":"x","shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products"}]}},{"text":"**Read the three key heights:** start, top of the hill, end.","marks":[{"t":"the peak is the transition state","note":"top of the hill"}],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0},{"series":"Reaction","x":5},{"series":"Reaction","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0,"text":"reactants: 30","at":"below"},{"series":"Reaction","x":5,"text":"transition state: 60","at":"above"},{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"products: 45","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Activation energy (Ea)** = how tall the hill is, measured from the **reactants**, not from 0. It sets the speed: lower hill = faster.","marks":[{"t":"activation energies","note":"height of the hill"}],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":30,"to":60,"text":"Ea = 30","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea = peak − reactants","Ea = 60 − 30 = **30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Compare with the cutoff:** 30 is below 40, so the reaction is **fast**.","marks":[{"t":"below about 40 kJ/mol","note":"fast if Ea is below this"}],"calc":["30 kJ/mol < 40 kJ/mol → **fast**"]},{"text":"**ΔG = products − reactants.** It says whether the reaction runs on its own (**spontaneous**, downhill, ΔG < 0) or not (uphill, ΔG > 0).","graph":{"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":30,"to":45,"text":"ΔG = +15","side":"right"}]},"calc":["ΔG = 45 − 30 = **+15 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Products end higher than the reactants: uphill.** So the forward reaction is **nonspontaneous**.","marks":[{"t":"The forward reaction","note":"left → right"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"ends higher = uphill","at":"above"},{"series":"Reaction","x":0,"text":"start","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** speed and spontaneity are separate. The hill height (Ea) sets speed; the start-to-end difference (ΔG) sets spontaneity. A fast reaction can still be uphill.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":30,"to":60,"text":"Ea → speed","side":"left"},{"x":9.5,"from":30,"to":45,"text":"ΔG → direction","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − reactants (small Ea = fast). ΔG = products − reactants (negative = spontaneous). Always measure from the reactant level, never from zero."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The compound shown is dissolved at pH 1, where each labeled group carries its acidic proton, and is then titrated with NaOH. Which labeled group loses its proton first?",idStem:"As pH rises, which group on a chlorinated amino acid loses its proton first?",fig:"acid-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"The group with the **lowest pKa** loses its proton first. Both –COOH groups are far more acidic than the –NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.3) or the alcohol O–H (≈ 16). Of the two, the one on the **CF₂ carbon (II)** is stronger: the electronegative F atoms pull electron density away and **stabilize the carboxylate**, lowering the pKa from about 4.8 to about 1.3.",src:"pe5-cp:45",how:{"ask":"Rank the labeled groups by pKa. Which acid has electronegative atoms on the carbon right next to it, and what do they do to its anion?","know":"• During a titration with base, protons come off in order of **increasing pKa**: the strongest acid first.\n• Typical pKa values: R–COOH ≈ 4–5; R–CH₂NH₃⁺ ≈ 9–10.5; alcohol O–H ≈ 16.\n• **Inductive effect:** electronegative atoms (F, Cl, O) on nearby carbons pull electron density through the bonds, spreading out the negative charge of the conjugate base and making the acid stronger. The effect fades quickly with distance: large on the adjacent carbon, small three or four bonds away.\n• Example: acetic acid pKa 4.8; difluoroacetic acid pKa 1.3.","steps":[{"text":"First off = strongest acid = lowest pKa. Rank the four labeled groups.","hl":"loses its proton first"},{"text":"Identify each labeled group and recall its typical pKa.","fig":"acid-sites","calc":["I, –CH₂NH₃⁺: pKa ≈ **9.3**","II, –CF₂COOH: pKa ≈ **1.3**","III, –CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH: pKa ≈ **4.8**","IV, –CH₂OH: pKa ≈ **16**"]},{"text":"The ammonium and the alcohol are far weaker acids than either –COOH.","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"The highlighted F atoms sit on the carbon next to carboxyl II. They pull electron density away and stabilize its anion, so II is the stronger acid; III has no nearby electronegative atoms.","fig":"acid-sites-f","strike":["III"]},{"text":"Answer: II. Rule: electronegative atoms one carbon away from an acid lower its pKa; the effect fades with each added carbon."}],"trap":"I, the –NH₃⁺, carries a positive charge, so it looks eager to lose H⁺, but its pKa is about 9.3: it gives up its proton only after both carboxylic acids."},teach:[{"text":"**Corners are carbons with hidden H**. F fluorine, O oxygen, N nitrogen. NH₃⁺ is N with an extra H, giving a + charge. I–IV each hold an H⁺.","fig":"acid-sites"},{"text":"**NaOH supplies OH⁻, which pulls H⁺ off one group at a time**. pKa measures grip: lowest pKa = loosest grip = loses H⁺ first.","fig":"t5-acid-q"},{"text":"**Site IV is an alcohol O–H**. After losing H⁺ its − charge sits on one O with no help, so it holds on tightly.","fig":"t5-acid-iv"},{"text":"**Site I, the –NH₃⁺, lets go of its H⁺ only at fairly high pH**.","fig":"t5-acid-i"},{"text":"**Sites II and III are COOH groups (carboxylic acids), pKa about 4–5**. After losing H⁺, the − charge is shared by two O atoms, which steadies it.","fig":"t5-acid-cooh"},{"text":"**Site III's COOH sits at the end of a plain carbon chain: a typical carboxylic acid**.","fig":"t5-acid-iii"},{"text":"**F pulls electrons harder than any atom**. Two F atoms draw electrons away and spread out the − charge, so site II's H⁺ leaves most easily.","fig":"t5-acid-ii-f"},{"text":"**Rule to remember**: lowest pKa loses H⁺ first. Order: COOH near F/Cl/O < plain COOH < NH₃⁺ < alcohol O–H. At each label ask: what group, and what pulls on it?","fig":"acid-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"The energy diagram shows the same reaction with and without a catalyst. What are the forward activation energy of the catalyzed reaction and the enthalpy change of the reaction?",idStem:"On an energy diagram, reactants sit at E2, products lower at E1, and the peak at E3. What is the forward activation energy?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Enthalpy along the reaction coordinate, with and without catalyst","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Enthalpy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":140,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,60],[1,60],[2,66],[3,85],[4,112],[5,130],[6,110],[7,70],[8,38],[9,23],[10,20]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,60],[1,60],[2,63],[3,72],[4,86],[5,95],[6,85],[7,58],[8,34],[9,22],[10,20]]}]},choices:["70 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol","35 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol","35 kJ/mol and +40 kJ/mol","95 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol"],ans:1,exp:"Catalyzed **Ea = 95 − 60 = 35 kJ/mol**, measured from the reactants up to the lower peak. **ΔH = 20 − 60 = −40 kJ/mol**, the same with or without the catalyst, because a catalyst changes only the path, not the starting or ending energies.",src:"pe5-cp:46",how:{"ask":"Read the reactants, the products and the catalyzed peak. Which two numbers give Ea, and which two give ΔH?","know":"• **Ea (forward) = peak − reactants.** A catalyst lowers the peak, so it lowers Ea, and it lowers the reverse Ea by the same amount.\n• **ΔH = products − reactants.** Negative = exothermic (ends lower). A catalyst does not change the reactants' or products' energies, so it changes neither ΔH nor K.\n• Both are differences: never read an energy straight off the y-axis as Ea.","steps":[{"text":"Read the levels on the catalyzed curve.","graph":{"focus":["Catalyzed"],"read":[{"series":"Catalyzed","x":0},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":5},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Catalyzed","x":0,"text":"60","at":"below"},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":5,"text":"95","at":"above"},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":10,"text":"20","at":"above"}]},"calc":["reactants: **60 kJ/mol**","catalyzed peak: **95 kJ/mol**","products: **20 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Ea is the climb from the reactants to the lower peak.","hl":"forward activation energy of the catalyzed reaction","graph":{"focus":["Catalyzed"],"yLine":60,"spans":[{"x":5,"from":60,"to":95,"text":"Ea = 35","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea, catalyzed = 95 − 60 = **35 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"70 is the uncatalyzed climb, 130 − 60; 95 is the catalyzed peak read from zero instead of from the reactants.","graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","Catalyzed"],"read":[{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":5}],"spans":[{"x":5.4,"from":60,"to":130,"text":"130 − 60 = 70 (uncatalyzed)","side":"right"},{"x":4.6,"from":0,"to":95,"text":"95 read from 0","side":"left"}]},"strike":["70 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol","95 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"The products end below the reactants: exothermic, so ΔH is negative.","calc":["ΔH = 20 − 60 = **−40 kJ/mol**","same for both curves: the catalyst moves only the peak"],"strike":["35 kJ/mol and +40 kJ/mol"],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","Catalyzed"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products"}],"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":60,"to":20,"text":"ΔH = −40","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: 35 kJ/mol and −40 kJ/mol. Rule: Ea = peak − reactants; ΔH = products − reactants, untouched by a catalyst."}],"trap":"95 kJ/mol is the catalyzed peak read from zero; Ea is the climb from the reactants at 60. And 70 kJ/mol is the uncatalyzed climb, taken from the higher curve."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = enthalpy**, the heat energy stored in the molecules. Higher = more energy.","marks":[{"t":"energy diagram","note":"y axis: enthalpy"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = the reaction happening**, from reactants (left) to products (right).","graph":{"axis":"x","shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products"}]}},{"text":"**A catalyst** is a helper that gives the reaction an easier path (a lower hill) and comes out unchanged. Two curves: same reaction, two paths.","marks":[{"t":"with and without a catalyst","note":"two curves, two paths"}],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","Catalyzed"],"labels":[{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":4,"text":"no catalyst: tall hill","at":"left"},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":6,"text":"catalyst: lower hill","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Read the levels on the catalyzed curve:** start, peak, end.","graph":{"focus":["Catalyzed"],"read":[{"series":"Catalyzed","x":0},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":5},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Catalyzed","x":0,"text":"reactants: 60","at":"below"},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":5,"text":"peak: 95","at":"above"},{"series":"Catalyzed","x":10,"text":"products: 20","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Activation energy (Ea)** = hill height measured from the reactants up to the peak. Use the catalyzed (lower) peak.","marks":[{"t":"forward activation energy of the catalyzed reaction","note":"lower peak − reactants"}],"graph":{"focus":["Catalyzed"],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":60,"to":95,"text":"Ea = 35","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea = 95 − 60 = **35 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 95 is the peak's height from 0, not the climb. 70 is the climb on the uncatalyzed hill (130 − 60).","graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":60,"to":130,"text":"no catalyst: 70","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"series":"Catalyzed","x":5,"text":"95 = height, not Ea","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**ΔH (enthalpy change)** = products − reactants. Ending lower means heat was released: **negative** ΔH (exothermic).","marks":[{"t":"enthalpy change of the reaction","note":"products − reactants"}],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":60,"to":20,"text":"ΔH = −40","side":"left"}]},"calc":["ΔH = 20 − 60 = **−40 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**A catalyst never moves the start or the end**, so ΔH is the same on both curves. +40 has the sign backward: the products sit lower.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":0,"text":"same start: 60","at":"above"},{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":10,"text":"same end: 20","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − reactants (a catalyst lowers it). ΔH = products − reactants (a catalyst never changes it). Measure from the reactant level, not from 0."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A 2.0 kg block and a 5.0 kg block hang from opposite ends of a light string passing over a massless, frictionless pulley. Using g = 9.8 m/s², what is the magnitude of the blocks' acceleration after they are released from rest?",idStem:"A 2 kg mass and a 5 kg mass hang on either side of a frictionless pulley. What is their acceleration once released?",choices:["4.2 m/s²","2.8 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","5.9 m/s²"],ans:0,exp:"Net force = **weight difference** = (5 − 2)g; total mass moved = 7 kg. **a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂)** = 3g/7 ≈ **4.2 m/s²**.",src:"pe5-cp:47",how:{"ask":"What net force drives the system, and how much total mass does it have to accelerate?","know":"**Newton's 2nd law: F_net = ma.** For two masses on a pulley (an Atwood machine), treat them as one system: the heavier weight pulls one way, the lighter weight the other; tension is internal. Both blocks move together.","steps":[{"text":"Treat both blocks and the string as one system: the heavy side falls, the light side rises, and both have the same |a|.","hl":"hang from opposite ends of a light string passing over a massless, frictionless pulley"},{"text":"The net external force is the difference of the weights; tension is internal and cancels.","calc":["F_net = m_heavy·g − m_light·g","= (5.0 − 2.0) × 9.8","= **29.4 N**"]},{"text":"Divide by the total mass that accelerates.","calc":["m_total = 5.0 + 2.0 = **7.0 kg**","a = 29.4 / 7.0","a = **4.2 m/s²**"]},{"text":"The blocks gain speed more slowly than free fall, because the light block's weight opposes the motion.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed after release","x":{"label":"t (s)","min":0,"max":3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Pulley blocks, a = 4.2 m/s²","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,4.2],[2,8.4],[3,12.6]]},{"name":"Free fall, a = 9.8 m/s²","marker":"triangle-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,9.8],[2,19.6],[3,29.4]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Pulley blocks, a = 4.2 m/s²","x":1},{"series":"Free fall, a = 9.8 m/s²","x":1}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":4.2,"text":"+4.2 m/s each second","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"series":"Free fall, a = 9.8 m/s²","x":2,"text":"free fall: 19.6 m/s","at":"left"},{"series":"Pulley blocks, a = 4.2 m/s²","x":3,"text":"pulley: 12.6 m/s","at":"below"}]},"strike":["9.8 m/s²"]},{"text":"5.9 m/s² divides by only 5 kg; 2.8 m/s² is 9.8 × 2/7. Answer: 4.2 m/s².","strike":["2.8 m/s²","5.9 m/s²"]}],"trap":"5.9 m/s² divides by only the heavy block's 5 kg. Both blocks accelerate, so divide by 7 kg."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"2.0 kg","note":"light block"},{"t":"5.0 kg","note":"heavy block"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity's pull per kg"}]},{"text":"**Picture it:** one string over a wheel, a block on each end. The heavy side falls, the light side rises, both at the same rate.","marks":[{"t":"opposite ends of a light string","note":"blocks tied together"},{"t":"massless, frictionless pulley","note":"only turns the string"}]},{"text":"**Weight** = gravity's pull on a block = mass × g.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 kg","note":"weight 49 N"},{"t":"2.0 kg","note":"weight 19.6 N"}],"calc":["W = m × g","heavy: 5.0 × 9.8 = **49 N**","light: 2.0 × 9.8 = **19.6 N**"]},{"text":"**Net force = the difference.** The two weights pull opposite ways around the pulley. String tension is inside the system and cancels.","calc":["F_net = 49 − 19.6","F_net = **29.4 N**"]},{"text":"**That net force has to move BOTH blocks**, so use the total mass.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 kg","note":"part of total mass"},{"t":"5.0 kg","note":"part of total mass"}],"calc":["m_total = 5.0 + 2.0 = **7.0 kg**"]},{"text":"**Acceleration** = how fast the speed grows. Newton's 2nd law: a = F_net ÷ m.","marks":[{"t":"acceleration","note":"a = F_net ÷ m_total"}],"calc":["a = 29.4 N ÷ 7.0 kg","a = **4.2 m/s²**"]},{"text":"**Sanity check:** the blocks speed up slower than a dropped block, because the light side holds them back.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed after release","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Free fall","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.5,4.9],[1,9.8]]},{"name":"Pulley blocks","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.5,2.1],[1,4.2]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Free fall","x":1,"text":"free fall: 9.8","at":"left"},{"series":"Pulley blocks","x":1,"text":"blocks: 4.2","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 9.8 m/s² is free fall (no light block). 5.9 m/s² divides by only 5 kg; 2.8 m/s² uses the light weight instead of the difference.","marks":[{"t":"released from rest","note":"both start together"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulley with two hanging masses → a = (heavy − light) × g ÷ (heavy + light). Pull = difference of weights; mass moved = sum."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"The heating curve shown was recorded for 5.0 g of water, starting as ice at 0 °C, while heat was added steadily. Which step requires the greatest heat?",idStem:"Turning ice at 0 °C into steam at 100 °C, which step takes the most heat?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 5.0 g of water","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":16,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1.67,0],[3.76,100],[15.06,100],[15.26,120]]}]},choices:["Melting the ice at 0 °C, about 1.7 kJ","Warming the liquid from 0 to 100 °C, about 2.1 kJ","Vaporizing the water at 100 °C, about 11 kJ","Warming the steam above 100 °C, about 15 kJ"],ans:2,exp:"On a heating curve, **the heat for a step is the horizontal width of its segment**. The 100 °C plateau runs from 3.76 to 15.06 kJ: **11.3 kJ**, far wider than melting (1.67 kJ) or warming the liquid (2.09 kJ). Check: 5.0 g × 2260 J/g = 11.3 kJ.",src:"pe5-cp:48",how:{"ask":"Each segment's width along the x-axis is the heat it absorbed. Which segment is widest?","know":"• A **heating curve** plots temperature against heat added. **Sloped** segments: one phase warms (q = m·c·ΔT). **Flat** segments: a phase change at constant temperature (q = m·ΔH).\n• The heat for any step is its **width along the x-axis**: end value minus start value, not the end value alone.\n• For water, ΔH_vap (2260 J/g) is about 7 times ΔH_fus (334 J/g), because boiling must pull the molecules completely apart, breaking nearly all of their hydrogen bonds.","steps":[{"text":"Heat is on the x-axis, so the heat for a step is the width of its segment.","hl":"Which step requires the greatest heat?","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"Read where each of the first two segments starts and ends.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Water","x":1.67},{"series":"Water","x":3.76}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1.67,"text":"melting: 1.67 kJ"},{"x1":1.67,"x2":3.76,"text":"liquid: 2.09 kJ"}]},"calc":["melting plateau: 1.67 − 0 = **1.67 kJ**","liquid warming: 3.76 − 1.67 = **2.09 kJ**"]},{"text":"The 100 °C plateau is by far the widest.","graph":{"yLine":100,"read":[{"series":"Water","x":15.06}],"shade":[{"x1":3.76,"x2":15.06,"text":"boiling: 11.3 kJ"}]},"calc":["boiling plateau: 15.06 − 3.76 = **11.3 kJ**","check: 5.0 g × 2260 J/g = **11 300 J**"]},{"text":"Melting and warming the liquid are short. The steam segment is tiny; 15 kJ is where it starts, not how wide it is.","calc":["steam warming: 15.26 − 15.06 = **0.20 kJ**"],"strike":["Melting the ice at 0 °C, about 1.7 kJ","Warming the liquid from 0 to 100 °C, about 2.1 kJ","Warming the steam above 100 °C, about 15 kJ"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":15.06,"x2":15.26,"text":"steam: 0.20 kJ"}],"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":15.06,"text":"15 kJ = where steam starts","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: vaporizing, about 11 kJ. Rule: on a heating curve, heat for a step = segment width; the boiling plateau is the widest."}],"trap":"About 15 kJ is the x-value where the steam segment starts, which is the total heat added so far, not the heat for that step. Read widths, not positions."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = temperature** of the water sample.","marks":[{"t":"heating curve","note":"temperature vs heat added"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = total heat added so far** (kJ). Moving right = more heat has gone in.","marks":[{"t":"heat was added steadily","note":"x axis: running total"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** The water starts as solid ice, right at its melting point.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 g of water","note":"mass"},{"t":"starting as ice at 0 °C","note":"solid, at melting point"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":0,"text":"ice at 0 °C","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Flat parts = phase changes.** Heat goes in but the temperature stays put: it is breaking the solid or liquid apart.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1.67,"text":"melting"},{"x1":3.76,"x2":15.06,"text":"boiling (100 °C)"}]}},{"text":"**Sloped parts = warming.** One phase gets hotter.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1.67,"x2":3.76,"text":"liquid warms"}],"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":3.76,"text":"liquid hits 100 °C","at":"left"},{"series":"Water","x":15.26,"text":"steam warms","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Heat for a step = how wide its segment is:** end − start on the x axis. Melting and warming the liquid:","marks":[{"t":"greatest heat","note":"widest segment"}],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Water","x":1.67},{"series":"Water","x":3.76}]},"calc":["melting: 1.67 − 0 = **1.67 kJ**","liquid warming: 3.76 − 1.67 = **2.09 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Boiling is by far the widest segment.**","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Water","x":3.76},{"series":"Water","x":15.06}],"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":15.06,"text":"boiling ends: 15.06","at":"below"}]},"calc":["boiling: 15.06 − 3.76 = **11.3 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Check with the heat of vaporization** (2260 J to boil 1 g of water):","marks":[{"t":"5.0 g of water","note":"× 2260 J/g"}],"calc":["q = m × ΔH_vap","q = 5.0 g × 2260 J/g = **11,300 J ≈ 11 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Trap:** the steam step starts at 15 on the x axis, but that is a position, not its size. Its width is only about 0.2 kJ.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":15.26,"text":"steam: only 0.2 kJ wide","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a heating curve, heat for a step = the width of its segment on the x axis. Flat = phase change (q = m·ΔH); sloped = warming (q = m·c·ΔT). Vaporizing usually wins."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"A 100 N force is applied perpendicular to the face of a rectangular paddle measuring 10 cm × 5.0 cm. What pressure does the force exert on the paddle face?",idStem:"A 100 N force pushes straight on a 10 cm × 5 cm paddle. What pressure does it apply?",choices:["2.0 kPa","200 kPa","20 kPa","2.0 Pa"],ans:2,exp:"**P = F/A**, with A in m². A = 0.10 m × 0.050 m = 0.0050 m²; P = 100 ÷ 0.0050 = 20,000 Pa = **20 kPa**.",src:"pe5-cp:49",how:{"ask":"Convert the paddle's dimensions to meters, find its area, then divide force by area.","know":"**Pressure P = F/A**, units N/m² = Pa. 1 kPa = 1000 Pa. **1 cm = 0.01 m**, so 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².","steps":[{"text":"A perpendicular force gives P = F/A directly, with no angle. Area must be in m² to get pascals.","hl":"perpendicular to the face"},{"text":"Convert the dimensions and find the area.","hl":"10 cm × 5.0 cm","calc":["10 cm = **0.10 m**, 5.0 cm = **0.050 m**","A = 0.10 × 0.050 = **0.0050 m²**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["P = F/A = 100 N / 0.0050 m²","= **20,000 Pa**","= **20 kPa**"]},{"text":"2.0 Pa treats 50 cm² as 50 m²; 2.0 kPa and 200 kPa are factor-of-10 slips. Answer: 20 kPa.","strike":["2.0 kPa","200 kPa","2.0 Pa"]}],"trap":"2.0 Pa comes from 100 N ÷ 50 cm² treated as if cm² were m². Convert to meters before dividing."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"100 N","note":"force (push)"},{"t":"10 cm × 5.0 cm","note":"paddle size"},{"t":"pressure","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**Pressure** = force spread over an area: P = F ÷ A. Its unit, the pascal (Pa), is 1 N on 1 m².","marks":[{"t":"pressure","note":"force ÷ area"}],"calc":["P = F / A","1 Pa = 1 N/m²"]},{"text":"**Perpendicular** = pushing straight on, at a right angle. The whole force presses the face, so no angle math.","marks":[{"t":"perpendicular to the face","note":"straight on: full force"}]},{"text":"**Pascals need meters**, so convert centimeters first.","marks":[{"t":"10 cm","note":"= 0.10 m"},{"t":"5.0 cm","note":"= 0.050 m"}],"calc":["1 cm = 0.01 m","10 cm = **0.10 m**","5.0 cm = **0.050 m**"]},{"text":"**Area of a rectangle** = length × width.","marks":[{"t":"rectangular paddle","note":"area = length × width"}],"calc":["A = 0.10 m × 0.050 m","A = **0.0050 m²**"]},{"text":"**Now divide force by area.**","marks":[{"t":"100 N","note":"÷ 0.0050 m²"}],"calc":["P = 100 N ÷ 0.0050 m²","P = **20,000 Pa**","1 kPa = 1000 Pa → **20 kPa**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 2.0 Pa treats 50 cm² as 50 m². 2.0 kPa and 200 kPa are slips by a factor of 10 in the cm → m step.","marks":[{"t":"10 cm × 5.0 cm","note":"50 cm², not 50 m²"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P = F ÷ A with A in m². Convert cm to m BEFORE multiplying (1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m²); 1 kPa = 1000 Pa."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A defibrillator's 25 μF capacitor is charged to 3000 V, storing about 110 J, and then discharges completely through a patient in 10 ms. What is the average current during the discharge?",idStem:"A 25 μF capacitor charged to 3000 V discharges completely in 10 ms. What is the average current?",choices:["7.5 A","0.75 A","75 A","7.5 mA"],ans:0,exp:"**Q = CV** = 25 × 10⁻⁶ F × 3000 V = 0.075 C. **I = Q/t** = 0.075 C ÷ 0.010 s = **7.5 A**. The energy value is not needed.",src:"pe5-cp:50",how:{"ask":"First find the charge that flows, then divide by the time it takes.","know":"**Capacitor: Q = CV** (Q in coulombs, C in farads, V in volts). **Current I = Q/t** (amperes = C/s). μ = 10⁻⁶; m = 10⁻³.","steps":[{"text":"Find the charge that flows: Q = CV.","hl":"25 μF capacitor is charged to 3000 V","calc":["Q = CV","Q = **25 × 10⁻⁶ F** × 3000 V","Q = **0.075 C**"]},{"text":"Average current is charge per unit time.","hl":"discharges completely through a patient in 10 ms","calc":["t = 10 ms = **0.010 s**","I_avg = Q/t = 0.075 / 0.010","I_avg = **7.5 A**"]},{"text":"The energy is a distractor; it simply checks out.","hl":"storing about 110 J","calc":["U = ½CV² = ½ × 25 × 10⁻⁶ × 3000²","≈ **112 J** ✓"]},{"text":"0.75 A and 75 A are factor-of-10 slips; 7.5 mA divides by 10 instead of 0.010 s. Answer: 7.5 A.","strike":["0.75 A","75 A","7.5 mA"]}],"trap":"7.5 mA comes from dividing by 10 instead of 0.010 s. Convert ms to s."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"25 μF","note":"capacitance"},{"t":"3000 V","note":"voltage"},{"t":"10 ms","note":"time to empty"}]},{"text":"**A capacitor** stores electric charge on two metal plates. Its **capacitance** (in farads, F) = how much charge it holds per volt.","marks":[{"t":"capacitor","note":"charge storage"}],"calc":["Q = C × V","charge = capacitance × voltage"]},{"text":"**Convert the prefix:** μ (micro) = one millionth.","marks":[{"t":"25 μF","note":"= 25 × 10⁻⁶ F"}],"calc":["μ = 10⁻⁶","25 μF = **25 × 10⁻⁶ F**"]},{"text":"**Charge stored:** Q = C × V.","marks":[{"t":"25 μF","note":"C"},{"t":"3000 V","note":"× V"}],"calc":["Q = 25 × 10⁻⁶ F × 3000 V","25 × 3000 = 75,000 → 75,000 × 10⁻⁶","Q = **0.075 C**"]},{"text":"**Current** = how much charge flows per second: I = Q ÷ t. All the charge leaves during the discharge.","marks":[{"t":"average current","note":"I = Q ÷ t"},{"t":"10 ms","note":"= 0.010 s"}],"calc":["m (milli) = 10⁻³","10 ms = **0.010 s**"]},{"text":"**Divide the charge by the time.**","marks":[{"t":"discharges completely","note":"all 0.075 C flows"}],"calc":["I = 0.075 C ÷ 0.010 s","I = **7.5 A**"]},{"text":"**The energy is a distractor.** You don't need it; it just checks out.","marks":[{"t":"storing about 110 J","note":"not needed"}],"calc":["U = ½CV² = ½ × 25 × 10⁻⁶ × 3000²","U ≈ **112 J** ✓"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 0.75 A and 75 A slip a power of 10; 7.5 mA uses 10 s instead of 10 ms.","marks":[{"t":"10 ms","note":"ms = 10⁻³ s, not 1 s"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** capacitor discharge current → Q = CV first, then I = Q ÷ t. Convert μ = 10⁻⁶ and m = 10⁻³ before dividing; ignore given numbers you don't need."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A parallel-plate capacitor is connected to a 9.0 V battery and remains connected. Which change would increase the charge stored on its plates?",idStem:"With the voltage fixed, which change increases the charge a parallel-plate capacitor stores?",choices:["Reducing the plate area, since the charge becomes more concentrated","Reducing the plate separation, since C = κε₀A/d increases","Removing the dielectric, since the field between the plates increases","Increasing the plate separation, since the voltage across the gap rises"],ans:1,exp:"With the battery attached, V is fixed, so **Q = CV rises only if C rises**. C = κε₀A/d: a **smaller separation d** increases C and therefore Q.",src:"pe5-cp:51",how:{"ask":"With the battery still connected, what stays fixed, and what must change for Q to grow?","know":"**Capacitance C = κε₀A/d**: κ = dielectric constant, ε₀ = permittivity of free space, A = plate area, d = gap. **Q = CV.** A connected battery holds **V constant**.","steps":[{"text":"A connected battery holds V at 9.0 V, so Q = CV changes only if C changes.","hl":"remains connected"},{"text":"Write C for a parallel-plate capacitor.","calc":["C = κε₀A/d","Q = CV = κε₀A·V / d","V fixed → Q ∝ κA/d"]},{"text":"Less area or removing the dielectric lowers C and therefore Q.","strike":["Reducing the plate area, since the charge becomes more concentrated","Removing the dielectric, since the field between the plates increases"]},{"text":"The battery keeps V fixed, so it can't rise. A larger d lowers C and Q.","strike":["Increasing the plate separation, since the voltage across the gap rises"]},{"text":"Compare each change by a factor of 2, with κ = 2 for the dielectric. Answer: reducing the plate separation.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Charge after each change, V fixed at 9.0 V","x":{"label":"Change"},"y":{"label":"Q ÷ original Q","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Original","Area halved","Dielectric out","d doubled","d halved"],"series":[{"name":"Q ÷ Q₀","values":[1,0.5,0.5,0.5,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["d halved","Q ÷ Q₀"]]}}],"trap":"\"Increasing separation, since the voltage rises\" ignores the battery: V is held at 9.0 V, and a larger d lowers C and Q."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"parallel-plate capacitor","note":"two plates, a gap"},{"t":"9.0 V battery","note":"voltage source"},{"t":"remains connected","note":"battery stays on"}]},{"text":"**A capacitor stores charge.** The charge it holds = capacitance × voltage.","marks":[{"t":"charge stored","note":"Q = C × V"}],"calc":["Q = C × V"]},{"text":"**A connected battery locks the voltage at 9.0 V.** So the only way to get more charge is to raise C.","marks":[{"t":"remains connected","note":"V stuck at 9.0 V"}],"calc":["V fixed → **Q ∝ C**"]},{"text":"**What sets C** for two plates: bigger plates and a dielectric (insulator filling the gap) raise it; a wider gap lowers it.","marks":[{"t":"its plates","note":"A = plate area"}],"calc":["C = κε₀A / d","κ = dielectric, A = plate area","ε₀ = a fixed constant","d = gap between plates"]},{"text":"**d is on the bottom**, so a smaller gap gives a bigger C. Halve the gap → double C.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Capacitance vs plate gap (C ∝ 1/d)","x":{"label":"Gap d (mm)","min":1,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"C (relative)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"C","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,1],[2,0.5],[3,0.333],[4,0.25],[5,0.2]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"C","x":2,"text":"gap 2 mm: C = 0.5","at":"right"},{"series":"C","x":1,"text":"halve gap: C doubles","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Smaller gap → bigger C → more charge**, with V held at 9.0 V.","marks":[{"t":"increase the charge","note":"needs a bigger C"}],"calc":["d ↓ → C ↑","Q = C × 9.0 V → **Q ↑**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** less plate area or removing the dielectric lowers C, so less charge. A wider gap lowers C too, and the battery won't let V rise.","marks":[{"t":"9.0 V battery","note":"V can't rise"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** battery connected → V constant, so Q follows C. Battery disconnected → Q constant. C = κε₀A/d: more area, smaller gap or a dielectric all raise C."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A 25 μF capacitor charged to 3000 V is replaced by a 30 μF capacitor that must hold the same charge. What voltage is required, and how does the stored energy compare with that of the original capacitor?",idStem:"A 25 μF capacitor at 3000 V is replaced with a 30 μF capacitor. What voltage stores the same charge?",choices:["3600 V, storing more energy than before","2500 V, storing the same energy as before","3000 V, storing more energy than before","2500 V, storing less energy than before"],ans:3,exp:"**Same Q = CV:** Q = 25 μF × 3000 V = 0.075 C; V = 0.075 C ÷ 30 μF = **2500 V**. U = ½QV falls from 112.5 J to **93.75 J**.",src:"pe5-cp:52",how:{"ask":"Keep Q fixed, solve for the new V, then compare the energies.","know":"**Q = CV**, so at fixed Q, V = Q/C: bigger C → smaller V. **Stored energy U = ½CV² = ½QV.**","steps":[{"text":"First find the charge, which must stay the same.","hl":"must hold the same charge","calc":["Q = CV = 25 × 10⁻⁶ F × 3000 V","Q = **0.075 C**"]},{"text":"A larger C needs less V for the same Q. 3600 V multiplies by 30/25 instead of dividing.","calc":["V = Q/C = 0.075 / **30 × 10⁻⁶**","V = **2500 V**"],"strike":["3600 V, storing more energy than before","3000 V, storing more energy than before"]},{"text":"Compare the stored energies at the same Q.","calc":["U = ½QV","U_old = ½ × 0.075 × 3000 = **112.5 J**","U_new = ½ × 0.075 × **2500** = **93.75 J**"]},{"text":"Same charge, lower voltage, so less energy. Answer: 2500 V, storing less energy than before.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Stored energy at Q = 0.075 C","x":{"label":"Capacitor"},"y":{"label":"U (J)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"categories":["25 μF at 3000 V","30 μF at 2500 V"],"series":[{"name":"Energy","values":[112.5,93.75]}]},"strike":["2500 V, storing the same energy as before"],"graph":{"bars":[["30 μF at 2500 V","Energy"]]}}],"trap":"3600 V multiplies by 30/25 instead of dividing. A larger capacitance needs less voltage for the same charge."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"25 μF","note":"old capacitance"},{"t":"3000 V","note":"old voltage"},{"t":"30 μF","note":"new capacitance"}]},{"text":"**A capacitor** stores charge. Charge = capacitance × voltage. The new one must hold the **same** charge.","marks":[{"t":"must hold the same charge","note":"Q stays fixed"}],"calc":["Q = C × V"]},{"text":"**Find the charge** the old capacitor holds.","marks":[{"t":"25 μF","note":"25 × 10⁻⁶ F"},{"t":"3000 V","note":"× 3000 V"}],"calc":["Q = 25 × 10⁻⁶ F × 3000 V","Q = **0.075 C**"]},{"text":"**Same charge, bigger C → needs less voltage.** Rearrange Q = CV to V = Q ÷ C.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"÷ this"},{"t":"What voltage is required","note":"V = Q ÷ C"}],"calc":["V = 0.075 C ÷ 30 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = **2500 V**"]},{"text":"**Trap:** 3600 V multiplies by 30/25 instead of dividing. A bigger bucket needs a lower fill level for the same water.","marks":[{"t":"3000 V","note":"× 25/30 → 2500 V"}]},{"text":"**Stored energy** = the work it took to pack the charge on: U = ½ × Q × V.","marks":[{"t":"stored energy","note":"U = ½QV"}],"calc":["U_old = ½ × 0.075 × 3000 = **112.5 J**","U_new = ½ × 0.075 × 2500 = **93.75 J**"]},{"text":"**Same charge at a lower voltage stores less energy.**","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Stored energy, same charge (0.075 C)","x":{"label":"Capacitor"},"y":{"label":"Energy (J)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"categories":["25 μF at 3000 V","30 μF at 2500 V"],"series":[{"name":"Energy (J)","values":[112.5,93.75]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["30 μF at 2500 V","Energy (J)"]]}},{"text":"**Trap:** \"same charge\" does not mean \"same energy\". Energy also depends on the voltage, which dropped.","marks":[{"t":"compare with that of the original capacitor","note":"less: 93.75 < 112.5 J"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fixed Q → V = Q/C (bigger C, smaller V) and U = ½QV = Q²/2C (bigger C, less energy). Fixed V → U = ½CV² (bigger C, more energy)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A tunable laser in vacuum is adjusted so that its wavelength changes from 400 nm to 800 nm. Which statement describes the light after the change?",idStem:"If a light wave's wavelength doubles, its frequency:",choices:["Its frequency doubles, since f is proportional to λ","Its frequency halves, since c = fλ is fixed","Its frequency is unchanged, since only λ was adjusted","Its frequency falls to one-quarter, since E ∝ 1/λ²"],ans:1,exp:"In vacuum, **c = fλ is constant**, so f and λ are inversely proportional: doubling λ **halves f**.",src:"pe5-cp:53",how:{"ask":"What links wavelength and frequency for light in vacuum, and what stays constant?","know":"**Wave equation: v = fλ.** For light in vacuum, v = c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, always. So **f = c/λ**: if λ goes up by some factor, f goes down by the same factor.","steps":[{"text":"In vacuum every electromagnetic wave travels at c, so c = fλ fixes the product of f and λ.","hl":"in vacuum"},{"text":"Compute f at both wavelengths.","calc":["f = c/λ","f₄₀₀ = 3.0 × 10⁸ / **400 × 10⁻⁹** = **7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz**","f₈₀₀ = 3.0 × 10⁸ / **800 × 10⁻⁹** = **3.75 × 10¹⁴ Hz**","ratio = **½**"]},{"text":"On the f–λ curve, doubling λ lands at half the frequency.","hl":"from 400 nm to 800 nm","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Frequency vs wavelength in vacuum, f = c/λ","x":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":400,"max":800,"step":100},"y":{"label":"f (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"f = c/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,7.5],[500,6],[600,5],[700,4.29],[800,3.75]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"f = c/λ","x":400},{"series":"f = c/λ","x":800}],"spans":[{"x":400,"from":0,"to":7.5,"text":"7.5","side":"right"},{"x":800,"from":0,"to":3.75,"text":"3.75 = ½ × 7.5","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"f = c/λ","x":400,"text":"λ = 400 nm","at":"right"},{"series":"f = c/λ","x":800,"text":"λ doubled to 800 nm","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"f is inversely proportional to λ, not proportional. \"Unchanged\" applies when light enters a new medium, and E ∝ 1/λ, not 1/λ². Answer: its frequency halves.","strike":["Its frequency doubles, since f is proportional to λ","Its frequency is unchanged, since only λ was adjusted","Its frequency falls to one-quarter, since E ∝ 1/λ²"]}],"trap":"\"Unchanged\" applies when light moves into a new medium (f fixed, λ changes). Here the source itself was changed in vacuum, where c is fixed."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"in vacuum","note":"empty space"},{"t":"400 nm","note":"start wavelength"},{"t":"800 nm","note":"new wavelength"}]},{"text":"**Wavelength (λ)** = distance from one wave crest to the next. **Frequency (f)** = how many crests pass each second.","marks":[{"t":"wavelength","note":"crest-to-crest distance"}]},{"text":"**In vacuum, all light moves at the same speed c.** Speed = frequency × wavelength, so f and λ must trade off.","marks":[{"t":"in vacuum","note":"speed fixed at c"}],"calc":["c = f × λ","c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, always","f = c ÷ λ"]},{"text":"**Longer waves → fewer crests per second.** On the curve, doubling λ lands at half the frequency.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Frequency vs wavelength in vacuum (f = c/λ)","x":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":400,"max":800,"step":100},"y":{"label":"f (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"f","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,7.5],[500,6],[600,5],[700,4.29],[800,3.75]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"f","x":400,"text":"400 nm: 7.5","at":"right"},{"series":"f","x":800,"text":"800 nm: 3.75 = half","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Do the numbers.**","marks":[{"t":"from 400 nm to 800 nm","note":"λ × 2"}],"calc":["f₄₀₀ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 400 × 10⁻⁹ = **7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz**","f₈₀₀ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 800 × 10⁻⁹ = **3.75 × 10¹⁴ Hz**","ratio = **½**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** f is NOT proportional to λ (it's the opposite). f can't stay the same, because c is fixed. And E ∝ 1/λ, not 1/λ²; the question asks about f anyway.","marks":[{"t":"its wavelength changes","note":"so f must change"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** light in vacuum: c = fλ is fixed, so f and λ are inversely proportional. Multiply λ by some factor → divide f by the same factor."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"Consider the data for two photons: photon 1 has an energy of 2.0 eV, and photon 2 has an energy of 4.0 eV. Compared with photon 1, photon 2 has:",idStem:"If a photon's energy doubles, which property also doubles?",choices:["twice the frequency and twice the wavelength","half the frequency and twice the wavelength","twice the frequency and half the wavelength","the same frequency and twice the amplitude"],ans:2,exp:"**E = hf**, so doubling E doubles f. **E = hc/λ**, so doubling E halves λ.",src:"pe5-cp:54",how:{"ask":"How are a photon's energy, frequency and wavelength linked?","know":"**Photon energy: E = hf = hc/λ** (h = Planck's constant, f = frequency, λ = wavelength, c = speed of light). E ∝ f (direct); E ∝ 1/λ (inverse). A photon's energy is set by f, not by amplitude.","steps":[{"text":"The energy doubles.","hl":"photon 1 has an energy of 2.0 eV, and photon 2 has an energy of 4.0 eV","calc":["E₂ / E₁ = 4.0 / 2.0 = **2**"]},{"text":"E = hf, so frequency is proportional to energy.","calc":["f = E/h → f₂ = **2** f₁"],"strike":["half the frequency and twice the wavelength","the same frequency and twice the amplitude"]},{"text":"E = hc/λ, so wavelength is inversely proportional to energy.","calc":["λ = hc/E = 1240 eV·nm / E","λ₁ = 1240 / 2.0 = **620 nm**","λ₂ = 1240 / 4.0 = **310 nm**"],"strike":["twice the frequency and twice the wavelength"]},{"text":"Twice the frequency and half the wavelength keep fλ = c. Answer: twice the frequency and half the wavelength.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"The two photons","x":{"label":"Photon"},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Photon 1, 2.0 eV","Photon 2, 4.0 eV"],"series":[{"name":"λ (nm)","values":[620,310]},{"name":"f (× 10¹² Hz)","values":[484,967]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Photon 2, 4.0 eV","λ (nm)"],["Photon 2, 4.0 eV","f (× 10¹² Hz)"]]}}],"trap":"\"Twice the frequency and twice the wavelength\" breaks c = fλ: f and λ can't both double."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** A **photon** is a single packet of light; each carries a set energy.","marks":[{"t":"two photons","note":"packets of light"},{"t":"2.0 eV","note":"photon 1 energy"},{"t":"4.0 eV","note":"photon 2 energy"}]},{"text":"**eV (electron-volt)** is just a tiny unit of energy. Compare the two energies.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 eV","note":"× 2"},{"t":"4.0 eV","note":"= 2 × photon 1"}],"calc":["E₂ ÷ E₁ = 4.0 ÷ 2.0 = **2**"]},{"text":"**E = hf:** energy is proportional to frequency (h is a fixed constant). Double E → double f.","marks":[{"t":"photon 2 has","note":"f doubles"}],"calc":["E = h × f","f = E ÷ h → f₂ = **2 × f₁**"]},{"text":"**E = hc/λ:** energy is inversely proportional to wavelength. Double E → half λ. Shortcut: hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 eV","note":"λ = 620 nm"},{"t":"4.0 eV","note":"λ = 310 nm"}],"calc":["λ = hc ÷ E = 1240 eV·nm ÷ E","λ₁ = 1240 ÷ 2.0 = **620 nm**","λ₂ = 1240 ÷ 4.0 = **310 nm**"]},{"text":"**Side by side:** photon 2's wavelength is half of photon 1's.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength of each photon","x":{"label":"Photon"},"y":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Photon 1 (2.0 eV)","Photon 2 (4.0 eV)"],"series":[{"name":"Wavelength (nm)","values":[620,310]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Photon 2 (4.0 eV)","Wavelength (nm)"]]}},{"text":"**Trap:** amplitude means brightness (how many photons), not the energy of one photon. Higher energy always means higher f and shorter λ.","marks":[{"t":"Compared with photon 1","note":"energy set by f, not size"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf = hc/λ. Energy goes UP with frequency and DOWN with wavelength: double E → double f, half λ."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"An orange LED emits light with a wavelength of 600 nm in air, where c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. The light then passes into a glass lens with an index of refraction of 1.5. What are the frequency and the wavelength of the light inside the glass?",idStem:"Light of wavelength 600 nm has about what frequency?",choices:["5 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 400 nm","5 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 900 nm","3.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 600 nm","5 × 10⁵ Hz and 400 nm"],ans:0,exp:"**f = c/λ** = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s ÷ 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = **5 × 10¹⁴ Hz**. The frequency is set by the source and **does not change at a boundary**; the speed drops to c/n, so **λ = 600 nm ÷ 1.5 = 400 nm**.",src:"pe5-cp:55",how:{"ask":"Get the frequency from the speed and wavelength in air, then decide which quantity stays fixed when the light enters glass.","know":"**c = fλ**: speed = frequency × wavelength, with λ in meters (**1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m**). In a medium of index n, the speed is **v = c/n**. Crossing a boundary, the **frequency stays the same** (set by the source), so the wavelength scales with the speed: **λ_medium = λ_air / n**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the wavelength in air to meters.","hl":"600 nm in air","calc":["600 nm = 600 × 10⁻⁹ m = **6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"Frequency from the wave equation. 5 × 10⁵ Hz skips the nm → m step.","calc":["f = c/λ","f = 3.0 × 10⁸ / **6.0 × 10⁻⁷**","f = 0.5 × 10¹⁵ = **5 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"],"strike":["5 × 10⁵ Hz and 400 nm"]},{"text":"In glass the light slows down, but the frequency stays the same, so the wavelength shrinks.","hl":"index of refraction of 1.5","calc":["v = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ / 1.5 = **2.0 × 10⁸ m/s**","f unchanged = 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz","λ_glass = v/f = 2.0 × 10⁸ / 5 × 10¹⁴ = **4.0 × 10⁻⁷ m** = **400 nm**"]},{"text":"The frequency is set by the LED and does not change at a boundary.","strike":["3.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 600 nm"]},{"text":"Slower light at the same frequency means a shorter wavelength, so 900 nm goes the wrong way. Answer: 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 400 nm.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Light in air vs in glass","x":{"label":"Medium"},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Air","Glass, n = 1.5"],"series":[{"name":"λ (nm)","values":[600,400]},{"name":"f (× 10¹² Hz)","values":[500,500]}]},"strike":["5 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 900 nm"],"graph":{"bars":[["Glass, n = 1.5","λ (nm)"],["Glass, n = 1.5","f (× 10¹² Hz)"]]}}],"trap":"\"5 × 10¹⁴ Hz and 900 nm\" multiplies by n instead of dividing. Light slows down in glass, and with f fixed the wavelength must get shorter, not longer."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"600 nm","note":"wavelength in air"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"speed in air"},{"t":"index of refraction of 1.5","note":"glass slows light 1.5×"}]},{"text":"**Wavelength** = crest-to-crest distance; **frequency** = crests per second. Speed = frequency × wavelength.","marks":[{"t":"frequency and the wavelength","note":"the two things asked"}],"calc":["c = f × λ → f = c ÷ λ"]},{"text":"**Use meters**, since c is in m/s.","marks":[{"t":"600 nm","note":"= 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"}],"calc":["1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m","600 nm = **6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"**Frequency in air.**","marks":[{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"÷ 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"}],"calc":["f = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 6.0 × 10⁻⁷","f = 0.5 × 10¹⁵ = **5 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"**Index of refraction (n)** = how many times slower light goes in the material.","marks":[{"t":"index of refraction of 1.5","note":"v = c ÷ 1.5"}],"calc":["v = c ÷ n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.5","v = **2.0 × 10⁸ m/s**"]},{"text":"**Frequency never changes at a boundary:** the LED sets how many crests per second, and every crest that enters must come out. So the waves bunch up instead.","marks":[{"t":"inside the glass","note":"f stays 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz"}]},{"text":"**Slower, same frequency → shorter wavelength**, by the same factor 1.5.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength of the orange light","x":{"label":"Where"},"y":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Air","Glass (n = 1.5)"],"series":[{"name":"Wavelength (nm)","values":[600,400]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Glass (n = 1.5)","Wavelength (nm)"]]},"calc":["λ_glass = 600 nm ÷ 1.5 = **400 nm**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 900 nm multiplies by n; 3.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz wrongly divides f by n; 5 × 10⁵ Hz forgets nm → m.","marks":[{"t":"passes into a glass lens","note":"only v and λ change"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** crossing into a new material, frequency stays fixed; speed and wavelength both divide by n. Always convert nm to m before using c = fλ."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A quantum dot emits photons with an energy of 2.0 eV. Taking h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s and c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, the wavelength of the emitted light is approximately:",idStem:"Using hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, what is the wavelength of a 2 eV photon?",choices:["620 nm","310 nm","2480 nm","1240 nm"],ans:0,exp:"**λ = hc/E**; hc = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 3.0 × 10⁸ ≈ 1.24 × 10⁻⁶ eV·m = **1240 eV·nm**. λ = 1240 ÷ 2.0 = **620 nm**.",src:"pe5-cp:56",how:{"ask":"Rearrange the photon-energy equation to solve for wavelength.","know":"**E = hc/λ** → **λ = hc/E**. Handy shortcut: **hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm**, so λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ E(eV).","steps":[{"text":"Solve E = hc/λ for the wavelength.","hl":"emits photons with an energy of 2.0 eV","calc":["E = hc/λ → λ = hc/E"]},{"text":"Find hc in eV·nm, a handy constant.","calc":["hc = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s × 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","= **1.24 × 10⁻⁶ eV·m**","= **1240 eV·nm**"]},{"text":"Divide by the energy. 2480 nm multiplies by E instead of dividing, and 1240 nm forgets to divide at all.","calc":["λ = 1240 eV·nm / 2.0 eV","λ = **620 nm**"],"strike":["2480 nm","1240 nm"]},{"text":"310 nm is the wavelength of a 4.0 eV photon. Answer: 620 nm, orange-red light.","strike":["310 nm"]}],"trap":"2480 nm multiplies by E instead of dividing. Higher energy means shorter wavelength."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.**","marks":[{"t":"2.0 eV","note":"energy of one photon"},{"t":"h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s","note":"Planck's constant"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"speed of light"}]},{"text":"**A photon** is one packet of light; a **quantum dot** is a tiny crystal that gives off photons of one energy. Energy and wavelength are linked by E = hc/λ.","marks":[{"t":"quantum dot","note":"tiny light source"}],"calc":["E = hc ÷ λ"]},{"text":"**We want λ**, so flip the equation around.","marks":[{"t":"the wavelength","note":"λ = hc ÷ E"}],"calc":["E = hc ÷ λ → **λ = hc ÷ E**"]},{"text":"**Multiply h × c first.** It's a handy constant worth remembering.","marks":[{"t":"h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s","note":"h"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"× c"}],"calc":["hc = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 3.0 × 10⁸","hc = **1.24 × 10⁻⁶ eV·m**","= **1240 eV·nm**"]},{"text":"**Divide by the photon energy.**","marks":[{"t":"2.0 eV","note":"÷ this"}],"calc":["λ = 1240 eV·nm ÷ 2.0 eV","λ = **620 nm** (orange-red light)"]},{"text":"**Higher energy → shorter wavelength.** The curve shows λ = 1240/E.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Wavelength vs photon energy (λ = 1240/E)","x":{"label":"Photon energy (eV)","min":1,"max":4,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":0,"max":1400,"step":200},"series":[{"name":"λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,1240],[1.5,827],[2,620],[3,413],[4,310]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"λ","x":2,"text":"2.0 eV → 620 nm","at":"right"},{"series":"λ","x":4,"text":"4.0 eV → 310 nm","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 2480 nm multiplies by E; 1240 nm forgets to divide; 310 nm is the wavelength of a 4.0 eV photon.","marks":[{"t":"approximately","note":"620 nm"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ (nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ E (eV). Higher energy, shorter wavelength."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Consider the data at 25 °C: HA: Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵, Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹; HB⁺: Ka = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰, Kw/Ka = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵. Based on these data, which statement about A⁻, the conjugate base of HA, is correct?",idStem:"A weak acid has pKa = 5. What is Kb for its conjugate base?",choices:["Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵, so A⁻ is the stronger base","Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹, so B is the stronger base","Kb of A⁻ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵, so A⁻ is the stronger base","Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹, so A⁻ is the stronger base"],ans:1,exp:"For a conjugate pair, **Kb = Kw/Ka of the acid**. Kb(A⁻) = Kw/Ka(HA) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ (pKa 5 + pKb 9 = 14). Kb(B) = Kw/Ka(HB⁺) = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ is larger, so **B is the stronger base**.",src:"pe5-cp:57",how:{"ask":"Which row and column give the base constant of HA's conjugate base, and how does it compare with the other base?","know":"An acid HA and its **conjugate base** A⁻ differ by one H⁺. **Ka × Kb = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴** at 25 °C, so **Kb(A⁻) = Kw/Ka(HA)** (equivalently pKa + pKb = 14). A larger Kb means a stronger base. The weaker the acid, the stronger its conjugate base.","steps":[{"text":"For a conjugate pair, Ka × Kb = Kw, so Kb of A⁻ comes from the Ka of HA.","hl":"HA: Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵, Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹","calc":["Ka(HA) × Kb(A⁻) = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴","Kb(A⁻) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ = **1.0 × 10⁻⁹**"],"strike":["Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵, so A⁻ is the stronger base","Kb of A⁻ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵, so A⁻ is the stronger base"]},{"text":"Do the same for B, the conjugate base of HB⁺.","hl":"HB⁺: Ka = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰, Kw/Ka = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵","calc":["Kb(B) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = **2.0 × 10⁻⁵**"]},{"text":"Compare on the pKb scale: lower pKb means a stronger base.","calc":["pKb(A⁻) = −log(1.0 × 10⁻⁹) = **9.0**","pKb(B) = −log(2.0 × 10⁻⁵) = **4.7**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Base strength","x":{"label":"Base"},"y":{"label":"pKb","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"categories":["A⁻","B"],"series":[{"name":"pKb","values":[9,4.7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["B","pKb"]]}},{"text":"1.0 × 10⁻⁹ is smaller than 2.0 × 10⁻⁵, so A⁻ is the weaker base. Answer: Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹, so B is the stronger base.","strike":["Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹, so A⁻ is the stronger base"]}],"trap":"\"Kb of A⁻ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹, so A⁻ is the stronger base\" has the right number but the wrong comparison. 10⁻⁹ is smaller than B's 2.0 × 10⁻⁵."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you:** data for two acids, and a question about A⁻.","marks":[{"t":"HA:","note":"acid #1"},{"t":"HB⁺:","note":"acid #2"},{"t":"A⁻, the conjugate base of HA","note":"what's asked"}]},{"text":"**Acid** = gives away H⁺. **Base** = takes H⁺. Remove one H⁺ from an acid and you get its **conjugate base**.","marks":[{"t":"the conjugate base of HA","note":"HA minus one H⁺"}],"calc":["HA ⇌ H⁺ + **A⁻**","HB⁺ ⇌ H⁺ + **B**"]},{"text":"**Ka** measures acid strength: bigger Ka = gives H⁺ away more easily = stronger acid.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵","note":"HA: stronger acid"},{"t":"Ka = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰","note":"HB⁺: much weaker acid"}]},{"text":"**Kb** measures base strength. For a conjugate pair, Ka × Kb = Kw (1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴), so Kw/Ka **is** the Kb of the base.","marks":[{"t":"Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹","note":"= Kb of A⁻"}],"calc":["Kb(A⁻) = Kw ÷ Ka(HA)","= 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ = **1.0 × 10⁻⁹**"]},{"text":"**Same for B**, the conjugate base of HB⁺.","marks":[{"t":"Kw/Ka = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵","note":"= Kb of B"}],"calc":["Kb(B) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = **2.0 × 10⁻⁵**"]},{"text":"**Compare on the pKb scale** (pKb = −log Kb). Lower pKb = stronger base. B wins by a lot.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pKb (lower = stronger base)","x":{"label":"Base"},"y":{"label":"pKb","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["A⁻","B"],"series":[{"name":"pKb","values":[9,4.7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["B","pKb"]]},"calc":["2.0 × 10⁻⁵ > 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ → **B is the stronger base**"]},{"text":"**Why:** the weaker the acid, the stronger its conjugate base. HB⁺ barely lets go of H⁺, so B grabs H⁺ hard.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁰","note":"weak acid → strong base"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ is HA's Ka, not A⁻'s Kb. 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ is B's Kb, not A⁻'s.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵","note":"Ka of HA, not Kb"},{"t":"Kw/Ka = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵","note":"belongs to B"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Kb(conjugate base) = Kw ÷ Ka(acid), or pKa + pKb = 14. Bigger Kb = stronger base; weaker acid → stronger conjugate base."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"A lipase catalyzes the esterification RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O in a closed vessel at 37 °C, where K = 4. Which change would NOT increase the amount of ester present at equilibrium?",idStem:"Which change will NOT raise the final yield of an ester made at equilibrium?",choices:["Doubling the amount of alcohol","Adding molecular sieves that remove water","Doubling the amount of lipase","Doubling the amount of carboxylic acid"],ans:2,exp:"A catalyst speeds forward and reverse reactions equally, so it **reaches equilibrium faster without shifting it**. Adding a reactant or removing water shifts the equilibrium toward ester (Le Châtelier).",src:"pe5-cp:58",how:{"ask":"Which change alters only the speed of reaching equilibrium, not its position?","know":"**Le Châtelier:** adding reactant or removing product shifts equilibrium toward products. **Catalysts (enzymes)** lower Ea for both directions equally; they don't change K or the equilibrium amounts.","steps":[{"text":"Set up the equilibrium yield, assuming 1 M acid and 1 M alcohol.","hl":"K = 4","calc":["K = [ester][H₂O] / ([acid][alcohol]) = 4","x² / (1 − x)² = 4 → x / (1 − x) = 2","x = **0.67 M** ester"]},{"text":"Add a reactant: the equilibrium shifts toward ester.","calc":["2 M alcohol: x² / ((1 − x)(2 − x)) = 4","3x² − 12x + 8 = 0","x = **0.85 M** ester ↑"],"strike":["Doubling the amount of alcohol","Doubling the amount of carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Water is a product, so removing it also pulls the reaction toward ester.","strike":["Adding molecular sieves that remove water"]},{"text":"A catalyst lowers Ea equally for both directions, so equilibrium arrives sooner but at the same position. Answer: doubling the lipase.","hl":"NOT increase the amount of ester present at equilibrium","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ester formed from 1 M acid + 1 M alcohol, K = 4","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"[ester] (M)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"1× lipase","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.3],[2,0.5],[4,0.62],[6,0.66],[8,0.67]]},{"name":"2× lipase","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.5],[2,0.62],[4,0.66],[6,0.67],[8,0.67]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"1× lipase","x":8},{"series":"2× lipase","x":8},{"series":"2× lipase","x":1},{"series":"1× lipase","x":1}],"labels":[{"series":"2× lipase","x":1,"text":"2×: 0.50 M at 1 h (faster)","at":"left"},{"series":"1× lipase","x":1,"text":"1×: 0.30 M at 1 h","at":"right"}],"shade":[{"x1":6,"x2":8,"text":"same end point"}],"spans":[{"x":8,"from":0,"to":0.67,"text":"0.67 M both","side":"left"}]}}],"trap":"Removing water may look unrelated to the ester, but water is a product: taking it out pulls the reaction forward."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** Careful: it asks which change does **NOT** help.","marks":[{"t":"lipase","note":"an enzyme"},{"t":"K = 4","note":"equilibrium constant"},{"t":"NOT increase","note":"find the one that fails"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation.** Left side = reactants; right side = products.","marks":[{"t":"RCOOH","note":"acid (reactant)"},{"t":"R′OH","note":"alcohol (reactant)"},{"t":"RCOOR′","note":"ester (product)"}]},{"text":"**⇌ means it runs both ways.** At **equilibrium** the forward and backward rates are equal, so the amounts stop changing.","marks":[{"t":"⇌","note":"goes both ways"},{"t":"H₂O","note":"water (product)"}]},{"text":"**K = products ÷ reactants** at equilibrium. K = 4 means the mixture settles with more products than reactants.","marks":[{"t":"K = 4","note":"products ÷ reactants"}],"calc":["K = [ester][H₂O] ÷ ([acid][alcohol]) = 4"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a reactant and the reaction shifts forward to use it up, making more ester.","marks":[{"t":"RCOOH","note":"add more → more ester"},{"t":"R′OH","note":"add more → more ester"}],"calc":["1 M acid + 1 M alcohol → **0.67 M** ester","double alcohol → **0.85 M** ester ↑"]},{"text":"**Remove a product** and the reaction shifts forward to replace it. Molecular sieves soak up water.","marks":[{"t":"H₂O","note":"remove it → pulls right"}]},{"text":"**A catalyst** (like lipase) lowers the energy hill for BOTH directions equally. It gets you to equilibrium faster but to the **same** amount of ester.","marks":[{"t":"A lipase catalyzes","note":"speeds both ways"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ester formed over time","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Ester (M)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"1× lipase","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.3],[20,0.48],[30,0.58],[40,0.63],[60,0.67]]},{"name":"2× lipase","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.48],[20,0.63],[30,0.66],[40,0.67],[60,0.67]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"2× lipase","x":10,"text":"faster","at":"left"},{"series":"1× lipase","x":60,"text":"same end: 0.67 M","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**So doubling the lipase is the change that does NOT increase the ester** at equilibrium.","marks":[{"t":"present at equilibrium","note":"amount at the end, not speed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** catalysts and enzymes change only the speed, never K or the equilibrium amounts. Adding reactant or removing product shifts toward product."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 5: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A worker pushes a crate 15 m across a floor. The graph shows the worker's push, which is parallel to the motion, and the kinetic friction on the crate, both plotted against position. How much work does the worker do on the crate?",idStem:"A pushing force drops steadily from 100 N to 0 N over 20 m. How much work is done?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Forces on the crate versus position","x":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Push","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,80],[3,68],[6,56],[9,44],[12,32],[15,20]]},{"name":"Friction","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,25],[15,25]]}]},choices:["375 J","450 J","750 J","1200 J"],ans:2,exp:"Work by a varying force = **area under its force–position graph**. Under the push line is a trapezoid with sides 80 N and 20 N over 15 m: **½(80 + 20)(15) = 750 J**. Friction acts on the crate too, but the question asks only for the worker's work.",src:"pe5-cp:59",how:{"ask":"Which line is the worker's force, and what area under it, from 0 to 15 m, equals the work?","know":"• **Work W = F·d** for a constant force along the motion. For a force that changes with position, W = **area under the F vs x graph**.\n• Trapezoid area = ½(side₁ + side₂) × width: here the sides are the forces at the two ends and the width is the distance.\n• A straight, steadily changing force averages to (start + end) ÷ 2.\n• Work by one force ≠ net work. Net work would add friction's negative work, −F_fr·d.","steps":[{"text":"Only the push counts. Friction is on the graph, but it is a different force.","hl":"How much work does the worker do on the crate?","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Push","x":6,"text":"push: counts","at":"above"},{"series":"Friction","x":15,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Read the push at the start and at the end.","graph":{"focus":["Push"],"read":[{"series":"Push","x":0},{"series":"Push","x":15}],"spans":[{"x":0,"from":0,"to":80,"text":"80 N","side":"right"},{"x":15,"from":0,"to":20,"text":"20 N","side":"left"}]},"calc":["push at 0 m: **80 N**","push at 15 m: **20 N**"]},{"text":"The area under a straight sloped line is a trapezoid.","calc":["W = area under the push line","W = ½ × (80 + 20) N × 15 m","W = **750 J**"],"graph":{"focus":["Push"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":15,"text":"trapezoid = 750 J"}]}},{"text":"Common slips: using the starting force throughout, or keeping only the triangle above 20 N.","calc":["80 N × 15 m = **1200 J**: treats the push as constant","½ × (80 − 20) × 15 = **450 J**: triangle only"],"strike":["1200 J","450 J"],"graph":{"focus":["Push"],"spans":[{"x":0.5,"from":20,"to":80,"text":"80 − 20 = 60: triangle only","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"x":7.5,"y":90,"text":"80 N × 15 m = 1200 J: as if constant","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Subtracting friction gives the net work, which the question did not ask for.","graph":{"focus":["Friction"],"spans":[{"x":7.5,"from":0,"to":25,"text":"25 N","side":"right"}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":15,"text":"25 × 15 = 375 J"}]},"calc":["750 − 25 × 15 = **375 J**: net work"],"strike":["375 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 750 J. Rule: work by a varying force = area under that force's line on the F vs x graph."}],"trap":"375 J subtracts friction's 25 N × 15 m; that is the net work on the crate, not the worker's work. 1200 J uses the starting 80 N for the whole 15 m."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = how strong a force is**, in newtons (N).","marks":[{"t":"worker's push","note":"a force"},{"t":"kinetic friction","note":"another force"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = where the crate is**, from 0 to 15 m.","marks":[{"t":"15 m","note":"total distance"},{"t":"plotted against position","note":"x axis: position"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two lines, two forces.** The question asks about only one: the worker's push.","graph":{"focus":["Push","Friction"],"labels":[{"series":"Push","x":3,"text":"push: this one","at":"above"},{"series":"Friction","x":15,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Work** = energy given by a force: force × distance moved along it. Here the push points along the motion, so all of it counts.","marks":[{"t":"How much work","note":"force × distance"},{"t":"parallel to the motion","note":"all of the push counts"}]},{"text":"**The push weakens as the crate moves**, so you can't use one force. Instead, **work = area under the force line**.","graph":{"focus":["Push"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":15,"text":"area under push = work"}]}},{"text":"**Read the push at the start and end.**","graph":{"focus":["Push"],"read":[{"series":"Push","x":0},{"series":"Push","x":15}],"spans":[{"x":0,"from":0,"to":80,"text":"80 N","side":"right"},{"x":15,"from":0,"to":20,"text":"20 N","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**The area is a trapezoid:** average of the two heights × width.","marks":[{"t":"15 m","note":"width"}],"calc":["W = ½ × (80 + 20) × 15","W = 50 N × 15 m","W = **750 J**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1200 J uses 80 N the whole way; 375 J subtracts friction (net work); 450 J uses only the triangle part.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Push","x":15,"text":"push drops to 20 N","at":"above"},{"series":"Friction","x":0,"text":"friction: ignore","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** work by a changing force = area under its force-vs-position line. Straight sloped line → trapezoid: ½ × (start + end) × distance. Use only the force asked about."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 1 · Chem/Phys (built 2026-11-11) ──
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"Four compounds are spotted side by side on a silica gel TLC plate, which is developed with 4:1 hexane/ethyl acetate. The silica surface is covered with Si–OH groups. Which compound shown will have the smallest Rf?",idStem:"In paper chromatography, water held on the paper is the stationary phase. What mainly controls how far each compound travels?",choiceFigs:["p1-dimethoxybenzene","p1-hydroquinone","p1-acetophenone","p1-dibromobenzene"],choices:["1,4-Dimethoxybenzene","Benzene-1,4-diol","Acetophenone","1,4-Dibromobenzene"],ans:1,exp:"Silica is the **polar stationary phase** (Si–OH groups), and the eluent is mostly nonpolar hexane. The compound that **hydrogen bonds most strongly to silica** is held back and has the **smallest Rf**. Benzene-1,4-diol has **two O–H groups** that donate hydrogen bonds to the Si–OH surface; the ether, ketone and dibromide have no O–H.",src:"pe1-cp:1",how:{"ask":"Which phase is polar here, and which structure can hold on to it most strongly through hydrogen bonds?","know":"• **TLC** = a race between two phases. The **stationary phase** (silica, coated with polar Si–OH groups) stays put; the **mobile phase** (here mostly nonpolar hexane) climbs the plate.\n• **Rf** = distance the spot moved ÷ distance the solvent moved, from 0 to 1. A compound that sticks to the stationary phase lags behind → **small Rf**.\n• What makes a compound stick to silica: **O–H or N–H groups** (hydrogen-bond donors) most of all, then C=O and ether O (acceptors only), and least of all C–H and C–halogen.\n• Molar mass matters little in TLC; polarity decides.","steps":[{"text":"Name the phases first. Silica, covered with Si–OH, is the polar stationary phase; the hexane-rich eluent is nonpolar.","hl":"The silica surface is covered with Si–OH groups"},{"text":"Rule: whatever sticks to the polar stationary phase travels least and has the smallest Rf. Rank what each structure can do with Si–OH.","calc":["Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent distance","diol: **2 O–H** donors + 2 O acceptors","ether: 0 donors, 2 O acceptors","ketone: 0 donors, 1 C=O acceptor","dibromide: 0 donors, no strong acceptor"]},{"text":"Look for the hydrogens on oxygen. Only the diol has them, highlighted: each O–H can hydrogen bond to silica.","fig":"p1-hydroquinone-oh"},{"text":"The dibromide is heaviest but nonpolar; it runs near the solvent front. The ketone has one acceptor and no donor.","strike":["1,4-Dibromobenzene","Acetophenone"]},{"text":"The diether has as many oxygens as the diol but no O–H, so it holds on to silica far less.","strike":["1,4-Dimethoxybenzene"]},{"text":"Answer: benzene-1,4-diol. Rule to reuse: on silica, more O–H/N–H → stickier → smaller Rf."}],"trap":"1,4-Dimethoxybenzene also has two oxygens, so it looks just as polar, but its oxygens carry no H: it can only accept hydrogen bonds. Count O–H and N–H groups, not oxygens. The dibromide is the heaviest, but mass does not slow a spot on TLC."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** A hexagon with three inner double lines is a benzene ring: six carbons. Letters are other atoms.","fig":"t1-hq-read"},{"text":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent distance.** The silica plate is covered with Si–OH groups; a compound that clings to them barely moves: small Rf.","fig":"t1-silica-model"},{"text":"**Polar = uneven charge.** O pulls electrons, so in O–H the O is slightly − and the H slightly +. Hexane, the solvent, is only C and H: nonpolar.","fig":"t1-hexane"},{"text":"**Hydrogen bond:** the slightly + H on an O is pulled toward an O on silica. This diol has two O–H groups to grab with.","fig":"t1-hq-oh"},{"text":"**No H on the oxygen** means it can only receive a hydrogen bond, not give one: a weaker grip.","fig":"t1-dimethoxy-o"},{"text":"C=O is a carbon double-bonded to oxygen. **One oxygen and no O–H** gives only a modest grip.","fig":"t1-acetophenone-o"},{"text":"**Weight doesn't matter on TLC.** Br makes no hydrogen bonds, so this compound rides along with the solvent.","fig":"t1-dibromo-br"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** more O–H or N–H groups → stickier on silica → smaller Rf. In each drawing, count the H atoms attached directly to O or N.","fig":"t1-hq-oh"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"A TLC plate is spotted on a pencil baseline drawn 1.0 cm above the plate's bottom edge. After development, the solvent front is 11.0 cm above the bottom edge and the center of one spot is 3.0 cm above the bottom edge. What is the Rf of that compound?",idStem:"On a TLC plate, a spot moves 2 cm while the solvent front moves 10 cm. What is its Rf?",choices:["0.27","0.20","5.0","0.30"],ans:1,exp:"**Rf = (spot distance from baseline) ÷ (solvent-front distance from baseline)** = (3.0 − 1.0) ÷ (11.0 − 1.0) = 2.0 ÷ 10.0 = 0.20. Both distances are measured from where the sample started.",src:"pe1-cp:3",how:{"ask":"How far did the spot travel and how far did the solvent travel, both measured from the starting line?","know":"**Rf (retention factor) = distance traveled by the compound ÷ distance traveled by the solvent front.** Both distances start at the **baseline** (where the spot was applied), not at the plate edge. Rf is always between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"Rf is measured from where the sample STARTED, the pencil baseline, not from the plate's edge.","hl":"1.0 cm above the plate's bottom edge"},{"text":"Convert both edge readings into baseline distances by subtracting 1.0 cm.","hl":"the solvent front is 11.0 cm above the bottom edge and the center of one spot is 3.0 cm above the bottom edge","calc":["spot: 3.0 cm − 1.0 cm = **2.0 cm**","solvent front: 11.0 cm − 1.0 cm = **10.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide spot distance by solvent-front distance.","calc":["Rf = 2.0 cm ÷ 10.0 cm","Rf = **0.20**"]},{"text":"Eliminate the slips: 0.27 = 3.0 ÷ 11.0 (both from the edge); 0.30 = 3.0 ÷ 10.0 (only one corrected); 5.0 is upside down, and Rf can never exceed 1.","strike":["0.27","0.30","5.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.20, because both distances must be measured from the baseline."}],"trap":"0.27 comes from 3.0 ÷ 11.0, using distances from the plate edge. The sample started at the baseline, so measure from there. 5.0 is the ratio flipped and is impossible, since Rf ≤ 1."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives. All three are measured from the **bottom edge** of the plate.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"pencil line height"},{"t":"11.0 cm","note":"solvent front height"},{"t":"3.0 cm","note":"spot height"}]},{"text":"**TLC (thin-layer chromatography)** = a coated plate dipped in liquid. The liquid creeps up and carries compounds with it; a compound that sticks less travels farther.","marks":[{"t":"TLC plate","note":"coated strip"},{"t":"pencil baseline","note":"where the sample starts"},{"t":"solvent front","note":"highest point liquid reached"}]},{"text":"**Picture the plate.** The sample did NOT start at the edge; it started on the pencil line, 1 cm up.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Heights measured from the plate's bottom edge","x":{"label":"Mark on the plate"},"y":{"label":"Height above edge (cm)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Baseline (start)","Spot","Solvent front"],"series":[{"name":"from edge","values":[1,3,11]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Baseline (start)","from edge"]]}},{"text":"**Rf (retention factor)** = how far the compound moved ÷ how far the liquid moved. Both trips start at the pencil line.","marks":[{"t":"Rf","note":"compound trip ÷ liquid trip"}],"calc":["Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline)"]},{"text":"**Subtract the starting line** from both heights to get real travel distances.","marks":[{"t":"drawn 1.0 cm above","note":"start line: subtract this"},{"t":"11.0 cm","note":"11.0 − 1.0 = 10.0 cm"},{"t":"3.0 cm","note":"3.0 − 1.0 = 2.0 cm"}],"calc":["compound: 3.0 − 1.0 = **2.0 cm**","liquid: 11.0 − 1.0 = **10.0 cm**"]},{"text":"**Now the true trips:** the compound went 2 cm while the liquid went 10 cm.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Distances measured from the baseline (where the sample started)","x":{"label":"What moved"},"y":{"label":"Distance traveled (cm)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Compound (spot)","Solvent front"],"series":[{"name":"from baseline","values":[2,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Compound (spot)","from baseline"],["Solvent front","from baseline"]]}},{"text":"**Divide** compound trip by liquid trip.","marks":[{"t":"11.0 cm","note":"liquid trip: 10.0 cm"},{"t":"3.0 cm","note":"compound trip: 2.0 cm"}],"calc":["Rf = 2.0 cm ÷ 10.0 cm","Rf = **0.20**"]},{"text":"**Tempting slips:** 3.0 ÷ 11.0 = 0.27 forgets the start line; 3.0 ÷ 10.0 = 0.30 fixes only one; 5.0 is upside down. Rf can never be above 1.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"forget this → 0.27"},{"t":"3.0 cm","note":"must also lose 1.0 cm"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = compound distance ÷ solvent distance, both measured from the starting line (subtract the baseline). The answer is always between 0 and 1."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"2-Phenyl-2-butanol, shown, is heated with concentrated H₃PO₄, and the alkene products are distilled from the mixture as they form. Which structure is NOT expected among the products?",idStem:"Acid-catalyzed dehydration of an alcohol goes through a carbocation. Which statement about it is FALSE?",fig:"p1-2-phenyl-2-butanol",choiceFigs:["p1-e-2-phenyl-2-butene","p1-z-2-phenyl-2-butene","p1-2-phenyl-1-butene","p1-3-phenyl-1-butene"],choices:["E-2-phenyl-2-butene","Z-2-phenyl-2-butene","2-Phenyl-1-butene","3-Phenyl-1-butene"],ans:3,exp:"A tertiary alcohol in hot acid dehydrates by **E1**: –OH is protonated, water leaves, and a **flat tertiary (and benzylic) carbocation** forms at C2. An H⁺ is then lost from a carbon **next to C2**: from C3 (giving **both E and Z** 2-phenyl-2-butene, because the flat cation can lose either H) or from the methyl (giving 2-phenyl-1-butene, minor). **3-Phenyl-1-butene** puts the C=C between C1 and C2 of a chain that is not attached to the cationic carbon; it cannot form from this cation.",src:"pe1-cp:4",how:{"ask":"Where does the carbocation form, which carbons next to it carry hydrogens, and does a flat cation give one alkene geometry or both?","know":"**Acid-catalyzed (E1) dehydration:**\n• 1. H⁺ protonates –OH → –OH₂⁺, a good leaving group.\n• 2. Water leaves → **carbocation** where the OH was (slow step). 3° and benzylic cations are the most stable.\n• 3. A base removes an H⁺ from a carbon **directly bonded to C⁺**; the C=C forms between those two carbons.\n• The C⁺ is **flat (sp²)**, so the H can leave with the groups on either side: **E and Z alkenes both form**. The more substituted alkene is major (Zaitsev), but the less substituted one still forms.\n• E = higher-priority groups on opposite sides of C=C; Z = same side.","steps":[{"text":"Classify the alcohol: the C–OH carbon carries three carbon groups (ethyl, methyl, phenyl), so it is tertiary. Hot concentrated acid + 3° alcohol → E1 dehydration.","hl":"is heated with concentrated H₃PO₄","fig":"p1-2-phenyl-2-butanol"},{"text":"Write the mechanism. The cation sits on the carbon that held the OH.","calc":["R₃C–OH + H⁺ → R₃C–OH₂⁺","R₃C–OH₂⁺ → R₃**C⁺** + H₂O  (slow)","R₃C⁺ → C=C + H⁺, H taken from a carbon **next to C⁺**"]},{"text":"Find the neighbors of C⁺ that carry H, highlighted: the CH₂ of the ethyl group and the CH₃. The phenyl carbon next to C⁺ has no H to lose.","fig":"p1-phenylbutyl-cation"},{"text":"Losing H from the CH₂ gives 2-phenyl-2-butene. The cation is flat, so the H can leave from either face: both E and Z form.","calc":["H from CH₂ → C2=C3 → **E and Z** 2-phenyl-2-butene","H from CH₃ → C1=C2 → 2-phenyl-1-butene (**minor**, still forms)"],"strike":["E-2-phenyl-2-butene","Z-2-phenyl-2-butene","2-Phenyl-1-butene"]},{"text":"3-Phenyl-1-butene has its C=C two bonds away from the former C–OH carbon, and its phenyl sits on a CH. No H loss from this cation can give it.","fig":"p1-3-phenyl-1-butene"},{"text":"Answer: 3-phenyl-1-butene. Rule to reuse: an E1 alkene must have the C=C touching the carbocation carbon, and E1 gives both E and Z."}],"trap":"Z-2-phenyl-2-butene is tempting to pick as 'not formed' if you think the reaction gives one stereoisomer. E1 is not stereospecific: the flat cation gives both E and Z. 2-Phenyl-1-butene is the minor product, but minor is not the same as absent."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read a drawing like this.** Every corner and every line end is a **carbon atom (C)**. Hydrogen atoms stuck to carbons are not drawn (they are understood to be there). Other atoms are written as letters. The hexagon with three double lines is a **benzene ring**: six carbons in a loop.","fig":"p1-2-phenyl-2-butanol"},{"text":"**Find the OH.** \"OH\" is an oxygen atom bonded to a hydrogen atom, attached to one carbon. It is highlighted. This OH is the part of the molecule the reaction acts on. Remember which carbon it is attached to.","fig":"p1-lesson-oh"},{"text":"**What the acid does.** H₃PO₄ is an acid. An acid hands out **H⁺** (a hydrogen atom without its electron). The OH grabs one H⁺ and becomes **OH₂⁺**: that is a water molecule (H₂O) still attached, with a + charge.","fig":"p1-lesson-oxonium"},{"text":"**Water falls off.** Heating makes the water (H₂O) leave. It takes its two shared electrons with it. The carbon it was attached to is left with only **3 bonds and a + charge**. A carbon with a + charge is called a **carbocation** (carbo = carbon, cation = positive).","fig":"p1-lesson-cation"},{"text":"**A + carbon is unstable**: carbon is happiest with 4 bonds. The fix: a hydrogen on a carbon **right next door** leaves (as H⁺), and those two carbons share a second bond. Two carbons joined by two bonds = a **double bond (C=C)**. A molecule with a C=C is called an **alkene**.","fig":"p1-phenylbutyl-cation"},{"text":"**Check each next-door carbon.** The + carbon touches three carbons: the CH₂ of the two-carbon chain, the CH₃ (one carbon), and a carbon of the ring. The two highlighted ones have hydrogens they can give.","fig":"p1-phenylbutyl-cation"},{"text":"**Neighbor 1, the CH₂:** if it gives up an H, the double bond forms between the + carbon and the CH₂, in the middle of the chain. A middle double bond can be built two ways: the two outer CH₃ groups on the same side (called Z) or on opposite sides (called E). So you get **two** products.","fig":["p1-e-2-phenyl-2-butene","p1-z-2-phenyl-2-butene"]},{"text":"**Neighbor 2, the CH₃:** if it gives up an H, the double bond forms at the end of the chain. That is a third product.","fig":"p1-2-phenyl-1-butene"},{"text":"**Neighbor 3, the ring carbon:** in a benzene ring, that carbon has no hydrogen to give (its bonds are already used inside the ring). So no double bond can form toward the ring.","fig":"p1-lesson-ring-c"},{"text":"**The rule you just learned:** in this kind of reaction (acid + heat + an OH), every product has its double bond **touching the carbon that held the OH**. Now look at the four answer drawings. In each one, find the double bond and ask: does it touch that carbon? The one where it does not is the answer.","fig":"p1-2-phenyl-2-butanol"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Alanine, with its carboxyl group activated, is mixed with serine. The product forms with loss of one water molecule, and the only new C=O stretch in its IR spectrum appears near 1650 cm⁻¹. Which structure is the product?",idStem:"When two amino acids join through a peptide bond, which functional group forms?",choiceFigs:["p1-ala-ser-ester","p1-ala-ser","p1-ala-ser-anhydride","p1-ala-ser-dkp"],choices:["Ester-linked dimer","Amide-linked dipeptide","Mixed anhydride","Cyclic dipeptide"],ans:1,exp:"Joining alanine's carboxyl group to serine's **amino group** releases one H₂O and makes an **amide (peptide) bond**. Amide C=O stretches appear at **about 1630–1690 cm⁻¹**, lower than ester C=O (about 1735–1750) or the two anhydride C=O bands (about 1760 and 1820). The cyclic dipeptide is also an amide, but it needs **two** waters lost.",src:"pe1-cp:5",how:{"ask":"How many waters were lost, and which kind of C=O absorbs as low as 1650 cm⁻¹?","know":"• **Condensation**: two molecules join and release H₂O. Carboxyl + amine → **amide**; carboxyl + alcohol → **ester**; carboxyl + carboxyl → **anhydride**. Each loses one H₂O.\n• **IR C=O positions** (wavenumber, cm⁻¹): anhydride ≈ 1820 and 1760 (two bands); ester ≈ 1740; carboxylic acid ≈ 1710; ketone ≈ 1715; **amide ≈ 1650**. The N lone pair shares into C=O (resonance), weakening it, so amides absorb lowest.\n• Serine has two nucleophiles, its –NH₂ and its side-chain –OH, so both an amide and an ester are possible on paper.","steps":[{"text":"Count the water. One H₂O lost means one new link; the cyclic dipeptide closes a second ring bond and loses two.","hl":"loss of one water molecule","calc":["Ala C₃H₇NO₂ + Ser C₃H₇NO₃ = C₆H₁₄N₂O₅","− 1 H₂O → **C₆H₁₂N₂O₄**  (amide, ester and anhydride all fit)","− 2 H₂O → C₆H₁₀N₂O₃  (cyclic dipeptide)"],"strike":["Cyclic dipeptide"]},{"text":"The three remaining structures are isomers, so use the IR clue. Find where each new C=O would absorb.","hl":"near 1650 cm⁻¹","calc":["amide C=O ≈ **1650** cm⁻¹","ester C=O ≈ 1740 cm⁻¹","anhydride C=O ≈ 1760 **and** 1820 cm⁻¹"]},{"text":"An ester or an anhydride would put a new C=O band well above 1700 cm⁻¹.","strike":["Ester-linked dimer","Mixed anhydride"]},{"text":"The amide link, highlighted: C=O bonded to N–H. Alanine's carboxyl carbon is now joined to serine's nitrogen.","fig":"p1-ala-ser-amide"},{"text":"Answer: the amide-linked dipeptide. Rule to reuse: C=O near 1650 cm⁻¹ = amide; near 1740 = ester; two bands above 1750 = anhydride."}],"trap":"The ester-linked dimer has exactly the same formula as the dipeptide, because serine's –OH can attack too. Only the C=O position tells them apart: an ester C=O would appear near 1740 cm⁻¹, not 1650."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H's; letters are other atoms; = is a double bond. This is alanine.","fig":"t1-ala-read"},{"text":"**Alanine's COOH reacts.** \"Activated\" means its carbon is primed to be attacked and to lose its OH.","fig":"t1-ala-cooh"},{"text":"An attacker needs a **spare electron pair** to make a new bond. Serine has two candidates: the N of NH₂ and the O of its side-chain OH.","fig":"t1-ser-nh2-oh"},{"text":"**Each new link releases one water.** This ring has two new links, so it loses two waters, not one.","fig":"t1-ala-ser-dkp-hl"},{"text":"**IR:** each bond absorbs infrared light at its own number (cm⁻¹); a stiffer bond gives a higher number. An ester C=O sits near 1740.","fig":"t1-ala-ser-ester-hl"},{"text":"**Two C=O around one O** give two bands, near 1760 and 1820 cm⁻¹, both far above 1650.","fig":"t1-ala-ser-anhydride-hl"},{"text":"**N next door weakens the C=O:** it shares its spare pair, so the bond is less stiff and absorbs lower, near 1650.","fig":"t1-ala-ser-amide-ir"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count waters lost, then read the C=O: ≈1650 amide, ≈1740 ester, two bands above 1750 anhydride. In each drawing, ask what sits next to the new C=O.","fig":"t1-ala-ser-amide-ir"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Each alkyl bromide shown is added separately to a solution of sodium iodide in acetone at 25 °C, and the time until NaBr begins to precipitate is recorded. Which compound reacts fastest?",idStem:"By an SN2 path, a primary alcohol becomes an alkyl halide faster than a secondary alcohol does. Why?",choiceFigs:["p1-2-bromopropane","p1-1-bromobutane","p1-tert-butyl-bromide","p1-neopentyl-bromide"],choices:["2-Bromopropane","1-Bromobutane","2-Bromo-2-methylpropane","1-Bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane"],ans:1,exp:"I⁻ in acetone (a polar aprotic solvent) substitutes by **SN2**: one step, with I⁻ attacking the back of the C–Br carbon. The rate drops as that carbon gets more crowded: **methyl > 1° > 2° ≫ 3°**. 1-Bromobutane is primary with an unbranched neighbor, so it is fastest. 1-Bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane is also primary, but its neighboring C(CH₃)₃ blocks the back side.",src:"pe1-cp:6",how:{"ask":"Which mechanism do NaI and acetone favor, and in which structure is the back of the C–Br carbon least crowded?","know":"• **SN2**: Nu⁻ attacks the carbon bearing the leaving group from the side **opposite** the leaving group, in one step. Rate = k[R–X][Nu⁻].\n• Favored by a strong nucleophile (I⁻) and a **polar aprotic solvent** (acetone, DMSO, DMF), which leaves the anion unsolvated and reactive.\n• **Steric rule**: methyl > primary (1°) > secondary (2°) ≫ tertiary (3°, essentially no SN2).\n• Branching on the **next** carbon also blocks the back side: neopentyl bromide, (CH₃)₃C–CH₂Br, is 1° but reacts about 10⁵ times slower than other 1° bromides.\n• In acetone, NaI dissolves but NaBr does not, so precipitate appears as substitution occurs.","steps":[{"text":"Name the mechanism from the conditions: iodide, a strong nucleophile, in acetone, a polar aprotic solvent → SN2.","hl":"sodium iodide in acetone","calc":["R–Br + I⁻ → R–I + Br⁻ (one step)","rate = k[R–Br][I⁻]","Na⁺Br⁻ is insoluble in acetone → **precipitate** marks the reaction"]},{"text":"In SN2, I⁻ must reach the back of the C–Br carbon. Count the carbons attached to that carbon in each structure.","calc":["2-bromo-2-methylpropane: **3** C → 3°, no SN2","2-bromopropane: **2** C → 2°, slow","1-bromobutane: **1** C → 1°","1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane: **1** C → 1°, but that C carries 3 CH₃"]},{"text":"Tertiary and secondary carbons are crowded at the back side.","strike":["2-Bromo-2-methylpropane","2-Bromopropane"]},{"text":"Compare the two primary bromides, C–Br carbon highlighted. In the neopentyl compound, the highlighted neighbor C(CH₃)₃ sits right where I⁻ must enter.","fig":["p1-1-bromobutane-hl","p1-neopentyl-bromide-hl"],"strike":["1-Bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-bromobutane. Rule to reuse: for SN2, look at the C–X carbon and the carbon next to it; the fewer branches on both, the faster."}],"trap":"Picking 2-bromo-2-methylpropane comes from thinking of SN1, where tertiary is fastest. NaI in acetone is an SN2 test, and a tertiary carbon cannot be attacked from the back. Also check the carbon next door: neopentyl bromide is primary on paper but behaves as if blocked."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** every corner and line end is a carbon; hydrogens on carbon are hidden. This is a 4-carbon chain with Br on the end.","fig":"t1-bb-read"},{"text":"**The swap:** I⁻ (a negative iodine) replaces Br. NaBr doesn't dissolve in acetone, so the cloudiness shows how fast the swap went.","fig":"t1-1-iodobutane"},{"text":"**Nucleophile** = an atom with a spare electron pair that grabs an electron-poor atom. I⁻ is one; it targets the carbon holding Br.","fig":"t1-bb-target"},{"text":"**SN2:** one step. I⁻ must enter from the side directly opposite Br, so that back side must be open.","fig":"t1-bb-backside"},{"text":"**Count carbons touching the C–Br carbon:** 1 = primary, 2 = secondary, 3 = tertiary. Each one blocks the back side more.","fig":["t1-2-bromopropane-hl","t1-tbu-br-hl"]},{"text":"**Tertiary: no SN2.** Three CH₃ groups wall off the back side. Secondary works, but slower than primary.","fig":"t1-tbu-br-block"},{"text":"**Look one carbon further.** Both are primary, but in one the next carbon holds three CH₃ groups that hang over the back side.","fig":["t1-bb-primary","t1-neo-primary"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 is fastest when the C–X carbon and its neighbor are least crowded (1° > 2° ≫ 3°). In each drawing, count carbons on and next to the C–Br carbon.","fig":"t1-bb-primary"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"3-Methyl-2-butanol, shown, is heated with concentrated HBr. Two alkyl bromides and two alkenes are isolated. Which product could not have formed unless a hydrogen or methyl group first moved within the intermediate?",idStem:"An alcohol forms a tertiary carbocation in HCl. Which product could form ONLY after a rearrangement?",fig:"p1-3-methyl-2-butanol",choiceFigs:["p1-2-bromo-3-methylbutane","p1-3-methyl-1-butene","p1-2-bromo-2-methylbutane","p1-2-methyl-2-butene"],choices:["2-Bromo-3-methylbutane","3-Methyl-1-butene","2-Bromo-2-methylbutane","2-Methyl-2-butene"],ans:2,exp:"Water leaves C2, giving a **2° carbocation at C2**. Br⁻ adding to C2 gives 2-bromo-3-methylbutane, and losing H⁺ from C1 or C3 gives 3-methyl-1-butene or 2-methyl-2-butene, all directly. **Br on C3** requires a **1,2-hydride shift** from C3 to C2 first, which turns the 2° cation into a more stable **3° cation at C3**.",src:"pe1-cp:7",how:{"ask":"Where does the first carbocation form, and which product has its new group on a different carbon from that one?","know":"• Alcohol + HX: –OH is protonated, water leaves, and a **carbocation** forms on the carbon that held the OH.\n• Direct products: X⁻ adds to that carbon (substitution), or H⁺ leaves from a **neighboring** carbon (alkene with C=C touching that carbon).\n• **Rearrangement**: an H (1,2-hydride shift) or CH₃ (1,2-methyl shift) on the next carbon moves over with its bonding pair, so the + charge moves to that next carbon. It happens when the new cation is more stable (2° → 3°).\n• Clue in a product: the new group sits on a carbon that never held the OH.","steps":[{"text":"Hot HBr with a 2° alcohol: –OH is protonated, water leaves C2, and a 2° cation forms there. Its neighbor C3, highlighted, carries one H.","hl":"3-Methyl-2-butanol, shown, is heated with concentrated HBr","fig":"p1-2-cation-hl","calc":["CH₃–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)₂ + H⁺ → CH₃–CH(OH₂⁺)–CH(CH₃)₂","→ CH₃–**C⁺H**–CH(CH₃)₂ + H₂O  (2° at C2)"]},{"text":"List what the C2 cation gives directly: Br⁻ on C2, or H⁺ lost from a neighbor (C1 or C3).","calc":["Br⁻ at C2 → 2-bromo-3-methylbutane","lose H from C1 → C1=C2 → 3-methyl-1-butene","lose H from C3 → C2=C3 → **2-methyl-2-butene**"],"strike":["2-Bromo-3-methylbutane","3-Methyl-1-butene","2-Methyl-2-butene"]},{"text":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane has Br on C3 of the original chain, a carbon that never held the OH. That needs the C3 hydrogen to shift to C2, leaving a 3° cation, highlighted.","fig":"p1-3-cation-hl","calc":["2° C⁺ at C2 → 1,2-H shift → **3° C⁺** at C3","3° C⁺ + Br⁻ → 2-bromo-2-methylbutane"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. Rule to reuse: products with the new group on the OH carbon or a C=C touching it form directly; a group on any other carbon signals a shift."}],"trap":"2-Methyl-2-butene looks like it came from the rearranged 3° cation, and it can, but it also forms directly when the 2° cation at C2 loses H⁺ from C3. A product that has any direct route is not the answer."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H's. The chain is numbered C1–C4; C3 also carries a CH₃ branch.","fig":"t1-mb-num"},{"text":"**Acid gives H⁺** (a hydrogen with no electron). The OH grabs it and becomes OH₂⁺: a water molecule, still attached.","fig":"t1-mb-oxonium"},{"text":"**Water leaves**, taking its electrons. C2 keeps 3 bonds and a + charge: a carbocation. It touches 2 carbons: secondary.","fig":"t1-mb-cation2"},{"text":"**Fix 1:** Br⁻ (negative, with spare electron pairs) bonds to the + carbon. Br lands right where the OH was.","fig":"t1-mb-br-c2"},{"text":"**Fix 2:** a neighbor loses H⁺ and makes a double bond (C=C) with C2. Both possible C=C bonds touch C2.","fig":["t1-mb-ene1","t1-mb-ene2"]},{"text":"**Fix 3:** a + carbon touched by more carbons is more stable. C3's H can slide to C2, taking its electron pair.","fig":"t1-mb-shift"},{"text":"**Then Br⁻ adds at old C3**, a carbon that never held the OH. The product's name renumbers the chain, so trust the drawing.","fig":["t1-mb-c3cation","t1-mb-br-c3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a new group on the OH carbon, or a C=C touching it, forms directly; on any other carbon, something moved first. Check each drawing.","fig":"t1-mb-num"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"A mixture of four compounds is injected into a gas chromatograph with a nonpolar column at 120 °C. Molar masses: 1-butanol, 74 g/mol; 1-butene, 56 g/mol; 1-chlorobutane, 93 g/mol; 1-bromobutane, 137 g/mol. Which compound reaches the detector first, and why?",idStem:"An alcohol, an alkene, an alkyl chloride and an alkyl bromide of similar size are injected into a gas chromatograph. Which comes out first?",choices:["1-butanol, since it is the most polar","1-butene, since it is the most volatile","1-bromobutane, since it is the most massive","1-chlorobutane, since it is the least polar"],ans:1,exp:"In GC, the compound with the **lowest boiling point (most volatile)** spends the most time in the gas and exits first. 1-Butene has only weak London forces and the lowest mass, so it elutes first; 1-butanol, which hydrogen bonds, elutes last.",src:"pe1-cp:8",how:{"ask":"In gas chromatography, what makes a compound spend more time moving in the gas than sitting on the column?","know":"**Gas chromatography (GC)**: the sample is vaporized and carried by a gas through a column. Compounds that stay in the gas phase move fastest.\n• **Volatility** = how easily a compound becomes gas; high volatility = low boiling point.\n• Boiling point rises with stronger intermolecular forces: London (dispersion, grows with mass/size) < dipole–dipole < hydrogen bonding.","steps":[{"text":"In GC the mobile phase is a gas. A compound that boils easily spends more time in the gas and reaches the detector first.","hl":"injected into a gas chromatograph with a nonpolar column at 120 °C"},{"text":"Rank intermolecular forces: 1-butanol has hydrogen bonding; the haloalkanes have dipoles and heavy, polarizable halogens; 1-butene has only weak London forces and the smallest mass.","hl":"1-butene, 56 g/mol","calc":["1-butanol: H-bonding + dipole + London","1-bromobutane, 1-chlorobutane: dipole + large London","1-butene: **London only, smallest mass**"]},{"text":"Weakest forces → lowest boiling point → most volatile. The boiling points confirm it.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Boiling points (°C)","x":{"label":"Compound"},"y":{"label":"Boiling point (°C)","min":-20,"max":140,"step":20},"categories":["1-butene","1-chlorobutane","1-bromobutane","1-butanol"],"series":[{"name":"bp","values":[-6,78,102,118]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["1-butene","bp"]]}},{"text":"Polarity and mass both RAISE the boiling point, so 1-butanol and 1-bromobutane come out late, not first.","strike":["1-butanol, since it is the most polar","1-bromobutane, since it is the most massive"]},{"text":"1-Chlorobutane is not the least polar compound; 1-butene is. Answer: 1-butene, since it is the most volatile.","strike":["1-chlorobutane, since it is the least polar"]}],"trap":"'1-butanol, since it is the most polar' reverses the logic: polarity and hydrogen bonding raise the boiling point, so 1-butanol comes out LAST."},teach:[{"text":"First, **what is the setup?** A machine separates a mixture of four compounds while they travel through a hot tube.","marks":[{"t":"gas chromatograph","note":"the separating machine"},{"t":"nonpolar column","note":"the long tube"},{"t":"120 °C","note":"tube is hot"}]},{"text":"**Gas chromatography (GC):** the sample is turned into gas and pushed down the tube. A molecule moves only while it is a gas; when it condenses or sticks, it waits.","marks":[{"t":"injected","note":"sample vaporized here"},{"t":"reaches the detector first","note":"the finish line"}]},{"text":"**Volatile** = turns into gas easily = **low boiling point**. The most volatile compound spends the most time as gas, so it finishes first.","marks":[{"t":"reaches the detector first","note":"= lowest boiling point"}]},{"text":"**Intermolecular forces** = the 'glue' between molecules. Stronger glue → higher boiling point. Strongest here: **hydrogen bonds** from an O–H group.","marks":[{"t":"1-butanol, 74 g/mol","note":"O–H: hydrogen bonds"}],"calc":["glue strength: London < dipole < **H-bond**"]},{"text":"**Haloalkanes** have a polar C–halogen bond (a dipole) and a heavy halogen atom. Heavier, bigger molecules have stronger **London (dispersion)** glue.","marks":[{"t":"1-chlorobutane, 93 g/mol","note":"C–Cl dipole + London"},{"t":"1-bromobutane, 137 g/mol","note":"heaviest: big London glue"}]},{"text":"**1-Butene** has no O–H and no halogen: only weak London glue, and it is the **lightest**. Weakest glue of all.","marks":[{"t":"1-butene, 56 g/mol","note":"lightest, London only"}]},{"text":"**The boiling points confirm it.** 1-Butene boils below 0 °C, far below the 120 °C tube, so it races through as gas.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Boiling points of the four compounds","x":{"label":"Compound"},"y":{"label":"Boiling point (°C)","min":-20,"max":140,"step":20},"categories":["1-butene","1-chlorobutane","1-bromobutane","1-butanol"],"series":[{"name":"bp","values":[-6,78,102,118]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["1-butene","bp"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** being polar or heavy RAISES the boiling point, so those compounds come out late, not first. And the least polar compound is the alkene, not chlorobutane.","marks":[{"t":"1-butanol, 74 g/mol","note":"most polar → last out"},{"t":"1-bromobutane, 137 g/mol","note":"most massive → late"},{"t":"1-chlorobutane, 93 g/mol","note":"polar C–Cl: not least"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in GC, order of exit = order of boiling point. Weakest intermolecular forces (no H-bonding, small mass) → most volatile → reaches the detector first."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"(S)-2-Bromopentane, shown, reacts with sodium hydrosulfide, NaSH, in acetone. The rate law is rate = k[2-bromopentane][HS⁻]. Which structure is the major product?",idStem:"An (R) stereocenter undergoes SN2 substitution, and the new group takes the leaving group's priority. What configuration results?",fig:"p1-2-bromopentane-s",choiceFigs:["p1-pentane-2-thiol-s","p1-pentane-2-thiol-r","p1-pentane-1-thiol","p1-e-2-pentene"],choices:["S-pentane-2-thiol","R-pentane-2-thiol","Pentane-1-thiol","E-2-pentene"],ans:1,exp:"A rate law first order in both reactants means **SN2**: HS⁻ attacks C2 from the side opposite Br, and the other three groups flip (**inversion**). SH takes Br's place as the highest-priority group (SH > propyl > methyl > H, as Br > propyl > methyl > H did), so inverting the 3D arrangement turns **S into R**: (R)-pentane-2-thiol.",src:"pe1-cp:9",how:{"ask":"What does the rate law say about the mechanism, what does that mechanism do to the arrangement at C2, and does the new group keep the old group's priority rank?","know":"• **Rate law → mechanism**: rate = k[substrate][Nu] → bimolecular → **SN2**; rate = k[substrate] → SN1.\n• **SN2**: the nucleophile enters opposite the leaving group in one step; the other three groups flip like an umbrella in the wind (**inversion**). No carbocation, so no racemization.\n• **R/S**: rank the four groups by atomic number of the first atom (S 16, Br 35, C 6, H 1); point the lowest (H) away; 1 → 2 → 3 clockwise = R, counterclockwise = S.\n• If the incoming group has the same rank as the leaving group, inversion switches the label (S → R). If ranks change, assign it again.\n• HS⁻ is a strong nucleophile but a weak base, so substitution beats elimination.","steps":[{"text":"Read the rate law: first order in each reactant → bimolecular, one step → SN2.","hl":"rate = k[2-bromopentane][HS⁻]","calc":["rate = k[RBr][HS⁻] → **SN2**","one step, no carbocation → no racemization, no shifts"]},{"text":"Find the stereocenter, highlighted: C2 carries Br, CH₃, propyl and H. HS⁻ enters from the side opposite Br, and the arrangement inverts.","fig":"p1-2-bromopentane-s-hl"},{"text":"Check whether the priority order changes when SH replaces Br.","calc":["before: **Br** (35) > propyl > methyl > H","after: **SH** (16) > propyl > methyl > H","same rank order + inverted arrangement → S becomes **R**"]},{"text":"Retention would need attack from the Br side. Moving SH to C1 or forming an alkene does not match an SN2 rate law with a strong, weakly basic nucleophile.","strike":["S-pentane-2-thiol","Pentane-1-thiol","E-2-pentene"]},{"text":"Answer: R-pentane-2-thiol. Rule to reuse: SN2 = inversion at the same carbon; the label flips when the new group takes the old group's priority rank.","fig":"p1-pentane-2-thiol-r"}],"trap":"S-pentane-2-thiol keeps the starting label: that would require front-side attack (retention), which SN2 never gives. Pentane-1-thiol puts SH on the wrong carbon: SN2 replaces the leaving group exactly where it was."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** corners are carbons with hidden H's. Wedge = toward you, dash = away. A carbon with 4 different groups gets an R or S name.","fig":"t1-bp-read"},{"text":"**rate = k[RBr][HS⁻]:** the speed depends on both, so they meet in one step. That one-step swap is called SN2.","fig":"t1-bp-sn2"},{"text":"HS⁻ has spare electron pairs and bonds to **the same carbon** Br leaves. It's a weak base, so no C=C forms.","fig":"t1-bp-leave"},{"text":"**Inversion:** HS⁻ enters opposite Br, and the other three groups flip like an umbrella in wind: the 3D arrangement becomes its mirror image.","fig":"t1-bp-backside"},{"text":"**Naming:** rank groups by atomic number (hidden H is 4th). With H away, 1→2→3 clockwise = R, counterclockwise = S. This start is S.","fig":"t1-bp-rank"},{"text":"**Same ranking after the swap:** S (16) still beats C (6). Same ranks + flipped arrangement means **S becomes R**.","fig":"t1-thiol-r-rank"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 = same carbon, inverted; if the new group keeps the old rank, R/S flips. In each drawing, check SH's carbon, any C=C, and the wedge.","fig":"t1-bp-sn2"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Benzoic acid is heated with methanol enriched in ¹⁸O, with a trace of H₂SO₄ as catalyst. The ester is purified and analyzed by mass spectrometry. Which structure shows where the ¹⁸O is found?",idStem:"To find out which reactant loses its –OH when a carboxylic acid and an alcohol form an ester, which label works best?",fig:["p1-benzoic-acid","p1-methanol-18o"],choiceFigs:["p1-mb-18o-bridge","p1-mb-18o-carbonyl","p1-mb-unlabeled","p1-mb-18o-both"],choices:["Label in the bridging O","Label in the C=O oxygen","No label in the ester","Label in both oxygens"],ans:0,exp:"In acid-catalyzed (Fischer) esterification, the **alcohol oxygen is the nucleophile**: CH₃–¹⁸OH attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon of the acid. Water then leaves from the **acid's** former –OH. So the ¹⁸O stays bonded to the CH₃ as the **bridging (–O–) oxygen** of methyl benzoate, and the water released is unlabeled.",src:"pe1-cp:10",how:{"ask":"Which molecule supplies the nucleophile that attacks the C=O carbon, and which molecule gives up the –OH that leaves as water?","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O, catalyzed by acid.\n• Mechanism (nucleophilic acyl substitution): 1) H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic. 2) The **alcohol O** attacks that carbon → tetrahedral intermediate. 3) Proton shuffles turn one of the acid's –OH groups into –OH₂⁺. 4) **Water leaves from the acid side**; C=O re-forms.\n• So the alcohol keeps its oxygen (it becomes the –O– between C=O and R′), and the acid loses one of its oxygens.\n• An isotope label marks an atom: wherever the labeled atom ends up shows which bonds broke.","steps":[{"text":"Mark where the label starts: on methanol's oxygen. Only one oxygen per methanol is labeled.","hl":"methanol enriched in ¹⁸O"},{"text":"Walk the mechanism and follow that oxygen.","calc":["PhCOOH + H⁺ → PhC(OH)₂⁺  (C=O protonated)","CH₃–**¹⁸O**H attacks the C → tetrahedral intermediate","an acid –OH becomes –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O (**unlabeled**)","C=O re-forms → PhC(=O)–**¹⁸O**–CH₃"]},{"text":"The ¹⁸O never left the CH₃ group, highlighted: it is the oxygen between C=O and CH₃.","fig":"p1-mb-18o-bridge-hl"},{"text":"An unlabeled ester would need methanol's C–O bond to break. A labeled C=O would need the alcohol oxygen to become the carbonyl oxygen, and it stays bonded to CH₃. Two labels would need a second ¹⁸O that the acid never had.","strike":["No label in the ester","Label in the C=O oxygen","Label in both oxygens"]},{"text":"Answer: the label is in the bridging O. Rule to reuse: in esterification, the alcohol keeps its O; the acid loses its OH as water."}],"trap":"'No label in the ester' assumes the alcohol loses its –OH, as an alcohol does in SN1/SN2 with HX. In esterification the C–O bond of methanol never breaks; the acid's C–OH bond does."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** hexagon = benzene ring (6 carbons). ¹⁸O is oxygen with 2 extra neutrons: same chemistry, heavier, a tag mass spec can find.","fig":["t1-bz-read","t1-meoh-read"]},{"text":"**Follow the tag.** Keep watching this O–CH₃ bond through every step.","fig":"t1-meoh-18o-hl"},{"text":"**The acid's H⁺** sticks to the C=O oxygen, making that carbon even more electron-poor: a better target.","fig":"t1-bzoh-protonated"},{"text":"**Methanol's ¹⁸O** uses a spare electron pair to bond to that carbon. The carbon now holds three oxygens.","fig":"t1-tet-int"},{"text":"**Water leaves from the acid side.** An original acid OH picks up H⁺ and departs, so the water is untagged.","fig":"t1-tet-oh2"},{"text":"**The C=O forms again** from the acid's last oxygen. The tag sits between C=O and CH₃: the O–CH₃ bond never broke.","fig":"t1-mb-bridge"},{"text":"**Test the alternatives:** a tagged C=O or a tag-free ester would need methanol's O–CH₃ bond to break; two tags need a second ¹⁸O.","fig":"t1-mb-carbonyl-x"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in acid-catalyzed ester formation the alcohol keeps its oxygen; the acid loses one as water. In each drawing, ask: is ¹⁸O still on CH₃?","fig":"t1-mb-bridge"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"In a model of the eye, a converging lens sits 2.0 cm in front of a screen that acts as the retina. A sign is 100 cm in front of the lens. What lens power is needed to form a sharp image of the sign on the screen? (Thin lens: 1/f = 1/o + 1/i)",idStem:"An eye's lens sits 2.0 cm from the retina and focuses an object 1.0 m away. About what is the lens strength? (1/f = 1/o + 1/i)",choices:["+0.51 D","+49 D","+51 D","−51 D"],ans:2,exp:"**Power (diopters) = 1/f with f in meters.** 1/f = 1/1.00 m + 1/0.020 m = 1 + 50 = **+51 D**. A real image on the retina needs a converging (positive) lens.",src:"pe1-cp:11",how:{"ask":"Find 1/f from the object and image distances, making sure the distances are in the unit that diopters require.","know":"• **Thin lens equation**: 1/f = 1/o + 1/i, where o = object distance, i = image distance, f = focal length.\n• **Power P = 1/f**, measured in **diopters (D) = m⁻¹**, so f must be in meters.\n• Converging lenses have f > 0 (positive power); diverging lenses have f < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the distances: object distance o = 100 cm, image distance i = 2.0 cm (the image must land on the screen).","hl":"a converging lens sits 2.0 cm in front of a screen"},{"text":"Power in diopters needs distances in METERS.","hl":"A sign is 100 cm in front of the lens","calc":["o = 100 cm = **1.00 m**","i = 2.0 cm = **0.020 m**"]},{"text":"Apply the thin-lens equation; P = 1/f.","calc":["P = 1/f = 1/o + 1/i","P = 1/1.00 m + 1/0.020 m","P = 1 + 50 = **51 m⁻¹ = +51 D**"]},{"text":"Eliminate: +0.51 D uses centimeters; +49 D subtracts instead of adding; a real image on a screen needs a converging lens, so the power is positive.","strike":["+0.51 D","+49 D","−51 D"]},{"text":"Answer: +51 D."}],"trap":"+0.51 D comes from using centimeters (1/100 + 1/2 = 0.51 cm⁻¹). Diopters are per METER, so convert first. +49 D comes from subtracting 1/o instead of adding it."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it measures.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm","note":"lens → screen distance"},{"t":"100 cm","note":"sign → lens distance"},{"t":"lens power","note":"what we must find"}]},{"text":"**A converging lens** bends light rays inward so they meet at one point. Where they meet, a **sharp image** forms. Here that point must land on the screen.","marks":[{"t":"converging lens","note":"bends rays together"},{"t":"sharp image","note":"rays meet on the screen"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation piece by piece.** Each letter is a distance from the lens.","marks":[{"t":"1/f","note":"f = focal length"},{"t":"1/o","note":"o = object distance"},{"t":"1/i","note":"i = image distance"}]},{"text":"**Match the numbers to the letters.** The sign is the object; the image must form on the screen.","marks":[{"t":"A sign is 100 cm","note":"o = 100 cm"},{"t":"2.0 cm in front of a screen","note":"i = 2.0 cm"}]},{"text":"**Lens power P = 1/f**, measured in **diopters, written D**. A diopter is 1 ÷ meters, so every distance must be in **meters**.","marks":[{"t":"lens power","note":"P = 1/f, in diopters"}],"calc":["P = 1/f = 1/o + 1/i","1 D = 1 m⁻¹"]},{"text":"**Convert cm to m** (divide by 100).","marks":[{"t":"100 cm","note":"= 1.00 m"},{"t":"2.0 cm","note":"= 0.020 m"}],"calc":["o = 100 cm ÷ 100 = **1.00 m**","i = 2.0 cm ÷ 100 = **0.020 m**"]},{"text":"**Plug in and add.** The tiny screen distance makes almost all the power.","marks":[{"t":"1/o","note":"1/1.00 m = 1"},{"t":"1/i","note":"1/0.020 m = 50"}],"calc":["P = 1/1.00 + 1/0.020","P = 1 + 50 = **51 D**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"The two pieces of the lens power","x":{"label":"Term"},"y":{"label":"Contribution (D)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"categories":["1/o (sign, 1.00 m)","1/i (screen, 0.020 m)"],"series":[{"name":"D","values":[1,50]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["1/i (screen, 0.020 m)","D"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** +0.51 D uses centimeters; +49 D subtracts instead of adding; a negative power is a diverging lens, which cannot put a real image on a screen.","marks":[{"t":"converging lens","note":"converging → power is +"},{"t":"1/o + 1/i","note":"add, don't subtract"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** power in diopters = 1/f = 1/o + 1/i with all distances in **meters**. Converging lens → positive power."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"The figure shows the free energy of a reacting system along the reaction coordinate, without and with an enzyme, at 37 °C. Which conclusion is supported by the figure?",idStem:"How does an enzyme speed up a reaction?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Free energy along the reaction coordinate, 37 °C","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,45],[5,80],[7,25],[9,-25],[10,-25]]},{"name":"With enzyme","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,25],[5,45],[7,5],[9,-25],[10,-25]]}]},choices:["The enzyme lowers Ea by 35 kJ/mol; ΔG° stays −25 kJ/mol","The enzyme lowers Ea by 35 kJ/mol; ΔG° becomes more negative","The enzyme lowers ΔG° by 35 kJ/mol; Ea stays 80 kJ/mol","The enzyme lowers Ea to 35 kJ/mol; ΔG° stays −25 kJ/mol"],ans:0,exp:"**Ea = peak − reactants**: 80 − 0 = 80 kJ/mol without the enzyme, 45 − 0 = 45 kJ/mol with it, so Ea falls **by 35 kJ/mol**. **ΔG° = products − reactants** = −25 − 0 = −25 kJ/mol on **both** curves: an enzyme speeds the reaction but does not change where equilibrium lies.",src:"pe1-cp:12",how:{"ask":"On each curve, which two points give Ea, and which two give ΔG°? Do the two curves differ at both, or only at one?","know":"**Reaction coordinate diagram:**\n• Left plateau = reactants; right plateau = products; highest point = transition state.\n• **Activation energy (Ea)** = transition-state energy − reactant energy. Lower Ea → faster reaction.\n• **ΔG°** = product energy − reactant energy. Negative → products favored at equilibrium.\n• **A catalyst (enzyme)** gives a lower path over the hill: it lowers Ea for both directions, but the start and end points do not move, so ΔG° and K stay the same.","steps":[{"text":"Read the start and end of the curves: both begin at 0 and finish at −25 kJ/mol.","graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"start"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"finish"}],"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":0,"to":-25,"text":"ΔG = −25 (both)"}]},"calc":["ΔG° = products − reactants = −25 − 0 = **−25 kJ/mol**","same on both curves → ΔG° **unchanged**"]},{"text":"Wrong choices that change ΔG° are out.","strike":["The enzyme lowers Ea by 35 kJ/mol; ΔG° becomes more negative","The enzyme lowers ΔG° by 35 kJ/mol; Ea stays 80 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Now read the two peaks, the transition states.","graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"spans":[{"x":4.4,"from":0,"to":80,"text":"Ea = 80","side":"left"},{"x":5.6,"from":0,"to":45,"text":"Ea = 45"}]},"calc":["Ea uncatalyzed = 80 − 0 = **80 kJ/mol**","Ea with enzyme = 45 − 0 = **45 kJ/mol**","decrease = 80 − 45 = **35 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"The new Ea is 45 kJ/mol; 35 is the amount it dropped, not its new value.","strike":["The enzyme lowers Ea to 35 kJ/mol; ΔG° stays −25 kJ/mol"],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"spans":[{"x":5.2,"from":45,"to":80,"text":"drop = 35"}]}},{"text":"Answer: Ea falls by 35 kJ/mol and ΔG° stays −25 kJ/mol. Rule to reuse: a catalyst lowers the hill, never the valley-to-valley difference."}],"trap":"'Lowers Ea to 35' confuses the drop with the new value: the catalyzed peak is 45 kJ/mol above the reactants, 35 less than before. And an enzyme never changes ΔG°; the two curves start and end at the same heights."},teach:[{"text":"**Up and down = free energy.** Higher on the graph = the molecules hold more energy at that moment.","marks":[{"t":"free energy","note":"up-down axis"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = the reaction happening.** Left edge = before, right edge = after.","marks":[{"t":"reaction coordinate","note":"left → right progress"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two curves, same reaction.** An **enzyme** is a biological helper (catalyst) that speeds a reaction without being used up.","marks":[{"t":"without and with an enzyme","note":"the two curves"}],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"labels":[{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":3,"text":"● no enzyme","at":"left"},{"series":"With enzyme","x":7,"text":"□ with enzyme","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Flat left = reactants** (start). **Flat right = products** (finish). **Top of the hill = transition state**, the hardest moment to get through.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products"}],"labels":[{"series":"Uncatalyzed","x":5,"text":"transition state","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Activation energy (Ea)** = height of the hill, from the start level up to the peak. Measure both hills.","calc":["no enzyme: 80 − 0 = **80 kJ/mol**","with enzyme: 45 − 0 = **45 kJ/mol**"],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"spans":[{"x":4.4,"from":0,"to":80,"text":"Ea = 80","side":"left"},{"x":5.6,"from":0,"to":45,"text":"Ea = 45"}]}},{"text":"**How much did the hill drop?** 80 − 45 = 35. Careful: 35 is the **drop**; the new Ea is 45, not 35.","calc":["80 − 45 = **35 kJ/mol lower**"],"graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"],"spans":[{"x":5.2,"from":45,"to":80,"text":"drop = 35"}]}},{"text":"**ΔG° = finish minus start.** Below the start means energy is released (negative). Read it on both curves.","calc":["ΔG° = −25 − 0 = **−25 kJ/mol**","same on both curves → **unchanged**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"start = 0"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"finish = −25"}],"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":0,"to":-25,"text":"ΔG° = −25 (both)"}]}},{"text":"**Why ΔG° can't change:** the enzyme only lowers the hill. Start and finish sit at the same heights, so it does not make the reaction more favorable.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"With enzyme","x":0,"text":"same start: 0","at":"above"},{"series":"With enzyme","x":10,"text":"same finish: −25","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst lowers the hill (Ea) but never the start-to-finish difference (ΔG°). Ea = peak − start; ΔG° = finish − start.","graph":{"focus":["Uncatalyzed","With enzyme"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Consider the data for 1.0 M acetic acid at 25 °C: Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵; percent ionization ≈ 0.42%. Which of the following changes would decrease the percent ionization of the acetic acid?",idStem:"Which change LOWERS the percent ionization of 1.0 M acetic acid?",choices:["Adding water, since dilution lowers [CH₃COOH]","Adding NaOH, since OH⁻ removes H⁺ from solution","Adding HCl, since the added H⁺ shifts the equilibrium left","Using ClCH₂COOH, since Cl stabilizes the anion"],ans:2,exp:"CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻. Adding a strong acid adds **H⁺, a common ion**, which by **Le Châtelier** pushes the equilibrium **left**, so less of the acetic acid ionizes. Dilution, removing H⁺ with base, and a chlorine substituent all increase ionization.",src:"pe1-cp:13",how:{"ask":"For each change, decide whether the acid ionizes more or less; you want the one that makes it ionize less.","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] formed ÷ starting [HA] × 100%.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product shifts an equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product shifts it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: adding an ion already in the equilibrium (here H⁺ or CH₃COO⁻) suppresses ionization.\n• Dilution increases percent ionization of a weak acid.\n• An electron-withdrawing Cl stabilizes the conjugate base → stronger acid → more ionization.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium and the quantity asked about: percent ionization = [A⁻] ÷ initial [HA] × 100.","hl":"percent ionization ≈ 0.42%","calc":["CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻","x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.0) = 4.2 × 10⁻³ M","% = 4.2 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.0 × 100 = **0.42%**"]},{"text":"Dilution: fewer particles per liter favors the side with more particles (the ions), so a larger fraction ionizes.","calc":["at 0.10 M: x = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.3 × 10⁻³ M","% = 1.3 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.10 × 100 = **1.3%**  (up)"],"strike":["Adding water, since dilution lowers [CH₃COOH]"]},{"text":"NaOH removes H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Adding NaOH, since OH⁻ removes H⁺ from solution"]},{"text":"Chloroacetic acid is a different, stronger acid (Ka ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻³): its Cl stabilizes the anion, so it ionizes MORE.","strike":["Using ClCH₂COOH, since Cl stabilizes the anion"]},{"text":"HCl adds H⁺, a common ion, so the equilibrium shifts left.","calc":["with 0.10 M HCl: x = Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.0 ÷ 0.10","x = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M → **0.018%**  (down)"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Percent ionization of the acid","x":{"label":"Condition"},"y":{"label":"% ionized","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["1.0 M CH₃COOH","+ 0.10 M HCl","diluted to 0.10 M","1.0 M ClCH₂COOH"],"series":[{"name":"% ionized","values":[0.42,0.018,1.3,3.7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["+ 0.10 M HCl","% ionized"]]}},{"text":"Answer: adding HCl, since the added H⁺ shifts the equilibrium left (the common-ion effect)."}],"trap":"Each reason given is chemically true, so check the DIRECTION. 'Adding water' sounds like it should weaken the acid, but dilution lowers [H⁺] while increasing the fraction ionized."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 M acetic acid","note":"starting amount of acid"},{"t":"Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵","note":"tiny → weak acid"},{"t":"percent ionization ≈ 0.42%","note":"share that splits apart"}]},{"text":"**Ionize = split apart.** Acetic acid (vinegar) can split into H⁺ and acetate. The ⇌ arrow means it goes **both ways**: an equilibrium, a balance.","marks":[{"t":"acetic acid","note":"CH₃COOH, a weak acid"}],"calc":["CH₃COOH ⇌ H⁺ + CH₃COO⁻","left = acid whole; right = split pieces"]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** = how many split ÷ how many you started with × 100. Only 42 out of every 10,000 split.","marks":[{"t":"percent ionization ≈ 0.42%","note":"42 in 10,000 split"}],"calc":["split [H⁺] = √(Ka × C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.0)","[H⁺] = **4.2 × 10⁻³ M**","4.2 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.0 × 100 = **0.42%**"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier's principle:** add something to one side and the balance shifts **away** from it; remove something and it shifts **toward** it. To decrease ionization we need a shift LEFT.","marks":[{"t":"decrease the percent ionization","note":"need shift LEFT"}]},{"text":"**Add HCl** (a strong acid): it dumps in extra H⁺, a piece already on the right side (a **common ion**). The balance shifts left, so less acetic acid splits.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵","note":"used: x = Ka·C ÷ [H⁺]"}],"calc":["with 0.10 M HCl: x = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.0 ÷ 0.10","x = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M → **0.018%**  (down)"]},{"text":"**Adding water** spreads things out; the side with more pieces (the right) is favored, so a **larger** share splits.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 M","note":"dilute to 0.10 M"}],"calc":["x = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.10) = 1.3 × 10⁻³ M","1.3 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.10 × 100 = **1.3%**  (up)"]},{"text":"**Adding NaOH:** OH⁻ grabs H⁺ to make water, removing a right-side piece. The balance shifts right: **more** splits.","marks":[{"t":"percent ionization ≈ 0.42%","note":"NaOH: goes UP"}],"calc":["OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O  (H⁺ removed)","shift right → **more ionized**"]},{"text":"**ClCH₂COOH** is a different, stronger acid: Cl pulls electrons and steadies the negative piece, so it splits **more**.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵","note":"ClCH₂COOH: Ka ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻³"}],"calc":["x = √(1.4 × 10⁻³ × 1.0) = 0.037 M → **3.7%**  (up)"]},{"text":"**Compare all four.** Only adding HCl pushes the percent DOWN.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Percent of the acid that splits apart","x":{"label":"Condition"},"y":{"label":"% ionized","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["1.0 M CH₃COOH","+ 0.10 M HCl","diluted to 0.10 M","1.0 M ClCH₂COOH"],"series":[{"name":"% ionized","values":[0.42,0.018,1.3,3.7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["+ 0.10 M HCl","% ionized"]]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to suppress a weak acid's ionization, add a common ion (extra H⁺ or A⁻): Le Châtelier shifts it left. Dilution, base, or electron-withdrawing groups increase ionization."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"In a biosensor circuit, an electrode with a constant resistance of 50 Ω is connected to an adjustable supply. A student records the current through the electrode as 20 mA at the first setting and 60 mA at the second setting. Which describes the voltage across the electrode between the two settings, and why?",idStem:"The current in a circuit rises while its resistance stays the same. What happens to the voltage?",choices:["It falls from 3 V to 1 V, since R is inversely proportional to I","It stays at 1 V, since the resistance does not change","It rises from 1 V to 3 V, since V is proportional to I at fixed R","It rises from 1000 V to 3000 V, since V is proportional to I at fixed R"],ans:2,exp:"**Ohm's law: V = IR.** With R fixed, V is proportional to I: 0.020 A × 50 Ω = 1.0 V and 0.060 A × 50 Ω = 3.0 V. Plugging in milliamps as if they were amps gives the 1000 V error.",src:"pe1-cp:14",how:{"ask":"With the resistance fixed, how does the voltage across the electrode depend on the current, and what are the two values in SI units?","know":"**Ohm's law: V = IR**, where V = voltage (volts), I = current (amperes), R = resistance (ohms). At fixed R, V and I are directly proportional. **1 mA = 10⁻³ A.**","steps":[{"text":"What is fixed and what changes? R is constant; the current triples.","hl":"a constant resistance of 50 Ω","calc":["V = I × R","R fixed → V ∝ I"]},{"text":"Convert milliamps to amps before multiplying.","hl":"20 mA at the first setting and 60 mA at the second setting","calc":["20 mA = **0.020 A**","60 mA = **0.060 A**"]},{"text":"Apply V = IR at each setting.","calc":["V₁ = 0.020 A × 50 Ω = **1.0 V**","V₂ = 0.060 A × 50 Ω = **3.0 V**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ohmic electrode, R = 50 Ω","x":{"label":"Current (mA)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Voltage (V)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"V = IR","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[20,1],[40,2],[60,3]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"V = IR","x":20},{"series":"V = IR","x":60}],"labels":[{"series":"V = IR","x":20,"text":"20 mA → 1.0 V","at":"left"},{"series":"V = IR","x":60,"text":"60 mA → 3.0 V","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":20,"from":0,"to":1,"text":"V₁ = 1.0 V"},{"x":60,"from":0,"to":3,"text":"V₂ = 3.0 V"}]}},{"text":"Eliminate: a fixed R means V cannot stay the same while I changes, and R is not 'inversely proportional to I' — it is constant. 1000 V comes from skipping the mA conversion.","strike":["It falls from 3 V to 1 V, since R is inversely proportional to I","It stays at 1 V, since the resistance does not change","It rises from 1000 V to 3000 V, since V is proportional to I at fixed R"]},{"text":"Answer: it rises from 1 V to 3 V, since V is proportional to I at fixed R."}],"trap":"\"It rises from 1000 V to 3000 V\" uses the right relationship but treats milliamps as amps; convert 20 mA to 0.020 A first."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"50 Ω","note":"resistance R (fixed)"},{"t":"20 mA","note":"current I, setting 1"},{"t":"60 mA","note":"current I, setting 2"}]},{"text":"**Three circuit words.** Current = how much charge flows (amps, A). Resistance = how hard the part resists flow (ohms, Ω). Voltage = the push (volts, V).","marks":[{"t":"constant resistance","note":"opposition to flow (Ω)"},{"t":"current","note":"flow of charge, in amps"},{"t":"voltage","note":"the push (V)"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law: V = I × R.** Here R never changes, so V rises and falls in step with I (directly proportional).","marks":[{"t":"constant resistance of 50 Ω","note":"R fixed → V ∝ I"}],"calc":["V = I × R","R fixed → triple I = **triple V**"]},{"text":"**Convert mA to A** first: milli = one-thousandth.","marks":[{"t":"20 mA","note":"= 0.020 A"},{"t":"60 mA","note":"= 0.060 A"}],"calc":["1 mA = 0.001 A","20 mA = **0.020 A**; 60 mA = **0.060 A**"]},{"text":"**Multiply** each current by the resistance.","marks":[{"t":"50 Ω","note":"× 50 Ω"},{"t":"20 mA","note":"0.020 A → 1.0 V"},{"t":"60 mA","note":"0.060 A → 3.0 V"}],"calc":["V₁ = 0.020 A × 50 Ω = **1.0 V**","V₂ = 0.060 A × 50 Ω = **3.0 V**"]},{"text":"**See it:** at fixed R, V vs I is a straight line through zero. Three times the current = three times the voltage.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fixed 50 Ω electrode: voltage vs current","x":{"label":"Current (mA)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Voltage (V)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"V = IR","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[20,1],[40,2],[60,3]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"V = IR","x":20},{"series":"V = IR","x":60}],"labels":[{"series":"V = IR","x":20,"text":"20 mA → 1.0 V","at":"left"},{"series":"V = IR","x":60,"text":"60 mA → 3.0 V","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 1000 V comes from using 20 'amps' instead of 0.020 A. R is fixed, not 'inversely proportional to I', and V cannot stay the same while I triples.","marks":[{"t":"20 mA","note":"not 20 A (→ 1000 V)"},{"t":"constant resistance","note":"R never changes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = IR. With R fixed, V is proportional to I. Always convert mA → A (÷ 1000) before multiplying."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A beam of light with a frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz passes from air into a clear fluid, where its speed is measured as 2.1 × 10⁸ m/s. (c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) What is the index of refraction of the fluid, and how does the light's wavelength change on entering it?",idStem:"Light travels at 2.1 × 10⁸ m/s in a fluid. What is the fluid's index of refraction?",choices:["1.4; the wavelength decreases","1.4; the wavelength increases","0.70; the wavelength decreases","0.70; the wavelength increases"],ans:0,exp:"**n = c/v** = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.1 × 10⁸ ≈ **1.4**. Frequency doesn't change at a boundary, so with v = fλ, a slower speed means a **shorter wavelength**.",src:"pe1-cp:15",how:{"ask":"Compute how many times slower light is in the fluid, then use v = fλ with f fixed.","know":"• **Index of refraction n = c/v**: c = speed in vacuum, v = speed in the material. n ≥ 1 always.\n• **v = fλ** (speed = frequency × wavelength).\n• When light crosses into a new medium, its **frequency stays the same**; speed and wavelength change together.","steps":[{"text":"Index of refraction compares light's speed in vacuum to its speed in the medium.","hl":"its speed is measured as 2.1 × 10⁸ m/s","calc":["n = c ÷ v","n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.1 × 10⁸","n = **1.43 ≈ 1.4**"]},{"text":"n is never below 1 for a material, because nothing slows light to faster than c. 0.70 is v/c, the ratio upside down.","strike":["0.70; the wavelength decreases","0.70; the wavelength increases"]},{"text":"At a boundary the frequency stays the same (the source sets it). Only v and λ change, linked by v = fλ.","hl":"a frequency of 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz","calc":["λ(air) = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = **600 nm**","λ(fluid) = 2.1 × 10⁸ ÷ 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = **420 nm**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength before and after entering the fluid","x":{"label":"Medium"},"y":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Air","Fluid"],"series":[{"name":"λ","values":[600,420]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Fluid","λ"]]}},{"text":"Slower speed at fixed f → shorter wavelength.","strike":["1.4; the wavelength increases"]},{"text":"Answer: n ≈ 1.4, and the wavelength decreases."}],"trap":"0.70 is v/c, the ratio flipped. An index below 1 would mean light travels faster than in vacuum, which is impossible."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz","note":"frequency f"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"speed in the fluid, v"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"speed in vacuum/air"}]},{"text":"**Index of refraction (n)** = how many times slower light goes in a material than in vacuum. Bigger n = slower light.","marks":[{"t":"index of refraction","note":"n = c ÷ v"}],"calc":["n = c ÷ v"]},{"text":"**Divide** the fast speed by the slow speed.","marks":[{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"top of the fraction"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"bottom of the fraction"}],"calc":["n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.1 × 10⁸","the 10⁸ cancel: 3.0 ÷ 2.1","n = **1.43 ≈ 1.4**"]},{"text":"**n is never below 1**, because nothing slows light to faster than c. 0.70 is v ÷ c, the fraction upside down.","marks":[{"t":"into a clear fluid","note":"light slows → n > 1"}]},{"text":"**Two wave words.** Frequency = how many waves pass each second. Wavelength = the length of one wave. They are tied to speed by v = f × λ.","marks":[{"t":"frequency","note":"waves per second (Hz)"},{"t":"wavelength","note":"length of one wave"}],"calc":["v = f × λ  →  λ = v ÷ f"]},{"text":"**Frequency stays the same** when light crosses into a new material (the source sets it). So when v drops, λ must drop.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz","note":"same in air and fluid"}],"calc":["λ air = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = **600 nm**","λ fluid = 2.1 × 10⁸ ÷ 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = **420 nm**"]},{"text":"**See the change:** the wave gets squeezed shorter in the fluid.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength before and after entering the fluid","x":{"label":"Medium"},"y":{"label":"Wavelength λ (nm)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"categories":["Air","Fluid"],"series":[{"name":"λ","values":[600,420]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Fluid","λ"]]}},{"text":"**Trap:** the wavelength does not grow. Slower speed at the same frequency always means shorter waves.","marks":[{"t":"wavelength","note":"shrinks: 600 → 420 nm"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"slower than c"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c ÷ v (always ≥ 1). At a boundary f stays fixed, so λ changes with v: slower medium → shorter wavelength."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A green laser beam (λ = 532 nm) passes through a neutral-density filter that reduces its intensity to one-half while leaving its color unchanged. Which quantity has been reduced by half, and why?",idStem:"For light of a single color, intensity is directly proportional to the:",choices:["Energy per photon, since intensity is energy per second","Wavelength, since E = hc/λ for each photon","Photon speed, since intensity depends on how fast energy arrives","Photons per second, since each photon's energy is fixed"],ans:3,exp:"Intensity = (energy per photon) × (photons per second) per area. **Same color → same frequency → same energy per photon (E = hf)**, so halving the intensity means **half as many photons per second**.",src:"pe1-cp:16",how:{"ask":"Intensity is a product of two things. Which of them can change if the color stays the same?","know":"• **Intensity** = power per area = (photons per second) × (energy of each photon) ÷ area.\n• **Photon energy E = hf = hc/λ**: set entirely by the color (frequency or wavelength).\n• The speed of light in air is fixed (~3.0 × 10⁸ m/s); it doesn't depend on brightness.","steps":[{"text":"Same color means the same frequency and wavelength, so every photon has the same energy as before.","hl":"leaving its color unchanged","calc":["E = hc ÷ λ","E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (532 × 10⁻⁹ m)","E ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J** per photon, before and after"],"strike":["Energy per photon, since intensity is energy per second","Wavelength, since E = hc/λ for each photon"]},{"text":"Light's speed in air is fixed at about c; a filter does not slow the photons that pass through it.","strike":["Photon speed, since intensity depends on how fast energy arrives"]},{"text":"Break intensity into its factors: only one factor is left to change.","hl":"reduces its intensity to one-half","calc":["I = (photons per second × energy per photon) ÷ area","½ I = (**½** photons per second) × (same energy per photon)"]},{"text":"See it as a before/after comparison.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Effect of the filter","x":{"label":"Quantity"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"categories":["Energy per photon","Photons per second"],"series":[{"name":"Before filter","values":[1,1]},{"name":"After filter","values":[1,0.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Photons per second","After filter"]]}},{"text":"Answer: photons per second, since each photon's energy is fixed by its unchanged color."}],"trap":"'Energy per photon' uses a true definition (intensity is energy per second) but picks the wrong factor. If photon energy changed, so would λ, and the color would change."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what the filter does** and what it leaves alone.","marks":[{"t":"λ = 532 nm","note":"wavelength = color"},{"t":"reduces its intensity to one-half","note":"beam half as bright"},{"t":"leaving its color unchanged","note":"same color"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in packets called photons.** A laser beam is a stream of photons, like raindrops in a shower.","marks":[{"t":"green laser beam","note":"a stream of photons"},{"t":"neutral-density filter","note":"blocks some photons"}]},{"text":"**Color = wavelength, and wavelength sets each photon's energy** (E = hc/λ). Same color → every photon carries the same energy as before.","marks":[{"t":"λ = 532 nm","note":"same λ → same E"}],"calc":["E = hc ÷ λ","E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (532 × 10⁻⁹)","E ≈ **3.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J** per photon, before and after"]},{"text":"**Intensity** = energy arriving each second (per area). It is made of two factors: how many photons arrive × how much energy each one carries.","marks":[{"t":"intensity","note":"energy per second"}],"calc":["I = (photons per second) × (energy per photon)"]},{"text":"**Only one factor is free to change.** Energy per photon is locked by the color, so the number of photons per second must halve.","marks":[{"t":"one-half","note":"½ the photons per second"}],"calc":["½ I = (**½** photons/s) × (same energy)"]},{"text":"**See it:** energy per photon stays at 1; photons per second drop to ½.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Effect of the filter (relative values)","x":{"label":"Quantity"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"categories":["Energy per photon","Photons per second"],"series":[{"name":"Before filter","values":[1,1]},{"name":"After filter","values":[1,0.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Photons per second","After filter"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** wavelength and photon energy are fixed by the unchanged color. Light's speed in air stays c; a filter removes photons, it doesn't slow them.","marks":[{"t":"leaving its color unchanged","note":"λ and E unchanged"},{"t":"neutral-density filter","note":"removes photons whole"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** brightness (intensity) = number of photons × energy per photon. Same color → same energy per photon, so dimmer means fewer photons per second."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A fluorescent dye used in a microscope must absorb at least 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per photon to reach its excited state. The excitation source is a diode laser that emits at a frequency of 610 THz. What is the energy of one laser photon, and can a single photon excite the dye? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s; c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s)",idStem:"What is the energy of a photon at 610 THz? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)",choices:["4.0 × 10⁻²² J; no, it falls below the threshold","1.1 × 10⁻⁴⁸ J; no, it falls below the threshold","4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; yes, it exceeds the threshold","4.0 × 10⁻³¹ J; no, it falls below the threshold"],ans:2,exp:"**E = hf**, with **1 THz = 10¹² Hz**: 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 6.1 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹ ≈ **4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**, which is above the 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J threshold, so one photon can excite the dye. c is not needed because f is given.",src:"pe1-cp:17",how:{"ask":"What is one photon's energy at this frequency once THz is converted to Hz, and is it above or below the dye's threshold?","know":"• **E = hf**: E = photon energy (J), h = Planck's constant (J·s), f = frequency (Hz = s⁻¹).\n• Prefixes: kilo 10³, mega 10⁶, giga 10⁹, **tera 10¹²**.\n• Check: visible light (400–750 THz) photons carry about 3–5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","steps":[{"text":"The frequency is given directly, so use E = hf; c is not needed.","hl":"emits at a frequency of 610 THz","calc":["E = h × f"]},{"text":"Convert the prefix: tera = 10¹².","calc":["610 THz = 610 × 10¹² Hz","= **6.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(6.1 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹)","6.6 × 6.1 ≈ 40 → 40 × 10⁻²⁰ J","E = **4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"Eliminate unit slips: 10⁻²² J uses 10⁹ for tera, 10⁻³¹ J forgets the prefix entirely, and 10⁻⁴⁸ J divides h by f.","strike":["4.0 × 10⁻²² J; no, it falls below the threshold","4.0 × 10⁻³¹ J; no, it falls below the threshold","1.1 × 10⁻⁴⁸ J; no, it falls below the threshold"]},{"text":"Compare with the threshold: 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J > 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, so one photon is enough.","hl":"at least 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per photon","calc":["4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J vs 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","4.0 > 3.5 → **excites**"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; yes, it exceeds the threshold."}],"trap":"\"4.0 × 10⁻²² J\" treats tera as 10⁹; tera is 10¹². Visible-light photons always carry roughly 10⁻¹⁹ J, so an answer far from that is a unit slip."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per photon","note":"minimum energy needed"},{"t":"610 THz","note":"laser frequency f"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"Planck's constant"}]},{"text":"**A photon is one packet of light.** The dye jumps to its **excited state** (a higher-energy state) only if ONE photon carries enough energy.","marks":[{"t":"excited state","note":"higher-energy state"},{"t":"single photon","note":"one light packet"}]},{"text":"**Photon energy: E = h × f.** The frequency is given directly, so the speed of light is **not needed**.","marks":[{"t":"frequency of 610 THz","note":"f: use this"},{"t":"c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s","note":"not needed: f is given"}],"calc":["E = h × f"]},{"text":"**Convert the prefix:** tera (T) = 10¹² = one trillion.","marks":[{"t":"610 THz","note":"= 6.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz"}],"calc":["610 THz = 610 × 10¹² Hz","= **6.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"**Multiply** h by f. Multiply the front numbers, add the powers of 10.","marks":[{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"× this"},{"t":"610 THz","note":"6.1 × 10¹⁴ Hz"}],"calc":["E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴)(6.1 × 10¹⁴)","6.6 × 6.1 ≈ 40;  10⁻³⁴ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻²⁰","E = 40 × 10⁻²⁰ = **4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"**Compare with the threshold:** 4.0 is bigger than 3.5 (same power of 10), so one photon is enough.","marks":[{"t":"at least 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","note":"threshold: 3.5"}],"calc":["4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J > 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J → **excites**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Energy of one photon vs what the dye needs","x":{"label":"Energy"},"y":{"label":"Energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["One laser photon","Dye threshold"],"series":[{"name":"energy","values":[4,3.5]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["One laser photon","energy"]]}},{"text":"**Traps are unit slips:** 10⁻²² J uses 10⁹ for tera; 10⁻³¹ J forgets the prefix; 10⁻⁴⁸ J divides h by f instead of multiplying.","marks":[{"t":"610 THz","note":"tera = 10¹², not 10⁹"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"multiply, don't divide"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf (use f directly; use E = hc/λ only when given λ). Convert prefixes first (T = 10¹²), then compare one photon's energy with the threshold."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A patient's blood pressure is 120/75 mmHg. Pulse pressure is the difference between the systolic and diastolic values. Approximately what percentage of atmospheric pressure is the pulse pressure? (1 atm = 760 mmHg = 101 kPa)",idStem:"Pulse pressure is 120 − 75 = 45 mmHg. About what percent of atmospheric pressure (760 mmHg) is that?",choices:["16%","10%","6%","45%"],ans:2,exp:"Pulse pressure = 120 − 75 = 45 mmHg. **Compare in the same units**: 45 ÷ 760 ≈ 0.059 ≈ **6%**.",src:"pe1-cp:19",how:{"ask":"Find the pressure difference, then compare it to 1 atm using the same unit.","know":"• **Pulse pressure** = systolic − diastolic.\n• To find a percentage, divide two quantities **in the same unit**: 1 atm = 760 mmHg (or 101 kPa).\n• Percentage = fraction × 100%.","steps":[{"text":"Find the pulse pressure from the reading: systolic − diastolic.","hl":"120/75 mmHg","calc":["pulse pressure = 120 − 75 = **45 mmHg**"]},{"text":"Compare in matching units: use 760 mmHg for 1 atm (no kPa needed).","hl":"1 atm = 760 mmHg = 101 kPa","calc":["45 mmHg ÷ 760 mmHg = 0.059","0.059 × 100% ≈ **6%**"]},{"text":"See the sizes on one scale.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Pressures compared with 1 atm","x":{"label":"Quantity"},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":0,"max":800,"step":100},"categories":["Systolic","Diastolic","Pulse","1 atm"],"series":[{"name":"mmHg","values":[120,75,45,760]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Pulse","mmHg"],["1 atm","mmHg"]]}},{"text":"Eliminate the slips: 16% = 120 ÷ 760 (systolic, not pulse); 10% = 75 ÷ 760 (diastolic); 45% = 45 ÷ 101 (mmHg divided by kPa).","strike":["16%","10%","45%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 6%."}],"trap":"16% divides the systolic value (120) by 760 instead of the difference. 45% divides 45 mmHg by 101 kPa, mixing two different units."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"120","note":"systolic (top number)"},{"t":"75 mmHg","note":"diastolic (bottom number)"},{"t":"1 atm = 760 mmHg","note":"air pressure at sea level"}]},{"text":"**Blood pressure is written top/bottom.** Systolic = highest pressure, when the heart squeezes. Diastolic = lowest, when it relaxes.","marks":[{"t":"systolic","note":"peak: 120"},{"t":"diastolic","note":"low point: 75"}]},{"text":"**Pulse pressure = the swing** between the peak and the low point of each beat.","marks":[{"t":"Pulse pressure is the difference","note":"120 − 75"}],"calc":["pulse pressure = 120 − 75 = **45 mmHg**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Blood pressure during one heartbeat","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.8,"step":0.1},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":60,"max":130,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Arterial pressure","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,75],[0.1,98],[0.2,117],[0.25,120],[0.35,106],[0.4,96],[0.5,88],[0.6,82],[0.7,78],[0.8,75]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0.25,"text":"systolic 120","at":"above"},{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0.8,"text":"diastolic 75","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":0.3,"from":75,"to":120,"text":"pulse = 45"}]}},{"text":"**'What percentage of X'** = part ÷ X × 100. Here the part is 45 mmHg and X is 1 atm.","marks":[{"t":"percentage of atmospheric pressure","note":"45 ÷ 1 atm × 100"}]},{"text":"**Use matching units.** The pulse pressure is in mmHg, so use 1 atm = 760 mmHg. The kPa value is not needed.","marks":[{"t":"760 mmHg","note":"use this (same units)"},{"t":"101 kPa","note":"not needed: other units"}],"calc":["45 mmHg ÷ 760 mmHg = 0.059","0.059 × 100 = **≈ 6%**"]},{"text":"**See the sizes:** the 45 mmHg swing is a small sliver of 760 mmHg.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Pressures compared with 1 atm","x":{"label":"Quantity"},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":0,"max":800,"step":100},"categories":["Systolic","Diastolic","Pulse","1 atm"],"series":[{"name":"mmHg","values":[120,75,45,760]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Pulse","mmHg"],["1 atm","mmHg"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 16% = 120 ÷ 760 (systolic, not the swing); 10% = 75 ÷ 760 (diastolic); 45% = 45 ÷ 101 (mmHg divided by kPa: units mixed).","marks":[{"t":"120","note":"120 ÷ 760 → 16% (wrong)"},{"t":"101 kPa","note":"45 ÷ 101 mixes units"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic. To compare two pressures, put them in the same unit first, then part ÷ whole × 100."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"The figure shows the measured activity of a radioactive tracer sample during the first 9 min after it is prepared. If the decay continues in the same way, what fraction of the original nuclei will have decayed 15 min after preparation, and what will the activity be then?",idStem:"An isotope has a 2-minute half-life. What fraction of a sample has decayed after 10 minutes?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of a tracer sample","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"Activity (Bq)","min":0,"max":1800,"step":300},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1600],[1.5,1131],[3,800],[4.5,566],[6,400],[7.5,283],[9,200]]}]},choices:["31/32 decayed; 50 Bq","1/32 decayed; 50 Bq","15/16 decayed; 100 Bq","1/16 decayed; 100 Bq"],ans:0,exp:"From the graph the activity halves every **3 min** (1600 → 800 at 3 min → 400 at 6 min). 15 min = **5 half-lives**, so (½)⁵ = **1/32 remains** and **31/32 has decayed**. Activity is proportional to the nuclei left: 1600 × 1/32 = **50 Bq**.",src:"pe1-cp:20",how:{"ask":"How long does the activity take to fall to half on the graph, and how many of those intervals fit into 15 min?","know":"• **Half-life (t½)**: the time for half of the remaining radioactive nuclei to decay. It is the same no matter how much is left.\n• Read t½ from a decay curve: find the starting value, find half of it on the y-axis, and read the time.\n• After n half-lives, fraction **remaining** = (½)ⁿ; fraction **decayed** = 1 − (½)ⁿ.\n• **Activity** (decays per second, Bq) is proportional to the number of nuclei left, so it follows the same (½)ⁿ.","steps":[{"text":"Read the half-life off the curve: start at 1600 Bq and find when the activity reaches 800 Bq.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":0},{"series":"Activity","x":3}],"yLine":800,"xLine":3,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":3,"text":"t½ = 3 min"}],"spans":[{"x":3,"from":1600,"to":800,"text":"1600 → 800","side":"left"}]},"calc":["1600 → 800 Bq at t = **3 min**","t½ = **3 min**"]},{"text":"Check with a second halving: 800 → 400 should take another 3 min.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":6}],"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":6,"text":"2nd t½ = 3 min"}],"spans":[{"x":6,"from":800,"to":400,"text":"800 → 400","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"400 Bq at 6 min","at":"right"}]},"calc":["400 Bq at 6 min ✓ (two half-lives)"]},{"text":"Count half-lives to 15 min and apply (½)ⁿ.","hl":"15 min after preparation","calc":["n = 15 ÷ 3 = **5**","remaining = (½)⁵ = **1/32**","decayed = 1 − 1/32 = **31/32**","activity = 1600 × 1/32 = **50 Bq**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":3,"text":"1"},{"x1":3,"x2":6,"text":"2"},{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"3"},{"x1":9,"x2":12,"text":"4"},{"x1":12,"x2":15,"text":"5"}],"labels":[{"x":15,"y":50,"text":"15 min: 1600 × 1/32 = 50 Bq","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"1/32 is what remains, not what decayed; 4 half-lives would be only 12 min.","strike":["1/32 decayed; 50 Bq","15/16 decayed; 100 Bq","1/16 decayed; 100 Bq"],"graph":{"xLine":12,"labels":[{"x":12,"y":100,"text":"12 min = only 4 t½ → 100 Bq","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Answer: 31/32 decayed; 50 Bq. Rule to reuse: read t½ where the curve hits half its start, then n = t ÷ t½ and multiply by (½)ⁿ."}],"trap":"1/32 is the fraction remaining, not the fraction decayed. And 15/16 comes from counting only 4 half-lives: 15 min ÷ 3 min = 5."},teach:[{"text":"**Up and down = activity**: how many nuclei decay each second, in becquerels (Bq). Higher = more decays per second.","marks":[{"t":"activity","note":"decays per second (Bq)"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = time** since the sample was made. Data stop at 9 min, but the question asks about 15 min.","marks":[{"t":"first 9 min","note":"data stop here"},{"t":"15 min after preparation","note":"we must predict here"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Radioactive** = made of unstable nuclei that break apart on their own. Fewer nuclei left → fewer decays per second.","marks":[{"t":"radioactive tracer","note":"unstable nuclei"}],"graph":{"focus":["Activity"],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":0,"text":"start: 1600 Bq","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Half-life (t½)** = time for the activity to fall to HALF. Half of 1600 is 800: find where the curve reaches 800.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":3}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":3,"text":"800 Bq = half of start","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Measure it:** from the start to 800 Bq takes 3 min. One half-life = 3 min.","calc":["1600 → 800 Bq at t = **3 min**","t½ = **3 min**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":3,"text":"one t½"}],"spans":[{"x":3,"from":1600,"to":800,"text":"1600 → 800","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Check:** every 3 min it halves again: 800 → 400 → 200.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":6,"text":"t½"},{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"t½"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"400 Bq at 6 min","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":9,"text":"200 Bq at 9 min","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Count half-lives to 15 min**, then halve that many times.","marks":[{"t":"15 min after preparation","note":"15 ÷ 3 = 5 half-lives"}],"calc":["n = 15 ÷ 3 = **5**","left = (½)⁵ = **1/32** of the nuclei","activity = 1600 × 1/32 = **50 Bq**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":9,"x2":12,"text":"4"},{"x1":12,"x2":15,"text":"5"}],"labels":[{"x":15,"y":50,"text":"15 min: 50 Bq","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Decayed ≠ remaining.** 1/32 is what is LEFT; the part that decayed is everything else.","marks":[{"t":"have decayed","note":"decayed = 1 − remaining"},{"t":"what will the activity be then","note":"1600 × 1/32 = 50 Bq"}],"calc":["decayed = 1 − 1/32 = **31/32**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1/32 'decayed' mixes up left vs gone. 15/16 and 100 Bq is only 4 half-lives = 12 min, one short.","graph":{"labels":[{"x":12,"y":100,"text":"12 min = 4 t½: 100 Bq","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read t½ where the curve hits half its start. n = time ÷ t½; remaining = (½)ⁿ; decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ; activity scales with what remains.","graph":{"focus":["Activity"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"During a 10-min ride on an exercise bike, the display reports an average power output of 200 W; the rider's heart rate averages 150 beats/min. How much work did the rider do on the bike?",idStem:"A heart pumps with an average power of 200 W for 10 minutes. How much work does it do?",choices:["2.0 kJ","120 kJ","12 kJ","18 MJ"],ans:1,exp:"**W = P × t** with t in **seconds**: 200 W × 600 s = 120,000 J = **120 kJ**. The heart rate is not needed.",src:"pe1-cp:21",how:{"ask":"Work is power multiplied by time. What must the time unit be?","know":"• **Power P = W/t** (watts = joules per second), so **W = P × t**.\n• SI time unit is the second: 1 min = 60 s.\n• 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Work from power: W = P × t. Power in watts is joules per second, so time must be in seconds.","hl":"average power output of 200 W","calc":["W = P × t"]},{"text":"Convert time.","hl":"During a 10-min ride","calc":["t = 10 min × 60 s/min = **600 s**"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["W = 200 J/s × 600 s","W = 120,000 J = **120 kJ**"]},{"text":"The heart rate is a distractor: it is not part of the bike's work. Multiplying by 150 gives 18 MJ; using minutes instead of seconds gives 2.0 kJ; using 1 minute (60 s) instead of 10 minutes gives 12 kJ.","hl":"the rider's heart rate averages 150 beats/min","strike":["18 MJ","2.0 kJ","12 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 120 kJ."}],"trap":"2.0 kJ multiplies by 10 minutes without converting to seconds. Since a watt is a joule per SECOND, minutes give a meaningless number."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"10-min","note":"time"},{"t":"200 W","note":"power"},{"t":"150 beats/min","note":"heart rate"}]},{"text":"**Work = energy handed over** (joules, J). **Power = how fast** energy is handed over: 1 watt = 1 joule every second.","marks":[{"t":"average power output","note":"joules each second"},{"t":"work","note":"total energy given (J)"}]},{"text":"**So work = power × time.** Because a watt is per SECOND, time must be in seconds.","marks":[{"t":"200 W","note":"200 J every second"}],"calc":["W = P × t","W (J) = P (J/s) × t (s)"]},{"text":"**Convert minutes to seconds.**","marks":[{"t":"10-min","note":"= 600 s"}],"calc":["t = 10 min × 60 s/min = **600 s**"]},{"text":"**Multiply** power by time in seconds.","marks":[{"t":"200 W","note":"P = 200 J/s"},{"t":"10-min","note":"t = 600 s"}],"calc":["W = 200 J/s × 600 s","W = 120,000 J = **120 kJ**"]},{"text":"**See it:** at a steady 200 W the work piles up in a straight line, reaching 120 kJ at 600 s.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Work done at a steady 200 W","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":600,"step":100},"y":{"label":"Work (kJ)","min":0,"max":140,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Work","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[300,60],[600,120]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Work","x":600}],"labels":[{"series":"Work","x":600,"text":"600 s → 120 kJ","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**The heart rate is a distractor.** It describes the rider's body, not the energy given to the bike. Exams add extra numbers on purpose.","marks":[{"t":"150 beats/min","note":"not needed: body, not bike"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 200 × 10 = 2.0 kJ forgets to convert minutes; multiplying by 150 too gives 18 MJ; dividing power by time does not give work.","marks":[{"t":"10-min","note":"not 10: use 600 s"},{"t":"150 beats/min","note":"× 150 → 18 MJ (wrong)"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** work = power × time, with power in watts (J/s) and time in **seconds**. Ignore numbers that don't belong to the equation."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Strands of the peptide shown are packed into a hydrogel. When the gel is exposed to air, it stiffens as links form between neighboring strands; adding dithiothreitol, a reducing agent, returns it to its original stiffness. Which labeled atom forms these links?",idStem:"Peptide fibers are cross-linked by disulfide bridges between cysteines. What kind of interaction is that?",fig:"p1-smck-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:2,exp:"Air **oxidizes** two cysteine thiols (–SH) on neighboring strands into a **disulfide (–S–S–)**, a covalent link; a reducing agent such as dithiothreitol breaks it back to two –SH. Site III is cysteine's S–H. Site II is methionine's sulfur, which is already bonded to two carbons and has no S–H to lose.",src:"pe1-cp:22",how:{"ask":"What link forms when air oxidizes a side chain and breaks when a reducing agent is added, and which labeled atom carries the hydrogen that must be removed?","know":"• **Disulfide bond**: 2 R–S–H → R–S–S–R + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻. Losing electrons = **oxidation** (O₂ in air can do it); adding electrons back = **reduction** (dithiothreitol, DTT, or β-mercaptoethanol).\n• Only **cysteine** has a thiol (–SH). **Methionine** has a thioether (C–S–C): sulfur, but no S–H.\n• Serine –OH, lysine –NH₂ and others can hydrogen bond or form salt bridges, but those are not created by oxidation or undone by reducing agents.\n• On a structure, look for S with an H on it.","steps":[{"text":"Read the redox clues: air oxidizes, dithiothreitol reduces. A link that forms on oxidation and breaks on reduction is a disulfide.","hl":"adding dithiothreitol, a reducing agent, returns it to its original stiffness","calc":["2 R–SH + ½ O₂ → R–S–S–R + H₂O  (air)","R–S–S–R + DTT → 2 R–SH  (reduced)"]},{"text":"Look at what each label points to on the structure: I = serine O–H, II = methionine S between two carbons, III = cysteine S–H, IV = lysine NH₂.","fig":"p1-smck-sites"},{"text":"O–H and NH₂ groups make hydrogen bonds or salt bridges, which air does not create and DTT does not break.","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"Methionine's sulfur has no H to lose, so it cannot pair into S–S.","strike":["II"]},{"text":"Two cysteines from neighboring strands join, S–S highlighted: a covalent, intermolecular cross-link.","fig":"p1-cystine-ss"},{"text":"Answer: III. Rule to reuse: oxidation-made, reducing-agent-broken links are disulfides, and they need an S–H, which only cysteine has."}],"trap":"II is a sulfur too, so it looks right, but methionine's S is bonded to two carbons. A disulfide needs each sulfur to give up an H, so only an –SH can do it."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** a peptide is a chain of amino acids. Corners are carbons with hidden H's; branches off the main chain are side chains. S = sulfur.","fig":"t1-smck-read"},{"text":"**Oxidation** = losing electrons (often as H atoms); air does it. **Reduction** gives them back; dithiothreitol does it.","fig":"t1-cys-sh"},{"text":"**Two S–H → one S–S.** Oxidation removes both H's and the sulfurs bond: a covalent disulfide. Reduction breaks it back.","fig":"t1-cystine-notes"},{"text":"Site III is cysteine's sulfur, and it **has an H**. It can lose that H and pair with another.","fig":"t1-smck-iii"},{"text":"Site II is a sulfur too, but it's bonded to two carbons with **no H to lose**.","fig":"t1-smck-ii"},{"text":"O–H and NH₂ make hydrogen bonds or charge pulls, which **air doesn't create and reducing agents don't break**.","fig":"t1-smck-i-iv"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** links made by air and broken by reducing agents are disulfides, and need an S with an H. Check each labeled atom for both.","fig":"t1-smck-rule"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A receptor binds the peptide shown through a salt bridge to one of the receptor's aspartate side chains, which is negatively charged at pH 7.4. Side-chain pKa values: His, 6.0; Lys, 10.5; Glu, 4.2. Which labeled group of the peptide forms this salt bridge at pH 7.4?",idStem:"Many cell-adhesion peptides contain the RGD motif. Which of its residues can form strong ionic attractions?",fig:"p1-hkes-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"A salt bridge pairs **opposite full charges**, so the peptide must supply a **positive** group. At pH 7.4 a group is protonated when its **pKa is above the pH**. Lysine's amine (II, pKa 10.5) is about **99.9% protonated (–NH₃⁺)**. Histidine (I, pKa 6.0) is mostly **neutral** at 7.4 (only about 4% protonated), glutamate (III) is **negative**, and serine (IV) does not ionize.",src:"pe1-cp:23",how:{"ask":"What charge must the peptide's group carry to pair with a negative aspartate, and at pH 7.4 which labeled group has a pKa high enough to stay protonated?","know":"• **Salt bridge** = an ionic attraction between a + side chain (Lys, Arg, protonated His) and a − side chain (Asp, Glu).\n• **pH vs pKa rule**: if pH < pKa, the group holds its H⁺ (protonated form); if pH > pKa, it has lost it.\n  – Amine: protonated = –NH₃⁺ (**+**), deprotonated = –NH₂ (0).\n  – Carboxylic acid: protonated = –COOH (0), deprotonated = –COO⁻ (**−**).\n• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH − pKa = log([base]/[acid]); each 1 unit = 10-fold.\n• Exam structures are often drawn neutral; you must work out the charge yourself.","steps":[{"text":"The partner is negative, so the peptide's group must be positive at pH 7.4.","hl":"which is negatively charged at pH 7.4"},{"text":"Identify each label: I = histidine ring N, II = lysine NH₂, III = glutamate COOH, IV = serine OH.","fig":"p1-hkes-sites"},{"text":"Compare each pKa with pH 7.4.","hl":"His, 6.0; Lys, 10.5; Glu, 4.2","calc":["Lys: pKa 10.5 > 7.4 → –NH₃⁺, charge **+1**","His: 7.4 − 6.0 = 1.4 → base:acid ≈ 10^1.4 ≈ **25:1** → mostly neutral","Glu: 7.4 > 4.2 → –COO⁻, charge **−1**","Ser: –OH, pKa ≈ 13+, **neutral**"]},{"text":"Glutamate is negative and would repel; serine is uncharged; histidine is only about 4% protonated at 7.4.","strike":["III","IV","I"]},{"text":"The lysine side chain, highlighted, ends in –NH₃⁺ at pH 7.4.","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Answer: II. Rule to reuse: pH below pKa → protonated; at pH 7.4 only Lys and Arg are reliably positive."}],"trap":"Histidine is the classic positive side chain in textbooks, but its pKa (6.0) is below 7.4, so at physiological pH most His side chains are neutral. Glutamate is charged too, but negatively, so it would repel aspartate."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** a peptide is amino acids in a chain. Corners are carbons with hidden H's. Each label points to a side-chain group.","fig":"t1-hkes-read"},{"text":"**Salt bridge** = the pull between a full + and a full − charge. The receptor's aspartate is −, so the peptide must supply a +.","fig":"t1-asp-minus"},{"text":"**Charges come from H⁺:** N groups turn + by gaining H⁺; COOH turns − by losing H⁺.","fig":["t1-lys-plus","t1-glu-minus"]},{"text":"**pKa** = the pH where a group is half with H⁺, half without. **pH below pKa → it keeps H⁺; pH above → it loses H⁺.**"},{"text":"**Lysine (II):** 7.4 is below 10.5, so it keeps the extra H⁺: NH₃⁺, positive.","fig":"t1-hkes-lys-plus"},{"text":"**Glutamate (III):** 7.4 is above 4.2, so it loses H⁺: COO⁻, negative. It would repel aspartate.","fig":"t1-hkes-glu-minus"},{"text":"**Histidine (I):** 7.4 is above 6.0, so it mostly loses H⁺. Only about 4% carry a + at any moment: mostly neutral.","fig":"t1-hkes-his"},{"text":"**Serine (IV):** its OH doesn't gain or lose H⁺ at body pH. It can hydrogen bond, but that isn't a salt bridge.","fig":"t1-hkes-ser"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare pKa with pH. An N group with pKa above pH is +; a COOH with pKa below pH is −. Find the label that is fully +.","fig":"t1-hkes-rule"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A reagent oxidizes primary alcohols all the way to carboxylic acids under conditions that leave secondary alcohols and phenols unchanged. The compound shown is treated with excess reagent. Which structure is the product?",idStem:"Which amino acid has a primary alcohol (–CH₂OH) side chain?",fig:"p1-triol",choiceFigs:["p1-ox-acid","p1-ox-ketoacid","p1-ox-aldehyde","p1-ox-ketone"],choices:["Hydroxy acid","Keto acid","Hydroxy aldehyde","Hydroxy ketone"],ans:0,exp:"Classify each –OH by the carbon it sits on. The **CH₂–OH at the chain end is primary** (that carbon has one C neighbor) → oxidized to **–COOH**. The **CH(OH) next to the ring is secondary** (two C neighbors) → unchanged. The **ring –OH is a phenol** → unchanged. Product: the hydroxy acid, with both other –OH groups intact.",src:"pe1-cp:24",how:{"ask":"For each –OH on the structure, how many carbons are bonded to the carbon that carries it, and what does the reagent do to that class?","know":"• **Alcohol class** = number of carbons bonded to the carbinol carbon (the C bearing –OH): 1 → **primary**, 2 → **secondary**, 3 → **tertiary**.\n• An –OH on an aromatic ring is a **phenol**, a separate class.\n• Strong oxidation: 1° alcohol → aldehyde → **carboxylic acid**; 2° alcohol → ketone; 3° → no reaction.\n• This reagent is selective: it acts only on 1° alcohols, and with excess reagent it does not stop at the aldehyde.","steps":[{"text":"Note the reagent's rule: 1° alcohols → acids; 2° alcohols and phenols untouched; reagent in excess.","hl":"oxidizes primary alcohols all the way to carboxylic acids"},{"text":"Classify each –OH by counting the carbons on the carbon that carries it; the two carbinol carbons are highlighted.","fig":"p1-triol-sites","calc":["I: –OH on the ring → **phenol** → unchanged","II: CH(OH) bonded to ring C and CH₂ → **2°** → unchanged","III: CH₂OH bonded to one C → **1°** → **–COOH**"]},{"text":"Any structure that changed the 2° alcohol to C=O is out.","strike":["Keto acid","Hydroxy ketone"]},{"text":"The aldehyde is only the halfway point; with excess reagent the 1° alcohol goes on to the acid.","calc":["R–CH₂OH → R–CHO → **R–COOH**"],"strike":["Hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"Answer: the hydroxy acid. Rule to reuse: classify every –OH by its carbon before predicting an oxidation; then apply only what the reagent does.","fig":"p1-ox-acid"}],"trap":"The keto acid is what a strong, unselective oxidant such as chromic acid would give. The stem says secondary alcohols are left alone. The hydroxy aldehyde stops one step short: excess reagent takes a primary alcohol all the way to the acid."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** hexagon = benzene ring (6 carbons); corners are carbons with hidden H's. This molecule has three OH groups.","fig":"t1-triol-read"},{"text":"**Classify each OH by its carbon:** count the carbons touching it. 1 = primary, 2 = secondary; OH right on the ring = phenol.","fig":"t1-triol-classify"},{"text":"The chain-end CH₂–OH touches only one carbon: a **primary alcohol**, the kind this reagent attacks.","fig":"t1-triol-primary"},{"text":"**Oxidation strips H's and adds bonds to O.** Primary CH₂OH → aldehyde (CH=O) → with excess reagent, carboxylic acid (COOH).","fig":["t1-ox-aldehyde-mid","t1-ox-acid-hl"]},{"text":"The middle OH's carbon touches **two** carbons: secondary. This reagent leaves it alone.","fig":"t1-triol-secondary"},{"text":"The ring OH is a **phenol**, also left alone by this reagent.","fig":"t1-triol-phenol"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** classify every OH first, then apply only what the reagent does; excess means all the way. In each drawing, check the chain end and the other OH's.","fig":"t1-triol-classify"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A designed peptide contains eight lysine residues, side-chain pKa ≈ 10.5, and no acidic residues. The figure shows the percentage of the peptide found in fibers after equilibration at each pH. Which explanation is most consistent with the data?",idStem:"A peptide self-assembles at low pH but not at high pH. Which feature explains this?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide found in fibers after equilibration","x":{"label":"pH","min":6,"max":14,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Peptide in fibers (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Fibers","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[6,2],[7,2],[8,3],[9,6],[10,24],[10.5,50],[11,76],[12,96],[13,98],[14,98]]}]},choices:["Deprotonated lysines no longer repel one another","Protonated lysines attract one another","Deprotonated lysines form salt bridges with one another","Protonated lysines bind water less strongly"],ans:0,exp:"Fibers form only **above about pH 10.5**, the lysine pKa. Below the pKa the side chains are **–NH₃⁺**, so the peptide carries up to +8 charges that **repel** other strands and keep it dissolved. Above the pKa the side chains become neutral **–NH₂**; the repulsion disappears and the strands can pack into fibers.",src:"pe1-cp:25",how:{"ask":"At what pH do half of the peptides sit in fibers, and what happens to the lysine side-chain charge as the pH passes that value?","know":"• **pKa** = the pH at which a group is half protonated, half deprotonated.\n• Lysine side chain: pH < 10.5 → mostly **–NH₃⁺ (+1)**; pH > 10.5 → mostly **–NH₂ (0)**.\n• Each pH unit away from the pKa changes the ratio 10-fold (Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH − pKa = log[base]/[acid]).\n• Like charges **repel**; a peptide with many like charges stays soluble. Removing the charge lets strands pack together.\n• A **salt bridge** needs one + and one − group.","steps":[{"text":"Trace the curve: the switch from dissolved to fibers is centered where 50% are in fibers.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Fibers","x":10.5}],"xLine":10.5,"yLine":50,"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":10.5,"text":"50% at pH 10.5 = pKa","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":10.5,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"50%","side":"left"}]},"calc":["50% in fibers at pH **10.5** = lysine pKa"]},{"text":"Work out the lysine charge on each side of the switch.","calc":["pH 7: pKa − pH = 3.5 → NH₃⁺:NH₂ ≈ **3000:1** → about +8 per peptide","pH 12: pH − pKa = 1.5 → NH₂:NH₃⁺ ≈ **30:1** → nearly neutral"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Fibers","x":7},{"series":"Fibers","x":12}],"shade":[{"x1":6.5,"x2":7.5,"text":"pH 7: about +8"},{"x1":11.5,"x2":12.5,"text":"pH 12: nearly neutral"}],"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":7,"text":"2% fibers","at":"above"},{"series":"Fibers","x":12,"text":"96% fibers","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Fibers form when the + charges are removed, so the protonated, charged form is the one that stays dissolved: like charges repel.","strike":["Protonated lysines attract one another","Protonated lysines bind water less strongly"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"charged: repel, stay dissolved"}],"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":12,"text":"charges gone → fibers","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"A salt bridge needs a negative partner, and the peptide has none.","hl":"no acidic residues","strike":["Deprotonated lysines form salt bridges with one another"]},{"text":"Answer: deprotonated lysines no longer repel one another. Rule to reuse: a sharp change in a pH graph usually sits at a pKa; ask which charge appears or disappears there.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":10.5,"text":"switch at the lysine pKa","at":"left"}]}}],"trap":"'Deprotonated lysines form salt bridges' sounds like a reason to stick together, but a salt bridge needs opposite charges, and this peptide has no acidic residues; neutral –NH₂ groups carry no charge at all. Protonated lysines are all +, so they repel rather than attract."},teach:[{"text":"**Up and down = how much of the peptide has clumped into fibers.** 0% = all dissolved; 100% = all stuck together.","marks":[{"t":"percentage of the peptide found in fibers","note":"up-down axis"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = pH.** pH measures H⁺: low pH = lots of H⁺; moving right = fewer H⁺ around, so groups give up their H⁺.","marks":[{"t":"at each pH","note":"left-right axis"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**A peptide** is a short protein chain. **Lysine** is a building block whose side chain ends in an amine (–NH₂) that can grab an H⁺ and become –NH₃⁺ (charge +1).","marks":[{"t":"peptide","note":"short protein chain"},{"t":"eight lysine residues","note":"8 side chains: –NH₂/–NH₃⁺"}]},{"text":"**pKa** = the pH where half the groups hold their H⁺. Below the pKa most lysines are –NH₃⁺ (+); above it most are –NH₂ (neutral).","marks":[{"t":"side-chain pKa ≈ 10.5","note":"half +, half neutral here"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":6,"x2":10.5,"text":"pH < pKa: lysines +"},{"x1":10.5,"x2":14,"text":"pH > pKa: neutral"}]}},{"text":"**Two flat parts and a switch.** Low flat = dissolved; high flat = fibers; the steep part between them is the switch.","graph":{"focus":["Fibers"],"shade":[{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"mostly dissolved"},{"x1":12,"x2":14,"text":"mostly fibers"}]}},{"text":"**Find the middle of the switch** (50% in fibers) and read its pH. It lands right on the lysine pKa.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Fibers","x":10.5}],"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":10.5,"text":"50% at pH 10.5 = pKa","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":10.5,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"50%","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Count the charges.** At pH 7 almost every lysine is +, so each chain carries about +8. At pH 12 nearly all are neutral.","calc":["pH 7: 3.5 below pKa → NH₃⁺:NH₂ ≈ **3000:1**","pH 12: 1.5 above pKa → NH₂:NH₃⁺ ≈ **30:1**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":7,"text":"≈ +8 charge: 2% fibers","at":"above"},{"series":"Fibers","x":12,"text":"≈ neutral: 96% fibers","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Like charges repel.** Chains that each carry +8 push one another away and stay dissolved. Remove the charges and nothing pushes them apart, so they pack into fibers.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"+ chains repel: dissolved"}],"labels":[{"series":"Fibers","x":13,"text":"charges gone → fibers","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** + charges never attract each other; charged groups bind water MORE, not less; a salt bridge needs a − partner, and this peptide has none.","marks":[{"t":"no acidic residues","note":"no − charges: no salt bridge"},{"t":"eight lysine residues","note":"all + below pH 10.5"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a sharp switch on a pH graph usually sits at a pKa. Ask which charge appears or disappears there and what that charge was doing (repelling, binding).","graph":{"focus":["Fibers"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biology",aamc:"4B",q:"Consider these data for the systemic circulation: aorta, total cross-sectional area 3 cm², mean blood speed 30 cm/s; capillaries, total cross-sectional area 3000 cm², individual diameter about 8 μm. Assuming the same volume of blood passes each level per second, what is the mean speed of blood in the capillaries, and why?",idStem:"Why does blood flow slowest in the capillaries?",choices:["30 cm/s, since the flow rate is the same at each level","30,000 cm/s, since the total area is largest there","0.03 cm/s, since each capillary is the narrowest vessel","0.03 cm/s, since the total area is largest there"],ans:3,exp:"**Continuity: Q = A × v is constant.** v = (3 cm² × 30 cm/s) ÷ 3000 cm² = **0.03 cm/s**. The capillaries are slowest because their **combined** cross-sectional area is the largest, not because each one is narrow.",src:"pe1-cp:26",how:{"ask":"Volume per second is the same everywhere. How does the speed change when that same volume spreads across a much larger total area?","know":"• **Continuity equation**: Q (volume flow rate, cm³/s) = A × v, where A = cross-sectional area and v = mean speed. For an incompressible fluid, Q is the same at every level.\n• For vessels in parallel, use the **total** area of all of them combined.\n• Larger total A → smaller v.","steps":[{"text":"Same volume per second at every level = constant flow rate Q (continuity).","hl":"Assuming the same volume of blood passes each level per second","calc":["Q = A × v = constant"]},{"text":"Find Q at the aorta.","hl":"total cross-sectional area 3 cm², mean blood speed 30 cm/s","calc":["Q = 3 cm² × 30 cm/s = **90 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"Solve for v in the capillaries using their TOTAL area.","hl":"total cross-sectional area 3000 cm²","calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 90 cm³/s ÷ 3000 cm²","v = **0.03 cm/s**","area × 1000 → speed ÷ 1000"],"strike":["30 cm/s, since the flow rate is the same at each level","30,000 cm/s, since the total area is largest there"]},{"text":"Check the reason: one capillary's diameter is not what slows the blood; the blood is split among many capillaries whose combined area is huge.","hl":"individual diameter about 8 μm","strike":["0.03 cm/s, since each capillary is the narrowest vessel"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.03 cm/s, since the total area is largest there."}],"trap":"'0.03 cm/s, since each capillary is the narrowest vessel' has the right number but the wrong reason. A single narrow tube would make blood go FASTER (like a nozzle); it is the huge combined area that slows it."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"3 cm²","note":"aorta: total area"},{"t":"30 cm/s","note":"aorta: blood speed"},{"t":"3000 cm²","note":"capillaries: total area"}]},{"text":"**The aorta** is the single big pipe leaving the heart. **Capillaries** are billions of tiny vessels; 'total area' adds up all their openings.","marks":[{"t":"aorta","note":"one big pipe from heart"},{"t":"capillaries","note":"billions of tiny vessels"},{"t":"individual diameter about 8 μm","note":"width of ONE capillary"}]},{"text":"**Flow rate Q** = volume of blood passing per second. The question says Q is the same at every level. **Continuity:** Q = area × speed.","marks":[{"t":"same volume of blood passes each level per second","note":"Q is the same everywhere"}],"calc":["Q = A × v = constant"]},{"text":"**Find Q at the aorta.**","marks":[{"t":"3 cm²","note":"A"},{"t":"30 cm/s","note":"v"}],"calc":["Q = 3 cm² × 30 cm/s = **90 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"**Use that same Q in the capillaries** with their TOTAL area.","marks":[{"t":"3000 cm²","note":"v = 90 ÷ 3000"}],"calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 90 cm³/s ÷ 3000 cm²","v = **0.03 cm/s**","area × 1000 → speed ÷ 1000"]},{"text":"**See it:** the capillary bar is almost invisible. A wide total opening makes the blood crawl, giving time for exchange with tissues.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Same flow, 1000× the area → 1/1000 the speed","x":{"label":"Vessel level"},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"categories":["Aorta (3 cm²)","Capillaries (3000 cm²)"],"series":[{"name":"speed","values":[30,0.03]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Capillaries (3000 cm²)","speed"]]}},{"text":"**Why it's slow:** not because one capillary is narrow, but because the blood is split among so many that their combined area is huge.","marks":[{"t":"individual diameter about 8 μm","note":"not the reason"},{"t":"total cross-sectional area 3000 cm²","note":"the real reason"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 30 cm/s confuses the same flow rate with the same speed; 30,000 cm/s multiplies instead of divides. Bigger area means slower, not faster.","marks":[{"t":"mean blood speed","note":"speed ≠ flow rate"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** continuity: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. More total area → slower speed. Use the COMBINED area of parallel vessels, not one vessel's size."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"In the complex ion [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, six ammonia ligands surround the cobalt ion, and every N–Co–N angle is either 90° or 180°. Which hybridization of the metal's orbitals is consistent with this geometry?",idStem:"Which hybridization gives an octahedral central atom?",choices:["d²sp³","dsp²","sp³","sp³d"],ans:0,exp:"Six ligands at 90° and 180° = **octahedral**, which needs **six hybrid orbitals**: d²sp³ (2 d + 1 s + 3 p = 6). dsp² gives 4 orbitals (square planar), sp³ gives 4 (tetrahedral), sp³d gives 5 (trigonal bipyramidal).",src:"pe1-cp:28",how:{"ask":"From the number of ligands and the angles, name the shape; then count how many hybrid orbitals that shape needs.","know":"• Number of hybrid orbitals = number of orbitals mixed: count the superscripts plus the letters without one (s = 1, p = 1 each, d = 1 each).\n• **2 → sp (linear, 180°); 3 → sp² (trigonal planar, 120°); 4 → sp³ (tetrahedral, 109.5°) or dsp² (square planar, 90°); 5 → sp³d (trigonal bipyramidal); 6 → d²sp³ or sp³d² (octahedral, 90°/180°).**","steps":[{"text":"Count the ligands and read the angles: 6 ligands at 90° and 180° = octahedral.","hl":"every N–Co–N angle is either 90° or 180°"},{"text":"One hybrid orbital is needed per ligand bond, so octahedral needs 6 hybrid orbitals.","hl":"six ammonia ligands surround the cobalt ion","calc":["6 ligands → **6** hybrid orbitals needed"]},{"text":"Count the orbitals each choice makes.","calc":["d²sp³: 2 + 1 + 3 = **6** → octahedral","sp³d: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 → trigonal bipyramidal","sp³: 1 + 3 = 4 → tetrahedral","dsp²: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 → square planar"]},{"text":"Only one gives six.","strike":["dsp²","sp³","sp³d"]},{"text":"Answer: d²sp³."}],"trap":"dsp² is tempting because square-planar complexes also have 90° angles, but it makes only 4 hybrid orbitals, too few for six ligands."},teach:[{"text":"First, **read what the question gives**: the formula, the count, and the angles.","marks":[{"t":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","note":"Co with 6 NH₃ attached"},{"t":"six ammonia ligands","note":"6 groups bonded"},{"t":"90° or 180°","note":"angles between bonds"}]},{"text":"**A ligand** is a molecule that donates a pair of electrons to bond to a metal. Here six NH₃ molecules each bond to the central cobalt.","marks":[{"t":"ligands","note":"e⁻-pair donors"},{"t":"cobalt ion","note":"central metal"}]},{"text":"**Shape:** 6 groups at 90° and 180° point up, down, left, right, front, back. That shape is **octahedral**.","marks":[{"t":"90° or 180°","note":"→ octahedral"}],"calc":["up–down, left–right, front–back = **6 directions**","neighbors 90° apart; opposite ones 180°"]},{"text":"**Hybridization** = mixing the metal's s, p and d orbitals into a set of equal orbitals, each pointing at one ligand. Each superscript counts the orbitals used.","marks":[{"t":"hybridization","note":"mixing orbitals"}],"calc":["sp³ = 1 s + 3 p = 4 orbitals"]},{"text":"**One hybrid orbital per bond.** Six ligands → six orbitals needed.","marks":[{"t":"six ammonia ligands","note":"need 6 hybrid orbitals"}],"calc":["6 bonds → **6** hybrid orbitals"]},{"text":"**Count each option's orbitals.** Only one makes six.","calc":["d²sp³: 2 + 1 + 3 = **6** → octahedral","sp³d: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 → trigonal bipyramidal","sp³: 1 + 3 = 4 → tetrahedral","dsp²: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 → square planar"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Hybrid orbitals each mix makes","x":{"label":"Hybridization"},"y":{"label":"Number of hybrid orbitals","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"categories":["d²sp³","sp³d","sp³","dsp²"],"series":[{"name":"orbitals","values":[6,5,4,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["d²sp³","orbitals"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** sp³ gives 109.5° angles; sp³d gives 120° angles; dsp² holds only 4 ligands. None fits six ligands at only 90° and 180°.","marks":[{"t":"every N–Co–N angle","note":"no 109.5° or 120°"},{"t":"six ammonia ligands","note":"4 or 5 orbitals: too few"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count the groups around the central atom = number of hybrid orbitals. 4 → sp³/dsp², 5 → sp³d, 6 (octahedral, 90°/180°) → d²sp³ or sp³d²."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A chemist compares 1.00 mol of helium at 25 °C and 1.00 atm with the prediction of the ideal-gas model, using R = 0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹. Which pair gives an assumption of the ideal-gas model and the volume the model predicts for this sample?",idStem:"Which is an assumption of the ideal gas model?",choices:["Its particles attract weakly; 24.5 L","Its particles occupy no volume; 24.5 L","Its particles occupy no volume; 22.4 L","Its particles attract weakly; 22.4 L"],ans:1,exp:"The ideal-gas model assumes particles of **negligible volume with no intermolecular attractions**. V = nRT/P = 1.00 × 0.0821 × 298 ÷ 1.00 = **24.5 L**; 22.4 L is the molar volume at **0 °C** (273 K).",src:"pe1-cp:29",how:{"ask":"Which statement is an assumption of the model rather than a description of a real gas, and what does PV = nRT give at 298 K?","know":"**Ideal gas model (kinetic molecular theory):**\n• Molecules have **negligible volume** compared with the container.\n• **No attractions or repulsions** between molecules; collisions are elastic.\n• PV = nRT, with T in **kelvin** (K = °C + 273).\n• 22.4 L/mol is the molar volume at STP (0 °C, 1 atm).","steps":[{"text":"Model half: an ideal gas has particles with no volume and NO attractions. 'Attract weakly' describes a real gas.","hl":"an assumption of the ideal-gas model","strike":["Its particles attract weakly; 24.5 L","Its particles attract weakly; 22.4 L"]},{"text":"Volume half: convert the temperature to kelvin.","hl":"at 25 °C and 1.00 atm","calc":["T = 25 + 273 = **298 K**"]},{"text":"Solve PV = nRT for V.","calc":["V = nRT ÷ P","V = (1.00 mol)(0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹)(298 K) ÷ 1.00 atm","V = **24.5 L**"]},{"text":"22.4 L is the molar volume at 273 K; at 298 K the volume is larger by 298/273.","calc":["22.4 L × 298/273 = **24.5 L**"],"strike":["Its particles occupy no volume; 22.4 L"]},{"text":"Answer: its particles occupy no volume; 24.5 L."}],"trap":"\"Its particles occupy no volume; 22.4 L\" has the right assumption but uses the molar volume at STP, which applies only at 0 °C; at 25 °C the gas takes up more room."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which letter of the gas law it is.","marks":[{"t":"1.00 mol","note":"n (amount of gas)"},{"t":"25 °C","note":"T (temperature)"},{"t":"1.00 atm","note":"P (pressure)"}]},{"text":"**The ideal-gas model** is a simplified picture of a gas: particles are treated as dots with **no size** that **never attract** each other.","marks":[{"t":"ideal-gas model","note":"simplified gas picture"}],"calc":["assumption 1: particles occupy **no volume**","assumption 2: **no attractions**"]},{"text":"**'Attract weakly' describes a real gas**, not the ideal model. The model assumes zero attraction.","marks":[{"t":"an assumption of the ideal-gas model","note":"no volume, NO attraction"}]},{"text":"**The ideal-gas law: PV = nRT.** P = pressure, V = volume, n = moles, R = gas constant, T = temperature in kelvin. Solve for V.","marks":[{"t":"R = 0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹","note":"gas constant R"}],"calc":["PV = nRT","V = nRT ÷ P"]},{"text":"**Gas laws need kelvin.** Add 273 to Celsius.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"+ 273 = 298 K"}],"calc":["T = 25 + 273 = **298 K**"]},{"text":"**Plug in and multiply.**","marks":[{"t":"1.00 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"R = 0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹","note":"R"},{"t":"1.00 atm","note":"÷ P"}],"calc":["V = (1.00)(0.0821)(298) ÷ 1.00","0.0821 × 298 = 24.47","V = **24.5 L**"]},{"text":"**Trap: 22.4 L is the volume at 0 °C (273 K)**, not 25 °C. Warmer gas takes up more room.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"not 0 °C: not 22.4 L"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Volume of 1.00 mol ideal gas at 1.00 atm","x":{"label":"Temperature (K)","min":250,"max":325,"step":25},"y":{"label":"Volume (L)","min":20,"max":27,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"V = nRT/P","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[250,20.5],[273,22.4],[298,24.5],[325,26.7]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":273},{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":298}],"labels":[{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":273,"text":"0 °C: 22.4 L","at":"left"},{"series":"V = nRT/P","x":298,"text":"25 °C: 24.5 L","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ideal gas = no particle volume + no attractions. V = nRT/P with T in kelvin; 22.4 L/mol only at 0 °C and 1 atm."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"The figure shows the relative absorbance of the three cone pigments. A 500-nm light and a 600-nm light are each adjusted in intensity so that the L pigment absorbs the same number of photons from both. How can the visual system still tell the two lights apart?",idStem:"Cone cells contain several opsins that absorb at different wavelengths. What does that allow?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Relative absorbance of the three human cone pigments","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":675,"step":25},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"S pigment","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.8],[420,1],[425,0.99],[450,0.61],[475,0.19],[500,0.03],[525,0],[550,0],[575,0],[600,0],[625,0],[650,0],[675,0]]},{"name":"M pigment","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0],[425,0.02],[450,0.11],[475,0.34],[500,0.7],[525,0.98],[534,1],[550,0.92],[575,0.59],[600,0.26],[625,0.08],[650,0.01],[675,0]]},{"name":"L pigment","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0],[425,0.01],[450,0.04],[475,0.14],[500,0.36],[525,0.69],[550,0.95],[564,1],[575,0.97],[600,0.73],[625,0.4],[650,0.16],[675,0.05]]}]},choices:["The L cones signal more strongly for the longer wavelength","The ratio of M to L absorption differs for the two lights","Each cone pigment absorbs only one of the two lights","The S cones respond strongly to the 600-nm light"],ans:1,exp:"A single pigment cannot report wavelength: once it absorbs the same number of photons, its cone gives the **same signal** whatever the color. Comparing pigments does: at 500 nm, M absorbs 0.7 vs L 0.36 (**M/L ≈ 1.9**); at 600 nm, M absorbs 0.26 vs L 0.73 (**M/L ≈ 0.36**). The brain reads color from these **ratios** across overlapping pigments.",src:"pe1-cp:30",how:{"ask":"If the L cones absorb equally, they send identical signals. Which other pigment absorbs the two lights very differently, and what comparison does that allow?","know":"• A photoreceptor only counts absorbed photons. Its output cannot tell a dim light at its best wavelength from a bright light at a poor one (the **principle of univariance**).\n• **Color vision** needs at least two pigments with **overlapping** absorption curves; the brain compares their responses. Humans have three cone pigments: S (≈420 nm), M (≈534 nm), L (≈564 nm). Rods (≈498 nm) work in dim light and give no color.\n• Reading an absorbance graph: go up from a wavelength on the x-axis to each curve, then across to the y-axis.","steps":[{"text":"Lock in the condition: L absorbs the same number of photons from both lights, so L cones send identical signals.","hl":"the L pigment absorbs the same number of photons from both","strike":["The L cones signal more strongly for the longer wavelength"],"graph":{"focus":["L pigment"],"labels":[{"series":"L pigment","x":500,"text":"500-nm light","at":"left"},{"series":"L pigment","x":600,"text":"600-nm light","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Read every pigment at 500 nm.","graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"read":[{"series":"M pigment","x":500},{"series":"L pigment","x":500},{"series":"S pigment","x":500}],"xLine":500,"spans":[{"x":497,"from":0,"to":0.7,"text":"M = 0.7","side":"left"},{"x":503,"from":0,"to":0.36,"text":"L = 0.36"}],"labels":[{"x":500,"y":0.9,"text":"M/L ≈ 1.9","at":"left"}]},"calc":["M = **0.7**, L = **0.36**, S ≈ 0.03","M/L ≈ 0.7 ÷ 0.36 ≈ **1.9**"]},{"text":"Now read them at 600 nm.","graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"read":[{"series":"M pigment","x":600},{"series":"L pigment","x":600},{"series":"S pigment","x":600}],"xLine":600,"spans":[{"x":597,"from":0,"to":0.26,"text":"M = 0.26","side":"left"},{"x":603,"from":0,"to":0.73,"text":"L = 0.73"}],"labels":[{"x":600,"y":0.9,"text":"M/L ≈ 0.36","at":"right"}]},"calc":["M = **0.26**, L = **0.73**, S = 0","M/L ≈ 0.26 ÷ 0.73 ≈ **0.36**"]},{"text":"Both M and L absorb both lights, because the curves overlap; S absorbs essentially nothing at 600 nm.","strike":["Each cone pigment absorbs only one of the two lights","The S cones respond strongly to the 600-nm light"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":475,"x2":625,"text":"M and L overlap"}],"labels":[{"series":"S pigment","x":600,"text":"S = 0 at 600","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Answer: the M-to-L ratio differs. Rule to reuse: one pigment gives brightness only; color comes from comparing overlapping pigments at the same wavelength.","graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"labels":[{"x":500,"y":0.9,"text":"M/L ≈ 1.9","at":"left"},{"x":600,"y":0.9,"text":"M/L ≈ 0.36","at":"right"}]}}],"trap":"'The L cones signal more strongly for the longer wavelength' ignores the setup: the intensities were adjusted so L absorbs equally, and a cone's signal depends only on photons absorbed. The S pigment absorbs almost nothing at 600 nm."},teach:[{"text":"**Up and down = how strongly a pigment absorbs** light of that color. 1 = its favorite color; 0 = it ignores that light.","marks":[{"t":"relative absorbance","note":"up-down axis"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = wavelength, i.e. color**: blue near 450 nm, green near 530 nm, orange-red near 600 nm.","marks":[{"t":"500-nm light","note":"blue-green"},{"t":"600-nm light","note":"orange"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Three curves = three kinds of cones**, the color-sensing cells of the eye. Each hill is one pigment; its peak is the color it absorbs best.","marks":[{"t":"three cone pigments","note":"3 color sensors: S, M, L"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"S pigment","x":420,"text":"S peaks at 420","at":"right"},{"series":"M pigment","x":534,"text":"M peaks at 534","at":"above"},{"series":"L pigment","x":564,"text":"L peaks at 564","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**The key condition:** the lights are dimmed or brightened until the L cone catches the same number of photons from each. A cone only counts photons, so **L sends the same signal for both**.","marks":[{"t":"the L pigment absorbs the same number of photons from both","note":"L signal identical"}],"graph":{"focus":["L pigment"],"labels":[{"series":"L pigment","x":500,"text":"500 nm","at":"left"},{"series":"L pigment","x":600,"text":"600 nm","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**The hills overlap.** Across the middle of the spectrum, M and L BOTH absorb the same light, just by different amounts.","graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"shade":[{"x1":475,"x2":625,"text":"M and L overlap"}]}},{"text":"**Read both pigments at 500 nm**: go straight up and read each curve's height.","calc":["M = **0.70**, L = **0.36**","M ÷ L ≈ **1.9** (M wins)"],"graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"read":[{"series":"M pigment","x":500},{"series":"L pigment","x":500}],"spans":[{"x":497,"from":0,"to":0.7,"text":"M = 0.7","side":"left"},{"x":503,"from":0,"to":0.36,"text":"L = 0.36"}]}},{"text":"**Now read them at 600 nm.**","calc":["M = **0.26**, L = **0.73**","M ÷ L ≈ **0.36** (L wins)"],"graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"],"read":[{"series":"M pigment","x":600},{"series":"L pigment","x":600}],"spans":[{"x":597,"from":0,"to":0.26,"text":"M = 0.26","side":"left"},{"x":603,"from":0,"to":0.73,"text":"L = 0.73"}]}},{"text":"**The ratio flips.** Even when L's signal is matched, M fires more than L at 500 nm and less than L at 600 nm. The brain reads color from this **M-to-L comparison**.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"M pigment","x":500,"text":"500: M/L ≈ 1.9","at":"left"},{"series":"M pigment","x":600,"text":"600: M/L ≈ 0.36","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** L's signal is matched, so it can't differ; M and L both absorb both lights; S absorbs essentially nothing at 600 nm.","marks":[{"t":"same number of photons","note":"L can't tell them apart"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"S pigment","x":600,"text":"S ≈ 0 at 600 nm","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** one receptor type can't report color (it can't separate color from brightness); color comes from the ratio of responses across overlapping receptors.","graph":{"focus":["M pigment","L pigment"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"A lipid-transfer protein carries cholesterol, shown, in a deep pocket shielded from water. Which amino acid would most likely make up most of the pocket's lining?",idStem:"A binding pocket holds retinal, a molecule made almost entirely of C–H bonds. What is the pocket most likely like?",fig:"cholesterol",choiceFigs:["p1-serine","p1-leucine","p1-aspartate","p1-lysine"],choices:["Serine","Leucine","Aspartate","Lysine"],ans:1,exp:"Cholesterol is **27 carbons and one –OH**: almost entirely hydrocarbon. Its rings and tail are best surrounded by **nonpolar side chains** such as leucine's isobutyl group (like dissolves like, through London dispersion forces). Charged aspartate and lysine, and polar serine, prefer water and would pay a cost to sit against hydrocarbon.",src:"pe1-cp:31",how:{"ask":"What fraction of cholesterol's surface is hydrocarbon, and which side chain shown is itself hydrocarbon?","know":"• **Side-chain classes**: nonpolar (Ala, Val, **Leu**, Ile, Met, Phe, Trp, Pro); polar uncharged (**Ser**, Thr, Asn, Gln, Tyr, Cys); acidic, negative at pH 7.4 (**Asp**, Glu); basic, positive (**Lys**, Arg; His partly).\n• **Like dissolves like**: hydrocarbon surfaces pack best against other hydrocarbon (London dispersion forces); burying a charge away from water costs a lot of energy.\n• On a structure: count C–H regions vs O and N atoms to judge polarity.","steps":[{"text":"Scan the ligand: four fused rings and a branched tail, with one –OH, highlighted.","fig":"p1-cholesterol-oh","calc":["C₂₇H₄₆O: **27 C**, 46 H, **1 O**","≈ 96% of heavy atoms are carbon → **hydrophobic**"]},{"text":"The pocket is shielded from water, so the lining must match a hydrocarbon surface.","hl":"in a deep pocket shielded from water"},{"text":"Look at each side chain. Aspartate ends in –COO⁻ and lysine in –NH₃⁺ at pH 7.4: charges that want water.","strike":["Aspartate","Lysine"]},{"text":"Serine's –CH₂OH is polar. Leucine's side chain, highlighted, is all C and H: it packs against cholesterol's rings and tail.","fig":"leucine-sc","strike":["Serine"]},{"text":"Answer: leucine. Rule to reuse: match the ligand. Mostly C–H → nonpolar residues line the pocket; charged groups → oppositely charged residues."}],"trap":"Serine is tempting because cholesterol has an –OH, and one polar residue may meet that –OH near the pocket's mouth. But a single OH on a 27-carbon molecule cannot set the character of most of the lining."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** each corner is a carbon with hidden H's. Cholesterol = four fused rings plus a carbon tail. Wedges/dashes show 3D; ignore them here.","fig":"t1-chol-read"},{"text":"**27 carbons, one OH.** Almost everything is carbon and hydrogen.","fig":"t1-chol-count"},{"text":"**Polar vs nonpolar:** O and N pull electrons, giving charged ends (polar). C–H shares evenly (nonpolar). Water is polar.","fig":"t1-chol-polar"},{"text":"**Like surrounds like:** a hydrocarbon in a water-free pocket is best lined by nonpolar groups, which attract through London dispersion forces.","fig":"t1-chol-pocket"},{"text":"**Amino acids share one backbone**; only the side chain differs. Leucine's side chain is only C and H: nonpolar.","fig":"t1-leucine-sc"},{"text":"Serine's side chain ends in **OH**: polar, so it prefers water.","fig":"t1-serine-sc"},{"text":"**Charged side chains:** at body pH aspartate is COO⁻ and lysine is NH₃⁺. Full charges strongly prefer water.","fig":["t1-asp-charged","t1-lys-charged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to line a pocket for a nonpolar molecule, pick side chains of only C and H. In each drawing, check the side chain for O, N or a charge.","fig":"t1-leucine-sc"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"A protein kinase is to be detected by the radioactivity it transfers to a peptide substrate. ATP is prepared with one radioactive atom at one of the labeled positions: ³²P at site I, II or III, or ¹⁴C at site IV. Which labeled ATP makes the phosphorylated peptide radioactive?",idStem:"A kinase phosphorylates a protein. Where does the phosphate come from?",fig:"p1-atp-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:2,exp:"A kinase transfers the **terminal (γ) phosphoryl group** of ATP to an –OH of the substrate (Ser, Thr or Tyr), leaving ADP. Site III is the **γ-phosphorus**, the one farthest from the ribose, so only that label moves to the peptide. The α (I) and β (II) phosphates and the ribose carbon (IV) all stay in ADP.",src:"pe1-cp:32",how:{"ask":"Which part of ATP leaves when a kinase acts, and which labeled atom belongs to that part?","know":"• **ATP** = adenine + ribose + three phosphates in a row. Counting out from the ribose: **α** (attached to C5′ of ribose), **β**, then **γ** (terminal).\n• **Kinase reaction**: ATP + substrate–OH → **ADP + substrate–O–PO₃²⁻**. The γ-phosphate moves; ADP keeps α and β.\n• Other enzymes break ATP elsewhere: adenylyl cyclase and aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases release PPi (β + γ) and keep α with AMP.\n• On a structure, follow the chain of P atoms from the sugar outward.","steps":[{"text":"The radioactivity must end up in the phosphorylated peptide, so the label must ride on the group the kinase moves.","hl":"makes the phosphorylated peptide radioactive","calc":["ATP + peptide–OH → ADP + peptide–O–**PO₃²⁻**","the moved group = the **terminal (γ)** phosphoryl"]},{"text":"Name the phosphates on the structure by walking out from the ribose: I is α, bonded through O to C5′; II is β; III is γ, the end of the chain. IV is the ribose C1′.","fig":"p1-atp-sites"},{"text":"α and β stay behind as ADP, and the ribose never leaves ADP.","strike":["I","II","IV"]},{"text":"The γ-phosphoryl group, highlighted, is what the kinase hands to the peptide.","fig":"p1-atp-gamma"},{"text":"Answer: III. Rule to reuse: kinases transfer the γ-phosphate; find it as the P farthest from the sugar."}],"trap":"Site I is the phosphate closest to the ribose (α). It is attached to the sugar, so it stays with ADP; only the outermost phosphate is transferred."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** ATP = a base (adenine: rings with N), a sugar ring, and a chain of three phosphates (P with O's).","fig":"t1-atp-read"},{"text":"**Phosphates are named from the sugar outward:** α, β, γ. γ is the last one, at the very end.","fig":"t1-atp-greek"},{"text":"**Kinase** = an enzyme that moves one phosphate from ATP onto another molecule. It always takes the end one, γ.","fig":"t1-atp-gamma-move"},{"text":"The γ phosphate lands on an OH of the peptide (here serine). **Any atom in that phosphate is now on the peptide.**","fig":["t1-ser-oh-hl","t1-phosphoserine"]},{"text":"**What's left is ADP** (two phosphates): base, sugar, α and β.","fig":"t1-adp"},{"text":"Labels on α, β or the sugar carbon **stay behind in ADP** and never reach the peptide.","fig":"t1-atp-stay"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a tag goes wherever its atom goes, and kinases move only ATP's γ (end) phosphate. For each label, ask: is it in the last phosphate?","fig":"t1-atp-greek"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"The molecule shown exists as two geometric isomers that do not interconvert at room temperature. Which labeled carbon has trigonal planar geometry and is part of the bond that cannot rotate?",idStem:"How are the carbons of a C=C double bond hybridized? (Each has three groups and no lone pairs.)",fig:"p1-pentenenitrile-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"Carbon II is one end of the **C=C**: it bonds to three atoms with no lone pair → **3 electron domains → sp², trigonal planar, ≈120°**. Its leftover **unhybridized p orbital** overlaps side by side with the p orbital on the other alkene carbon to make the **π bond**, and twisting would break that overlap, so cis and trans forms do not interconvert. I and III are sp³; IV is the nitrile carbon, **sp** and linear.",src:"pe1-cp:33",how:{"ask":"Count the atoms bonded to each labeled carbon (lone pairs too), convert to a hybridization, then ask which bond's π overlap would be destroyed by twisting.","know":"• **Hybridization from electron domains** (each atom bonded = 1 domain, each lone pair = 1, a multiple bond counts once): **4 → sp³**, tetrahedral, 109.5°; **3 → sp²**, trigonal planar, 120°; **2 → sp**, linear, 180°.\n• **σ bond** = head-on overlap; **π bond** = side-by-side overlap of **unhybridized p orbitals**. A double bond = 1 σ + 1 π; a triple bond = 1 σ + 2 π.\n• Rotating a C=C would break the π overlap → no free rotation → **cis/trans (E/Z) isomers** when each alkene carbon has two different groups.\n• A C≡C or C≡N is linear, so it has no cis/trans forms.","steps":[{"text":"Geometric isomers that do not interconvert point to a C=C: rotation there would break the π bond.","hl":"two geometric isomers that do not interconvert"},{"text":"Count domains for each labeled carbon on the structure.","fig":"p1-pentenenitrile-sites","calc":["I, CH₃: 4 atoms → **sp³**","II, =CH–: 3 atoms, 0 lone pairs → **sp²**","III, CH₂: 4 atoms → **sp³**","IV, –C≡N: 2 atoms → **sp**"]},{"text":"sp³ carbons have only single bonds, which rotate freely.","strike":["I","III"]},{"text":"The nitrile carbon is sp and linear; its π bonds cannot make cis/trans forms.","strike":["IV"]},{"text":"The two alkene carbons, highlighted, are sp². Each has one unhybridized p orbital; the two overlap side by side as the π bond that locks the geometry.","fig":"p1-pentenenitrile-hl"},{"text":"Answer: II. Rule to reuse: 3 domains = sp² = 120°; a π bond from p-orbital overlap is what stops rotation."}],"trap":"IV is part of a multiple bond too, the C≡N. But the nitrile carbon bonds to only two atoms: sp, linear. A straight line has no 'same side' or 'opposite side', so it cannot create geometric isomers."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** corners and line ends are carbons; hidden H's give each carbon 4 bonds. = is a double bond, ≡ a triple bond.","fig":"t1-pn-read"},{"text":"**Electron groups:** each neighboring atom counts once (even via = or ≡), plus lone pairs. **4 → tetrahedral (109.5°), 3 → flat triangle (120°), 2 → straight line (180°).**"},{"text":"Carbon I is a CH₃: one carbon plus three hidden H's = **4 groups, tetrahedral**.","fig":"t1-pn-i"},{"text":"Carbon II touches carbon I, the other C=C carbon, and one hidden H: **3 groups, trigonal planar** (flat), and part of the C=C.","fig":"t1-pn-ii"},{"text":"Carbon III is a CH₂: two carbons plus two hidden H's = **4 groups, tetrahedral**.","fig":"t1-pn-iii"},{"text":"Carbon IV touches only the CH₂ and the triple-bonded N: **2 groups, linear**.","fig":"t1-pn-iv"},{"text":"**Why C=C can't rotate:** its second (π) bond is side-by-side overlap above and below; twisting breaks it. So cis and trans forms stay separate.","fig":"t1-pn-pi"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** neighbors + lone pairs → 4 tetrahedral, 3 trigonal planar, 2 linear; only a C=C locks rotation. Count for each labeled carbon.","fig":"p1-pentenenitrile-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Pancreatic lipase is incubated with the triacylglycerol shown in an unbuffered solution. The enzyme hydrolyzes only the esters on the two end carbons of glycerol, and the pH of the solution falls. Besides butanoic acid, which product forms?",idStem:"What reaction does a lipase catalyze?",fig:"p1-tributyrin",choiceFigs:["p1-2-mag","p1-1-mag","p1-12-dag","p1-glycerol"],choices:["2-Monoacylglycerol","1-Monoacylglycerol","1,2-Diacylglycerol","Glycerol"],ans:0,exp:"Lipase **hydrolyzes ester bonds**: each ester + H₂O → carboxylic acid + alcohol. Cutting the esters on **C1 and C3** releases **two butanoic acids** (which lower the pH) and leaves glycerol with its **C2 ester intact**: a 2-monoacylglycerol.",src:"pe1-cp:34",how:{"ask":"Which ester bonds are cut, what does each cut release, and what is left attached to glycerol afterward?","know":"• A **triacylglycerol** = glycerol (three –OH carbons: C1, C2, C3) joined by **ester** bonds to three fatty acids.\n• **Ester hydrolysis**: R–C(=O)–O–R′ + H₂O → R–COOH + HO–R′. The bond that breaks is between the C=O carbon and the bridging O.\n• **Pancreatic lipase** cuts at C1 and C3 only → **2 free fatty acids + a 2-monoacylglycerol**.\n• Released carboxylic acids (pKa ≈ 4.8) give up H⁺ in water → pH falls.","steps":[{"text":"Read which bonds are cut: only the esters at the two end carbons.","hl":"only the esters on the two end carbons of glycerol"},{"text":"Find those two ester links on the structure, highlighted: each C=O carbon and its bridging O on C1 and C3.","fig":"p1-tributyrin-cut"},{"text":"Write the hydrolysis twice.","calc":["ester + H₂O → R–COOH + HO–glycerol","× **2** (C1 and C3) → **2** butanoic acid","C2 ester **untouched** → 2-monoacylglycerol","butanoic acid pKa ≈ 4.8 → releases H⁺ → **pH falls**"]},{"text":"Glycerol needs three cuts; a diacylglycerol only one; a 1-monoacylglycerol would mean the middle ester was cut.","strike":["Glycerol","1,2-Diacylglycerol","1-Monoacylglycerol"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-monoacylglycerol. Rule to reuse: count which esters break; each one gives a free acid, and whatever is not cut stays on glycerol.","fig":"p1-2-mag"}],"trap":"Glycerol would require all three esters to be cut, but the enzyme leaves the middle one. 1-Monoacylglycerol keeps an end ester, the kind the enzyme does cut, and has lost the middle one, which it does not."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** corners are carbons with hidden H's. The middle 3 carbons (C1–C3) are glycerol; each holds an O linked to a C=O chain.","fig":"t1-tributyrin-num"},{"text":"**C(=O)–O–C is an ester.** This fat has three, one on each glycerol carbon.","fig":"t1-tributyrin-esters"},{"text":"**Hydrolysis** = splitting with water. The C=O side becomes COOH; the glycerol side keeps the O as OH.","fig":"t1-trib-cut1"},{"text":"**Each cut frees butanoic acid.** Acids release H⁺, so the pH falls: proof that esters were cut.","fig":"t1-butanoic-acid"},{"text":"**Lipase cuts only the end esters** (C1 and C3), so two butanoic acids leave.","fig":"t1-trib-ends"},{"text":"**What's left:** glycerol with one chain still on (\"mono-acyl\"). The number in the name says which carbon holds it.","fig":"t1-2-mag-c2"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mark every ester, cut only those the enzyme allows, keep the rest. In each drawing, count chains left and see which carbon holds them.","fig":"t1-tributyrin-num"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"At pH 7.4, a protein carries three point mutations: Arg45→Gly, Lys72→Asp and Met90→Asp. Side-chain pKa values: Arg, 12.5; Lys, 10.5; Asp, 3.9; Gly and Met have no ionizable side chain. By how much does the protein's net charge change?",idStem:"At pH 7, a protein has three mutations: Arg → Gly, Lys → Asp and Met → Asp. What is the change in net charge?",choices:["−4","+4","−3","−2"],ans:0,exp:"At pH 7.4, Arg and Lys are **+1**, Asp is **−1**, Gly and Met are 0. Changes: R→G = **−1**; K→D = +1 → −1 = **−2**; M→D = **−1**. Total = **−4**.",src:"pe1-cp:35",how:{"ask":"For each mutation, write the side-chain charge before and after at pH 7.4, then add the changes.","know":"• **pH < pKa → protonated; pH > pKa → deprotonated.**\n• At pH 7.4: Arg, Lys → +1 (protonated basic group); Asp, Glu → −1 (deprotonated acid); His mostly 0; nonpolar residues 0.\n• Change in charge = charge after − charge before.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each side chain's charge at pH 7.4 from its pKa.","hl":"Side-chain pKa values: Arg, 12.5; Lys, 10.5; Asp, 3.9","calc":["Arg: 7.4 < 12.5 → **+1**","Lys: 7.4 < 10.5 → **+1**","Asp: 7.4 > 3.9 → **−1**","Gly, Met → **0**"]},{"text":"Find the change at each site: new charge minus old charge.","hl":"Arg45→Gly, Lys72→Asp and Met90→Asp","calc":["R45G: 0 − (+1) = **−1**","K72D: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","M90D: (−1) − 0 = **−1**"],"fig":["arginine-sc","lysine-sc","aspartate-sc"]},{"text":"Compare before and after at each site.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain charge at pH 7.4","x":{"label":"Site"},"y":{"label":"Charge","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Site 45","Site 72","Site 90"],"series":[{"name":"Before","values":[1,1,0]},{"name":"After","values":[0,-1,-1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Site 72","Before"],["Site 72","After"]]}},{"text":"Add them: −1 − 2 − 1 = −4. −2 or −3 come from treating K→D as only −1; +4 has the sign flipped.","calc":["Δ = −1 + (−2) + (−1) = **−4**"],"strike":["+4","−3","−2"]},{"text":"Answer: −4."}],"trap":"−3 comes from counting Lys→Asp as only −1 (losing the + but forgetting the new −). Swapping a positive residue for a negative one changes the charge by 2."},teach:[{"text":"**Start with what you're given:** the pH around the protein, three swaps, and a pKa number for each side chain.","marks":[{"t":"pH 7.4","note":"acidity of the surroundings"},{"t":"Arg45→Gly","note":"swap #1 of 3"},{"t":"Side-chain pKa values","note":"when each one drops its H⁺"}]},{"text":"**The arrow means \"replaced by\".** The number is the position in the protein chain.","marks":[{"t":"Arg45→Gly","note":"45: Arg out, Gly in"},{"t":"Lys72→Asp","note":"72: Lys out, Asp in"},{"t":"Met90→Asp","note":"90: Met out, Asp in"}]},{"text":"**pKa = the pH where a group holds its H⁺ half the time.** Below its pKa it keeps the H⁺; above its pKa the H⁺ comes off.","marks":[{"t":"Asp, 3.9","note":"Asp pKa = 3.9"},{"t":"Lys, 10.5","note":"Lys pKa = 10.5"}]},{"text":"**See it at pH 7.4:** Lys is still holding its H⁺; Asp has already lost it.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Share of side chains still holding H⁺","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Fraction holding H⁺","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"series":[{"name":"Asp (pKa 3.9)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[2,0.99],[3.9,0.5],[6,0.01],[7.4,0],[9,0],[10.5,0],[12,0],[14,0]]},{"name":"Lys (pKa 10.5)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[2,1],[3.9,1],[6,1],[7.4,1],[9,0.97],[10.5,0.5],[12,0.03],[14,0]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Lys (pKa 10.5)","x":7.4,"text":"Lys keeps H⁺ → +1","at":"above"},{"series":"Asp (pKa 3.9)","x":7.4,"text":"Asp lost H⁺ → −1","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Charge at pH 7.4:** bases (Arg, Lys) holding an extra H⁺ are **+1**; an acid (Asp) that lost its H⁺ is **−1**.","marks":[{"t":"Arg, 12.5","note":"7.4 < 12.5 → +1"},{"t":"Lys, 10.5","note":"7.4 < 10.5 → +1"},{"t":"Asp, 3.9","note":"7.4 > 3.9 → −1"}]},{"text":"**No ionizable group = nothing to gain or lose = charge 0.**","marks":[{"t":"Gly and Met have no ionizable side chain","note":"Gly = 0, Met = 0"}]},{"text":"**Change at each site = charge after − charge before.**","marks":[{"t":"Arg45→Gly","note":"0 − (+1) = −1"},{"t":"Lys72→Asp","note":"(−1) − (+1) = −2"},{"t":"Met90→Asp","note":"(−1) − 0 = −1"}],"calc":["R45G: 0 − (+1) = **−1**","K72D: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","M90D: (−1) − 0 = **−1**"]},{"text":"**Site 72 is the trap:** it loses a + AND gains a −, so it drops by **2**, not 1.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain charge at pH 7.4","x":{"label":"Site"},"y":{"label":"Charge","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Site 45","Site 72","Site 90"],"series":[{"name":"Before","values":[1,1,0]},{"name":"After","values":[0,-1,-1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Site 72","Before"],["Site 72","After"]]}},{"text":"**Add the three changes.** −3 or −2 come from counting site 72 as only −1; +4 has the sign flipped.","marks":[{"t":"net charge change","note":"sum of the 3 changes"}],"calc":["Δ = (−1) + (−2) + (−1)","Δ = **−4**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare pH to pKa (pH below pKa → H⁺ on; above → H⁺ off), give each side chain its charge, then sum (after − before) at every site."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Gastric fluid with a pH of 1.5 empties into the duodenum, where secreted bicarbonate partially neutralizes it; the mixed duodenal fluid has a pH of 4.5. By what factor, and in which direction, has [H⁺] changed?",idStem:"Solution B has a pH 3 units lower than solution A. By what factor is [H⁺] higher in B?",choices:["It decreased by a factor of 3","It decreased by a factor of 30","It increased by a factor of 1000","It decreased by a factor of 1000"],ans:3,exp:"**pH = −log[H⁺]**, so each pH unit is a **factor of 10**. The pH rose by 4.5 − 1.5 = 3 units, so [H⁺] **fell** by 10³ = **1000**: from about 3 × 10⁻² M to 3 × 10⁻⁵ M.",src:"pe1-cp:37",how:{"ask":"How many pH units apart are the two fluids, what factor in [H⁺] does each unit stand for, and does a higher pH mean more or less H⁺?","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units means [H⁺] differs by a factor of **10^Δ**.\n• Lower pH = higher [H⁺].","steps":[{"text":"pH is a log scale: [H⁺] = 10^(−pH). One pH unit = a factor of 10.","calc":["[H⁺] = 10^(−pH)","ΔpH of 1 → [H⁺] × or ÷ **10**"]},{"text":"Find the pH change.","hl":"a pH of 1.5","calc":["ΔpH = 4.5 − 1.5 = **3** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"the mixed duodenal fluid has a pH of 4.5","calc":["factor = 10³ = **1000**","pH 1.5: [H⁺] = 10^−1.5 ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻² M","pH 4.5: [H⁺] = 10^−4.5 ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ M","3.2 × 10⁻² ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ = **1000**"],"strike":["It decreased by a factor of 3","It decreased by a factor of 30"]},{"text":"Direction: pH went UP, so H⁺ went DOWN, as expected when bicarbonate neutralizes acid.","hl":"secreted bicarbonate partially neutralizes it","strike":["It increased by a factor of 1000"]},{"text":"Answer: it decreased by a factor of 1000."}],"trap":"\"It increased by a factor of 1000\" has the right size but the wrong direction: a HIGHER pH means LESS H⁺. \"A factor of 3\" treats the log scale as linear."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers:** a starting pH, an ending pH, and the thing you must compare, [H⁺].","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 1.5","note":"start: stomach"},{"t":"a pH of 4.5","note":"end: duodenum"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**[H⁺] = how much acid (H⁺) is dissolved**, in mol per liter. More H⁺ = more acidic = LOWER pH.","marks":[{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"acid level, mol/L"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH). So every 1 pH unit is a factor of **10** in H⁺.","calc":["pH 1 → [H⁺] = 10⁻¹ M","pH 2 → [H⁺] = 10⁻² M (10× less)","1 pH unit = **× or ÷ 10**"],"marks":[{"t":"pH of 1.5","note":"10^−1.5 ≈ 0.03 M"}]},{"text":"**How far did pH move?** Subtract.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 1.5","note":"from 1.5"},{"t":"a pH of 4.5","note":"to 4.5"}],"calc":["ΔpH = 4.5 − 1.5 = **3 units**"]},{"text":"**3 units = three factors of 10 stacked:** 10 × 10 × 10.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"How many times more H⁺ than at pH 4.5","x":{"label":"pH"},"y":{"label":"Times more H⁺","min":0,"max":1000,"step":200},"categories":["pH 1.5","pH 2.5","pH 3.5","pH 4.5"],"series":[{"name":"[H⁺]","values":[1000,100,10,1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["pH 1.5","[H⁺]"],["pH 4.5","[H⁺]"]]},"calc":["10³ = **1000**"]},{"text":"**Check with real numbers:** both concentrations, then divide.","marks":[{"t":"By what factor","note":"10^ΔpH = 1000"}],"calc":["pH 1.5: 10^−1.5 ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻² M","pH 4.5: 10^−4.5 ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ M","ratio = **1000**"]},{"text":"**Direction:** bicarbonate is a base; it soaks up H⁺. pH went UP, so [H⁺] went **DOWN**.","marks":[{"t":"secreted bicarbonate partially neutralizes it","note":"base removes H⁺"},{"t":"in which direction","note":"down: fewer H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Tempting wrong numbers:** 3 is the pH change itself, not the factor; 30 is 3 × 10. Neither uses 10^ΔpH.","marks":[{"t":"By what factor","note":"not 3, not 30"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a pH change of Δ means [H⁺] changes by 10^Δ; pH up → [H⁺] down, pH down → [H⁺] up."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Cellulase digests cellulose into the disaccharide shown. In a Haworth projection of the residue whose C1 forms the link, the bond from C1 to the bridging oxygen points to the same face of the ring as that residue's CH₂OH group. The linkage is best categorized as:",idStem:"Glycogen synthase links glucose units into chains with which bond?",fig:"p1-cellobiose",choices:["α-1,4-glycosidic","β-1,4-glycosidic","α-1,6-glycosidic","β-1,6-glycosidic"],ans:1,exp:"The bridging O joins **C1** of one glucose (its anomeric carbon, bonded to two oxygens) to **C4** of the other: a **1,4** link. In a Haworth projection of a D-sugar, an anomeric substituent on the **same face as C6 (CH₂OH) is β**; the opposite face is α. So the bond is **β-1,4**, the linkage of cellulose (glycogen and starch use α-1,4).",src:"pe1-cp:39",how:{"ask":"Which two carbons does the bridging oxygen connect, counted from each ring's anomeric carbon, and is the C1 bond on the same face as the CH₂OH or the opposite face?","know":"• **Anomeric carbon (C1)** of an aldose: the ring carbon bonded to **two oxygens** (the ring O and one more).\n• **Numbering**: C1 is anomeric; go around the ring away from the ring O: C2, C3, C4, then C5 (bonded to the ring O), which carries C6 (the CH₂OH).\n• **Glycosidic bond** is named by the two carbons joined: 1,4 or 1,6.\n• **α vs β** for D-sugars in a Haworth projection: C1 substituent on the face **opposite** the CH₂OH → α; **same** face → **β**.\n• Cellulose = β-1,4; amylose, glycogen = α-1,4 with α-1,6 branches.","steps":[{"text":"Find the anomeric carbon of the linking residue, labeled C1: the ring carbon bonded to two oxygens, the ring O and the bridging O. The bridging O and the carbon it joins on the other ring are highlighted too.","fig":"p1-cellobiose-num"},{"text":"Number the other ring from its own anomeric carbon (the C bearing a free OH next to the ring O): C1 → C2 → C3 → C4. The bridging O sits on C4, not on the CH₂OH carbon (C6).","calc":["C1 – O – **C4′**  ⇒  **1,4**","C6 = the CH₂OH carbon → not involved"],"strike":["α-1,6-glycosidic","β-1,6-glycosidic"]},{"text":"Now the anomer. The stem says the C1 bond points to the same face as CH₂OH.","hl":"points to the same face of the ring as that residue's CH₂OH group"},{"text":"Rule for D-sugars: same face as CH₂OH → β; opposite face → α. Compare with the two glucose anomers, anomeric C highlighted.","fig":["beta-glucopyranose","alpha-glucopyranose"],"calc":["same face as C6 → **β**","opposite face → α"],"strike":["α-1,4-glycosidic"]},{"text":"Answer: β-1,4-glycosidic, as in cellulose. Rule to reuse: name the link by the two carbon numbers, then α/β by comparing C1's bond with the CH₂OH."}],"trap":"Any glucose drawing shows a CH₂OH, which tempts '1,6'. Check where the bridging O actually attaches: to a ring carbon two carbons away from the ring O (C4), not to the CH₂OH carbon (C6). And 'same face as CH₂OH' is β, not α."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** each 6-sided ring with one O is a glucose. Corners are carbons with hidden H's; wedge = toward you, dash = away.","fig":"t1-cello-read"},{"text":"**Find C1:** the carbon bonded to two oxygens (ring O + one outside). C6 is the CH₂OH outside the ring.","fig":"t1-cello-c1"},{"text":"Count around the right ring from its own C1: C2, C3, **C4**. So the bridge is a **1,4 link**. A 1,6 link would reach a CH₂ outside the ring.","fig":"t1-cellobiose-c4"},{"text":"**Haworth projection:** the ring drawn flat, each group pointing up or down. Compare C1's outside bond with the same ring's CH₂OH.","fig":"t1-cello-face"},{"text":"**Same side as CH₂OH = β; opposite side = α.** The question says same face.","fig":"t1-cello-beta"},{"text":"**Check:** cellulose (fiber humans can't digest) uses β links; starch and glycogen use α. Only C1's bond direction differs.","fig":["t1-cello-cellulose","t1-maltose"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find C1 (two O's), count to the partner carbon, then compare C1's bond with CH₂OH: same side β, opposite α. Apply both parts to each option.","fig":"t1-cellobiose-c4"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Deoxythymidine triphosphate, dTTP, is completely hydrolyzed, and the sugar is isolated from the products. Its formula is C₅H₁₀O₄. Which structure shown is the sugar?",idStem:"The nucleotide part of UDP-glucose contains which sugar?",choiceFigs:["deoxyribose","ribose","p1-3-deoxyribose","p1-2-deoxyglucose"],choices:["2-Deoxyribose","Ribose","3-Deoxyribose","2-Deoxyglucose"],ans:0,exp:"The 'd' in dTTP means **deoxy**: DNA nucleotides carry **2-deoxyribose**, a five-carbon furanose with **no –OH on C2′** (C₅H₁₀O₄). Ribose (C₅H₁₀O₅) has one more O; 2-deoxyglucose has six carbons. 3-Deoxyribose has the same formula, but its missing –OH is on the wrong carbon.",src:"pe1-cp:40",how:{"ask":"Which structures match C₅H₁₀O₄, and of those, which is missing its –OH at the carbon right next to C1?","know":"• **Nucleotide** = base + sugar + phosphate(s). RNA nucleotides (ATP, UTP, UDP…) use **ribose**, C₅H₁₀O₅; DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…) use **2-deoxyribose**, C₅H₁₀O₄.\n• **Numbering a furanose**: C1′ is the carbon bonded to two oxygens (ring O + OH, or the base in a nucleotide); go around the ring away from the ring O: C2′, C3′, C4′; C5′ is the CH₂OH outside the ring.\n• **2-Deoxy** = the –OH at C2′ is replaced by H. The C3′ –OH stays: it forms the next phosphodiester bond in DNA.","steps":[{"text":"Use the formula first: count C and O on each structure.","hl":"Its formula is C₅H₁₀O₄","calc":["ribose: 5 C, **5 O** → C₅H₁₀O₅ ✗","2-deoxyglucose: **6 C** → C₆H₁₂O₅ ✗","2-deoxyribose, 3-deoxyribose: 5 C, 4 O → C₅H₁₀O₄ ✓"],"strike":["Ribose","2-Deoxyglucose"]},{"text":"Two isomers remain. The 'd' in dTTP is the clue: a DNA nucleotide, whose sugar lacks the 2′ –OH.","hl":"Deoxythymidine triphosphate, dTTP"},{"text":"Number the ring: C1′ is the carbon with two oxygens; the next carbon, C2′ (highlighted), is a bare CH₂ in the DNA sugar.","fig":"p1-deoxyribose-c2"},{"text":"3-Deoxyribose has its CH₂ one carbon further around, at C3′; DNA needs the 3′ –OH for its phosphodiester links.","strike":["3-Deoxyribose"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-deoxyribose. Rule to reuse: check the formula, then number from C1′ to find which carbon lost its –OH."}],"trap":"3-Deoxyribose has exactly the formula given, so a formula check alone cannot pick it out. DNA lacks the 2′ –OH, and it keeps the 3′ –OH that links each nucleotide to the next."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read:** a 5-sided ring with one O is a sugar; corners are carbons with hidden H's. Number from C1′, bonded to two O's (′ marks sugar carbons).","fig":"t1-dr-read"},{"text":"**dTTP** = a DNA building block: a base (thymine) + sugar + three phosphates. Splitting it with water frees the sugar.","fig":"t1-thymine-base"},{"text":"**d = deoxy = missing one oxygen.** RNA's ribose (left) has an OH on C2′; DNA's deoxyribose (right) does not.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Count first:** C₅H₁₀O₄ = 5 carbons and 4 oxygens. Ribose has 5 oxygens (highlighted): one too many.","fig":"t1-ribose-o5"},{"text":"2-Deoxyglucose has an extra CH₂OH carbon: **6 carbons**, too many.","fig":"t1-2-deoxyglucose-c6"},{"text":"**Same formula, different spot:** one lacks the OH on C2′ (left), the other on C3′ (right). Find which carbon lost it.","fig":["t1-dr-c2","t1-3-deoxyribose-c3"]},{"text":"**DNA needs C3′–OH:** it bonds the next nucleotide's phosphate to grow the strand. So DNA's sugar lacks the C2′ OH instead.","fig":"t1-deoxyribose-3oh"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count carbons, then oxygens, then find which carbon lacks its OH; in DNA it's always C2′. Check each drawing for a plain CH₂ next to C1′.","fig":"t1-dr-c2"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Two tracers, ¹⁴C and ³²P, each decay by emitting an electron and an antineutrino from the nucleus. Which daughter nuclides form?",idStem:"Carbon-14 and phosphorus-32 both undergo β⁻ decay. Which atoms form?",choices:["¹⁴B and ³²Si","¹⁴N and ³²S","¹⁴N and ³²Si","¹⁰Be and ²⁸Al"],ans:1,exp:"Emitting an electron from the nucleus is **β⁻ decay: a neutron → proton + electron**, so **Z rises by 1 and A stays the same**. C (Z = 6) → N (7); P (15) → S (16).",src:"pe1-cp:42",how:{"ask":"When the nucleus emits an electron, what happens to its atomic number Z and mass number A?","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. **Z + 1, A unchanged**.\n• **β⁺ decay**: p → n + e⁺. Z − 1, A unchanged.\n• **α decay**: lose ⁴He. Z − 2, A − 4.\n• Element identity is set by Z: B 5, C 6, N 7, Al 13, Si 14, P 15, S 16.","steps":[{"text":"An electron leaving the NUCLEUS means a neutron turned into a proton: β⁻ decay. Z goes up by 1; A stays the same.","hl":"each decay by emitting an electron and an antineutrino from the nucleus","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + ν̄","Z → Z **+ 1**,  A → A"]},{"text":"Apply it to carbon-14 (Z = 6).","hl":"¹⁴C","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 6 = 7 + (−1) ✓   A: 14 = 14 + 0 ✓"]},{"text":"Apply it to phosphorus-32 (Z = 15).","hl":"³²P","calc":["³²₁₅P → ³²₁₆S + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 15 = 16 + (−1) ✓   A: 32 = 32 ✓"]},{"text":"Eliminate: B and Si lower Z (positron emission); ¹⁰Be and ²⁸Al lose 4 mass units (alpha decay); one right and one wrong is still wrong.","strike":["¹⁴B and ³²Si","¹⁰Be and ²⁸Al","¹⁴N and ³²Si"]},{"text":"Answer: ¹⁴N and ³²S."}],"trap":"¹⁴B and ³²Si move Z DOWN by 1, which is positron (β⁺) decay. Losing a negative charge from the nucleus leaves it MORE positive."},teach:[{"text":"**Two radioactive atoms** each throw something out of their nucleus. You must say what each one becomes.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁴C","note":"carbon-14"},{"t":"³²P","note":"phosphorus-32"},{"t":"an electron and an antineutrino","note":"what flies out"}]},{"text":"**Read the symbol:** the small top number is **A** (protons + neutrons). The element name tells **Z** (protons), and Z decides the element.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁴C","note":"A = 14, C means Z = 6"},{"t":"³²P","note":"A = 32, P means Z = 15"}]},{"text":"**A nucleus holds no electrons.** For one to come out of it, a neutron must change into a proton + electron. This is **β⁻ (beta-minus) decay**.","marks":[{"t":"from the nucleus","note":"a neutron split up here"}],"calc":["n → p⁺ + e⁻ + ν̄"]},{"text":"**The antineutrino** is a tiny particle with no charge and almost no mass: it changes nothing in the count.","marks":[{"t":"antineutrino","note":"no charge, ~no mass"}]},{"text":"**One neutron became a proton:** protons +1, neutrons −1, mass number A unchanged.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Particles in the nucleus: carbon-14 vs nitrogen-14","x":{"label":"Nucleus"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["¹⁴C (before)","¹⁴N (after)"],"series":[{"name":"Protons","values":[6,7]},{"name":"Neutrons","values":[8,7]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["¹⁴C (before)","Protons"],["¹⁴N (after)","Protons"]]},"calc":["Z → Z **+ 1**,  A stays"]},{"text":"**Carbon-14:** Z goes 6 → 7, and Z = 7 is nitrogen.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁴C","note":"Z 6 → 7: ¹⁴N"}],"calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","top: 14 = 14 + 0 ✓  bottom: 6 = 7 − 1 ✓"]},{"text":"**Phosphorus-32:** Z goes 15 → 16, and Z = 16 is sulfur.","marks":[{"t":"³²P","note":"Z 15 → 16: ³²S"}],"calc":["³²₁₅P → ³²₁₆S + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","top: 32 = 32 + 0 ✓  bottom: 15 = 16 − 1 ✓"]},{"text":"**Traps:** B and Si have Z going DOWN (positron decay). ¹⁰Be and ²⁸Al lost 4 mass units (alpha decay). A pair with one right and one wrong is still wrong.","marks":[{"t":"Which daughter nuclides","note":"both must be right"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ (electron out of nucleus) → Z + 1, A same. β⁺ → Z − 1, A same. α → Z − 2, A − 4. Then look up the new element by Z."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The figure shows the titration of 20.0 mL of 0.10 M H₃PO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH. Based on the curve, what is the approximate Ka of H₂PO₄⁻?",idStem:"Which acid dissociates LEAST in water?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 20.0 mL of 0.10 M H₃PO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1.6],[5,1.8],[10,2.1],[15,2.6],[18,3.2],[20,4.7],[22,6.2],[25,6.7],[30,7.2],[35,7.7],[38,8.4],[40,9.7],[42,11],[45,11.6],[50,11.9]]}]},choices:["8 × 10⁻³","2 × 10⁻⁵","6 × 10⁻⁸","2 × 10⁻¹⁰"],ans:2,exp:"H₃PO₄ is triprotic; 20.0 mL × 0.10 M = 2.0 mmol, so each equivalence point needs **20 mL** of NaOH (at 20 and 40 mL). H₂PO₄⁻ is the acid titrated in the **second** stage, and its half-equivalence point is at **30 mL**, where **pH = pKa₂ ≈ 7.2**. Ka = 10^−7.2 ≈ **6 × 10⁻⁸**.",src:"pe1-cp:44",how:{"ask":"Where on the curve are the equivalence points, which stage titrates H₂PO₄⁻, and what is the pH halfway through that stage?","know":"• **Polyprotic acid**: loses H⁺ one at a time, each step with its own Ka; each Ka is far smaller than the one before (Ka₁ > Ka₂ > Ka₃).\n• **Equivalence point**: moles OH⁻ added = moles of the H⁺ being removed; on the curve, the steep vertical jump.\n• **Half-equivalence point**: halfway to an equivalence point, [acid] = [conjugate base], so by Henderson–Hasselbalch **pH = pKa**. It sits in the flat buffer region.\n• Ka = 10^(−pKa).","steps":[{"text":"Find the equivalence volumes. One H⁺ per 2.0 mmol needs 20 mL of 0.10 M NaOH.","hl":"20.0 mL of 0.10 M H₃PO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","calc":["n(H₃PO₄) = 20.0 mL × 0.10 M = **2.0 mmol**","V per equivalence = 2.0 mmol ÷ 0.10 M = **20 mL**","equivalence points at **20** and **40** mL"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"1st equivalence: 20 mL","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":40,"text":"2nd equivalence: 40 mL","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Match each stage to its acid: 0–20 mL removes H⁺ from H₃PO₄; 20–40 mL removes H⁺ from H₂PO₄⁻.","calc":["stage 1: H₃PO₄ → H₂PO₄⁻ (Ka₁)","stage 2: **H₂PO₄⁻ → HPO₄²⁻** (Ka₂)"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":20,"text":"stage 1: Ka₁"},{"x1":20,"x2":40,"text":"stage 2: Ka₂"}]}},{"text":"Read the pH halfway through stage 2, at 30 mL, where [H₂PO₄⁻] = [HPO₄²⁻].","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":30}],"xLine":30,"yLine":7.2,"shade":[{"x1":20,"x2":40,"text":"stage 2"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":30,"text":"midpoint: pH 7.2 = pKa₂","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":30,"from":0,"to":7.2,"text":"pKa₂ = 7.2","side":"left"}]},"calc":["pH at 30 mL = **7.2** = pKa₂","Ka₂ = 10^−7.2 ≈ **6 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"The other values come from the wrong spots: pH 2.1 at 10 mL is pKa₁; pH 4.7 at 20 mL and 9.7 at 40 mL are equivalence points.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":10},{"series":"pH","x":20},{"series":"pH","x":40}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"pKa₁ = 2.1","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"equiv: 4.7","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":40,"text":"equiv: 9.7","at":"left"}]},"strike":["8 × 10⁻³","2 × 10⁻⁵","2 × 10⁻¹⁰"]},{"text":"Answer: about 6 × 10⁻⁸. Rule to reuse: pKa = pH at the midpoint of a flat buffer region, never at a steep jump."}],"trap":"2 × 10⁻⁵ comes from reading pH 4.7 at 20 mL, but that is an equivalence point, the middle of a steep jump, not a half-equivalence point. 8 × 10⁻³ is Ka₁, read at 10 mL, the first stage, H₃PO₄ itself."},teach:[{"text":"**What's in the flask, what's being added, what's asked.**","marks":[{"t":"20.0 mL of 0.10 M H₃PO₄","note":"acid in the flask"},{"t":"0.10 M NaOH","note":"base poured in"},{"t":"Ka of H₂PO₄⁻","note":"what we must find"}]},{"text":"**Up and down = pH.** Low = acidic (lots of H⁺); high = basic (little H⁺).","graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = mL of NaOH poured in.** Every mL of base pulls some H⁺ off the acid.","graph":{"axis":"x","labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0,"text":"start: pH 1.6","at":"right"},{"series":"pH","x":50,"text":"end: pH 11.9","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**H₃PO₄ has 3 H⁺ it can give away**, one at a time. Take one H⁺ off and you get H₂PO₄⁻, the acid the question asks about.","marks":[{"t":"H₃PO₄","note":"3 removable H⁺"},{"t":"H₂PO₄⁻","note":"H₃PO₄ minus one H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Ka = how readily an acid gives up H⁺.** Chemists use pKa = −log Ka; to go back, Ka = 10^(−pKa).","marks":[{"t":"Ka","note":"acid strength"}],"calc":["pKa = −log Ka","Ka = **10^(−pKa)**"]},{"text":"**Where is each H⁺ used up?** 2.0 mmol of acid needs 20 mL of base per H⁺. That's where the curve jumps.","marks":[{"t":"20.0 mL of 0.10 M H₃PO₄","note":"2.0 mmol acid"},{"t":"0.10 M NaOH","note":"20 mL per H⁺"}],"calc":["n = 20.0 mL × 0.10 M = **2.0 mmol**","V = 2.0 mmol ÷ 0.10 M = **20 mL**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"1st H⁺ gone: 20 mL","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":40,"text":"2nd H⁺ gone: 40 mL","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Each stage strips a different acid.** Between the two jumps, the base is pulling H⁺ off **H₂PO₄⁻**.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":20,"text":"H₃PO₄ stage"},{"x1":20,"x2":40,"text":"H₂PO₄⁻ stage"}]},"marks":[{"t":"H₂PO₄⁻","note":"the 20–40 mL stage"}]},{"text":"**Halfway through a stage, half the acid has lost its H⁺:** acid = partner, so **pH = pKa** there. Example: stage 1 midpoint, 10 mL.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"pH 2.1 = pKa₁","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Do it for H₂PO₄⁻:** midpoint of stage 2 = 30 mL. Read the pH there.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":30}],"shade":[{"x1":20,"x2":40,"text":"stage 2"}],"spans":[{"x":30,"from":0,"to":7.2,"text":"pKa₂ = 7.2","side":"left"}]},"calc":["pH at 30 mL = **7.2** = pKa₂","Ka = 10^−7.2 ≈ **6 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"**Wrong spots:** 10 mL gives Ka₁ (≈ 8 × 10⁻³). The jumps at 20 and 40 mL are equivalence points, not pKa values.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"Ka₁: wrong acid","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"jump: not a pKa","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":40,"text":"jump: not a pKa","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKa = pH at the MIDDLE of a flat buffer region (halfway to an equivalence point), never at a steep jump. Then Ka = 10^(−pKa).","graph":{"focus":["pH"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"An object is placed 30 cm in front of a converging lens with a focal length of 10 cm. What is the ratio of image height to object height, and what is the image's orientation? (1/f = 1/o + 1/i; m = −i/o)",idStem:"An object sits 3 focal lengths from a converging lens. What is the image height ÷ object height?",choices:["2:1, inverted","1:3, inverted","1:2, upright","1:2, inverted"],ans:3,exp:"1/i = 1/10 − 1/30 = 2/30 → **i = 15 cm**. **m = −i/o** = −15/30 = **−0.5**: the image is half the height (1:2) and the negative sign means **inverted**.",src:"pe1-cp:45",how:{"ask":"Find where the image forms, then use the magnification formula; the size of m gives the ratio and its sign gives the orientation.","know":"• **Thin lens**: 1/f = 1/o + 1/i (f > 0 for converging; i > 0 means a real image on the far side).\n• **Magnification m = −i/o = image height ÷ object height**. |m| < 1 → smaller; **m < 0 → inverted** (real images from a single lens are inverted).","steps":[{"text":"Solve the thin-lens equation for the image distance.","hl":"placed 30 cm in front of a converging lens with a focal length of 10 cm","calc":["1/i = 1/f − 1/o","1/i = 1/10 − 1/30 = 3/30 − 1/30 = 2/30","i = **+15 cm** (real image)"]},{"text":"Magnification gives size and orientation.","calc":["m = −i/o = −15/30","m = **−0.5**","|m| = 0.5 → image : object = **1 : 2**"]},{"text":"The size is half, not double (2:1 divides o by i), and not 1:3 (f ÷ o).","strike":["2:1, inverted","1:3, inverted"]},{"text":"A negative m means inverted; any real image from a single converging lens is inverted.","strike":["1:2, upright"]},{"text":"Answer: 1:2, inverted."}],"trap":"'2:1, inverted' is the ratio flipped (o/i instead of i/o). An object beyond 2f always gives an image SMALLER than the object."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the givens and the question.**","marks":[{"t":"30 cm","note":"o: object to lens"},{"t":"10 cm","note":"f: focal length"},{"t":"ratio of image height to object height","note":"how big the image is"}]},{"text":"**A converging lens** (like a magnifying glass) bends light inward to a point. That point is the **focal point**; its distance from the lens is the focal length.","marks":[{"t":"converging lens","note":"bends light inward"},{"t":"focal length","note":"where light meets"}]},{"text":"**Read the formulas:** o = object distance, i = image distance (unknown), f = focal length, m = magnification.","marks":[{"t":"1/f = 1/o + 1/i","note":"finds i"},{"t":"m = −i/o","note":"size and flip"}]},{"text":"**Solve for i:** move 1/o to the other side.","marks":[{"t":"30 cm","note":"o = 30"},{"t":"10 cm","note":"f = 10"}],"calc":["1/i = 1/f − 1/o","1/i = 1/10 − 1/30 = 3/30 − 1/30","1/i = 2/30 → i = **15 cm**"]},{"text":"**See it:** farther object → image forms closer to the focal point. At o = 30 cm the image lands at 15 cm.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Converging lens, f = 10 cm: where the image forms","x":{"label":"Object distance o (cm)","min":10,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Image distance i (cm)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"i","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[14,35],[15,30],[20,20],[30,15],[40,13.3],[60,12]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"i","x":30,"text":"o = 30 → i = 15","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":30,"from":0,"to":15,"text":"i = 15 cm","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Magnification:** plug i and o into m.","marks":[{"t":"m = −i/o","note":"−15/30 = −0.5"}],"calc":["m = −15 ÷ 30","m = **−0.5**"]},{"text":"**Size:** |m| = 0.5, so the image is half as tall: **1 : 2**. **Sign:** minus means upside down: **inverted**.","marks":[{"t":"ratio of image height to object height","note":"0.5 → 1 : 2"},{"t":"image's orientation","note":"minus = inverted"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 2:1 is o ÷ i (upside-down ratio); 1:3 is f ÷ o; \"upright\" ignores the minus sign. A real image from one converging lens is always inverted.","marks":[{"t":"converging lens","note":"real image → inverted"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** thin lens 1/f = 1/o + 1/i gives i; m = −i/o gives size (|m|) and orientation (minus = inverted, plus = upright)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Technetium-99m, a medical imaging isotope, emits radiation and becomes technetium-99: its mass number and atomic number do not change. The radiation passes undeflected between charged plates. The emission is best categorized as:",idStem:"In gamma decay, a nucleus emits:",choices:["a neutron, since it carries no charge","an electron, since the mass number is unchanged","a photon, since it carries no charge or mass","a positron, since the atomic number is unchanged"],ans:2,exp:"Undeflected → **no charge**. A and Z unchanged → no nucleons and no charge left the nucleus. That's **gamma decay**: the nucleus drops from an excited state by emitting a **high-energy photon**.",src:"pe1-cp:46",how:{"ask":"Which emissions are uncharged, and which of those leaves both A and Z the same?","know":"• **α**: ⁴He nucleus, charge +2 (A − 4, Z − 2).\n• **β⁻**: electron, charge −1 (Z + 1).\n• **β⁺**: positron, charge +1 (Z − 1).\n• **Neutron emission**: no charge, but A − 1.\n• **γ**: photon, no charge, no mass (A and Z unchanged). The 'm' in ⁹⁹ᵐTc means metastable (excited).","steps":[{"text":"Passing undeflected between charged plates → the radiation has no charge (rules out electrons and positrons).","hl":"The radiation passes undeflected between charged plates","strike":["an electron, since the mass number is unchanged","a positron, since the atomic number is unchanged"]},{"text":"Balance the nuclear equation with A and Z unchanged.","hl":"its mass number and atomic number do not change","calc":["⁹⁹ᵐ₄₃Tc → ⁹⁹₄₃Tc + ?","A: 99 = 99 + **0**","Z: 43 = 43 + **0**","? has A = 0, Z = 0 → γ photon"]},{"text":"A neutron has A = 1; emitting one would give ⁹⁸Tc.","calc":["⁹⁹Tc → ⁹⁸Tc + ¹₀n  (not observed)"],"strike":["a neutron, since it carries no charge"]},{"text":"The 'm' means metastable: an excited nucleus drops to its ground state by releasing a high-energy photon. Answer: a photon, since it carries no charge or mass.","hl":"Technetium-99m"}],"trap":"'A neutron, since it carries no charge' fits the deflection test, but losing a neutron would drop the mass number from 99 to 98."},teach:[{"text":"**Before and after:** Tc-99m becomes Tc-99, and you get two clues about what came out.","marks":[{"t":"Technetium-99m","note":"start: excited nucleus"},{"t":"technetium-99","note":"end: same element"},{"t":"passes undeflected between charged plates","note":"clue: path not bent"}]},{"text":"**Two counts describe a nucleus:** mass number A (protons + neutrons) and atomic number Z (protons; it decides the element).","marks":[{"t":"mass number","note":"A: protons + neutrons"},{"t":"atomic number","note":"Z: protons"}]},{"text":"**The \"m\" means metastable:** the nucleus has extra energy, like a stretched spring, and can relax without changing any particles.","marks":[{"t":"Technetium-99m","note":"m = excited (metastable)"}]},{"text":"**Charged plates bend charged particles.** Electrons bend toward +, alpha toward −. Only an uncharged emission flies straight.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Paths between charged plates","x":{"label":"Distance travelled (cm)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Sideways shift (mm)","min":-10,"max":10,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"β⁻ (electron)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,0.5],[4,2],[6,4.5],[8,8]]},{"name":"α (+2)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[4,-1],[8,-3]]},{"name":"γ (photon)","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[4,0],[8,0]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"β⁻ (electron)","x":8,"text":"bends: charged","at":"left"},{"series":"γ (photon)","x":8,"text":"straight: no charge","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Undeflected → no charge.** That rules out the electron (−1) and the positron (+1).","marks":[{"t":"undeflected","note":"no charge"}]},{"text":"**Balance the nuclear equation:** the missing piece must have A = 0 and Z = 0: no mass, no charge. That is a **gamma photon** (γ).","marks":[{"t":"do not change","note":"A and Z stay the same"}],"calc":["⁹⁹ᵐ₄₃Tc → ⁹⁹₄₃Tc + ?","A: 99 = 99 + **0**","Z: 43 = 43 + **0**","? = **γ photon**"]},{"text":"**Why not a neutron?** It has no charge, but it has mass (A = 1): losing one would leave Tc-98, not Tc-99.","marks":[{"t":"mass number","note":"A unchanged → no neutron"}],"calc":["⁹⁹Tc → ⁹⁸Tc + ¹₀n  ✗ (not what happened)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A and Z both unchanged + no deflection → gamma (photon) from an excited nucleus. Deflected → charged (α, β⁻, β⁺); A drops → α or neutron."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"Solid calcium carbonate is added to 1 M HCl at 25 °C and dissolves with vigorous bubbling: CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g); ΔH° ≈ −15 kJ/mol. Which signs of ΔG° and ΔS° are most consistent with these observations?",idStem:"A solid reacts with an acid to produce a gas, spontaneously at 25 °C. What are the signs of ΔG° and ΔS°?",choices:["ΔG° < 0 and ΔS° < 0","ΔG° > 0 and ΔS° > 0","ΔG° > 0 and ΔS° < 0","ΔG° < 0 and ΔS° > 0"],ans:3,exp:"The reaction proceeds on its own → **spontaneous → ΔG° < 0**. A **gas forms from a solid and solution** → many more ways to arrange the particles → **ΔS° > 0**.",src:"pe1-cp:47",how:{"ask":"What does 'happens on its own' say about ΔG, and what does making a gas say about ΔS?","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. ΔG < 0 → spontaneous**.\n• **ΔS (entropy)** measures disorder/number of arrangements: gas ≫ liquid/solution > solid. Producing gas (or more moles of gas) → ΔS > 0.\n• Here ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0, so ΔG < 0 at every temperature.","steps":[{"text":"Proceeds on its own, vigorously, at 25 °C → spontaneous under these conditions → ΔG° < 0.","hl":"dissolves with vigorous bubbling","strike":["ΔG° > 0 and ΔS° > 0","ΔG° > 0 and ΔS° < 0"]},{"text":"Count gas moles on each side: a gas forms from a solid and a solution, so disorder rises.","hl":"CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)","calc":["gas moles: reactants **0** → products **1**","Δn(gas) = +1 → **ΔS° > 0**"],"strike":["ΔG° < 0 and ΔS° < 0"]},{"text":"Check consistency with ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°.","hl":"ΔH° ≈ −15 kJ/mol","calc":["ΔH° < 0  (−15 kJ/mol)","−TΔS° < 0  (since ΔS° > 0)","ΔG° = (−) + (−) → **negative** at every T ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔG° < 0 and ΔS° > 0."}],"trap":"'ΔG° < 0 and ΔS° < 0' gets spontaneity right but misses the entropy gain from the bubbles. Any reaction that makes gas from no gas has ΔS > 0."},teach:[{"text":"**What the question gives:** what you see, the reaction, and one number.","marks":[{"t":"dissolves with vigorous bubbling","note":"what you see"},{"t":"ΔH° ≈ −15 kJ/mol","note":"heat given off"},{"t":"ΔG° and ΔS°","note":"the 2 signs we need"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation:** left side = used up, arrow = turns into, right side = made.","marks":[{"t":"CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq)","note":"used up"},{"t":"CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)","note":"made"}]},{"text":"**The letters in brackets say the phase:** (s) solid, (aq) dissolved in water, (l) liquid, (g) gas.","marks":[{"t":"CaCO₃(s)","note":"solid"},{"t":"2HCl(aq)","note":"dissolved"},{"t":"CO₂(g)","note":"gas = the bubbles"}]},{"text":"**ΔG° (free energy change):** negative means the reaction runs on its own. It dissolved by itself, fizzing, so **ΔG° < 0**.","marks":[{"t":"vigorous bubbling","note":"runs by itself → ΔG° < 0"},{"t":"ΔG°","note":"negative = spontaneous"}]},{"text":"**ΔS° (entropy) = disorder**, the number of ways particles can be arranged. Gas molecules fly everywhere: a gas has far more entropy than a solid or liquid.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard entropy S° (disorder) of each phase","x":{"label":"Substance"},"y":{"label":"S° (J/mol·K)","min":0,"max":250,"step":50},"categories":["CaCO₃(s)","H₂O(l)","CO₂(g)"],"series":[{"name":"S°","values":[93,70,214]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["CO₂(g)","S°"],["CaCO₃(s)","S°"]]}},{"text":"**Count gas molecules on each side.** A gas is made where there was none, so disorder rises: **ΔS° > 0**.","marks":[{"t":"CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq)","note":"0 gas"},{"t":"CO₂(g)","note":"1 gas"}],"calc":["gas: 0 → 1","Δn(gas) = +1 → **ΔS° > 0**"]},{"text":"**Check it fits ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°.** Both terms are negative, so ΔG° is negative at any temperature.","marks":[{"t":"ΔH° ≈ −15 kJ/mol","note":"ΔH° negative"}],"calc":["ΔH° < 0","−TΔS° < 0 (ΔS° > 0)","ΔG° = (−) + (−) = **negative** ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** happens on its own → ΔG < 0. Gas produced from solids/liquids (more gas moles) → ΔS > 0. Confirm with ΔG = ΔH − TΔS."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A proposed mechanism for the oxidation of SO₂ is: Step 1: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂. Step 2: NO₂ + SO₂ → NO + SO₃. The figure shows the concentrations of NO, NO₂ and SO₃ measured during one run. Which statement correctly describes NO and NO₂?",idStem:"A two-step mechanism: H₂O₂ + I⁻ → H₂O + IO⁻, then H₂O₂ + IO⁻ → H₂O + O₂ + I⁻. What is the overall reaction?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Concentrations during the reaction","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Concentration (mM)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"NO","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1],[1,0.5],[2,0.4],[4,0.5],[6,0.7],[8,0.9],[10,1]]},{"name":"NO₂","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.5],[2,0.6],[4,0.5],[6,0.3],[8,0.1],[10,0]]},{"name":"SO₃","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.6],[2,1.4],[4,2.6],[6,3.3],[8,3.7],[10,3.9]]}]},choices:["NO is a catalyst and NO₂ is an intermediate","NO₂ is a catalyst and NO is an intermediate","NO and NO₂ are both intermediates","NO is a reactant and NO₂ is a product"],ans:0,exp:"Add the steps (step 2 twice) and cancel what appears on both sides: **2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃**. NO is **consumed in step 1 and regenerated in step 2**: it starts at 1.0 mM, dips, and returns to 1.0 mM, so it is a **catalyst**. NO₂ is **made in step 1 and used up in step 2**: it starts at 0, rises, and returns to 0, so it is an **intermediate**.",src:"pe1-cp:48",how:{"ask":"Which species is present at the start and again at the end in the same amount, and which appears only in the middle of the run?","know":"• **Catalyst**: consumed in an early step and **regenerated** in a later one. Present at the start; same amount at the end; absent from the overall equation.\n• **Intermediate**: **produced** in an early step and **consumed** in a later one. Zero at the start, rises, falls back toward zero; absent from the overall equation.\n• **Overall reaction**: add the steps (multiply so the shared species cancel) and cross out anything on both sides.\n• On a concentration–time graph: a product rises and levels off; a reactant falls; an intermediate is a hump from 0 to 0; a catalyst is a dip that recovers.","steps":[{"text":"Add the steps; step 2 must run twice to use both NO₂.","hl":"Step 1: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂. Step 2: NO₂ + SO₂ → NO + SO₃","calc":["2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂","2NO₂ + 2SO₂ → 2NO + 2SO₃","sum: **2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃**  (NO and NO₂ cancel)"]},{"text":"Trace NO on the graph: present at the start, dips, and returns to the same 1.0 mM. Used, then given back → catalyst.","graph":{"focus":["NO"],"read":[{"series":"NO","x":0},{"series":"NO","x":2},{"series":"NO","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"NO","x":0,"text":"1.0 at start","at":"above"},{"series":"NO","x":2,"text":"dips to 0.4","at":"below"},{"series":"NO","x":10,"text":"back to 1.0","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Trace NO₂: zero at the start, peaks, back to zero at the end. Made, then used → intermediate.","graph":{"focus":["NO₂"],"read":[{"series":"NO₂","x":0},{"series":"NO₂","x":2},{"series":"NO₂","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"NO₂","x":0,"text":"0 at start","at":"above"},{"series":"NO₂","x":2,"text":"peak 0.6","at":"above"},{"series":"NO₂","x":10,"text":"0 at end","at":"above"}]},"calc":["NO + NO₂ = 1.0 mM at every time: the nitrogen just cycles"]},{"text":"SO₃ is the true product: it rises and levels off. NO₂ is not a final product, and NO is not used up.","graph":{"focus":["SO₃"],"labels":[{"series":"SO₃","x":10,"text":"product: rises, stays up","at":"left"}]},"strike":["NO is a reactant and NO₂ is a product","NO₂ is a catalyst and NO is an intermediate","NO and NO₂ are both intermediates"]},{"text":"Answer: NO is a catalyst and NO₂ is an intermediate. Rule to reuse: catalyst = in first, back at the end; intermediate = made first, used later, zero at both ends."}],"trap":"Both NO and NO₂ cancel out of the overall equation, so both look like 'helpers'. The order tells them apart: the species you start with and get back (NO) is the catalyst; the species made and then used (NO₂) is the intermediate."},teach:[{"text":"This reaction happens in **two steps**. Read each step like a recipe.","marks":[{"t":"Step 1: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂","note":"step 1"},{"t":"Step 2: NO₂ + SO₂ → NO + SO₃","note":"step 2"}]},{"text":"The arrow means **\"turns into\"**: left side = used up, right side = made.","marks":[{"t":"2NO + O₂","note":"used up"},{"t":"2NO₂.","note":"made"}]},{"text":"**Follow NO** through both steps.","marks":[{"t":"2NO + O₂","note":"NO used here"},{"t":"NO + SO₃","note":"NO made again here"}]},{"text":"Used, then given back = a **catalyst**: a helper that comes out the same as it went in.","calc":["catalyst = in at the start → **back at the end**"]},{"text":"**Follow NO₂** through both steps.","marks":[{"t":"2NO₂.","note":"NO₂ made here"},{"t":"NO₂ + SO₂","note":"NO₂ used here"}]},{"text":"Made, then used up = an **intermediate**: it only exists in the middle.","calc":["intermediate = made → **used up** (gone at the end)"]},{"text":"Now check the graph. **NO**:","graph":{"focus":["NO"],"labels":[{"series":"NO","x":0,"text":"starts at 1","at":"right"},{"series":"NO","x":2,"text":"used in step 1","at":"below"},{"series":"NO","x":10,"text":"back to 1 = catalyst","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**NO₂** on the graph:","graph":{"focus":["NO₂"],"labels":[{"series":"NO₂","x":0,"text":"0 at start","at":"below"},{"series":"NO₂","x":2,"text":"made","at":"above"},{"series":"NO₂","x":10,"text":"0 at end = intermediate","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**SO₃** only goes up: it is a plain **product**.","graph":{"focus":["SO₃"],"labels":[{"series":"SO₃","x":10,"text":"keeps rising = product","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** same amount at start and end = catalyst; zero at both ends = intermediate. Now find the choice that says this about NO and NO₂.","graph":{"focus":["NO","NO₂"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"In an industrial synthesis, a soluble rhodium complex dissolved in the reaction solvent catalyzes the conversion of dissolved reactants. After the reaction, the catalyst cannot be removed by filtration or distillation. Which outcome is most likely, and why?",idStem:"A homogeneous catalyst can't be separated from the products after a reaction. What is the result?",choices:["The product is contaminated, since catalyst and product share a phase","The yield falls, since the catalyst shifts the equilibrium","The product is pure, since catalysts are not consumed","The reaction reverses, since the catalyst remains in the flask"],ans:0,exp:"A **homogeneous catalyst is in the same phase** as the reactants and products, here dissolved in the same solution. If it can't be separated, it **stays mixed in with the product**. Catalysts don't shift equilibrium.",src:"pe1-cp:50",how:{"ask":"Where is the catalyst physically located relative to the product, and what happens if you can't pull it out?","know":"• **Homogeneous catalyst**: same phase as the reactants (e.g. all dissolved). Hard to separate afterward.\n• **Heterogeneous catalyst**: different phase (e.g. solid metal in a liquid or gas). Easy to filter off.\n• Catalysts are not consumed and do not change K or the equilibrium yield.","steps":[{"text":"Classify the catalyst by phase: it is dissolved in the same solution as reactants and products → homogeneous.","hl":"a soluble rhodium complex dissolved in the reaction solvent","calc":["catalyst phase = solution","product phase = solution","same phase → **homogeneous**"]},{"text":"Catalysts speed both directions equally; they do not move the equilibrium or reverse a reaction.","strike":["The yield falls, since the catalyst shifts the equilibrium","The reaction reverses, since the catalyst remains in the flask"]},{"text":"Not being consumed does not mean it leaves the product. If it cannot be filtered or distilled out, it stays mixed in.","hl":"the catalyst cannot be removed by filtration or distillation","strike":["The product is pure, since catalysts are not consumed"]},{"text":"Answer: the product is contaminated, since catalyst and product share a phase."}],"trap":"'The product is pure, since catalysts are not consumed' uses a true fact (catalysts aren't used up) to reach a wrong conclusion. Not being consumed means the catalyst is still there, mixed with the product."},teach:[{"text":"**Who's who:** the rhodium complex is the catalyst; it is mixed into the liquid with the reactants.","marks":[{"t":"a soluble rhodium complex","note":"the catalyst"},{"t":"dissolved reactants","note":"starting materials"},{"t":"cannot be removed by filtration or distillation","note":"it can't be taken out"}]},{"text":"**A catalyst speeds up a reaction by lowering the energy hill**, and comes out unchanged at the end. It does not change where the reaction starts or ends.","marks":[{"t":"catalyzes","note":"speeds up, not used up"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy along the reaction, with and without catalyst","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No catalyst","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[2,40],[5,85],[8,20],[10,20]]},{"name":"With catalyst","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[2,40],[5,60],[8,20],[10,20]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"No catalyst","x":5,"text":"high hill: slow","at":"above"},{"series":"With catalyst","x":5,"text":"lower hill: fast","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Phase** = solid, liquid, gas or dissolved. Catalyst and reactants are both dissolved: same phase = a **homogeneous** catalyst.","marks":[{"t":"dissolved in the reaction solvent","note":"catalyst: in solution"},{"t":"dissolved reactants","note":"reactants: in solution"}]},{"text":"**How chemists separate things:** filtration catches solids; distillation boils off by boiling point. Neither works here.","marks":[{"t":"filtration","note":"catches solids only"},{"t":"distillation","note":"separates by boiling"}]},{"text":"**So the catalyst stays dissolved in the product mixture:** the product is contaminated.","marks":[{"t":"cannot be removed by filtration or distillation","note":"→ stays in the product"}]},{"text":"**Catalysts don't shift equilibrium:** start and end energies are the same with or without one, so the yield doesn't fall and nothing reverses.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy along the reaction, with and without catalyst","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No catalyst","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[2,40],[5,85],[8,20],[10,20]]},{"name":"With catalyst","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[2,40],[5,60],[8,20],[10,20]]}]},"graph":{"spans":[{"x":9,"from":20,"to":40,"text":"same ΔE both ways","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"series":"With catalyst","x":0,"text":"same start","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**\"Not consumed\" is not \"removed\".** The catalyst survives the reaction, which is exactly why it is still sitting in the product.","marks":[{"t":"catalyzes the conversion","note":"survives intact"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** homogeneous catalyst (same phase) → hard to separate, may contaminate product. Heterogeneous (solid in liquid/gas) → filter it off. No catalyst changes K or yield."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"A 1.0-g cube of a solid catalyst, 0.50 cm on each side, is ground into identical cubic particles 0.0050 cm on each side with no loss of mass. If the rate of the catalyzed reaction is proportional to the exposed surface area of the catalyst, by what factor does the rate increase?",idStem:"A solid catalyst is ground into a fine powder (same mass). What happens to the reaction rate?",choices:["100","10,000","1","1,000,000"],ans:0,exp:"Heterogeneous catalysts work at their **surface**. One cube: area = 6(0.50)² = 1.5 cm². Particles: (0.50/0.0050)³ = 10⁶ cubes × 6(0.0050)² = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ cm² each = **150 cm²**. The area, and so the rate, rises by **100×** (= ratio of edge lengths).",src:"pe1-cp:51",how:{"ask":"Calculate the total surface area before and after grinding, keeping the total volume the same.","know":"• A solid catalyst only works where reactants touch it: its **surface area**.\n• Cube with edge L: surface area = 6L²; volume = L³.\n• Grinding a cube of edge L into cubes of edge l gives (L/l)³ pieces; total area = (L/l)³ × 6l² = 6L² × (L/l). So area grows by the factor **L/l**.","steps":[{"text":"The rate tracks exposed surface area, so compare areas before and after grinding.","hl":"proportional to the exposed surface area"},{"text":"Area of the single cube.","hl":"0.50 cm on each side","calc":["A = 6 × (0.50 cm)²","A = 6 × 0.25 = **1.5 cm²**"]},{"text":"How many small cubes? Same total volume.","hl":"0.0050 cm on each side","calc":["edge ratio = 0.50 ÷ 0.0050 = **100**","number = 100³ = **10⁶** cubes"]},{"text":"Total area of the small cubes.","calc":["one cube: 6 × (0.0050)² = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ cm²","all: 10⁶ × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ = **150 cm²**","150 ÷ 1.5 = **100**"]},{"text":"Eliminate: 1 ignores the area change (mass is unchanged, area is not); 10,000 and 1,000,000 square or cube the edge ratio instead of using it once.","strike":["1","10,000","1,000,000"]},{"text":"Answer: the rate increases 100-fold, equal to the edge-length ratio."}],"trap":"10,000 squares the edge ratio, comparing the area of one tiny face to one big face while ignoring how many particles there are. 1 assumes only mass matters, but a surface catalyst depends on area."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** one big cube, ground into tiny cubes, and the rule that rate follows surface area.","marks":[{"t":"0.50 cm on each side","note":"big cube edge"},{"t":"0.0050 cm on each side","note":"tiny cube edge"},{"t":"exposed surface area","note":"the outside faces"}]},{"text":"**A solid catalyst only works where reactants touch it:** its outside surface. Twice the area → twice the rate.","marks":[{"t":"solid catalyst","note":"works on its surface"},{"t":"proportional to","note":"rate × k ↔ area × k"}]},{"text":"**Extra number:** the mass stays the same, so the total volume stays the same. The 1.0 g itself is never used.","marks":[{"t":"1.0-g","note":"not needed"},{"t":"no loss of mass","note":"same total volume"}]},{"text":"**Area of the big cube:** a cube has 6 square faces, each L × L.","marks":[{"t":"0.50 cm on each side","note":"L = 0.50 cm"}],"calc":["A = 6 × L²","A = 6 × (0.50)² = 6 × 0.25 = **1.5 cm²**"]},{"text":"**How many tiny cubes?** 100 fit along each edge, so 100 × 100 × 100 fill the volume.","marks":[{"t":"0.50 cm on each side","note":"0.50 ÷ 0.0050 = 100"},{"t":"0.0050 cm on each side","note":"100 per edge"}],"calc":["edge ratio = **100**","number = 100³ = **10⁶ cubes**"]},{"text":"**Area of all tiny cubes together.**","marks":[{"t":"0.0050 cm on each side","note":"l = 0.0050 cm"}],"calc":["one: 6 × (0.0050)² = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ cm²","all: 10⁶ × 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ = **150 cm²**","150 ÷ 1.5 = **100**"]},{"text":"**Each 10× smaller cut multiplies the area by 10**, not by 100 or 1000.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Total surface area as the cube is cut smaller","x":{"label":"Pieces"},"y":{"label":"Total area (cm²)","min":0,"max":160,"step":20},"categories":["1 cube, 0.50 cm","10³ cubes, 0.050 cm","10⁶ cubes, 0.0050 cm"],"series":[{"name":"Area","values":[1.5,15,150]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["1 cube, 0.50 cm","Area"],["10⁶ cubes, 0.0050 cm","Area"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 1,000,000 is the number of pieces, 10,000 squares the edge ratio, 1 assumes same mass = same area.","marks":[{"t":"by what factor","note":"area ratio = 100"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** cutting a cube into cubes k times smaller in edge multiplies the total surface area (and a surface-limited rate) by k."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A circuit consists of a 1.5-V cell, copper connecting wire and a 10-Ω bulb. Spare parts include a 5-Ω bulb, a 20-Ω bulb, a thinner copper wire and a longer copper wire of the same thickness. Which single change will increase the current through the bulb, and why?",idStem:"Which change increases the current in a simple battery-and-bulb circuit?",choices:["Use the thinner wire, since R = ρL/A","Use the longer wire, since R = ρL/A","Swap in the 5-Ω bulb, since I = V/R","Swap in the 20-Ω bulb, since I = V/R"],ans:2,exp:"**I = V/R**: with the cell's voltage fixed, **lower total resistance → more current**. A 5-Ω bulb halves the main resistance (≈0.15 A → 0.30 A). Thinner or longer wire raises R = ρL/A and lowers the current.",src:"pe1-cp:52",how:{"ask":"For each change, does the circuit's total resistance go up or down, and how does that affect current with a fixed voltage?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**. Fixed V → current is inversely proportional to total resistance.\n• **Wire resistance R = ρL/A**: ρ = resistivity of the material, L = length, A = cross-sectional area. Longer (larger L) or thinner (smaller A) → larger R.","steps":[{"text":"With a fixed cell voltage, current depends only on total resistance: I = V/R.","hl":"a 1.5-V cell","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Original current (wire resistance is tiny).","hl":"a 10-Ω bulb","calc":["I = 1.5 V ÷ 10 Ω = **0.15 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the bulb swaps.","calc":["5 Ω: 1.5 ÷ 5 = **0.30 A** (up)","20 Ω: 1.5 ÷ 20 = **0.075 A** (down)"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Current with each bulb","x":{"label":"Bulb"},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":0.35,"step":0.05},"categories":["10 Ω, original","5 Ω","20 Ω"],"series":[{"name":"I","values":[0.15,0.3,0.075]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["5 Ω","I"]]},"strike":["Swap in the 20-Ω bulb, since I = V/R"]},{"text":"Wire changes: R = ρL/A, so a thinner (smaller A) or longer (larger L) wire RAISES resistance and lowers the current.","hl":"a thinner copper wire and a longer copper wire","strike":["Use the thinner wire, since R = ρL/A","Use the longer wire, since R = ρL/A"]},{"text":"Answer: swap in the 5-Ω bulb, since I = V/R."}],"trap":"The wire choices quote the correct formula R = ρL/A but apply it in the wrong direction: thinner (smaller A) and longer (larger L) both RAISE resistance."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** a battery, a bulb, and spare parts. You must make the current go up.","marks":[{"t":"1.5-V cell","note":"the push (voltage)"},{"t":"10-Ω bulb","note":"resistance now"},{"t":"increase the current","note":"goal: more flow"}]},{"text":"**Three words:** voltage V = the push; resistance R (ohms, Ω) = how hard it is to get through; current I (amps) = how much charge flows.","marks":[{"t":"1.5-V","note":"V = voltage (push)"},{"t":"10-Ω","note":"R = resistance"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law: I = V ÷ R.** The cell's push is fixed, so the only way to raise I is to LOWER R.","marks":[{"t":"1.5-V cell","note":"V fixed at 1.5"}],"calc":["I = V ÷ R","now: 1.5 V ÷ 10 Ω = **0.15 A**"]},{"text":"**Try each bulb.**","marks":[{"t":"5-Ω bulb","note":"1.5 ÷ 5 = 0.30 A ↑"},{"t":"20-Ω bulb","note":"1.5 ÷ 20 = 0.075 A ↓"}],"calc":["5 Ω: **0.30 A** (current doubles)","20 Ω: **0.075 A** (current halves)"]},{"text":"**Less resistance → more current.** The curve falls as R grows.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 1.5-V cell vs bulb resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":0.35,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"I","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.3],[7.5,0.2],[10,0.15],[15,0.1],[20,0.075],[25,0.06]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"I","x":5,"text":"5 Ω: 0.30 A","at":"right"},{"series":"I","x":10,"text":"now: 0.15 A","at":"above"},{"series":"I","x":20,"text":"20 Ω: 0.075 A","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Wire resistance R = ρL/A:** ρ = the material, L = length, A = thickness (cross-section area).","calc":["longer → bigger L → **bigger R**","thinner → smaller A → **bigger R**"],"marks":[{"t":"thinner copper wire","note":"smaller A → more R"},{"t":"longer copper wire","note":"bigger L → more R"}]},{"text":"**So both wire swaps RAISE resistance and lower the current.** The formula they cite is right, but it points the wrong way.","marks":[{"t":"Which single change","note":"only the 5-Ω swap works"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fixed voltage → I = V/R, so lower resistance means more current. Wires get MORE resistive when longer or thinner (R = ρL/A)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"Standard reduction potentials: Zn²⁺/Zn, −0.76 V; 2H⁺/H₂, 0.00 V. A metal M is more easily oxidized than zinc. When a strip of M is placed in 0.1 M HCl, will H₂ gas form?",idStem:"A metal is more easily oxidized than zinc. Will it release H₂ from 0.1 M HCl?",choices:["Yes; M is oxidized and H⁺ is reduced","Yes; M is reduced and H⁺ is oxidized","No; M is reduced and Cl⁻ is oxidized","No; M is oxidized and only water forms"],ans:0,exp:"M is **more easily oxidized than Zn**, so its reduction potential is below −0.76 V, far below H⁺/H₂ (0.00 V). It gives electrons to H⁺: **M is oxidized to Mⁿ⁺ and H⁺ is reduced to H₂**, so gas forms.",src:"pe1-cp:53",how:{"ask":"Compare M with hydrogen on the reduction-potential scale; which species gives electrons to which?","know":"• **Oxidation** = loss of electrons; **reduction** = gain of electrons.\n• A lower (more negative) E°red means the metal is more easily **oxidized**.\n• A metal reacts with H⁺ to release H₂ if its E°red < 0.00 V: M → Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ and 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂.\n• Neutral metals form cations, so they are oxidized, never reduced, in these reactions.","steps":[{"text":"'More easily oxidized than zinc' means M's reduction potential is LOWER than zinc's.","hl":"A metal M is more easily oxidized than zinc","calc":["easier to oxidize → lower E°red","E°red(M) **< −0.76 V**"]},{"text":"Place M, Zn and H⁺ on one scale (M's bar is an example value below Zn).","hl":"Zn²⁺/Zn, −0.76 V; 2H⁺/H₂, 0.00 V","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials","x":{"label":"Half-reaction"},"y":{"label":"E°red (V)","min":-1.5,"max":0.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["M (below Zn)","Zn²⁺/Zn","2H⁺/H₂"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[-1.2,-0.76,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["M (below Zn)","E°"],["2H⁺/H₂","E°"]]}},{"text":"The species with the higher E°red (H⁺) is reduced; the lower one (M) is oxidized. The cell potential is positive.","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode","= 0.00 − (below −0.76)","= **> +0.76 V** → spontaneous"]},{"text":"M is the electron donor, so it cannot be reduced; Cl⁻ is not oxidized here; the reaction makes H₂, not just water.","strike":["Yes; M is reduced and H⁺ is oxidized","No; M is reduced and Cl⁻ is oxidized","No; M is oxidized and only water forms"]},{"text":"Answer: yes; M is oxidized and H⁺ is reduced to H₂ gas."}],"trap":"'Yes; M is reduced and H⁺ is oxidized' has the right yes but reverses the electron transfer. H⁺ has no electrons to lose; it can only gain them to form H₂."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** two reduction potentials and one fact about the mystery metal M.","marks":[{"t":"Zn²⁺/Zn, −0.76 V","note":"zinc's number"},{"t":"2H⁺/H₂, 0.00 V","note":"acid's number"},{"t":"more easily oxidized than zinc","note":"fact about M"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation = losing electrons; reduction = gaining electrons** (OIL RIG). In any redox reaction one side gives e⁻, the other takes them.","marks":[{"t":"oxidized","note":"loses electrons"}],"calc":["Oxidation Is Loss of e⁻","Reduction Is Gain of e⁻"]},{"text":"**A reduction potential (E°) = how strongly something grabs electrons.** High E° = grabber (gets reduced). Low E° = giver (gets oxidized).","marks":[{"t":"Standard reduction potentials","note":"electron-grabbing power"}]},{"text":"**M gives electrons away even more easily than zinc**, so its E° sits BELOW −0.76 V.","marks":[{"t":"more easily oxidized than zinc","note":"E°(M) < −0.76 V"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials (M is an example below Zn)","x":{"label":"Half-reaction"},"y":{"label":"E°red (V)","min":-1.5,"max":0.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["M (below Zn)","Zn²⁺/Zn","2H⁺/H₂"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[-1.2,-0.76,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["M (below Zn)","E°"],["2H⁺/H₂","E°"]]}},{"text":"**Higher one takes electrons, lower one gives them.** H⁺ (0.00 V) is far above M, so H⁺ is reduced to H₂ gas and M is oxidized.","marks":[{"t":"0.1 M HCl","note":"source of H⁺"},{"t":"strip of M","note":"electron giver"}],"calc":["M → Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻  (oxidized)","2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → **H₂**  (reduced)"]},{"text":"**Check the cell voltage:** positive means it happens on its own.","calc":["E°cell = E°(reduced) − E°(oxidized)","= 0.00 − (below −0.76)","= **more than +0.76 V** → yes"],"marks":[{"t":"H₂ gas","note":"forms: yes"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** a neutral metal can only lose electrons, so M can't be reduced. Cl⁻ just watches. The H⁺ becomes H₂ gas, not only water.","marks":[{"t":"HCl","note":"Cl⁻ only watches"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** any metal with E°red below 0.00 V (more active than H₂) is oxidized by acid and releases H₂; the higher-E° species is always the one reduced."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A strip of aluminum metal is placed in a blue solution of Cu(NO₃)₂; the solution fades and a reddish solid coats the strip. Standard reduction potentials: Al³⁺/Al, −1.66 V; Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.34 V. Which balanced net ionic equation describes the reaction?",idStem:"Aluminum metal is placed in a Cu²⁺ solution. Which balanced reaction occurs?",choices:["3Al + 2Cu²⁺ → 3Al³⁺ + 2Cu","2Al³⁺ + 3Cu → 2Al + 3Cu²⁺","Al + Cu²⁺ → Al³⁺ + Cu","2Al + 3Cu²⁺ → 2Al³⁺ + 3Cu"],ans:3,exp:"Al (lower E°) is **oxidized** to Al³⁺ and Cu²⁺ is **reduced** to Cu (the red solid). Balance electrons: 2 Al lose 6 e⁻; 3 Cu²⁺ gain 6 e⁻. **Check charge**: left 3 × (+2) = +6; right 2 × (+3) = +6 ✓.",src:"pe1-cp:54",how:{"ask":"Which metal is oxidized and which ion is reduced, and how many of each are needed to make electrons lost equal electrons gained?","know":"• The species with the **lower E°red is oxidized**; the higher one is reduced.\n• **Balance redox by electrons**: electrons lost = electrons gained. Use the least common multiple.\n• A balanced equation must have equal **atoms** and equal **total charge** on each side.","steps":[{"text":"Use the observations: blue Cu²⁺ fades and red Cu metal appears → Cu²⁺ is reduced, so Al is oxidized (it has the lower E°).","hl":"the solution fades and a reddish solid coats the strip","strike":["2Al³⁺ + 3Cu → 2Al + 3Cu²⁺"]},{"text":"Write the half-reactions.","hl":"Al³⁺/Al, −1.66 V; Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.34 V","calc":["oxidation: Al → Al³⁺ + **3e⁻**","reduction: Cu²⁺ + **2e⁻** → Cu"]},{"text":"Match electrons: LCM of 3 and 2 is 6.","calc":["2Al → 2Al³⁺ + 6e⁻","3Cu²⁺ + 6e⁻ → 3Cu","sum: **2Al + 3Cu²⁺ → 2Al³⁺ + 3Cu**"]},{"text":"Check charge on each choice: the correct one must balance.","calc":["2Al + 3Cu²⁺: +6 → 2Al³⁺: +6 ✓","Al + Cu²⁺: +2 → Al³⁺: +3 ✗","3Al + 2Cu²⁺: +4 → 3Al³⁺: +9 ✗"],"strike":["Al + Cu²⁺ → Al³⁺ + Cu","3Al + 2Cu²⁺ → 3Al³⁺ + 2Cu"]},{"text":"Answer: 2Al + 3Cu²⁺ → 2Al³⁺ + 3Cu."}],"trap":"'3Al + 2Cu²⁺ → 3Al³⁺ + 2Cu' swaps the coefficients: its charges are +4 on the left and +9 on the right. Always add up the charges on each side."},teach:[{"text":"**What you see:** a metal strip in a blue solution; the blue fades and a red coat appears.","marks":[{"t":"aluminum metal","note":"Al atoms (neutral)"},{"t":"blue solution of Cu(NO₃)₂","note":"blue = Cu²⁺ ions"},{"t":"a reddish solid","note":"copper metal"}]},{"text":"**Read the clues:** Cu²⁺ ions leave the solution and become Cu metal. Gaining electrons = **reduction**.","marks":[{"t":"the solution fades","note":"Cu²⁺ being used up"},{"t":"coats the strip","note":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu"}]},{"text":"**The potentials agree:** the higher E° (Cu²⁺) takes electrons; the lower (Al) gives them and is **oxidized**.","marks":[{"t":"Al³⁺/Al, −1.66 V","note":"lower → oxidized"},{"t":"Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.34 V","note":"higher → reduced"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials","x":{"label":"Couple"},"y":{"label":"E°red (V)","min":-2,"max":0.5,"step":0.5},"categories":["Al³⁺/Al","Cu²⁺/Cu"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[-1.66,0.34]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Al³⁺/Al","E°"],["Cu²⁺/Cu","E°"]]}},{"text":"**Write each half:** Al loses 3 electrons; Cu²⁺ needs 2.","calc":["oxidation: Al → Al³⁺ + **3e⁻**","reduction: Cu²⁺ + **2e⁻** → Cu"],"marks":[{"t":"Al³⁺","note":"Al lost 3 e⁻"},{"t":"Cu²⁺","note":"takes 2 e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Electrons given must equal electrons taken.** Smallest number both 3 and 2 go into: **6**.","calc":["2Al → 2Al³⁺ + 6e⁻","3Cu²⁺ + 6e⁻ → 3Cu","sum: **2Al + 3Cu²⁺ → 2Al³⁺ + 3Cu**"],"marks":[{"t":"balanced","note":"e⁻ lost = e⁻ gained"}]},{"text":"**Net ionic = only the particles that change.** NO₃⁻ floats around unchanged, so it is left out.","marks":[{"t":"net ionic equation","note":"drop the bystanders"},{"t":"Cu(NO₃)₂","note":"NO₃⁻ is a bystander"}]},{"text":"**Charge check:** a correct equation has the same total charge on both sides.","calc":["2Al + 3Cu²⁺: 3 × (+2) = +6 → 2 × (+3) = +6 ✓","Al + Cu²⁺: +2 → +3 ✗","3Al + 2Cu²⁺: +4 → +9 ✗"],"marks":[{"t":"balanced","note":"charge equal too"}]},{"text":"**The reversed equation** makes Cu²⁺ from Cu: the opposite of the red copper you actually see forming.","marks":[{"t":"a reddish solid","note":"Cu is made, not used"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** lower E° is oxidized, higher E° reduced; multiply halves so electrons cancel, add, then check atoms AND total charge."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"A galvanic cell is built from a lead electrode in 1 M Pb(NO₃)₂ and a copper electrode in 1 M Cu(NO₃)₂. As the cell operates, the copper electrode gains mass. Standard reduction potentials: Pb²⁺/Pb, −0.127 V; Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.339 V; Ag⁺/Ag, +0.800 V. What is E°cell, and is the cell reaction spontaneous?",idStem:"Pb²⁺/Pb has E° = −0.127 V and Cu²⁺/Cu has E° = +0.339 V. In a cell where Cu²⁺ is reduced, what is E°cell, and is it spontaneous?",choices:["+0.466 V, spontaneous","+0.212 V, not spontaneous","−0.466 V, spontaneous","−0.212 V, not spontaneous"],ans:0,exp:"Copper gains mass → Cu²⁺ is reduced → **Cu is the cathode, Pb the anode**. **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** = 0.339 − (−0.127) = **+0.466 V**. **Positive E°cell → ΔG° = −nFE° < 0 → spontaneous.**",src:"pe1-cp:55",how:{"ask":"Use the mass gain to decide which electrode is the cathode, then apply the cell-potential formula and its sign rule.","know":"• **Cathode = reduction** (metal ions plate out, so the electrode gains mass). **Anode = oxidation**.\n• **E°cell = E°red(cathode) − E°red(anode)**, using reduction potentials straight from the table.\n• **ΔG° = −nFE°cell**: E°cell > 0 ↔ ΔG° < 0 ↔ spontaneous.","steps":[{"text":"Use the observation to assign the electrodes: copper gains mass → Cu²⁺ is reduced there → Cu is the cathode, Pb the anode.","hl":"the copper electrode gains mass","calc":["cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu","anode: Pb → Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻"]},{"text":"Silver is a distractor: it is not in this cell.","hl":"Ag⁺/Ag, +0.800 V","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials","x":{"label":"Couple"},"y":{"label":"E°red (V)","min":-0.4,"max":1,"step":0.2},"categories":["Pb²⁺/Pb","Cu²⁺/Cu","Ag⁺/Ag"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[-0.127,0.339,0.8]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Pb²⁺/Pb","E°"],["Cu²⁺/Cu","E°"]]}},{"text":"Compute E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.","calc":["E°cell = 0.339 − (−0.127)","= 0.339 + 0.127 = **+0.466 V**"],"strike":["+0.212 V, not spontaneous","−0.212 V, not spontaneous"]},{"text":"Sign tells spontaneity: ΔG° = −nFE°cell, so positive E° → negative ΔG° → spontaneous. A negative E° would contradict copper plating out.","calc":["ΔG° = −(2)(96,485)(0.466) ≈ **−90 kJ/mol**"],"strike":["−0.466 V, spontaneous"]},{"text":"Answer: +0.466 V, spontaneous."}],"trap":"+0.212 V comes from 0.339 + (−0.127), adding the potentials instead of subtracting the anode's. Also, a galvanic cell that runs on its own always has a positive E°cell."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** two metals in their ions, one observation, and three potentials (one is extra).","marks":[{"t":"Pb²⁺/Pb, −0.127 V","note":"lead's E°"},{"t":"Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.339 V","note":"copper's E°"},{"t":"Ag⁺/Ag, +0.800 V","note":"silver: not in this cell"}]},{"text":"**A galvanic cell is a battery:** a reaction pushes electrons through a wire on its own. Each electrode is a metal strip sitting in its own ions.","marks":[{"t":"galvanic cell","note":"a battery"},{"t":"lead electrode","note":"metal strip in Pb²⁺"}]},{"text":"**Copper gains mass:** Cu²⁺ ions take electrons and plate out as Cu metal. Gaining electrons = reduction; where reduction happens = the **cathode**.","marks":[{"t":"the copper electrode gains mass","note":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu"}],"calc":["cathode = **reduction** (Cu)","anode = **oxidation** (Pb → Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻)"]},{"text":"**The potentials agree:** copper (higher) is reduced, lead (lower) is oxidized. Silver's bar is ignored.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials","x":{"label":"Couple"},"y":{"label":"E°red (V)","min":-0.4,"max":1,"step":0.2},"categories":["Pb²⁺/Pb","Cu²⁺/Cu","Ag⁺/Ag"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[-0.127,0.339,0.8]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Pb²⁺/Pb","E°"],["Cu²⁺/Cu","E°"]]}},{"text":"**Cell voltage = cathode − anode**, using the table values as written.","marks":[{"t":"−0.127 V","note":"anode (Pb)"},{"t":"+0.339 V","note":"cathode (Cu)"}],"calc":["E°cell = 0.339 − (−0.127)","= 0.339 + 0.127 = **+0.466 V**"]},{"text":"**Trap:** 0.212 V comes from adding the raw numbers (0.339 + (−0.127)). Subtracting a negative turns into adding.","marks":[{"t":"E°cell","note":"cathode − anode"}],"calc":["0.339 − 0.127 = 0.212 ✗ (sign lost)"]},{"text":"**Spontaneous = happens on its own.** ΔG° = −nFE°: positive E° → negative ΔG° → spontaneous. Copper plating out on its own confirms it.","marks":[{"t":"spontaneous","note":"E° > 0 → yes"}],"calc":["ΔG° = −(2)(96,485)(0.466) ≈ **−90 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the cathode (gains mass, higher E°), then E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode. Positive E°cell ↔ negative ΔG° ↔ spontaneous."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A 2.0-V source is connected across a small lamp whose filament has a resistance of 500 mΩ. Assuming the resistance stays constant, what current flows through the lamp?",idStem:"A 2.0 V source drives current through a 0.5 Ω bulb. What current flows?",choices:["4.0 mA","1.0 A","4.0 A","250 A"],ans:2,exp:"**I = V/R** with R in ohms: 500 mΩ = **0.50 Ω**, so I = 2.0 ÷ 0.50 = **4.0 A**. Dividing by a number less than 1 gives a bigger result.",src:"pe1-cp:56",how:{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide voltage by resistance.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (amperes = volts ÷ ohms).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**, so 500 mΩ = 0.500 Ω. Don't confuse with **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the resistance to ohms first: m = milli = 10⁻³.","hl":"a resistance of 500 mΩ","calc":["500 mΩ = 500 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.50 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"A 2.0-V source","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 2.0 V ÷ 0.50 Ω","I = **4.0 A**"]},{"text":"Eliminate: 4.0 mA treats 500 mΩ as 500 Ω; 1.0 A multiplies V × R; 250 A divides R in mΩ by V.","strike":["4.0 mA","1.0 A","250 A"]},{"text":"Sense check: a resistance below 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number. Answer: 4.0 A."}],"trap":"4.0 mA comes from dividing by 500 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 1.0 A comes from multiplying V × R instead of dividing."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** a voltage, a resistance with a tiny prefix, and the current to find.","marks":[{"t":"2.0-V source","note":"voltage: the push"},{"t":"500 mΩ","note":"resistance"},{"t":"what current","note":"flow, in amps"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law: I = V ÷ R.** Current = push ÷ opposition. Constant resistance means the law applies directly.","marks":[{"t":"resistance stays constant","note":"Ohm's law works"}],"calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**m = milli = one thousandth.** Convert to plain ohms before dividing. (Capital M = mega = a million: a different prefix!)","marks":[{"t":"500 mΩ","note":"m = milli = ÷ 1000"}],"calc":["500 mΩ = 500 ÷ 1000 Ω = **0.50 Ω**"]},{"text":"**Divide.** Dividing by a number less than 1 makes the answer BIGGER.","marks":[{"t":"2.0-V","note":"V = 2.0"},{"t":"500 mΩ","note":"R = 0.50 Ω"}],"calc":["I = 2.0 V ÷ 0.50 Ω","I = **4.0 A**"]},{"text":"**See it:** below 1 Ω, the current is larger than the voltage number.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 2.0-V source vs resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"I","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,8],[0.5,4],[1,2],[1.5,1.33],[2,1]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"I","x":0.5,"text":"0.50 Ω → 4.0 A","at":"right"},{"series":"I","x":2,"text":"2 Ω → 1 A","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 4.0 mA treats it as 500 Ω; 1.0 A multiplies V × R; 250 A divides 500 by 2 without converting.","marks":[{"t":"500 mΩ","note":"= 0.50 Ω, not 500 Ω"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert every prefix to base units first (m = 10⁻³, k = 10³, M = 10⁶), then I = V/R. Small R → big current."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Consider the data at 25 °C: BaCrO₄, Ksp = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰; Na₂CrO₄ is very soluble. Solid Na₂CrO₄ is added to a saturated BaCrO₄ solution until [CrO₄²⁻] = 0.010 M. Which best describes the result?",idStem:"Na₂CrO₄ is dissolved in a saturated BaCrO₄ solution. What happens?",choices:["BaCrO₄ precipitates, since Ksp decreases","BaCrO₄ precipitates, since Q now exceeds Ksp","More BaCrO₄ dissolves, since Q is below Ksp","Nothing changes, since Ksp is constant"],ans:1,exp:"CrO₄²⁻ is a **common ion**. In the saturated solution [Ba²⁺] ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻⁵ M, so **Q = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻]** = 1.1 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.010 = 1.1 × 10⁻⁷ **> Ksp** → solid BaCrO₄ forms. Ksp itself doesn't change at constant temperature.",src:"pe1-cp:57",how:{"ask":"Compute the ion product after the addition and compare it with Ksp.","know":"• **Ksp** = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻] at saturation; it depends only on temperature.\n• **Q (ion product)**: same expression, using current concentrations. **Q > Ksp → precipitate forms; Q < Ksp → more dissolves; Q = Ksp → saturated.**\n• **Common-ion effect**: adding an ion already in the equilibrium lowers the salt's solubility.","steps":[{"text":"Find [Ba²⁺] in the saturated solution before anything is added.","hl":"BaCrO₄, Ksp = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰","calc":["Ksp = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻] = s²","s = √(1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰) ≈ **1.1 × 10⁻⁵ M**"]},{"text":"Add the common ion and compute Q.","hl":"until [CrO₄²⁻] = 0.010 M","calc":["Q = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻] = (1.1 × 10⁻⁵)(0.010)","Q = **1.1 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"Compare Q with Ksp: Q is about 1000 times larger → the solution is supersaturated → solid forms.","calc":["1.1 × 10⁻⁷ > 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ → **Q > Ksp**","precipitate until [Ba²⁺] = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ ÷ 0.010 = 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ M"],"strike":["More BaCrO₄ dissolves, since Q is below Ksp","Nothing changes, since Ksp is constant"]},{"text":"Ksp depends only on temperature, so it did not decrease; Q rose instead.","strike":["BaCrO₄ precipitates, since Ksp decreases"]},{"text":"Answer: BaCrO₄ precipitates, since Q now exceeds Ksp (the common-ion effect)."}],"trap":"'BaCrO₄ precipitates, since Ksp decreases' has the right outcome but the wrong reason. Ksp is a constant at a given temperature; it's Q that rose."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** one salt that barely dissolves, one that dissolves fully, and a new chromate level.","marks":[{"t":"BaCrO₄, Ksp = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰","note":"barely dissolves"},{"t":"Na₂CrO₄ is very soluble","note":"dissolves completely"},{"t":"[CrO₄²⁻] = 0.010 M","note":"chromate after adding"}]},{"text":"**Saturated = holding as much dissolved salt as it can**, with extra solid sitting at the bottom. Solid and ions are in balance.","marks":[{"t":"saturated BaCrO₄ solution","note":"dissolved to the max"}],"calc":["BaCrO₄(s) ⇌ Ba²⁺ + CrO₄²⁻"]},{"text":"**Ksp = the ion product at saturation**, [Ba²⁺] × [CrO₄²⁻]. It is the ceiling; it only changes with temperature.","marks":[{"t":"Ksp = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰","note":"the ceiling"},{"t":"at 25 °C","note":"T fixed → Ksp fixed"}],"calc":["Ksp = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻] = s²","s = √(1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰) ≈ **1.1 × 10⁻⁵ M** Ba²⁺"]},{"text":"**Common ion:** Na₂CrO₄ dissolves and pours in extra CrO₄²⁻, the same ion BaCrO₄ makes.","marks":[{"t":"Solid Na₂CrO₄ is added","note":"adds extra CrO₄²⁻"}]},{"text":"**Q = the same product, using the concentrations right now.**","marks":[{"t":"[CrO₄²⁻] = 0.010 M","note":"× 0.010"}],"calc":["Q = [Ba²⁺][CrO₄²⁻]","Q = (1.1 × 10⁻⁵)(0.010) = **1.1 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"**Q is about 900 times the ceiling.** Too many ions → they join up and fall out as solid.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Ion product Q after adding chromate, vs Ksp","x":{"label":"Value"},"y":{"label":"Size (Ksp = 1)","min":0,"max":1000,"step":200},"categories":["Ksp (the limit)","Q (now)"],"series":[{"name":"size","values":[1,917]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Q (now)","size"]]},"calc":["1.1 × 10⁻⁷ > 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ → **Q > Ksp**"]},{"text":"**Ksp did not decrease:** temperature never changed. What rose was Q. So more dissolving or \"nothing changes\" are both wrong.","marks":[{"t":"at 25 °C","note":"Ksp unchanged"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute Q with current ion concentrations. Q > Ksp → precipitate; Q < Ksp → more dissolves; Q = Ksp → saturated. Ksp changes only with T."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A protocol calls for 14.0 g of glucose (M = 180 g/mol), to be delivered from a 0.120 M glucose stock solution. What volume of the stock is needed?",idStem:"What volume of a 0.120 M solution contains 0.078 mol of solute?",choices:["9.4 mL","1.5 L","650 mL","65 mL"],ans:2,exp:"Moles = 14.0 g ÷ 180 g/mol = **0.0778 mol**. **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0778 ÷ 0.120 mol/L = 0.648 L ≈ **650 mL**.",src:"pe1-cp:58",how:{"ask":"Turn the mass into moles first, then find the volume of stock that holds that many moles.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass** (mol = g ÷ g/mol).\n• **Molarity M = n ÷ V** (mol/L), so **V = n ÷ M**.\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Convert grams to moles.","hl":"14.0 g of glucose (M = 180 g/mol)","calc":["n = 14.0 g ÷ 180 g/mol","n = **0.0778 mol**"]},{"text":"Use molarity = moles ÷ liters, solved for volume.","hl":"a 0.120 M glucose stock solution","calc":["V = n ÷ M","V = 0.0778 mol ÷ 0.120 mol/L","V = **0.648 L**"]},{"text":"Convert to mL and round.","calc":["0.648 L × 1000 mL/L = **648 mL ≈ 650 mL**"]},{"text":"Eliminate: 65 mL is off by a factor of 10 in the last conversion; 1.5 L and 9.4 mL come from setting the ratio up wrong. Carry units through every line: g → mol → L → mL.","strike":["65 mL","1.5 L","9.4 mL"]},{"text":"Answer: 650 mL."}],"trap":"9.4 mL comes from multiplying moles × molarity instead of dividing (0.078 × 0.120 = 0.0094). 1.5 L is the ratio flipped (0.120 ÷ 0.078). Check units: mol ÷ (mol/L) = L."},teach:[{"text":"**The givens:** how much glucose you need, how heavy one mole is, and how concentrated the stock is.","marks":[{"t":"14.0 g","note":"mass wanted"},{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"mass of 1 mole"},{"t":"0.120 M","note":"stock strength"}]},{"text":"**A mole is a counting unit** (like a dozen, but 6 × 10²³). Molar mass = grams per mole.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"1 mol weighs 180 g"}]},{"text":"**Watch out: \"M\" means two things here.** Inside the brackets it is molar mass; after 0.120 it is molarity = moles per liter.","marks":[{"t":"M = 180","note":"M = molar mass"},{"t":"0.120 M","note":"M = mol per liter"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: grams → moles.** Divide by grams per mole.","marks":[{"t":"14.0 g","note":"÷ 180 g/mol"}],"calc":["n = 14.0 g ÷ 180 g/mol","n = **0.0778 mol**"]},{"text":"**Step 2: moles → liters.** Each liter of stock holds 0.120 mol, so divide.","marks":[{"t":"0.120 M","note":"÷ 0.120 mol/L"}],"calc":["V = n ÷ M","V = 0.0778 ÷ 0.120 = **0.648 L**"]},{"text":"**Step 3: liters → mL** (× 1000). 648 mL ≈ 650 mL.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Stock volume needed vs glucose wanted (0.120 M)","x":{"label":"Glucose (g)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Stock volume (mL)","min":0,"max":1000,"step":200},"series":[{"name":"V","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[5,231],[10,463],[14,648],[20,926]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"V","x":14,"text":"14 g → 648 mL","at":"left"}]},"calc":["0.648 L × 1000 = **648 mL**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 65 mL slips a decimal; 1.5 L and 9.4 mL come from flipping a division. Keep units on every line: g → mol → L → mL.","marks":[{"t":"What volume of the stock","note":"≈ 650 mL"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass → moles (÷ molar mass) → volume (÷ molarity) → convert units. Write the units every line so a flipped ratio shows itself."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 1: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A physiologist reports the heart's mechanical output using several unit expressions. Which of the following can NOT be a unit of power?",idStem:"Which unit can NOT express power?",choices:["N·m·s⁻¹","kg·m²·s⁻²","kg·m²·s⁻³","mmHg·mL·s⁻¹"],ans:1,exp:"**Power = energy ÷ time.** kg·m²·s⁻² is a **joule** (energy) with no 'per second'. N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s; kg·m²·s⁻³ = J/s in base units; mmHg·mL is pressure × volume = energy, so mmHg·mL·s⁻¹ is also power.",src:"pe1-cp:59",how:{"ask":"Reduce each unit to base units, or to energy over time, and find the one with no 'per time'.","know":"• **Power P = W/t**; 1 W = 1 J/s.\n• **1 J = 1 N·m = 1 kg·m²·s⁻²** (force × distance).\n• So **1 W = kg·m²·s⁻³** in base units.\n• **Pressure × volume = energy** (Pa·m³ = N/m² × m³ = N·m = J), whatever the units.","steps":[{"text":"Power = energy ÷ time = J/s, and a joule in base units is kg·m²·s⁻².","hl":"can NOT be a unit of power","calc":["J = N·m = kg·m·s⁻² × m = **kg·m²·s⁻²**","W = J/s = **kg·m²·s⁻³**"]},{"text":"Test each choice.","calc":["N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s → power ✓","kg·m²·s⁻³ = J/s → power ✓","mmHg·mL = pressure × volume = energy → ·s⁻¹ = power ✓","kg·m²·s⁻² = **J** → energy only ✗"],"strike":["N·m·s⁻¹","kg·m²·s⁻³","mmHg·mL·s⁻¹"]},{"text":"The odd one out is missing the 'per second'. Answer: kg·m²·s⁻², which is a joule, a unit of energy.","hl":"heart's mechanical output"}],"trap":"mmHg·mL·s⁻¹ looks odd, but pressure × volume is a form of work (as in a heart's PV loop), so per second it is power. kg·m²·s⁻³ looks unfamiliar, yet it is exactly a watt."},teach:[{"text":"**Read the task carefully:** find the one unit that is NOT power.","marks":[{"t":"NOT","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"unit of power","note":"energy per second"},{"t":"heart's mechanical output","note":"work the heart does"}]},{"text":"**Power = energy per second.** Its unit is the watt: 1 W = 1 J/s. Energy alone (joules) is NOT power.","marks":[{"t":"power","note":"J ÷ s = watt"}],"calc":["power = energy ÷ time","1 W = **1 J/s**"]},{"text":"**Power is the slope:** how fast energy piles up. A heart does about 1.3 J every second.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy delivered by a heart over time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Energy (J)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Work","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,1.3],[2,2.6],[3,3.9],[4,5.2]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Work","x":1,"text":"1.3 J in 1 s","at":"left"},{"series":"Work","x":4,"text":"slope = J/s = power","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Joule in base units:** work = force × distance, and force = mass × acceleration.","marks":[{"t":"mechanical output","note":"work = force × distance"}],"calc":["N = kg·m·s⁻²","J = N·m = **kg·m²·s⁻²**","W = J/s = **kg·m²·s⁻³**"]},{"text":"**Test each expression:** does it end with a \"per second\" on top of an energy?","marks":[{"t":"unit expressions","note":"test each one"}],"calc":["N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s → power ✓","kg·m²·s⁻³ = J/s → power ✓","kg·m²·s⁻² = J → **energy only ✗**"]},{"text":"**Pressure × volume is also energy** (N/m² × m³ = N·m = J), so mmHg·mL·s⁻¹ is energy per second: power.","marks":[{"t":"physiologist","note":"pressure × volume units"}],"calc":["Pa·m³ = N/m² × m³ = **J**","mmHg·mL·s⁻¹ → J/s ✓"]},{"text":"**The odd one out is missing the \"per second\":** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule, a unit of energy.","marks":[{"t":"can NOT be a unit of power","note":"energy, no ÷ time"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** reduce units to kg, m, s. Energy = kg·m²·s⁻²; power = kg·m²·s⁻³ (one more ÷ s). Pressure × volume = energy."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 3 · Chem/Phys (built 2026-11-11) ──
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"A conjugated diene reacts with one equivalent of HBr to give two constitutional isomers, A and B. The graph shows the product mixture under two sets of conditions. At 40 °C for 24 h, the products interconvert; at −80 °C for 5 min, they do not. Which conclusion is best supported by these data?",idStem:"A reaction is run under thermodynamic control, and isomer X makes up 80% of the mixture while isomer Y makes up 20%. What can you conclude?",chart:{"type":"bar","caption":"Product distribution, diene + HBr","x":{"label":"Conditions"},"y":{"label":"Percent of product mixture","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["−80 °C, 5 min","40 °C, 24 h"],"series":[{"name":"Product A","values":[75,15]},{"name":"Product B","values":[25,85]}]},choices:["A is more stable than B, since A predominates at −80 °C","B is more stable than A, since B predominates once the products equilibrate","B forms faster than A, since B predominates at 40 °C","A and B are equally stable, since both form under each set of conditions"],ans:1,exp:"Only the 40 °C, 24 h experiment lets the products interconvert, so only it reaches equilibrium. **At equilibrium the more stable product predominates**: B, 85%. The −80 °C result shows which product forms **faster** (A, lower Ea), not which is more stable.",src:"pe3-cp:2",how:{"ask":"In which experiment can the products reach equilibrium, and which bar wins there?","know":"• **Kinetic control** (low T, short time, products cannot interconvert): the product with the lower activation energy, Ea, forms faster and dominates.\n• **Thermodynamic control** (higher T, long time, products interconvert): the mixture reaches equilibrium, and the product with the lower free energy, G, dominates.\n• At equilibrium K = [B]/[A] and ΔG° = −RT ln K; K > 1 means B is lower in energy.\n• On a bar graph of product ratios, find the condition labeled \"interconvert\" or \"long time, warm\": that pair of bars tells you stability.","steps":[{"text":"First find which experiment reaches equilibrium: only the one where the products can turn into each other.","hl":"At 40 °C for 24 h, the products interconvert"},{"text":"Read the equilibrium bars. B wins, so B is lower in free energy.","graph":{"bars":[["40 °C, 24 h","Product A"],["40 °C, 24 h","Product B"]]},"calc":["K = [B]/[A] = 85/15 ≈ **5.7**","ΔG° = −RT ln K = −(8.314)(313)(ln 5.7)","ΔG° ≈ **−4.5 kJ/mol** → B is more stable"]},{"text":"The cold bars show which product forms faster: A, through the lower barrier. Speed is not stability, and B is not the faster one.","hl":"at −80 °C for 5 min, they do not","graph":{"bars":[["−80 °C, 5 min","Product A"],["−80 °C, 5 min","Product B"]]},"strike":["A is more stable than B, since A predominates at −80 °C","B forms faster than A, since B predominates at 40 °C"]},{"text":"Picture both paths: A has the lower peak, so it forms first; B ends lower, so it wins at equilibrium. The two ends are at different heights, so they are not equally stable.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two competing paths from the same intermediate","x":{"label":"Reaction coordinate","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Path to A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[2,50],[5,90],[8,30],[10,30]]},{"name":"Path to B","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[2,50],[5,100],[8,20],[10,20]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Path to A","x":5},{"series":"Path to B","x":10}],"spans":[{"x":4.6,"from":50,"to":90,"text":"A: hill = 40","side":"left"},{"x":5.4,"from":50,"to":100,"text":"B: hill = 50"}],"labels":[{"series":"Path to A","x":8,"text":"A ends at 30","at":"above"},{"series":"Path to B","x":10,"text":"B ends at 20","at":"below"}]},"strike":["A and B are equally stable, since both form under each set of conditions"]},{"text":"Answer: B is more stable, since it predominates once the products equilibrate. Rule: cold and short → faster product; warm and long → more stable product."}],"trap":"\"A is more stable, since it predominates at −80 °C\" reads the cold, fast experiment as if it were at equilibrium. At −80 °C the products are frozen in place, so the ratio reflects speed, not stability."},teach:[{"text":"**Start with what the question gives:** one reaction makes two products, A and B, and it was run two different ways.","marks":[{"t":"two constitutional isomers, A and B","note":"2 products, same atoms"},{"t":"At 40 °C for 24 h","note":"warm + long"},{"t":"at −80 °C for 5 min","note":"very cold + short"}]},{"text":"**Bar height = percent of the product mixture.** Each pair of bars is one experiment; the two colors are A and B.","marks":[{"t":"product mixture","note":"bar height = % of each"}],"graph":{"bars":[["−80 °C, 5 min","Product A"],["−80 °C, 5 min","Product B"]]}},{"text":"**Interconvert** = A and B can turn back into each other. Given time, the mix settles at **equilibrium**, where the lower-energy (more **stable**) product wins.","marks":[{"t":"the products interconvert","note":"A ⇌ B: reaches equilibrium"},{"t":"they do not","note":"no switching back"}]},{"text":"**Cold and short = a speed race.** Nothing can switch back, so the product that forms **faster** piles up. In the cold, A wins 75 to 25: A is the **faster** product.","marks":[{"t":"at −80 °C for 5 min","note":"race: faster product wins"}],"graph":{"bars":[["−80 °C, 5 min","Product A"],["−80 °C, 5 min","Product B"]]}},{"text":"**Warm and long = equilibrium.** Products swap back and forth until the more stable one dominates. Here B wins 85 to 15: B is the **more stable** product.","marks":[{"t":"At 40 °C for 24 h","note":"equilibrium: stable wins"}],"graph":{"bars":[["40 °C, 24 h","Product A"],["40 °C, 24 h","Product B"]]}},{"text":"**Picture both paths.** Speed is set by the **hill** (lower hill = faster: A). Stability is set by where it **ends** (lower end = more stable: B).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two competing paths from the same intermediate","x":{"label":"Reaction coordinate","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Path to A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[2,50],[5,90],[8,30],[10,30]]},{"name":"Path to B","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[2,50],[5,100],[8,20],[10,20]]}]},"graph":{"spans":[{"x":4.6,"from":50,"to":90,"text":"A: hill = 40","side":"left"},{"x":5.4,"from":50,"to":100,"text":"B: hill = 50"}],"labels":[{"series":"Path to A","x":8,"text":"A ends at 30","at":"above"},{"series":"Path to B","x":10,"text":"B ends at 20","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Put a number on it** with the equilibrium bars. K > 1 and a negative ΔG° mean B sits lower in energy.","graph":{"bars":[["40 °C, 24 h","Product A"],["40 °C, 24 h","Product B"]]},"calc":["K = [B]/[A] = 85/15 ≈ **5.7**","ΔG° = −RT ln K = −(8.314)(313)(ln 5.7)","ΔG° ≈ **−4.5 kJ/mol** → B more stable"]},{"text":"**Traps:** the cold bars tell speed, not stability; B is not the faster one (it lost the race); and both forming does not make them equally stable.","marks":[{"t":"at −80 °C for 5 min","note":"speed only, not stability"}],"graph":{"bars":[["−80 °C, 5 min","Product A"]]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** cold + short (no interconversion) → the **faster** product wins (kinetic control). Warm + long (interconversion) → the **more stable** product wins (thermodynamic control)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Acetaldehyde, CH₃CHO, is stirred with dilute aqueous NaOH at 5 °C. The isolated product has the molecular formula C₄H₈O₂ and shows IR bands at 3420 cm⁻¹ and 1725 cm⁻¹. Which structure is the product?",idStem:"A ketone enolate attacks a second ketone (an aldol addition), and NO dehydration follows. What kind of product forms?",choiceFigs:["crotonaldehyde","aldol-3-hydroxybutanal","2-hydroxybutanal","ethyl-acetate"],choices:["But-2-enal","3-Hydroxybutanal","2-Hydroxybutanal","Ethyl acetate"],ans:1,exp:"Base removes an α-H, and the enolate adds to the C=O of a second acetaldehyde: an **aldol addition**. Two C₂H₄O units give C₄H₈O₂ with no water lost. The old C=O becomes an **O–H on the carbon β to the remaining C=O** (IR 3420 cm⁻¹), and the aldehyde stays (1725 cm⁻¹): **3-hydroxybutanal**.",src:"pe3-cp:3",how:{"ask":"Does the formula show that water was lost, does the IR show an O–H, and on which carbon does the new O–H end up relative to the C=O?","know":"• **Aldol addition:** base pulls an H off the α-carbon (the carbon next to C=O), making an enolate. The enolate's α-carbon attacks the C=O carbon of a second molecule.\n• The new C–C bond joins α-carbon to carbonyl carbon, so the attacked C=O becomes C–OH **two carbons away** from the surviving C=O: a **β-hydroxy** carbonyl.\n• Heating dehydrates it (loses H₂O) to an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl, C=C next to C=O.\n• IR: broad 3200–3550 cm⁻¹ = O–H; sharp ≈1700–1750 cm⁻¹ = C=O. An ester has C=O but no O–H.\n• Count formula: 2 × C₂H₄O = C₄H₈O₂ means nothing was lost.","steps":[{"text":"The conditions are base and cold: base makes an enolate from one acetaldehyde, and it adds to a second. Cold means no dehydration afterward.","hl":"dilute aqueous NaOH at 5 °C"},{"text":"Check the formula: two acetaldehydes added together, nothing lost. But-2-enal has lost a water.","hl":"C₄H₈O₂","calc":["2 × C₂H₄O = **C₄H₈O₂** ✓ no H₂O lost","but-2-enal = C₄H₈O₂ − H₂O = **C₄H₆O** ✗"],"strike":["But-2-enal"]},{"text":"Read the IR: 3420 cm⁻¹ is an O–H, and 1725 cm⁻¹ is a C=O. Ethyl acetate, an ester, has the same formula but no O–H.","hl":"IR bands at 3420 cm⁻¹ and 1725 cm⁻¹","fig":"ethyl-acetate","strike":["Ethyl acetate"]},{"text":"Find where the O–H lands. The new C–C bond, highlighted, joins the α-carbon of one molecule to the carbonyl carbon of the other, so that carbon carries the O–H two carbons from the C=O.","fig":"aldol-3-hydroxybutanal-hl","calc":["C1 = CHO, the C=O that survives","C2 = α-carbon from the enolate","C3 = former C=O carbon → **C–OH, β**"],"strike":["2-Hydroxybutanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxybutanal. Rule: an aldol puts the O–H on the β-carbon; a C=C conjugated with C=O means water was lost."}],"trap":"2-Hydroxybutanal has the right formula and both IR bands, but it puts the O–H on the α-carbon. In an aldol the attacked C=O carbon ends up β to the remaining C=O, never α."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Every corner or line end is a carbon; its hydrogens are hidden. Letters are other atoms. A double line is a double bond. This is acetaldehyde.","fig":"t3-ald-read"},{"text":"**The α-carbon** is the carbon right next to the C=O. The C=O makes the H atoms on it easy to pull off.","fig":"t3-ald-alpha"},{"text":"**Base pulls off H⁺.** NaOH gives OH⁻, which takes an H⁺ (a hydrogen with no electron). The bond's 2 electrons stay on carbon: an **enolate**.","fig":"t3-ald-enolate"},{"text":"**A second acetaldehyde.** O pulls electrons, so its C=O carbon is electron-poor. An atom with a spare pair (a **nucleophile**) attacks it.","fig":"t3-ald-target"},{"text":"**New C–C bond.** The enolate carbon bonds to the C=O carbon. One bond of that C=O breaks; the O keeps the electrons and turns −.","fig":"t3-ald-alkoxide"},{"text":"**O⁻ takes H⁺ from water.** Count from the C=O that survived: the OH sits on C3, two carbons away. Name: 3-hydroxybutanal.","fig":"t3-ald-product"},{"text":"**Count atoms.** 2 × C₂H₄O = C₄H₈O₂, the given formula: nothing lost. Heat would remove water and make a C=C (C₄H₆O); the cold prevents that.","fig":["t3-ald-sum","t3-ald-dehyd"]},{"text":"**IR** shows which bonds absorb light: about 3200–3550 cm⁻¹ = O–H, about 1700–1750 = C=O. The product needs both; an ester has no O–H.","fig":["t3-ald-ir","t3-ester-ir"]},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** base + cold joins two C=O molecules; the OH lands on the 2nd carbon from the remaining C=O. In each drawing, find the C=O and ask: is an OH exactly 2 carbons away?"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A portable stimulator charges a 100 mF capacitor from a 12 V battery that has an internal resistance of 0.5 Ω. Once the capacitor is fully charged, which of the following gives the charge on the capacitor and the energy it stores?",idStem:"A 100 mF capacitor is connected to a 12 V battery. How much charge and energy does it store?",choices:["1.2 C and 7.2 J","1200 C and 7200 J","0.0083 C and 7.2 J","1.2 C and 14.4 J"],ans:0,exp:"**Q = CV** = 0.100 F × 12 V = 1.2 C and **U = ½CV²** = ½ × 0.100 × 144 = 7.2 J. The internal resistance only affects how fast it charges, not the final values.",src:"pe3-cp:5",how:{"ask":"Convert the capacitance to farads, then use the formulas for charge and stored energy on a capacitor.","know":"• A **capacitor** stores charge Q on its plates when a voltage V is across it. **C** (capacitance, in farads, F) = how much charge per volt.\n• **Q = CV** (coulombs).\n• **U = ½CV²** = ½QV (joules). Don't forget the ½.\n• Prefix: m (milli) = 10⁻³, so 100 mF = 0.100 F.\n• When fully charged, no current flows, so the battery's internal resistance drops no voltage and the capacitor sits at the full 12 V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the capacitance to farads first.","hl":"100 mF","calc":["100 mF = 100 × 10⁻³ F","= **0.100 F**"]},{"text":"The internal resistance only sets how fast it charges. Once fully charged, I = 0, so no voltage drops across r: the full 12 V sits on C.","hl":"internal resistance of 0.5 Ω","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Charging the capacitor through the 0.5 Ω internal resistance (τ = rC = 0.05 s)","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.3,"step":0.05},"y":{"label":"Charge Q (C)","min":0,"max":1.4,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Charge on C","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.05,0.759],[0.1,1.038],[0.15,1.14],[0.2,1.178],[0.25,1.192],[0.3,1.197]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Charge on C","x":0.3}],"yLine":1.2,"shade":[{"x1":0.2,"x2":0.3,"text":"fully charged: I = 0"}],"labels":[{"series":"Charge on C","x":0.05,"text":"τ = 0.05 s","at":"right"},{"series":"Charge on C","x":0.3,"text":"levels off at 1.2 C","at":"below"}]},"calc":["τ = rC = 0.5 Ω × 0.100 F = **0.05 s**","fully charged: I = 0 → V_r = Ir = **0** → V_C = **12 V**"]},{"text":"Charge: Q = CV.","calc":["Q = CV","Q = **0.100 F** × **12 V**","Q = **1.2 C**"],"strike":["1200 C and 7200 J","0.0083 C and 7.2 J"]},{"text":"Energy: U = ½CV² (or ½QV). Don't drop the ½.","calc":["U = ½CV²","U = ½ × 0.100 × **12²**","U = ½ × 0.100 × **144**","U = **7.2 J**","check: ½QV = ½ × 1.2 × 12 = 7.2 J"],"strike":["1.2 C and 14.4 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.2 C and 7.2 J. 1200 C came from leaving C in mF; 14.4 J forgot the ½."}],"trap":"\"1.2 C and 14.4 J\" has the right charge but uses U = CV² (or QV) and drops the ½. \"1200 C and 7200 J\" skips the milli conversion and treats 100 mF as 100 F."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** the question gives and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"100 mF capacitor","note":"capacitance C"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"0.5 Ω","note":"battery's own resistance"}]},{"text":"**A capacitor** = two metal plates that store charge. **Capacitance C** (farads, F) = how much charge it holds per volt. We want two things:","marks":[{"t":"charge on the capacitor","note":"Q, in coulombs"},{"t":"energy it stores","note":"U, in joules"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefix first.** m (milli) means one-thousandth.","marks":[{"t":"100 mF","note":"= 0.100 F"}],"calc":["m (milli) = 10⁻³","100 mF = 100 × 10⁻³ F","= **0.100 F**"]},{"text":"**Fully charged = current stops.** The 0.5 Ω only slows the charging. With no current, it drops no voltage, so the capacitor sits at the full 12 V.","marks":[{"t":"fully charged","note":"current = 0"},{"t":"0.5 Ω","note":"sets speed only"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Charging through the 0.5 Ω internal resistance (τ = rC = 0.05 s)","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.3,"step":0.05},"y":{"label":"Charge Q (C)","min":0,"max":1.4,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Charge on C","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.05,0.759],[0.1,1.038],[0.15,1.14],[0.2,1.178],[0.25,1.192],[0.3,1.197]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.2,"x2":0.3,"text":"fully charged: I = 0"}],"labels":[{"series":"Charge on C","x":0.3,"text":"levels off at 1.2 C","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Charge: Q = CV.** Multiply capacitance by voltage.","marks":[{"t":"100 mF","note":"C = 0.100 F"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"V = 12 V"}],"calc":["Q = CV","Q = **0.100 F** × **12 V**","Q = **1.2 C**"]},{"text":"**Energy: U = ½CV².** Square the voltage first, and keep the ½.","marks":[{"t":"energy it stores","note":"U = ½CV²"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"12² = 144"}],"calc":["U = ½CV²","U = ½ × 0.100 × **144**","U = **7.2 J**","check: ½QV = ½ × 1.2 × 12 = **7.2 J**"]},{"text":"**Where the wrong numbers come from:** leaving mF as F, forgetting the ½, or dividing instead of multiplying.","marks":[{"t":"100 mF","note":"not 100 F!"}],"calc":["100 × 12 = 1200 C ✗ (used mF as F)","0.100 × 144 = 14.4 J ✗ (forgot ½)","0.100 ÷ 12 = 0.0083 ✗ (divided)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a fully charged capacitor, Q = CV and U = ½CV², using the full battery voltage. Convert mF/μF to F first; resistance only changes how fast it charges."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A passenger sits on a railcar moving at a constant 20 m/s along a straight track. A stationary horn beside the track emits a steady tone of frequency f. The speed of sound in air is 340 m/s. Which describes the frequency f′ the passenger hears before and after the railcar passes the horn?",idStem:"You ride past a horn that emits frequency f. What pitch do you hear before and after you pass it?",choices:["f′ < f before passing; f′ > f after passing","f′ = f before passing; f′ = f after passing","f′ > f before passing; f′ < f after passing","f′ > f before passing; f′ > f after passing"],ans:2,exp:"**Observer moving toward a source → higher f′; moving away → lower f′.** Before passing, the railcar approaches the horn (f′ = f·360/340 > f); after passing, it recedes (f′ = f·320/340 < f).",src:"pe3-cp:6",how:{"ask":"Translate \"before passing\" and \"after passing\" into whether the listener is moving toward or away from the horn, then decide what that does to the heard frequency.","know":"• When a listener and a sound source move relative to each other, the frequency heard f′ differs from the emitted f.\n• **f′ = f (v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s)**, where v = speed of sound, v_o = observer speed, v_s = source speed. Use the top signs for motion TOWARD each other.\n• Rule of thumb: getting closer → higher pitch; getting farther → lower pitch.","steps":[{"text":"Identify who moves: the horn is stationary; the observer (passenger) moves at 20 m/s. For a moving observer, f′ = f(v ± v_o)/v, with + when moving toward the source.","hl":"moving at a constant 20 m/s"},{"text":"Before passing, the passenger moves toward the horn: use the + sign.","calc":["f′ = f (v + v_o)/v","f′ = f (340 + **20**)/340","f′ = f × 360/340 = **1.06 f** > f"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Frequency the passenger hears as the railcar passes the horn (t = 0 at passing)","x":{"label":"Time relative to passing (s)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Frequency (in units of f)","min":0.9,"max":1.1,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"Heard f′","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1.06],[-2,1.06],[0,1.06],[0,0.94],[2,0.94],[4,0.94]]},{"name":"Emitted f","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1],[0,1],[4,1]]}]},"graph":{"focus":["Heard f′","Emitted f"],"read":[{"series":"Heard f′","x":-2}],"shade":[{"x1":-4,"x2":0,"text":"approaching"}],"spans":[{"x":-2,"from":1,"to":1.06,"text":"1.06 f"}]}},{"text":"After passing, the passenger moves away: use the − sign.","calc":["f′ = f (340 − **20**)/340","f′ = f × 320/340 = **0.94 f** < f"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Frequency the passenger hears as the railcar passes the horn (t = 0 at passing)","x":{"label":"Time relative to passing (s)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Frequency (in units of f)","min":0.9,"max":1.1,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"Heard f′","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1.06],[-2,1.06],[0,1.06],[0,0.94],[2,0.94],[4,0.94]]},{"name":"Emitted f","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1],[0,1],[4,1]]}]},"graph":{"focus":["Heard f′"],"read":[{"series":"Heard f′","x":2}],"yLine":1,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":4,"text":"moving away"}],"spans":[{"x":2,"from":1,"to":0.94,"text":"0.94 f"}]}},{"text":"The pitch is constant on each side (constant speed) and drops suddenly as the car passes. Eliminate the patterns that don't match the graph.","strike":["f′ < f before passing; f′ > f after passing","f′ = f before passing; f′ = f after passing","f′ > f before passing; f′ > f after passing"]},{"text":"Answer: f′ > f before passing and f′ < f after passing — about 1.06f, then 0.94f."}],"trap":"\"f′ = f both times\" assumes only a moving SOURCE causes a shift. A moving observer also changes how many wave crests reach the ear per second. \"f′ > f both times\" forgets that the shift flips sign once the listener starts moving away."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"20 m/s","note":"listener's speed"},{"t":"frequency f","note":"pitch the horn sends"},{"t":"340 m/s","note":"speed of sound"}]},{"text":"**Frequency** = how many sound waves reach your ear per second (high = high pitch). **Doppler effect:** moving relative to the source changes how many you meet per second.","marks":[{"t":"frequency f′ the passenger hears","note":"f′ = pitch heard"}]},{"text":"**Who moves?** The horn stands still; only the listener (the passenger) moves.","marks":[{"t":"stationary horn","note":"source: not moving"},{"t":"passenger sits on a railcar","note":"listener: moving"}]},{"text":"**Before passing**, the passenger rides INTO the waves, meeting more of them per second → **higher** pitch. Moving toward → use +.","marks":[{"t":"before","note":"moving toward the horn"}],"calc":["f′ = f (v + v_o)/v","f′ = f (340 + **20**)/340","f′ = f × 360/340 = **1.06 f** > f"]},{"text":"**After passing**, the passenger rides AWAY from the waves, meeting fewer per second → **lower** pitch. Moving away → use −.","marks":[{"t":"after the railcar passes the horn","note":"moving away"}],"calc":["f′ = f (340 − **20**)/340","f′ = f × 320/340 = **0.94 f** < f"]},{"text":"**See it:** the heard pitch sits steady above f, then drops below f at the moment of passing.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pitch the passenger hears (t = 0 at passing)","x":{"label":"Time relative to passing (s)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Frequency (in units of f)","min":0.9,"max":1.1,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"Heard f′","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1.06],[-2,1.06],[0,1.06],[0,0.94],[2,0.94],[4,0.94]]},{"name":"Emitted f","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-4,1],[0,1],[4,1]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":-4,"x2":0,"text":"approaching"}],"labels":[{"series":"Heard f′","x":-2,"text":"1.06 f: higher","at":"above"},{"series":"Heard f′","x":2,"text":"0.94 f: lower","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** constant speed means the pitch is steady on each side, but never equal to f (they are moving), and it cannot be high on both sides or low then high.","marks":[{"t":"constant 20 m/s","note":"steady pitch each side"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** getting closer → higher pitch; getting farther → lower pitch, whether the listener or the source moves. Moving listener: f′ = f(v ± v_o)/v."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A 1200-kg car travels on a level road at a constant speed of 40 m/s. Air resistance and rolling friction together exert a force of 1000 N opposing its motion. What power must the engine deliver to the wheels to keep the car moving at this speed?",idStem:"A 1000 N friction force acts on a car moving at a steady 40 m/s. What power keeps it moving?",choices:["40 kW","48 kW","25 W","960 kW"],ans:0,exp:"At constant speed the net force is zero, so the drive force equals the 1000 N of resistance. **P = Fv** = 1000 N × 40 m/s = 40,000 W = 40 kW. The mass is not needed.",src:"pe3-cp:7",how:{"ask":"What force does the engine have to supply at constant speed, and how do you turn a force and a speed into power?","know":"• **Power** = rate of doing work, in watts (1 W = 1 J/s).\n• For a force F pushing an object moving at speed v in the same direction: **P = Fv**.\n• **Constant velocity → net force = 0** (Newton's first law), so the driving force equals the total resistive force.\n• k = 10³.","steps":[{"text":"Constant speed means zero acceleration, so the forces balance: the drive force equals the 1000 N of resistance.","hl":"constant speed of 40 m/s","calc":["F_net = F_drive − 1000 N = ma = **0**","F_drive = **1000 N**"]},{"text":"Power delivered at constant speed is force × velocity.","calc":["P = Fv","P = **1000 N** × **40 m/s**","P = **40,000 W** = **40 kW**"]},{"text":"The mass is a distractor: it matters only for acceleration. Multiplying it in, dividing F by v, or computing kinetic energy gives the wrong choices.","hl":"A 1200-kg car","calc":["1200 × 40 = 48,000 → '48 kW' (mass used as force)","1000 ÷ 40 = 25 → '25 W' (divided)","½ × 1200 × 40² = 960,000 J → energy, not power"],"strike":["48 kW","25 W","960 kW"]},{"text":"Answer: 40 kW, since P = Fv with F equal to the 1000 N resistive force."}],"trap":"960 kW is the car's kinetic energy (½ × 1200 × 40² = 960,000 J) treated as a power; energy is not power, and at constant speed KE doesn't change. 48 kW uses mass × speed, which is momentum, and 25 W divides F by v instead of multiplying."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"1200-kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"40 m/s","note":"speed"},{"t":"1000 N","note":"drag pushing back"}]},{"text":"**Power** = how fast energy is delivered: joules per second = watts (W). 1 kW = 1000 W.","marks":[{"t":"power","note":"energy per second (W)"}]},{"text":"**Constant speed = forces balance** (Newton's first law): no acceleration, so the engine's forward push equals the 1000 N backward drag.","marks":[{"t":"constant speed","note":"no acceleration"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Forces on the car at constant speed","x":{"label":"Force"},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":1200,"step":200},"categories":["Engine push (forward)","Drag (backward)"],"series":[{"name":"Force","values":[1000,1000]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Engine push (forward)","Force"],["Drag (backward)","Force"]]},"calc":["F_net = F_drive − 1000 N = **0**","F_drive = **1000 N**"]},{"text":"**Power of a push on a moving object: P = Fv.** Multiply the engine force by the speed.","marks":[{"t":"1000 N","note":"F = engine push"},{"t":"40 m/s","note":"v"}],"calc":["P = Fv","P = **1000 N** × **40 m/s**","P = **40,000 W** = **40 kW**"]},{"text":"**Units check:** N × m/s = (N·m)/s = J/s = W. A watt is a joule every second.","marks":[{"t":"power must the engine deliver","note":"answer in watts"}],"calc":["N·m = J","J/s = **W**"]},{"text":"**The mass is a distractor:** it only matters if the car speeds up. Using it, dividing, or finding kinetic energy gives the wrong numbers.","marks":[{"t":"1200-kg","note":"not needed: no speeding up"}],"calc":["1200 × 40 = 48,000 → 48 kW ✗ (mass as force)","1000 ÷ 40 = 25 W ✗ (divided)","½ × 1200 × 40² = 960 kJ ✗ (energy, not power)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** constant speed → driving force = total resistance; then P = Fv. Mass only matters when the speed changes."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A 2.0-kg cart rolls along a level, frictionless track and enters a magnetic braking region at 8.0 m/s. Its speed inside the region is plotted against time. Which statement best describes the braking force on the cart?",idStem:"A braking force is directly proportional to an object's speed. How does its speed change over time?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Cart speed in the braking region","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Cart","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,8],[1,4],[2,2],[3,1],[4,0.5],[5,0.25]]}]},choices:["Constant, since speed drops by equal amounts each second","Proportional to speed, since speed halves each second","Growing with time, since the curve flattens as time passes","Proportional to time, since the cart slows more each second"],ans:1,exp:"The speed halves every second: 8 → 4 → 2 → 1 m/s, so the deceleration, the slope, shrinks as the cart slows. F = ma, and dividing F by the average speed in each second gives the same value, ≈1.3 N·s/m, so **F ∝ v**: a drag-like force F = −bv.",src:"pe3-cp:8",how:{"ask":"On a speed–time graph the slope is the acceleration. Does the slope stay the same, grow or shrink, and does it track the speed?","know":"• On a v–t graph, **slope = acceleration**. A straight line means constant acceleration, so constant force.\n• Newton's second law: F = ma, so the force is proportional to the slope.\n• A force proportional to speed, F = −bv, gives an exponential decay: the speed falls by the **same fraction** in each equal time step, so the curve is steep at first and then levels off.\n• Test: equal amounts lost per second → constant force; equal fractions lost per second → force ∝ v.","steps":[{"text":"Level and frictionless: the brake is the only horizontal force, so the slope of this graph is set by the brake alone.","hl":"level, frictionless track","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":5,"text":"braking region: brake is the only force"}]}},{"text":"Read the speed at each second.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":0},{"series":"Cart","x":1},{"series":"Cart","x":2},{"series":"Cart","x":3}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":8,"to":4,"text":"−4"},{"x":2,"from":4,"to":2,"text":"−2"},{"x":3,"from":2,"to":1,"text":"−1"}]},"calc":["0 → 1 s: 8 → 4, lost **4 m/s**","1 → 2 s: 4 → 2, lost **2 m/s**","2 → 3 s: 2 → 1, lost **1 m/s**","same fraction, **½**, each second"]},{"text":"The amount lost shrinks each second, so the force is not constant, and it is not growing.","strike":["Constant, since speed drops by equal amounts each second","Proportional to time, since the cart slows more each second"],"graph":{"focus":["Cart"],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":8,"to":4,"text":"lost 4"},{"x":3,"from":2,"to":1,"text":"lost 1"}]}},{"text":"Compare force with speed using F = ma.","calc":["0–1 s: F = 2.0 × 4 = **8 N**, average v = 6 m/s","2–3 s: F = 2.0 × 1 = **2 N**, average v = 1.5 m/s","F ÷ v = 8/6 = 2/1.5 ≈ **1.3** → F ∝ v"],"graph":{"focus":["Cart"],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":8,"to":4,"text":"Δv = 4 → F = 8 N"},{"x":3,"from":2,"to":1,"text":"Δv = 1 → F = 2 N"}]}},{"text":"A flatter curve means a smaller slope and a smaller force, not a larger one.","strike":["Growing with time, since the curve flattens as time passes"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":5,"text":"flatter = smaller force"}]}},{"text":"Answer: proportional to speed. Rule: on a v–t graph, equal fractions lost per equal time mean F ∝ v; equal amounts lost mean constant F."}],"trap":"\"Growing with time\" mistakes the flattening curve for a stronger brake. A flatter curve means a smaller slope, so a smaller force."},teach:[{"text":"**Up the side = speed** (m/s). Higher dot = faster cart at that moment.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"2.0-kg","note":"mass m"},{"t":"8.0 m/s","note":"entry speed"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = time** inside the braking region, one tick per second.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"plotted against time","note":"x axis: seconds"}]},{"text":"**The brake is the only force** along the track (level, no friction), so all the slowing is the brake's doing.","marks":[{"t":"level, frictionless track","note":"no other force"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":0,"text":"enters at 8 m/s","at":"right"},{"series":"Cart","x":5,"text":"0.25 m/s at 5 s","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Slope = how fast speed changes = deceleration.** Since F = ma, a steep part means a big force and a flat part means a small force.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"steep: big force"},{"x1":3,"x2":5,"text":"flat: small force"}]},"marks":[{"t":"braking force","note":"F = m × slope"}]},{"text":"**Measure the speed lost each second** with arrows from one dot down to the next.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":8,"to":4,"text":"−4"},{"x":2,"from":4,"to":2,"text":"−2"},{"x":3,"from":2,"to":1,"text":"−1"}]},"calc":["0 → 1 s: 8 → 4, lost **4 m/s**","1 → 2 s: 4 → 2, lost **2 m/s**","2 → 3 s: 2 → 1, lost **1 m/s**","same fraction, **½**, each second"]},{"text":"**Equal fractions, not equal amounts.** The amount lost shrinks every second, so the force is shrinking: not constant, not growing.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":1,"text":"halved","at":"right"},{"series":"Cart","x":2,"text":"halved again","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Compare force with speed** using F = ma. Force ÷ speed comes out the same, so the force is **proportional to speed**.","marks":[{"t":"2.0-kg","note":"m in F = ma"}],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":8,"to":4,"text":"F = 8 N, avg v 6"},{"x":3,"from":2,"to":1,"text":"F = 2 N, avg v 1.5"}]},"calc":["0–1 s: F = 2.0 × 4 = **8 N**, v ≈ 6 m/s","2–3 s: F = 2.0 × 1 = **2 N**, v ≈ 1.5 m/s","F ÷ v = 8/6 = 2/1.5 ≈ **1.3** → F ∝ v"]},{"text":"**Trap:** a flatter curve means a smaller slope, so a smaller force, not a bigger one.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":5,"text":"flatter = smaller force"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a speed–time graph, slope = acceleration ∝ force. Equal amounts lost per second → constant force; equal fractions lost → force ∝ speed (drag-like)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"The graph shows the heat released when one mole of each liquid C₆H₁₄ isomer burns completely to CO₂ and H₂O. Which isomer is the most thermodynamically stable?",idStem:"Which measurement best compares the thermodynamic stability of two isomers?",chart:{"type":"bar","caption":"Heat released on complete combustion, liquid C₆H₁₄ isomers","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Heat released (kJ/mol)","min":4140,"max":4170,"step":10},"categories":["Hexane","2-Methylpentane","2,2-Dimethylbutane"],"series":[{"name":"Heat released","values":[4163,4157,4149]}]},choices:["Hexane, since it releases the most heat","2,2-Dimethylbutane, since it releases the least heat","2-Methylpentane, since its value is in the middle","All three equally, since they share a formula"],ans:1,exp:"All three isomers burn to the **same products**, 6 CO₂ + 7 H₂O. The one that releases the **least heat** started **lowest in enthalpy**, so it is the most stable: 2,2-dimethylbutane, 14 kJ/mol below hexane. More branching → more stable alkane.",src:"pe3-cp:9",how:{"ask":"If every isomer ends at the same products, what does releasing less heat say about where each one started?","know":"• **Heat of combustion** = energy released going from the compound to CO₂ + H₂O.\n• Isomers have the same formula, so they all reach the **same final energy**. The difference in heat released is the difference in their starting energies.\n• **Less heat released → lower starting energy → more stable.**\n• Branched alkanes are slightly more stable than straight-chain ones.\n• Check the y-axis: here it starts at 4140, which makes small differences look large.","steps":[{"text":"Every isomer burns to the same products. That shared end point is what lets you compare them.","hl":"burns completely to CO₂ and H₂O","calc":["C₆H₁₄ + 9.5 O₂ → **6 CO₂ + 7 H₂O**","same formula → same products for all three"]},{"text":"Read the bars. Note that the axis starts at 4140, so the differences are small.","graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Heat released"],["2,2-Dimethylbutane","Heat released"]]},"calc":["hexane: **4163** kJ/mol","2,2-dimethylbutane: **4149** kJ/mol","difference: **14 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Flip the picture: the heat released is how far each isomer sits above the shared products. The shortest bar sits lowest, so it is the most stable.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Enthalpy above 6 CO₂ + 7 H₂O, the shared products","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Height above products (kJ/mol)","min":4140,"max":4170,"step":10},"categories":["Hexane","2-Methylpentane","2,2-Dimethylbutane"],"series":[{"name":"Starting enthalpy","values":[4163,4157,4149]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["2,2-Dimethylbutane","Starting enthalpy"]]}},{"text":"Releasing the most heat means starting highest: least stable. The middle value is not special, and the isomers differ by 14 kJ/mol, so they are not equal.","strike":["Hexane, since it releases the most heat","2-Methylpentane, since its value is in the middle","All three equally, since they share a formula"],"graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"Answer: 2,2-dimethylbutane. Rule: for isomers, less heat of combustion means more stable; more branching usually means more stable."}],"trap":"\"Hexane, since it releases the most heat\" treats more energy released as more stability. More heat released means it started higher in energy, which is less stable."},teach:[{"text":"**Read what the bars measure:** the heat given off when 1 mole of each isomer burns. Taller bar = more heat out.","marks":[{"t":"heat released","note":"bar height"},{"t":"CO₂ and H₂O","note":"what they all burn to"}],"graph":{"focus":["Heat released"]}},{"text":"**Isomers** = same atoms, different arrangement. All three are C₆H₁₄; left to right the carbon chain gets more **branched**.","marks":[{"t":"C₆H₁₄","note":"6 C + 14 H in each"}],"graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Heat released"],["2-Methylpentane","Heat released"],["2,2-Dimethylbutane","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"**Careful: the axis starts at 4140, not 0.** The bars look very different, but the real gap is only 14 kJ/mol.","graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Heat released"],["2,2-Dimethylbutane","Heat released"]]},"calc":["hexane: **4163** kJ/mol","2,2-dimethylbutane: **4149** kJ/mol","gap: 4163 − 4149 = **14 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Thermodynamically stable** = sits **lower in energy**, like a ball resting at the bottom of a hill.","marks":[{"t":"thermodynamically stable","note":"lowest energy = most stable"}]},{"text":"**Same formula → same finish line.** Every isomer burns to exactly the same products, so they all end at the same energy.","marks":[{"t":"burns completely to CO₂ and H₂O","note":"same products for all 3"}],"calc":["C₆H₁₄ + 9.5 O₂ → **6 CO₂ + 7 H₂O**"]},{"text":"**Heat released = how far each one falls** to that shared finish line. A bigger fall means it **started higher** in energy.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Starting energy above the shared products (6 CO₂ + 7 H₂O)","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Height above products (kJ/mol)","min":4140,"max":4170,"step":10},"categories":["Hexane","2-Methylpentane","2,2-Dimethylbutane"],"series":[{"name":"Starting energy","values":[4163,4157,4149]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Starting energy"]]}},{"text":"**The shortest bar started lowest**, so it is the most stable: 2,2-dimethylbutane, 14 kJ/mol below hexane.","marks":[{"t":"most thermodynamically stable","note":"least heat released"}],"graph":{"bars":[["2,2-Dimethylbutane","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** hexane releases the most because it started highest, so it is the LEAST stable. 'Middle' means nothing, and the same formula does not mean the same energy.","marks":[{"t":"Which isomer","note":"more heat = less stable"}],"graph":{"bars":[["Hexane","Heat released"],["2-Methylpentane","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for isomers burning to the same products, LESS heat of combustion = lower starting energy = MORE stable. Branched alkanes are a bit more stable than straight chains."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Tritium-labeled guanine is used to follow how quickly a nucleobase is taken up by cells incubated in aqueous buffer for 48 hours. Each labeled site in the structure shown, I through IV, marks a hydrogen that could be replaced with ³H. Which site would best retain the label throughout the experiment?",idStem:"A tritium (³H) label must stay put in water. Which kind of position should carry it?",fig:"guanine-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:2,exp:"**Hydrogens on N or O exchange rapidly with the protons of water**, so a ³H placed there is lost to the solvent. Site III is the only C–H (C8 of the imidazole ring); C–H hydrogens do not exchange, so the label stays on guanine.",src:"pe3-cp:10",how:{"ask":"The label must stay on the molecule while it sits in water for two days. Which kind of hydrogen does not trade places with the hydrogens of water?","know":"• **Exchangeable hydrogens:** H on N, O or S is acidic or basic enough to swap with the solvent's H within seconds to minutes. (That is why N–H and O–H peaks disappear when D₂O is added in NMR.)\n• **Non-exchangeable hydrogens:** H on carbon stays put under normal conditions.\n• So a label meant to last must sit on a C–H. On an exam figure, look at the atom each arrow points to: N or O means it washes out; C means it stays.","steps":[{"text":"Find the condition that matters: the molecule spends a long time in water.","hl":"incubated in aqueous buffer for 48 hours"},{"text":"Look at the atom each label points to. Sites I and IV are hydrogens on ring nitrogens (N–H): they exchange with water.","fig":"guanine-sites","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"Site II is the NH₂ group. Its hydrogens are also on nitrogen, so they exchange too, and very quickly.","strike":["II"]},{"text":"Site III, highlighted, is the C8–H: the only hydrogen on carbon. It does not exchange, so the ³H stays.","fig":"guanine-c8"},{"text":"Answer: III. Rule to remember: put isotope labels on C–H, never on N–H or O–H, when the experiment runs in water."}],"trap":"II, the NH₂, looks like a sturdy group, but its hydrogens sit on nitrogen and exchange with water fastest of all. Any H on N or O is a poor place for a lasting label."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Unlabeled corners are carbons (H hidden); letters are atoms, N = nitrogen, O = oxygen. Labels I–IV point to hydrogens that could carry ³H.","fig":"t3-gua-read"},{"text":"**³H (tritium)** is a heavy, radioactive hydrogen. It acts like ordinary H, so it can sit at any of these sites. The label only helps if it stays on guanine.","fig":"guanine-sites"},{"text":"**Water trades H⁺.** An H on N or O can come off as H⁺ (a hydrogen without its electron) and be replaced from water. A ³H there is lost.","fig":"t3-gua-atoms"},{"text":"**Site I** is a hydrogen on a ring nitrogen. N–H swaps with water, so the label washes out.","fig":"t3-gua-1"},{"text":"**Site II** is an NH₂: two hydrogens on nitrogen. They swap with water very fast.","fig":"t3-gua-2"},{"text":"**Site IV** is the hydrogen on the five-atom ring's nitrogen. N–H again, so it swaps too.","fig":"t3-gua-4"},{"text":"**Site III** is a hydrogen on carbon. Carbon has no spare electron pair and holds its H tightly, so C–H does not swap with water here.","fig":"t3-gua-3"},{"text":"**Why 48 hours matters:** over two days even a slowish swap happens many times. Only a hydrogen that does not swap at all keeps the label.","fig":"t3-gua-atoms"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** in water, a hydrogen label lasts only on C–H. For each label, ask: what atom is this H on? N or O means it washes out; C means it stays."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A breath sample of volume V is collected at pressure P and held at body temperature, 37 °C. R is the gas constant and N_A is Avogadro's number. Which of the following gives the number of gas molecules in the sample?",idStem:"Which expression gives the number of gas PARTICLES (not moles) in volume V?",choices:["PV/RT, with T = 310 K","PVN_A/RT, with T = 310 K","PVN_A/RT, with T = 37 K","PV/RTN_A, with T = 310 K"],ans:1,exp:"**n = PV/RT** gives moles; multiply by **N_A** to convert moles to molecules. T must be absolute: **37 °C + 273 = 310 K**.",src:"pe3-cp:11",how:{"ask":"The ideal gas law gives moles. What do you multiply moles by to get a count of molecules, and what unit must temperature be in?","know":"• **Ideal gas law: PV = nRT**. P = pressure, V = volume, n = moles, R = gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K), T = temperature in **kelvin**.\n• Rearranged: n = PV/RT.\n• **Avogadro's number N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ molecules per mole.** Molecules = n × N_A.\n• **K = °C + 273.**","steps":[{"text":"The question asks for a COUNT of molecules, not moles.","hl":"number of gas molecules"},{"text":"The ideal gas law gives moles; multiply by Avogadro's number to get molecules.","calc":["PV = nRT → n = **PV/RT** (mol)","N = n × N_A","N = **PVN_A/RT** (molecules)"],"strike":["PV/RT, with T = 310 K","PV/RTN_A, with T = 310 K"]},{"text":"The gas law needs absolute temperature.","hl":"body temperature, 37 °C","calc":["T = 37 + 273 = **310 K**"],"strike":["PVN_A/RT, with T = 37 K"]},{"text":"Unit check confirms the expression.","calc":["PV/RT: (Pa·m³)/(J/mol·K × K) = J/(J/mol) = **mol**","mol × (molecules/mol) = **molecules**"]},{"text":"Answer: PVN_A/RT, with T = 310 K."}],"trap":"\"PV/RT\" stops at moles. \"PV/RTN_A\" divides by Avogadro's number, which turns moles into an even smaller number instead of a count of molecules. \"T = 37\" plugs in Celsius, which the gas law never accepts."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every quantity** the question gives and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"volume V","note":"V = space the gas fills"},{"t":"pressure P","note":"P = how hard it pushes"},{"t":"37 °C","note":"T, but in °C"}]},{"text":"**A mole** is a counting unit, like a dozen: 1 mol = 6.02 × 10²³ particles. That number is **Avogadro's number, N_A**.","marks":[{"t":"R is the gas constant","note":"R = 8.314 J/mol·K"},{"t":"Avogadro's number","note":"6.02 × 10²³ per mole"}]},{"text":"**What is asked?** A COUNT of molecules, not moles. Keep that in mind; it decides the last step.","marks":[{"t":"number of gas molecules","note":"a count, not moles"}]},{"text":"**Ideal gas law: PV = nRT** links pressure, volume, moles (n) and temperature. Solve it for moles.","marks":[{"t":"volume V","note":"V"},{"t":"pressure P","note":"P"}],"calc":["PV = nRT","divide both sides by RT:","n = **PV/RT** (moles)"]},{"text":"**Moles → molecules:** multiply by N_A (how many molecules in each mole).","marks":[{"t":"N_A is Avogadro's number","note":"× N_A: mol → molecules"}],"calc":["N = n × N_A","N = **PVN_A/RT** (molecules)"]},{"text":"**Gas laws need kelvin** (the absolute scale, where 0 means no motion at all). Add 273 to °C.","marks":[{"t":"body temperature, 37 °C","note":"37 + 273 = 310 K"}],"calc":["T = 37 + 273 = **310 K**"]},{"text":"**Unit check** confirms the expression gives a count.","marks":[{"t":"number of gas molecules","note":"units: molecules ✓"}],"calc":["PV/RT: J ÷ (J/mol) = **mol**","mol × (molecules/mol) = **molecules**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** PV/RT stops at moles; dividing by N_A gives nonsense units; 37 K is colder than liquid nitrogen, not body heat.","marks":[{"t":"37 °C","note":"°C: never plug in directly"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = PV/RT gives moles; × N_A gives molecules. Always convert °C to kelvin (+273) before using a gas law."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Consider the data for the carbonic acid system in water at 25 °C: H₂CO₃: Ka = 4.3 × 10⁻⁷, Kw/Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸; HCO₃⁻: Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹, Kw/Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴. Based on these data, HCO₃⁻ is best categorized as:",idStem:"For an ion, Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹ and Kb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ in water. How does it mainly act?",choices:["an acid, since its Ka is larger than its Kb","a base, since its Ka is larger than its Kb","an acid, since its Kb is larger than its Ka","a base, since its Kb is larger than its Ka"],ans:3,exp:"HCO₃⁻ acting as a base makes H₂CO₃, so **Kb(HCO₃⁻) = Kw/Ka(H₂CO₃) = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸** (the H₂CO₃ row). Its own Ka is 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹. **The larger constant wins**: Kb > Ka, so HCO₃⁻ behaves as a base.",src:"pe3-cp:12",how:{"ask":"HCO₃⁻ can either give away or pick up H⁺. Find the constant for each process in the table (one of them is not in the HCO₃⁻ row) and compare them.","know":"• **Ka** = how strongly a species donates H⁺; **Kb** = how strongly it accepts H⁺.\n• For a conjugate pair HA / A⁻: **Ka(HA) × Kb(A⁻) = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴**, so **Kb(A⁻) = Kw/Ka(HA)**.\n• An amphoteric ion like HCO₃⁻ does both; whichever constant is larger tells you how it mostly behaves.","steps":[{"text":"HCO₃⁻ is amphoteric. Decide by comparing ITS OWN Ka (as an acid) with ITS OWN Kb (as a base).","hl":"HCO₃⁻ is best categorized as"},{"text":"As an acid, HCO₃⁻ → CO₃²⁻ + H⁺: that Ka is in the HCO₃⁻ row.","hl":"HCO₃⁻: Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹","calc":["Ka(HCO₃⁻) = **5.6 × 10⁻¹¹**"]},{"text":"As a base, HCO₃⁻ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ + OH⁻. Its conjugate acid is H₂CO₃, so its Kb is Kw/Ka in the H₂CO₃ row.","hl":"H₂CO₃: Ka = 4.3 × 10⁻⁷, Kw/Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸","calc":["Kb(HCO₃⁻) = Kw / Ka(H₂CO₃)","= 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / **4.3 × 10⁻⁷**","= **2.3 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"The 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ in the HCO₃⁻ row is the 'wrong row' trap: Kw divided by HCO₃⁻'s OWN Ka gives Kb of its conjugate base, CO₃²⁻.","calc":["Kw / Ka(HCO₃⁻) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹","= **1.8 × 10⁻⁴** = Kb of **CO₃²⁻**, not HCO₃⁻"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pK = −log K for the values involved (a SHORTER bar means a LARGER K)","x":{"label":"Constant"},"y":{"label":"pK = −log K","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Ka of HCO₃⁻","Kb of HCO₃⁻","1.8 × 10⁻⁴ value"],"series":[{"name":"pK","values":[10.25,7.64,3.74]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["1.8 × 10⁻⁴ value","pK"]]}},{"text":"Compare the right pair: Kb (pK 7.6) beats Ka (pK 10.3) by a factor of about 400.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pK = −log K for the values involved (a SHORTER bar means a LARGER K)","x":{"label":"Constant"},"y":{"label":"pK = −log K","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Ka of HCO₃⁻","Kb of HCO₃⁻","1.8 × 10⁻⁴ value"],"series":[{"name":"pK","values":[10.25,7.64,3.74]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Ka of HCO₃⁻","pK"],["Kb of HCO₃⁻","pK"]]},"calc":["Kb / Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸ / 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹ ≈ **410**","Kb > Ka → acts mainly as a **base**"],"strike":["an acid, since its Ka is larger than its Kb","a base, since its Ka is larger than its Kb","an acid, since its Kb is larger than its Ka"]},{"text":"Answer: a base, since its Kb is larger than its Ka (this is why NaHCO₃ solutions are mildly basic, pH ≈ 8.3)."}],"trap":"\"A base, since its Ka is larger than its Kb\" has the right claim but backwards reasoning: a bigger Ka would make it an acid. Also watch the row: 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ belongs to carbonate; using it gives the right answer for the wrong reason."},teach:[{"text":"**Two rows of data**, one per species, and we must judge the ion HCO₃⁻.","marks":[{"t":"H₂CO₃: Ka = 4.3 × 10⁻⁷","note":"row for carbonic acid"},{"t":"HCO₃⁻: Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹","note":"row for bicarbonate"},{"t":"HCO₃⁻ is best categorized","note":"the one we judge"}]},{"text":"**Acid** = gives away H⁺. **Base** = picks up H⁺. HCO₃⁻ can do **both** (it is *amphoteric*), so we ask which it does more.","marks":[{"t":"HCO₃⁻ is best categorized","note":"can give OR take H⁺"}],"calc":["as acid: HCO₃⁻ → CO₃²⁻ + H⁺","as base: HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃"]},{"text":"**Ka** = how strongly something gives H⁺; **Kb** = how strongly it takes H⁺. Bigger K = stronger. HCO₃⁻'s own Ka is in its own row.","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹","note":"Ka of HCO₃⁻ (as acid)"}]},{"text":"**Kb of a base = Kw ÷ Ka of the acid it turns into.** HCO₃⁻ picks up H⁺ and becomes H₂CO₃, so use the **H₂CO₃ row**.","marks":[{"t":"H₂CO₃: Ka = 4.3 × 10⁻⁷","note":"acid HCO₃⁻ turns into"},{"t":"Kw/Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"= Kb of HCO₃⁻ ✓"}],"calc":["Kb(HCO₃⁻) = Kw / Ka(H₂CO₃)","= 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 4.3 × 10⁻⁷","= **2.3 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"**Wrong-row trap:** Kw ÷ HCO₃⁻'s OWN Ka gives the Kb of CO₃²⁻, a different ion. Ignore it.","marks":[{"t":"Kw/Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴","note":"Kb of CO₃²⁻: trap"}],"calc":["1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴","= Kb of **CO₃²⁻**, not HCO₃⁻"]},{"text":"**Compare negative exponents carefully:** 10⁻⁸ is a bigger number than 10⁻¹¹ (fewer zeros after the decimal point).","marks":[{"t":"Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹","note":"acid strength: 10⁻¹¹"},{"t":"Kw/Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"base strength: 10⁻⁸"}],"calc":["10⁻⁸ = 0.000 000 01","10⁻¹¹ = 0.000 000 000 01","10⁻⁸ is **1000×** bigger"]},{"text":"**Kb beats Ka** by about 400×, so HCO₃⁻ mostly takes H⁺: it acts as a **base**. (That is why baking soda solution is mildly basic.)","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pK = −log K (a SHORTER bar means a LARGER K)","x":{"label":"Constant"},"y":{"label":"pK = −log K","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["Ka of HCO₃⁻","Kb of HCO₃⁻","1.8 × 10⁻⁴ (Kb of CO₃²⁻)"],"series":[{"name":"pK","values":[10.25,7.64,3.74]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Ka of HCO₃⁻","pK"],["Kb of HCO₃⁻","pK"]]},"calc":["Kb / Ka = 2.3 × 10⁻⁸ / 5.6 × 10⁻¹¹ ≈ **410**","Kb > Ka → **base**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for an ion that can be acid or base, compare its OWN Ka with its OWN Kb = Kw ÷ Ka(its conjugate acid). The larger constant tells you how it mostly behaves."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A student heats 2.0 g of zinc powder with 2.0 g of I₂ in ethanol, and the reaction Zn + I₂ → ZnI₂ goes to completion. Unreacted zinc is recovered afterward. (Molar masses: Zn 65.4 g/mol, I 126.9 g/mol, ZnI₂ 319.2 g/mol.) Which reactant was limiting, and what is the theoretical yield of ZnI₂?",idStem:"Zn + I₂ → ZnI₂. With 0.0079 mol I₂ and 0.030 mol Zn, and unreacted zinc left over, which is limiting?",choices:["I₂; 2.5 g of ZnI₂","Zn; 9.8 g of ZnI₂","Zn; 2.5 g of ZnI₂","I₂; 5.0 g of ZnI₂"],ans:0,exp:"Moles: Zn = 2.0/65.4 = 0.031 mol; I₂ = 2.0/253.8 = 0.0079 mol. With a **1:1 ratio, the reactant with fewer moles (I₂) is limiting**, which fits the leftover zinc. Yield = 0.0079 mol × 319.2 g/mol ≈ 2.5 g.",src:"pe3-cp:13",how:{"ask":"Convert both masses to moles (watch which molar mass belongs to I₂), compare using the 1:1 ratio, then turn the limiting moles into grams of product.","know":"• **Limiting reactant** = the one that runs out first; it sets how much product forms. The other is in excess (some is left over).\n• Compare MOLES, not grams, using the balanced equation's ratio.\n• Moles = mass ÷ molar mass. **I₂ is diatomic: 2 × 126.9 = 253.8 g/mol.**\n• Theoretical yield = moles of limiting reactant × (product ratio) × product molar mass.","steps":[{"text":"Convert both masses to moles; equal grams do not mean equal moles.","hl":"2.0 g of zinc powder with 2.0 g of I₂","calc":["Zn: 2.0 g ÷ 65.4 g/mol = **0.0306 mol**"]},{"text":"I₂ is diatomic, so use 2 × 126.9.","calc":["M(I₂) = 2 × 126.9 = **253.8 g/mol**","I₂: 2.0 g ÷ 253.8 g/mol = **0.0079 mol**"]},{"text":"The reaction is 1 : 1, so the smaller amount runs out first. The leftover zinc confirms it.","hl":"Unreacted zinc is recovered afterward","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles available (Zn + I₂ react 1 : 1)","x":{"label":"Reactant"},"y":{"label":"Amount (mmol)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"categories":["Zn","I₂"],"series":[{"name":"mmol available","values":[30.6,7.9]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["I₂","mmol available"]]},"strike":["Zn; 9.8 g of ZnI₂","Zn; 2.5 g of ZnI₂"]},{"text":"Yield comes from the limiting reagent.","calc":["n(ZnI₂) = n(I₂) = **0.0079 mol**","m = 0.0079 mol × 319.2 g/mol","m = **2.5 g**","(0.0306 × 319.2 = 9.8 g would wrongly use Zn)"],"strike":["I₂; 5.0 g of ZnI₂"]},{"text":"Answer: I₂ is limiting, and the theoretical yield is 2.5 g of ZnI₂."}],"trap":"\"I₂; 5.0 g\" uses 126.9 g/mol for I₂, forgetting it is diatomic, which doubles the moles. \"Zn; 9.8 g\" compares grams instead of moles (equal masses look like equal amounts) and contradicts the recovered zinc."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** the question gives and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 g of zinc powder","note":"mass of Zn"},{"t":"2.0 g of I₂","note":"mass of I₂"},{"t":"Unreacted zinc is recovered","note":"Zn left over!"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation:** left side is used up, the arrow means 'turns into', right side is made. No numbers in front = 1 of each: a **1 : 1** ratio.","marks":[{"t":"Zn + I₂","note":"reactants: 1 each"},{"t":"→ ZnI₂","note":"turns into 1 product"}]},{"text":"**Limiting reactant** = the one that runs out first and stops the reaction. The other is in **excess** and has leftovers, like the zinc here.","marks":[{"t":"limiting","note":"runs out first"},{"t":"Unreacted zinc is recovered","note":"Zn = excess"}]},{"text":"**Compare moles, not grams.** Atoms react one-for-one by COUNT, and an I₂ is much heavier than a Zn. Moles = grams ÷ molar mass.","marks":[{"t":"Zn 65.4 g/mol","note":"1 mol Zn = 65.4 g"}],"calc":["Zn: 2.0 g ÷ 65.4 g/mol = **0.0306 mol**"]},{"text":"**I₂ is two iodine atoms**, so its molar mass is double the per-atom value.","marks":[{"t":"I 126.9 g/mol","note":"per I atom: I₂ has 2"}],"calc":["M(I₂) = 2 × 126.9 = **253.8 g/mol**","I₂: 2.0 g ÷ 253.8 = **0.0079 mol**"]},{"text":"**See it:** at 1 : 1, the smaller pile runs out first. I₂ has far fewer moles, so **I₂ is limiting**, which matches the leftover zinc.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles available (Zn + I₂ react 1 : 1)","x":{"label":"Reactant"},"y":{"label":"Amount (mmol)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"categories":["Zn","I₂"],"series":[{"name":"mmol available","values":[30.6,7.9]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["I₂","mmol available"]]},"marks":[{"t":"Which reactant was limiting","note":"I₂: fewer moles"}]},{"text":"**Theoretical yield** = the most product possible, set by the limiting reactant. 1 I₂ makes 1 ZnI₂.","marks":[{"t":"ZnI₂ 319.2 g/mol","note":"× this"},{"t":"theoretical yield","note":"max product possible"}],"calc":["n(ZnI₂) = n(I₂) = **0.0079 mol**","m = 0.0079 mol × 319.2 g/mol","m = **2.5 g**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** using the zinc moles gives 9.8 g (but zinc was left over), and 5.0 g doubles the I₂ moles.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 g of zinc powder","note":"excess: does not set yield"}],"calc":["0.0306 × 319.2 = 9.8 g ✗ (used Zn)","2 × 2.5 = 5.0 g ✗ (doubled)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert every reactant to MOLES, divide by its coefficient, and the smallest is limiting. Yield = limiting moles × ratio × product molar mass. Diatomics (I₂, O₂…) use 2 × atomic mass."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"The energy diagram shows a one-step reaction, A → B. What is the activation energy of the forward reaction?",idStem:"On a reaction energy diagram, how is the activation energy defined?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Energy diagram for A → B","x":{"label":"Reaction coordinate","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":140,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"A → B","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[2,50],[5,130],[8,20],[10,20]]}]},choices:["80 kJ/mol","110 kJ/mol","130 kJ/mol","−30 kJ/mol"],ans:0,exp:"**Ea (forward) = transition state − reactants** = 130 − 50 = **80 kJ/mol**. 130 reads the peak from zero, 110 is the reverse barrier (130 − 20), and −30 is ΔH (20 − 50).",src:"pe3-cp:14",how:{"ask":"Which two points on the diagram does the forward barrier connect?","know":"• On an energy diagram, the left plateau is the reactants, the peak is the **transition state**, and the right plateau is the products.\n• **Forward Ea = peak − reactants.** Reverse Ea = peak − products.\n• **ΔH = products − reactants** (negative = exothermic). ΔH is not a barrier.\n• Always measure from the starting plateau, not from the axis at zero.","steps":[{"text":"Forward means starting from A, the left plateau.","hl":"activation energy of the forward reaction","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"A (start)"}]}},{"text":"Read the reactant level.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"A → B","x":2}],"yLine":50,"labels":[{"series":"A → B","x":2,"text":"reactants = 50","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Read the peak, the transition state, and subtract.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"A → B","x":5}],"yLine":50,"spans":[{"x":5,"from":50,"to":130,"text":"Ea = 80"}],"labels":[{"series":"A → B","x":5,"text":"TS = 130","at":"right"}]},"calc":["Ea = E(TS) − E(reactants)","Ea = 130 − 50 = **80 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"130 measures the peak from zero, not from the reactants.","strike":["130 kJ/mol"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":3.5,"from":0,"to":130,"text":"130 = from zero ✗","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Read the products: 110 is the barrier going backward, and −30 is ΔH.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"A → B","x":8}],"yLine":20,"spans":[{"x":6,"from":20,"to":130,"text":"reverse Ea = 110"},{"x":9,"from":50,"to":20,"text":"ΔH = −30"}]},"calc":["reverse Ea = 130 − 20 = **110**","ΔH = 20 − 50 = **−30**"],"strike":["110 kJ/mol","−30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 80 kJ/mol. Rule: forward Ea is always peak minus the starting plateau."}],"trap":"130 kJ/mol reads the peak's height from zero. The barrier is measured up from the reactants, which already sit at 50 kJ/mol."},teach:[{"text":"**Up the side = energy** (kJ/mol). The higher a point, the more energy the molecules have at that moment.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"energy diagram","note":"graph of energy"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = the reaction happening.** A (the reactant) is at the left, B (the product) at the right.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"A → B","note":"A turns into B"}]},{"text":"**Flat left = A. Flat right = B. Top of the hill = the transition state**, the hardest, highest-energy moment. One step = one hill.","marks":[{"t":"one-step reaction","note":"one hill only"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"A"},{"x1":8,"x2":10,"text":"B"}],"labels":[{"series":"A → B","x":5,"text":"transition state","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Activation energy (Ea)** = the height of the hill that molecules must climb, measured **from where they start**, not from zero.","marks":[{"t":"activation energy","note":"hill height from the start"}]},{"text":"**Forward = left to right**, so you start at A. Read A's level.","marks":[{"t":"forward reaction","note":"start at A (left)"}],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"A → B","x":2}],"labels":[{"series":"A → B","x":2,"text":"A = 50","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Read the peak** of the hill.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"A → B","x":5}],"labels":[{"series":"A → B","x":5,"text":"peak = 130","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Measure the climb:** from A's level up to the peak.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":50,"to":130,"text":"Ea = 80"}]},"marks":[{"t":"activation energy","note":"peak − start"}],"calc":["Ea = peak − reactants","Ea = 130 − 50 = **80 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 130 measures from zero; 110 is the climb starting from B (the reverse reaction); −30 is the overall drop A → B (ΔH), not a hill.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":3.5,"from":0,"to":130,"text":"130 = from zero ✗","side":"left"},{"x":6.5,"from":20,"to":130,"text":"reverse Ea = 110"},{"x":9,"from":50,"to":20,"text":"ΔH = −30"}]},"calc":["reverse Ea = 130 − 20 = 110","ΔH = 20 − 50 = −30"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** forward Ea = peak − reactant level; reverse Ea = peak − product level; ΔH = products − reactants. Always measure from a plateau, never from the axis."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Zinc, Z = 30, forms only the Zn²⁺ ion in its compounds, and its salts are colorless and diamagnetic. Which of the following gives the ground-state electron configuration of Zn²⁺, and why?",idStem:"In its ground state, zinc (Z = 30) has:",choices:["[Ar]3d⁸4s², since the 3d electrons are higher in energy and are lost first","[Ar]3d⁹4s¹, since one electron is lost from each of the outer subshells","[Ar]3d¹⁰, since the 4s electrons are lost before any 3d electrons","[Ar]3d¹⁰4s², since a filled d subshell keeps the ion stable"],ans:2,exp:"Neutral Zn is **[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²** (18 + 2 + 10 = 30). **Transition metals lose 4s electrons before 3d**, so Zn²⁺ (28 electrons) is **[Ar]3d¹⁰**. Every electron is paired, so it is diamagnetic, and a full d subshell allows no d–d transitions, so its salts are colorless.",src:"pe3-cp:15",how:{"ask":"First write neutral zinc's configuration by counting electrons past argon; then decide which subshell the two lost electrons come from, and check the result against the magnetic data.","know":"• Electrons FILL subshells in order: … 3p, **4s, then 3d**, then 4p.\n• Transition metals IONIZE from the highest n first: **4s electrons leave before 3d**.\n• Argon (Z = 18) = [Ar] core; s holds 2 electrons, d holds 10.\n• **Diamagnetic** = all electrons paired; **paramagnetic** = at least one unpaired electron.\n• A d¹⁰ ion has no d–d transitions, so its compounds are usually colorless.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons in the neutral atom and fill past [Ar].","hl":"Z = 30","calc":["30 − 18 = **12** electrons beyond [Ar]","4s takes **2** → 10 left","3d takes **10** → Zn = **[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²**"]},{"text":"Zn²⁺ has two fewer electrons, so any answer with 30 electrons is the neutral atom.","hl":"forms only the Zn²⁺ ion","calc":["Zn²⁺: 30 − 2 = **28** electrons = 18 + **10**"],"strike":["[Ar]3d¹⁰4s², since a filled d subshell keeps the ion stable"]},{"text":"Ionization removes the outermost electrons first: 4s (n = 4) before 3d (n = 3), even though 4s filled first.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Electrons in zinc's outer subshells","x":{"label":"Subshell"},"y":{"label":"Electrons","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["4s","3d"],"series":[{"name":"Zn atom","values":[2,10]},{"name":"Zn²⁺ ion","values":[0,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["4s","Zn atom"],["4s","Zn²⁺ ion"]]},"calc":["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2 e⁻","[Ar]3d¹⁰**4s²** → [Ar]3d¹⁰ + 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"Check with the data: d¹⁰ has every electron paired (diamagnetic) and no d–d color. A d⁸ or d⁹ ion would have unpaired electrons.","hl":"colorless and diamagnetic","strike":["[Ar]3d⁸4s², since the 3d electrons are higher in energy and are lost first","[Ar]3d⁹4s¹, since one electron is lost from each of the outer subshells"]},{"text":"Answer: [Ar]3d¹⁰, since the 4s electrons are lost before any 3d electrons."}],"trap":"\"[Ar]3d⁸4s²\" follows the filling order backward: 4s fills before 3d, but once 3d is occupied the 4s electrons are outermost and leave first. A d⁸ ion would also have unpaired electrons, contradicting the diamagnetic data. \"[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²\" holds 30 electrons, so it is the neutral atom, not the ion."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives** and what each clue means.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 30","note":"30 protons, 30 e⁻"},{"t":"Zn²⁺ ion","note":"lost 2 electrons"},{"t":"colorless and diamagnetic","note":"clue about electrons"}]},{"text":"**Electron configuration** = which 'rooms' (subshells) the electrons sit in. An s room holds 2, a d room holds 10. [Ar] = the first 18 electrons.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state electron configuration","note":"electron 'address'"}]},{"text":"**Fill neutral zinc** after the argon core: 4s fills before 3d.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 30","note":"30 e⁻ in neutral Zn"}],"calc":["30 − 18 = **12** beyond [Ar]","4s takes **2** → 10 left","3d takes **10**","Zn = **[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²**"]},{"text":"**The ion has 2 fewer electrons.** Any configuration that still holds 30 is the neutral atom, not Zn²⁺.","marks":[{"t":"Zn²⁺ ion","note":"30 − 2 = 28 e⁻"}],"calc":["Zn²⁺: 30 − 2 = **28** = 18 + **10**"]},{"text":"**4s leaves first.** Electrons are removed from the outermost shell (n = 4) before 3d, even though 4s filled first.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Electrons in zinc's outer subshells","x":{"label":"Subshell"},"y":{"label":"Electrons","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["4s","3d"],"series":[{"name":"Zn atom","values":[2,10]},{"name":"Zn²⁺ ion","values":[0,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["4s","Zn atom"],["4s","Zn²⁺ ion"]]},"calc":["[Ar]3d¹⁰**4s²** → [Ar]**3d¹⁰** + 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"**Check the clues.** *Diamagnetic* = every electron paired. *Colorless* = a full d room has no d-to-d jumps to absorb light. 3d¹⁰ fits both.","marks":[{"t":"colorless","note":"full d: no color ✓"},{"t":"diamagnetic","note":"all paired ✓"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** 3d⁸4s² and 3d⁹4s¹ remove d electrons (wrong order) and leave unpaired electrons; 3d¹⁰4s² is still the neutral atom.","marks":[{"t":"configuration of Zn²⁺","note":"28 e⁻, 4s emptied"}],"calc":["3d⁸4s²: 2 unpaired ✗","3d⁹4s¹: 2 unpaired ✗","3d¹⁰4s²: 30 e⁻ = neutral ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** transition-metal ions lose 4s electrons BEFORE 3d. Write the neutral atom first, then remove from the highest n."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Zinc powder is stirred into a brown solution of I₂ in ethanol. The color fades, the zinc slowly disappears, and the electrical conductivity of the solution rises as ZnI₂ forms. This reaction is best categorized as:",idStem:"Zinc metal reacts with I₂ to form Zn²⁺ and I⁻. What type of reaction is this?",choices:["precipitation, since an insoluble solid forms from dissolved ions","redox, since Zn goes from 0 to −2 and I goes from 0 to +1","acid–base, since Zn²⁺ accepts electron pairs from I⁻","redox, since Zn goes from 0 to +2 and I goes from 0 to −1"],ans:3,exp:"**Any change in oxidation state means redox.** Zn metal (0) becomes Zn²⁺ (+2, oxidized), and I₂ (0) becomes I⁻ (−1, reduced). The rising conductivity shows ions forming in solution, not a precipitate.",src:"pe3-cp:16",how:{"ask":"Assign oxidation numbers to zinc and iodine before and after. Did any of them change, and in which direction?","know":"• **Oxidation state**: elements alone (Zn metal, I₂) are 0; a simple ion's oxidation state equals its charge.\n• **Redox reaction**: electrons move, so oxidation states change. **Oxidation = loss of electrons (number goes up); reduction = gain (number goes down).**\n• Precipitation: ions combine into an insoluble solid. Acid–base: H⁺ transfer (or Lewis electron-pair sharing) with no change in oxidation state.","steps":[{"text":"Observations: Zn metal dissolves and ions appear (conductivity rises). Assign oxidation states before and after.","hl":"the zinc slowly disappears","calc":["Zn(s): **0**","I₂: **0** (element)"]},{"text":"In ZnI₂, iodide is −1, so zinc must be +2.","calc":["ZnI₂: Zn + 2(I) = 0","I = **−1** (halide) → Zn = **+2**"]},{"text":"Zn lost electrons (0 → +2, oxidized); each I gained one (0 → −1, reduced). The signs in choice B are reversed.","calc":["Zn → Zn²⁺ + **2 e⁻** (oxidation)","I₂ + **2 e⁻** → 2 I⁻ (reduction)"],"strike":["redox, since Zn goes from 0 to −2 and I goes from 0 to +1"]},{"text":"Rising conductivity means dissolved ions form, not a solid; no proton or electron-pair transfer is the main event here.","hl":"electrical conductivity of the solution rises","strike":["precipitation, since an insoluble solid forms from dissolved ions","acid–base, since Zn²⁺ accepts electron pairs from I⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: redox, since Zn goes from 0 to +2 and I goes from 0 to −1."}],"trap":"One redox choice has the signs reversed (Zn −2, I +1). A metal always forms positive ions, and iodine, the more electronegative element, takes the electrons. \"Precipitation\" fails because the ZnI₂ dissolves: conductivity rises."},teach:[{"text":"**Name the players:** what you start with and what forms.","marks":[{"t":"Zinc powder","note":"Zn metal: start"},{"t":"I₂ in ethanol","note":"iodine: start"},{"t":"ZnI₂ forms","note":"product"}]},{"text":"**Read the clues.** Each observation tells you something changed.","marks":[{"t":"The color fades","note":"brown I₂ used up"},{"t":"the zinc slowly disappears","note":"Zn metal → ions"},{"t":"conductivity of the solution rises","note":"more ions dissolved"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation state** = the charge an atom would carry. Any pure element (Zn metal, I₂) is **0**.","marks":[{"t":"Zinc powder","note":"Zn: 0"},{"t":"I₂ in ethanol","note":"I: 0"}]},{"text":"**In ZnI₂**, iodide is always −1, and the charges must add to 0, so zinc is +2.","marks":[{"t":"ZnI₂ forms","note":"Zn +2, each I −1"}],"calc":["Zn + 2(I) = 0","I = **−1** → Zn = **+2**"]},{"text":"**Redox** = electrons move. Losing e⁻ = **oxidized** (number goes up); gaining e⁻ = **reduced** (number goes down).","marks":[{"t":"the zinc slowly disappears","note":"Zn loses 2 e⁻"}],"calc":["Zn → Zn²⁺ + **2 e⁻** (oxidation)","I₂ + **2 e⁻** → 2 I⁻ (reduction)"]},{"text":"**See the change:** Zn rises 0 → +2, I falls 0 → −1. Any change in oxidation number = **redox**.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Oxidation states before and after","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Oxidation state","min":-2,"max":3,"step":1},"categories":["Zn","I"],"series":[{"name":"Before","values":[0,0]},{"name":"After (in ZnI₂)","values":[2,-1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Zn","After (in ZnI₂)"],["I","After (in ZnI₂)"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** rising conductivity means ions dissolve (no solid, so not precipitation); no H⁺ moves (not acid–base); and Zn goes UP to +2, not down to −2.","marks":[{"t":"conductivity of the solution rises","note":"ions dissolve: no solid"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** assign oxidation states before and after; if any change, it is redox. Metals go up (oxidized), halogens go down to −1 (reduced)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A \"cold\" atmospheric plasma device used for wound treatment is specified to keep its gas at 95 °F. To use this temperature in the ideal gas law, PV = nRT, which value of T should be entered?",idStem:"A \"cold\" plasma is about 35 °C. Which kelvin range contains that temperature?",choices:["368 K","308 K","336 K","35 K"],ans:1,exp:"**°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9** = (95 − 32) × 5/9 = 35 °C, then **K = °C + 273** = 308 K. Gas-law temperatures must be absolute.",src:"pe3-cp:17",how:{"ask":"Which scale is the given value on, and which two conversions take it to the absolute scale that the gas law needs?","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**; the 5/9 is there because a Fahrenheit degree is smaller than a Celsius degree.\n• **K = °C + 273** (273.15 exactly); a kelvin and a Celsius degree are the same size.\n• Gas-law calculations need T in **kelvin**, an absolute scale starting at 0 K.\n• Anchor: body temperature, 98.6 °F = 37 °C = 310 K.","steps":[{"text":"The given value is in Fahrenheit, and the gas law needs kelvin. Two conversions are needed, in order.","hl":"95 °F"},{"text":"Fahrenheit → Celsius: subtract 32, then scale by 5/9.","calc":["°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9","°C = (**95** − 32) × 5/9","°C = 63 × 5/9 = **35 °C**","(skipping × 5/9: 63 + 273 = 336 K ✗)"],"strike":["336 K"]},{"text":"Celsius → kelvin: add 273. The gas law needs the absolute scale.","hl":"PV = nRT","calc":["K = °C + 273","K = **35** + 273 = **308 K**"],"strike":["35 K","368 K"]},{"text":"Check on the conversion line: 95 °F sits between freezing (32 °F, 273 K) and boiling (212 °F, 373 K), just below body temperature (310 K).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Converting °F to kelvin: K = (°F − 32) × 5/9 + 273","x":{"label":"Temperature (°F)","min":0,"max":220,"step":40},"y":{"label":"Temperature (K)","min":250,"max":400,"step":25},"series":[{"name":"Kelvin value","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[32,273],[95,308],[212,373]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Kelvin value","x":95}],"labels":[{"series":"Kelvin value","x":32,"text":"freezing: 32 °F = 273 K","at":"right"},{"series":"Kelvin value","x":212,"text":"boiling: 212 °F = 373 K","at":"left"},{"series":"Kelvin value","x":95,"text":"95 °F = 308 K","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Answer: 308 K."}],"trap":"\"368 K\" adds 273 straight to the Fahrenheit number; that offset only works from Celsius. \"336 K\" subtracts 32 but forgets that a Fahrenheit degree is only 5/9 of a kelvin. \"35 K\" stops at Celsius."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the number and what is asked.** We are given °F but need T for a gas law.","marks":[{"t":"95 °F","note":"Fahrenheit"},{"t":"PV = nRT","note":"ideal gas law"},{"t":"value of T","note":"must be kelvin"}]},{"text":"**Three temperature scales.** °F (US weather), °C (water freezes at 0, boils at 100), and **kelvin** = °C + 273, where 0 K is absolute zero.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Converting °F to kelvin: K = (°F − 32) × 5/9 + 273","x":{"label":"Temperature (°F)","min":0,"max":220,"step":40},"y":{"label":"Temperature (K)","min":250,"max":400,"step":25},"series":[{"name":"Kelvin value","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[32,273],[95,308],[212,373]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Kelvin value","x":32,"text":"freezing: 32 °F = 273 K","at":"right"},{"series":"Kelvin value","x":212,"text":"boiling: 212 °F = 373 K","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Gas laws need kelvin.** Only on that scale does 0 really mean 'no motion', so doubling T truly doubles the energy.","marks":[{"t":"PV = nRT","note":"T in kelvin only"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: °F → °C.** Subtract 32 first, then multiply by 5/9.","marks":[{"t":"95 °F","note":"− 32, then × 5/9"}],"calc":["°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9","°C = (**95** − 32) × 5/9","°C = 63 × 5/9 = **35 °C**"]},{"text":"**Step 2: °C → K.** Add 273.","marks":[{"t":"value of T","note":"35 + 273 = 308 K"}],"calc":["K = °C + 273","K = **35** + 273 = **308 K**"]},{"text":"**Sanity check on the line:** 95 °F is a warm day, just below body temperature (310 K), and 308 K lands right there.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Converting °F to kelvin: K = (°F − 32) × 5/9 + 273","x":{"label":"Temperature (°F)","min":0,"max":220,"step":40},"y":{"label":"Temperature (K)","min":250,"max":400,"step":25},"series":[{"name":"Kelvin value","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[32,273],[95,308],[212,373]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Kelvin value","x":95}],"labels":[{"series":"Kelvin value","x":95,"text":"95 °F = 308 K","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong number skips or misorders a step.","marks":[{"t":"95 °F","note":"not °C: convert first"}],"calc":["63 + 273 = 336 K ✗ (skipped × 5/9)","95 + 273 = 368 K ✗ (treated °F as °C)","35 K ✗ (forgot + 273)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** °F → °C: (°F − 32) × 5/9; °C → K: + 273. Gas-law temperatures are ALWAYS in kelvin."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A plasma source is driven by square voltage pulses. Each pulse holds 8.0 kV for 500 ns, the voltage then stays at 0 V for 500 ns, and the pattern repeats. Which of the following gives the period and frequency of the pulse train?",idStem:"A voltage pulse is on for 500 ns, then off for 500 ns, repeating. What is its period?",choices:["1000 ns; 1 MHz","2000 ns; 0.5 MHz","1000 ns; 1 kHz","500 ns; 2 MHz"],ans:0,exp:"**Period = time for one full repeat** = on + off = 500 ns + 500 ns = 1000 ns = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s. **f = 1/T** = 1/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s) = 1.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 1 MHz.",src:"pe3-cp:18",how:{"ask":"What is one complete cycle of this signal, and how do you get frequency from the period?","know":"• **Period (T)**: the time for one complete cycle, until the pattern starts over.\n• **Frequency (f) = 1/T**, in hertz (Hz = s⁻¹).\n• Prefixes: n = 10⁻⁹, k = 10³, M = 10⁶.\n• The time the pulse is \"on\" is the pulse width, not the period.","steps":[{"text":"Draw the waveform: 8.0 kV for 500 ns, then 0 V for 500 ns.","hl":"holds 8.0 kV for 500 ns","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Square voltage pulses driving the plasma source","x":{"label":"Time (ns)","min":0,"max":2000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Voltage (kV)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Pulse","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,8],[500,8],[500,0],[1000,0],[1000,8],[1500,8],[1500,0],[2000,0]]}]},"graph":{"xLine":500,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":500,"text":"on: 8.0 kV"},{"x1":500,"x2":1000,"text":"off: 0 V"}]}},{"text":"One period is one full on + off cycle before the pattern repeats, not just the pulse width.","hl":"the voltage then stays at 0 V for 500 ns, and the pattern repeats","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Square voltage pulses driving the plasma source","x":{"label":"Time (ns)","min":0,"max":2000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Voltage (kV)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Pulse","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,8],[500,8],[500,0],[1000,0],[1000,8],[1500,8],[1500,0],[2000,0]]}]},"graph":{"xLine":1000,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1000,"text":"one period = 1000 ns"}]},"calc":["T = 500 ns + 500 ns = **1000 ns**"],"strike":["500 ns; 2 MHz","2000 ns; 0.5 MHz"]},{"text":"Convert to seconds, then invert.","calc":["T = 1000 × 10⁻⁹ s = **1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s**","f = 1/T = 1/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s)","f = **1.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 1 MHz**"],"strike":["1000 ns; 1 kHz"]},{"text":"Answer: 1000 ns and 1 MHz. The 8.0 kV height is extra data."}],"trap":"\"500 ns; 2 MHz\" uses only the on-time (pulse width) as the period. \"1000 ns; 1 kHz\" gets the period right but treats nanoseconds as microseconds when inverting."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 kV for 500 ns","note":"ON for 500 ns"},{"t":"0 V for 500 ns","note":"OFF for 500 ns"},{"t":"the pattern repeats","note":"cycle repeats"}]},{"text":"**A square pulse** = the voltage snaps ON, stays flat, snaps OFF, stays flat, and repeats.","marks":[{"t":"square voltage pulses","note":"on/off switching"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Square voltage pulses driving the plasma source","x":{"label":"Time (ns)","min":0,"max":2000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Voltage (kV)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Pulse","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,8],[500,8],[500,0],[1000,0],[1000,8],[1500,8],[1500,0],[2000,0]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":500,"text":"on: 8.0 kV"},{"x1":500,"x2":1000,"text":"off: 0 V"}]}},{"text":"**Period (T)** = the time for ONE complete cycle, on + off, before the pattern starts again.","marks":[{"t":"period","note":"time of 1 full cycle"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Square voltage pulses driving the plasma source","x":{"label":"Time (ns)","min":0,"max":2000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Voltage (kV)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Pulse","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,8],[500,8],[500,0],[1000,0],[1000,8],[1500,8],[1500,0],[2000,0]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1000,"text":"one period"}]}},{"text":"**Add the two parts** of one cycle.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 kV for 500 ns","note":"on: 500 ns"},{"t":"0 V for 500 ns","note":"+ off: 500 ns"}],"calc":["T = 500 ns + 500 ns","T = **1000 ns**"]},{"text":"**Frequency (f)** = cycles per second, in hertz (Hz). f = 1/T, so first put T in seconds (n = 10⁻⁹).","marks":[{"t":"frequency","note":"cycles per second (Hz)"}],"calc":["T = 1000 × 10⁻⁹ s","T = **1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s**"]},{"text":"**Invert** to get the frequency. M (mega) = 10⁶.","marks":[{"t":"frequency of the pulse train","note":"f = 1/T"}],"calc":["f = 1/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ s)","f = **1.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 1 MHz**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** the 8.0 kV height is not needed. 500 ns is only the ON part; 2000 ns is two cycles; 1 kHz is a prefix slip.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 kV","note":"height: not needed"}],"calc":["500 ns → 2 MHz ✗ (on time only)","2000 ns ✗ (two cycles)","1 kHz ✗ (10⁶ is mega, not kilo)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** period = one full repeat (on + off); f = 1/T with T in seconds. Prefixes: n = 10⁻⁹, μ = 10⁻⁶, k = 10³, M = 10⁶."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"An ultraviolet lamp emits a continuous spectrum of wavelengths from 200 nm to 300 nm. Which of the following gives the maximum energy of a single photon emitted by the lamp, and the wavelength at which it occurs? (Use hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m.)",idStem:"A lamp emits UV from 200 to 300 nm. What is the MAXIMUM photon energy? (hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m)",choices:["7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, at 300 nm","1 × 10⁻²⁷ J, at 200 nm","1 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, at 200 nm","4 × 10⁻³² J, at 200 nm"],ans:2,exp:"**E = hc/λ**, so photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength: **the shortest wavelength gives the highest energy**. E = 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 1 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.",src:"pe3-cp:19",how:{"ask":"Which end of the wavelength range carries the more energetic photons, and what do you need to convert before dividing?","know":"• A **photon**'s energy: **E = hf = hc/λ**. h = Planck's constant, c = speed of light, λ = wavelength.\n• E and λ are inversely related: shorter λ → higher E.\n• **nm = 10⁻⁹ m**; convert λ to meters to match hc's units (J·m).","steps":[{"text":"E = hc/λ: energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, so the MAXIMUM energy is at the SHORTEST wavelength, 200 nm.","hl":"maximum energy of a single photon","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy across the lamp's spectrum, E = hc/λ","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":200,"max":300,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":6,"max":11,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hc/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[200,10],[220,9.09],[240,8.33],[260,7.69],[280,7.14],[300,6.67]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":200},{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":300}],"labels":[{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":200,"text":"shortest λ: highest E","at":"right"},{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":300,"text":"longest λ: lowest E","at":"left"}]},"strike":["7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, at 300 nm"]},{"text":"Convert nanometers to meters before dividing.","calc":["200 nm = 200 × 10⁻⁹ m","= **2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"Divide hc by λ.","hl":"hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","calc":["E = hc/λ","E = 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ **2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m**","E = **1 × 10⁻¹⁸ J**"]},{"text":"The other 200 nm answers come from skipping the nm → m conversion (2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 200) or multiplying instead of dividing.","calc":["2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 200 = 1 × 10⁻²⁷ ✗","2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 4 × 10⁻³² ✗"],"strike":["1 × 10⁻²⁷ J, at 200 nm","4 × 10⁻³² J, at 200 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, at 200 nm."}],"trap":"\"7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, at 300 nm\" is a correct calculation for the wrong end: longer wavelength means lower energy. 1 × 10⁻²⁷ J divides by 200 without converting nm to m; 4 × 10⁻³² J multiplies by λ instead of dividing."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives** and what it wants.","marks":[{"t":"200 nm to 300 nm","note":"range of wavelengths"},{"t":"maximum energy","note":"biggest E"},{"t":"hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","note":"constant for E = hc/λ"}]},{"text":"**A photon** = one tiny packet of light. **Wavelength (λ)** = the distance from one wave crest to the next; 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.","marks":[{"t":"single photon","note":"1 packet of light"},{"t":"wavelengths","note":"λ: crest to crest"}]},{"text":"**E = hc/λ: λ is on the bottom.** A shorter wave packs MORE energy. So the maximum energy is at the shortest wavelength.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy across the lamp's spectrum, E = hc/λ","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":200,"max":300,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":6,"max":11,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hc/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[200,10],[220,9.09],[240,8.33],[260,7.69],[280,7.14],[300,6.67]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":200,"text":"shortest λ: highest E","at":"right"},{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":300,"text":"longest λ: lowest E","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Pick the wavelength:** the short end of the lamp's range.","marks":[{"t":"200 nm","note":"shortest λ → max E"},{"t":"300 nm","note":"longest λ → min E"}]},{"text":"**Convert nm to meters**, because hc is in J·m.","marks":[{"t":"200 nm","note":"= 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m"}],"calc":["200 nm = 200 × 10⁻⁹ m","= **2.0 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"**Divide hc by λ.** Same 2.0 on top and bottom; subtract the exponents.","marks":[{"t":"hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","note":"÷ λ in meters"}],"calc":["E = 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁷","E = 1 × 10⁻²⁵⁻⁽⁻⁷⁾","E = **1 × 10⁻¹⁸ J**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 300 nm gives the LOWEST energy; skipping nm → m or multiplying instead of dividing gives tiny wrong numbers.","marks":[{"t":"300 nm","note":"lowest E, not highest"}],"calc":["hc/300 nm ≈ 7 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ✗ (minimum)","2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 200 = 1 × 10⁻²⁷ ✗ (no nm→m)","2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 4 × 10⁻³² ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hc/λ, so photon energy goes UP as wavelength goes DOWN. Convert nm to m (× 10⁻⁹) before dividing."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"Two parallel metal plates 2.0 mm apart are held at +500 V and 0 V. Away from the edges, the electric field between them has the same magnitude, 2.5 × 10⁵ V/m, at every point. Which of the following describes the electric field lines between the plates?",idStem:"The electric field between two flat electrodes is uniform. What does that mean for the field lines?",choices:["Evenly spaced and parallel, pointing from the +500 V plate to the 0 V plate","Evenly spaced and parallel, pointing from the 0 V plate to the +500 V plate","Crowded near the +500 V plate and pointing toward it","Crowded near the 0 V plate and pointing away from it"],ans:0,exp:"**Line density shows field strength**, so a field that is the same everywhere has **evenly spaced, parallel lines**. **Field lines point from high to low potential**: from the +500 V plate toward the 0 V plate.",src:"pe3-cp:20",how:{"ask":"What does the spacing of field lines represent, and which way do field lines point relative to the higher-voltage plate?","know":"• **Electric field lines** show the direction a positive test charge would be pushed.\n• **Spacing = strength**: closer lines = stronger field. Equal strength everywhere → equal spacing.\n• Lines start on positive charge and end on negative charge, so they point **from higher potential to lower potential**.\n• For parallel plates, E = ΔV/d.","steps":[{"text":"Check that the numbers fit a uniform field.","hl":"2.0 mm apart are held at +500 V and 0 V","calc":["E = ΔV/d","E = 500 V ÷ **0.0020 m**","E = **2.5 × 10⁵ V/m** ✓"]},{"text":"Same magnitude everywhere → uniform field → field lines evenly spaced and parallel. Crowded lines would mean a stronger field in one region.","hl":"the same magnitude, 2.5 × 10⁵ V/m, at every point","strike":["Crowded near the +500 V plate and pointing toward it","Crowded near the 0 V plate and pointing away from it"]},{"text":"Direction: E points 'downhill' in potential, from high V to low V. The potential falls steadily (constant slope) from the +500 V plate to the 0 V plate.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Potential between the plates","x":{"label":"Distance from the +500 V plate (mm)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"V(x)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,500],[0.5,375],[1,250],[1.5,125],[2,0]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"V(x)","x":1}],"labels":[{"series":"V(x)","x":0,"text":"+500 V plate","at":"right"},{"series":"V(x)","x":2,"text":"0 V plate","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":2,"from":500,"to":0,"text":"ΔV = 500 V","side":"left"}]},"strike":["Evenly spaced and parallel, pointing from the 0 V plate to the +500 V plate"]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced and parallel, pointing from the +500 V plate to the 0 V plate."}],"trap":"The other \"evenly spaced\" choice has the spacing right but the direction backward. A positive charge is pushed away from the +500 V plate, so the lines leave that plate."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 mm apart","note":"gap d"},{"t":"+500 V and 0 V","note":"plate voltages"},{"t":"2.5 × 10⁵ V/m","note":"field strength E"}]},{"text":"**Electric field** = the push a + charge would feel at each spot. **Field lines** draw it: the arrow shows the direction, and how close the lines are shows the strength.","marks":[{"t":"electric field lines","note":"arrows of the push"}]},{"text":"**Voltage (potential) is like height** for charges: + charges roll 'downhill' from high V to low V. Here V falls steadily across the gap.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Potential (voltage) across the gap","x":{"label":"Distance from the +500 V plate (mm)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"V(x)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,500],[0.5,375],[1,250],[1.5,125],[2,0]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"V(x)","x":0,"text":"+500 V plate (high)","at":"right"},{"series":"V(x)","x":2,"text":"0 V plate (low)","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Check the numbers:** a steady drop means E = ΔV/d.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 mm","note":"= 0.0020 m"},{"t":"2.5 × 10⁵ V/m","note":"✓ matches"}],"calc":["E = ΔV/d","E = 500 V ÷ **0.0020 m**","E = **2.5 × 10⁵ V/m** ✓"]},{"text":"**Same strength everywhere = a uniform field** → lines are **evenly spaced and parallel**. Crowded lines would mean a stronger field in one spot.","marks":[{"t":"the same magnitude","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"at every point","note":"→ evenly spaced lines"}]},{"text":"**Direction:** field lines point from high potential to low potential, from the +500 V plate toward the 0 V plate.","marks":[{"t":"+500 V","note":"lines start here"},{"t":"and 0 V","note":"lines end here"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Potential (voltage) across the gap","x":{"label":"Distance from the +500 V plate (mm)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"V(x)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,500],[0.5,375],[1,250],[1.5,125],[2,0]]}]},"graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":500,"to":0,"text":"downhill: + → 0"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** lines pointing from 0 V to +500 V run uphill (backwards); 'crowded near a plate' contradicts 'same magnitude at every point'.","marks":[{"t":"Away from the edges","note":"only edges bulge"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** field lines point from high V to low V; equal spacing = uniform field; between parallel plates E = ΔV/d."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A glucose standard solution has a concentration of 72 g/L (glucose: 180 g/mol). A test strip absorbs a 2.0 μL drop of this solution. What mass of glucose is in the drop?",idStem:"A solution contains 72 g/L glucose. How much glucose is in a 2 μL drop?",choices:["1.4 × 10⁻⁴ g","1.4 × 10⁻¹ g","8.0 × 10⁻⁷ g","3.6 × 10⁻⁷ g"],ans:0,exp:"Convert first: **2.0 μL = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L**. Mass = 72 g/L × 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L = 1.44 × 10⁻⁴ g. The molar mass is not needed because the concentration is already in grams per liter.",src:"pe3-cp:21",how:{"ask":"The concentration is in grams per liter, but the volume is in microliters. What must you do before multiplying?","know":"• **Mass = concentration (g/L) × volume (L).**\n• **μ (micro) = 10⁻⁶**, so 1 μL = 10⁻⁶ L. (m = 10⁻³: 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.)\n• Molar mass converts grams ↔ moles; you only need it if the question asks for moles.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the drop volume to liters to match the concentration unit.","hl":"2.0 μL","calc":["1 μL = 10⁻⁶ L","2.0 μL = **2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L**"]},{"text":"Mass = concentration × volume; the L units cancel.","hl":"72 g/L","calc":["m = 72 g/L × **2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L**","m = 144 × 10⁻⁶ g","m = **1.4 × 10⁻⁴ g**"]},{"text":"The molar mass is not needed for a MASS answer; using it gives moles. A mL slip gives an answer 1000× too large.","hl":"glucose: 180 g/mol","calc":["1.44 × 10⁻⁴ g ÷ 180 g/mol = 8.0 × 10⁻⁷ **mol** (not grams)","72 × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.14 g (μL treated as mL)"],"strike":["8.0 × 10⁻⁷ g","1.4 × 10⁻¹ g","3.6 × 10⁻⁷ g"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.4 × 10⁻⁴ g."}],"trap":"1.4 × 10⁻¹ g treats μL as mL (10⁻³ instead of 10⁻⁶). 8.0 × 10⁻⁷ is the number of moles (1.44 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 180), labeled as grams."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"72 g/L","note":"grams in each liter"},{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"grams per mole"},{"t":"2.0 μL","note":"drop volume"}]},{"text":"**Concentration 72 g/L** = every 1 liter holds 72 g of glucose. A tiny drop holds a tiny share of that.","marks":[{"t":"concentration of 72 g/L","note":"72 g in 1 L"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Same solution, different volumes","x":{"label":"Volume"},"y":{"label":"Glucose (g)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"categories":["1 L","0.5 L","2.0 μL drop"],"series":[{"name":"Glucose","values":[72,36,0.000144]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["2.0 μL drop","Glucose"]]}},{"text":"**Match the units first.** μ (micro) = one-millionth, so μL must become L.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 μL","note":"= 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L"}],"calc":["1 μL = 10⁻⁶ L","2.0 μL = **2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L**"]},{"text":"**Mass = concentration × volume.** The L units cancel, leaving grams.","marks":[{"t":"72 g/L","note":"×"},{"t":"2.0 μL","note":"2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L"}],"calc":["m = 72 g/L × 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ L","m = 144 × 10⁻⁶ g","m = **1.4 × 10⁻⁴ g**"]},{"text":"**The molar mass is a distractor.** It turns grams into moles, but the question asks for a MASS.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"not needed: asks grams"},{"t":"What mass","note":"answer in grams"}],"calc":["1.44 × 10⁻⁴ g ÷ 180 = 8.0 × 10⁻⁷ **mol** ✗"]},{"text":"**Prefix trap:** treating μL as mL (10⁻³) makes the answer 1000× too large.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 μL","note":"μ = 10⁻⁶, not 10⁻³"}],"calc":["72 × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.14 g ✗ (μL as mL)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass = (g/L) × (L). Convert the volume to the concentration's unit first (μL × 10⁻⁶ → L), and skip the molar mass unless moles are asked for."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"An enzyme converts the open-chain sugar on the left into the open-chain sugar on the right, and no cofactor or second substrate is consumed. To which enzyme class does it belong?",idStem:"Glucose oxidase converts glucose into gluconolactone by removing electrons. Which enzyme class is it?",fig:["glucose-open","fructose-open"],choices:["Oxidoreductase, since the C=O moves from C1 to C2","Isomerase, since atoms are rearranged within one molecule","Lyase, since a carbon–carbon bond is broken","Transferase, since an OH group moves between molecules"],ans:1,exp:"Both sugars are **C₆H₁₂O₆**: one substrate, one product, same formula, carbon chain intact. The aldehyde at C1 becomes CH₂OH while C2 becomes a ketone, an internal shift with no net oxidation. Rearranging one molecule into its isomer is the job of an **isomerase** (here, an aldose–ketose isomerase).",src:"pe3-cp:22",how:{"ask":"Compare the two drawings: same formula? Is anything added, removed, or split, and is there any net loss of electrons?","know":"The six enzyme classes, read from a drawn reaction:\n• **Oxidoreductase:** the substrate loses or gains H₂/electrons overall, usually with NAD⁺, FAD or O₂.\n• **Transferase:** a group moves from one molecule to another.\n• **Hydrolase:** water splits a bond.\n• **Lyase:** a bond is broken without water or redox, often forming a C=C or releasing CO₂.\n• **Isomerase:** one molecule rearranged into an isomer, same formula.\n• **Ligase:** two molecules joined using ATP.\nFirst check: count atoms on both sides.","steps":[{"text":"Only one substrate and one product, and nothing else is consumed.","hl":"no cofactor or second substrate is consumed"},{"text":"Count atoms on both sides.","fig":["glucose-open","fructose-open"],"calc":["left: **C₆H₁₂O₆**, aldehyde at C1","right: **C₆H₁₂O₆**, ketone at C2","same formula → isomers"]},{"text":"C1 goes from CHO to CH₂OH, gaining H; C2 goes from CHOH to C=O, losing H. The changes cancel, and no cofactor is used, so there is no net oxidation.","calc":["C1: CHO → CH₂OH, **reduced**","C2: CHOH → C=O, **oxidized**","net change: **0**"],"strike":["Oxidoreductase, since the C=O moves from C1 to C2"]},{"text":"The six-carbon chain is intact, and no group goes to another molecule.","strike":["Lyase, since a carbon–carbon bond is broken","Transferase, since an OH group moves between molecules"]},{"text":"Answer: isomerase. Rule: same formula, one in and one out → isomerase."}],"trap":"\"Oxidoreductase\" is tempting because the C=O changes position, but C2 is oxidized while C1 is reduced by the same amount. Nothing leaves the molecule, so there is no net redox and no cofactor."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawings.** Each zigzag corner is a carbon; hydrogens on carbons are hidden. Wedges and dashes only show 3D direction; they don't change here.","fig":["t3-glc-read","t3-fru-read"]},{"text":"**Number the carbons from the C=O end.** In the left sugar the C=O is on C1, at the very end: an **aldehyde**.","fig":"t3-glc-c1c2"},{"text":"**What changed:** only C1 and C2. In the right sugar the C=O moved to C2, inside the chain: a **ketone**.","fig":["t3-glc-c1c2","t3-fru-c1c2"]},{"text":"**Count every C, H and O (add the hidden H).** Both are C₆H₁₂O₆. Same atoms, new arrangement = an **isomer**. One molecule in, one out.","fig":["t3-glc-count","t3-fru-count"]},{"text":"**Oxidoreductase?** Oxidized = a carbon loses H or gains O; reduced = the reverse. Here the two changes cancel, and no cofactor (like NAD⁺) is used.","fig":["t3-glc-ox","t3-fru-ox"]},{"text":"**Lyase?** A lyase breaks a bond (often C–C) without using water. All six carbons are still one unbroken chain.","fig":"t3-fru-chain"},{"text":"**Transferase?** It moves a group from one molecule to a different one. There is only one molecule here, so nothing was transferred.","fig":"fructose-open"},{"text":"**Isomerase** = an enzyme that rearranges atoms within one molecule, giving its isomer. That fits what you counted.","fig":["glucose-open","fructose-open"]},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** count atoms and molecules on both sides first. Same formula, one in, one out, no cofactor = a rearrangement. Then check each answer's reason against your count."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"In an amperometric glucose sensor, each glucose molecule oxidized delivers 2 electrons to the electrode. During one test, a total charge of 64 mC is collected. Faraday's constant is 96,500 C/mol and glucose has a molar mass of 180 g/mol (= 180 μg/μmol). About what mass of glucose was oxidized?",idStem:"Each glucose molecule gives up 2 electrons. If a sensor measures 0.66 μmol of electrons, what mass of glucose reacted? (180 μg/μmol)",choices:["120 μg","60 μg","30 μg","0.33 μg"],ans:1,exp:"Charge → moles of electrons: 0.064 C ÷ 96,500 C/mol = 6.6 × 10⁻⁷ mol = 0.66 μmol e⁻. **Divide by 2 electrons per glucose** → 0.33 μmol glucose × 180 μg/μmol ≈ 60 μg.",src:"pe3-cp:23",how:{"ask":"Turn the charge into moles of electrons, then into moles of glucose using the electrons-per-molecule ratio, then into mass.","know":"• **Faraday's constant F = 96,500 C per mole of electrons.** Moles of e⁻ = charge (C) ÷ F.\n• Use the stoichiometric ratio: here 2 mol e⁻ per 1 mol glucose.\n• Mass = moles × molar mass. 180 g/mol = 180 μg/μmol.\n• m (milli) = 10⁻³, μ (micro) = 10⁻⁶.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the charge to coulombs.","hl":"64 mC","calc":["64 mC = **0.064 C**"]},{"text":"Charge → moles of electrons with Faraday's constant.","calc":["n(e⁻) = Q/F","n(e⁻) = 0.064 C ÷ 96,500 C/mol","n(e⁻) = **6.6 × 10⁻⁷ mol = 0.66 μmol**"]},{"text":"Each glucose gives 2 electrons, so divide by 2.","hl":"each glucose molecule oxidized delivers 2 electrons to the electrode","calc":["n(glucose) = 0.66 μmol ÷ **2**","= **0.33 μmol**"],"strike":["120 μg","0.33 μg"]},{"text":"Moles → mass, using 180 μg/μmol.","calc":["m = 0.33 μmol × 180 μg/μmol","m ≈ **60 μg**","(÷ 2 twice → 30 μg ✗; no ÷ 2 → 120 μg ✗)"],"strike":["30 μg"]},{"text":"Answer: about 60 μg."}],"trap":"120 μg skips the ÷2 and treats each electron as a glucose molecule. 0.33 μg stops at moles of glucose and reports it as a mass."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2 electrons","note":"e⁻ per glucose"},{"t":"64 mC","note":"total charge"},{"t":"96,500 C/mol","note":"charge of 1 mol e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Charge** (coulombs, C) is just electrons counted in bulk. **Faraday's constant** = the charge of 1 mole of electrons. *Oxidized* = loses electrons.","marks":[{"t":"Faraday's constant","note":"C per mole of e⁻"},{"t":"molecule oxidized","note":"gives up electrons"}]},{"text":"**Plan:** charge → moles of electrons → moles of glucose → mass.","calc":["Q ÷ F = mol e⁻","mol e⁻ ÷ 2 = mol glucose","mol glucose × 180 = mass"],"marks":[{"t":"About what mass","note":"goal: mass"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefix:** m (milli) = 10⁻³.","marks":[{"t":"64 mC","note":"= 0.064 C"}],"calc":["64 mC = **0.064 C**"]},{"text":"**Charge → moles of electrons** with Faraday's constant.","marks":[{"t":"96,500 C/mol","note":"÷ this"}],"calc":["n(e⁻) = 0.064 ÷ 96,500","n(e⁻) = **6.6 × 10⁻⁷ mol = 0.66 μmol**"]},{"text":"**Each glucose gives 2 electrons**, so there are half as many glucose molecules as electrons.","marks":[{"t":"2 electrons","note":"÷ 2"}],"calc":["n(glucose) = 0.66 μmol ÷ 2","= **0.33 μmol**"]},{"text":"**Moles → mass** using the μg-per-μmol form of the molar mass.","marks":[{"t":"180 μg/μmol","note":"× this"}],"calc":["m = 0.33 μmol × 180 μg/μmol","m ≈ **60 μg**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** no ÷ 2 gives 120 μg; dividing by 2 twice gives 30 μg; 0.33 μg stops at moles.","marks":[{"t":"each glucose molecule","note":"÷ 2 exactly once"}],"calc":["0.66 × 180 = 120 μg ✗ (no ÷ 2)","0.165 × 180 = 30 μg ✗ (÷ 2 twice)","0.33 = μmol, not μg ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** electrochemistry chain: Q ÷ F = mol e⁻; ÷ electrons per molecule = mol substance; × molar mass = mass."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Glucose oxidase removes two hydrogen atoms (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻) from glucose. A cofactor tightly bound in the active site accepts them and later passes them to O₂. Which cofactor accepts the hydrogens, and what happens to it?",idStem:"An enzyme oxidizes glucose by removing two hydrogens. Which cofactor accepts them?",choices:["NADH, which is oxidized to NAD⁺","FADH₂, which is oxidized to FAD","FAD, which is reduced to FADH₂","NADPH, which is oxidized to NADP⁺"],ans:2,exp:"A species that **accepts** hydrogens (electrons) must be in its **oxidized form**, and it becomes **reduced**. **FAD → FADH₂** fits. NADH, FADH₂ and NADPH are already reduced; they can only donate.",src:"pe3-cp:24",how:{"ask":"Something that takes electrons away from glucose must itself be ready to receive electrons. Which form of each cofactor can do that, and what does receiving them do to it?","know":"• Electron carriers come in pairs: **oxidized form (can accept e⁻)** and **reduced form (can donate e⁻)**.\n• FAD (oxidized) + 2 H → **FADH₂** (reduced).\n• NAD⁺ (oxidized) + H⁻ → NADH (reduced); NADP⁺/NADPH likewise.\n• Gaining electrons = reduction; losing them = oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"Losing H atoms (H⁺ + e⁻) is oxidation, so glucose is oxidized and the acceptor is reduced.","hl":"removes two hydrogen atoms (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻) from glucose","fig":"beta-glucopyranose","calc":["glucose − 2 H → lactone: **oxidation**","acceptor + 2 H: **reduction**"]},{"text":"A cofactor that ACCEPTS H must start in its oxidized form. FADH₂, NADH and NADPH are already reduced; each choice says they are oxidized, which is the reverse.","hl":"accepts them","strike":["NADH, which is oxidized to NAD⁺","FADH₂, which is oxidized to FAD","NADPH, which is oxidized to NADP⁺"]},{"text":"'Tightly bound' fits FAD, a prosthetic group of flavoproteins (NAD⁺ diffuses on and off). Write its two half-steps.","hl":"tightly bound in the active site","calc":["E–FAD + glucose → E–**FADH₂** + lactone","E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–**FAD** + H₂O₂ (regenerated)"]},{"text":"Answer: FAD, which is reduced to FADH₂."}],"trap":"\"NADH, oxidized to NAD⁺\" describes a cofactor GIVING electrons. When glucose is being oxidized, the partner must be reduced, so a choice in which the cofactor is oxidized cannot be right."},teach:[{"text":"**Name the players.** An enzyme pulls hydrogens off glucose and a helper molecule catches them.","marks":[{"t":"Glucose oxidase","note":"enzyme"},{"t":"two hydrogen atoms (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)","note":"H = H⁺ + e⁻"},{"t":"A cofactor","note":"helper molecule"}]},{"text":"**Losing H (its electrons) = oxidation; gaining H = reduction.** Glucose loses 2 H, so the cofactor gains them and is **reduced**.","marks":[{"t":"removes two hydrogen atoms","note":"glucose: oxidized"},{"t":"accepts them","note":"cofactor: reduced"}]},{"text":"**Each carrier has an empty (oxidized) form and a loaded (reduced) form.** Only an EMPTY form can accept hydrogens.","calc":["NAD⁺ (empty) → **NADH** (loaded)","FAD (empty) → **FADH₂** (loaded)","NADP⁺ (empty) → **NADPH** (loaded)"],"marks":[{"t":"accepts the hydrogens","note":"must start empty"}]},{"text":"**Which can still accept?** NADH, FADH₂ and NADPH are already loaded; they can only give hydrogens away. FAD is empty.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Hydrogens each form can still accept","x":{"label":"Carrier form"},"y":{"label":"H it can accept","min":0,"max":2,"step":1},"categories":["NADH","FADH₂","NADPH","FAD"],"series":[{"name":"Room for H","values":[0,0,0,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["FAD","Room for H"]]}},{"text":"**'Tightly bound' fits FAD**: it is built into flavoprotein enzymes, while NAD⁺ floats on and off.","marks":[{"t":"tightly bound in the active site","note":"FAD: built-in"}]},{"text":"**Then it is reset:** FADH₂ hands the hydrogens to O₂ and becomes FAD again, ready for the next glucose.","marks":[{"t":"passes them to O₂","note":"FADH₂ → FAD again"}],"calc":["E–FAD + glucose → E–**FADH₂** + lactone","E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–**FAD** + H₂O₂"]},{"text":"**Traps:** every 'is oxidized' option describes a loaded carrier giving H away, the reverse of accepting.","marks":[{"t":"what happens to it","note":"accepts → reduced"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** accepting H or e⁻ = being REDUCED, so the acceptor must start in its oxidized form (NAD⁺, FAD, NADP⁺) and end as NADH, FADH₂, NADPH."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A lipid-soluble hormone diffuses across the plasma membrane and binds a receptor in the cytoplasm, and the complex then moves to the nucleus to regulate transcription. If the hormone is a steroid, which structure could it be?",idStem:"Which ring pattern identifies a steroid, the kind of molecule a steroid hormone receptor binds?",choiceFigs:["11-cis-retinal","testosterone","dopamine","oleic-acid"],choices:["Retinal","Testosterone","Dopamine","Oleic acid"],ans:1,exp:"Every steroid is built on the **four fused rings of cholesterol: three six-membered and one five-membered**, 17 ring carbons. Only testosterone has that core. Retinal has one ring and a polyene chain, dopamine has one benzene ring with an amine, and oleic acid has no rings.",src:"pe3-cp:25",how:{"ask":"What ring pattern defines a steroid, and which drawing shows exactly that pattern?","know":"• **Steroid nucleus:** four fused rings in a 6-6-6-5 pattern, called A, B, C, D, usually with methyl groups at two ring junctions and an O on ring A.\n• Steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, estradiol, testosterone) are made from cholesterol, cross membranes, and bind intracellular receptors.\n• **Catecholamines** such as dopamine: one benzene ring with two OH groups and an aminoethyl chain; water-soluble, act on surface receptors.\n• **Fatty acids:** long unbranched chain ending in COOH, no rings.\n• **Retinoids:** one six-membered ring plus a long chain of alternating double bonds, from vitamin A.\n• To identify: count rings and look at how many share edges.","steps":[{"text":"The question fixes the class: a steroid. Look for the steroid ring pattern, not just lipid solubility.","hl":"If the hormone is a steroid"},{"text":"The steroid nucleus: four fused rings, three six-membered and one five-membered. Count the rings in each drawing.","fig":"cholesterol","calc":["steroid core: **6-6-6-5**, four fused rings","retinal: **1** ring + polyene chain","dopamine: **1** benzene ring + amine","oleic acid: **0** rings"]},{"text":"Dopamine is a catecholamine: one aromatic ring and an amine, water-soluble.","fig":"dopamine","strike":["Dopamine"]},{"text":"Oleic acid is a fatty acid: a long chain ending in COOH, no rings at all. Retinal has a single ring on a long chain of double bonds.","fig":["oleic-acid","11-cis-retinal"],"strike":["Oleic acid","Retinal"]},{"text":"Testosterone shows the 6-6-6-5 fused core, with a C=O on ring A and an OH on the five-membered ring D.","fig":"testosterone"},{"text":"Answer: testosterone. Rule: four fused rings, three of six and one of five, means steroid."}],"trap":"Retinal is tempting because retinoids are lipid-soluble and some act through nuclear receptors, but it has one ring and a long chain, not the four fused rings of a steroid."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading ring drawings.** Each corner is a carbon (hydrogens hidden). A polygon is a ring; rings that share a side are **fused**. This is cholesterol.","fig":"t3-chol-read"},{"text":"**Every steroid has this core:** four fused rings, three with 6 corners and one with 5 (6-6-6-5). Side groups vary; the core never does.","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd"},{"text":"**Why steroids cross membranes.** A membrane is oily inside. A molecule that is mostly C and H is oily too, so it slips through. Steroid hormones are made from cholesterol.","fig":"t3-chol-oily"},{"text":"**Oily but not a steroid.** A long chain ending in COOH (an acid group), with **no rings**: a fatty acid.","fig":"t3-oleic"},{"text":"**One ring is not enough.** One six-corner ring plus a long chain of alternating single and double bonds: a vitamin A relative.","fig":"t3-retinal"},{"text":"**A ring plus an amine (NH₂).** One benzene ring (six corners, alternating double lines) with two OH groups. It dissolves in water and has one ring.","fig":"t3-dopamine"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** a steroid = four fused rings, 6-6-6-5. In each answer drawing, count rings and corners and ask: four rings sharing sides, three of 6 and one of 5?","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The graph shows the titration of H₂PO₄⁻ with NaOH, where H₂PO₄⁻ ⇌ HPO₄²⁻ + H⁺. A buffer made from NaH₂PO₄ and Na₂HPO₄ reads pH 6.6. To bring it to the pH where its buffer capacity is greatest, which compound should be added, and why?",idStem:"A phosphate buffer (pKa 7.2) reads pH 7.6, but you need pH 7.2. What should you do?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of H₂PO₄⁻ with NaOH","x":{"label":"Equivalents of OH⁻ added","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.05,5.92],[0.2,6.6],[0.35,6.93],[0.5,7.2],[0.65,7.47],[0.8,7.8],[0.95,8.48]]}]},choices:["Na₂HPO₄, since the base:acid ratio must rise from 0.25 to 1","NaH₂PO₄, since the base:acid ratio must rise from 0.25 to 1","Na₂HPO₄, since the base:acid ratio must fall from 4 to 1","HCl, since the base:acid ratio must rise from 0.25 to 1"],ans:0,exp:"The flattest part of the curve, at half-equivalence, is where pH = pKa = **7.2** and capacity is greatest. At pH 6.6, [HPO₄²⁻]/[H₂PO₄⁻] = 10^(6.6 − 7.2) = **0.25**. To raise the ratio to 1, add the base form, **Na₂HPO₄**.",src:"pe3-cp:26",how:{"ask":"Where on the curve is the buffer strongest, what base:acid ratio does pH 6.6 correspond to, and which form must be added to move toward that point?","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]). Here base = HPO₄²⁻, acid = H₂PO₄⁻.\n• On a titration curve, the **flat middle region** is the buffer region; its center, at half-equivalence, is where pH = pKa and [base] = [acid]. Buffer capacity is greatest there.\n• Below the pKa there is too much acid form: add the base form to raise the ratio.\n• Adding the conjugate partner, not a strong acid or base, raises the total buffer concentration rather than using it up.","steps":[{"text":"Find the point of greatest capacity: the flattest part of the curve, at half-equivalence. Its pH is the pKa.","hl":"the pH where its buffer capacity is greatest","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":0.5}],"yLine":7.2,"shade":[{"x1":0.35,"x2":0.65,"text":"flattest"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.5,"text":"pKa = 7.2","at":"left"}]},"calc":["half-equivalence: [HPO₄²⁻] = [H₂PO₄⁻]","pH = pKa = **7.2**"]},{"text":"Locate the buffer as it is now, at pH 6.6, and get its ratio.","hl":"reads pH 6.6","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2}],"yLine":6.6,"spans":[{"x":0.2,"from":6.6,"to":7.2,"text":"log ratio = −0.6"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2,"text":"now: pH 6.6","at":"below"}]},"calc":["6.6 = 7.2 + log(base/acid)","log(base/acid) = **−0.6**","base/acid = 10⁻⁰·⁶ = **0.25**"]},{"text":"The ratio is 0.25, not 4, and it must rise to 1, not fall.","strike":["Na₂HPO₄, since the base:acid ratio must fall from 4 to 1"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.5,"text":"base:acid < 1"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2,"text":"ratio 0.25","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Raising the ratio needs more base form. Adding NaH₂PO₄, the acid form, or HCl lowers the ratio instead.","strike":["NaH₂PO₄, since the base:acid ratio must rise from 0.25 to 1","HCl, since the base:acid ratio must rise from 0.25 to 1"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.2,"x2":0.5,"text":"add base form →"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2,"text":"start","at":"below"},{"series":"pH","x":0.5,"text":"target","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: add Na₂HPO₄, moving the buffer right along the curve to the flat center at pH 7.2. Rule: below the pKa, add the base form; above it, add the acid form.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.5,"text":"pH 7.2 = pKa, ratio 1","at":"left"}]}}],"trap":"HCl seems like a quick pH adjuster, but adding acid lowers the pH further and lowers the base:acid ratio. The buffer here needs more of its base form."},teach:[{"text":"**Up the side = pH.** Low pH = acidic, high pH = basic.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"reads pH 6.6","note":"the buffer now"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = base (OH⁻) added**, as a fraction of what it takes to convert all the acid form. 0 = none, 1 = all converted.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"with NaOH","note":"base being added"}]},{"text":"**⇌ = the reaction goes both ways.** H₂PO₄⁻ is the acid form (still has the H⁺); HPO₄²⁻ is the base form (lost it).","marks":[{"t":"where H₂PO₄⁻","note":"acid form"},{"t":"HPO₄²⁻ + H⁺","note":"base form + H⁺"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.05,"text":"mostly H₂PO₄⁻","at":"right"},{"series":"pH","x":0.95,"text":"mostly HPO₄²⁻","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**A buffer** = a mix of the acid and base forms that soaks up added acid or base, so the pH barely moves: the flat middle of the curve.","marks":[{"t":"NaH₂PO₄ and Na₂HPO₄","note":"acid form + base form"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.2,"x2":0.8,"text":"buffer region"}]}},{"text":"**Greatest capacity = the flattest point**, where acid and base forms are equal (half-way). There **pH = pKa = 7.2**.","marks":[{"t":"buffer capacity is greatest","note":"flattest point"}],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":0.5}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.5,"text":"pKa = 7.2, ratio 1","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Find the buffer now** and its base:acid ratio with Henderson–Hasselbalch.","marks":[{"t":"pH 6.6","note":"0.6 below pKa"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2,"text":"now: pH 6.6","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":0.2,"from":6.6,"to":7.2,"text":"log ratio = −0.6"}]},"calc":["pH = pKa + log(base/acid)","6.6 = 7.2 + log(base/acid)","base/acid = 10⁻⁰·⁶ = **0.25**"]},{"text":"**To reach the center, move right** along the curve: more base form. Adding Na₂HPO₄ raises the ratio from 0.25 to 1.","marks":[{"t":"which compound should be added","note":"add the base form"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.2,"x2":0.5,"text":"add base form →"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** the ratio is 0.25 (not 4) because pH is below pKa. NaH₂PO₄ or HCl add acid and push the pH the wrong way.","marks":[{"t":"NaH₂PO₄","note":"acid form: wrong way"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":0.2,"text":"ratio 0.25, not 4","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** best buffer = pH = pKa (ratio 1). Below pKa, add the base form; above pKa, add the acid form. base/acid = 10^(pH − pKa)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Serine, 105 Da, and alanine, 89 Da, are joined to form the dipeptide shown, 176 Da. To which labeled atom is the carbonyl carbon of the serine residue bonded through the bond formed in this reaction?",idStem:"Two amino acids form a peptide bond. What is the mass of the small molecule released?",fig:"ser-ala-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:0,exp:"194 − 176 = **18 Da: one H₂O is released**, a condensation. The peptide (amide) bond joins the **carbonyl carbon of serine to the α-amino N of alanine**: the N–H between the two residues, site I. II is serine's side-chain OH, III is the free N-terminal NH₂, and IV is the C-terminal COOH.",src:"pe3-cp:27",how:{"ask":"In the drawing, which C=O carbon sits between the two amino acids, and what atom is it bonded to?","know":"• **Peptide bond formation is a condensation:** the COOH of one amino acid and the NH₂ of the next lose H₂O (18 Da) and form an amide, –C(=O)–NH–.\n• In a drawn peptide, find the amide: a C=O directly bonded to an N–H in the middle of the chain.\n• The **N-terminus** keeps a free NH₂ (or NH₃⁺); the **C-terminus** keeps a free COOH (or COO⁻).\n• Hydrolysis is the reverse: water adds across the amide C–N bond.","steps":[{"text":"Check the masses: the dipeptide is lighter than the two amino acids by one water.","hl":"Serine, 105 Da, and alanine, 89 Da","calc":["105 + 89 = **194 Da**","194 − 176 = **18 Da** = H₂O","→ condensation: COOH + H₂N → C(=O)–NH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Look at each label. III is the nitrogen at the left end, still a free NH₂: the N-terminus. IV is the OH of the COOH at the right end: the C-terminus.","fig":"ser-ala-sites","strike":["III","IV"]},{"text":"II is the OH on serine's side chain. Bonding the C=O to it would make an ester, not a peptide.","strike":["II"]},{"text":"I is the N–H between the residues, bonded directly to serine's C=O carbon: the amide, highlighted.","fig":"ser-ala-amide-hl"},{"text":"Answer: I. Rule: in a peptide drawing, the peptide bond is the C(=O)–N–H in the middle of the chain; the ends keep NH₂ and COOH."}],"trap":"II, serine's side-chain OH, is tempting because an OH could also condense with a COOH, but that would make an ester. A peptide bond always links C=O to the next residue's backbone nitrogen."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Zigzag corners are carbons (hidden H). N = nitrogen, O = oxygen; a double line to O is C=O. Labels I–IV mark four atoms.","fig":"t3-sa-read"},{"text":"**Each amino acid** has an amino group (NH₂) at one end, an acid group (COOH) at the other, and a side group in the middle.","fig":["t3-ser-parts","t3-ala-parts"]},{"text":"**Check the masses:** 105 + 89 = 194; 194 − 176 = 18, one water (H₂O). Serine's COOH gives up OH, alanine's NH₂ gives up one H.","fig":["t3-ser-loses","t3-ala-loses"]},{"text":"**The new bond:** serine's C=O carbon (highlighted) bonds to alanine's N. This C(=O)–N link is the **peptide bond**; making it releases water.","fig":"t3-sa-carbonyl"},{"text":"**Site III** is the NH₂ at the left end. It is still free, so it never reacted. This end is the **N-terminus**.","fig":"t3-sa-3"},{"text":"**Site IV** is the OH of a whole COOH at the right end; it never reacted. This end is the **C-terminus**.","fig":"t3-sa-4"},{"text":"**Site II** is serine's side-group OH. A C=O bonded to an O would be an **ester**, C(=O)–O, not a peptide bond.","fig":"t3-sa-2"},{"text":"**Look between the two residues:** an N–H sits directly on serine's C=O carbon. That C(=O)–N–H unit is the peptide link.","fig":"t3-sa-1"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** the peptide bond is a C=O carbon bonded straight to an N–H mid-chain; the ends keep free NH₂ and COOH. For each label ask: free end, side group, or that N–H?"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"To get one point for a Michaelis–Menten plot, a student mixes an enzyme with 2 mM substrate and records the product formed over 20 min, as graphed. Which value should be plotted as the reaction rate at this substrate concentration?",idStem:"Which condition is NOT needed for good Michaelis–Menten kinetics data?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed, 2 mM substrate","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"y":{"label":"[Product] (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,18],[2,33],[5,63],[10,86],[15,95],[20,98]]}]},choices:["About 18 μM/min, the slope over the first minute","About 1.2 μM/min, the slope from 10 to 20 min","About 4.9 μM/min, the product at 20 min divided by 20 min","About 98 μM, the product present at 20 min"],ans:0,exp:"Michaelis–Menten analysis uses the **initial rate, v₀**: the slope at the start, while [S] is still the stated value and product has not built up. The first-minute slope is 18 − 0 = **≈18 μM/min**. Later slopes are lower because substrate is used up and the reaction nears its end point.",src:"pe3-cp:28",how:{"ask":"Which part of the curve still reflects the substrate concentration the student set, before the reaction slows down?","know":"• **v₀ (initial velocity)** = the slope of [product] vs time at the very start, the steep straight part.\n• Michaelis–Menten requires v₀ because only then is [S] known, product is negligible, and the reverse reaction is not yet significant.\n• As the reaction runs, [S] falls and the curve bends over, so later slopes and averages underestimate v₀.\n• A rate has units of concentration per time; a plateau concentration is not a rate.","steps":[{"text":"A Michaelis–Menten point needs the rate at the substrate concentration set: 2 mM. That is only true at the start.","hl":"one point for a Michaelis–Menten plot","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"[S] still 2 mM"}]}},{"text":"Read the steep, nearly straight start and take its slope.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Product","x":0},{"series":"Product","x":1}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":18,"text":"rise 18 in 1 min"}]},"calc":["v₀ ≈ (18 − 0) μM ÷ 1 min","v₀ ≈ **18 μM/min**"]},{"text":"By 10–20 min the curve is nearly flat: substrate is running out, so this slope is far below v₀.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Product","x":10},{"series":"Product","x":20}],"shade":[{"x1":10,"x2":20,"text":"nearly flat"}],"spans":[{"x":20,"from":86,"to":98,"text":"rise 12 in 10 min","side":"left"}]},"calc":["(98 − 86) ÷ 10 = **1.2 μM/min**"],"strike":["About 1.2 μM/min, the slope from 10 to 20 min"]},{"text":"Dividing the final product by the total time averages the fast and slow parts. A concentration with no time unit is not a rate at all.","calc":["98 ÷ 20 = **4.9 μM/min**, an average","98 μM: units are not per minute"],"strike":["About 4.9 μM/min, the product at 20 min divided by 20 min","About 98 μM, the product present at 20 min"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":20,"from":0,"to":98,"text":"98 over 20 min = average","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: about 18 μM/min, the initial slope. Rule: always take v₀ from the first few percent of conversion, where the curve is straight.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":1,"text":"v₀ = 18 μM/min","at":"right"}]}}],"trap":"The average over 20 min, about 4.9 μM/min, looks like a fair overall rate, but it mixes the fast start with the slow, substrate-depleted end, so it is far below v₀."},teach:[{"text":"**Up the side = product made** (μM). Higher = more product in the tube.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"product formed","note":"y axis: product"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = time** since mixing, 0 to 20 min.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"over 20 min","note":"x axis: time"}]},{"text":"**An enzyme** is a protein that speeds up a reaction; the **substrate** is what it works on. The curve rises fast, then levels off as substrate runs out.","marks":[{"t":"an enzyme","note":"protein catalyst"},{"t":"2 mM substrate","note":"what it converts"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":1,"text":"fast start","at":"right"},{"series":"Product","x":20,"text":"levels off","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**A Michaelis–Menten plot** graphs rate against substrate level. Each run gives ONE point: the rate at its own substrate level (here 2 mM).","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten plot","note":"rate vs [substrate]"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"A Michaelis–Menten plot: one point per substrate level","x":{"label":"[Substrate] (mM)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Initial rate v₀ (μM/min)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"v₀","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,12],[2,18],[4,24],[6,27],[10,30]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"v₀","x":2,"text":"this run's point","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**A rate is a slope:** rise ÷ run, in μM per minute. A plain concentration (μM) is not a rate.","marks":[{"t":"reaction rate","note":"slope: μM per min"}],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":10,"from":63,"to":86,"text":"rise 23 in 5 min"}]}},{"text":"**[S] is 2 mM only at the start**; after that it is being used up. So the rate to plot is the **initial rate, v₀**, from the first, straight part.","marks":[{"t":"at this substrate concentration","note":"only true at the start"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"[S] still ≈ 2 mM"}]}},{"text":"**Measure v₀:** rise over the first minute.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Product","x":1}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":18,"text":"rise 18 in 1 min"}]},"calc":["v₀ = (18 − 0) μM ÷ 1 min","v₀ ≈ **18 μM/min**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** the late slope is low because substrate is gone; 98 ÷ 20 averages fast and slow parts; 98 μM has no 'per minute'.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":10,"x2":20,"text":"substrate running out"}],"spans":[{"x":20,"from":86,"to":98,"text":"only 12 in 10 min","side":"left"}]},"calc":["(98 − 86) ÷ 10 = 1.2 μM/min ✗ (late)","98 ÷ 20 = 4.9 μM/min ✗ (average)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Michaelis–Menten rates are INITIAL rates: the slope of the first straight part of product vs time, while [S] still equals the value you set."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"An enzyme carries out its reaction in two steps: (1) E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺ → E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺; (2) E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂. Which of the following is the overall balanced reaction catalyzed by the enzyme?",idStem:"An enzyme uses FAD at intermediate steps, but FAD is regenerated each cycle. Does FAD appear in the overall balanced equation?",choices:["FADH₂ + O₂ → FAD + H₂O₂","FAD + NADPH + H⁺ → FADH₂ + NADP⁺","NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂","NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ + FAD → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂ + FADH₂"],ans:2,exp:"Add the two steps and cancel species that appear on both sides. **E–FAD is used in step 1 and remade in step 2**, and E–FADH₂ is made in step 1 and used in step 2, so both cancel, just as a catalyst does. Net: **NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂**.",src:"pe3-cp:29",how:{"ask":"Add the two equations together. Which species show up on both sides and therefore cancel?","know":"• To get an overall reaction from steps, **add all the steps and cancel anything that appears on both the left and the right**.\n• **Intermediates** are made then used; **catalysts** (and regenerated cofactors) are used then remade. Neither appears in the net equation.\n• Check: atoms and charge balance in the net equation.","steps":[{"text":"Write step 1: FAD is reduced by NADPH.","hl":"(1) E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺ → E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺"},{"text":"Write step 2: FADH₂ is reoxidized by O₂, regenerating E–FAD.","hl":"(2) E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂"},{"text":"Add the steps and cancel anything on both sides: the cofactor is regenerated, so it drops out.","calc":["E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺ + E–FADH₂ + O₂ →","   E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺ + E–FAD + H₂O₂","cancel **E–FAD** and **E–FADH₂**","net: **NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂**"],"strike":["NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ + FAD → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂ + FADH₂"]},{"text":"Check balance, and eliminate the choices that are just one half-step.","calc":["H: 1 + 1 = 2 → H₂O₂ has **2** ✓","charge: +1 (H⁺) → +1 (NADP⁺) ✓"],"strike":["FADH₂ + O₂ → FAD + H₂O₂","FAD + NADPH + H⁺ → FADH₂ + NADP⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂."}],"trap":"The longest choice keeps FAD and FADH₂ in the equation, as if FAD were consumed. It is regenerated in step 2, so like a catalyst it cancels. The other two choices are just one step each."},teach:[{"text":"**Two steps happen one after the other.** The question wants the single overall reaction they add up to.","marks":[{"t":"(1) E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺ → E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺","note":"step 1"},{"t":"(2) E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂","note":"step 2"},{"t":"overall balanced reaction","note":"both steps added up"}]},{"text":"**The arrow means \"turns into\".** Left side = used up; right side = made.","marks":[{"t":"E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺","note":"left: used up"},{"t":"→","note":"turns into"},{"t":"E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺","note":"right: made"}]},{"text":"**E–FAD is the enzyme's helper (a cofactor).** E = enzyme. FAD can carry 2 hydrogens; FADH₂ is FAD loaded with them.","marks":[{"t":"(1) E–FAD","note":"enzyme + empty FAD"},{"t":"E–FADH₂","note":"FAD loaded with 2 H"}]},{"text":"**Follow E–FAD.** Used in step 1, made again in step 2: it comes out as it went in, like a **catalyst**.","marks":[{"t":"(1) E–FAD","note":"used in step 1"},{"t":"E–FAD + H₂O₂","note":"made again in step 2"}],"calc":["used, then given back → **cancels out**"]},{"text":"**Follow E–FADH₂.** Made in step 1, used in step 2: an **intermediate**, it only exists in the middle.","marks":[{"t":"E–FADH₂","note":"made in step 1"},{"t":"E–FADH₂ + O₂","note":"used in step 2"}],"calc":["made, then used up → **cancels out**"]},{"text":"**Add the two steps, then cross out anything on both sides.** What is left is the overall reaction.","marks":[{"t":"NADPH + H⁺","note":"kept: left side"},{"t":"NADP⁺","note":"kept: right side"},{"t":"H₂O₂","note":"kept: right side"}],"calc":["left: E–FAD + NADPH + H⁺ + E–FADH₂ + O₂","right: E–FADH₂ + NADP⁺ + E–FAD + H₂O₂","cross out **E–FAD** and **E–FADH₂**","net: **NADPH + H⁺ + O₂ → NADP⁺ + H₂O₂**"]},{"text":"**Check the atoms and charge balance.** Same count on both sides means the net reaction is right.","marks":[{"t":"H⁺","note":"1 H, charge +1"},{"t":"O₂","note":"2 O in"},{"t":"H₂O₂","note":"2 H + 2 O out"}],"calc":["O: 2 (O₂) → 2 (H₂O₂) ✓","H: 1 from NADPH + 1 from H⁺ = 2 → H₂O₂ ✓","charge: +1 (H⁺) → +1 (NADP⁺) ✓"]},{"text":"**Traps:** one step alone is only half the story, and leaving FAD and FADH₂ in means you forgot to cancel.","marks":[{"t":"(2) E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂","note":"one step = not overall"}],"calc":["FAD on left + FADH₂ on right → should cancel ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to get an overall reaction, add all the steps and cancel anything on both sides. Catalysts (used then remade) and intermediates (made then used) never appear in it."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"In the last step of an oxidase reaction, the enzyme's bound cofactor transfers electrons to oxygen: O₂ + 4 H⁺ + 4 e⁻ → 2 H₂O. Which of the following describes what happens to oxygen in this step?",idStem:"In a reaction, O₂ becomes H₂O. What happens to oxygen?",choices:["It is oxidized, since it gains electrons","It is reduced, since its oxidation state goes from −2 to 0","It is reduced, since its oxidation state goes from 0 to −2","It is oxidized, since it combines with hydrogen"],ans:2,exp:"O in O₂ (an element) has oxidation state **0**; in H₂O it is **−2**. **A decrease in oxidation state (gain of electrons) is reduction**, so O₂ is reduced and acts as the oxidizing agent.",src:"pe3-cp:32",how:{"ask":"Assign an oxidation state to oxygen before and after, and decide which way it changed.","know":"• **Oxidation state rules**: an element by itself = 0; H in a compound = +1; O in most compounds = −2; the states in a neutral molecule add to 0.\n• **OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain** (of electrons).\n• Reduction → oxidation state decreases; oxidation → it increases.","steps":[{"text":"Assign oxygen's oxidation state on each side.","hl":"O₂ + 4 H⁺ + 4 e⁻ → 2 H₂O","calc":["O₂: element → O = **0**","H₂O: 2(+1) + O = 0 → O = **−2**"],"strike":["It is reduced, since its oxidation state goes from −2 to 0"]},{"text":"0 → −2 is a decrease: each O gains 2 electrons, matching the 4 e⁻ in the equation.","calc":["2 O × 2 e⁻ = **4 e⁻** ✓"]},{"text":"Gaining electrons is reduction (OIL RIG), and gaining H in an organic sense is also reduction.","hl":"transfers electrons to oxygen","strike":["It is oxidized, since it gains electrons","It is oxidized, since it combines with hydrogen"]},{"text":"Answer: oxygen is reduced, since its oxidation state goes from 0 to −2."}],"trap":"\"Oxidized, since it gains electrons\" pairs a true fact with the wrong label: gaining electrons is reduction. The \"−2 to 0\" choice has the right label but the before and after reversed."},teach:[{"text":"**Read the equation:** oxygen gas takes in H⁺ and electrons and comes out as water.","marks":[{"t":"O₂ + 4 H⁺ + 4 e⁻","note":"left: used up"},{"t":"2 H₂O","note":"right: made (water)"},{"t":"transfers electrons to oxygen","note":"oxygen receives e⁻"}]},{"text":"**e⁻ = an electron**, a tiny negative particle. Electrons on the left are being taken in by oxygen.","marks":[{"t":"4 e⁻","note":"4 electrons taken in"},{"t":"→","note":"turns into"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation state** = an atom's bookkeeping charge. Gained electrons make it more negative. An element on its own counts as **0**.","marks":[{"t":"O₂","note":"pure element: O = 0"}],"calc":["O₂ is just oxygen bonded to oxygen","oxidation state of O = **0**"]},{"text":"**In water, O carries −2.** H counts +1, and a neutral molecule adds up to 0.","marks":[{"t":"2 H₂O","note":"O = −2 here"},{"t":"4 H⁺","note":"H counts +1"}],"calc":["H₂O: 2 × (+1) + O = 0","O = **−2**"]},{"text":"**0 → −2: the number went down.** Each O gained 2 electrons, matching the 4 e⁻ in the equation.","marks":[{"t":"4 e⁻","note":"2 O × 2 e⁻ = 4 ✓"}],"calc":["2 O atoms × 2 e⁻ each = **4 e⁻** ✓"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Oxidation state of oxygen before and after","x":{"label":"Where the O is"},"y":{"label":"Oxidation state","min":-3,"max":1,"step":1},"categories":["O in O₂","O in H₂O"],"series":[{"name":"Oxidation state","values":[0,-2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["O in H₂O","Oxidation state"]]}},{"text":"**OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain** (of electrons). Oxygen gains electrons, so it is **reduced**.","marks":[{"t":"transfers electrons to oxygen","note":"gain e⁻ = reduction"}],"calc":["gain e⁻ → oxidation state **down** → **reduced**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** gaining electrons is never oxidation, adding H is not oxidation, and the direction is 0 → −2, not −2 → 0.","marks":[{"t":"O₂","note":"start: 0"},{"t":"2 H₂O","note":"end: −2"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the oxidation state on each side (element alone = 0, O in compounds = −2, H = +1). Number goes down = gained electrons = reduced; up = lost = oxidized."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Arginine has pKa values of 2.2 for its α-carboxyl group, 9.0 for its α-amino group, and 12.5 for its side chain. Which structure shows the predominant form of arginine at pH 7.4?",idStem:"Lysine and similar amino acids have side-chain amines that are protonated in water. How are they classified?",choiceFigs:["arg-uncharged","arg-sc-neutral","arg-net-plus1","arg-net-plus2"],choices:["Fully neutral form","Zwitterion with neutral side chain","Net charge +1","Net charge +2"],ans:2,exp:"Compare pH 7.4 with each pKa. 7.4 > 2.2 → carboxyl is **COO⁻**; 7.4 < 9.0 → amino is **NH₃⁺**; 7.4 < 12.5 → the guanidino side chain is **protonated, +**. Net charge −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1**.",src:"pe3-cp:33",how:{"ask":"For each ionizable group, is the pH above or below its pKa, and so which form, protonated or not, do you draw?","know":"• **pH below pKa → the group keeps its H⁺** (protonated). **pH above pKa → it loses H⁺.**\n• Carboxyl: protonated = COOH (0), deprotonated = COO⁻ (−1).\n• Amino: protonated = NH₃⁺ (+1), deprotonated = NH₂ (0).\n• Arginine's guanidino side chain, –NH–C(=NH)NH₂: protonated form carries + (pKa ≈ 12.5), so it is charged at any physiological pH.\n• On structure choices, count the charges drawn, then check each against its pKa.\n• A fully uncharged amino acid does not exist in significant amounts in water at any pH.","steps":[{"text":"The condition to test every group against: pH 7.4.","hl":"at pH 7.4"},{"text":"Compare 7.4 with each pKa: below the pKa keeps H⁺, above loses it.","hl":"pKa values of 2.2 for its α-carboxyl group, 9.0 for its α-amino group, and 12.5 for its side chain","calc":["α-COOH: 7.4 > 2.2 → **COO⁻**, −1","α-NH₂: 7.4 < 9.0 → **NH₃⁺**, +1","side chain: 7.4 < 12.5 → **protonated**, +1","net = −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"Find the side chain in each drawing: the guanidino group at the end of the chain, highlighted. It stays protonated until pH 12.5.","fig":"arginine-sc","strike":["Zwitterion with neutral side chain"]},{"text":"A drawing with COOH still protonated needs pH below 2.2; a drawing with no charges at all does not exist in water near neutral pH.","strike":["Net charge +2","Fully neutral form"]},{"text":"Answer: COO⁻, NH₃⁺ and the protonated side chain, net +1.","fig":"arg-net-plus1"},{"text":"Rule: on a protonation-state question, write pH vs pKa for each group, assign each charge, then find the drawing with exactly those charges."}],"trap":"The zwitterion with a neutral side chain looks like the \"standard\" amino acid drawing, but arginine's side chain pKa is 12.5, so at 7.4 it is still protonated and positive."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawings.** Corners are carbons (hidden H); N = nitrogen, O = oxygen. A + or − on an atom is its charge: NH₃⁺ holds an extra H⁺, COO⁻ has lost one.","fig":"t3-arg-read"},{"text":"**Three groups can gain or lose H⁺** (a hydrogen without its electron). Each has its own pKa, given in the question.","fig":"t3-arg-3groups"},{"text":"**pH** = how much H⁺ is around (low pH = lots). **pKa** = the pH where a group is half holding H⁺. pH below pKa: it keeps H⁺. pH above: it loses it."},{"text":"**Acid group:** 7.4 is above 2.2, so it loses its H⁺. COOH becomes COO⁻, charge −1.","fig":"t3-arg-coo"},{"text":"**Amino group:** 7.4 is below 9.0, so it holds an H⁺. NH₂ becomes NH₃⁺, charge +1.","fig":"t3-arg-nh3"},{"text":"**Side chain:** 7.4 is far below 12.5, so it holds an extra H⁺, charge +1. The + is shared over its three nitrogens.","fig":"t3-arg-scplus"},{"text":"**Add the charges:** −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1** overall at pH 7.4.","fig":"t3-arg-sum"},{"text":"**Trap:** a drawing with no charges. Near pH 7 in water the COOH has lost H⁺ and the NH₂ has gained one, so this form is essentially absent.","fig":"t3-arg-trap"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** for each group, pH below pKa keeps H⁺, above loses it; write each charge and add. In each drawing, check the acid end, amino end and side-chain end."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A carbonate buffer for an enzyme assay is adjusted to pH 10.0 at 25 °C, where Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴. What is [OH⁻] in the buffer, and how does it compare with [H⁺]?",idStem:"What is [OH⁻] in a solution at pH 10?",choices:["10⁻⁴ M, a million times greater than [H⁺]","10⁻⁴ M, a million times smaller than [H⁺]","10⁻¹⁰ M, a million times smaller than [H⁺]","10⁻¹⁰ M, the same as [H⁺]"],ans:0,exp:"**pH + pOH = 14** at 25 °C → pOH = 4 → [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁴ M. [H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁰ M, so [OH⁻]/[H⁺] = 10⁻⁴/10⁻¹⁰ = 10⁶: a million times greater, as expected for a basic solution.",src:"pe3-cp:34",how:{"ask":"From pH, how do you get to pOH and then to the hydroxide concentration? Then compare it with the hydrogen ion concentration.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]**, **pOH = −log[OH⁻]**.\n• At 25 °C, [H⁺][OH⁻] = Kw = 10⁻¹⁴, so **pH + pOH = 14**.\n• Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in concentration.","steps":[{"text":"Get [H⁺] straight from the pH.","hl":"pH 10.0","calc":["[H⁺] = 10^(−pH) = **10⁻¹⁰ M**"]},{"text":"Use Kw to get [OH⁻].","hl":"Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴","calc":["[OH⁻] = Kw / [H⁺]","= 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / **10⁻¹⁰**","= **10⁻⁴ M** (pOH = 4)"],"strike":["10⁻¹⁰ M, a million times smaller than [H⁺]","10⁻¹⁰ M, the same as [H⁺]"]},{"text":"Compare the two: they differ by 6 powers of ten, and the basic side has more OH⁻.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"log of [H⁺] and [OH⁻] across the pH scale (25 °C)","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"y":{"label":"log₁₀ concentration (M)","min":-14,"max":0,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"[H⁺]","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[7,-7],[10,-10],[14,-14]]},{"name":"[OH⁻]","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,-14],[7,-7],[10,-4],[14,0]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"[H⁺]","x":10},{"series":"[OH⁻]","x":10}],"spans":[{"x":10,"from":-10,"to":-4,"text":"6 powers of 10 → 10⁶"}],"labels":[{"series":"[H⁺]","x":10,"text":"[H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁰","at":"below"},{"series":"[OH⁻]","x":10,"text":"[OH⁻] = 10⁻⁴","at":"above"}]},"calc":["[OH⁻]/[H⁺] = 10⁻⁴ / 10⁻¹⁰ = **10⁶**"],"strike":["10⁻⁴ M, a million times smaller than [H⁺]"]},{"text":"Answer: 10⁻⁴ M, a million times greater than [H⁺] (pH > 7, so the solution is basic)."}],"trap":"10⁻¹⁰ M is [H⁺], read straight off the pH. The \"10⁻⁴ M, a million times smaller\" choice gets the value but flips the comparison: 10⁻⁴ is larger than 10⁻¹⁰."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you** and what it wants.","marks":[{"t":"pH 10.0","note":"how acidic/basic"},{"t":"Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴","note":"[H⁺] × [OH⁻] always"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**[H⁺] = concentration of H⁺**, the acid particle. **pH** is its power of ten with the minus sign dropped.","marks":[{"t":"pH 10.0","note":"[H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁰ M"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺: the acid particle"}],"calc":["[H⁺] = 10^(−pH)","[H⁺] = 10^(−10) = **10⁻¹⁰ M**"]},{"text":"**Kw:** water always splits a little into H⁺ and OH⁻. At 25 °C their concentrations multiply to 10⁻¹⁴.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C"},{"t":"Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴","note":"[H⁺] × [OH⁻] = this"}],"calc":["[H⁺] × [OH⁻] = **10⁻¹⁴**"]},{"text":"**Solve for [OH⁻]:** divide Kw by [H⁺]. Dividing powers of ten = subtract the exponents.","marks":[{"t":"Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴","note":"÷ by [H⁺]"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"= 10⁻⁴ M"}],"calc":["[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻¹⁰","exponent: −14 − (−10) = **−4**","[OH⁻] = **10⁻⁴ M**  (shortcut: pOH = 14 − 10 = 4)"]},{"text":"**See it on the pH scale:** at pH 10 the two lines are 6 powers of ten apart, with OH⁻ on top.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"log of [H⁺] and [OH⁻] across the pH scale (25 °C)","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"y":{"label":"log₁₀ concentration (M)","min":-14,"max":0,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"[H⁺]","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[7,-7],[10,-10],[14,-14]]},{"name":"[OH⁻]","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,-14],[7,-7],[10,-4],[14,0]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"[H⁺]","x":10},{"series":"[OH⁻]","x":10}],"spans":[{"x":10,"from":-10,"to":-4,"text":"6 powers of 10"}],"labels":[{"series":"[H⁺]","x":10,"text":"[H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁰","at":"below"},{"series":"[OH⁻]","x":10,"text":"[OH⁻] = 10⁻⁴","at":"left"},{"series":"[H⁺]","x":7,"text":"pH 7: equal (neutral)","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Compare:** 10⁻⁴ is bigger than 10⁻¹⁰ (less negative power). The ratio is 10⁶, a million.","marks":[{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"10⁻⁴: the bigger one"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"10⁻¹⁰: the smaller one"}],"calc":["[OH⁻] ÷ [H⁺] = 10⁻⁴ ÷ 10⁻¹⁰","= 10^(−4 + 10) = **10⁶**","10⁶ = **1,000,000** (a million times)"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 10⁻¹⁰ M is [H⁺], not [OH⁻]. And pH above 7 means basic, so OH⁻ must be the larger one.","marks":[{"t":"pH 10.0","note":"above 7 = basic"},{"t":"carbonate buffer","note":"just sets the pH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at 25 °C, pH + pOH = 14 and [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴. Each pH unit is ×10, so a gap of n units = 10ⁿ times."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Horseradish peroxidase (HRP) uses H₂O₂ to remove one electron and one proton from its substrate, taking the proton from the substrate's most acidic X–H bond. The substrate acetaminophen is shown. Which structure is the product of its oxidation by HRP?",idStem:"An enzyme oxidizes phenol side chains into radicals. Which amino acid side chain could it act on?",fig:"acetaminophen",choices:["Phenoxyl radical","Amidyl radical","Ring carbon radical","Methyl radical"],choiceFigs:["apap-o-radical","apap-n-radical","apap-ring-radical","apap-methyl-radical"],ans:0,exp:"The most acidic H in acetaminophen is the **phenol O–H (pKa ≈ 10)**; the amide N–H is far weaker (pKa ≈ 15–17) and C–H bonds are not acidic. Removing H⁺ and one e⁻ from O–H leaves the unpaired electron **on that oxygen: a phenoxyl radical** (the dot on O).",src:"pe3-cp:36",how:{"ask":"Which X–H bond in acetaminophen is the most acidic, and where is the unpaired electron left after a proton and one electron are taken from it?","know":"• **H⁺ + e⁻ together = one hydrogen atom.** Taking both from X–H leaves X• — a radical: one unpaired electron, drawn as a dot on that atom.\n• Acidity of X–H: carboxylic acid ≈ 4–5 > **phenol ≈ 10** > alcohol ≈ 16 ≈ amide N–H ≈ 15–17 > C–H > 40.\n• Phenoxyl radicals are stabilized by resonance with the ring; that is why peroxidases attack phenols, such as tyrosine side chains.\n• On structure choices, find the dot first, then ask whether that atom lost an H.","steps":[{"text":"The question tells you exactly what HRP removes: one electron and one proton, from the most acidic X–H.","hl":"remove one electron and one proton"},{"text":"List every X–H in acetaminophen and how acidic it is.","fig":"acetaminophen","calc":["phenol O–H: pKa ≈ **10**","amide N–H: pKa ≈ **15–17**","ring and methyl C–H: pKa > **40**"]},{"text":"The phenol O–H is by far the most acidic, so H⁺ + e⁻ leave from there: the dot goes on the oxygen.","fig":"apap-o-radical"},{"text":"The other structures put the dot where no acidic H was removed: on the amide N, a ring carbon, or the methyl carbon.","strike":["Amidyl radical","Ring carbon radical","Methyl radical"]},{"text":"Answer: the phenoxyl radical, with the dot on the phenol oxygen."}],"trap":"The amidyl radical, with the dot on N, is tempting because the amide N–H also has an H, but it is about a million times less acidic than the phenol O–H. HRP takes the most acidic one."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Corners are carbons (hidden H); O, N are oxygen, nitrogen. In the answer drawings a **dot** on an atom = one unpaired electron: a **radical**.","fig":"t3-apap-read"},{"text":"**H⁺ + one electron = a whole H atom.** Remove it from an X–H bond and X keeps one lone electron: the dot lands on the atom that lost the H.","fig":"t3-apap-xh"},{"text":"**Which H leaves?** The most acidic one: the one that gives up H⁺ most easily. Lower **pKa** = more acidic; each 1 unit is a factor of 10."},{"text":"**Phenol O–H** (OH on a ring): pKa about 10. After H⁺ leaves, the − charge on O spreads into the ring, making it easier to leave.","fig":"t3-apap-oh2"},{"text":"**Amide N–H** (N next to C=O): pKa about 15–17, at least 100,000 times weaker than the phenol.","fig":"t3-apap-nh2"},{"text":"**C–H bonds:** pKa above 40. Carbon holds its H tightly; these never give up H⁺ here.","fig":"t3-apap-ch2"},{"text":"**Compare:** 10 is far lower than 15–17 or 40. So HRP takes the H from the phenol O–H, and that oxygen is left with the dot.","fig":"t3-apap-rank"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** removing H⁺ + one electron puts the dot on the atom whose H was most acidic (COOH > phenol > amide N–H > C–H). In each drawing, find the dot and ask: did that atom lose the most acidic H?"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"In a calcium-binding loop, Ca²⁺ is held by several contacts from nearby residues. An aspartate residue from the loop is shown within the peptide backbone, with four atoms labeled I through IV. Which labeled atom most likely contacts Ca²⁺ directly?",idStem:"Which atom in a protein most likely binds (coordinates) a Ca²⁺ ion?",fig:"asp-ca-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:0,exp:"Ca²⁺ is a hard, positive ion; it binds **electron-rich oxygen atoms with lone pairs**. Site I is a **carboxylate O⁻**, negatively charged with lone pairs. The amide N's lone pair is tied up in resonance and it carries a δ+ H; the α-carbon has no lone pair; and the carboxylate carbon is δ+.",src:"pe3-cp:37",how:{"ask":"Ca²⁺ is a positive ion. Which labeled atom carries negative charge and lone pairs it can point toward the ion?","know":"• Metal cations bind **Lewis bases**: atoms with lone pairs, best if negatively charged.\n• Ca²⁺ especially prefers **oxygen**: carboxylate O⁻ from Asp and Glu, backbone C=O oxygens, and water.\n• An **amide N** (–C(=O)–NH–) donates its lone pair into the C=O by resonance, so it is a poor ligand.\n• Carbons in C=O or COO⁻ are δ+, electron-poor, and sp³ carbons have no lone pairs; neither binds cations.\n• On a labeled-site figure: identify the element at each label first, then its charge.","steps":[{"text":"The partner is Ca²⁺, a positive ion: look for a negative atom with lone pairs.","hl":"contacts Ca²⁺ directly"},{"text":"Name the element and charge at each label.","fig":"asp-ca-sites","calc":["I: carboxylate **O⁻**, lone pairs, −","II: amide **N–H**, lone pair in resonance","III: α-**C**, sp³, no lone pair","IV: carboxylate **C**, δ+"]},{"text":"Carbon atoms have no lone pair to offer, and IV is electron-poor.","strike":["III","IV"]},{"text":"The amide N shares its lone pair with the C=O and carries a δ+ H, which would point toward the cation.","strike":["II"]},{"text":"The carboxylate oxygens, highlighted, carry the negative charge and lone pairs. Answer: I.","fig":"asp-ca-sites-o"},{"text":"Rule: cations bind O⁻ and C=O oxygens; never a carbon, and rarely an amide N."}],"trap":"IV, the carboxylate carbon, sits at the center of the charged group, so it looks like the binding point. But that carbon is δ+; the negative charge and lone pairs are on the oxygens around it."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Corners are carbons (hidden H); N = nitrogen, O = oxygen, O⁻ = oxygen with a − charge. The zigzag is the backbone; the branch is the side chain.","fig":"t3-asp-read"},{"text":"**Ca²⁺** is calcium missing 2 electrons: charge +2. Opposite charges attract, so it sits against an atom that is negative or has a **lone pair** (2 unbonded electrons).","fig":"asp-ca-sites"},{"text":"**Site I:** an oxygen carrying the − charge, plus lone pairs. The − is shared by both oxygens of this group.","fig":"t3-asp-1"},{"text":"**Site IV:** the carbon between two O atoms. O pulls its electrons away, so it is slightly positive (written **δ+**). No lone pair.","fig":"t3-asp-4"},{"text":"**Site III:** a backbone carbon with four single bonds. All its electrons are in bonds, so it has nothing to offer Ca²⁺.","fig":"t3-asp-3"},{"text":"**Site II:** an amide N (next to a C=O). Its lone pair is shared into the C=O, so it isn't free, and its H is slightly positive.","fig":"t3-asp-2"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** a + metal ion binds atoms that are negative or have a free lone pair, especially O. For each label ask: which element, is it −, is its lone pair free?","fig":"asp-ca-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"In myoglobin, the heme iron is bound from below by an amino acid side chain whose aromatic ring contains five atoms, two of them nitrogen: one bearing an H and one whose lone pair binds Fe²⁺. Which structure is that ring?",idStem:"Heme's porphyrin ring is built from four units of which ring?",choiceFigs:["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine","furan"],choices:["Pyrrole","Imidazole","Pyridine","Furan"],ans:1,exp:"Count ring atoms and heteroatoms in each drawing. Only **imidazole** is five-membered with **two N**: one N–H and one N with a free lone pair, which binds Fe²⁺. It is the side chain of histidine, the proximal ligand of heme.",src:"pe3-cp:38",how:{"ask":"For each drawing, how many atoms are in the ring, and how many of them are nitrogen?","know":"Aromatic rings to recognize on sight:\n• **Pyrrole:** 5-membered, 1 N (N–H). Four pyrroles make the porphyrin of heme.\n• **Imidazole:** 5-membered, 2 N: one N–H and one =N– with a free lone pair. Side chain of histidine.\n• **Furan:** 5-membered, 1 O.\n• **Pyridine:** 6-membered, 1 N.\n• Read a ring by counting corners, then letters: every unlabeled corner is carbon.\n• A ring N that binds a metal needs a lone pair that is not part of the aromatic π system, like the =N– of imidazole or pyridine.","steps":[{"text":"Two features to match: a five-atom ring, with two nitrogens.","hl":"contains five atoms, two of them nitrogen"},{"text":"Count corners and letters in each drawing.","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine","furan"],"calc":["pyrrole: **5** atoms, **1** N","imidazole: **5** atoms, **2** N","pyridine: **6** atoms, **1** N","furan: **5** atoms, **0** N, 1 O"]},{"text":"Pyridine has six atoms; furan has oxygen and no nitrogen.","strike":["Pyridine","Furan"]},{"text":"Pyrrole has the right size but only one N, and it is the N–H.","strike":["Pyrrole"]},{"text":"Imidazole is histidine's side chain: its N–H and its =N–, whose lone pair binds Fe²⁺. Answer: imidazole.","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"Rule: count ring atoms, then heteroatoms: 5 + one N = pyrrole; 5 + two N = imidazole; 6 + one N = pyridine; 5 + O = furan."}],"trap":"Pyrrole is tempting because heme's own ring system is made of pyrroles, but each pyrrole has only one N, and its lone pair is part of the aromatic ring. The ligand below the iron has two N."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading ring drawings.** Each unlabeled corner is a carbon; its H is hidden. Letters are other atoms. **Ring size = corners + letters.**","fig":"t3-pyrrole-read"},{"text":"**Aromatic** = a flat ring with alternating double bonds whose electrons are shared all around the loop, like benzene.","fig":"t3-benzene-read"},{"text":"**A lone pair** = 2 electrons not in a bond. Fe²⁺ (iron with charge +2) is held by an N whose lone pair points out of the ring, free.","fig":"t3-pyridine-lp"},{"text":"**A ring N–H is different:** its lone pair is part of the ring's shared electrons, so it is not free to bind iron.","fig":"t3-pyrrole-lp"},{"text":"**Count:** 5 ring atoms, 1 N. Right size, but only one nitrogen.","fig":"t3-pyrrole-count"},{"text":"**Count:** 6 ring atoms, 1 N. Wrong size.","fig":"t3-pyridine-count"},{"text":"**Count:** 5 ring atoms, but the letter is O. No nitrogen at all.","fig":"t3-furan-count"},{"text":"**Histidine** is the amino acid that holds heme iron. Its side-chain ring has 5 atoms and 2 N: one N–H, one N with a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** count ring atoms, then letters: 5 + one N = pyrrole; 5 + two N = imidazole; 6 + one N = pyridine; 5 + O = furan. Count each drawing that way."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"Consider the electrical conductivity data at 37 °C: muscle: 5.0 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹; fat: 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹. What is the resistivity of muscle?",idStem:"A material's conductivity is 5.0 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹. What is its resistivity?",choices:["200 Ω·cm","20 Ω·cm","2500 Ω·cm","5.0 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm"],ans:0,exp:"**Resistivity ρ = 1/σ (conductivity).** ρ = 1 ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ = 200 Ω·cm. The inverted unit, (Ω·cm)⁻¹, signals that you must take the reciprocal.",src:"pe3-cp:39",how:{"ask":"How are resistivity and conductivity related? Use the units to check.","know":"• **Resistivity (ρ)**: how strongly a material opposes current, in Ω·m or Ω·cm.\n• **Conductivity (σ)**: how easily it carries current, in (Ω·cm)⁻¹ or S/cm.\n• **ρ = 1/σ.** Resistance of a piece: R = ρL/A.","steps":[{"text":"The data are conductivities σ. Resistivity is the reciprocal: the unit (Ω·cm)⁻¹ flips to Ω·cm.","hl":"muscle: 5.0 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹"},{"text":"Take the reciprocal of muscle's σ.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ","ρ = 1 ÷ (**5.0 × 10⁻³**)","ρ = 0.20 × 10³ = **200 Ω·cm**"],"strike":["5.0 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm","20 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"The fat value belongs to the other row: its reciprocal is the 2500 Ω·cm distractor.","hl":"fat: 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","calc":["ρ(fat) = 1 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = **2500 Ω·cm**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Resistivity ρ = 1/σ of the two tissues","x":{"label":"Tissue"},"y":{"label":"Resistivity (Ω·cm)","min":0,"max":3000,"step":500},"categories":["Muscle","Fat"],"series":[{"name":"ρ","values":[200,2500]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Muscle","ρ"]]},"strike":["2500 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 Ω·cm. Muscle, rich in electrolyte, conducts far better than fat."}],"trap":"2500 Ω·cm is the reciprocal of the fat row, not muscle. 5.0 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm copies the number without inverting it; the units don't match."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers.** Each tissue has one value, and the question asks about muscle only.","marks":[{"t":"muscle: 5.0 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"muscle's conductivity"},{"t":"fat: 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"fat's conductivity"},{"t":"resistivity of muscle","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**Conductivity (σ)** = how easily current flows. **Resistivity (ρ)** = how hard it is. They are opposites.","marks":[{"t":"electrical conductivity","note":"σ: ease of flow"},{"t":"resistivity","note":"ρ: opposition to flow"}],"calc":["ρ = 1 / σ   (flip it over)"]},{"text":"**The unit gives it away.** ⁻¹ means \"one over\". Flip it and you get Ω·cm, the unit of resistivity.","marks":[{"t":"(Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"⁻¹ = one over (Ω·cm)"}],"calc":["(Ω·cm)⁻¹ = 1/(Ω·cm)","flip → **Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"**Flip muscle's value.** Split it into the number part and the power-of-ten part.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10⁻³","note":"flip this"}],"calc":["ρ = 1 ÷ (5.0 × 10⁻³)","1 ÷ 5.0 = **0.20**","1 ÷ 10⁻³ = **10³**","ρ = 0.20 × 10³ = **200 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"**Sanity check in plain decimals:** 5.0 × 10⁻³ is 0.005, and 1 ÷ 0.005 = 200.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10⁻³","note":"= 0.005"}],"calc":["1 ÷ 0.005 = **200** ✓"]},{"text":"**Fat is a different row.** Flipping fat's value gives 2500 Ω·cm: a trap answer for the wrong tissue.","marks":[{"t":"fat: 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"not needed: other tissue"}],"calc":["ρ(fat) = 1 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 2500 Ω·cm ✗"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Resistivity ρ = 1/σ of the two tissues","x":{"label":"Tissue"},"y":{"label":"Resistivity (Ω·cm)","min":0,"max":3000,"step":500},"categories":["Muscle","Fat"],"series":[{"name":"ρ","values":[200,2500]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Muscle","ρ"]]}},{"text":"**Other traps:** copying σ with the unit flipped is not a reciprocal, and 20 is a decimal slip.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 × 10⁻³","note":"not the answer itself"}],"calc":["5.0 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm ✗ (never flipped)","20 Ω·cm ✗ (decimal slip)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** resistivity ρ = 1/conductivity σ. A unit with ⁻¹, like (Ω·cm)⁻¹, tells you to take the reciprocal."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"In the pathway shown, enzyme 1 converts the first structure into the second, and enzyme 2, which consumes O₂, converts the second structure into the third. To which enzyme classes do enzymes 1 and 2 belong?",idStem:"Cytosine is first methylated, then its new CH₃ is oxidized to CH₂OH. Which enzyme classes are needed?",fig:["uracil","thymine","hydroxymethyluracil"],choices:["Transferase; oxidoreductase","Ligase; oxidoreductase","Oxidoreductase; transferase","Transferase; hydrolase"],ans:0,exp:"Step 1 puts a **CH₃ on C5**: a one-carbon group carried in from a donor, so enzyme 1 is a **transferase** (a methyltransferase). Step 2 turns that **CH₃ into CH₂OH** using O₂: the carbon gains a bond to O, an **oxidation**, so enzyme 2 is an **oxidoreductase**.",src:"pe3-cp:40",how:{"ask":"Compare each pair of drawings: what changed at C5, and does the change add a group, add water, or change how many bonds a carbon has to oxygen?","know":"• Spot the change between two drawings, then name it:\n• **Transferase:** a group, such as CH₃, phosphate or an amino group, is moved from a donor molecule onto the substrate.\n• **Oxidoreductase:** a carbon gains bonds to O or loses bonds to H (oxidation), or the reverse; often uses O₂, NAD⁺ or FAD. CH₃ → CH₂OH → CHO → COOH is a ladder of oxidations.\n• **Hydrolase:** water breaks a bond, splitting one molecule into two.\n• **Ligase:** joins two molecules using ATP.\n• Formula check: adding CH₂ = transfer of a methyl; adding one O with no change in H = oxidation; adding H₂O = hydration or hydrolysis.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the first two drawings. The only change is at C5: H becomes CH₃.","fig":["uracil","thymine-methyl-hl"],"calc":["C₄H₄N₂O₂ → C₅H₆N₂O₂","change: **+CH₂**, an H swapped for CH₃","a methyl moved from a donor → **transferase**"]},{"text":"Nothing was oxidized or joined with ATP in step 1, so enzyme 1 is not an oxidoreductase or a ligase.","strike":["Oxidoreductase; transferase","Ligase; oxidoreductase"]},{"text":"Compare the second and third drawings: the CH₃ becomes CH₂OH, and O₂ is used.","hl":"which consumes O₂","fig":["thymine-methyl-hl","hydroxymethyluracil-hl"],"calc":["C₅H₆N₂O₂ → C₅H₆N₂O₃","change: **+O**, same H count","that carbon: oxidation state −3 → **−1**, oxidized"]},{"text":"A hydrolase adds a full H₂O and splits the molecule. Here one molecule stays one molecule and gains only an O.","strike":["Transferase; hydrolase"]},{"text":"Answer: transferase; oxidoreductase. Rule: new CH₃ from a donor = transferase; C–H → C–OH with O₂ = oxidoreductase."}],"trap":"\"Transferase; hydrolase\" is tempting because step 2 adds an OH, but hydrolysis adds a whole H₂O and splits a bond. Here only an O is inserted into a C–H bond, an oxidation that consumes O₂."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawings.** Unlabeled corners are carbons (hidden H). N = nitrogen, O = oxygen. All three molecules share this same six-atom ring.","fig":"t3-ura-read"},{"text":"**Find where the change happens:** carbon 5 (C5). In the first molecule it holds only a hidden H.","fig":"t3-ura-c5"},{"text":"**Step 1:** the H on C5 becomes **CH₃** (a methyl: one carbon with three H). Nothing else changes.","fig":["t3-ura-c5","t3-thy-ch3"]},{"text":"**A carbon can't appear from nowhere:** a donor molecule handed over the CH₃. An enzyme that moves a group between molecules is a **transferase**.","fig":"t3-thy-donor"},{"text":"**Step 2:** the CH₃ becomes **CH₂OH**. One C–H bond was replaced by a C–O bond, using O₂.","fig":["t3-thy-ch","t3-hmu-co"]},{"text":"**A carbon that gains a bond to O (or loses one to H) is oxidized.** Enzymes that oxidize or reduce, often with O₂, are **oxidoreductases**.","fig":"t3-hmu-ox"},{"text":"**Ligase** joins two molecules using ATP (the cell's energy carrier). **Hydrolase** uses water to split one molecule in two. Neither happens here."},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** a group brought in from a donor = transferase; C–H turned into C–O (often with O₂) = oxidoreductase. Check each answer pair: first name for step 1, second for step 2."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"A DNA hairpin unfolds in solution: native ⇌ unfolded. At 310 K, fluorescence measurements show that 25% of the molecules are unfolded at equilibrium. If [N] and [U] are the equilibrium concentrations of the native and unfolded forms, which expression gives ΔG°′ for unfolding, and what is its sign?",idStem:"What is ΔG°′ for a DNA structure unfolding (native ⇌ unfolded)?",choices:["−RT ln [N]/[U]; negative","−RT ln [U]/[N]; positive","−RT ln [U]/[N]; negative","+RT ln [U]/[N]; positive"],ans:1,exp:"**ΔG° = −RT ln K**, with K = products/reactants = **[U]/[N]** for unfolding. Here K = 25/75 = 1/3 < 1, so ln K < 0 and **ΔG°′ is positive**: unfolding is unfavorable, as expected when most molecules stay folded.",src:"pe3-cp:41",how:{"ask":"Write K for the unfolding direction first, then use the measured fractions to decide whether ln K is positive or negative.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K.** R = gas constant, T in kelvin, K = equilibrium constant.\n• **K = [products]/[reactants]** as written. For native ⇌ unfolded, K = [U]/[N].\n• K > 1 → ΔG° < 0 (products favored). K < 1 → ΔG° > 0 (reactants favored).","steps":[{"text":"Write K for the process as written: products over reactants.","hl":"native ⇌ unfolded","calc":["N ⇌ U","K = **[U]/[N]**","ΔG°′ = −RT ln K = −RT ln [U]/[N]"],"strike":["−RT ln [N]/[U]; negative"]},{"text":"Plug in the measured fractions.","hl":"25% of the molecules are unfolded","calc":["K = 25/75 = **0.33**","ln 0.33 = **−1.10**"]},{"text":"Compute ΔG°′: −RT × (negative number) is positive.","calc":["RT = 8.314 × 310 = **2577 J/mol**","ΔG°′ = −(2577)(−1.10)","ΔG°′ ≈ **+2.8 kJ/mol**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ΔG°′ for unfolding vs percent unfolded at 310 K","x":{"label":"Unfolded at equilibrium (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":25},"y":{"label":"ΔG°′ unfolding (kJ/mol)","min":-6,"max":6,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N])","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[10,5.66],[25,2.83],[50,0],[75,-2.83],[90,-5.66]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N])","x":25}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":50,"text":"K < 1: ΔG°′ > 0"}],"spans":[{"x":25,"from":0,"to":2.83,"text":"+2.8 kJ/mol"}]},"strike":["−RT ln [U]/[N]; negative","+RT ln [U]/[N]; positive"]},{"text":"Answer: −RT ln [U]/[N]; positive. Less than half unfolded means K < 1 and ΔG°′ > 0: the native form is favored."}],"trap":"\"−RT ln [N]/[U]; negative\" builds K for folding, the reverse direction. Its value is consistent with itself but describes the wrong reaction. \"+RT ln [U]/[N]\" drops the minus sign, which would make the result negative, not positive."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** A folded DNA loop can open up, and we know how much is open at equilibrium.","marks":[{"t":"native ⇌ unfolded","note":"the reaction"},{"t":"310 K","note":"temperature"},{"t":"25% of the molecules are unfolded","note":"the measurement"}]},{"text":"**⇌ means both directions at once.** At equilibrium, folding and unfolding run at equal rates, so the amounts stop changing.","marks":[{"t":"native","note":"start: folded (N)"},{"t":"⇌","note":"goes both ways"},{"t":"unfolded","note":"product: open (U)"}]},{"text":"**K = products ÷ reactants** at equilibrium. For unfolding, the product is U, so U goes on top.","marks":[{"t":"[N] and [U]","note":"N = folded, U = unfolded"},{"t":"for unfolding","note":"U on top"}],"calc":["N ⇌ U","K = **[U] / [N]**"]},{"text":"**Put in the measurement.** 25% unfolded means the other 75% is still folded.","marks":[{"t":"25% of the molecules are unfolded","note":"U = 25%, so N = 75%"}],"calc":["K = 25 / 75 = **1/3**","K < 1 → more folded than unfolded"]},{"text":"**ΔG°′ = the energy push of the reaction.** Negative = goes forward on its own; positive = uphill. It links to K by ΔG°′ = −RT ln K.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG°′","note":"energy change"},{"t":"310 K","note":"T in RT"}],"calc":["ΔG°′ = −RT ln K = **−RT ln [U]/[N]**","R = 8.314 J/mol·K, T = 310 K"]},{"text":"**ln of a number below 1 is negative.** Minus times negative = positive.","marks":[{"t":"25% of the molecules are unfolded","note":"K = 1/3 → ln K < 0"}],"calc":["ln(1/3) ≈ **−1.10**","RT = 8.314 × 310 ≈ 2577 J/mol","ΔG°′ = −(2577)(−1.10) ≈ **+2.8 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**See it:** below 50% unfolded, K < 1 and ΔG°′ sits above zero. At 25% it is about +2.8 kJ/mol.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ΔG°′ for unfolding vs percent unfolded at 310 K","x":{"label":"Unfolded at equilibrium (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":25},"y":{"label":"ΔG°′ unfolding (kJ/mol)","min":-6,"max":6,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N])","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[10,5.66],[25,2.83],[50,0],[75,-2.83],[90,-5.66]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N])","x":25}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":50,"text":"K < 1: ΔG°′ > 0"}],"spans":[{"x":25,"from":0,"to":2.83,"text":"+2.8 kJ/mol"}],"labels":[{"series":"ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N])","x":50,"text":"50%: K = 1, ΔG°′ = 0","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** [N]/[U] is the reverse reaction (folding). +RT flips the formula's sign. And a negative sign would mean most is unfolded.","marks":[{"t":"for unfolding","note":"[U] on top, not [N]"},{"t":"25% of the molecules are unfolded","note":"minority → ΔG°′ > 0"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K, with K = products/reactants as written. K < 1 → ΔG° positive (reactants favored); K > 1 → negative."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Thermal unfolding of two proteins, P and Q, is followed by circular dichroism, and the fraction folded is plotted against temperature. Which conclusion is best supported by the data?",idStem:"On an unfolding curve (fraction folded vs. pH), what shows HIGH cooperativity?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Thermal unfolding followed by circular dichroism","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":40,"max":80,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Protein P","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,1],[50,1],[55,0.97],[57,0.88],[58,0.79],[60,0.5],[62,0.21],[63,0.12],[65,0.03],[70,0],[80,0]]},{"name":"Protein Q","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[40,0.97],[45,0.92],[50,0.84],[55,0.7],[60,0.5],[65,0.3],[70,0.16],[75,0.08],[80,0.03]]}]},choices:["Q unfolds more cooperatively, since its transition spans a wider range","Both unfold equally cooperatively, since they share a midpoint of 60 °C","P unfolds more cooperatively, since its transition spans a narrower range","Q unfolds more cooperatively, since it begins to unfold at a lower temperature"],ans:2,exp:"Both curves cross 0.5 at **60 °C**, so they have the same midpoint, Tm. Cooperativity is read from the **steepness**: P goes from about 0.9 to 0.1 folded in ≈6 °C, Q in ≈30 °C. The sharp, all-or-none transition of P is the **more cooperative** one.",src:"pe3-cp:42",how:{"ask":"Both curves share a midpoint, so look at their shapes: which one falls from mostly folded to mostly unfolded over the smaller temperature change?","know":"• **Melting temperature, Tm:** the temperature where the fraction folded = 0.5. It measures stability.\n• **Cooperativity:** how all-or-none the transition is. In a cooperative transition, unfolding of one part promotes unfolding of the rest, so the curve is **steep**, a narrow transition.\n• A broad, gradual curve means parts unfold independently: low cooperativity.\n• Midpoint and steepness are separate readings: same Tm does not mean same cooperativity.","steps":[{"text":"Read the midpoint of each curve, the temperature where fraction folded = 0.5.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein P","x":60},{"series":"Protein Q","x":60}],"yLine":0.5,"labels":[{"series":"Protein P","x":60,"text":"Tm = 60 °C (both)","at":"right"}]},"calc":["Tm(P) = **60 °C**","Tm(Q) = **60 °C**","same midpoint → same stability, not same cooperativity"]},{"text":"Sharing a midpoint says nothing about how sharp each transition is.","strike":["Both unfold equally cooperatively, since they share a midpoint of 60 °C"],"graph":{"focus":["Protein P","Protein Q"],"labels":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":60,"text":"same midpoint","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Measure the width of P's transition, from about 0.9 to 0.1 folded.","graph":{"focus":["Protein P"],"read":[{"series":"Protein P","x":57},{"series":"Protein P","x":63}],"shade":[{"x1":57,"x2":63,"text":"P: ≈ 6 °C"}]},"calc":["P: 0.88 at 57 °C → 0.12 at 63 °C","width ≈ **6 °C**"]},{"text":"Measure Q's width the same way.","graph":{"focus":["Protein Q"],"read":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":45},{"series":"Protein Q","x":75}],"shade":[{"x1":45,"x2":75,"text":"Q: ≈ 30 °C"}]},"calc":["Q: 0.92 at 45 °C → 0.08 at 75 °C","width ≈ **30 °C**"]},{"text":"A wide, gradual transition, or one that starts early, is the less cooperative one.","strike":["Q unfolds more cooperatively, since its transition spans a wider range","Q unfolds more cooperatively, since it begins to unfold at a lower temperature"],"graph":{"focus":["Protein P","Protein Q"],"shade":[{"x1":45,"x2":75,"text":"wide = gradual"}],"labels":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":45,"text":"Q already unfolding","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Answer: P unfolds more cooperatively. Rule: midpoint = stability; steepness = cooperativity."}],"trap":"\"Both equally cooperative, since they share a midpoint\" confuses Tm with steepness. The midpoint tells you where the transition is centered, not how sharp it is."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = more of the protein still folded.** 1 means all folded; 0 means all unfolded.","marks":[{"t":"fraction folded","note":"y axis: 1 = all folded"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = hotter.** Heat shakes proteins apart, so they unfold.","marks":[{"t":"against temperature","note":"x axis: hotter →"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two curves, one per protein.** Each starts near 1 (folded) and falls to 0 (unfolded) as it is heated.","marks":[{"t":"two proteins, P and Q","note":"one curve each"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Protein P","x":57,"text":"P","at":"left"},{"series":"Protein Q","x":70,"text":"Q","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Midpoint (Tm)** = where half is folded (0.5). It measures stability. Both curves cross 0.5 at 60 °C.","calc":["Tm(P) = **60 °C**","Tm(Q) = **60 °C**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein P","x":60},{"series":"Protein Q","x":60}],"yLine":0.5,"labels":[{"series":"Protein P","x":60,"text":"Tm = 60 °C (both)","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Cooperative = all-or-none:** once one part unfolds, the rest follows fast. On the graph that is a **steep, narrow** drop.","marks":[{"t":"Thermal unfolding","note":"heat-driven unfolding"}],"graph":{"focus":["Protein P"],"shade":[{"x1":57,"x2":63,"text":"steep = cooperative"}]}},{"text":"**Measure P's drop:** from about 0.9 to 0.1 folded.","calc":["P: 0.88 at 57 °C → 0.12 at 63 °C","width ≈ **6 °C**"],"graph":{"focus":["Protein P"],"read":[{"series":"Protein P","x":57},{"series":"Protein P","x":63}],"shade":[{"x1":57,"x2":63,"text":"P: ≈ 6 °C"}]}},{"text":"**Measure Q's drop the same way.**","calc":["Q: 0.92 at 45 °C → 0.08 at 75 °C","width ≈ **30 °C**","6 °C (P) is narrower → **P more cooperative**"],"graph":{"focus":["Protein Q"],"read":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":45},{"series":"Protein Q","x":75}],"shade":[{"x1":45,"x2":75,"text":"Q: ≈ 30 °C"}]}},{"text":"**Same midpoint ≠ same cooperativity.** Midpoint says where; width says how sharp.","graph":{"focus":["Protein P","Protein Q"],"labels":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":60,"text":"same midpoint","at":"left"},{"series":"Protein P","x":63,"text":"different widths","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** Q starts unfolding earlier, but that only shows its drop is spread out (gradual), not more cooperative.","graph":{"focus":["Protein Q"],"labels":[{"series":"Protein Q","x":45,"text":"Q already unfolding","at":"below"}],"shade":[{"x1":45,"x2":75,"text":"wide = gradual"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an unfolding curve, the midpoint (Tm, 0.5) = stability; the steepness (narrow 0.9 → 0.1 width) = cooperativity. Read them separately.","graph":{"focus":["Protein P","Protein Q"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biology",aamc:"5D",q:"A single-stranded DNA fragment has the base composition: A 31%, T 24%, C 45%, G 0%. A complementary strand is then synthesized. Which nucleoside will be absent from the complementary strand, and what percentage of that strand will be deoxyguanosine?",idStem:"A DNA strand contains no guanine. Which nucleoside will its complementary strand lack?",choices:["Deoxyguanosine; 0%","Deoxycytidine; 45%","Deoxycytidine; 31%","Deoxythymidine; 24%"],ans:1,exp:"**Complementary pairing: A↔T and G↔C.** Each C in the template gives a G in the complement (45% G), and with no G in the template there is nothing to pair with C, so **deoxycytidine is absent**.",src:"pe3-cp:44",how:{"ask":"Pair each base in the template with its partner to build the composition of the new strand.","know":"• **Base pairing: A pairs with T; G pairs with C.**\n• The complement's composition: %T = template %A, %A = template %T, %G = template %C, %C = template %G.\n• Nucleosides: deoxyadenosine (A), deoxythymidine (T), deoxyguanosine (G), deoxycytidine (C).\n• Chargaff's rule (A = T, G = C) applies to double-stranded DNA, not to one strand.","steps":[{"text":"This is ONE strand, so Chargaff's A = T, C = G does not apply within it. Use base pairing to build the other strand.","hl":"A 31%, T 24%, C 45%, G 0%"},{"text":"Each template base dictates its partner in the new strand.","calc":["template A **31%** → complement T **31%**","template T **24%** → complement A **24%**","template C **45%** → complement G **45%**","template G **0%** → complement C **0%**"]},{"text":"Read the complement bars: G is 45% and C is absent.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Base composition of the template and its complement","x":{"label":"Base"},"y":{"label":"Percent of strand","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"categories":["A","T","C","G"],"series":[{"name":"Template","values":[31,24,45,0]},{"name":"Complement","values":[24,31,0,45]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["G","Complement"],["C","Complement"]]},"strike":["Deoxyguanosine; 0%","Deoxycytidine; 31%","Deoxythymidine; 24%"]},{"text":"The missing base is cytosine (its nucleoside is deoxycytidine), and deoxyguanosine is 45%.","fig":["cytosine","guanine"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxycytidine is absent; deoxyguanosine is 45%."}],"trap":"\"Deoxyguanosine; 0%\" copies the template's missing base instead of pairing it. The template lacks G, so its partner, C, is what the complement lacks."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers:** the share of each DNA letter (base) in this one strand.","marks":[{"t":"A 31%, T 24%","note":"adenine, thymine"},{"t":"C 45%","note":"cytosine"},{"t":"G 0%","note":"guanine: none"}]},{"text":"**DNA is usually two strands zipped together.** Here we have only one; a partner strand is then built to match it.","marks":[{"t":"single-stranded","note":"one strand only"},{"t":"complementary strand","note":"its matching partner"}]},{"text":"**Pairing rule: A always pairs with T, and G always pairs with C.** Each letter in the template decides its partner.","marks":[{"t":"A 31%","note":"A pairs with T"},{"t":"C 45%","note":"C pairs with G"}],"calc":["A ↔ T","G ↔ C"]},{"text":"**Translate each letter into its partner.** The percentages carry straight across.","marks":[{"t":"A 31%","note":"→ T 31%"},{"t":"T 24%","note":"→ A 24%"},{"t":"C 45%","note":"→ G 45%"}],"calc":["template A 31% → new T **31%**","template T 24% → new A **24%**","template C 45% → new G **45%**"]},{"text":"**No G in the template → no C in the new strand.** Nothing is there to pair with C.","marks":[{"t":"G 0%","note":"→ C 0%: none"}],"calc":["template G 0% → new C **0%**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Base composition of the template and its complement","x":{"label":"Base"},"y":{"label":"Percent of strand","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"categories":["A","T","C","G"],"series":[{"name":"Template","values":[31,24,45,0]},{"name":"Complement","values":[24,31,0,45]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["G","Complement"],["C","Complement"]]}},{"text":"**Nucleoside = base + sugar.** The name tells you the base: deoxy**cytidine** has C, deoxy**guanosine** has G.","marks":[{"t":"nucleoside","note":"base + sugar"},{"t":"absent","note":"C at 0% → deoxycytidine"},{"t":"deoxyguanosine","note":"G: 45%"}],"calc":["C → deoxy**cytidine** (absent)","G → deoxy**guanosine** (45%)"]},{"text":"**Trap:** Chargaff's A = T, G = C is for a double strand. In one strand the numbers can be anything, and G 0% here becomes 45% G in the partner.","marks":[{"t":"single-stranded","note":"Chargaff's A = T not here"},{"t":"G 0%","note":"template only"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to build the partner strand, swap A↔T and G↔C: partner %G = template %C, partner %T = template %A, and so on."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5C",q:"The graph shows the net charge of a protein as a function of pH. The protein is to be purified on a DEAE anion-exchange column, whose beads carry fixed positively charged groups. At which buffer pH will the protein bind the column, and why?",idStem:"A cation-exchange column binds positively charged proteins. When is a protein positively charged?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge of the protein vs pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-12,"max":12,"step":4},"series":[{"name":"Net charge","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,12],[4,7],[5,2.5],[5.5,0],[6,-2],[7,-5],[8,-8],[9,-11]]}]},choices:["pH 4.0, since the protein is positive there","pH 7.0, since the protein is negative there","pH 5.5, since the protein has no net charge there","pH 4.0, since the protein is negative there"],ans:1,exp:"The curve crosses zero at **pH 5.5, the pI**. Above the pI the protein carries a **net negative charge**; at pH 7.0 it is about −5, so it sticks to the **positive** DEAE groups. At pH 4.0 it is +7 and is repelled; at pH 5.5 it has no net charge and does not bind.",src:"pe3-cp:45",how:{"ask":"What charge must the protein carry to stick to positive beads, and on which side of the zero-crossing does the curve give that charge?","know":"• **pI (isoelectric point):** the pH where net charge = 0; on a charge-vs-pH graph, where the curve crosses zero.\n• **pH below pI → net positive** (extra H⁺ bound). **pH above pI → net negative.**\n• **Anion exchanger** (DEAE, positive beads) binds negatively charged proteins → run it at pH above the pI.\n• **Cation exchanger** (CM, negative beads) binds positive proteins → run it at pH below the pI.\n• Opposite charges attract: match the protein's charge to the opposite of the beads.","steps":[{"text":"The beads are positive, so the protein must be negative to bind.","hl":"fixed positively charged groups","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":5.5,"x2":9,"text":"protein negative"}]}},{"text":"Find where the curve crosses zero: the pI.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5}],"yLine":0,"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5,"text":"pI = 5.5","at":"right"}]},"calc":["net charge = 0 at **pH 5.5** = pI"]},{"text":"With no net charge, the protein has no reason to bind the beads.","strike":["pH 5.5, since the protein has no net charge there"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5,"text":"no net charge → no binding","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Read the charge at pH 4.0: positive, so it is repelled, not negative.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":4}],"yLine":0,"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":7,"text":"+7"}]},"calc":["pH 4.0: charge ≈ **+7**"],"strike":["pH 4.0, since the protein is positive there","pH 4.0, since the protein is negative there"]},{"text":"Read the charge at pH 7.0: negative, so it binds the positive beads.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":7}],"yLine":0,"spans":[{"x":7,"from":0,"to":-5,"text":"−5 → binds"}]},"calc":["pH 7.0: charge ≈ **−5** → binds"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 7.0. Rule: above the pI, negative → anion exchanger; below the pI, positive → cation exchanger."}],"trap":"\"pH 4.0, since the protein is negative there\" reverses the rule. Below the pI the protein has picked up extra protons and is positive, so the positive beads repel it."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = more positive charge, down = more negative.** The 0 line in the middle means no net charge.","marks":[{"t":"net charge of a protein","note":"y axis: + up, − down"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = higher pH**, a less acidic, more basic buffer.","marks":[{"t":"function of pH","note":"x axis: pH"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**As pH rises, the protein loses H⁺** (each H⁺ carries +1), so its charge falls from positive to negative.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":3,"text":"+12 at pH 3","at":"right"},{"series":"Net charge","x":9,"text":"−11 at pH 9","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Where the curve crosses 0 is the pI** (isoelectric point): the pH where the protein has no net charge.","calc":["net charge = 0 at **pH 5.5** = pI"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5}],"yLine":0,"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5,"text":"pI = 5.5: charge 0","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Below the pI the protein is positive; above it, negative.**","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":5.5,"text":"positive"},{"x1":5.5,"x2":9,"text":"negative"}]}},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** The beads are positive, so they hold a **negative** protein. (Anion = negative ion.)","marks":[{"t":"fixed positively charged groups","note":"beads are +"},{"t":"anion-exchange","note":"grabs negative things"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":5.5,"x2":9,"text":"protein − → binds"}]}},{"text":"**Read pH 4.0:** the charge is +7, positive like the beads, so it is pushed away.","calc":["pH 4.0: charge ≈ **+7** → no binding"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":4}],"yLine":0,"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":7,"text":"+7: repelled"}]}},{"text":"**Read pH 5.5:** charge 0, so nothing pulls it to the beads.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":5.5,"text":"0 charge → no binding","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Read pH 7.0:** the charge is −5, negative, so it sticks to the positive beads.","calc":["pH 7.0: charge ≈ **−5** → **binds**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":7}],"yLine":0,"spans":[{"x":7,"from":0,"to":-5,"text":"−5 → binds"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** above the pI a protein is negative (binds an anion exchanger, + beads); below the pI it is positive (binds a cation exchanger, − beads).","graph":{"focus":["Net charge"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5C",q:"Gel-filtration beads are made of a cross-linked polymer whose repeating segment is shown. Some small proteins elute later than their size predicts, suggesting a weak attraction to the bead material. Which interaction is most likely responsible?",idStem:"Dextran, a glucose polymer, is used as column material. How does it mainly interact with proteins?",fig:"polyacrylamide-segment",choices:["Hydrogen bonding with the amide groups","Ionic attraction to charged carboxylate groups","π-stacking with aromatic rings","Hydrophobic interaction with long alkyl chains"],ans:0,exp:"Scan the drawing: a short carbon backbone in which every other carbon carries a **–C(=O)NH₂ amide**. Amides have **N–H donors and C=O acceptors**, so proteins can hydrogen bond to them. There are no charges, no rings, and no long hydrocarbon chains.",src:"pe3-cp:46",how:{"ask":"List what the drawing actually contains: any charges, any rings, any long carbon chains, any N–H or O–H next to N or O?","know":"• To predict what a surface can do, list its functional groups from the drawing.\n• **H-bond donor:** H attached to N, O or F. **H-bond acceptor:** lone pair on N, O or F (C=O oxygen is a strong one).\n• A primary amide, –C(=O)NH₂, is both donor and acceptor.\n• **Ionic attraction** needs a drawn charge, such as COO⁻ or NH₃⁺. **π-stacking** needs aromatic rings. **Hydrophobic interaction** needs long or bulky C–H regions.\n• Rule out any interaction whose group is not in the picture.","steps":[{"text":"The beads attract the protein weakly: look for a weak, noncovalent interaction the polymer can make.","hl":"suggesting a weak attraction"},{"text":"Inventory the drawing.","fig":"polyacrylamide-segment","calc":["charges drawn: **none**","rings: **none**","chain: short CH₂–CH units, **no long alkyl chain**","groups: **–C(=O)NH₂ ×3**"]},{"text":"No charges means no ionic attraction, and no rings means no π-stacking.","strike":["Ionic attraction to charged carboxylate groups","π-stacking with aromatic rings"]},{"text":"Each short backbone stretch is flanked by polar amides, so there is no extended nonpolar surface.","strike":["Hydrophobic interaction with long alkyl chains"]},{"text":"The highlighted amides offer N–H donors and C=O acceptors to the protein's polar groups. Answer: hydrogen bonding with the amide groups.","fig":"polyacrylamide-amides"},{"text":"Rule: on a structure question about interactions, only count groups that are drawn: N–H/O–H and C=O → H-bonds; charges → ionic; rings → π; long C–H chains → hydrophobic."}],"trap":"Hydrophobic interaction is tempting because the backbone is made of carbons, but they are short CH₂–CH units, each next to a polar amide. Nothing in the drawing forms a long nonpolar surface."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawing.** Corners are carbons (hidden H). A **polymer** is one long molecule made of this segment repeated many times.","fig":"t3-pa-read"},{"text":"**Gel filtration:** small proteins enter the bead pores and come out late. Coming out even later than size predicts means the protein is weakly **sticking** to the beads.","fig":"polyacrylamide-segment"},{"text":"**List the groups you can see.** Every branch is the same **amide** group, –C(=O)NH₂.","fig":"t3-pa-amides"},{"text":"**Hydrogen bond:** an H on N or O is slightly positive and is drawn to a lone pair (2 unbonded electrons) on another N or O. Amides have both parts.","fig":"t3-amide-hb"},{"text":"**Ionic attraction** needs a drawn charge (+ or −), like COO⁻. **π-stacking** needs flat rings stacking face to face. This drawing has neither.","fig":"t3-pa-none"},{"text":"**Hydrophobic (water-fearing)** attraction needs long oily C–H chains. Here every second carbon carries a polar amide, so there is no long chain.","fig":"t3-pa-backbone"},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** count only drawn groups: N–H/O–H and C=O → H-bonds; + or − → ionic; rings → π-stacking; long C–H chains → hydrophobic. For each answer, ask: is it drawn?","fig":"polyacrylamide-segment"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Consider the atomic radii of the halogens: F 64 pm, Cl 99 pm, Br 114 pm, I 133 pm. Which halogen has the highest first ionization energy, and why?",idStem:"Which halogen has the highest first ionization energy?",choices:["Fluorine, since its valence electrons are closest to the nucleus","Chlorine, since it has the highest electron affinity","Fluorine, since it has the most valence electrons","Iodine, since its nucleus has the most protons"],ans:0,exp:"**Ionization energy increases up a group**: smaller atoms hold their valence electrons closer to the nucleus, with less shielding, so more energy is needed to remove one. Fluorine (64 pm) is the smallest.",src:"pe3-cp:47",how:{"ask":"Ionization energy is the energy to pull off one electron. Which atom holds its outermost electron most tightly, and what in the data shows that?","know":"• **First ionization energy (IE₁)**: energy to remove one electron from a gas-phase atom.\n• Down a group, electrons sit in higher shells, farther away and more shielded → **IE decreases**. Up a group → IE increases.\n• Across a period (left → right), IE generally increases.\n• **Electron affinity** (energy released on gaining an electron) is a different property; Cl has the highest EA, an exception.","steps":[{"text":"All halogens have 7 valence electrons, so count can't decide it; size can. Radius grows down the group.","hl":"F 64 pm, Cl 99 pm, Br 114 pm, I 133 pm","strike":["Fluorine, since it has the most valence electrons"]},{"text":"Coulomb's law: attraction ∝ Z_eff/r². Z_eff is similar for all, so the smallest atom holds its electrons hardest.","calc":["F ∝ Z_eff e² / r²","(r_I / r_F)² = (133/64)² ≈ **4.3**","→ F's valence electron pulled ~4× harder than I's"]},{"text":"Actual IE₁ values fall as radius grows: fluorine is highest.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"First ionization energy vs atomic radius for the halogens","x":{"label":"Atomic radius (pm)","min":50,"max":150,"step":25},"y":{"label":"IE₁ (kJ/mol)","min":800,"max":1800,"step":200},"series":[{"name":"F, Cl, Br, I","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[64,1681],[99,1251],[114,1140],[133,1008]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":64},{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":133}],"labels":[{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":64,"text":"F (smallest): highest IE₁","at":"right"},{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":133,"text":"I (largest): lowest","at":"left"}]},"strike":["Iodine, since its nucleus has the most protons"]},{"text":"Electron affinity is about GAINING an electron, a different property.","strike":["Chlorine, since it has the highest electron affinity"]},{"text":"Answer: fluorine, since its valence electrons are closest to the nucleus. Iodine's extra protons are offset by extra shielding."}],"trap":"\"Chlorine, since it has the highest electron affinity\" uses a true fact about the wrong property: electron affinity is about gaining an electron. \"Fluorine, since it has the most valence electrons\" is the right element with a false reason: every halogen has seven."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers:** the size of each atom. pm (picometer) is a tiny length unit.","marks":[{"t":"atomic radii","note":"size of each atom"},{"t":"F 64 pm","note":"fluorine: smallest"},{"t":"I 133 pm","note":"iodine: biggest"}]},{"text":"**Halogens** are one column of the periodic table. All have 7 outer (valence) electrons, so they differ mainly in size.","marks":[{"t":"halogens","note":"group 17: 7 outer e⁻"}]},{"text":"**First ionization energy (IE₁)** = the energy needed to pull ONE electron off the atom. Higher = harder to remove.","marks":[{"t":"first ionization energy","note":"energy to pull off 1 e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Closer = held tighter.** The + nucleus pulls the − electron, and the pull fades fast with distance (about 1/r²).","marks":[{"t":"F 64 pm","note":"closest e⁻"},{"t":"I 133 pm","note":"farthest e⁻"}],"calc":["(133 / 64)² ≈ **4.3**","F's outer e⁻ pulled ~4× harder than I's"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Relative pull on an outer electron (≈ 1/r², F = 1)","x":{"label":"Atomic radius (pm)","min":50,"max":150,"step":25},"y":{"label":"Relative pull","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"relative pull","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[64,1],[99,0.42],[114,0.32],[133,0.23]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"relative pull","x":64,"text":"F: strongest pull","at":"right"},{"series":"relative pull","x":133,"text":"I: weakest","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Real data agree:** IE₁ falls as the atom gets bigger. Fluorine is highest.","marks":[{"t":"Cl 99 pm","note":"in between"},{"t":"Br 114 pm","note":"in between"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"First ionization energy vs atomic radius for the halogens","x":{"label":"Atomic radius (pm)","min":50,"max":150,"step":25},"y":{"label":"IE₁ (kJ/mol)","min":800,"max":1800,"step":200},"series":[{"name":"F, Cl, Br, I","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[64,1681],[99,1251],[114,1140],[133,1008]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":64},{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":133}],"labels":[{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":64,"text":"F: highest IE₁","at":"right"},{"series":"F, Cl, Br, I","x":133,"text":"I: lowest","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Trap: more protons doesn't win.** Iodine has the most protons, but its outer electron is far away and shielded by many inner shells.","marks":[{"t":"I 133 pm","note":"most protons, but far"}]},{"text":"**Other traps:** every halogen has 7 valence electrons, so none has \"the most\". Electron affinity is about ADDING an electron, a different property.","marks":[{"t":"first ionization energy","note":"removing, not adding e⁻"},{"t":"halogens","note":"all have 7 valence e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** smaller atom → outer electrons closer to the nucleus → higher ionization energy. IE rises up a group and across a period."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A cart starts from rest and moves in a straight line with constant acceleration. Its position is plotted against time. Which relationship do the data show, and what is the cart's acceleration?",idStem:"An object starts from rest with constant acceleration. A graph shows d = 36 at t = 6 and d = 100 at t = 10. What is the relationship?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Cart position vs time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Cart","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,2],[2,8],[3,18],[4,32],[5,50]]}]},choices:["d ∝ t²; a = 2 m/s²","d ∝ t²; a = 4 m/s²","d ∝ t; a = 0 m/s²","d ∝ t; a = 10 m/s²"],ans:1,exp:"Doubling t from 1 to 2 s quadruples d from 2 to 8 m, so **d ∝ t²**. From rest, d = ½at², so a = 2d/t² = 2(50)/5² = **4 m/s²**. The curve here is d = 2t², and the 2 is ½a, not a.",src:"pe3-cp:48",how:{"ask":"What happens to d when t doubles, and which equation links d and t for a start from rest?","know":"• Starting from rest with constant acceleration: **d = ½at²**.\n• So d ∝ t²: doubling t multiplies d by 4; tripling multiplies it by 9. On a d–t graph this is an upward-curving parabola.\n• A straight d–t line means constant velocity, a = 0.\n• To get a: pick any point and use **a = 2d/t²**. The coefficient in d = kt² is ½a, so a = 2k.\n• The slope of a d–t graph is velocity, not acceleration.","steps":[{"text":"From rest with constant acceleration: expect d = ½at², a parabola.","hl":"starts from rest and moves in a straight line with constant acceleration","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":0,"text":"rest: v = 0","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Test the shape: read d at 1 s and 2 s.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":1},{"series":"Cart","x":2}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":2,"text":"d = 2"},{"x":2,"from":0,"to":8,"text":"d = 8 (×4)"}]},"calc":["t: 1 → 2 s, ×2","d: 2 → 8 m, **×4**","→ **d ∝ t²**, curved, not a straight line"]},{"text":"A straight-line relationship would need d to double with t, and a curving graph cannot have a = 0.","strike":["d ∝ t; a = 0 m/s²","d ∝ t; a = 10 m/s²"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":3,"text":"curving up, not straight","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Read one clear point and solve for a.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":5}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"d = 50 at t = 5","side":"left"}]},"calc":["d = ½at² → a = 2d/t²","a = 2 × 50 ÷ 5² = 100 ÷ 25","a = **4 m/s²**"]},{"text":"The data fit d = 2t²; that 2 is ½a, not a.","calc":["d = **2**t² = ½at² → ½a = 2 → a = **4**"],"strike":["d ∝ t²; a = 2 m/s²"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":3,"text":"2 × 3² = 18 ✓","at":"left"},{"series":"Cart","x":4,"text":"2 × 4² = 32 ✓","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: d ∝ t²; a = 4 m/s². Rule: from rest, a = 2d/t², twice the coefficient of t²."}],"trap":"2 m/s² comes from reading d = 2t² and taking the 2 as the acceleration. The 2 is ½a, so a is twice that."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = farther along the track** (position, in meters from the start).","marks":[{"t":"position","note":"y axis: how far (m)"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = time passing**, one tick per second.","marks":[{"t":"against time","note":"x axis: seconds"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Acceleration = how fast the speed changes.** Constant acceleration = speeding up by the same amount every second.","marks":[{"t":"starts from rest","note":"speed 0 at t = 0"},{"t":"constant acceleration","note":"speeds up steadily"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":0,"text":"start: at rest","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**From rest: d = ½at².** Distance grows with time squared, so the graph bends upward, not a straight line.","calc":["d = ½ × a × t²"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"slow"},{"x1":3,"x2":5,"text":"fast"}]}},{"text":"**Test the shape: double the time.** At 1 s it is 2 m; at 2 s it is 8 m: four times as far.","calc":["t: 1 → 2 s (×2)","d: 2 → 8 m (**×4**)","×2 in time → ×4 in distance → **d ∝ t²**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":1},{"series":"Cart","x":2}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":2,"text":"d = 2"},{"x":2,"from":0,"to":8,"text":"d = 8 (×4)"}]}},{"text":"**Not d ∝ t:** that would be a straight line (constant speed, a = 0). This curve bends up.","marks":[{"t":"Which relationship","note":"d ∝ t² (curve)"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":3,"text":"curving up, not straight","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Get a from one clear point:** t = 5 s, d = 50 m.","marks":[{"t":"cart's acceleration","note":"a = 4 m/s²"}],"calc":["d = ½at² → a = 2d / t²","a = 2 × 50 ÷ 5²","a = 100 ÷ 25 = **4 m/s²**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":5}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":0,"to":50,"text":"d = 50 at t = 5","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** the data fit d = 2t². That 2 is ½a, not a, so a = 4.","calc":["d = **2**t² = ½at²","½a = 2 → a = **4 m/s²**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":3,"text":"2 × 3² = 18 ✓","at":"left"},{"series":"Cart","x":4,"text":"2 × 4² = 32 ✓","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest, d = ½at². Doubling t quadruples d (d ∝ t²), and a = 2d/t², twice the number in front of t².","graph":{"focus":["Cart"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A 2.0 MHz ultrasound pulse travels from a water bath, where sound moves at 1480 m/s, into muscle, where it moves at 1580 m/s. Which of the following describes the pulse in the muscle?",idStem:"An ultrasound wave passes from water into body tissue. Which property stays the same?",choices:["Frequency 2.0 MHz; wavelength shorter than in water","Frequency higher than 2.0 MHz; wavelength unchanged","Frequency 2.0 MHz; wavelength longer than in water","Frequency lower than 2.0 MHz; wavelength unchanged"],ans:2,exp:"**Frequency is set by the source and does not change at a boundary.** Since **v = fλ**, a higher speed at the same frequency means a longer wavelength: 1580/2.0 × 10⁶ = 0.79 mm vs. 1480/2.0 × 10⁶ = 0.74 mm.",src:"pe3-cp:49",how:{"ask":"When a wave crosses into a new medium, which quantity is fixed and which adjust? Then compare the speeds.","know":"• **v = fλ**: wave speed = frequency × wavelength.\n• **Frequency is fixed by the source**: crests arrive at the boundary and leave it at the same rate.\n• **Speed depends on the medium.** So when v changes, λ changes in proportion.","steps":[{"text":"Frequency is set by the source and stays the same when a wave crosses into a new medium.","hl":"A 2.0 MHz ultrasound pulse travels from a water bath","strike":["Frequency higher than 2.0 MHz; wavelength unchanged","Frequency lower than 2.0 MHz; wavelength unchanged"]},{"text":"Wavelength in water: λ = v/f.","calc":["λ = 1480 m/s ÷ **2.0 × 10⁶ Hz**","λ = 7.4 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.74 mm**"]},{"text":"Wavelength in muscle: the speed rises, f stays fixed, so λ grows.","hl":"where it moves at 1580 m/s","calc":["λ = 1580 m/s ÷ 2.0 × 10⁶ Hz","λ = **0.79 mm**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength of the 2.0 MHz pulse in each medium","x":{"label":"Medium"},"y":{"label":"Wavelength (mm)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"categories":["Water","Muscle"],"series":[{"name":"λ","values":[0.74,0.79]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Muscle","λ"]]},"strike":["Frequency 2.0 MHz; wavelength shorter than in water"]},{"text":"Answer: frequency 2.0 MHz; wavelength longer than in water."}],"trap":"\"Wavelength shorter\" keeps f fixed correctly but reverses the speed effect, which applies to a slower medium. The choices with a changing frequency assume the medium changes the pitch, which it can't."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers:** one frequency and two speeds, one for each material.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 MHz","note":"frequency"},{"t":"1480 m/s","note":"speed in water"},{"t":"1580 m/s","note":"speed in muscle"}]},{"text":"**Frequency** = how many wave crests pass each second. **Wavelength (λ)** = the distance from one crest to the next.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"A sound wave in water (2.0 MHz)","x":{"label":"Distance (mm)","min":0,"max":1.6,"step":0.4},"y":{"label":"Pressure (relative)","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Pressure wave","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.185,1],[0.37,0],[0.555,-1],[0.74,0],[0.925,1],[1.11,0],[1.295,-1],[1.48,0]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.185,"x2":0.925,"text":"1 wavelength = 0.74 mm"}],"labels":[{"series":"Pressure wave","x":0.185,"text":"crest","at":"above"},{"series":"Pressure wave","x":0.925,"text":"next crest","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**M = mega = one million (10⁶).** Hz = per second.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 MHz","note":"2.0 × 10⁶ crests per s"}],"calc":["2.0 MHz = **2.0 × 10⁶ Hz**"]},{"text":"**Frequency is set by the source** and never changes at a boundary: every crest that arrives at the muscle goes on into it.","marks":[{"t":"from a water bath","note":"crossing a boundary"},{"t":"into muscle","note":"still 2.0 MHz here"}]},{"text":"**v = f × λ**, so λ = v ÷ f. Wavelength in water first.","marks":[{"t":"1480 m/s","note":"v in water"}],"calc":["λ = v ÷ f","λ = 1480 ÷ 2.0 × 10⁶","λ = 7.4 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.74 mm**"]},{"text":"**In muscle the speed is higher, f is the same, so λ must grow.**","marks":[{"t":"1580 m/s","note":"faster → longer λ"}],"calc":["λ = 1580 ÷ 2.0 × 10⁶ = **0.79 mm**","0.79 mm > 0.74 mm → **longer**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Wavelength of the 2.0 MHz pulse in each medium","x":{"label":"Medium"},"y":{"label":"Wavelength (mm)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"categories":["Water","Muscle"],"series":[{"name":"λ","values":[0.74,0.79]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Muscle","λ"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** anything that changes the frequency is wrong, and if speed changes, λ can't stay the same.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 MHz","note":"stays 2.0 MHz"}],"calc":["same f, faster v → λ **longer**, not shorter or unchanged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** crossing into a new material, frequency stays fixed (set by the source); speed changes, so wavelength changes in proportion (λ = v/f)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"Fluid flows through a membrane pore 40 μm long with a cross-sectional area of 2.5 × 10⁴ μm². The average fluid speed in the pore is 0.30 mm/s. What is the volume flow rate through the pore?",idStem:"Fluid moves at 0.30 mm/s through an opening of area 2.5 × 10⁴ μm². What is the volume flow rate?",choices:["7.5 × 10³ μm³/s","7.5 × 10⁶ μm³/s","3.0 × 10⁸ μm³/s","1.2 × 10⁻² μm³/s"],ans:1,exp:"**Volume flow rate Q = A × v.** First match units: 0.30 mm/s = 300 μm/s. Q = 2.5 × 10⁴ μm² × 300 μm/s = 7.5 × 10⁶ μm³/s. The pore length is not needed.",src:"pe3-cp:50",how:{"ask":"Which two quantities multiply to give volume per time, and are their length units the same?","know":"• **Volume flow rate Q = A·v** (area × average speed), units of volume/time.\n• Units must match: μm² × μm/s = μm³/s.\n• **1 mm = 1000 μm.**\n• The length of the tube does not enter Q = Av.","steps":[{"text":"Put everything in μm first.","hl":"0.30 mm/s","calc":["0.30 mm/s × 1000 μm/mm = **300 μm/s**"]},{"text":"Volume flow rate = area × average speed.","hl":"cross-sectional area of 2.5 × 10⁴ μm²","calc":["Q = Av","Q = 2.5 × 10⁴ μm² × **300 μm/s**","Q = 750 × 10⁴ = **7.5 × 10⁶ μm³/s**","units: μm² × μm/s = μm³/s ✓"]},{"text":"The pore length doesn't enter Q = Av. Multiplying by it, skipping the mm → μm step, or dividing give the other choices.","hl":"40 μm long","calc":["7.5 × 10⁶ × 40 = 3.0 × 10⁸ ✗","2.5 × 10⁴ × 0.30 = 7.5 × 10³ ✗","300 ÷ 2.5 × 10⁴ = 1.2 × 10⁻² ✗"],"strike":["3.0 × 10⁸ μm³/s","7.5 × 10³ μm³/s","1.2 × 10⁻² μm³/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.5 × 10⁶ μm³/s."}],"trap":"7.5 × 10³ μm³/s skips the mm → μm conversion (2.5 × 10⁴ × 0.30). 3.0 × 10⁸ multiplies in the pore length too, which gives the wrong units."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers** and what each one is.","marks":[{"t":"40 μm long","note":"length of the pore"},{"t":"2.5 × 10⁴ μm²","note":"area of the opening"},{"t":"0.30 mm/s","note":"fluid speed"}]},{"text":"**Volume flow rate Q** = how much fluid volume passes through each second (like liters per minute from a tap).","marks":[{"t":"volume flow rate","note":"volume per second"}]},{"text":"**Picture a slab of fluid.** In 1 s the fluid moves forward a distance v. That slab's volume = face area × v.","marks":[{"t":"cross-sectional area","note":"face of the slab"},{"t":"average fluid speed","note":"distance moved in 1 s"}],"calc":["Q = A × v"]},{"text":"**Units must match.** Area is in μm, speed is in mm. 1 mm = 1000 μm.","marks":[{"t":"0.30 mm/s","note":"= 300 μm/s"},{"t":"2.5 × 10⁴ μm²","note":"already in μm"}],"calc":["0.30 mm/s × 1000 = **300 μm/s**"]},{"text":"**Multiply area × speed.**","marks":[{"t":"2.5 × 10⁴ μm²","note":"A"},{"t":"0.30 mm/s","note":"v = 300 μm/s"}],"calc":["Q = 2.5 × 10⁴ μm² × 300 μm/s","2.5 × 300 = 750","Q = 750 × 10⁴ = **7.5 × 10⁶ μm³/s**","units: μm² × μm/s = **μm³/s** ✓"]},{"text":"**See it:** volume keeps piling up at a steady 7.5 × 10⁶ μm³ every second. That steady rate is Q.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Volume through the pore vs time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Volume (× 10⁶ μm³)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Volume passed","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,7.5],[2,15],[3,22.5]]}]},"graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":7.5,"text":"7.5 per second"}],"labels":[{"series":"Volume passed","x":2,"text":"+7.5 each second","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**The length is extra data.** It doesn't enter Q = Av. Using it, skipping mm → μm, or dividing gives the trap numbers.","marks":[{"t":"40 μm long","note":"not needed"}],"calc":["× 40 μm → 3.0 × 10⁸ ✗","2.5 × 10⁴ × 0.30 → 7.5 × 10³ ✗ (no mm→μm)","300 ÷ 2.5 × 10⁴ → 1.2 × 10⁻² ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** volume flow rate Q = area × average speed. Put both in the same length unit first; tube length does not matter."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"An ultrasound transducer 1.0 cm in diameter emits waves at 2.3 MHz into soft tissue, where sound travels at 1500 m/s. About what is the wavelength of the ultrasound in the tissue?",idStem:"A 2.3 MHz ultrasound wave travels through tissue at 1500 m/s. About what is its wavelength?",choices:["0.65 mm","6.5 mm","650 m","3.5 × 10⁹ m"],ans:0,exp:"**λ = v/f** = 1500 m/s ÷ 2.3 × 10⁶ Hz ≈ 6.5 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.65 mm. The MHz prefix (10⁶) must be applied.",src:"pe3-cp:51",how:{"ask":"Which wave equation links speed, frequency and wavelength, and what is 2.3 MHz in hertz?","know":"• **v = fλ → λ = v/f.**\n• **M (mega) = 10⁶**: 2.3 MHz = 2.3 × 10⁶ Hz (s⁻¹).\n• 1 m = 1000 mm.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the frequency to hertz.","hl":"2.3 MHz","calc":["2.3 MHz = **2.3 × 10⁶ Hz**"]},{"text":"λ = v/f with the tissue speed.","hl":"where sound travels at 1500 m/s","calc":["λ = v/f","λ = 1500 m/s ÷ **2.3 × 10⁶ Hz**","λ ≈ **6.5 × 10⁻⁴ m**","× 1000 mm/m = **0.65 mm**"]},{"text":"Spot the slips: forgetting the 10⁶ gives 650 m; multiplying v × f gives 3.5 × 10⁹; a misplaced decimal gives 6.5 mm.","calc":["1500 ÷ 2.3 ≈ 650 ✗","1500 × 2.3 × 10⁶ ≈ 3.5 × 10⁹ ✗"],"strike":["650 m","3.5 × 10⁹ m","6.5 mm"]},{"text":"The transducer diameter is extra data; it affects beam width, not wavelength.","hl":"1.0 cm in diameter"},{"text":"Answer: about 0.65 mm."}],"trap":"650 m divides by 2.3 instead of 2.3 × 10⁶, forgetting the mega prefix. 6.5 mm misplaces the decimal when converting from meters. 3.5 × 10⁹ m multiplies v × f."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers** and what each one is.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm in diameter","note":"size of the device"},{"t":"2.3 MHz","note":"frequency"},{"t":"1500 m/s","note":"speed in tissue"}]},{"text":"**Wavelength (λ)** = distance from one wave crest to the next. **Frequency** = crests per second.","marks":[{"t":"wavelength","note":"λ: what we want"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Ultrasound wave in soft tissue (2.3 MHz)","x":{"label":"Distance (mm)","min":0,"max":1.4,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"Pressure (relative)","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Sound wave","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.163,1],[0.325,0],[0.488,-1],[0.65,0],[0.812,1],[0.975,0],[1.137,-1],[1.3,0]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.163,"x2":0.812,"text":"1 wavelength"}],"labels":[{"series":"Sound wave","x":0.163,"text":"crest","at":"above"},{"series":"Sound wave","x":0.812,"text":"next crest","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Speed = frequency × wavelength.** Crests per second × distance between crests = distance per second.","marks":[{"t":"1500 m/s","note":"v"},{"t":"2.3 MHz","note":"f"}],"calc":["v = f × λ","λ = **v ÷ f**"]},{"text":"**M = mega = one million (10⁶).** Never drop it.","marks":[{"t":"2.3 MHz","note":"2.3 × 10⁶ per second"}],"calc":["2.3 MHz = **2.3 × 10⁶ Hz**"]},{"text":"**Divide speed by frequency.**","marks":[{"t":"1500 m/s","note":"top"},{"t":"2.3 MHz","note":"bottom (× 10⁶)"}],"calc":["λ = 1500 ÷ (2.3 × 10⁶)","1500 ÷ 2.3 ≈ 650","λ ≈ 650 × 10⁻⁶ = **6.5 × 10⁻⁴ m**"]},{"text":"**Convert to mm:** 1 m = 1000 mm.","marks":[{"t":"About what","note":"≈ 0.65 mm"}],"calc":["6.5 × 10⁻⁴ m × 1000 = **0.65 mm**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** forgetting the 10⁶ gives 650 m; multiplying v × f gives 3.5 × 10⁹; a slipped decimal gives 6.5 mm.","marks":[{"t":"2.3 MHz","note":"forget 10⁶ → 650 m ✗"}],"calc":["1500 ÷ 2.3 = 650 (no 10⁶) ✗","1500 × 2.3 × 10⁶ ≈ 3.5 × 10⁹ ✗"]},{"text":"**The diameter is extra data.** It affects how wide the beam is, not the wavelength.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm in diameter","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = v/f. Convert MHz to Hz (× 10⁶) first, then convert m to mm (× 1000) at the end."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"An arteriole of cross-sectional area A carries blood at average speed v and divides into n identical capillaries, each of cross-sectional area a. Blood is incompressible, and the total capillary area na is greater than A. Which expression gives the blood speed u in each capillary, and how does u compare with v?",idStem:"A vessel of area A at speed v feeds n capillaries, each of area a. What is the speed u in each capillary?",choices:["u = nav/A; larger than v","u = Av/na; smaller than v","u = Av/na; larger than v","u = Av/a; smaller than v"],ans:1,exp:"**Continuity: volume flow in = volume flow out**, so Av = n·a·u → **u = Av/na**. Because na > A, the ratio A/na < 1 and **u < v**: blood slows in the capillary beds.",src:"pe3-cp:52",how:{"ask":"Write the volume flow rate entering and leaving the branch point, set them equal, and solve for u.","know":"• **Continuity equation** for an incompressible fluid: **Q = A·v is the same everywhere** along the flow.\n• When one tube splits into n identical tubes, the outflow is n × (area of one) × (speed in one).\n• Larger total area → slower speed.","steps":[{"text":"Blood is incompressible, so the volume flow in equals the total flow out through all n capillaries.","hl":"divides into n identical capillaries","calc":["Q_in = **Av**","Q_out = n × a × u = **nau**"]},{"text":"Set them equal and solve for u. Forgetting n or flipping the ratio gives the wrong expressions.","calc":["Av = nau","u = **Av/(na)**"],"strike":["u = nav/A; larger than v","u = Av/a; smaller than v"]},{"text":"Compare: the factor A/(na) is less than 1 because the total capillary area is larger.","hl":"the total capillary area na is greater than A","calc":["na > A → A/(na) < 1 → **u < v**","example: A = 1, na = 4 → u = **v/4**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Example: A = 1 unit, na = 4 units","x":{"label":"Vessel"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["Arteriole","All capillaries"],"series":[{"name":"Total area","values":[1,4]},{"name":"Speed","values":[1,0.25]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["All capillaries","Speed"],["Arteriole","Speed"]]},"strike":["u = Av/na; larger than v"]},{"text":"Answer: u = Av/na; smaller than v. This is why blood moves slowest in the capillary beds, allowing exchange."}],"trap":"\"u = Av/na; larger than v\" has the right formula but the wrong comparison: the total capillary area is larger, so speed drops. \"u = Av/a\" forgets that flow is shared among n capillaries."},teach:[{"text":"**One big tube splits into many small ones.** Find each letter.","marks":[{"t":"cross-sectional area A","note":"big vessel's opening"},{"t":"average speed v","note":"speed in big vessel"},{"t":"n identical capillaries","note":"n small tubes"}]},{"text":"**More letters:** a is one capillary's opening; na is all n openings added up; u is what we want.","marks":[{"t":"cross-sectional area a","note":"opening of ONE capillary"},{"t":"total capillary area na","note":"all n openings added"},{"t":"blood speed u","note":"speed in one capillary"}]},{"text":"**Incompressible** = can't be squeezed smaller. So whatever volume goes in each second must come out each second.","marks":[{"t":"incompressible","note":"can't be squeezed"}]},{"text":"**Flow in = area × speed** (volume flow rate). For the arteriole that is A × v.","marks":[{"t":"cross-sectional area A","note":"A"},{"t":"average speed v","note":"× v"}],"calc":["Q_in = **A × v**"]},{"text":"**Flow out:** each capillary carries a × u, and there are n of them.","marks":[{"t":"n identical capillaries","note":"× n"},{"t":"cross-sectional area a","note":"a"},{"t":"blood speed u","note":"× u"}],"calc":["Q_out = n × a × u = **nau**"]},{"text":"**Set in = out and solve for u.**","marks":[{"t":"blood speed u","note":"u = Av/(na)"}],"calc":["Av = nau","divide both sides by na","u = **Av / (na)**"]},{"text":"**Compare u with v.** Total capillary area is bigger, so A/(na) is less than 1 and u is smaller than v.","marks":[{"t":"na is greater than A","note":"so A/(na) < 1"}],"calc":["u = v × A/(na)","example: A = 1, na = 4 → u = **v/4**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Example: A = 1 unit, na = 4 units","x":{"label":"Vessel"},"y":{"label":"Relative value","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["Arteriole","All capillaries"],"series":[{"name":"Total area","values":[1,4]},{"name":"Speed","values":[1,0.25]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["All capillaries","Speed"],["Arteriole","Speed"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** leaving out n gives Av/a; flipping the ratio gives nav/A, which would make blood speed up.","marks":[{"t":"n identical capillaries","note":"n must be in it"}],"calc":["Av/a ✗ (forgot there are n tubes)","nav/A ✗ (upside down)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for an incompressible fluid, area × speed is the same everywhere (A₁v₁ = A₂v₂). Bigger total area → slower flow."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A saturated solution of calcium carbonate is in contact with excess solid at 25 °C: CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq). In this solution, [Ca²⁺] = [CO₃²⁻] = 5.8 × 10⁻⁵ M. Which of the following gives the solubility product expression and its value?",idStem:"What is the Ksp expression for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻?",choices:["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]²[CO₃²⁻]² = 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁷","Ksp = [Ca²⁺] + [CO₃²⁻] = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴","Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] = 3.4 × 10⁻⁹","Ksp = 2[Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] = 6.7 × 10⁻⁹"],ans:2,exp:"**Pure solids are left out of equilibrium expressions**, and each ion is raised to its coefficient, here 1. Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] = (5.8 × 10⁻⁵)² = 3.4 × 10⁻⁹.",src:"pe3-cp:53",how:{"ask":"Which species belong in the equilibrium expression, what exponent does each get, and what number results?","know":"• **Ksp** (solubility product) = equilibrium constant for a solid dissolving into its ions.\n• Write products over reactants, each raised to its coefficient in the balanced equation.\n• **Solids and pure liquids are omitted** (their activity is 1).\n• Concentrations are multiplied, never added.","steps":[{"text":"Write Ksp from the dissolution equation: products only, solid omitted, each concentration raised to its coefficient (here 1).","hl":"CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq)","calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]¹[CO₃²⁻]¹","(CaCO₃(s) is a pure solid → left out)"],"strike":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺] + [CO₃²⁻] = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴","Ksp = [Ca²⁺]²[CO₃²⁻]² = 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁷"]},{"text":"Plug in the saturated concentrations.","hl":"[Ca²⁺] = [CO₃²⁻] = 5.8 × 10⁻⁵ M","calc":["Ksp = (**5.8 × 10⁻⁵**)(**5.8 × 10⁻⁵**)","= 33.6 × 10⁻¹⁰","= **3.4 × 10⁻⁹**"]},{"text":"There is no factor of 2: that would only appear as a coefficient (e.g. Ag₂CO₃ gives [Ag⁺]²).","strike":["Ksp = 2[Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] = 6.7 × 10⁻⁹"]},{"text":"Answer: Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] = 3.4 × 10⁻⁹."}],"trap":"Squaring each ion is what you'd do if the coefficients were 2. They are 1 here. Adding the concentrations is never how K is built."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what the question gives you.** Chalk (CaCO₃) dissolves a tiny bit in water.","marks":[{"t":"saturated solution","note":"dissolved as much as it can"},{"t":"CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq)","note":"the dissolving reaction"},{"t":"5.8 × 10⁻⁵ M","note":"amount of each ion"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation.** (s) = solid; (aq) = dissolved in water. ⇌ = dissolving and re-forming at equal rates.","marks":[{"t":"CaCO₃(s)","note":"solid"},{"t":"⇌","note":"goes both ways"},{"t":"Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq)","note":"dissolved ions"}]},{"text":"**Ksp (solubility product)** = the equilibrium constant for a solid dissolving: multiply the dissolved ion concentrations.","marks":[{"t":"solubility product expression","note":"Ksp"}],"calc":["Ksp = [products] multiplied together"]},{"text":"**Leave the solid out.** A pure solid doesn't change its concentration, so it never goes in K.","marks":[{"t":"CaCO₃(s)","note":"solid: left out"},{"t":"excess solid","note":"still not in Ksp"}],"calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]"]},{"text":"**Each ion is raised to its coefficient** (the number in front). Here both are 1, so no squares.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺(aq)","note":"coefficient 1 → power 1"},{"t":"CO₃²⁻(aq)","note":"coefficient 1 → power 1"}],"calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]¹ × [CO₃²⁻]¹"]},{"text":"**Plug in the numbers and multiply.**","marks":[{"t":"[Ca²⁺] = [CO₃²⁻] = 5.8 × 10⁻⁵ M","note":"each = 5.8 × 10⁻⁵"}],"calc":["Ksp = (5.8 × 10⁻⁵)(5.8 × 10⁻⁵)","5.8 × 5.8 = 33.6; 10⁻⁵ × 10⁻⁵ = 10⁻¹⁰","= 33.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ = **3.4 × 10⁻⁹**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** concentrations are multiplied, never added; no squares (coefficients are 1); no factor of 2.","marks":[{"t":"[Ca²⁺] = [CO₃²⁻]","note":"multiply, don't add"}],"calc":["added → 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ ✗","each squared → 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁷ ✗","× 2 → 6.7 × 10⁻⁹ ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp = product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient. Leave out solids and pure liquids; never add."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"The graph shows the first four ionization energies of a period-3 element. To which element do the data belong?",idStem:"Calcium and magnesium belong to which group?",chart:{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of a period-3 element","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":12000,"step":2000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃","IE₄"],"series":[{"name":"Ionization energy","values":[578,1817,2745,11577]}]},choices:["magnesium, since the large jump comes after the second electron","aluminum, since the large jump comes after the third electron","silicon, since four electrons are removed in the graph","sodium, since the large jump comes after the first electron"],ans:1,exp:"IE rises steadily for the first three electrons, then jumps about **4×** at IE₄ (2745 → 11,577 kJ/mol). The fourth electron must come from the noble-gas core, so the atom has **three valence electrons**: group 13 in period 3, **aluminum**.",src:"pe3-cp:54",how:{"ask":"Between which two bars is the jump that is far larger than the others, and how many electrons come off before it?","know":"• Successive ionization energies always rise, since each electron is pulled from an increasingly positive ion.\n• A **huge jump** appears when the next electron must come from the filled inner shell (the core).\n• **Number of electrons removed before the big jump = number of valence electrons = group** (for main-group elements).\n• Group 1: jump after IE₁. Group 2: after IE₂. Group 13: after IE₃. Group 14: after IE₄.\n• Compare ratios of neighboring bars, not raw heights.","steps":[{"text":"The element is in period 3, so the core after its valence electrons is the neon shell.","hl":"first four ionization energies of a period-3 element"},{"text":"Compare each bar with the one before it.","graph":{"bars":[["IE₃","Ionization energy"],["IE₄","Ionization energy"]]},"calc":["IE₂/IE₁ = 1817/578 ≈ **3.1**","IE₃/IE₂ = 2745/1817 ≈ **1.5**","IE₄/IE₃ = 11,577/2745 ≈ **4.2** ← big jump","IE₂→IE₃: +928; IE₃→IE₄: **+8832** kJ/mol"]},{"text":"A jump after IE₁ or IE₂ would mean one or two valence electrons. The rise from IE₁ to IE₂ is ordinary next to the IE₄ jump.","strike":["sodium, since the large jump comes after the first electron","magnesium, since the large jump comes after the second electron"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"Silicon has four valence electrons, so its big jump would come after IE₄, which the graph does not show. Four bars are just four bars.","strike":["silicon, since four electrons are removed in the graph"]},{"text":"Three electrons come off before the jump: three valence electrons, group 13. Answer: aluminum. Rule: count bars before the big jump to get the group.","graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"],["IE₃","Ionization energy"]]}}],"trap":"Magnesium is tempting because IE₂ is about three times IE₁, but that rise is ordinary. The real core jump is the much larger one between IE₃ and IE₄."},teach:[{"text":"**Bar height = energy needed to pull off that electron.** Taller = harder to remove.","marks":[{"t":"first four ionization energies","note":"4 bars, one per electron"}],"graph":{"focus":["Ionization energy"]}},{"text":"**Left to right = 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th electron removed**, one at a time from the same atom.","marks":[{"t":"period-3 element","note":"row 3: Na, Mg, Al, Si…"}],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Valence electrons** = the outer ones. Under them sits a full inner shell (the core), held far more tightly.","calc":["outer (valence) e⁻: easy to remove","core e⁻: **much** harder"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₄","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Each electron is a bit harder than the last**, since the ion left behind is more positive. A modest rise is normal.","calc":["IE₂ / IE₁ = 1817 / 578 ≈ **3.1**","IE₃ / IE₂ = 2745 / 1817 ≈ **1.5**"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Find the giant jump.** IE₄ is about 4× IE₃: the 4th electron is coming from the core.","calc":["IE₄ / IE₃ = 11,577 / 2745 ≈ **4.2** ← big jump","IE₃ → IE₄: **+8832** kJ/mol"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₃","Ionization energy"],["IE₄","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Count the bars before the jump: 3.** So the atom has 3 valence electrons → group 13.","calc":["period 3: Na (1), Mg (2), **Al (3)**, Si (4)","3 valence e⁻ → **aluminum**"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"],["IE₃","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** a jump after IE₁ would be sodium; after IE₂, magnesium. Here IE₁ → IE₂ is an ordinary rise next to the IE₄ jump.","graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"**Silicon trap:** four bars are just four measurements. Silicon's jump would come after IE₄, which isn't shown.","marks":[{"t":"first four","note":"4 measured, not 4 valence"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in successive ionization energies, count the electrons removed before the huge jump. That number = valence electrons = group (main-group elements).","graph":{"focus":["Ionization energy"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"For a reaction at 298 K, ΔG° = −11.4 kJ/mol. Which of the following describes the equilibrium constant K? (R = 8.314 J/mol·K; ln 100 ≈ 4.6)",idStem:"If a reaction is spontaneous under standard conditions, K must be:",choices:["K ≈ 100, so products are favored","K ≈ 100, so reactants are favored","K ≈ 4.6, so products are favored","K ≈ 0.01, so reactants are favored"],ans:0,exp:"**ΔG° = −RT ln K** → ln K = 11,400 ÷ (8.314 × 298) ≈ 4.6 → K ≈ e^4.6 ≈ 100. **Negative ΔG° ⇔ K > 1 ⇔ products favored at equilibrium.**",src:"pe3-cp:55",how:{"ask":"Rearrange the link between ΔG° and K, watch the kJ vs. J units, and then decide what the size of K says about the equilibrium mixture.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K.** Rearranged: **ln K = −ΔG°/RT**, K = e^(−ΔG°/RT).\n• Units: R is in J, so convert ΔG° from kJ to J.\n• **K > 1 → products favored; K < 1 → reactants favored.** ΔG° < 0 always gives K > 1.","steps":[{"text":"Put ΔG° in J/mol to match R, and compute RT.","hl":"ΔG° = −11.4 kJ/mol","calc":["ΔG° = **−11,400 J/mol**","RT = 8.314 × 298 ≈ **2478 J/mol**"]},{"text":"Solve ΔG° = −RT ln K for ln K. Watch the two minus signs.","calc":["ln K = −ΔG°/RT","ln K = −(**−11,400**)/2478","ln K = **+4.6**"],"strike":["K ≈ 0.01, so reactants are favored"]},{"text":"4.6 is ln K, not K: undo the log.","hl":"ln 100 ≈ 4.6","calc":["K = e^4.6 ≈ **100**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ln K = −ΔG°/RT at 298 K","x":{"label":"ΔG° (kJ/mol)","min":-20,"max":20,"step":5},"y":{"label":"ln K","min":-10,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ln K","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-20,8.07],[-11.4,4.6],[0,0],[11.4,-4.6],[20,-8.07]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ln K","x":-11.4}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":-20,"x2":0,"text":"ΔG° < 0: K > 1"}],"spans":[{"x":-11.4,"from":0,"to":4.6,"text":"ln K = 4.6 → K ≈ 100"}]},"strike":["K ≈ 4.6, so products are favored"]},{"text":"K > 1 (ΔG° < 0) means products are favored at equilibrium.","strike":["K ≈ 100, so reactants are favored"]},{"text":"Answer: K ≈ 100, so products are favored."}],"trap":"\"K ≈ 4.6\" stops at ln K and forgets to exponentiate. \"K ≈ 0.01\" drops the minus sign in ΔG° = −RT ln K. \"K ≈ 100, reactants favored\" has the right number but the wrong reading of it."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers** and what each one is.","marks":[{"t":"298 K","note":"temperature T"},{"t":"ΔG° = −11.4 kJ/mol","note":"energy change"},{"t":"R = 8.314 J/mol·K","note":"gas constant"}]},{"text":"**ΔG° = the energy push of a reaction.** Negative = downhill: the reaction tends to go forward toward products.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG° = −11.4 kJ/mol","note":"negative = downhill"}]},{"text":"**K = products ÷ reactants** at equilibrium. K > 1 → more products; K < 1 → more reactants.","marks":[{"t":"equilibrium constant K","note":"products ÷ reactants"}]},{"text":"**The link: ΔG° = −RT ln K.** ln is the natural log; ln K tells you the power that K is.","marks":[{"t":"ln 100 ≈ 4.6","note":"hint to undo ln at the end"}],"calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K","ln K = **−ΔG° / RT**"]},{"text":"**Units first:** R is in joules, so turn kJ into J (× 1000).","marks":[{"t":"−11.4 kJ/mol","note":"= −11,400 J/mol"},{"t":"J/mol·K","note":"R uses J, not kJ"}],"calc":["ΔG° = **−11,400 J/mol**","RT = 8.314 × 298 ≈ **2478 J/mol**"]},{"text":"**Divide, and watch the two minus signs.** Minus a negative = positive.","marks":[{"t":"298 K","note":"T"},{"t":"−11.4 kJ/mol","note":"−11,400"}],"calc":["ln K = −(−11,400) / 2478","ln K = **+4.6**"]},{"text":"**4.6 is ln K, not K.** The question tells you ln 100 ≈ 4.6, so K ≈ 100.","marks":[{"t":"ln 100 ≈ 4.6","note":"so K ≈ 100"}],"calc":["K = e^4.6 ≈ **100**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ln K = −ΔG°/RT at 298 K","x":{"label":"ΔG° (kJ/mol)","min":-20,"max":20,"step":5},"y":{"label":"ln K","min":-10,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ln K","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-20,8.07],[-11.4,4.6],[0,0],[11.4,-4.6],[20,-8.07]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ln K","x":-11.4}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":-20,"x2":0,"text":"ΔG° < 0: K > 1"}],"spans":[{"x":-11.4,"from":0,"to":4.6,"text":"ln K = 4.6 → K ≈ 100"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** K ≈ 4.6 forgets to undo the ln; K ≈ 0.01 loses a minus sign; K > 1 never means reactants are favored.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG° = −11.4 kJ/mol","note":"< 0 → K > 1 → products"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ln K = −ΔG°/RT (ΔG° in J). Negative ΔG° ↔ K > 1 ↔ products favored; positive ΔG° ↔ K < 1 ↔ reactants favored."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A water softener removes hardness ions by the equilibrium: Ca²⁺(aq) + 2 Na–R(s) ⇌ Ca–R₂(s) + 2 Na⁺(aq), where R is a fixed anionic site on the resin. Once the resin is saturated with Ca²⁺, it is regenerated by flushing it with a concentrated solution. Which solution should be used, and why?",idStem:"In a water softener, Ca²⁺ + 2 Na-resin ⇌ Ca-resin + 2Na⁺. What shifts it to the LEFT (regenerating the resin)?",choices:["CaCl₂, since added Ca²⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left","NaCl, since added Na⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left","NaCl, since added Na⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the right","Distilled water, since removing ions shifts the equilibrium to the left"],ans:1,exp:"Regeneration means turning Ca–R₂ back into Na–R, which is the **reverse (leftward) reaction**. **Le Châtelier: adding a product (Na⁺) shifts the equilibrium back toward reactants.** That's why softeners are flushed with brine.",src:"pe3-cp:56",how:{"ask":"Which direction of the reaction regenerates the resin, and which added species would push the equilibrium that way?","know":"• **Le Châtelier's principle**: if you disturb an equilibrium, it shifts to partly undo the disturbance.\n• **Adding a product → shifts left** (toward reactants). Adding a reactant → shifts right.\n• Solids (the resin forms) do not appear in K, but the dissolved ions do.","steps":[{"text":"Regenerating means turning Ca–R₂ back into Na–R: the equilibrium must be driven to the LEFT.","hl":"regenerated"},{"text":"Write Q (solids omitted) and see what a flood of Na⁺ does to it.","hl":"flushing it with a concentrated solution","calc":["Q = [Na⁺]² / [Ca²⁺]","[Na⁺] × 10 → Q × 10² = **100 K**","Q > K → shift **left** (Ca²⁺ released)"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Reaction quotient Q = [Na⁺]²/[Ca²⁺] relative to K","x":{"label":"Condition"},"y":{"label":"Q / K","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["At equilibrium","Na⁺ raised 10×"],"series":[{"name":"Q/K","values":[1,100]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na⁺ raised 10×","Q/K"]]}},{"text":"Eliminate: adding Na⁺ shifts left, not right; Ca²⁺ is a reactant, so adding it drives MORE Ca onto the resin; dilution with water lowers Q (Q/d), which shifts right.","strike":["NaCl, since added Na⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the right","CaCl₂, since added Ca²⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left","Distilled water, since removing ions shifts the equilibrium to the left"]},{"text":"Answer: NaCl, since added Na⁺ shifts the equilibrium to the left; Ca²⁺ is washed out and the resin is back in its Na form."}],"trap":"\"NaCl, since added Na⁺ shifts it to the right\" picks the right solution with the wrong direction: Na⁺ is a product, so adding it pushes left. Adding Ca²⁺ would push right and load more calcium onto the resin."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's going on.** A water softener swaps \"hard\" calcium ions out of tap water.","marks":[{"t":"hardness ions","note":"Ca²⁺ in tap water"},{"t":"Ca²⁺(aq) + 2 Na–R(s) ⇌ Ca–R₂(s) + 2 Na⁺(aq)","note":"the equilibrium"},{"t":"regenerated","note":"reset the resin"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation left to right (softening):** the resin grabs Ca²⁺ and lets go of Na⁺.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺(aq) + 2 Na–R(s)","note":"left: Ca²⁺ + Na-resin"},{"t":"⇌","note":"goes both ways"},{"t":"Ca–R₂(s) + 2 Na⁺(aq)","note":"right: Ca-resin + Na⁺"}]},{"text":"**R = a negative spot fixed on the resin beads.** It holds whichever positive ion sits on it: Na⁺ or Ca²⁺.","marks":[{"t":"fixed anionic site","note":"negative spot on bead"},{"t":"resin","note":"plastic beads"}]},{"text":"**Saturated = every spot holds Ca²⁺.** To regenerate, we need Na–R back: run the reaction **right to left**.","marks":[{"t":"saturated with Ca²⁺","note":"all spots hold Ca²⁺"}],"calc":["goal: Ca–R₂ + 2 Na⁺ → Ca²⁺ + 2 Na–R","= shift **left**"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** push an equilibrium and it shifts to push back. Add lots of a product → the reaction uses it up by going left.","marks":[{"t":"2 Na⁺(aq)","note":"add lots → shifts left"}],"calc":["Q = [Na⁺]² / [Ca²⁺]  (solids left out)","[Na⁺] × 10 → Q × 100","Q > K → shift **left**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Reaction quotient Q = [Na⁺]²/[Ca²⁺] relative to K","x":{"label":"Condition"},"y":{"label":"Q / K","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["At equilibrium","Na⁺ raised 10×"],"series":[{"name":"Q/K","values":[1,100]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na⁺ raised 10×","Q/K"]]}},{"text":"**So flush with concentrated NaCl (brine).** The flood of Na⁺ pushes Ca²⁺ off the beads and washes it away.","marks":[{"t":"concentrated solution","note":"NaCl: floods Na⁺"},{"t":"flushing","note":"washes Ca²⁺ away"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** CaCl₂ adds a reactant (shifts right, more Ca on resin). Water dilutes and lowers Q (shifts right). Na⁺ shifts left, not right.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺(aq)","note":"adding this → right"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Le Châtelier: add a product → shift left; add a reactant → shift right. Solids are left out of Q and K, but dissolved ions count."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Boron trifluoride and nitrogen trifluoride are drawn below. In each, the central atom forms three single bonds to fluorine. Which of the following compares their molecular geometries?",idStem:"Which molecule has an octahedral shape?",fig:["bf3","nf3"],choices:["Both trigonal planar, since each has three bonded atoms","BF₃ trigonal planar and NF₃ trigonal pyramidal, since N has a lone pair","BF₃ trigonal pyramidal and NF₃ trigonal planar, since B has a lone pair","Both tetrahedral, since each has four electron domains"],ans:1,exp:"B has 3 valence electrons, all used in the three B–F bonds: **3 domains, no lone pair → trigonal planar**. N has 5: three go into bonds and two form **one lone pair → 4 domains, tetrahedral electron geometry, trigonal pyramidal molecular shape**.",src:"pe3-cp:57",how:{"ask":"The drawings do not show lone pairs. How many valence electrons does each central atom have left after making three bonds?","know":"• Skeletal drawings often leave out lone pairs. Add them: **lone-pair electrons on the central atom = valence electrons − electrons used in bonds** (one per single bond from that atom).\n• Electron domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs.\n• 3 domains, 0 lone pairs → **trigonal planar**, 120°.\n• 4 domains, 1 lone pair → electron geometry tetrahedral, **molecular geometry trigonal pyramidal**, ≈107°.\n• Boron is often electron-deficient: only 6 electrons around it in BF₃.","steps":[{"text":"Both drawings show three bonds; what differs is what the central atom has left over.","hl":"the central atom forms three single bonds to fluorine"},{"text":"Add the missing lone pairs by counting valence electrons.","fig":["bf3","nf3"],"calc":["B: 3 valence − 3 in bonds = **0** → 0 lone pairs","N: 5 valence − 3 in bonds = **2** → 1 lone pair","domains: BF₃ = **3**, NF₃ = **4**"]},{"text":"Boron has no lone pair and only three domains, so it is not pyramidal or tetrahedral.","strike":["BF₃ trigonal pyramidal and NF₃ trigonal planar, since B has a lone pair","Both tetrahedral, since each has four electron domains"]},{"text":"NF₃'s lone pair makes four domains: tetrahedral electron geometry, but with only three atoms the shape is a pyramid. It is not planar.","fig":"nf3","strike":["Both trigonal planar, since each has three bonded atoms"]},{"text":"Answer: BF₃ trigonal planar, NF₃ trigonal pyramidal. Rule: always add the lone pairs before naming a shape; molecular shape counts atoms only."}],"trap":"\"Both trigonal planar\" counts only the three fluorines in the drawing. The lone pair on N is invisible in the picture but still takes up space and pushes the F atoms down into a pyramid."},teach:[{"text":"**Reading the drawings.** Letters are atoms; each line is a bond = 2 shared electrons. **Lone pairs** (2 unbonded electrons) are often not drawn; add them yourself.","fig":["t3-bf3-read","t3-nf3-read"]},{"text":"**Valence electrons** are an atom's outer electrons, the ones used in bonds. From the periodic table: B has 3, N has 5.","fig":["t3-bf3-val","t3-nf3-val"]},{"text":"**Boron:** each B–F bond uses one B electron. 3 bonds use all 3, so B has **no lone pair**.","fig":"t3-bf3-lp"},{"text":"**Nitrogen:** 3 bonds use 3 of its 5 electrons. The 2 left over form **one lone pair** on N, though it isn't drawn.","fig":"t3-nf3-lp"},{"text":"**Electron domains** (each bond or lone pair around the center) spread as far apart as possible. 3 domains → a flat triangle, 120°: **trigonal planar**.","fig":"t3-bf3-shape"},{"text":"**NF₃ has 4 domains** (3 bonds + 1 lone pair), arranged like a tetrahedron. Shapes are named by atoms only: N atop three F = **trigonal pyramidal**.","fig":"t3-nf3-shape"},{"text":"**Two traps:** \"three atoms means flat\" forgets nitrogen's hidden lone pair; \"both have four domains\" gives boron a lone pair it doesn't have."},{"text":"**The rule to remember:** add lone pairs first (valence electrons − one per bond), count domains, then name the shape by atoms only. Check each answer's claim for B and for N.","fig":["t3-bf3-lp","t3-nf3-lp"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"A long vertical tube, closed at the top and with its open bottom end in a reservoir of water, has the air pumped out of it, so atmospheric pressure (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa) pushes water up the tube. The density of water is 1000 kg/m³, the density of mercury is 13,600 kg/m³, and g = 10 m/s². To what maximum height can the water rise?",idStem:"Air is pumped out of a tall tube standing in water, and atmospheric pressure (10⁵ Pa) pushes water up into it. How high does the water rise?",choices:["100 m","0.74 m","1.0 × 10⁸ m","10 m"],ans:3,exp:"The water column rises until its pressure at the bottom equals atmospheric pressure: **P = ρgh** → h = P/(ρg) = 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) = **10 m**. One atmosphere supports about 10 m of water (or 0.76 m of mercury).",src:"pe3-cp:58",how:{"ask":"What pressure does the water column have to balance, and how do you turn a pressure into the height of a liquid column?","know":"• **Hydrostatic pressure: P = ρgh**. ρ = liquid density (kg/m³), g = 9.8 ≈ 10 m/s², h = column height (m).\n• With a vacuum above the column, the only push upward is atmospheric pressure at the reservoir surface, so ρgh = P_atm at maximum height.\n• Pa = N/m² = kg/(m·s²).","steps":[{"text":"With vacuum above the water, nothing pushes down from the top. The column rises until its own pressure at the base equals atmospheric pressure.","hl":"has the air pumped out of it","calc":["P_top = **0**","ρ_water g h = P_atm"]},{"text":"Solve for h.","calc":["h = P_atm / (ρg)","h = 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa ÷ (**1000** kg/m³ × **10** m/s²)","h = 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁴ = **10 m**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pressure of the water column vs atmospheric pressure","x":{"label":"Column height h (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Pressure (× 10⁴ Pa)","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ρgh of water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[5,5],[10,10],[12,12]]},{"name":"Atmospheric pressure","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,10],[12,10]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ρgh of water","x":10}],"yLine":10,"spans":[{"x":10,"from":0,"to":10,"text":"ρgh = 10 × 10⁴ Pa","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"ρgh of water","x":10,"text":"meets P_atm at h = 10 m","at":"right"}]},"strike":["100 m","1.0 × 10⁸ m"]},{"text":"The mercury density is for a different fluid: it gives the familiar 0.74 m (760 mm) mercury barometer.","hl":"the density of mercury is 13,600 kg/m³","calc":["h(Hg) = 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (13,600 × 10) = **0.74 m** (not water)"],"strike":["0.74 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 10 m. (Forgetting g gives 100 m; multiplying instead of dividing gives 1.0 × 10⁸ m.)"}],"trap":"0.74 m uses mercury's density (10⁵ ÷ 136,000), but the tube holds water. 100 m forgets g, and 1.0 × 10⁸ m multiplies instead of dividing."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers** and what each one is.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 × 10⁵ Pa","note":"air pressure"},{"t":"1000 kg/m³","note":"water density"},{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Pressure** = push per area (Pa = N/m²). The air above us presses on the reservoir surface with 10⁵ Pa.","marks":[{"t":"atmospheric pressure","note":"weight of the air above"}]},{"text":"**Vacuum on top:** nothing pushes down on the water inside the tube. Air outside pushes water up until the column's weight pushes back equally.","marks":[{"t":"closed at the top","note":"sealed"},{"t":"has the air pumped out of it","note":"nothing pushes down"}]},{"text":"**A column of liquid presses on its base: P = ρgh.** ρ = density, g = gravity, h = height. Taller = more pressure.","marks":[{"t":"density of water","note":"ρ"}],"calc":["P = ρ × g × h"]},{"text":"**Water stops rising when its pressure equals the air's.** Set them equal and solve for h.","marks":[{"t":"maximum height","note":"h: what we want"}],"calc":["ρgh = P_atm","h = **P_atm / (ρg)**"]},{"text":"**Put in the numbers.**","marks":[{"t":"1.0 × 10⁵ Pa","note":"top"},{"t":"1000 kg/m³","note":"bottom"},{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"bottom"}],"calc":["h = 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10)","h = 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁴","h = **10 m**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pressure of the water column vs atmospheric pressure","x":{"label":"Column height h (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Pressure (× 10⁴ Pa)","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ρgh of water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[5,5],[10,10],[12,12]]},{"name":"Atmospheric pressure","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,10],[12,10]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ρgh of water","x":10}],"spans":[{"x":10,"from":0,"to":10,"text":"ρgh = 10 × 10⁴ Pa","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"ρgh of water","x":10,"text":"meets P_atm: h = 10 m","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Mercury is a different liquid.** Its density gives the 0.74 m mercury barometer, not the water height.","marks":[{"t":"13,600 kg/m³","note":"not needed: not water"}],"calc":["h(Hg) = 10⁵ ÷ (13,600 × 10) = 0.74 m ✗ (mercury)"]},{"text":"**Other traps:** forgetting g gives 100 m; multiplying instead of dividing gives 1.0 × 10⁸ m.","marks":[{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"don't forget g"}],"calc":["10⁵ ÷ 1000 = 100 m ✗ (no g)","10⁵ × 1000 = 10⁸ m ✗ (multiplied)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a liquid column rises until ρgh = the pressure pushing it, so h = P/(ρg). One atmosphere holds up about 10 m of water."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 3: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Consider the melting points of some period-2 elements: Li 181 °C; Be 1287 °C; C about 3600 °C, where it sublimes; N₂ −210 °C; O₂ −218 °C. Which element exists in its standard state as a covalent network solid, and why?",idStem:"Which period-2 element forms a covalent network solid in its standard state?",choices:["Nitrogen, since its N≡N triple bonds are very strong","Beryllium, since its high melting point requires strong bonds","Carbon, since each atom bonds covalently to several neighbors","Oxygen, since O=O double bonds link its atoms together"],ans:2,exp:"In diamond and graphite, **each carbon is covalently bonded to 3 or 4 neighbors in an extended network**, so melting means breaking covalent bonds, which explains the ~3600 °C value. N₂ and O₂ are small separate molecules held together only by weak forces.",src:"pe3-cp:59",how:{"ask":"A network solid is one in which strong bonds run through the whole sample. Which element's atoms bond to many neighbors, and which form small separate molecules or metallic solids?","know":"• **Covalent network solid**: atoms linked by covalent bonds in a continuous 3-D (or 2-D) web. Examples: diamond, graphite, SiO₂. Very high melting points.\n• **Molecular solid**: separate molecules (N₂, O₂) held by weak intermolecular forces → low melting points, even when the bonds inside the molecule are strong.\n• **Metallic solid**: cations in a sea of electrons (Li, Be). Melting points vary.","steps":[{"text":"Plot the melting points: carbon is far above everything else.","hl":"C about 3600 °C, where it sublimes","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Melting points of period-2 elements","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Melting point (°C)","min":-500,"max":4000,"step":500},"categories":["Li","Be","C","N₂","O₂"],"series":[{"name":"m.p.","values":[181,1287,3600,-210,-218]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C","m.p."]]}},{"text":"N₂ and O₂ are small discrete molecules. Melting breaks only weak London forces, not the strong N≡N or O=O bonds inside each molecule.","hl":"N₂ −210 °C; O₂ −218 °C","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Melting points of period-2 elements","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Melting point (°C)","min":-500,"max":4000,"step":500},"categories":["Li","Be","C","N₂","O₂"],"series":[{"name":"m.p.","values":[181,1287,3600,-210,-218]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["N₂","m.p."],["O₂","m.p."]]},"strike":["Nitrogen, since its N≡N triple bonds are very strong","Oxygen, since O=O double bonds link its atoms together"]},{"text":"Li and Be are metals: their atoms are held by delocalized electrons (metallic bonding), not directional covalent bonds.","strike":["Beryllium, since its high melting point requires strong bonds"]},{"text":"Answer: carbon, since each atom bonds covalently to several neighbors (4 in diamond, 3 in graphite sheets), so melting means breaking covalent bonds."}],"trap":"\"Nitrogen, since N≡N is very strong\" confuses bonds inside a molecule with forces between molecules. The triple bond is strong, but N₂ molecules are only weakly attracted to each other, so it is a gas. Beryllium's high melting point comes from metallic bonding."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the numbers:** a melting point for each element.","marks":[{"t":"Li 181 °C; Be 1287 °C","note":"metals"},{"t":"C about 3600 °C","note":"carbon: hottest by far"},{"t":"N₂ −210 °C; O₂ −218 °C","note":"gases at room temp"}]},{"text":"**Melting point** = the temperature where a solid comes apart. Higher = the particles are held together more strongly.","marks":[{"t":"melting points","note":"how hard to pull apart"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Melting points of period-2 elements","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Melting point (°C)","min":-500,"max":4000,"step":500},"categories":["Li","Be","C","N₂","O₂"],"series":[{"name":"m.p.","values":[181,1287,3600,-210,-218]}]},"graph":{"focus":["m.p."]}},{"text":"**Covalent bond** = two atoms sharing electrons, a very strong link. **Covalent network solid** = every atom linked this way into one giant web.","marks":[{"t":"covalent network solid","note":"all atoms in 1 bonded web"}]},{"text":"**Sublimes** = goes straight from solid to gas. Carbon needs ~3600 °C because covalent bonds themselves must break.","marks":[{"t":"where it sublimes","note":"solid → gas directly"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Melting points of period-2 elements","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Melting point (°C)","min":-500,"max":4000,"step":500},"categories":["Li","Be","C","N₂","O₂"],"series":[{"name":"m.p.","values":[181,1287,3600,-210,-218]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C","m.p."]]}},{"text":"**In carbon each atom bonds to several neighbors:** 4 in diamond, 3 in graphite sheets. No separate molecules, just one network.","marks":[{"t":"C about 3600 °C","note":"network: bonds must break"}],"calc":["diamond: each C → **4** neighbors","graphite: each C → **3** neighbors"]},{"text":"**N₂ and O₂ are tiny separate molecules.** The N≡N and O=O bonds are strong, but melting only breaks the weak pull **between** molecules.","marks":[{"t":"N₂ −210 °C","note":"separate N₂ molecules"},{"t":"O₂ −218 °C","note":"separate O₂ molecules"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Melting points of period-2 elements","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Melting point (°C)","min":-500,"max":4000,"step":500},"categories":["Li","Be","C","N₂","O₂"],"series":[{"name":"m.p.","values":[181,1287,3600,-210,-218]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["N₂","m.p."],["O₂","m.p."]]}},{"text":"**Li and Be are metals:** positive ions in a sea of shared electrons (metallic bonding), not a covalent network.","marks":[{"t":"Be 1287 °C","note":"metal: sea of electrons"},{"t":"Li 181 °C","note":"metal too"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** very high melting point + nonmetal (C, Si, SiO₂) → covalent network. Strong bonds inside small molecules (N₂, O₂) don't raise the melting point."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 4 · Chem/Phys (built 2026-11-11) ──
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"Heating limestone decomposes it: CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). For this reaction, ΔH° = +178 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +161 J/(mol·K). In a kiln held at 900 K under standard conditions, no net decomposition is observed. Which statement best describes the reaction at 900 K?",idStem:"A decomposition is endothermic and does NOT happen at 900 K. How do ΔH and TΔS compare at that temperature?",choices:["ΔH > TΔS, so ΔG > 0","ΔH < TΔS, so ΔG > 0","ΔH > TΔS, so ΔG < 0","ΔH < TΔS, so ΔG < 0"],ans:0,exp:"TΔS = 900 K × 0.161 kJ/(mol·K) ≈ 145 kJ/mol, which is less than ΔH = 178 kJ/mol. **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS > 0 → not spontaneous**, matching the observation. Above ~1100 K, TΔS would win.",src:"pe4-cp:1",how:{"ask":"Compare the size of ΔH with the size of TΔS at 900 K, then decide what sign ΔG has.","know":"**ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.** ΔG < 0 → spontaneous; ΔG > 0 → not spontaneous.\n• ΔH = heat absorbed (+ = endothermic), usually in kJ/mol.\n• ΔS = entropy change, usually in J/(mol·K); **convert to kJ before combining with ΔH**.\n• T must be in kelvin.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: 'no net decomposition' means the reaction is not spontaneous at 900 K, so ΔG must be positive. Find which term, ΔH or TΔS, is bigger.","hl":"no net decomposition is observed"},{"text":"Put ΔS in kJ before combining it with ΔH, then compute TΔS.","hl":"ΔS° = +161 J/(mol·K)","calc":["ΔS = 161 J/(mol·K) ÷ 1000 = **0.161 kJ/(mol·K)**","TΔS = **900 K** × 0.161 kJ/(mol·K)","TΔS ≈ **145 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Apply ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","calc":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS","ΔG = **178** − **145** kJ/mol","ΔG ≈ **+33 kJ/mol** > 0"],"strike":["ΔH > TΔS, so ΔG < 0","ΔH < TΔS, so ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"Plot ΔG against T: the line stays above zero at 900 K and crosses zero only near 1100 K (178 ÷ 0.161).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ΔG = 178 − 0.161T for CaCO₃ decomposition","x":{"label":"T (K)","min":800,"max":1200,"step":100},"y":{"label":"ΔG (kJ/mol)","min":-20,"max":60,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"ΔG","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[800,49.2],[900,33.1],[1000,17],[1100,0.9],[1200,-15.2]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ΔG","x":900}],"yLine":0,"labels":[{"series":"ΔG","x":900,"text":"900 K: ΔG ≈ +33 (above 0)","at":"right"},{"series":"ΔG","x":1100,"text":"crosses 0 near 1100 K","at":"above"}],"spans":[{"x":900,"from":0,"to":33.1,"text":"ΔG = +33","side":"left"}],"shade":[{"x1":800,"x2":1100,"text":"ΔG > 0: no decomposition"}]}},{"text":"ΔH (178) is larger than TΔS (145), so ΔG > 0. 'ΔH < TΔS' only appears if you skip the J → kJ conversion.","strike":["ΔH < TΔS, so ΔG > 0"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH > TΔS, so ΔG > 0, which matches the absence of decomposition at 900 K."}],"trap":"\"ΔH < TΔS\" comes from skipping the J → kJ conversion: 900 × 161 = 144,900, which looks bigger than 178. Always put ΔH and ΔS in the same energy unit."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"Solid CaCO₃ is heated in a sealed vessel at 1100 K until equilibrium is reached: CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g). An additional 5.0 g of CaO(s) is then added while the temperature is held constant. Which equilibrium expression applies, and how does the partial pressure of CO₂ respond?",idStem:"What is the equilibrium constant for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g)?",choices:["K = [CaO]P_CO₂/[CaCO₃]; P_CO₂ decreases","K = P_CO₂/[CaCO₃]; P_CO₂ is unchanged","K = P_CO₂; P_CO₂ is unchanged","K = P_CO₂; P_CO₂ decreases"],ans:2,exp:"**Pure solids and liquids are omitted from K** because their activity is 1. So K = P_CO₂, and adding more solid CaO changes nothing in the expression: at constant T, P_CO₂ stays the same.",src:"pe4-cp:2",how:{"ask":"Which species belong in the equilibrium expression, and does adding more of a solid shift anything?","know":"An equilibrium constant includes only species whose concentration can change: **gases (partial pressure) and dissolved species (molarity)**. **Pure solids and pure liquids have activity = 1** and are left out. K changes only with temperature.","steps":[{"text":"Label each species' phase: two solids and one gas.","hl":"CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g)"},{"text":"Rule: pure solids and liquids have activity 1 and drop out of K. Only the gas remains.","calc":["K = (a_CaO × P_CO₂) ÷ a_CaCO₃","K = (**1** × P_CO₂) ÷ **1**","K = **P_CO₂**"],"strike":["K = [CaO]P_CO₂/[CaCO₃]; P_CO₂ decreases","K = P_CO₂/[CaCO₃]; P_CO₂ is unchanged"]},{"text":"Adding solid CaO does not change any term in K, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts.","hl":"An additional 5.0 g of CaO(s) is then added","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"P_CO₂ at 1100 K before and after adding CaO(s)","x":{"label":"Condition"},"y":{"label":"P_CO₂ (relative)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"categories":["At equilibrium","After adding 5.0 g CaO"],"series":[{"name":"P_CO₂","values":[1,1]}]},"strike":["K = P_CO₂; P_CO₂ decreases"],"graph":{"focus":["P_CO₂"],"bars":[["At equilibrium","P_CO₂"],["After adding 5.0 g CaO","P_CO₂"]]}},{"text":"Answer: K = P_CO₂, and P_CO₂ is unchanged at constant temperature; only a temperature change could alter it."}],"trap":"\"P_CO₂ decreases\" applies Le Châtelier's principle to a solid. Adding a product only shifts an equilibrium if that product appears in K, and solids do not."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Ammonia gas is bubbled into hydrochloric acid, forming aqueous ammonium chloride: NH₃(g) + HCl(aq) → NH₄Cl(aq). Which statement best describes the nitrogen atom in this reaction?",idStem:"In NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺, what happens to nitrogen's oxidation state?",choices:["Its oxidation state changes from −3 to −4, since it gains a bond to H","Its oxidation state changes from −3 to −2, since it shares its lone pair","Its oxidation state stays −3, since only an H⁺ is transferred","Its oxidation state stays +3, since only an H⁺ is transferred"],ans:2,exp:"In NH₃, x + 3(+1) = 0 → N = −3. In NH₄⁺, x + 4(+1) = +1 → N = −3. **Adding H⁺ is a proton transfer (acid–base), not redox**, so the oxidation state does not change.",src:"pe4-cp:3",how:{"ask":"Calculate nitrogen's oxidation state before and after, and decide what kind of reaction this is.","know":"**Oxidation state rules:** H = +1 (with nonmetals), O = −2, and the oxidation states in a species add up to its overall charge. An acid–base reaction moves H⁺ (a proton with no electrons), so no atom gains or loses electrons.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: you must assign N's oxidation state before and after, then decide whether this is redox or acid–base.","hl":"NH₃(g) + HCl(aq) → NH₄Cl(aq)"},{"text":"Oxidation state in NH₃: H is +1 and the molecule is neutral.","calc":["N + 3(+1) = 0","N = **−3**"]},{"text":"Oxidation state in NH₄⁺: same rule, but the ion's charge is +1.","calc":["N + 4(+1) = +1","N = **−3**"],"strike":["Its oxidation state changes from −3 to −4, since it gains a bond to H","Its oxidation state changes from −3 to −2, since it shares its lone pair"]},{"text":"Nothing changed: H⁺ moved from HCl to N's lone pair. A proton transfer is acid–base, not redox. The +3 value has the wrong sign for N bonded to less electronegative H.","strike":["Its oxidation state stays +3, since only an H⁺ is transferred"]},{"text":"Answer: N stays −3, since only an H⁺ is transferred."}],"trap":"\"−3 to −4\" counts the new N–H bond as an extra electron for N, but the H arrives as H⁺ and the ion's charge rises by +1. Set up the sum with the ion's +1 charge."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"Sodium bicarbonate decomposes on heating: 2 NaHCO₃(s) → Na₂CO₃(s) + H₂O(g) + CO₂(g). If 168 g of NaHCO₃ (M = 84 g/mol) decomposes completely, what volume of CO₂ is produced, measured at STP?",idStem:"A reaction produces 1 mol of gas. What volume does it occupy at STP?",choices:["11.2 L","22.4 L","44.8 L","89.6 L"],ans:1,exp:"168 g ÷ 84 g/mol = 2.0 mol NaHCO₃ → 2 : 1 ratio → 1.0 mol CO₂. **1 mol of ideal gas at STP occupies 22.4 L.**",src:"pe4-cp:4",how:{"ask":"Turn the mass of solid into moles, use the equation's ratio to find moles of CO₂ only, then convert moles of gas to liters.","know":"**Mass → moles:** n = mass ÷ molar mass. **Mole ratio** comes from the balanced equation's coefficients. **At STP (0 °C, 1 atm), 1 mol of any ideal gas = 22.4 L.**","steps":[{"text":"Translate: grams of NaHCO₃ → moles → mole ratio → moles of CO₂ → liters at STP.","hl":"168 g of NaHCO₃ (M = 84 g/mol)"},{"text":"Convert mass to moles.","calc":["n = m ÷ M","n = **168 g** ÷ **84 g/mol**","n = **2.0 mol NaHCO₃**"]},{"text":"Use the coefficients: 2 NaHCO₃ give 1 CO₂.","hl":"2 NaHCO₃(s) → Na₂CO₃(s) + H₂O(g) + CO₂(g)","calc":["n(CO₂) = 2.0 mol × (1 CO₂ ÷ 2 NaHCO₃)","n(CO₂) = **1.0 mol**"],"strike":["44.8 L","89.6 L"]},{"text":"At STP, 1 mol of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L.","calc":["V = n × 22.4 L/mol","V = **1.0 mol** × 22.4 L/mol = **22.4 L**"],"strike":["11.2 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 22.4 L. 44.8 L ignores the 2 : 1 ratio; 11.2 L halves twice."}],"trap":"44.8 L is tempting two ways: skipping the 2 : 1 ratio, or counting the H₂O(g) as well. The question asks only about CO₂."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"A spectrophotometer is zeroed with buffer in cuvette 1. A sample is then read in cuvette 2, which has a scratched wall, giving A = 0.48. When cuvette 2 is filled with buffer only, it reads A = 0.06. What absorbance is due to the analyte, and why?",idStem:"Why must a sample cuvette and a reference cuvette have identical optical properties?",choices:["0.48, since the instrument was zeroed with buffer","0.54, since the scratch scatters light out of the beam","0.06, since only the cuvette difference is measured","0.42, since cuvette 2 itself contributes 0.06"],ans:3,exp:"A blank cancels everything in the light path **only if the blank and sample cuvettes are optically identical**. The scratched cuvette adds 0.06 on its own, so the analyte's absorbance is 0.48 − 0.06 = 0.42.",src:"pe4-cp:5",how:{"ask":"Which part of the 0.48 reading comes from the sample, and which part comes from the container?","know":"Absorbance readings are **relative to the blank**: the instrument subtracts whatever the blank cuvette absorbs or scatters. This works only if the sample cuvette has the **same optical properties** as the blank's. Any extra loss of light from the glass (scratches, smudges) reads as false absorbance.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the blank was read in a different cuvette than the sample, so the zero does not cancel cuvette 2's own absorbance.","hl":"zeroed with buffer in cuvette 1"},{"text":"Measure cuvette 2's contribution: with buffer only, it reads 0.06.","hl":"When cuvette 2 is filled with buffer only, it reads A = 0.06"},{"text":"Absorbances add: sample reading = analyte + cuvette. Subtract the cuvette part.","calc":["A(sample in cuvette 2) = A(analyte) + A(cuvette 2)","0.48 = A(analyte) + **0.06**","A(analyte) = **0.42**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Absorbance contributions in cuvette 2","x":{"label":"Reading"},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":0.6,"step":0.1},"categories":["Buffer in cuvette 2","Sample in cuvette 2","Analyte alone"],"series":[{"name":"A","values":[0.06,0.48,0.42]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Analyte alone","A"]]}},{"text":"0.48 ignores the cuvette, 0.54 adds it instead of subtracting, and 0.06 is the cuvette alone.","strike":["0.48, since the instrument was zeroed with buffer","0.54, since the scratch scatters light out of the beam","0.06, since only the cuvette difference is measured"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.42, since cuvette 2 itself contributes 0.06."}],"trap":"0.48 assumes zeroing removed all background, but the zero was set with a different cuvette. Matched cuvettes are what make the blank subtraction valid."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A plant pigment absorbs most strongly at 625 nm. Approximately how much energy does each absorbed photon deliver? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s; c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s; 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)",idStem:"Light of 625 nm is absorbed. About what is the photon energy? (hc ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m; 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)",choices:["0.50 eV","2.0 eV","3.2 eV","5.0 eV"],ans:1,exp:"**E = hc/λ** = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (625 × 10⁻⁹ m) ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ **2.0 eV**. Shortcut: E (eV) ≈ 1240 ÷ λ (nm).",src:"pe4-cp:6",how:{"ask":"Find the energy of one photon from its wavelength, then express it in electron-volts.","know":"**E = hc/λ.** h = Planck's constant, c = speed of light, λ = wavelength **in meters** (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m). Shorter λ → more energy. To convert J to eV, divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹. Shortcut: **E (eV) ≈ 1240/λ (nm)**.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: photon energy from wavelength, E = hc/λ, then convert J → eV.","hl":"absorbs most strongly at 625 nm"},{"text":"Convert λ to meters and compute E in joules.","calc":["λ = 625 nm = **6.25 × 10⁻⁷ m**","hc = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸ = **2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m**","E = 2.0 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 6.25 × 10⁻⁷","E ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"Convert joules to eV by dividing by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV. Stopping at 3.2 is the joule mantissa, not eV.","calc":["E = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV","E ≈ **2.0 eV**"],"strike":["3.2 eV"]},{"text":"Check with the shortcut E(eV) ≈ 1240 ÷ λ(nm): red light sits near 2 eV on the curve.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy vs wavelength, E ≈ 1240/λ","x":{"label":"λ (nm)","min":400,"max":700,"step":50},"y":{"label":"E (eV)","min":1.5,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"E = hc/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,3.1],[450,2.76],[500,2.48],[550,2.25],[625,1.98],[700,1.77]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":625}],"labels":[{"series":"E = hc/λ","x":625,"text":"625 nm → 1.98 ≈ 2.0 eV","at":"right"}],"shade":[{"x1":600,"x2":700,"text":"red light"}]},"strike":["0.50 eV","5.0 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: about 2.0 eV per photon."}],"trap":"3.2 eV is the joule mantissa with the J → eV conversion skipped. Visible photons (400–700 nm) always carry about 1.8–3.1 eV."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A Bradford assay is calibrated with the protein standards plotted in the figure. A cell lysate gives A = 1.05 at 595 nm. Which procedure would give the most reliable protein concentration for the lysate?",idStem:"A sample reads above the highest standard on a calibration curve. What gives the most reliable reading?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Bradford protein assay standards","x":{"label":"Protein (mg/mL)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Absorbance at 595 nm","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.15],[0.5,0.3],[0.75,0.45],[1,0.6]]}]},choices:["Extend the line to A = 1.05 and read 1.75 mg/mL","Report the lysate as 1.0 mg/mL, the top standard","Dilute the lysate threefold, remeasure and multiply by 3","Read the lysate in a 2 cm cuvette to raise the signal"],ans:2,exp:"The standards only prove the line is straight **up to A = 0.60 (1.0 mg/mL)**. A = 1.05 lies above the highest standard, where the response may bend. Diluting threefold brings the reading to about 0.35, **inside the calibrated range**; read the concentration there and multiply by 3.",src:"pe4-cp:8",how:{"ask":"Does the lysate's absorbance fall inside the range the standards actually cover? If not, how do you bring it inside without guessing?","know":"• A **calibration curve** is trusted only between the lowest and highest standards. Outside that range you do not know whether the line stays straight.\n• Beer's law, A = εbc (ε = molar absorptivity, b = path length, c = concentration), often fails at high A because the detector sees too little light.\n• Fix a high reading by **diluting by a known factor**, measuring inside the range, then **multiplying back** by that factor.\n• On a graph: find the top standard, draw a line across at the sample's A, and see whether it hits the data or empty space.","steps":[{"text":"Find the sample's reading in the question: A = 1.05.","hl":"A = 1.05 at 595 nm"},{"text":"Trace the standards to their top point: the last one is 1.0 mg/mL at A = 0.60. A line across at 1.05 runs above every standard.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Standards","x":1}],"yLine":1.05,"labels":[{"series":"Standards","x":1,"text":"top standard: 1.0 mg/mL, A = 0.60","at":"below"},{"x":1.75,"y":1.05,"text":"lysate A = 1.05","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0.6,"to":1.05,"text":"above every standard","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Past the last standard there are no data, so neither extending the line nor capping at the top standard is reliable. A longer cuvette would push A even higher.","strike":["Extend the line to A = 1.05 and read 1.75 mg/mL","Report the lysate as 1.0 mg/mL, the top standard","Read the lysate in a 2 cm cuvette to raise the signal"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":2,"text":"no standards: no data"}]}},{"text":"Dilute threefold and the new reading lands in the middle of the standards.","calc":["A after dilution = 1.05 ÷ **3** = **0.35**","0.35 lies between 0.30 and 0.45: inside the range","slope = 0.60 ÷ 1.0 = **0.60 per mg/mL**","diluted c = 0.35 ÷ 0.60 ≈ **0.58 mg/mL**","lysate c = 0.58 × 3 ≈ **1.75 mg/mL**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Standards","x":0.5}],"yLine":0.35,"spans":[{"x":1.75,"from":1.05,"to":0.35,"text":"÷ 3"},{"x":0.583,"from":0,"to":0.35,"text":"A = 0.35","side":"left"}],"shade":[{"x1":0.5,"x2":0.75,"text":"between 0.30 and 0.45"}],"labels":[{"x":0.583,"y":0.35,"text":"0.35 ÷ 0.60 ≈ 0.58 mg/mL","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: dilute threefold, remeasure and multiply by 3. Rule: a reading above the top standard is diluted into the range, never extrapolated.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"range of the standards"}]}}],"trap":"Extending the line feels like using the graph, but past the last point there are no data, so 1.75 mg/mL is a guess. Reading the top standard just caps the answer at a number you know is too low."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = more absorbance.** The more blue dye the protein binds, the more light is absorbed and the higher the point.","graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Right = more protein.** Each dot is a standard: a tube of known protein concentration that was measured.","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**The dots form a straight calibration line.** Known protein in, measured absorbance out. This line is your ruler.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Standards","x":0.5,"text":"standards (calibration line)","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**How to use the ruler:** start at a reading on the up-axis, go across to the line, then down to the protein amount. Example: A = 0.30 ↔ 0.5 mg/mL.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Standards","x":0.5}],"spans":[{"x":0.5,"from":0,"to":0.3,"text":"A = 0.30","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**The ruler only exists where there are dots.** The top standard is 1.0 mg/mL at A = 0.60; beyond it nobody checked that the line stays straight.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"calibrated range"}],"labels":[{"series":"Standards","x":1,"text":"top standard: A = 0.60","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Now place the lysate:** its reading, A = 1.05, sits far above the highest dot.","graph":{"yLine":1.05,"labels":[{"x":1.75,"y":1.05,"text":"lysate A = 1.05","at":"below"}],"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":2,"text":"no standards here"}]}},{"text":"**Your job:** pick the procedure that brings the lysate's reading back inside the shaded calibrated range, instead of guessing beyond it.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"calibrated range"}]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5C",q:"Four liquids of nearly equal molar mass, 72 to 74 g/mol, are shown. A mixture of all four is separated by fractional distillation at 1 atm. Which compound collects first in the receiving flask?",idStem:"Why can 1-chlorobutane and 1-butanol be separated by fractional distillation?",choiceFigs:["diethyl-ether","butanal","1-butanol","propanoic-acid"],choices:["Diethyl ether","Butanal","1-Butanol","Propanoic acid"],ans:0,exp:"With the masses nearly equal, dispersion forces are similar, so **the strongest extra attraction decides the boiling point**. Propanoic acid and 1-butanol have O–H groups and hydrogen bond with themselves; butanal has a strong C=O dipole. **Diethyl ether has no O–H and only a weak dipole**, so it boils lowest, about 35 °C, and distills first.",src:"pe4-cp:9",how:{"ask":"Masses are the same, so look at each drawing for an O–H, a C=O or neither. Which one has the weakest attractions between its own molecules?","know":"• Distillation collects the **lowest-boiling** liquid first.\n• Boiling point rises with intermolecular attraction: **dispersion** (grows with size) < **dipole–dipole** (polar bonds like C=O) < **hydrogen bonding** (needs H on O, N or F).\n• To hydrogen bond with itself a molecule needs an **O–H or N–H**; an O with no H can only accept.\n• Carboxylic acids pair up through two hydrogen bonds, so they boil highest of all.","steps":[{"text":"The masses are nearly equal, so dispersion forces are a tie; the boiling order comes from the polar groups.","hl":"nearly equal molar mass, 72 to 74 g/mol"},{"text":"Scan each drawing for an O–H. Propanoic acid and 1-butanol have one, so they hydrogen bond with themselves and boil high.","fig":"1-butanol-oh-hl","strike":["1-Butanol","Propanoic acid"]},{"text":"Compare the two without O–H: butanal has a C=O, a strong dipole; the ether O has two weaker C–O dipoles and no H to donate.","calc":["diethyl ether: no H-bond, weak dipole → bp ≈ **35 °C**","butanal: C=O dipole → bp ≈ **75 °C**","1-butanol: O–H → bp ≈ **118 °C**","propanoic acid: O–H + C=O → bp ≈ **141 °C**"],"strike":["Butanal"]},{"text":"Lowest boiling point distills first.","fig":"diethyl-ether"},{"text":"Answer: diethyl ether. Rule: at equal mass, rank boiling points by O–H > C=O > ether O > hydrocarbon."}],"trap":"Butanal looks like the simplest molecule, but its C=O is strongly polar, so it boils near 75 °C. An ether O has no H and its two C–O dipoles partly cancel, so the ether wins."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Corners and line ends are carbons; H's on carbons are hidden. Letters are other atoms.","fig":["t4-ether-read","t4-butanol-read"]},{"text":"**Distillation:** heat the mix; the liquid with the **lowest boiling point** turns to vapor first and collects first.","fig":["diethyl-ether","propanoic-acid"]},{"text":"**Molecules stick to each other; stickier means a higher boiling point.** These four weigh the same, so size-based stickiness ties. The O groups decide.","fig":["diethyl-ether","1-butanol"]},{"text":"**O pulls electrons harder than C.** That makes a + end and a − end (a **dipole**). Opposite ends of neighbors attract. C=O is a strong dipole.","fig":"t4-butanal-dipole"},{"text":"**An H sitting right on O is very positive** and grips an O on the next molecule. That grip is a **hydrogen bond**, the strongest of these.","fig":"t4-butanol-hbond"},{"text":"**Propanoic acid has both** a C=O and an O–H. Two acid molecules pair up with two hydrogen bonds: stickiest of all.","fig":"t4-propanoic-both"},{"text":"**Diethyl ether's O sits between two carbons: no H to share**, and only a weak dipole. Least sticky.","fig":"t4-ether-noh"},{"text":"**Measured boiling points:** ether ≈ 35 °C, butanal ≈ 75 °C, 1-butanol ≈ 118 °C, propanoic acid ≈ 141 °C. Same order as the stickiness.","fig":["t4-ether-35","t4-propanoic-141"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at equal size, O–H > C=O > O between carbons > only C, H. In each drawing find the O and ask: H on it? double-bonded to C? Least sticky distills first.","fig":["t4-ether-noh","t4-butanol-hbond"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Each alcohol shown is warmed with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, which is orange and turns green when Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(III). Which alcohol would leave the solution orange?",idStem:"Which oxidation is unlikely to occur?",choiceFigs:["cyclohexanol","1-methylcyclohexanol","benzyl-alcohol","3-3-dimethyl-2-butanol"],choices:["Cyclohexanol","1-Methylcyclohexanol","Benzyl alcohol","3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol"],ans:1,exp:"Dichromate oxidizes an alcohol by removing **the H on the carbon that holds the OH** (the carbinol carbon). Primary and secondary alcohols have that H and are oxidized, turning the solution green. In **1-methylcyclohexanol the carbinol carbon is bonded to three carbons and has no H**, so no oxidation occurs and the solution stays orange.",src:"pe4-cp:10",how:{"ask":"Find the carbon bearing the OH in each drawing. Does it still carry a hydrogen that the oxidant can remove?","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon attached to the OH.\n• Classify by how many carbons it is bonded to: 1 → primary (1°), 2 → secondary (2°), 3 → tertiary (3°).\n• Oxidation to C=O needs an **H on the carbinol carbon**: 1° → aldehyde/acid, 2° → ketone, 3° → no reaction.\n• Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange, Cr +6) turning green (Cr³⁺) means an oxidation happened.\n• In skeletal drawings the H atoms are hidden: a carbon with only three lines plus the OH has no H left.","steps":[{"text":"Green means oxidized; you want the alcohol that cannot be oxidized.","hl":"leave the solution orange"},{"text":"Count the carbons on each carbinol carbon. Cyclohexanol: 2, so secondary. Benzyl alcohol: 1, so primary. Both have an H there and turn the solution green.","calc":["cyclohexanol: C on carbinol C = **2** → 2°, has H","benzyl alcohol: C on carbinol C = **1** → 1°, has 2 H"],"strike":["Cyclohexanol","Benzyl alcohol"]},{"text":"Look only at the highlighted carbinol carbon of 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol: two carbon neighbors plus OH, so one H remains. The bulky group is one carbon away and does not matter.","fig":"3-3-dimethyl-2-butanol-hl","strike":["3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol"]},{"text":"In 1-methylcyclohexanol the carbinol carbon has two ring carbons, the methyl and the OH: four bonds, no H.","fig":"1-methylcyclohexanol-hl","calc":["bonds on carbinol C: 2 ring C + CH₃ + OH = **4**","H left on carbinol C = **0** → 3°, no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-methylcyclohexanol. Rule: on the drawing, find the OH carbon and ask whether it still has an H; tertiary means no."}],"trap":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol looks crowded because a tert-butyl group sits next door, but the carbinol carbon itself is bonded to only two carbons and still has an H, so it is a secondary alcohol and is oxidized."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Corners are carbons. **Each carbon makes 4 bonds**; the bonds not drawn are hidden H's.","fig":"t4-cyclohexanol-read"},{"text":"**The color test.** Orange dichromate turns green when it **oxidizes** (strips H's from) an alcohol. **Staying orange = no reaction.**","fig":"cyclohexanol"},{"text":"**Find the carbon holding the OH.** It is called the **carbinol carbon**, and it is the only carbon that matters here.","fig":"t4-cyclohexanol-carbinol"},{"text":"**Oxidation removes the H on O and an H on the carbinol carbon**, making a C=O. No H on that carbon means no C=O can form.","fig":["t4-cyclohexanol-loses","t4-cyclohexanone-new"]},{"text":"**Count hidden H's:** 4 minus carbon neighbors minus 1 for the OH. Cyclohexanol: 4 − 2 − 1 = **1 H**, so it turns green.","fig":"t4-cyclohexanol-1h"},{"text":"**Benzyl alcohol:** 4 − 1 − 1 = **2 H** on the carbinol carbon. It is oxidized too.","fig":"t4-benzyl-2h"},{"text":"**3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol:** 4 − 2 − 1 = **1 H**. The crowded carbon next door does not count; only H's on the carbinol carbon matter.","fig":"t4-dmb-1h"},{"text":"**1-Methylcyclohexanol:** 4 − 3 − 1 = **0 H**. A C=O would need a C–C bond broken, which dichromate does not do.","fig":"t4-mch-0h"},{"text":"**Names:** 1 carbon neighbor = **primary (1°)**, 2 = secondary (2°), 3 = **tertiary (3°)**. Tertiary alcohols cannot be oxidized.","fig":["t4-benzyl-1deg","t4-mch-3deg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon **still has an H**. In each drawing, find the OH carbon, count its carbon neighbors, and ask: any H left?","fig":"t4-cyclohexanol-carbinol"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"The alkene shown has a chlorine atom on one carbon of the double bond, and its two methyl groups lie on the same side of the double bond. What is the name of this compound?",idStem:"CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CH₂CH₃ has its two higher-priority groups (CH₃ on C2, ethyl on C3) on the same side. What is its name?",fig:"2-chlorobut-2-ene-e",choices:["Z-2-chlorobut-2-ene","E-2-chlorobut-2-ene","Z-3-chlorobut-2-ene","E-3-chlorobut-2-ene"],ans:1,exp:"Numbering gives Cl the lower number, so the parent is 2-chlorobut-2-ene. On C2, **Cl (Z = 17) outranks CH₃**; on C3, **CH₃ outranks H**. Cl and the C3 methyl lie on **opposite** sides, so the alkene is **E**, even though the two methyls are cis.",src:"pe4-cp:11",how:{"ask":"On each carbon of the double bond, which attached atom has the higher atomic number, and are those two winners on the same side or opposite sides?","know":"• **E/Z (Cahn–Ingold–Prelog):** on each alkene carbon, rank its two substituents by atomic number of the first atom (Cl 17 > O 8 > C 6 > H 1).\n• Higher-priority groups on the **same side → Z** (zusammen); **opposite sides → E** (entgegen).\n• **cis/trans** only compares identical groups, so it can disagree with E/Z.\n• Number the chain so the double bond gets the lowest number, then the substituent.","steps":[{"text":"The question points at the methyls, but E/Z compares the highest-priority group on each carbon, not the matching groups.","hl":"its two methyl groups lie on the same side"},{"text":"Number the four-carbon chain from the end that gives the double bond and then Cl the lowest numbers: the double bond is C2=C3 either way, and Cl lands on C2, not C3.","calc":["left to right: C=C at **2**, Cl at **2**","right to left: C=C at **2**, Cl at **3**","parent: **2-chlorobut-2-ene**"],"strike":["Z-3-chlorobut-2-ene","E-3-chlorobut-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank each end. C2 carries Cl and CH₃; C3 carries CH₃ and H.","calc":["C2: Cl **17** > C 6 → Cl wins","C3: C **6** > H 1 → CH₃ wins"]},{"text":"The two winners are highlighted: Cl and the C3 methyl sit on opposite sides of the double bond, so the alkene is E.","fig":"2-chlorobut-2-ene-e-hl","strike":["Z-2-chlorobut-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: E-2-chlorobut-2-ene. Rule: find the top-ranked atom on each alkene carbon first, then look at sides; ignore cis/trans of look-alike groups."}],"trap":"The two methyls are on the same side, which looks like 'cis' and tempts Z. E/Z ignores that: Cl beats CH₃ on C2, so compare Cl with the C3 methyl, and they are opposite."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Line ends and corners are carbons; hidden H's fill the rest. The flat zigzag shows which side each group is on.","fig":"t4-clb-read"},{"text":"**A double bond cannot twist**, so groups are locked on one side or the other. Each arrangement is a different compound, named **E** or **Z**.","fig":"t4-clb-locked"},{"text":"**Number the 4-carbon chain** so the double bond (C2=C3) and then Cl get the lowest numbers: Cl sits on **C2**. Base name: 2-chlorobut-2-ene.","fig":"t4-clb-num"},{"text":"**Rank the two groups on C2** by atomic number (protons in the first atom; bigger wins). Cl = 17 beats C = 6.","fig":"t4-clb-c2"},{"text":"**Rank the two groups on C3.** The CH₃ (carbon, 6) beats the hidden H (1).","fig":"t4-clb-c3"},{"text":"**E/Z compares only the two winners.** Same side = **Z** (zusammen, \"together\"). Opposite sides = **E** (entgegen, \"opposite\").","fig":"t4-clb-winners"},{"text":"**Look at the winners here:** Cl and the C3 methyl sit on **opposite sides** of the double bond.","fig":"t4-clb-opposite"},{"text":"**The trap:** the two CH₃'s are on the same side (cis). But cis compares look-alike groups; E/Z compares winners, and one CH₃ lost to Cl.","fig":"t4-clb-methyls"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the chain, find the top-ranked atom on each double-bond carbon, then ask: same side (Z) or opposite (E)? Check each answer's number and letter.","fig":"t4-clb-winners"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"Two forces of 8 N and 5 N act on the same point, and the angle between them can be set anywhere from 0° to 180°. Which of the following could NOT be the magnitude of the net force?",idStem:"Vectors of 8 units and 5 units can point at any angle to each other. Which magnitude can their sum NOT have?",choices:["13 N","9.4 N","3 N","2 N"],ans:3,exp:"The resultant ranges from **|8 − 5| = 3 N** (opposite) to **8 + 5 = 13 N** (parallel). 9.4 N is the perpendicular case, √(8² + 5²). Only 2 N is outside 3–13 N.",src:"pe4-cp:12",how:{"ask":"What are the smallest and largest possible sizes of the sum of two vectors?","know":"When two vectors A and B add, the size of the sum satisfies **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Same direction → largest (A + B).\n• Opposite → smallest (|A − B|).\n• Perpendicular → √(A² + B²).","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the angle can vary, so find the full range of possible resultants; anything outside it is impossible.","hl":"anywhere from 0° to 180°"},{"text":"Extremes: parallel forces add, opposite forces subtract.","calc":["Max (θ = 0°): 8 + 5 = **13 N**","Min (θ = 180°): 8 − 5 = **3 N**"],"strike":["13 N","3 N"]},{"text":"Check a middle case: perpendicular forces combine by Pythagoras.","calc":["R = √(8² + 5²) = √(64 + 25)","R = √89 ≈ **9.4 N**"],"strike":["9.4 N"]},{"text":"The resultant shrinks smoothly from 13 N to 3 N as the angle opens; it never goes below 3 N.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Resultant of 8 N and 5 N vs angle","x":{"label":"Angle between forces (°)","min":0,"max":180,"step":45},"y":{"label":"Net force (N)","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"R = √(89 + 80 cos θ)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,13],[45,12.1],[90,9.4],[135,5.7],[180,3]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"R = √(89 + 80 cos θ)","x":180}],"yLine":2,"labels":[{"series":"R = √(89 + 80 cos θ)","x":0,"text":"max 13 N (θ = 0°)","at":"right"},{"series":"R = √(89 + 80 cos θ)","x":90,"text":"9.4 N (θ = 90°)","at":"right"},{"series":"R = √(89 + 80 cos θ)","x":180,"text":"min 3 N (θ = 180°)","at":"above"},{"x":135,"y":2,"text":"2 N: below the curve, impossible","at":"below"}],"spans":[{"x":170,"from":0,"to":3,"text":"never below 3 N","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: 2 N is below the 3 N minimum, so it could NOT be the net force."}],"trap":"3 N looks too small, but it is exactly the opposite-direction case, so it is allowed. The minimum is the difference, not zero."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"In a nearsighted eye, images of distant objects form in front of the retina. Laser surgery reshapes the cornea so that its central surface becomes flatter. Which change best explains why this corrects the vision?",idStem:"A nearsighted eye focuses too strongly. To correct it, laser surgery flattens the cornea. What does that change?",choices:["The radius of curvature increases, so the focal length increases","The radius of curvature decreases, so the focal length increases","The refractive index increases, so the focal length decreases","The radius of curvature increases, so the focal length decreases"],ans:0,exp:"**Flatter surface = larger radius of curvature = weaker refraction = longer focal length.** The image moves back onto the retina. Surgery changes shape, not the tissue's refractive index.",src:"pe4-cp:13",how:{"ask":"What does 'flatter' mean in terms of curvature, and how does curvature affect how strongly a surface bends light?","know":"Lensmaker idea: **1/f ∝ (n − 1)(1/R)**. n = refractive index, R = radius of curvature, f = focal length.\n• A flat surface has a huge R.\n• Larger R → smaller 1/f → **longer f (weaker focusing)**.\n• Nearsighted eye = too strong (f too short), so it needs weaker focusing.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: images form in front of the retina, so the eye's focal length is too short. The fix must lengthen it.","hl":"images of distant objects form in front of the retina"},{"text":"Flatter surface means a larger radius of curvature.","hl":"its central surface becomes flatter","strike":["The radius of curvature decreases, so the focal length increases"]},{"text":"For a refracting surface, 1/f ∝ (n − 1)/R: larger R bends light less and gives a longer focal length.","calc":["1/f = (n − 1)/R","R ↑ → 1/f ↓ → **f ↑**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Focal length vs radius, simplified single surface, n = 1.376","x":{"label":"Radius of curvature (mm)","min":6,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Focal length (mm)","min":14,"max":26,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"f = R/(n − 1)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[6,16],[7,18.6],[8,21.3],[9,23.9]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"f = R/(n − 1)","x":8}],"labels":[{"series":"f = R/(n − 1)","x":6,"text":"steeper (R = 6): f = 16","at":"right"},{"series":"f = R/(n − 1)","x":8,"text":"flatter (R = 8): f = 21.3","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":8,"from":16,"to":21.3,"text":"f longer"}]},"strike":["The radius of curvature increases, so the focal length decreases"]},{"text":"Surgery changes shape, not the tissue's refractive index, so the index option is out.","strike":["The refractive index increases, so the focal length decreases"]},{"text":"Answer: the radius of curvature increases, so the focal length increases and the image moves back onto the retina."}],"trap":"\"R increases, f decreases\" has the right first half and the wrong second half. Focal length moves in the same direction as R."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"In an electroporation cuvette, two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart are connected to an 8.0 kV pulse. What is the magnitude of the electric field between the plates?",idStem:"8.0 kV is applied across a 4.0 mm gap. What is the electric field?",choices:["2.0 V/m","2.0 × 10³ V/m","2.0 × 10⁶ V/m","32 V/m"],ans:2,exp:"**E = V/d** = 8.0 × 10³ V ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻³ m = 2.0 × 10⁶ V/m. Convert kV → V and mm → m first.",src:"pe4-cp:14",how:{"ask":"For two parallel plates, how is the field related to the voltage and the gap, and what units are needed?","know":"Between parallel plates the field is uniform: **E = V/d**. E = field (V/m), V = potential difference (V), d = separation (m). **k = 10³, m(milli) = 10⁻³.**","steps":[{"text":"Translate: uniform field between parallel plates → E = V/d.","hl":"two parallel plate electrodes 4.0 mm apart are connected to an 8.0 kV pulse"},{"text":"Convert to SI units first.","calc":["V = 8.0 kV = **8.0 × 10³ V**","d = 4.0 mm = **4.0 × 10⁻³ m**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["E = V/d","E = 8.0 × 10³ V ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻³ m","E = **2.0 × 10⁶ V/m**"]},{"text":"2.0 V/m uses kV and mm unconverted, 2.0 × 10³ converts only one unit, and 32 multiplies instead of dividing.","strike":["2.0 V/m","2.0 × 10³ V/m","32 V/m"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 × 10⁶ V/m."}],"trap":"2.0 × 10³ V/m converts kV but leaves the gap in mm. 32 V/m multiplies V × d instead of dividing."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A tattoo-removal laser fires pulses whose start times are separated by 250 ms. Each pulse lasts 10 ns and delivers 0.50 J. What are the pulse repetition frequency and the beam's average power?",idStem:"A laser fires one pulse every 250 ms. What is the pulse frequency?",choices:["4.0 Hz; average power 2.0 W","4.0 Hz; average power 5.0 × 10⁷ W","0.004 Hz; average power 2.0 × 10⁻³ W","1.0 × 10⁸ Hz; average power 5.0 × 10⁷ W"],ans:0,exp:"The period is the start-to-start time, 0.250 s, so **f = 1/T = 4.0 Hz**. **Average power = energy per pulse × pulses per second** = 0.50 J × 4.0 s⁻¹ = 2.0 W. Dividing 0.50 J by 10 ns gives the peak power, 5.0 × 10⁷ W, not the average.",src:"pe4-cp:15",how:{"ask":"Find how often the pulses repeat, then find the energy delivered per second averaged over many pulses.","know":"**Period T** = time for one cycle to repeat (s). **Frequency f = 1/T** (Hz = s⁻¹). Convert to seconds first: 1 ms = 10⁻³ s, 1 ns = 10⁻⁹ s.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the repetition period is the time from one pulse start to the next. The 10 ns is how long each pulse lasts, a different time.","hl":"start times are separated by 250 ms"},{"text":"Frequency is the reciprocal of the period, in seconds.","calc":["T = 250 ms = **0.250 s**","f = 1/T = 1 ÷ 0.250 s","f = **4.0 Hz**"],"strike":["0.004 Hz; average power 2.0 × 10⁻³ W","1.0 × 10⁸ Hz; average power 5.0 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"Picture the pulse train: a 10 ns spike every 0.25 s, dark almost the whole time.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pulse train, not to scale in width","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Power (relative)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Laser output","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[0.01,0],[0.25,0],[0.25,1],[0.26,0],[0.5,0],[0.5,1],[0.51,0],[0.75,0],[0.75,1],[0.76,0],[1,0]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"x":0.25,"y":1,"text":"10 ns pulse","at":"right"},{"x":0.62,"y":0.1,"text":"dark almost all the time","at":"above"}],"shade":[{"x1":0.25,"x2":0.5,"text":"T = 0.25 s → 4 pulses/s"}]}},{"text":"Average power = energy per pulse × number of pulses per second.","hl":"delivers 0.50 J","calc":["P_avg = E × f","P_avg = **0.50 J** × **4.0 s⁻¹**","P_avg = **2.0 W**"]},{"text":"Peak power uses the 10 ns duration and is a different quantity.","calc":["P_peak = 0.50 J ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ s = **5.0 × 10⁷ W**","peak ≠ average"],"strike":["4.0 Hz; average power 5.0 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0 Hz with an average power of 2.0 W."}],"trap":"\"5.0 × 10⁷ W\" is the peak power: 0.50 J ÷ 10 ns. Average power spreads each pulse's energy over the whole 250 ms period, so it is far smaller."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"5E",q:"A dermatologist can deliver laser light to skin either as a continuous beam or as 10-ms pulses separated by 90-ms gaps, at the same peak power. Compared with the continuous beam, the pulsed beam is most likely to:",idStem:"Why use a pulsed laser instead of a continuous one on living tissue?",choices:["deposit more energy per second, since its peak power is higher","change the tissue's refractive index, so less light is absorbed","spread over a larger area, so the light's intensity is lower","keep nearby tissue cooler, since heat diffuses between pulses"],ans:3,exp:"Light is on only 10% of the time, so **average power is lower** and **heat can dissipate during the gaps**, limiting damage to surrounding tissue while each pulse still acts on its target.",src:"pe4-cp:16",how:{"ask":"What happens to the tissue during the 90-ms gaps when no light arrives?","know":"**Peak power** = power while the beam is on. **Average power** = peak × duty cycle (fraction of time on). Heat flows from hot regions to cooler surroundings over time (thermal diffusion). Gaps between pulses let deposited heat spread away before the next pulse.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: same peak power, but the pulsed beam is on only part of the time. Compare energy per second and what happens during the gaps.","hl":"10-ms pulses separated by 90-ms gaps, at the same peak power"},{"text":"Find the duty cycle: the fraction of time the light is on.","calc":["Duty cycle = 10 ms ÷ (10 + 90) ms","Duty cycle = **0.10 = 10%**","P_avg(pulsed) = **10%** of P(continuous)"],"strike":["deposit more energy per second, since its peak power is higher"]},{"text":"Plot both beams: the pulsed beam reaches the same peak, then goes dark for 90 ms in which heat can diffuse away.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Laser power vs time","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":200,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Power (fraction of peak)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[200,1]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[10,1],[10,0],[100,0],[100,1],[110,1],[110,0],[200,0]]}]},"graph":{"focus":["Pulsed"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":10,"text":"on 10 ms"},{"x1":10,"x2":100,"text":"off 90 ms: heat diffuses"}],"labels":[{"series":"Continuous","x":200,"text":"continuous: never off","at":"left"},{"series":"Pulsed","x":110,"text":"same peak power","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Pulsing changes timing, not the tissue's optics or the beam area, so those choices describe nothing in the stem.","strike":["change the tissue's refractive index, so less light is absorbed","spread over a larger area, so the light's intensity is lower"]},{"text":"Answer: the pulsed beam keeps nearby tissue cooler, since heat diffuses between pulses."}],"trap":"\"More energy per second\" confuses peak with average power. The peak is the same, but the beam is off 90% of the time."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Four compounds are shown. Which one, as a pure liquid, has hydrogen bonds between its own molecules in addition to dipole–dipole and dispersion forces?",idStem:"Which intermolecular forces act between NH₃ molecules in the gas?",choiceFigs:["triethylamine","diethylamine","diethyl-ether","acetone"],choices:["Triethylamine","Diethylamine","Diethyl ether","Acetone"],ans:1,exp:"A hydrogen bond between identical molecules needs **both a donor, an H on N, O or F, and an acceptor lone pair**. All four have an acceptor N or O, but **only diethylamine has an N–H** to donate. Triethylamine's N, the ether O and the acetone C=O carry no H.",src:"pe4-cp:17",how:{"ask":"Every drawing has an N or O with lone pairs. Which one also has a hydrogen attached directly to that N or O?","know":"• **Hydrogen bond:** an H bonded to N, O or F (the donor) attracted to a lone pair on another N, O or F (the acceptor).\n• Self-hydrogen-bonding needs a **donor H on N/O/F**. C–H does not count.\n• Molecules with only an acceptor (ethers, ketones, tertiary amines) can H-bond with water, but not with each other.\n• All molecules have dispersion forces; polar molecules add dipole–dipole forces; hydrogen bonding is the strongest kind of dipole–dipole attraction.","steps":[{"text":"The question asks about hydrogen bonds between identical molecules, so each must both donate and accept.","hl":"hydrogen bonds between its own molecules"},{"text":"Check each heteroatom for an attached H.","calc":["triethylamine N: H on N = **0**","diethylamine N: H on N = **1**","ether O: H on O = **0**","acetone O: H on O = **0**"]},{"text":"No donor H means no self-hydrogen-bonding, even with a lone pair present.","strike":["Triethylamine","Diethyl ether","Acetone"]},{"text":"Diethylamine's N–H, highlighted, donates to the lone pair on a neighbor's N.","fig":"diethylamine-nh-hl"},{"text":"Answer: diethylamine. Rule: look for H directly on N, O or F; a lone pair alone only accepts."}],"trap":"Triethylamine has a nitrogen with a lone pair, which looks like the classic hydrogen-bonding atom, but with three ethyl groups there is no N–H left to donate."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. Letters are N (nitrogen) and O (oxygen). An H is drawn only when it sits on N or O.","fig":["t4-tea-read","t4-dea-read"]},{"text":"**Lone pairs:** N and O carry electron pairs not used in bonds. A lone pair is a small negative patch that can attract a + H.","fig":"t4-ether-lp"},{"text":"**Hydrogen bond:** an H on N, O or F is very positive and grips a lone pair on another molecule. That H is the **donor**; the lone pair is the **acceptor**.","fig":"t4-dea-donor"},{"text":"**Between identical molecules, each must do both jobs**, so the compound needs a donor H on N or O, not just a lone pair. H on carbon never counts.","fig":["t4-ether-lp","t4-dea-both"]},{"text":"**Triethylamine:** N's three bonds all go to carbons, so **no H on N**. Acceptor only.","fig":"t4-tea-noh"},{"text":"**Diethyl ether:** O's two bonds go to carbons, so **no H on O**. Acceptor only.","fig":"t4-ether-noh"},{"text":"**Acetone:** O is double-bonded to C, both bonds used, so **no H on O**. Acceptor only.","fig":"t4-acetone-noh"},{"text":"**Diethylamine:** N has an H **and** a lone pair. One molecule's N–H grips the next molecule's N.","fig":"t4-dea-both"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** self hydrogen bonding needs an **H directly on N, O or F**. In each drawing, find every N and O and ask: is an H attached?","fig":["t4-dea-both","t4-tea-noh"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Concentrated aqueous ammonia is about 10 M NH₃. Consider the data: NH₃, Kb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵; NH₄⁺, Ka = 5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰. Approximately what is [NH₄⁺] in this solution at equilibrium?",idStem:"For 10 M NH₃, Kb ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵. About what is [NH₄⁺] at equilibrium?",choices:["7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M","4.2 × 10⁻³ M","1.3 × 10⁻² M"],ans:3,exp:"NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻. **For a weak base, x ≈ √(Kb·C)** = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 10) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻² M. Under 1% of the NH₃ ionizes.",src:"pe4-cp:18",how:{"ask":"Write NH₃'s reaction with water, then find how much NH₄⁺ forms from 10 M of a weak base.","know":"For a weak base B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻: **Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B]**. If x = [BH⁺] = [OH⁻] and x is small compared with C (starting concentration), Kb ≈ x²/C, so **x ≈ √(Kb·C)**. Use Kb because NH₃ is the species you start with; Ka belongs to NH₄⁺.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: NH₃ is a weak base; [NH₄⁺] equals the x formed by NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻.","hl":"about 10 M NH₃"},{"text":"Pick the right constant: the reaction uses NH₃ as a base, so use Kb. Ka of NH₄⁺ is extra data.","hl":"Kb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵"},{"text":"Set up the weak-base approximation x² ≈ Kb·C.","calc":["Kb = x² ÷ (C − x) ≈ x² ÷ C","x² = **1.8 × 10⁻⁵** × **10**","x² = **1.8 × 10⁻⁴**","x = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ **1.3 × 10⁻²** M"]},{"text":"Wrong choices come from forgetting the square root, using Kb alone, or using Ka.","calc":["Kb × C without √ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴","√(Ka × C) = √(5.6 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 7.5 × 10⁻⁵"],"strike":["7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M","4.2 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: 0.013 M ÷ 10 M ≈ 0.13% ionized, far under 5%, so dropping x from C − x was valid."},{"text":"Answer: [NH₄⁺] ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻² M."}],"trap":"1.8 × 10⁻⁴ M is x² (you forgot the square root). 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M uses Ka from the wrong row. 4.2 × 10⁻³ M takes √Kb and leaves out the concentration."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"In a demonstration, a metal can is filled with NH₃(g), a few milliliters of water are injected, and the can is sealed; it collapses within seconds. If the demonstration were repeated with CH₄(g) in place of NH₃, which result would be expected?",idStem:"A can filled with NH₃ gas crushes when its gas dissolves in water. What would happen with CH₄ instead?",choices:["The can collapses, since CH₄ dissolves as readily as NH₃","The can does not collapse, since nonpolar CH₄ is poorly soluble in water","The can collapses, since CH₄ reacts with water to form an acid","The can does not collapse, since polar CH₄ stays in the gas phase"],ans:1,exp:"The can collapses because gas leaves the gas phase and **pressure inside drops**. NH₃ is polar and very water-soluble. **CH₄ is nonpolar** (symmetric tetrahedron), barely dissolves, so the pressure stays nearly the same.",src:"pe4-cp:19",how:{"ask":"Why does the pressure inside the can fall with NH₃, and would the same thing happen with CH₄?","know":"**Like dissolves like:** polar gases (and those that H-bond, like NH₃) dissolve well in polar water; nonpolar gases dissolve poorly. Gas pressure ∝ moles of gas (PV = nRT): if gas molecules dissolve, n drops and P drops, so outside air crushes the can.","steps":[{"text":"Translate the demo: the can collapses because NH₃ leaves the gas phase, so the pressure inside drops below the outside air.","hl":"it collapses within seconds"},{"text":"Link moles of gas to pressure at fixed V and T.","calc":["PV = nRT → P ∝ n","NH₃ dissolves: n(gas) ↓ → **P(inside) ↓**","P(outside) > P(inside) → can crushes"]},{"text":"Now judge CH₄: four identical C–H bonds in a tetrahedron cancel, so CH₄ is nonpolar and barely dissolves in water. It is not polar and does not form an acid.","hl":"with CH₄(g) in place of NH₃","strike":["The can collapses, since CH₄ reacts with water to form an acid","The can does not collapse, since polar CH₄ stays in the gas phase"]},{"text":"Little CH₄ dissolves, so n(gas) and the pressure stay almost constant.","calc":["CH₄: n(gas) ≈ constant → **P(inside) ≈ P(outside)**","→ no collapse"],"strike":["The can collapses, since CH₄ dissolves as readily as NH₃"]},{"text":"Answer: the can does not collapse, since nonpolar CH₄ is poorly soluble in water."}],"trap":"\"Polar CH₄\" has the right outcome but the wrong reason. CH₄ is nonpolar, which is exactly why it doesn't dissolve. Check both halves of the choice."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"The bar graph shows the first four ionization energies of a metal X found in a cell-culture additive. Based on these data, X belongs to which group, and what ion does it most likely form?",idStem:"Ca(OH)₂ and CaCl₂ are compounds of which kind of element?",chart:{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of metal X","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":12000,"step":2000},"categories":["1st","2nd","3rd","4th"],"series":[{"name":"IE","values":[578,1817,2745,11577]}]},choices:["Group 1, forming a 1+ ion","Group 2, forming a 2+ ion","Group 13, forming a 3+ ion","Group 14, forming a 4+ ion"],ans:2,exp:"The energies climb gradually for the first three electrons, then **jump about fourfold at the 4th** (2745 → 11 577 kJ/mol). The 4th electron must come from a filled inner shell, so X has **3 valence electrons**: Group 13, forming X³⁺, as aluminum does.",src:"pe4-cp:20",how:{"ask":"Where is the biggest jump between neighboring bars? The number of bars before that jump is the number of valence electrons.","know":"• **Ionization energy (IE):** energy to remove one electron from a gaseous atom or ion; each later IE is larger.\n• Valence electrons come off with modest increases. Once they are gone, the next electron comes from a **filled inner shell**, closer to the nucleus, so the IE jumps several-fold.\n• Electrons removed before the jump = valence electrons = the ion's charge: 1 → Group 1, 2 → Group 2, 3 → Group 13.","steps":[{"text":"The graph shows four successive ionization energies; find where it breaks.","hl":"first four ionization energies"},{"text":"Look at the 4th bar: it towers over the first three.","graph":{"bars":[["4th","IE"]]}},{"text":"Compare each step as a ratio.","calc":["IE2 ÷ IE1 = 1817 ÷ 578 ≈ **3.1**","IE3 ÷ IE2 = 2745 ÷ 1817 ≈ **1.5**","IE4 ÷ IE3 = 11 577 ÷ 2745 ≈ **4.2** ← biggest break"],"graph":{"focus":["IE"],"bars":[["3rd","IE"],["4th","IE"]]}},{"text":"Three electrons come off before the break, so X has three valence electrons. One or two valence electrons, or four, do not fit.","strike":["Group 1, forming a 1+ ion","Group 2, forming a 2+ ion","Group 14, forming a 4+ ion"],"graph":{"focus":["IE"],"bars":[["1st","IE"],["2nd","IE"],["3rd","IE"]]}},{"text":"Answer: Group 13, forming a 3+ ion. Rule: count the bars before the big jump; that count is the valence electrons and the ion's charge."}],"trap":"Every bar is taller than the last, so it is tempting to call the first rise, 578 → 1817, the jump. That is only about 3×; the real break is the 4th bar, which is where an inner shell is reached."},teach:[{"text":"**Each bar = the energy needed to pull off one more electron.** A taller bar means that electron is harder to remove.","graph":{"focus":["IE"]}},{"text":"**Left to right = 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th electron removed**, one after another from the same atom.","graph":{"focus":["IE"],"bars":[["1st","IE"]]}},{"text":"**Look for one huge jump.** Small rises are normal; a giant jump means you have started pulling electrons from the inner core.","graph":{"focus":["IE"],"bars":[["3rd","IE"],["4th","IE"]]}},{"text":"**Your job:** count how many bars come BEFORE the giant jump. That count is the number of valence electrons, and so the ion's charge.","graph":{"focus":["IE"],"bars":[["1st","IE"],["2nd","IE"],["3rd","IE"]]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Inhibitor A has a measured IC₅₀ of 0.6 nM, and inhibitor B has an IC₅₀ of 0.45 μM against the same enzyme. A collaborator's database records all concentrations in μM. Which value should be entered for A, and how do the two inhibitors compare?",idStem:"Convert 0.6 nM into μM.",choices:["6 × 10⁻⁴ μM; A is about 750 times more potent","6 × 10² μM; B is about 1300 times more potent","6 × 10⁻⁷ μM; A is about 7.5 × 10⁵ times more potent","6 × 10⁻⁴ μM; B is about 750 times more potent"],ans:0,exp:"**1 μM = 1000 nM**, so 0.6 nM = 6 × 10⁻⁴ μM. **A lower IC₅₀ means a more potent inhibitor**: 0.45 μM ÷ 6 × 10⁻⁴ μM = 750, so A is about 750 times more potent.",src:"pe4-cp:22",how:{"ask":"Convert A's IC₅₀ to μM, then compare the two IC₅₀ values in the same unit and decide which inhibitor needs less to work.","know":"Metric prefixes: **m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹.** Going from nano to micro is a step of 10³. A bigger unit means fewer of them: **divide** when converting to a larger unit.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: two tasks, a unit conversion nM → μM, then a potency comparison in the same unit.","hl":"IC₅₀ of 0.6 nM"},{"text":"Convert. μM is the larger unit, so the number gets smaller.","calc":["1 μM = **1000 nM**","0.6 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.0006 μM**","= **6 × 10⁻⁴ μM**"],"strike":["6 × 10² μM; B is about 1300 times more potent","6 × 10⁻⁷ μM; A is about 7.5 × 10⁵ times more potent"]},{"text":"Compare in the same unit: divide the larger IC₅₀ by the smaller.","hl":"0.45 μM","calc":["0.45 μM ÷ 6 × 10⁻⁴ μM","= **750**"]},{"text":"A lower IC₅₀ means less inhibitor is needed for 50% inhibition, so A is the more potent one.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"IC₅₀ values in μM, lower = more potent","x":{"label":"Inhibitor"},"y":{"label":"IC₅₀ (μM)","min":0,"max":0.5,"step":0.1},"categories":["A","B"],"series":[{"name":"IC₅₀","values":[0.0006,0.45]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["A","IC₅₀"]]},"strike":["6 × 10⁻⁴ μM; B is about 750 times more potent"]},{"text":"Answer: enter 6 × 10⁻⁴ μM; A is about 750 times more potent than B."}],"trap":"\"B is about 750 times more potent\" has the right number but the wrong direction: the inhibitor with the smaller IC₅₀ reaches half-inhibition at a lower concentration, so it is the more potent one."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"In acetone, (CH₃)₂C=O, the carbon–oxygen bond is 122 pm long, compared with 143 pm for the C–O bond in 2-propanol. Which bonding description best accounts for the shorter bond in acetone?",idStem:"What bonds connect the C and O in a carbonyl group?",choices:["Two σ bonds between C and O","One σ bond and two π bonds between C and O","One π bond only between C and O","One σ bond and one π bond between C and O"],ans:3,exp:"Acetone's carbonyl is a **double bond = 1 σ + 1 π**. More shared electron pairs pull atoms closer, so C=O is shorter than C–O. **The first bond between two atoms is always σ.**",src:"pe4-cp:25",how:{"ask":"What kind of bond joins C and O in acetone, and what does it consist of?","know":"**σ bond**: head-on orbital overlap; every single bond is one σ. **π bond**: side-by-side p-orbital overlap; added on top of a σ.\n• Single = 1σ.\n• Double = 1σ + 1π.\n• Triple = 1σ + 2π.\nHigher bond order → shorter, stronger bond.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the data say acetone's C–O link is shorter than a C–O single bond; find the bonding that makes it shorter.","hl":"122 pm long, compared with 143 pm"},{"text":"Draw acetone: the carbonyl is a C=O double bond, while 2-propanol has a C–O single bond.","fig":"acetone","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Carbon–oxygen bond lengths","x":{"label":"Compound"},"y":{"label":"Bond length (pm)","min":0,"max":160,"step":40},"categories":["2-Propanol C–O","Acetone C=O"],"series":[{"name":"length","values":[143,122]}]},"graph":{"focus":["length"],"bars":[["Acetone C=O","length"]]}},{"text":"Rule: the first bond between two atoms is σ; each additional bond is π. A double bond is 1σ + 1π.","calc":["Bond order 2 = **1 σ** + **1 π**","Bond order ↑ → bond length ↓"],"strike":["Two σ bonds between C and O","One π bond only between C and O"]},{"text":"One σ plus two π would be a triple bond, as in a nitrile, which acetone does not have.","strike":["One σ bond and two π bonds between C and O"]},{"text":"Answer: one σ bond and one π bond between C and O."}],"trap":"\"Two σ bonds\" is impossible: two atoms can share only one head-on σ overlap; any extra bonds are π. \"1σ + 2π\" is a triple bond."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Consider these ground-state electron configurations: Ar, [Ne]3s²3p⁶; Br, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁵; Co, [Ar]3d⁷4s²; Na, [Ne]3s¹. Which atom most readily gains one electron, and why?",idStem:"Which atom gains one electron most readily?",choices:["Ar, since its 3p subshell can take one more electron","Na, since its half-filled 3s orbital can take one more","Br, since one added electron completes its p subshell","Co, since its partly filled 3d subshell can take one more"],ans:2,exp:"Br is one electron short of a noble-gas configuration (4p⁵ → 4p⁶), so **halogens have large, negative (exothermic) electron affinities**. Ar's 3p is already full; metals like Na and Co tend to lose electrons.",src:"pe4-cp:26",how:{"ask":"Which atom would reach a stable, filled configuration by adding just one electron?","know":"**Electron affinity** = energy change when an atom gains an electron. Most favorable for atoms one short of a filled p subshell (**halogens, Group 17**). A p subshell holds 6 electrons, so p⁶ is full. Noble gases (p⁶) would have to start a new shell; metals have low effective nuclear charge on their valence electrons and prefer to lose them.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: 'most readily gains one electron' means the most favorable electron affinity. Look at what one extra electron does to each configuration.","hl":"Which atom most readily gains one electron"},{"text":"Ar's 3p is already full; an extra electron would start a new shell, which is unfavorable.","hl":"Ar, [Ne]3s²3p⁶","strike":["Ar, since its 3p subshell can take one more electron"]},{"text":"Na and Co are metals; they tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","strike":["Na, since its half-filled 3s orbital can take one more","Co, since its partly filled 3d subshell can take one more"]},{"text":"Br: 4p⁵ + 1 e⁻ → 4p⁶, a filled subshell with a noble-gas configuration. Measured values agree.","hl":"Br, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁵","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Energy released when one electron is added","x":{"label":"Atom"},"y":{"label":"Energy released (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"categories":["Ar","Br","Co","Na"],"series":[{"name":"−EA","values":[0,325,64,53]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Br","−EA"]]}},{"text":"Answer: Br, since one added electron completes its p subshell."}],"trap":"\"Ar, since its 3p subshell can take one more\" misreads 3p⁶. Six electrons is the maximum for a p subshell, so Ar has no room."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"5E",q:"The phase diagram of substance Q is shown. A sample of Q is held at 1.0 atm and −40 °C, where two phases coexist in equilibrium. Which process occurs at this point?",idStem:"On a phase diagram, a point lies on the line between the solid and gas regions. What does it represent?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of substance Q","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-80,"max":40,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-80,0.2],[-60,0.5],[-40,1],[-20,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-20,2],[-18,5],[-16,8]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[-20,2],[0,3.5],[20,5.5],[40,8]]}]},choices:["Boiling, on the liquid–gas line","Melting, on the solid–liquid line","Sublimation, on the solid–gas line","Freezing, at the triple point"],ans:2,exp:"Reading 1.0 atm across to −40 °C lands **on the solid–gas line**, which lies below the triple point at about 2 atm and −20 °C. Along that line solid and gas coexist, so the process is **sublimation**. At 1 atm, Q has no liquid phase at all.",src:"pe4-cp:27",how:{"ask":"Find −40 °C on the x-axis and 1.0 atm on the y-axis. Which curve passes through that point, and which two phases does it separate?","know":"• A **phase diagram** maps pressure (y) against temperature (x). Each region is one phase: solid at low T/high P, gas at high T/low P, liquid in between.\n• Each **line** is where two phases coexist: solid–gas = sublimation/deposition, solid–liquid = melting/freezing, liquid–gas = boiling/condensation.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet. Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so a heated solid sublimes.","steps":[{"text":"Note the conditions to locate on the diagram.","hl":"1.0 atm and −40 °C"},{"text":"Go to −40 °C and up to 1.0 atm: the point sits on the solid–gas curve.","graph":{"focus":["Solid–gas"],"read":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-40}],"xLine":-40,"yLine":1,"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-40,"text":"−40 °C, 1.0 atm: on the solid–gas line","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Find the triple point where the three curves meet: about 2.0 atm and −20 °C. The solid–liquid and liquid–gas lines both start there, above 1 atm.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":-20}],"yLine":2,"labels":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":-20,"text":"triple point ≈ 2.0 atm, −20 °C","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":-30,"from":1,"to":2,"text":"2.0 > 1.0 atm","side":"left"}]},"calc":["triple point P ≈ **2.0 atm** > 1.0 atm","so at 1.0 atm the liquid region is never reached"]},{"text":"A point at 1.0 atm cannot be on a line that only exists above 2 atm, and it is not the triple point.","strike":["Boiling, on the liquid–gas line","Melting, on the solid–liquid line","Freezing, at the triple point"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":-80,"x2":-20,"text":"below the triple point: solid ↔ gas only"}]}},{"text":"Answer: sublimation, on the solid–gas line. Rule: read which curve the point sits on; a pressure below the triple point means solid goes straight to gas.","graph":{"focus":["Solid–gas"],"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-40,"text":"sublimation","at":"right"}]}}],"trap":"Melting is the usual thing a solid does when warmed, but at 1.0 atm Q is below its triple-point pressure, so the solid–liquid line never reaches 1 atm and the solid turns straight to gas."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = more pressure** pushing on the sample.","graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Right = hotter.** Each point on the map is one temperature and pressure.","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Three lines = three borders.** Each line separates two phases: solid–gas, solid–liquid and liquid–gas.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-60,"text":"solid–gas line","at":"right"},{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-18,"text":"solid–liquid line","at":"left"},{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":20,"text":"liquid–gas line","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Between the lines are the phases.** Cold and high pressure = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; the wedge in between = liquid. **ON a line, the two neighbors coexist.**","graph":{"labels":[{"x":-60,"y":5,"text":"SOLID"},{"x":15,"y":7.5,"text":"LIQUID"},{"x":20,"y":1.5,"text":"GAS"}]}},{"text":"**The triple point** is where all three lines meet: the only spot where solid, liquid and gas coexist.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":-20,"text":"triple point","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**How to place a sample:** go right to its temperature, then up to its pressure. Example: 0 °C and 3.5 atm lands on the liquid–gas line, so liquid and gas coexist there (boiling).","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":0}],"labels":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":0,"text":"0 °C, 3.5 atm","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Crossing a line = a process:** solid–liquid = melting/freezing, liquid–gas = boiling/condensing, solid–gas = sublimation/deposition.","graph":{"focus":["Solid–gas","Solid–liquid","Liquid–gas"]}},{"text":"**Your job:** find −40 °C and 1.0 atm, see which line the point sits on, and name that line's process.","graph":{"xLine":-40,"yLine":1}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"An atom has energy levels at −10.0 eV, −6.0 eV, −4.0 eV and −3.5 eV. Which downward transition emits the photon with the longest wavelength?",idStem:"Which electron transition gives off the photon with the LONGEST wavelength?",choices:["−3.5 eV → −10.0 eV","−3.5 eV → −4.0 eV","−4.0 eV → −10.0 eV","−6.0 eV → −10.0 eV"],ans:1,exp:"**E = hc/λ**, so the longest wavelength comes from the **smallest energy gap**: −3.5 → −4.0 eV, ΔE = 0.5 eV.",src:"pe4-cp:28",how:{"ask":"How is a photon's wavelength related to the energy difference between the two levels?","know":"An electron dropping from a higher to a lower level emits a photon with **E = ΔE between the levels**. **E = hc/λ**, so wavelength and energy are inversely related: **small gap → long wavelength**; big gap → short wavelength.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the longest wavelength means the smallest photon energy, because E = hc/λ.","hl":"the longest wavelength"},{"text":"Compute each transition's energy gap.","calc":["−3.5 → −10.0: ΔE = **6.5 eV**","−3.5 → −4.0: ΔE = **0.5 eV**","−4.0 → −10.0: ΔE = **6.0 eV**","−6.0 → −10.0: ΔE = **4.0 eV**"]},{"text":"Compare the gaps: λ ∝ 1/ΔE, so the smallest bar gives the longest wavelength.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Energy gap of each transition","x":{"label":"Transition"},"y":{"label":"ΔE (eV)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"categories":["−3.5 → −10.0","−3.5 → −4.0","−4.0 → −10.0","−6.0 → −10.0"],"series":[{"name":"ΔE","values":[6.5,0.5,6,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["−3.5 → −4.0","ΔE"]]},"strike":["−3.5 eV → −10.0 eV","−4.0 eV → −10.0 eV","−6.0 eV → −10.0 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: −3.5 eV → −4.0 eV, the smallest gap. The 6.5 eV jump gives the shortest wavelength, not the longest."}],"trap":"−3.5 eV → −10.0 eV is the biggest drop, which gives the highest energy and so the shortest wavelength. Wavelength runs opposite to energy."}},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The drug candidate shown contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled I through IV. Which labeled nitrogen belongs to a tertiary amine?",idStem:"A nitrogen is bonded to three carbons, none of which is a C=O. What is the functional group?",fig:"drug-nitrogens-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"A **tertiary amine N** has single bonds to **three carbons, none of them a C=O carbon**, and no H. N-II fits. N-I also has three carbons, but one is a **carbonyl carbon, so it is a tertiary amide**; N-III has an H and two carbons, a **secondary amine**; N-IV is **double-bonded to carbon, an imine**.",src:"pe4-cp:29",how:{"ask":"For each labeled N, how many carbons are attached, is any of them a C=O carbon, and is any bond to N a double bond?","know":"• **Amine:** N single-bonded only to C or H, and none of those carbons is C=O.\n• **Amide:** N bonded to a carbonyl carbon, N–C(=O).\n• **Imine:** C=N double bond.\n• Amines are **primary, secondary or tertiary** by the number of carbons on N: 1, 2 or 3. In skeletal drawings, an N with three lines and no H drawn has three carbons; 'NH' with two lines has two.","steps":[{"text":"You need an N with three carbons and no carbonyl next door.","hl":"tertiary amine"},{"text":"Look at each labeled N and what it touches.","fig":"drug-nitrogens-sites","calc":["I: C=O carbon + CH₃ + CH₂ → **3 C, one is C=O**","II: CH₃ + CH₂ + CH₂ → **3 C, no C=O**","III: CH₂ + CH₂ + H → **2 C**","IV: C=N double bond → **imine**"]},{"text":"N-I sits on a carbonyl carbon, so it is an amide; N-IV has a double bond to C, so it is an imine.","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"N-III carries an H and only two carbons: a secondary amine.","strike":["III"]},{"text":"N-II, highlighted, has three plain carbon neighbors and no H. Answer: II. Rule: count carbons on N, then check whether any is a C=O.","fig":"drug-nitrogens-ii"}],"trap":"N-I also has three carbon neighbors, so it looks tertiary, but one of them carries a C=O. That makes it a tertiary amide, a different functional group with a non-basic N."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. N is nitrogen; \"NH\" means an H on N. I–IV label the four nitrogens to judge.","fig":"drug-nitrogens-sites"},{"text":"**A neutral N makes 3 bonds** (a double line counts as 2). Count the lines at each N; if fewer than 3 go to carbons, the rest are H's.","fig":"t4-drug-count"},{"text":"**Three N families.** Amine: only single bonds to C or H, no C=O beside it. **Amide**: next to a C=O. **Imine**: double bond to C.","fig":"t4-drug-families"},{"text":"**Amines are ranked by carbons on N:** 1 = primary, 2 = secondary, **3 = tertiary** (and then no H).","fig":"t4-drug-ccount"},{"text":"**N-I** has 3 carbons, but one carries a C=O. That makes it an **amide**, not an amine.","fig":"t4-drug-n1"},{"text":"**N-III** is \"NH\": an H plus 2 carbons, a **secondary amine**.","fig":"t4-drug-n3"},{"text":"**N-IV** has a double bond to carbon: an **imine**.","fig":"t4-drug-n4"},{"text":"**N-II:** single bonds to a CH₃ and two CH₂'s. Three carbons, no H, no C=O beside it.","fig":"t4-drug-n2"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each N, check for C=N (imine), then a C=O neighbor (amide), then count carbons. Ask of each labeled N: three plain carbons, no H?","fig":"drug-nitrogens-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Ethyl 5-oxohexanoate, shown, is treated with NaBH₄, which reduces ketones but not esters. The new hydroxyl group then attacks the ester carbon within the same molecule, releasing ethanol. Which structure is the organic product?",idStem:"NaBH₄ reduces a ketone to an alcohol, and that OH then attacks an ester in the same molecule. What forms?",fig:"ethyl-5-oxohexanoate",choiceFigs:["delta-hexalactone","gamma-hexalactone","epsilon-caprolactone","hexane-1-5-diol"],choices:["Six-membered lactone","Five-membered lactone","Seven-membered lactone","Diol"],ans:0,exp:"NaBH₄ turns the C5 ketone into a C5 alcohol. That O attacks C1, the ester carbon. The ring contains **C1, C2, C3, C4, C5 and the O: six atoms**, so the product is a **six-membered lactone** (a cyclic ester) with a methyl on the carbon next to the ring O.",src:"pe4-cp:30",how:{"ask":"After the ketone becomes an OH, count every atom in the loop from the ester carbon to that OH oxygen. How many atoms are in the ring?","know":"• **NaBH₄** is a mild hydride donor: it reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols but leaves esters alone.\n• **Lactone** = cyclic ester, formed when an OH and an ester or acid in the same molecule react; the leaving alcohol (here ethanol) departs.\n• **Ring size** = number of atoms in the ring: the carbonyl carbon, every chain carbon to the OH carbon, and the ring O.\n• Five- and six-membered rings form most easily.","steps":[{"text":"Only the ketone is reduced; the ester stays to be attacked.","hl":"reduces ketones but not esters"},{"text":"So the ester is not reduced, and both carbonyls cannot become alcohols.","strike":["Diol"]},{"text":"After reduction, trace the ring that forms: from the ester carbon along the chain to the new OH oxygen, highlighted.","fig":"ethyl-5-hydroxyhexanoate-ring"},{"text":"Count ring atoms.","calc":["carbons: C1 ester, C2, C3, C4, C5 = **5**","ring oxygen = **1**","ring size = 5 + 1 = **6**"],"strike":["Five-membered lactone","Seven-membered lactone"]},{"text":"Answer: the six-membered lactone, with the methyl beside the ring O.","fig":"delta-hexalactone"},{"text":"Rule: ring size = carbonyl carbon through OH carbon, plus one for the ring O."}],"trap":"The five-membered lactone has the same formula and looks familiar, but it would need the OH on C4. Count the ring O as a ring atom: C1 through C5 plus O makes six."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. O is oxygen. A double line is a double bond; C=O is called a **carbonyl**.","fig":"t4-oxo-read"},{"text":"**Two kinds of C=O.** A **ketone** C=O sits between two carbons. An **ester** C=O carbon also holds a second O, which leads on to an ethyl group.","fig":"t4-oxo-two"},{"text":"**Number the chain from the ester carbon:** C1, C2, C3, C4, then the ketone carbon is **C5** and the end CH₃ is C6.","fig":"t4-oxo-num"},{"text":"**NaBH₄ hands out H⁻** (hydrogen with two electrons). The ketone becomes an alcohol, CH–OH. It is too weak to touch the ester.","fig":"t4-hydroxy-new"},{"text":"**The new OH's oxygen bonds to C1**, made electron-poor by its two O's. The O–ethyl piece leaves as ethanol, and the chain closes into a ring.","fig":"t4-hydroxy-attack"},{"text":"**A ring containing an ester is a lactone.** Count its atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4, C5 and the ring O: **6**.","fig":"t4-lactone-count"},{"text":"**C6 was never on the path**, so it hangs off the ring on C5, right beside the ring O.","fig":"t4-lactone-methyl"},{"text":"**Other shapes need other starting points:** a 5-atom ring needs the OH on C4, a 7-atom ring on C6. A diol would need the ester reduced too.","fig":["t4-gamma-count","t4-eps-count"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ring size = carbonyl carbon through the OH carbon, **plus 1 for the ring O**. In each drawing, count ring atoms, find the CH₃, and ask: is the ester C=O still there?","fig":"t4-lactone-count"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Morpholine is supplied as its hydrochloride salt and is meant to displace bromide from 1-bromobutane. The chemist first stirs the salt with K₂CO₃, whose conjugate acid HCO₃⁻ has pKa = 10.3. Which species then attacks the 1-bromobutane?",idStem:"Base is added to an ammonium salt (R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻) before an SN2 step. Why?",choiceFigs:["morpholinium","morpholine","morpholine-anion","1-bromobutyl-carbanion"],choices:["Morpholinium ion","Morpholine","Morpholine anion","Bromobutyl carbanion"],ans:1,exp:"In the salt, nitrogen's lone pair is tied up in an N–H bond, so it cannot attack. Carbonate is a strong enough base to remove that H⁺ (**pKa 8.4 < 10.3**), giving **neutral morpholine with a free lone pair on N**, which attacks the C–Br carbon. Carbonate cannot remove a second H from N (pKa ≈ 36) or a C–H (pKa > 40).",src:"pe4-cp:32",how:{"ask":"Which drawing has a nitrogen lone pair available, and can carbonate actually reach that form from the salt?","know":"• A **nucleophile** donates a lone pair to an electron-poor carbon; here the C bonded to Br.\n• R₂NH₂⁺ (an ammonium ion) has **no lone pair** on N, so it cannot attack.\n• A base removes a proton only when **its conjugate acid has a higher pKa** than the acid being deprotonated.\n• Approximate pKa values: R₂NH₂⁺ ≈ 8–11, HCO₃⁻ 10.3, R₂NH (to R₂N⁻) ≈ 36, C–H > 40.","steps":[{"text":"The comparison number is given: the base's conjugate acid has pKa 10.3.","hl":"HCO₃⁻ has pKa = 10.3"},{"text":"Compare every proton carbonate might try to remove.","calc":["morpholinium N–H⁺: pKa ≈ **8.4** < 10.3 → removed","morpholine N–H: pKa ≈ **36** > 10.3 → not removed","C–H next to Br: pKa > **40** → not removed"]},{"text":"So carbonate stops after one proton: no amide anion, no carbanion.","strike":["Morpholine anion","Bromobutyl carbanion"]},{"text":"The salt itself has four bonds on N and no lone pair, so it cannot attack.","strike":["Morpholinium ion"]},{"text":"Neutral morpholine has its lone pair back on N, highlighted, ready to attack the carbon bearing Br.","fig":"morpholine-n-hl"},{"text":"Answer: morpholine. Rule: an ammonium salt must be deprotonated to the neutral amine before N can act as a nucleophile."}],"trap":"The morpholine anion looks like a stronger nucleophile, but carbonate is about 10²⁶ times too weak a base to make it. The base frees the lone pair; it does not create a negative charge."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Each is a six-atom ring; corners are carbons with hidden H's. A + sign means one electron short.","fig":["t4-morph-salt-read","t4-morph-read"]},{"text":"**The goal:** an N must bond to the carbon holding Br. The attacker is a **nucleophile**: an atom using a spare electron pair (**lone pair**) to grab an electron-poor atom.","fig":"t4-bromobutane-target"},{"text":"**The salt cannot attack:** its N has 4 bonds, and its lone pair is used up holding an extra H⁺.","fig":"t4-morph-salt-nolp"},{"text":"**A base pulls off H⁺.** **pKa** = how tightly an acid holds its H⁺; lower lets go easier. Carbonate removes an H⁺ only if its pKa is below **10.3**.","fig":"t4-morph-salt-pka"},{"text":"**8.4 < 10.3, so that H⁺ comes off.** Neutral morpholine has its **lone pair back** on N.","fig":"t4-morph-lp"},{"text":"**The next N–H has pKa ≈ 36, far above 10.3**, so carbonate cannot remove it: no N⁻ anion forms.","fig":["t4-morph-pka36","morpholine-anion"]},{"text":"**A C–H on bromobutane has pKa above 40**: no carbanion (− charged carbon) forms either.","fig":["t4-bromobutane-pka40","1-bromobutyl-carbanion"]},{"text":"**The attack:** morpholine's lone pair bonds to the CH₂, and Br leaves as Br⁻.","fig":["t4-morph-attacks","t4-butylmorpholine"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** N attacks only with a **free lone pair**; a base removes only an H⁺ with lower pKa. For each drawing ask: does carbonate make it, and is its N lone pair free?","fig":"t4-morph-lp"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Initial rates of an enzyme are measured with and without inhibitor I, and the data are plotted in double-reciprocal form as shown. Which type of inhibition does I show, and what is its effect on Vmax?",idStem:"An inhibitor can bind an enzyme both before and after the substrate binds, without blocking substrate binding. What type is it?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Double-reciprocal plot with and without inhibitor I","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.2,"max":0.6,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.2,0],[-0.05,0.075],[0,0.1],[0.2,0.2],[0.4,0.3],[0.6,0.4]]},{"name":"+ Inhibitor I","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.1,0],[-0.05,0.075],[0,0.15],[0.2,0.45],[0.4,0.75],[0.6,1.05]]}]},choices:["Competitive; Vmax is unchanged","Noncompetitive; Vmax decreases","Mixed; Vmax decreases","Uncompetitive; Vmax decreases"],ans:2,exp:"The y-intercept, 1/Vmax, rises from 0.10 to 0.15, so **Vmax falls from 10 to about 6.7 μM/s**. The x-intercept, −1/KM, moves from −0.2 to −0.1, so **KM rises from 5 to 10 μM**. The lines cross **left of the y-axis and above the x-axis**, at about (−0.05, 0.075): that pattern is **mixed inhibition**.",src:"pe4-cp:34",how:{"ask":"Where do the two lines cross: on the y-axis, on the x-axis, somewhere else, or never? Then read the y-intercepts to see what happens to Vmax.","know":"• **Lineweaver–Burk plot:** 1/v against 1/[S]. y-intercept = **1/Vmax**; x-intercept = **−1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Where the lines cross tells the type:\n  – on the y-axis → **competitive** (Vmax same, KM up);\n  – on the x-axis → **noncompetitive** (Vmax down, KM same);\n  – parallel lines → **uncompetitive** (both down);\n  – elsewhere, left of the y-axis → **mixed** (Vmax down, KM changes).","steps":[{"text":"The figure is a double-reciprocal plot: read intercepts, not curves.","hl":"double-reciprocal form","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":-0.2,"x2":0,"text":"extended lines (intercepts)"}]}},{"text":"Read the y-intercepts at 1/[S] = 0.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0},{"series":"+ Inhibitor I","x":0}],"xLine":0,"spans":[{"x":-0.03,"from":0,"to":0.1,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.10","side":"left"},{"x":0.03,"from":0,"to":0.15,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.15"}]},"calc":["no inhibitor: 1/Vmax = 0.10 → Vmax = **10 μM/s**","with I: 1/Vmax = 0.15 → Vmax ≈ **6.7 μM/s**"],"strike":["Competitive; Vmax is unchanged"]},{"text":"Read the x-intercepts, where 1/v = 0.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.2},{"series":"+ Inhibitor I","x":-0.1}],"yLine":0,"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.2,"text":"−1/KM = −0.2","at":"above"},{"series":"+ Inhibitor I","x":-0.1,"text":"−1/KM = −0.1","at":"below"}]},"calc":["no inhibitor: −1/KM = −0.2 → KM = **5 μM**","with I: −1/KM = −0.1 → KM = **10 μM**"],"strike":["Noncompetitive; Vmax decreases"]},{"text":"The lines are not parallel; they cross at about (−0.05, 0.075), left of the y-axis and above the x-axis.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.05}],"xLine":-0.05,"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.05,"text":"cross ≈ (−0.05, 0.075)","at":"above"}],"shade":[{"x1":-0.2,"x2":0,"text":"left of the y-axis"}]},"strike":["Uncompetitive; Vmax decreases"]},{"text":"Answer: mixed; Vmax decreases. Rule: crossing on y-axis = competitive, on x-axis = noncompetitive, parallel = uncompetitive, anywhere else = mixed.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.05,"text":"not on an axis → mixed","at":"above"}]}}],"trap":"Noncompetitive inhibition also lowers Vmax, but its lines meet exactly on the x-axis because KM is unchanged. Here the x-intercepts differ, so KM changed too."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = 1/v, upside-down speed.** The SLOWER the reaction, the HIGHER the point.","graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Right = 1/[S]: LESS substrate as you go right.** Left of zero is not measured; the lines are just extended there to reach the axes.","graph":{"axis":"x","shade":[{"x1":-0.2,"x2":0,"text":"extended (not measured)"}]}},{"text":"**Two straight lines:** the enzyme alone, and the enzyme with inhibitor I.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0.6,"text":"no inhibitor","at":"below"},{"series":"+ Inhibitor I","x":0.4,"text":"+ inhibitor I","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Where a line hits the up-axis = 1/Vmax.** Higher intercept → smaller Vmax. Example on the no-inhibitor line:","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor"],"spans":[{"x":0,"from":0,"to":0.1,"text":"1/Vmax","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0,"text":"y-intercept","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Where a line hits the across-axis (1/v = 0) = −1/KM.** Farther left → smaller KM.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor"],"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.2,"text":"x-intercept = −1/KM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Inhibitor type = where the two lines cross.** On the y-axis = competitive; on the x-axis = noncompetitive; parallel = uncompetitive; anywhere else = mixed.","graph":{"xLine":0,"yLine":0,"focus":["No inhibitor","+ Inhibitor I"]}},{"text":"**Your job:** compare the two y-intercepts (did Vmax change?) and find where the lines cross relative to both axes.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","+ Inhibitor I"],"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0},{"series":"+ Inhibitor I","x":0}]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"In kinetic studies of a two-substrate enzyme, substrate B shows no detectable binding to free enzyme but binds tightly once A is bound. No product is released until both A and B are bound. This mechanism is best described as:",idStem:"An enzyme must bind substrate A before it can bind substrate B. What is the binding mechanism called?",choices:["ordered sequential, since A must bind before B","random sequential, since both bind before any product leaves","ping-pong, since B binds only after A has bound","ping-pong, since a product leaves before B binds"],ans:0,exp:"Both substrates bind before any product leaves → **sequential**. B can bind only after A → **ordered**. Ping-pong would release a first product before the second substrate binds.",src:"pe4-cp:35",how:{"ask":"Do both substrates bind before a product leaves, and does the order of binding matter?","know":"Two-substrate mechanisms:\n• **Sequential**: both substrates bind (forming a ternary complex) before any product is released. It is **ordered** if one substrate must bind first, and **random** if either can.\n• **Ping-pong (double displacement)**: the first product leaves before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"Translate the first clue: no product leaves until both substrates are bound → sequential, not ping-pong.","hl":"No product is released until both A and B are bound","strike":["ping-pong, since B binds only after A has bound","ping-pong, since a product leaves before B binds"]},{"text":"Second clue: B cannot bind free enzyme, only E·A, so the order is fixed.","hl":"B shows no detectable binding to free enzyme but binds tightly once A is bound"},{"text":"Write the pathway.","calc":["E + A → **E·A**","E·A + B → **E·A·B**","E·A·B → E + P + Q"],"strike":["random sequential, since both bind before any product leaves"]},{"text":"Answer: ordered sequential, since A must bind before B. 'Random' would allow B to bind free E first."}],"trap":"\"Ping-pong, since B binds only after A\" mistakes the order of binding for ping-pong. The stem rules ping-pong out: no product leaves before both substrates are bound."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the clues.** This enzyme grabs two molecules, A and B, and turns them into products. Two sentences tell you how.","marks":[{"t":"two-substrate enzyme","note":"joins 2 molecules"},{"t":"substrate B","note":"molecule #2"},{"t":"No product is released","note":"nothing leaves early"}]},{"text":"**Words first.** Substrate = a molecule the enzyme works on. Product = what comes out. **Free enzyme** = enzyme with nothing bound yet.","marks":[{"t":"substrate B","note":"a substrate"},{"t":"free enzyme","note":"enzyme, nothing on it yet"},{"t":"product","note":"what comes out"}]},{"text":"**Two families of 2-substrate mechanisms.** Sequential: both bind, THEN products leave. Ping-pong: a product leaves BEFORE the second substrate binds.","calc":["Sequential: E + A + B → E·A·B → **products leave**","Ping-pong: E + A → E* + **P leaves** → E* + B → Q"],"marks":[{"t":"This mechanism","note":"which family?"}]},{"text":"**Clue 1:** nothing leaves until A and B are both on the enzyme → **sequential**, not ping-pong.","marks":[{"t":"No product is released until both A and B are bound","note":"both on first = sequential"}]},{"text":"**Sequential comes in two kinds.** Ordered = a fixed order (A must come first). Random = either one can bind first.","calc":["Ordered: E → E·A → E·A·B (only this way)","Random: E → E·A or E·B → E·A·B (either way)"],"marks":[{"t":"best described","note":"ordered or random?"}]},{"text":"**Clue 2:** B cannot stick to the empty enzyme; it only sticks after A is on. The order is fixed → **ordered**.","marks":[{"t":"no detectable binding to free enzyme","note":"B can't bind empty enzyme"},{"t":"binds tightly once A is bound","note":"B binds only after A"}]},{"text":"**Write the pathway** step by step.","calc":["E + A → **E·A**","E·A + B → **E·A·B** (both on)","E·A·B → E + **products** (only now)"],"marks":[{"t":"A is bound","note":"step 1: A binds"},{"t":"both A and B are bound","note":"then products leave"}]},{"text":"**Why the others fail:** ping-pong needs a product to leave before B binds (none does). Random would let B bind the free enzyme first (it can't).","marks":[{"t":"No product is released","note":"rules out ping-pong"},{"t":"free enzyme","note":"rules out random"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** products leave only after all substrates are on → sequential; one substrate can't bind until another is on → ordered; a product leaves between substrates → ping-pong."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"In a crystal structure, the highlighted isobutyl group of ibuprofen, shown, sits deep in a binding pocket that contains no water. Which amino acid side chain would most likely line that part of the pocket?",idStem:"A drug's strongest contacts with an enzyme come from valine and phenylalanine side chains. What interaction is that?",fig:"ibuprofen-isobutyl-hl",choiceFigs:["leucine-sc","aspartate-sc","serine-sc","lysine-sc"],choices:["Leucine","Aspartate","Serine","Lysine"],ans:0,exp:"The isobutyl group is **all C and H, so it is nonpolar**. It is best stabilized by another nonpolar surface through **hydrophobic contacts**, and leucine's side chain is itself an isobutyl group. Aspartate (−), lysine (+) and serine (–OH) are charged or polar and prefer water or other polar partners.",src:"pe4-cp:36",how:{"ask":"Is the highlighted part of the drug polar or nonpolar? Which side chain is made only of C and H?","know":"• **Hydrophobic interactions:** nonpolar groups (C–H only) cluster together, away from water.\n• Nonpolar side chains: Gly, Ala, Val, **Leu**, Ile, Met, Phe, Trp, Pro.\n• Polar uncharged: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln, Cys, Tyr. Charged at pH 7: Asp⁻, Glu⁻, Lys⁺, Arg⁺, His (partly).\n• On a drawing, a side chain with only carbons and hydrogens, no O or N, is hydrophobic.","steps":[{"text":"The pocket has no water, and the highlighted group must be matched.","hl":"contains no water"},{"text":"The highlighted isobutyl group is only carbon and hydrogen: nonpolar.","fig":"ibuprofen-isobutyl-hl"},{"text":"Scan each side chain for O or N.","calc":["leucine: CH₂–CH(CH₃)₂ → **C, H only**","aspartate: –CH₂COO⁻ → **charged**","serine: –CH₂OH → **polar**","lysine: –(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺ → **charged**"],"strike":["Aspartate","Serine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Leucine's side chain is the same isobutyl shape: like packs with like.","fig":"leucine-sc"},{"text":"Answer: leucine. Rule: match nonpolar drug groups with C/H-only side chains, charged groups with opposite charges."}],"trap":"Lysine has a long chain of CH₂ groups that looks greasy, but it ends in a charged –NH₃⁺, which wants water or a negative partner, not a nonpolar pocket."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's; the hexagon is a benzene ring. The highlighted part is the group in question.","fig":"t4-ibu-read"},{"text":"**Polar vs nonpolar.** O and N pull electrons unevenly: **polar**, water-loving. C–H bonds share evenly: **nonpolar**.","fig":"t4-ibu-polar"},{"text":"**The isobutyl group is only C and H**, so it is nonpolar, also called **hydrophobic** (\"water-fearing\").","fig":"t4-ibu-isobutyl"},{"text":"**With no water around, nonpolar groups pack best against other nonpolar groups** (a hydrophobic interaction).","fig":["t4-ibu-isobutyl","t4-leu-sc"]},{"text":"**Reading an amino acid:** every one has the same backbone; the **side chain** is the part that differs.","fig":"t4-leu-backbone"},{"text":"**Aspartate:** at body pH its side chain is **COO⁻**, a negative charge. Very polar.","fig":"t4-asp-sc"},{"text":"**Serine:** its side chain ends in **OH**, polar and water-loving.","fig":"t4-ser-sc"},{"text":"**Lysine:** at body pH its side chain ends in **NH₃⁺**, a positive charge.","fig":"t4-lys-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse: like sticks to like.** C/H-only groups pair with C/H-only side chains. In each drawing ask: does the side chain contain any O or N?","fig":["t4-ibu-isobutyl","t4-leu-sc"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"How many π bonds are present in the molecule shown, which contains a benzene ring, a ketone, an alkyne and a nitrile?",idStem:"A molecule has 4 C=C bonds, 1 C=O and 1 C≡N. How many π bonds does it have?",fig:"cyanophenyl-butynone",choices:["4","6","8","10"],ans:2,exp:"Every double bond has **one π bond** and every triple bond has **two**. Benzene has 3 C=C, so 3 π; the ketone C=O, 1; the alkyne C≡C, 2; the nitrile C≡N, 2. **Total 3 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 8.**",src:"pe4-cp:37",how:{"ask":"Count the double and triple bonds in the drawing, including those in the ring. How many π bonds does each kind contribute?","know":"• A **single bond** = 1 σ bond. A **double bond** = 1 σ + **1 π**. A **triple bond** = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• A benzene ring is drawn with 3 alternating double bonds: **3 π bonds**, shared around the ring by resonance.\n• On a drawing: count each = as one π and each ≡ as two π.","steps":[{"text":"The question names the four groups; find each in the drawing.","hl":"a benzene ring, a ketone, an alkyne and a nitrile"},{"text":"The highlighted atoms all take part in multiple bonds.","fig":"cyanophenyl-butynone-pi"},{"text":"Add π bonds group by group.","calc":["benzene: 3 C=C × 1 = **3**","ketone C=O: **1**","alkyne C≡C: 2 × 1 = **2**","nitrile C≡N: **2**","total = 3 + 1 + 2 + 2 = **8**"]},{"text":"Common miscounts: triple bonds counted as one π each gives 6; the ring counted once gives 4; all three bonds of each triple bond counted as π gives 10.","strike":["4","6","10"]},{"text":"Answer: 8. Rule: = is one π, ≡ is two π, benzene is three."}],"trap":"6 comes from counting each triple bond as one π bond; a triple bond has one σ and two π. 4 comes from counting the whole ring as a single π system instead of three π bonds."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. One line = single bond, two = double, three = triple.","fig":"t4-cpb-read"},{"text":"**The first bond between two atoms is σ (sigma); every extra bond is π (pi).** So a double bond has 1 π and a triple bond has 2 π.","fig":"t4-cpb-sigma-pi"},{"text":"**The benzene ring** is drawn with 3 double bonds: **3 π**.","fig":"t4-cpb-ring"},{"text":"**The ketone C=O** is one double bond: **1 π**.","fig":"t4-cpb-ketone"},{"text":"**The alkyne C≡C** is a triple bond: **2 π**.","fig":"t4-cpb-alkyne"},{"text":"**The nitrile C≡N** is also a triple bond: **2 π**.","fig":"t4-cpb-nitrile"},{"text":"**Add the four groups.** Every other line is a single bond with **no π**.","fig":"t4-cpb-all"},{"text":"**Common slips:** a triple bond counted as 1 π, the ring counted as 1, or all 3 lines of a triple bond counted (one is σ).","fig":"t4-cpb-sigma-pi"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each = is 1 π, each ≡ is 2 π, a benzene ring is 3 π. Add your counts group by group and ask which answer number matches.","fig":"t4-cpb-all"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"A chromophore solution in a 1.0 cm cuvette has A = 0.70 at 280 nm. Its molar absorptivity is 1.4 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 280 nm and 2.1 × 10² M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 340 nm. What is the concentration of the chromophore?",idStem:"A = εbc. With A = 0.7, ε ≈ 1400 M⁻¹cm⁻¹ and b = 1 cm, what is c?",choices:["50 μM","3.3 mM","500 μM","980 M"],ans:2,exp:"**A = εbc → c = A/(εb)** = 0.70 ÷ (1.4 × 10³ × 1.0) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M = **500 μM**. Use ε at the wavelength where A was measured.",src:"pe4-cp:38",how:{"ask":"Rearrange the absorbance equation to solve for concentration, and pick the ε for the wavelength measured.","know":"**Beer–Lambert law: A = εbc.** A = absorbance (no units), ε = molar absorptivity (M⁻¹cm⁻¹, different at each wavelength), b = path length (cm), c = concentration (M). Rearranged: **c = A/(εb)**. 1 M = 10⁶ μM.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: Beer's law, A = εbc. A was measured at 280 nm, so use ε at 280 nm; the 340 nm value is extra.","hl":"A = 0.70 at 280 nm"},{"text":"Solve for c.","calc":["c = A ÷ (ε b)","c = 0.70 ÷ (**1.4 × 10³** M⁻¹cm⁻¹ × **1.0** cm)","c = **5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M**"]},{"text":"Convert to μM.","calc":["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M × 10⁶ μM/M","= **500 μM**"],"strike":["50 μM","980 M"]},{"text":"Using ε at 340 nm by mistake gives 0.70 ÷ 210 ≈ 3.3 mM.","calc":["0.70 ÷ 2.1 × 10² = 3.3 × 10⁻³ M (wrong ε)"],"strike":["3.3 mM"]},{"text":"Answer: 500 μM."}],"trap":"3.3 mM uses the 340 nm ε (0.70 ÷ 210). 980 M multiplies A × ε instead of dividing."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** A chromophore is a molecule that absorbs light. Each number has a job.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm cuvette","note":"path length b"},{"t":"A = 0.70","note":"absorbance"},{"t":"1.4 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹","note":"ε at 280 nm"}]},{"text":"**Absorbance, A,** = how much light the sample soaks up. More molecules in the light's path → more light absorbed, in a straight line.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs concentration, 1.0 cm cuvette","x":{"label":"Concentration (μM)","min":0,"max":1000,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Absorbance A","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.25},"series":[{"name":"Chromophore","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[250,0.35],[500,0.7],[750,1.05],[1000,1.4]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Chromophore","x":750,"text":"double c → double A","at":"left"}]},"marks":[{"t":"A = 0.70","note":"absorbance: no units"}]},{"text":"**Beer–Lambert law: A = εbc.** Each letter is one thing in the question.","calc":["A = ε × b × c","A: absorbance (no units)","ε: how strongly 1 M absorbs (M⁻¹cm⁻¹)","b: path length (cm); c: concentration (M)"],"marks":[{"t":"molar absorptivity","note":"ε"},{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"b"},{"t":"concentration","note":"c: what we want"}]},{"text":"**ε changes with the wavelength (color) of light.** Use the ε measured at the same wavelength as A. The 340 nm value is extra.","marks":[{"t":"A = 0.70 at 280 nm","note":"measured at 280 nm"},{"t":"1.4 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 280 nm","note":"same 280 nm: use"},{"t":"2.1 × 10² M⁻¹cm⁻¹ at 340 nm","note":"other color: not needed"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange for c**: divide both sides by ε and b.","calc":["A = εbc","divide both sides by εb","**c = A ÷ (εb)**"],"marks":[{"t":"concentration","note":"c = A ÷ (εb)"}]},{"text":"**Plug in** the matching numbers.","marks":[{"t":"A = 0.70","note":"top"},{"t":"1.4 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹","note":"ε, bottom"},{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"b, bottom"}],"calc":["c = 0.70 ÷ (1.4 × 10³ × 1.0)","0.70 ÷ 1.4 = **0.50**","c = 0.50 × 10⁻³ M = **5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M**"]},{"text":"**Convert M → μM.** μ (micro) = one millionth, so 1 M = 10⁶ μM. Check on the line: A = 0.70 sits at 500 μM.","calc":["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M × 10⁶ μM/M","= 5.0 × 10² μM = **500 μM**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs concentration, 1.0 cm cuvette","x":{"label":"Concentration (μM)","min":0,"max":1000,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Absorbance A","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.25},"series":[{"name":"Chromophore","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[250,0.35],[500,0.7],[750,1.05],[1000,1.4]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Chromophore","x":500}],"labels":[{"series":"Chromophore","x":500,"text":"A = 0.70 → 500 μM","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** using the 340 nm ε gives a concentration almost 7× too big.","marks":[{"t":"2.1 × 10² M⁻¹cm⁻¹","note":"wrong ε → 3.3 mM"}],"calc":["0.70 ÷ 2.1 × 10² = 3.3 × 10⁻³ M (wrong wavelength)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A ÷ (εb). Use the ε for the wavelength where A was measured, then convert units (1 M = 10⁶ μM = 10³ mM)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"A plate reader's response is linear only up to A = 1.5. Samples read in 1.0 cm cuvettes give absorbances from 0.3 to 2.6. Which change would bring the most strongly absorbing samples into the linear range?",idStem:"A detector can't read high absorbances. What fixes this for the samples that absorb most strongly?",choices:["Dilute them twofold or read them in 0.5 cm cuvettes","Concentrate them twofold or read them in 2.0 cm cuvettes","Read them in cuvettes with a wider cross section","Read them at a higher temperature in the same cuvettes"],ans:0,exp:"**A = εbc**: halving **c** (dilution) or **b** (path length) halves A, so 2.6 → 1.3, inside the linear range. Width of the cuvette does not change the path the light travels.",src:"pe4-cp:39",how:{"ask":"Which quantities in the absorbance equation can you lower, and by how much do they need to fall?","know":"**A = εbc.** A is directly proportional to concentration c and path length b (the distance light travels through the sample). ε is fixed for a compound at a given wavelength. To lower A, lower c (dilute) or b (shorter cuvette), then correct the calculation for the change.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the strongest samples (A = 2.6) must drop below the instrument's 1.5 limit.","hl":"linear only up to A = 1.5"},{"text":"Beer's law: A = εbc, so A is proportional to both c and the path length b.","calc":["A = ε × b × c","Need A from 2.6 to < 1.5 → reduce by > **1.7×**"]},{"text":"Halving c or b halves A: 2.6 → 1.3, inside the linear range.","calc":["c ÷ 2: A = 2.6 ÷ 2 = **1.3**","b = 0.5 cm: A = 2.6 × 0.5 = **1.3**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs relative concentration, 1.0 cm path","x":{"label":"Relative concentration","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"1.0 cm","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.65],[0.5,1.3],[0.75,1.95],[1,2.6]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"1.0 cm","x":0.5}],"yLine":1.5,"labels":[{"series":"1.0 cm","x":1,"text":"A = 2.6: too high","at":"left"},{"series":"1.0 cm","x":0.5,"text":"half: A = 1.3","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":1.3,"to":2.6,"text":"÷ 2","side":"left"}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.577,"text":"linear (A < 1.5)"}]}},{"text":"Concentrating or using a longer path raises A; a wider cross section does not change the path light travels; changing temperature does not reliably lower A.","strike":["Concentrate them twofold or read them in 2.0 cm cuvettes","Read them in cuvettes with a wider cross section","Read them at a higher temperature in the same cuvettes"]},{"text":"Answer: dilute them twofold or read them in 0.5 cm cuvettes, then correct for the factor."}],"trap":"\"Wider cross section\" changes the cuvette's width, not the distance light travels through the sample, so A doesn't change. Concentrating or lengthening the path raises A."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** A plate reader measures absorbance (how much light a sample soaks up).","marks":[{"t":"A = 1.5","note":"top trusted reading"},{"t":"1.0 cm cuvettes","note":"light's path length"},{"t":"0.3 to 2.6","note":"readings we got"}]},{"text":"**Linear** = readings rise in a straight line with concentration. The reader is only trustworthy below A = 1.5.","marks":[{"t":"linear only up to","note":"straight-line zone only"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs relative concentration, 1.0 cm path","x":{"label":"Relative concentration","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Absorbance A","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"1.0 cm","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.65],[0.5,1.3],[0.75,1.95],[1,2.6]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.577,"text":"linear: A < 1.5"}]}},{"text":"**The problem samples** read 2.6, far above the 1.5 limit.","marks":[{"t":"most strongly absorbing samples","note":"the A = 2.6 ones"},{"t":"2.6","note":"too high: > 1.5"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs relative concentration, 1.0 cm path","x":{"label":"Relative concentration","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Absorbance A","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"1.0 cm","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.65],[0.5,1.3],[0.75,1.95],[1,2.6]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"1.0 cm","x":1,"text":"A = 2.6: too high","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Beer–Lambert: A = εbc.** ε is fixed for the compound. To lower A, shrink b (path) or c (concentration).","calc":["A = ε × b × c","ε: fixed for this compound","b: distance light travels in sample","c: concentration"],"marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm cuvettes","note":"b = 1.0 cm"}]},{"text":"**Halve c or halve b → A halves.** 2.6 drops to 1.3, inside the linear range.","calc":["dilute twofold: 2.6 ÷ 2 = **1.3**","0.5 cm cuvette: 2.6 × 0.5 = **1.3**","1.3 < 1.5 → **trusted**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance vs relative concentration, 1.0 cm path","x":{"label":"Relative concentration","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Absorbance A","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"1.0 cm","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.25,0.65],[0.5,1.3],[0.75,1.95],[1,2.6]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"1.0 cm","x":0.5}],"labels":[{"series":"1.0 cm","x":0.5,"text":"half: A = 1.3","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":1.3,"to":2.6,"text":"÷ 2","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Wrong moves:** concentrating or a longer cuvette RAISES A. A wider cuvette doesn't change how far light travels through it. Temperature isn't in A = εbc.","calc":["×2 c or b = 2.0 cm → A = 5.2 (worse)","wider cuvette: b still 1.0 cm → A = 2.6"],"marks":[{"t":"linear range","note":"goal: A below 1.5"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A = εbc, so A is proportional to concentration and path length. Too high → dilute or use a shorter path; then multiply back by the dilution factor."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The titration curve of a weak acid HA is shown. A buffer made from HA and its conjugate base is considered effective only when the ratio of base form to acid form lies between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1. At which pH would this buffer NOT be effective?",idStem:"Tris has a pKa of 8.1. Over what pH range does it buffer well?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 20 mL of weak acid HA with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.9],[2,3.85],[5,4.32],[10,4.8],[15,5.28],[18,5.75],[19.5,6.39],[20,8.7],[20.5,11],[25,12],[30,12.3]]}]},choices:["pH 4.0, since base : acid would be about 1 : 6","pH 4.8, since base : acid would be about 1 : 1","pH 5.5, since base : acid would be about 5 : 1","pH 6.3, since base : acid would be about 30 : 1"],ans:3,exp:"At the **half-equivalence point, 10 mL** (half of the 20 mL equivalence volume), pH = pKa = **4.8**. By Henderson–Hasselbalch, base : acid = 10^(pH − pKa). At pH 6.3 that is 10^1.5 ≈ **32 : 1**, outside 10 : 1, so the buffer fails there. The other pH values are within 1 unit of 4.8.",src:"pe4-cp:40",how:{"ask":"Read the pKa from the curve at half the equivalence volume. Which pH lies more than 1 unit away from it?","know":"• **Equivalence point:** the steep jump, where moles of OH⁻ added = moles of HA.\n• **Half-equivalence point:** half that volume; there [A⁻] = [HA], so **pH = pKa**. It sits in the flat middle of the curve.\n• **Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]), so [A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa).\n• A buffer works within **pKa ± 1**, where the ratio stays between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1.","steps":[{"text":"The buffer limit is a ratio of 10 either way, which means pKa ± 1.","hl":"between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1","graph":{"spans":[{"x":10,"from":3.8,"to":5.8,"text":"pKa ± 1"}]}},{"text":"Find the equivalence point: the steep jump at 20 mL.","graph":{"xLine":20,"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"equivalence: 20 mL","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Half of 20 mL is 10 mL; read the pH there. That is the pKa.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":10}],"xLine":10,"spans":[{"x":10,"from":0,"to":4.8,"text":"pKa = 4.8","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"half-equivalence: 10 mL","at":"above"}]},"calc":["V½ = 20 ÷ 2 = **10 mL**","pH at 10 mL = **4.8** = pKa"]},{"text":"Convert each pH to a base : acid ratio.","calc":["pH 4.0: 10^(4.0 − 4.8) = 10^−0.8 ≈ **1 : 6**","pH 4.8: 10^0 = **1 : 1**","pH 5.5: 10^0.7 ≈ **5 : 1**","pH 6.3: 10^1.5 ≈ **32 : 1** → outside 10 : 1"],"strike":["pH 4.0, since base : acid would be about 1 : 6","pH 4.8, since base : acid would be about 1 : 1","pH 5.5, since base : acid would be about 5 : 1"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":8,"from":3.8,"to":5.8,"text":"buffer band 3.8–5.8","side":"left"},{"x":12,"from":4.8,"to":6.3,"text":"pH 6.3 = +1.5 → 32 : 1"}]}},{"text":"Answer: pH 6.3. Rule: pKa = pH at half the equivalence volume; a buffer works only within 1 pH unit of it.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":18,"text":"buffer region"}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"pKa = 4.8","at":"above"}]}}],"trap":"pH 8.7 at the jump is the equivalence point, not the pKa. Reading pKa there makes 6.3 look closer and 4.0 look far. Always read pKa at half the equivalence volume."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = higher pH (more basic); down = more acidic.**","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"titration curve","note":"the graph"}]},{"text":"**Right = more NaOH (a base) added.** We start with pure weak acid HA and slowly neutralize it.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"weak acid HA","note":"starts as pure acid"}]},{"text":"**A weak acid HA** gives up its H⁺. Take the H away and you get its **conjugate base A⁻**. NaOH does exactly that.","calc":["HA + OH⁻ → **A⁻** + H₂O","HA = acid form; A⁻ = base form"],"marks":[{"t":"weak acid HA","note":"acid form"},{"t":"conjugate base","note":"base form A⁻"}]},{"text":"**A buffer** = a mix of HA and A⁻ that resists pH change. On the curve it is the flat stretch.","marks":[{"t":"buffer","note":"HA + A⁻ mixture"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":18,"text":"buffer region"}]}},{"text":"**The steep cliff = the equivalence point:** all HA has been turned into A⁻. Here it is at 20 mL.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":20,"text":"equivalence: 20 mL","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**pKa** = the pH where HA and A⁻ are equal (1 : 1). That happens halfway to the cliff: 10 mL.","calc":["half of 20 mL = **10 mL**","there HA = A⁻ → pH = **pKa = 4.8**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"pH","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":10,"text":"10 mL: pH 4.8 = pKa","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Each pH unit = ×10 in base : acid.** So the 1 : 10 to 10 : 1 window is pKa ± 1.","marks":[{"t":"between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1","note":"= pKa ± 1"}],"calc":["base : acid = 10^(pH − pKa)","10 : 1 → pH = 4.8 + 1 = **5.8**","1 : 10 → pH = 4.8 − 1 = **3.8**"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":10,"from":3.8,"to":5.8,"text":"pKa ± 1"}]}},{"text":"**Test each pH:** how far from 4.8? Only one is more than 1 unit away.","marks":[{"t":"NOT be effective","note":"find the pH outside"}],"calc":["4.0: 10^−0.8 ≈ 1 : 6 → inside","5.5: 10^0.7 ≈ 5 : 1 → inside","6.3: 10^1.5 ≈ **32 : 1** → outside 10 : 1"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":12,"from":4.8,"to":6.3,"text":"6.3 − 4.8 = 1.5"},{"x":8,"from":3.8,"to":5.8,"text":"works 3.8–5.8","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKa = pH at half the equivalence volume. A buffer works only within 1 pH unit of pKa (ratio 1 : 10 to 10 : 1).","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":18,"text":"buffer region"}]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"In a phosphatase, a histidine side-chain nitrogen attacks the substrate 4-nitrophenyl phosphate, shown, forming a covalent phosphohistidine intermediate and releasing 4-nitrophenolate. Which labeled atom does the histidine nitrogen attack?",idStem:"An aspartate side chain attacks a phosphate group, forming a covalent enzyme intermediate. Which atom does it attack?",fig:"pnpp-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"The histidine N uses its lone pair as a **nucleophile**, so it attacks the **most electron-poor atom**. Phosphorus (II) is bonded to four electronegative oxygens, which pull electron density away and leave **P strongly δ+**. The new N–P bond forms as the **bridging O (III) leaves with the aryl group**, giving phosphohistidine plus 4-nitrophenolate.",src:"pe4-cp:41",how:{"ask":"The nitrogen brings a lone pair. Which labeled atom is electron-poor enough to accept it, and which atom must break away for 4-nitrophenolate to be released?","know":"• **Nucleophile** (lone-pair donor) attacks an **electrophile** (electron-poor atom).\n• Electronegativity: O 3.4 > C 2.6 > P 2.2. An atom bonded to several O atoms carries a large partial positive charge (δ+).\n• In a phosphoryl transfer, the nucleophile bonds to **P** and the **bridging O–R** group leaves.\n• A negatively charged O is itself electron-rich: it is a nucleophile, not a target.","steps":[{"text":"The intermediate is a phosphohistidine: N bonded to P. That already says where the N ends up.","hl":"covalent phosphohistidine intermediate"},{"text":"Look at what each labeled atom is.","fig":"pnpp-sites","calc":["I: O⁻, extra electrons → **electron-rich**","II: P bonded to 4 O → **strongly δ+**","III: bridging O → **leaves with the aryl ring**","IV: ring C → stays on 4-nitrophenolate"]},{"text":"An electron-rich O⁻ repels a lone pair, and the ring carbon is not where the phosphoryl group goes.","strike":["I","IV"]},{"text":"Atom III is in the leaving group: the P–O3 bond breaks, so 4-nitrophenolate leaves with that O.","strike":["III"]},{"text":"Phosphorus, highlighted, is the electrophile. Answer: II. Rule: in phosphoryl transfer the nucleophile attacks P, and the bridging O–R leaves.","fig":"pnpp-p-hl"}],"trap":"The bridging O (III) is where the bond breaks, which makes it look like the reaction site. It is the leaving group's atom; the nucleophile bonds to the P on the other side of it."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read this drawing.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. Letters are atoms; a − or + sign marks an extra or missing electron.","fig":"t4-pnpp-read"},{"text":"**The labels I–IV** mark the four atoms you must choose between.","fig":"pnpp-sites"},{"text":"**Histidine's ring N has a lone pair** (spare electron pair). It is a **nucleophile**: it bonds to an electron-poor atom.","fig":"t4-his-n"},{"text":"**O pulls electrons harder than P.** With four O's around it, P is left **slightly positive (δ+)**: electron-poor.","fig":"t4-pnpp-pplus"},{"text":"**Atom I is O⁻:** already electron-rich, so it pushes a lone pair away.","fig":"t4-pnpp-o1"},{"text":"**Atom III bridges P and the ring.** The P–O(III) bond breaks, and O(III) leaves with the ring.","fig":"t4-pnpp-o3"},{"text":"**The released piece is 4-nitrophenolate**, which carries atoms III and IV away. So histidine does not end up on either.","fig":"t4-nitrophenolate"},{"text":"**\"Phosphohistidine\" means histidine bonded to phosphorus.** The new N–P bond forms as the P–O(III) bond breaks.","fig":"t4-pnpp-target"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lone pair attacks the most electron-poor atom; in phosphate transfer that is P, and the bridging O–R leaves. Ask of each label: electron-poor, and does it stay?","fig":"pnpp-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A cell culture medium reads pH 6.0 at 25 °C. The lab notebook records hydronium concentration in μM. Which entry is correct, and how does the medium compare with pure water?",idStem:"What is [H₃O⁺] at pH 6?",choices:["0.1 μM; more acidic than pure water","6.0 μM; more acidic than pure water","1.0 μM; more acidic than pure water","1.0 μM; less acidic than pure water"],ans:2,exp:"**[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ** = 10⁻⁶ M = **1.0 μM**. Pure water at 25 °C has 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM, so the medium has 10× more H₃O⁺ → more acidic.",src:"pe4-cp:42",how:{"ask":"Turn the pH into a molar concentration, convert to μM, and compare with water at pH 7.","know":"**pH = −log[H₃O⁺], so [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ M.** μ (micro) = 10⁻⁶, so 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M. Pure water at 25 °C: pH 7 → 10⁻⁷ M. Lower pH = more H₃O⁺ = more acidic.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: convert pH to [H₃O⁺], express it in μM, and compare with pure water.","hl":"reads pH 6.0 at 25 °C"},{"text":"Use [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ and convert M → μM.","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·⁰ M","= 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ M × 10⁶ μM/M","= **1.0 μM**"],"strike":["0.1 μM; more acidic than pure water","6.0 μM; more acidic than pure water"]},{"text":"Pure water at 25 °C has pH 7: [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM. The medium has 10 times more H₃O⁺.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"[H₃O⁺] at 25 °C","x":{"label":"Solution"},"y":{"label":"[H₃O⁺] (μM)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"categories":["Pure water, pH 7.0","Medium, pH 6.0"],"series":[{"name":"[H₃O⁺]","values":[0.1,1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Medium, pH 6.0","[H₃O⁺]"]]},"strike":["1.0 μM; less acidic than pure water"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 μM; more acidic than pure water."}],"trap":"0.1 μM is pure water's value (pH 7), not this medium's. 6.0 μM confuses the pH number with a concentration."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given and what's asked.** One pH reading; the answer must be in μM and compared with pure water.","marks":[{"t":"pH 6.0","note":"how acidic"},{"t":"hydronium concentration","note":"amount of H₃O⁺"},{"t":"μM","note":"micromolar units"}]},{"text":"**Hydronium (H₃O⁺)** = a water molecule carrying an extra H⁺. It is the acid particle: more H₃O⁺ = more acidic.","marks":[{"t":"hydronium","note":"H₃O⁺: the acid particle"}]},{"text":"**pH is a power-of-10 scale.** The pH number is the (minus) exponent of [H₃O⁺]. Lower pH = more H₃O⁺.","calc":["pH = −log[H₃O⁺]","so [H₃O⁺] = 10^(−pH) M","each pH step down = **10× more** H₃O⁺"],"marks":[{"t":"pH 6.0","note":"the exponent: 10⁻⁶"}]},{"text":"**Turn pH 6.0 into a concentration.**","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·⁰ M","= **1.0 × 10⁻⁶ M**"],"marks":[{"t":"pH 6.0","note":"→ 10⁻⁶ M"}]},{"text":"**μ (micro) = one millionth = 10⁻⁶.** So 10⁻⁶ M is exactly 1 μM.","calc":["1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ M = **1.0 μM**"],"marks":[{"t":"μM","note":"1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M"}]},{"text":"**Pure water** at 25 °C has pH 7.0.","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M","= 0.1 × 10⁻⁶ M = **0.1 μM**"],"marks":[{"t":"pure water","note":"pH 7 at 25 °C"},{"t":"25 °C","note":"temperature for pH 7"}]},{"text":"**Compare:** the medium has 10× more H₃O⁺ than pure water → **more acidic**.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"[H₃O⁺] at 25 °C","x":{"label":"Solution"},"y":{"label":"[H₃O⁺] (μM)","min":0,"max":1.2,"step":0.2},"categories":["Pure water, pH 7.0","Medium, pH 6.0"],"series":[{"name":"[H₃O⁺]","values":[0.1,1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Medium, pH 6.0","[H₃O⁺]"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 6.0 μM just copies the pH number (pH is an exponent, not a concentration). 0.1 μM is pure water's value, not the medium's.","marks":[{"t":"compare with pure water","note":"water: 0.1 μM"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [H₃O⁺] = 10^(−pH) M. Pure water (25 °C) = 10⁻⁷ M. Lower pH = more H₃O⁺ = more acidic; each pH unit is a factor of 10."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"The graph shows initial rates for a wild-type enzyme and a mutant, each at 0.010 μM enzyme. The wild-type curve levels off at 1.0 μM/s and the mutant curve at 0.40 μM/s. Which conclusion about catalytic efficiency is best supported?",idStem:"Which quantity measures an enzyme's catalytic efficiency?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Initial rate vs [S], [E] = 0.010 μM for both enzymes","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":200,"step":40},"y":{"label":"v₀ (μM/s)","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Wild type","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[4,0.167],[20,0.5],[40,0.667],[80,0.8],[120,0.857],[160,0.889],[200,0.909]]},{"name":"Mutant","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[4,0.2],[20,0.333],[40,0.364],[80,0.381],[120,0.387],[160,0.39],[200,0.392]]}]},choices:["Wild type, since its Vmax is higher","Wild type, since its kcat/KM is twice as large","Mutant, since its kcat/KM is twice as large","Neither, since their kcat/KM values are equal"],ans:2,exp:"kcat = Vmax/[E]: **100 s⁻¹** for wild type, **40 s⁻¹** for the mutant. KM = [S] at half of Vmax: **20 μM** (v = 0.50) for wild type, **4 μM** (v = 0.20) for the mutant. kcat/KM = 100/20 = **5** versus 40/4 = **10 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**, so the **mutant is twice as efficient**.",src:"pe4-cp:43",how:{"ask":"Catalytic efficiency is kcat/KM. Get kcat from each plateau and KM from the [S] where each curve reaches half its plateau.","know":"• **Vmax** = the plateau; **kcat = Vmax ÷ [E]**, the turnovers per enzyme per second.\n• **KM** = the [S] that gives v = ½Vmax; lower KM means the enzyme works well at low [S].\n• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**: it sets the rate at low [S], where most enzymes operate.\n• On the graph: draw a line across at half the plateau and drop down to the x-axis to read KM.","steps":[{"text":"Both plateaus and the enzyme concentration are given, so kcat follows directly.","hl":"each at 0.010 μM enzyme","calc":["wild type kcat = 1.0 ÷ 0.010 = **100 s⁻¹**","mutant kcat = 0.40 ÷ 0.010 = **40 s⁻¹**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Wild type","x":200,"text":"levels off ≈ 1.0 → kcat 100 s⁻¹","at":"above"},{"series":"Mutant","x":200,"text":"levels off ≈ 0.40 → kcat 40 s⁻¹","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Wild type: half of 1.0 is 0.50 μM/s, reached at [S] = 20 μM. That is its KM.","graph":{"focus":["Wild type"],"read":[{"series":"Wild type","x":20}],"yLine":0.5,"xLine":20,"spans":[{"x":20,"from":0,"to":0.5,"text":"Vmax/2 = 0.50","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Wild type","x":20,"text":"KM = 20 μM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Mutant: half of 0.40 is 0.20 μM/s, reached already at [S] = 4 μM.","graph":{"focus":["Mutant"],"read":[{"series":"Mutant","x":4}],"yLine":0.2,"xLine":4,"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":0.2,"text":"Vmax/2 = 0.20","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Mutant","x":4,"text":"KM = 4 μM","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Divide kcat by KM for each.","calc":["wild type: 100 ÷ 20 = **5 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","mutant: 40 ÷ 4 = **10 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","ratio = 10 ÷ 5 = **2** in favor of the mutant"],"strike":["Wild type, since its Vmax is higher","Wild type, since its kcat/KM is twice as large","Neither, since their kcat/KM values are equal"],"graph":{"focus":["Wild type","Mutant"],"labels":[{"series":"Wild type","x":20,"text":"100 ÷ 20 = 5","at":"above"},{"series":"Mutant","x":4,"text":"40 ÷ 4 = 10","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: the mutant, since its kcat/KM is twice as large. Rule: efficiency = kcat/KM, never the height of the plateau alone."}],"trap":"The wild-type curve is higher everywhere you look, so it seems better. Efficiency is not the plateau; the mutant's much smaller KM more than makes up for its lower kcat."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = faster reaction.** v₀ = initial rate: how fast product is made at the very start.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"initial rates","note":"y axis: v₀"}]},{"text":"**Right = more substrate** ([S] = the molecule the enzyme works on).","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two curves:** the normal (wild-type) enzyme and a mutant, same amount of each.","marks":[{"t":"wild-type enzyme","note":"circles"},{"t":"a mutant","note":"squares"},{"t":"0.010 μM enzyme","note":"same amount: [E]"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Wild type","x":120,"text":"wild type","at":"above"},{"series":"Mutant","x":120,"text":"mutant","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Vmax** = top speed: the curve flattens when every enzyme is busy.","marks":[{"t":"levels off at 1.0 μM/s","note":"wild-type Vmax"},{"t":"0.40 μM/s","note":"mutant Vmax"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":120,"x2":200,"text":"plateau = Vmax"}]}},{"text":"**kcat** = how many molecules ONE enzyme handles per second = Vmax ÷ [E].","marks":[{"t":"0.010 μM enzyme","note":"divide by this"},{"t":"1.0 μM/s","note":"wild Vmax"},{"t":"0.40 μM/s","note":"mutant Vmax"}],"calc":["kcat = Vmax ÷ [E]","wild: 1.0 ÷ 0.010 = **100 s⁻¹**","mutant: 0.40 ÷ 0.010 = **40 s⁻¹**"]},{"text":"**KM** = the [S] that gives half of top speed. Small KM = works well with little substrate. Wild type: half of 1.0 is 0.50 → 20 μM.","graph":{"focus":["Wild type"],"read":[{"series":"Wild type","x":20}],"spans":[{"x":20,"from":0,"to":0.5,"text":"Vmax/2 = 0.50","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Wild type","x":20,"text":"KM = 20 μM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Mutant:** half of 0.40 is 0.20, reached already at [S] = 4 μM.","graph":{"focus":["Mutant"],"read":[{"series":"Mutant","x":4}],"spans":[{"x":4,"from":0,"to":0.2,"text":"Vmax/2 = 0.20","side":"right"}],"labels":[{"series":"Mutant","x":4,"text":"KM = 4 μM","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Catalytic efficiency = kcat ÷ KM.** Fast (big kcat) AND good at low [S] (small KM).","marks":[{"t":"catalytic efficiency","note":"= kcat ÷ KM"}],"calc":["wild: 100 ÷ 20 = **5 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","mutant: 40 ÷ 4 = **10 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","10 ÷ 5 → mutant is **2×** as efficient"]},{"text":"**A higher plateau is NOT efficiency.** At low [S], where efficiency counts, the mutant is actually faster.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":20,"text":"low [S]"}],"labels":[{"series":"Mutant","x":4,"text":"mutant faster here","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** kcat = Vmax ÷ [E]; KM = [S] at half Vmax (read across, then down); efficiency = kcat/KM. Compare the ratio, not the plateaus.","graph":{"focus":["Wild type","Mutant"]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"A 10 mg water strider stands on a pond. Its legs dimple the surface without breaking through, and together they displace only about 0.1 mm³ of water. Which property of water mainly supports the insect? (density of water = 1.0 g/cm³)",idStem:"What lets small insects walk on water?",choices:["Buoyancy, from the weight of water its legs displace","Viscosity, from water's resistance to flow","Hydrostatic pressure, from the water depth below it","Surface tension, from cohesion between surface molecules"],ans:3,exp:"Buoyancy from 0.1 mm³ supports only 0.1 mg, which is 1% of the insect's weight. The rest comes from **surface tension**: **hydrogen-bonded surface molecules form an elastic 'skin'** that resists being stretched by the dimples.",src:"pe4-cp:44",how:{"ask":"Check whether the displaced water could hold up the insect. If not, what does a dimpled but unbroken surface tell you?","know":"**Buoyant force = weight of fluid displaced** (Archimedes). **Surface tension** = the cohesive pull between surface molecules (H-bonds in water) that makes a surface act like a stretched membrane. Units: 1 cm³ = 1000 mm³; water's density is 1 mg/mm³.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: test whether buoyancy can account for the insect's weight, using the displaced volume.","hl":"displace only about 0.1 mm³ of water"},{"text":"Buoyant support equals the mass of water displaced.","calc":["ρ = 1.0 g/cm³ = **1.0 mg/mm³**","m(displaced) = 0.1 mm³ × 1.0 mg/mm³ = **0.1 mg**","fraction = 0.1 mg ÷ 10 mg = **1%**"],"strike":["Buoyancy, from the weight of water its legs displace"]},{"text":"So 99% of the support comes from something else.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Support for a 10 mg water strider","x":{"label":"Source"},"y":{"label":"Mass supported (mg)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["Weight to support","Buoyancy","Surface tension"],"series":[{"name":"mg","values":[10,0.1,9.9]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Surface tension","mg"]]}},{"text":"The legs dimple the surface without breaking it: the hydrogen-bonded surface acts as an elastic skin. Viscosity resists flow and hydrostatic pressure acts below the surface, neither of which applies here.","hl":"Its legs dimple the surface without breaking through","strike":["Viscosity, from water's resistance to flow","Hydrostatic pressure, from the water depth below it"]},{"text":"Answer: surface tension, from cohesion between surface molecules."}],"trap":"Buoyancy is the reflex for 'floating', but the numbers rule it out: it supplies only 1% of the weight. An object held up by buoyancy sinks in until it has displaced its own weight."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** We test whether these numbers can explain how the insect is held up.","marks":[{"t":"10 mg","note":"insect's mass"},{"t":"0.1 mm³","note":"water pushed aside"},{"t":"1.0 g/cm³","note":"density of water"}]},{"text":"**Buoyancy** = water's upward push. It equals the weight of the water you push aside (Archimedes).","marks":[{"t":"displace","note":"push aside → buoyancy"}]},{"text":"**Convert the density** so it matches mm³ and mg.","calc":["1 cm = 10 mm → 1 cm³ = 1000 mm³","1 g = 1000 mg","1.0 g/cm³ = 1000 mg ÷ 1000 mm³ = **1.0 mg/mm³**"],"marks":[{"t":"1.0 g/cm³","note":"= 1.0 mg per mm³"}]},{"text":"**How much can buoyancy hold?** Only the mass of the pushed-aside water.","calc":["0.1 mm³ × 1.0 mg/mm³ = **0.1 mg**","0.1 mg ÷ 10 mg = **1%** of its weight"],"marks":[{"t":"0.1 mm³","note":"holds up only 0.1 mg"},{"t":"10 mg","note":"needs 10 mg held"}]},{"text":"**Buoyancy covers only 1%.** Something else holds up the other 99%.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Support for a 10 mg water strider","x":{"label":"Source"},"y":{"label":"Mass supported (mg)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["Weight to support","Buoyancy","Still missing"],"series":[{"name":"mg","values":[10,0.1,9.9]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Still missing","mg"]]}},{"text":"**Surface tension:** water molecules at the surface grip each other (hydrogen bonds), so the surface acts like a stretched elastic skin.","marks":[{"t":"dimple the surface without breaking through","note":"surface acts like a skin"}]},{"text":"**Other choices:** viscosity = resistance to flowing (matters when moving through water). Hydrostatic pressure = push from depth (insect is on top, not under).","marks":[{"t":"stands","note":"standing still"},{"t":"the surface","note":"on top: no depth"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** check buoyancy with numbers (mass displaced vs weight). If it's tiny, a light object resting on water is held by surface tension: cohesion from hydrogen bonds."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"A radiotracer has a half-life of 2.0 h. A sample is prepared at 8:00 a.m., and at 2:00 p.m. the same day it contains 24 g of the isotope. What mass of the isotope was present at 8:00 a.m.?",idStem:"An isotope has a 2-hour half-life. After 6 hours, 24 g remains. What was the starting amount?",choices:["3 g","72 g","144 g","192 g"],ans:3,exp:"8:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. = 6 h = **3 half-lives**, so ⅛ remains. Working backward: **24 g × 2³ = 192 g**.",src:"pe4-cp:45",how:{"ask":"How many half-lives fit between the two times, and do you need to go forward or backward in time?","know":"**Half-life (t½)**: time for half of the radioactive nuclei to decay. After n half-lives, **remaining = initial × (½)ⁿ**. To find the initial amount, multiply by 2 for each half-life you go back.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: work backward in time from the 2:00 p.m. mass to the 8:00 a.m. mass.","hl":"at 2:00 p.m. the same day it contains 24 g"},{"text":"Count half-lives in the elapsed time.","hl":"half-life of 2.0 h","calc":["8:00 a.m. → 2:00 p.m. = **6 h**","n = 6 h ÷ 2.0 h = **3** half-lives"]},{"text":"Going backward, each half-life doubles the mass.","calc":["m₀ = 24 g × 2³","m₀ = 24 × **8** = **192 g**"]},{"text":"Check on the decay curve: 192 → 96 → 48 → 24 g.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Mass of radiotracer after 8:00 a.m.","x":{"label":"Hours after 8:00 a.m.","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Mass (g)","min":0,"max":200,"step":40},"series":[{"name":"Isotope","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,192],[2,96],[4,48],[6,24]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Isotope","x":6},{"series":"Isotope","x":0}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"t½ 1"},{"x1":2,"x2":4,"text":"t½ 2"},{"x1":4,"x2":6,"text":"t½ 3"}],"labels":[{"series":"Isotope","x":0,"text":"192 g at 8:00 a.m.","at":"right"},{"series":"Isotope","x":6,"text":"24 g at 2:00 p.m.","at":"above"}]},"strike":["3 g","72 g","144 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 192 g. 3 g decays forward instead of backward, and 72 g multiplies by 3 instead of 2³."}],"trap":"3 g goes forward in time (24 ÷ 8), but 24 g is the later amount. 72 g multiplies by the number of half-lives (3) instead of by 2³."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** We know the END mass and must work BACK to the start.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 2.0 h","note":"halves every 2 h"},{"t":"8:00 a.m.","note":"start: unknown"},{"t":"24 g","note":"mass at 2:00 p.m."}]},{"text":"**Radioactive** atoms break apart over time. **Half-life** = the time for half of them to be gone. Every 2 h, half disappears.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Any radioactive sample, half-life 2 h","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":6,"step":2},"y":{"label":"% remaining","min":0,"max":100,"step":25},"series":[{"name":"Remaining","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,100],[2,50],[4,25],[6,12.5]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Remaining","x":2,"text":"2 h: half left","at":"right"},{"series":"Remaining","x":4,"text":"4 h: a quarter","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**How much time passed?**","marks":[{"t":"8:00 a.m.","note":"start"},{"t":"2:00 p.m.","note":"end"}],"calc":["8 a.m. → 12 noon = 4 h","noon → 2 p.m. = 2 h","total = **6 h**"]},{"text":"**Count half-lives** in those 6 hours.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 h","note":"one half-life"}],"calc":["6 h ÷ 2.0 h = **3 half-lives**"]},{"text":"**Going back in time, the mass doubles** each half-life.","marks":[{"t":"24 g","note":"double 3 times"}],"calc":["2:00 p.m.: 24 g","noon: 24 × 2 = **48 g**","10:00 a.m.: 48 × 2 = **96 g**","8:00 a.m.: 96 × 2 = **192 g**"]},{"text":"**Check on the decay curve:** 192 → 96 → 48 → 24 g, one halving per shaded block.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Mass of radiotracer after 8:00 a.m.","x":{"label":"Hours after 8:00 a.m.","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Mass (g)","min":0,"max":200,"step":40},"series":[{"name":"Isotope","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,192],[2,96],[4,48],[6,24]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"t½ 1"},{"x1":2,"x2":4,"text":"t½ 2"},{"x1":4,"x2":6,"text":"t½ 3"}],"labels":[{"series":"Isotope","x":0,"text":"192 g at 8:00 a.m.","at":"right"},{"series":"Isotope","x":6,"text":"24 g at 2:00 p.m.","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 3 g halves forward (wrong direction: earlier must be MORE). 72 g multiplies by 3 half-lives instead of by 2³ = 8.","marks":[{"t":"was present","note":"earlier → more mass"}],"calc":["wrong: 24 ÷ 8 = 3 g","wrong: 24 × 3 = 72 g","right: 24 × 2³ = **192 g**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = time ÷ half-life. Forward: remaining = start × (½)ⁿ. Backward: start = remaining × 2ⁿ."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"A red laser beam (λ = 650 nm) is aimed at a single strand of hair about 80 μm thick. On a wall 2 m away, evenly spaced bright and dark bands appear on both sides of the hair's shadow. Which phenomenon is primarily responsible for the bands?",idStem:"Light passing a thin thread makes alternating bright and dark fringes on a screen. What causes this?",choices:["Refraction of light passing through the hair","Polarization of light by the hair's surface","Diffraction of light around the hair's edges","Reflection of light from the hair's surface"],ans:2,exp:"Light **bends around the edges of a narrow obstacle**, and the spreading waves **interfere**, giving bright and dark fringes: **diffraction**. A hair acts like a single slit (Babinet's principle).",src:"pe4-cp:46",how:{"ask":"What makes light spread into a pattern of bright and dark bands when it meets a very thin object?","know":"• **Diffraction**: waves bend around obstacles or through openings, most noticeably when the size is near the wavelength (tens of μm or less for light). Interference between the bent waves gives **fringes**.\n• Refraction: bending when light enters a new medium.\n• Polarization: filtering the direction of the field's oscillation.\n• Reflection: bouncing off a surface.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: evenly spaced bright and dark bands on both sides of a narrow obstacle are an interference pattern.","hl":"evenly spaced bright and dark bands appear on both sides of the hair's shadow"},{"text":"Compare the obstacle with the wavelength: the hair is only about 120 wavelengths wide, narrow enough to spread light noticeably.","calc":["80 μm ÷ 0.65 μm ≈ **120** wavelengths"]},{"text":"Predict the band spacing with the single-slit formula; a hair acts like a slit of the same width.","calc":["Δy ≈ λL ÷ a","Δy ≈ (6.5 × 10⁻⁷ m)(2 m) ÷ (8.0 × 10⁻⁵ m)","Δy ≈ **1.6 × 10⁻² m ≈ 1.6 cm**, easily visible"]},{"text":"Refraction and reflection off a curved strand do not produce evenly spaced fringes, and polarization changes only the orientation of the light, not a band pattern.","strike":["Refraction of light passing through the hair","Polarization of light by the hair's surface","Reflection of light from the hair's surface"]},{"text":"Answer: diffraction of light around the hair's edges."}],"trap":"Refraction does happen inside a hair, but it can't produce a regular fringe pattern on both sides of the shadow. Evenly spaced bands are an interference pattern, which here comes from diffraction."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** A laser shines past one hair onto a wall.","marks":[{"t":"λ = 650 nm","note":"wavelength of light"},{"t":"80 μm thick","note":"width of the hair"},{"t":"2 m away","note":"hair-to-wall distance"}]},{"text":"**Light is a wave.** λ (lambda) = wavelength: the distance from one crest to the next. Red light: 650 nm (billionths of a meter).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"A light wave, λ = 650 nm","x":{"label":"Distance (nm)","min":0,"max":1300,"step":325},"y":{"label":"Wave height","min":-1,"max":1,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Light wave","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[162.5,1],[325,0],[487.5,-1],[650,0],[812.5,1],[975,0],[1137.5,-1],[1300,0]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":162.5,"x2":812.5,"text":"one λ = 650 nm"}],"labels":[{"series":"Light wave","x":162.5,"text":"crest","at":"above"}]},"marks":[{"t":"red laser beam","note":"one pure wavelength"}]},{"text":"**Four wave effects to choose from.** Only one makes light go AROUND an object.","calc":["Reflection: bounces off a surface","Refraction: bends entering a new material","Polarization: filters the wiggle direction","**Diffraction: bends around edges, waves overlap**"],"marks":[{"t":"phenomenon","note":"which wave effect?"}]},{"text":"**Size matters:** light bends around an object noticeably only when the object is tiny, near the wavelength. A hair is.","marks":[{"t":"650 nm","note":"= 0.65 μm"},{"t":"80 μm","note":"only ~120 λ wide"}],"calc":["80 μm ÷ 0.65 μm ≈ **120** wavelengths","small obstacle → light spreads behind it"]},{"text":"**Bands = overlapping waves.** Waves bent around both edges meet: crest + crest → bright; crest + trough → dark. That is **diffraction**.","marks":[{"t":"evenly spaced bright and dark bands","note":"overlap pattern (fringes)"},{"t":"both sides of the hair's shadow","note":"light got behind edges"}]},{"text":"**Check the spacing** with the diffraction formula: the hair acts like a slit of the same width.","marks":[{"t":"λ = 650 nm","note":"λ"},{"t":"2 m away","note":"L"},{"t":"80 μm thick","note":"a"}],"calc":["Δy ≈ λL ÷ a","= (6.5 × 10⁻⁷ m)(2 m) ÷ (8.0 × 10⁻⁵ m)","Δy ≈ **1.6 cm** apart: easy to see"]},{"text":"**Why not the others?** Reflection and refraction off a round strand don't make evenly spaced fringes; polarization only changes wiggle direction.","marks":[{"t":"evenly spaced","note":"the giveaway: fringes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bright/dark fringes around a small obstacle or through a narrow slit (size near λ) = diffraction plus interference. Spacing ≈ λL/a."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Four saturated fatty acids have molar masses of 200, 228, 256 and 284 g/mol. Which would be expected to have the greatest solubility in water at 25 °C?",idStem:"Which saturated fatty acid is most soluble in water?",choices:["C₁₇H₃₅COOH","C₁₃H₂₇COOH","C₁₅H₃₁COOH","C₁₁H₂₃COOH"],ans:3,exp:"Each acid has one polar COOH head; the rest is a nonpolar hydrocarbon tail. **The shorter the nonpolar tail, the more water-soluble**, so the 12-carbon acid, C₁₁H₂₃COOH, dissolves best.",src:"pe4-cp:47",how:{"ask":"All four share the same polar group. What differs between them, and how does that affect solubility in water?","know":"**Like dissolves like.** Water dissolves polar groups (COOH can H-bond) but not long hydrocarbon chains (nonpolar, hydrophobic). For a series with the same polar head, **solubility falls as the carbon chain gets longer**.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: all four are saturated fatty acids with the same polar COOH head; only the nonpolar tail differs.","hl":"greatest solubility in water"},{"text":"Match each formula to its molar mass; length rises with mass.","calc":["C₁₁H₂₃COOH = C₁₂H₂₄O₂ = **200** g/mol","C₁₃H₂₇COOH = **228**","C₁₅H₃₁COOH = **256**","C₁₇H₃₅COOH = **284**"]},{"text":"Longer tails, like stearic and palmitic acid, add more hydrophobic surface and dissolve less.","fig":["stearic-acid","palmitic-acid"],"strike":["C₁₇H₃₅COOH","C₁₅H₃₁COOH"]},{"text":"The shortest tail is the least hydrophobic.","strike":["C₁₃H₂₇COOH"]},{"text":"Answer: C₁₁H₂₃COOH, the 12-carbon acid."}],"trap":"C₁₇H₃₅COOH is first in the list and the heaviest. Larger hydrocarbon molecules are less soluble in water, not more."},teach:[{"text":"**Read the question.** Four fatty acids differ only in size. Which dissolves best in water?","marks":[{"t":"saturated fatty acids","note":"chain + COOH head"},{"t":"200, 228, 256 and 284 g/mol","note":"bigger = longer chain"},{"t":"greatest solubility in water","note":"dissolves best"}]},{"text":"**A fatty acid** = a long carbon chain (tail) ending in a COOH group (head). **Saturated** = only single C–C bonds, full of H.","calc":["C₁₁H₂₃–COOH","tail: C₁₁H₂₃ (only C and H)","head: COOH (has O and H)"],"marks":[{"t":"saturated","note":"only C–C single bonds"}]},{"text":"**Polar** = uneven charge, can H-bond with water. **Nonpolar** = even charge; water ignores it. **Like dissolves like.**","calc":["COOH head: **polar** → likes water","C–H tail: **nonpolar** → avoids water"],"marks":[{"t":"water","note":"polar solvent"}]},{"text":"**Same head, different tail.** Each extra CH₂ adds 14 g/mol, so each acid is 2 carbons longer than the last.","marks":[{"t":"molar masses","note":"mass ↔ chain length"}],"calc":["CH₂ = 12 + 2 = 14 g/mol","228 − 200 = 28 = **2 CH₂**","256 − 228 = 28; 284 − 256 = 28"]},{"text":"**Match each formula to its mass.**","calc":["C₁₁H₂₃COOH (12 C) = **200** g/mol","C₁₃H₂₇COOH (14 C) = 228","C₁₅H₃₁COOH (16 C) = 256","C₁₇H₃₅COOH (18 C) = 284"],"marks":[{"t":"200, 228, 256 and 284 g/mol","note":"12, 14, 16, 18 carbons"}]},{"text":"**Longer tail = more water-avoiding part** attached to the same small polar head → less soluble.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Nonpolar tail carbons (head is one COOH in all four)","x":{"label":"Fatty acid"},"y":{"label":"Carbons in tail","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"categories":["C₁₁H₂₃COOH","C₁₃H₂₇COOH","C₁₅H₃₁COOH","C₁₇H₃₅COOH"],"series":[{"name":"Tail carbons","values":[11,13,15,17]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C₁₁H₂₃COOH","Tail carbons"]]}},{"text":"**So the lightest acid wins:** shortest tail, least nonpolar, most water-soluble.","marks":[{"t":"greatest solubility","note":"shortest tail wins"},{"t":"200","note":"C₁₁H₂₃COOH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** same polar head, different nonpolar chain → the shorter chain (lower molar mass) is more soluble in water; longer chains are more hydrophobic."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Four tautomers of uracil are shown. Which tautomer predominates in RNA at physiological pH and gives the standard pairing with adenine?",idStem:"Which tautomers of guanine and thymine dominate at physiological pH?",choiceFigs:["uracil","uracil-4-enol","uracil-2-enol","uracil-dienol"],choices:["Diketo form","4-Enol form","2-Enol form","Dienol form"],ans:0,exp:"Uracil, like thymine and guanine, exists almost entirely in the **keto (lactam) form**: C=O groups and ring N–H. Its pairing face then shows **N3–H as a donor and C4=O as an acceptor**, which matches adenine's N1 acceptor and N6–H donor. An enol at C4 would flip that pattern to O–H donor and N3 acceptor, a face that fits guanine instead.",src:"pe4-cp:48",how:{"ask":"Which drawing has C=O groups and N–H on the ring, and does that give the donor/acceptor pattern adenine needs?","know":"• **Tautomers** differ only in where an H and a double bond sit, e.g. keto C=O / N–H versus enol C–OH / C=N.\n• In water at pH 7, G, T, U and C exist mainly as **keto/amino** forms; rare enol or imino forms cause mispairing.\n• Base pairing is a **donor (D) / acceptor (A) match** across the pair: A presents A (N1) and D (N6–H); U/T presents D (N3–H) and A (C4=O).\n• On a structure, look for C=O plus N–H in the ring: that is the keto form.","steps":[{"text":"You want the common form in RNA, the one that pairs with A.","hl":"predominates in RNA at physiological pH"},{"text":"Spot the keto form: both oxygens as C=O and both ring nitrogens carrying H. Only the first drawing has that.","fig":"uracil"},{"text":"Check the face that meets adenine, highlighted: N3–H donates and C4=O accepts.","fig":"uracil-pairing-face","calc":["uracil keto face: N3–H = **D**, C4=O = **A**","adenine face: N1 = **A**, N6–H = **D**","D↔A and A↔D: **match**"]},{"text":"An enol at C4 turns that face into O–H donor and N3 acceptor, the guanine-pairing pattern; the 2-enol and dienol are rare forms that do not predominate.","strike":["4-Enol form","2-Enol form","Dienol form"]},{"text":"Answer: the diketo form. Rule: nucleobases in DNA and RNA are drawn as keto/amino tautomers; enol or imino forms mean mispairing."}],"trap":"The enol and dienol forms look 'more aromatic' with C–OH on a fully conjugated ring, which makes them seem more stable. In water the keto form wins by a wide margin, and enols are the rare forms behind mutations."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Corners are carbons with hidden H's. N and O are nitrogen and oxygen. The ring is numbered N1 to C6.","fig":"t4-uracil-read"},{"text":"**Tautomers** are the same atoms with one H moved from N to O and a double bond shifted. Keto: C=O + N–H. Enol: C–OH + C=N.","fig":["t4-uracil-keto-c4","t4-uracil-enol-c4"]},{"text":"**In water at pH 7, uracil is almost entirely keto**: both O's as C=O, both ring N's carrying H. Enol forms are tiny fractions.","fig":"t4-uracil-keto-all"},{"text":"**Base pairs hold by hydrogen bonds.** A **donor (D)** is an H on N or O; an **acceptor (A)** is a lone pair on N or O. Each D must face an A.","fig":"t4-uracil-face"},{"text":"**Adenine's pairing edge** shows N1 (acceptor) and the NH₂ (donor).","fig":"t4-adenine-face"},{"text":"**Keto uracil vs adenine:** N3–H (D) faces N1 (A); C4=O (A) faces NH₂ (D). **D–A, A–D: a match.**","fig":["t4-uracil-face","t4-adenine-face"]},{"text":"**The 4-enol flips the edge:** OH donor, N3 acceptor. Each now faces the same kind on adenine, so it mispairs.","fig":"t4-uracil-4enol-face"},{"text":"**The 2-enol and dienol** move H's onto the C2 oxygen. They are rare forms in water, so they do not predominate.","fig":["t4-uracil-2enol-rare","t4-uracil-dienol-rare"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** normal DNA/RNA bases are **keto** (C=O + ring N–H). In each drawing ask: are both O's C=O, and do both ring N's carry an H?","fig":"t4-uracil-keto-all"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"A DNA strand is labeled with phosphorus-32 (Z = 15) in its backbone. When a ³²P atom decays by emitting a β⁻ particle and an antineutrino, which element replaces it, and how does the new nucleus compare with the ³²P nucleus?",idStem:"³²P undergoes β⁻ decay. What atom is left?",choices:["Sulfur; same mass number, one fewer neutron","Silicon; same mass number, one more neutron","Sulfur; same mass number, one more neutron","Phosphorus; mass number 31, one fewer neutron"],ans:0,exp:"**β⁻ decay: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino.** Z rises 15 → 16, so the atom becomes **sulfur**; A stays 32. ³²P has 32 − 15 = 17 neutrons, ³²S has 32 − 16 = 16, **one fewer**.",src:"pe4-cp:49",how:{"ask":"Write what happens inside the nucleus during β⁻ decay, then track Z, A and the neutron count.","know":"Nuclide notation: ᴬX, A = mass number (protons + neutrons), Z = atomic number (protons).\n• **β⁻**: a neutron becomes a proton and emits an electron → **Z + 1, A same**.\n• β⁺: Z − 1, A same.\n• α: Z − 2, A − 4.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: β⁻ emission means a neutron in the nucleus turns into a proton and an electron.","hl":"emitting a β⁻ particle and an antineutrino","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + ν̄"]},{"text":"Balance the nuclear equation: mass numbers and charges add up on both sides.","calc":["³²₁₅P → ᴬ_ZX + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","A: 32 = A + 0 → A = **32**","Z: 15 = Z − 1 → Z = **16** = sulfur"],"strike":["Silicon; same mass number, one more neutron","Phosphorus; mass number 31, one fewer neutron"]},{"text":"Count neutrons before and after.","calc":["³²P: N = 32 − 15 = **17**","³²S: N = 32 − 16 = **16**","→ **one fewer** neutron"],"strike":["Sulfur; same mass number, one more neutron"]},{"text":"Compare the nucleon counts side by side.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Nucleons before and after β⁻ decay","x":{"label":"Particle"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"categories":["Protons","Neutrons"],"series":[{"name":"³²P","values":[15,17]},{"name":"³²S","values":[16,16]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Neutrons","³²S"]]}},{"text":"Answer: sulfur; same mass number, one fewer neutron."}],"trap":"\"Silicon; one more neutron\" is β⁺ decay or electron capture, where a proton becomes a neutron and Z drops. In β⁻ decay a neutron becomes a proton."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given.** A radioactive phosphorus atom in DNA breaks down.","marks":[{"t":"phosphorus-32","note":"32 = mass number"},{"t":"Z = 15","note":"15 protons"},{"t":"β⁻ particle","note":"an electron shot out"}]},{"text":"**The nucleus** holds protons (+) and neutrons (no charge). Z = protons = which element. Mass number = protons + neutrons.","calc":["³²P: protons Z = **15**","neutrons = 32 − 15 = **17**"],"marks":[{"t":"³²P atom","note":"mass number 32"}]},{"text":"**β⁻ decay:** inside the nucleus a neutron turns into a proton, shooting out an electron (β⁻) and an antineutrino.","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + ν̄","neutrons −1, protons **+1**"],"marks":[{"t":"β⁻ particle","note":"fast electron"},{"t":"antineutrino","note":"tiny, no charge, no mass"}]},{"text":"**Balance the nuclear equation:** top numbers (mass) and bottom numbers (charge) must add up on both sides.","calc":["³²₁₅P → ᴬ_ZX + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","top: 32 = A + 0 → A = **32**","bottom: 15 = Z − 1 → Z = **16**"],"marks":[{"t":"Z = 15","note":"bottom number: 15"}]},{"text":"**Z = 16 is sulfur.** Changing the proton number changes the element.","calc":["periodic table: 14 = Si, 15 = P, **16 = S**"],"marks":[{"t":"which element replaces it","note":"Z = 16 → sulfur"}]},{"text":"**Compare nuclei:** same mass number 32, but one neutron became a proton.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Nucleons before and after β⁻ decay","x":{"label":"Particle"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"categories":["Protons","Neutrons"],"series":[{"name":"³²P","values":[15,17]},{"name":"³²S","values":[16,16]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Neutrons","³²S"]]},"calc":["³²S: neutrons = 32 − 16 = **16** → one fewer"],"marks":[{"t":"the new nucleus","note":"³²S: 16 p, 16 n"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** silicon (Z = 14) would need Z to go DOWN (β⁺). Mass number 31 would need a particle with mass to leave; an electron has none.","marks":[{"t":"emitting a β⁻","note":"Z goes UP by 1"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻: Z + 1, A same (one neutron → proton). β⁺: Z − 1, A same. α: Z − 2, A − 4. Neutrons = A − Z."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"In a zinc enzyme, the side-chain carboxylate of Glu70 coordinates the Zn²⁺ ion, and Zn²⁺ binding is required for activity. Which replacement for Glu70, with its side chain shown, is most likely to preserve activity?",idStem:"An enzyme's Asp binds metal ions through its carboxylate. Which mutation most likely keeps the enzyme active?",choiceFigs:["aspartate-sc","glutamine-sc","arginine-sc","valine-sc"],choices:["Aspartate","Glutamine","Arginine","Valine"],ans:0,exp:"Zn²⁺ is held by the **negatively charged carboxylate oxygens**. Aspartate keeps a **–COO⁻**, only one CH₂ shorter, so it can still bind the metal. Glutamine has the same length but a neutral amide, arginine is positive and repels Zn²⁺, and valine has no oxygen to coordinate.",src:"pe4-cp:50",how:{"ask":"What part of glutamate actually touches the zinc? Which drawn side chain still ends in that same group?","know":"• Metal cations such as Zn²⁺, Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺ are held by **electron-rich ligands**: carboxylates (Asp, Glu), His imidazole, Cys thiolate.\n• **Conservative substitution** keeps the key chemistry, here a –COO⁻; size matters less.\n• Side-chain charges at pH 7: Asp −1, Glu −1, Lys +1, Arg +1; Gln and Asn are neutral amides.\n• On a side-chain drawing, look at the end group: COO⁻ (acid), CONH₂ (amide), NH₃⁺ or guanidinium (base), or only C and H (nonpolar).","steps":[{"text":"The job of the side chain is to coordinate a 2+ metal through its carboxylate.","hl":"side-chain carboxylate of Glu70 coordinates the Zn²⁺ ion"},{"text":"Read the end group of each drawn side chain.","calc":["aspartate: –CH₂–COO⁻ → **charge −1**","glutamine: –(CH₂)₂–CONH₂ → **charge 0**","arginine: –(CH₂)₃–guanidinium → **charge +1**","valine: –CH(CH₃)₂ → **no O or N**"]},{"text":"A positive side chain repels Zn²⁺, and a C/H-only side chain has nothing to bind it.","strike":["Arginine","Valine"]},{"text":"Glutamine matches the size but not the charge: its amide is neutral.","strike":["Glutamine"]},{"text":"Aspartate keeps the carboxylate, highlighted. Answer: aspartate. Rule: preserve the functional group that does the job; length is secondary.","fig":"aspartate-sc"}],"trap":"Glutamine is the same size and shape as glutamate and differs by a single atom, so it looks like the safest swap, but the amide is neutral and binds a cation far more weakly than a carboxylate."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read these drawings.** Each shows an amino acid; the highlighted **side chain** is the part that differs. Corners are carbons with hidden H's.","fig":"t4-glu-read"},{"text":"**Glu70's job:** at pH 7 its side chain ends in **COO⁻**. Zn²⁺ is positive, and the negative O's hold it with their lone pairs (**coordinating** it).","fig":"t4-glu-zn"},{"text":"**A good swap keeps the working group** (a **conservative substitution**). Matching the group matters more than matching the length.","fig":["t4-glu-zn","t4-asp-zn"]},{"text":"**Aspartate** also ends in COO⁻ at pH 7, just one CH₂ shorter.","fig":"t4-asp-zn"},{"text":"**Glutamine** has the same length, but its end is an **amide**, C(=O)NH₂, with **no charge**.","fig":"t4-gln-sc"},{"text":"**Arginine's** end group is **positive** at pH 7, so it repels Zn²⁺.","fig":"t4-arg-sc"},{"text":"**Valine** is only C and H: no lone pairs to offer the metal.","fig":"t4-val-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** keep the side chain's working group; length is secondary. In each drawing, read the side-chain end and ask: will it be negative at pH 7?","fig":"t4-glu-zn"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A synthetic 100-base-pair DNA duplex is analyzed, and guanine makes up 20% of all the bases in the duplex. Assuming standard Watson–Crick pairing throughout, how many interbase hydrogen bonds hold the two strands together?",idStem:"How many hydrogen bonds hold a normal G–C pair and a normal A–T pair together?",choices:["220","240","260","300"],ans:1,exp:"A 100-bp duplex has 200 bases. **Chargaff: G = C**, so G + C = 40% of 200 = 80 bases = **40 G–C pairs**; the other 60 pairs are A–T. **G–C has 3 H-bonds, A–T has 2:** 40 × 3 + 60 × 2 = **240**.",src:"pe4-cp:51",how:{"ask":"Turn the guanine percentage into numbers of G–C and A–T base pairs, then count hydrogen bonds per pair.","know":"**Chargaff's rule:** in double-stranded DNA, [G] = [C] and [A] = [T].\n• A duplex of n base pairs has 2n bases.\n• **G–C pairs form 3 hydrogen bonds; A–T pairs form 2.**","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the percentage is of bases, not base pairs. A 100-bp duplex has 200 bases.","hl":"guanine makes up 20% of all the bases"},{"text":"Use Chargaff's rule G = C to count G–C pairs.","calc":["bases = 2 × 100 = **200**","G = 20% × 200 = **40**","each G pairs with one C → **40 G–C pairs**","A–T pairs = 100 − 40 = **60**"],"strike":["220"]},{"text":"Count hydrogen bonds per pair: G–C has 3, A–T has 2.","fig":["guanine","cytosine"],"calc":["G–C: 40 × **3** = 120","A–T: 60 × **2** = 120","Total = **240**"]},{"text":"Swapping the counts gives 260, and giving every pair 3 bonds gives 300.","calc":["swapped: 60 × 3 + 40 × 2 = 260","all G–C: 100 × 3 = 300"],"strike":["260","300"]},{"text":"Answer: 240 hydrogen bonds."}],"trap":"\"220\" treats 20% of the 100 pairs as G–C pairs. Guanine is 20% of the 200 bases, and every G sits in a G–C pair with an equal number of C, so G–C pairs are 40."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given.** A short piece of DNA; we count the bonds holding its two strands together.","marks":[{"t":"100-base-pair DNA duplex","note":"100 rungs, 2 strands"},{"t":"20% of all the bases","note":"G's share of ALL bases"},{"t":"interbase hydrogen bonds","note":"what we count"}]},{"text":"**DNA duplex** = two strands zipped like a ladder. Each rung = a **base pair**: one base from each strand.","calc":["100 base pairs × 2 bases = **200 bases**"],"marks":[{"t":"duplex","note":"2 strands"}]},{"text":"**Watson–Crick pairing:** A always pairs with T, and G always with C. So every G has a C partner (Chargaff: G = C).","marks":[{"t":"Watson–Crick pairing","note":"A–T and G–C only"}],"calc":["#G = #C and #A = #T"]},{"text":"**Count the G–C pairs.** 20% is of the 200 bases, not of the 100 pairs.","marks":[{"t":"guanine","note":"G"},{"t":"20%","note":"of 200 bases = 40"}],"calc":["G = 20% × 200 = **40**","each G pairs with a C → **40 G–C pairs**","A–T pairs = 100 − 40 = **60**"]},{"text":"**Hydrogen bond** = a weak attraction between an H and an N or O. **G–C pairs hold with 3, A–T pairs with 2.**","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Hydrogen bonds per base pair","x":{"label":"Base pair"},"y":{"label":"H-bonds per pair","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"categories":["G–C","A–T"],"series":[{"name":"H-bonds","values":[3,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["G–C","H-bonds"]]},"marks":[{"t":"hydrogen bonds","note":"weak H···N/O links"}]},{"text":"**Add them up.**","calc":["G–C: 40 × 3 = **120**","A–T: 60 × 2 = **120**","total = **240**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Hydrogen bonds in the 100-bp duplex","x":{"label":"Pair type"},"y":{"label":"Total H-bonds","min":0,"max":150,"step":30},"categories":["40 G–C pairs","60 A–T pairs"],"series":[{"name":"H-bonds","values":[120,120]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["40 G–C pairs","H-bonds"],["60 A–T pairs","H-bonds"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong total comes from one slip.","calc":["20% of 100 pairs: 20×3 + 80×2 = 220","swapped counts: 60×3 + 40×2 = 260","every pair 3 bonds: 100×3 = 300"],"marks":[{"t":"of all the bases","note":"bases, not pairs"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n base pairs = 2n bases; %G = %C; G–C pairs = #G; H-bonds = 3 × (G–C pairs) + 2 × (A–T pairs)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A DNA polymerase requires a divalent metal cation in its active site. Valence configurations: Zn, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²; Fe, [Ar]3d⁶4s²; Mg, [Ne]3s²; Na, [Ne]3s¹. Which ion could NOT fill this role, and why?",idStem:"A DNA polymerase needs a divalent metal ion. Which metal could NOT fill this role?",choices:["Mg, since it has no d electrons","Fe, since it can also form a 3+ ion","Zn, since its d subshell is full","Na, since it forms only a 1+ ion"],ans:3,exp:"Na has a single valence electron; losing a second would break into the [Ne] core, so **Na forms only Na⁺**. Mg (Mg²⁺, the usual polymerase cofactor), Fe and Zn can all form 2+ ions.",src:"pe4-cp:52",how:{"ask":"Which of these metals cannot lose two electrons to become a 2+ ion?","know":"**Divalent** = 2+ charge. Metals lose their outer s electrons first: an atom with **ns²** loses both → 2+. **Group 1 (ns¹)** loses one electron → 1+ only; removing a core electron costs far too much energy. d electrons are not needed for 2+.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the enzyme needs a 2+ cation. Find which metal cannot lose two electrons easily.","hl":"requires a divalent metal cation"},{"text":"Read the valence configurations: Zn, Fe and Mg each have two outer s electrons to lose. Full d subshells or a 3+ option do not prevent a 2+ ion.","hl":"Zn, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²; Fe, [Ar]3d⁶4s²; Mg, [Ne]3s²","strike":["Mg, since it has no d electrons","Fe, since it can also form a 3+ ion","Zn, since its d subshell is full"]},{"text":"Na has one valence electron; the second would come from the [Ne] core. The second ionization energies show the cost.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"First and second ionization energies","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"categories":["Na","Mg","Fe","Zn"],"series":[{"name":"IE₁","values":[496,738,762,906]},{"name":"IE₂","values":[4562,1451,1562,1733]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na","IE₂"]]}},{"text":"Quantify the jump.","calc":["Na: IE₂ ÷ IE₁ = 4562 ÷ 496 ≈ **9.2**","Mg: IE₂ ÷ IE₁ = 1451 ÷ 738 ≈ **2.0**"]},{"text":"Answer: Na, since it forms only a 1+ ion."}],"trap":"\"Mg, since it has no d electrons\" is tempting because the others are transition metals, but d electrons aren't needed. Mg²⁺ is the classic polymerase metal."},teach:[{"text":"**Read the question.** The enzyme needs a metal ion of a certain charge. Which metal can't make it?","marks":[{"t":"divalent metal cation","note":"needs a 2+ ion"},{"t":"Valence configurations","note":"outer electrons"},{"t":"NOT fill this role","note":"find the misfit"}]},{"text":"**Cation** = an atom that lost electrons, so it is positive. **Divalent** = 2+ charge: the metal must lose 2 electrons.","marks":[{"t":"divalent","note":"charge 2+"},{"t":"cation","note":"positive ion"}],"calc":["lose 1 e⁻ → 1+; lose **2** e⁻ → **2+**"]},{"text":"**Read a configuration:** [Ar] or [Ne] = the stable inner core (not lost). What follows = outer electrons; the small number = how many.","marks":[{"t":"Mg, [Ne]3s²","note":"core [Ne] + 2 outer e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Metals lose their outer s electrons first.** Two outer s electrons → an easy 2+ ion.","marks":[{"t":"Zn, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²","note":"lose 4s² → Zn²⁺"},{"t":"Fe, [Ar]3d⁶4s²","note":"lose 4s² → Fe²⁺"},{"t":"Mg, [Ne]3s²","note":"lose 3s² → Mg²⁺"}]},{"text":"**Na has only ONE outer electron.** A second one would have to come out of the stable [Ne] core.","marks":[{"t":"Na, [Ne]3s¹","note":"1 outer e⁻ → Na⁺ only"}]},{"text":"**Ionization energy** = energy to pull off an electron. Na's 2nd one costs about 9× its 1st, far more than the others.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"First and second ionization energies","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"categories":["Na","Mg","Fe","Zn"],"series":[{"name":"IE₁","values":[496,738,762,906]},{"name":"IE₂","values":[4562,1451,1562,1733]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Na","IE₂"]]},"calc":["Na: 4562 ÷ 496 ≈ **9×**","Mg: 1451 ÷ 738 ≈ **2×**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** d electrons are not needed for 2+ (Mg has none and is the usual polymerase metal). A full d subshell or a 3+ option doesn't stop a 2+ ion.","marks":[{"t":"3d¹⁰","note":"full d: still makes 2+"},{"t":"3d⁶","note":"3+ possible, 2+ too"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count the outer s electrons: ns² → 2+ ion easily; ns¹ (group 1) → 1+ only, because the next electron is in the core."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A defibrillator's 200 μF capacitor is charged to 2.0 kV. To store the same amount of energy as gravitational potential energy, a 5.0 kg mass would have to be raised how high? (g ≈ 10 m/s²)",idStem:"A capacitor stores 400 J. To what height would a 5 kg stone need to be lifted to store the same energy? (g ≈ 10 m/s²)",choices:["0.8 m","8 m","16 m","80 m"],ans:1,exp:"Capacitor: **U = ½CV²** = ½ (2.0 × 10⁻⁴ F)(2.0 × 10³ V)² = 400 J. **PE = mgh** → h = 400 ÷ (5.0 × 10) = **8 m**.",src:"pe4-cp:53",how:{"ask":"Find the energy stored in the capacitor, then find the height that gives a 5 kg mass the same energy.","know":"• Energy in a capacitor: **U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts; 1 μF = 10⁻⁶ F, 1 kV = 10³ V).\n• Gravitational potential energy: **PE = mgh** (m in kg, g = 10 m/s², h in m).\nSet them equal and solve for h.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: find the capacitor's stored energy, then the height h at which mgh equals it.","hl":"200 μF capacitor is charged to 2.0 kV"},{"text":"Convert to SI units.","calc":["C = 200 μF = **2.0 × 10⁻⁴ F**","V = 2.0 kV = **2.0 × 10³ V**"]},{"text":"Energy stored in a capacitor.","calc":["U = ½ C V²","U = ½ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × (2.0 × 10³)²","U = ½ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × **4.0 × 10⁶**","U = **400 J**"]},{"text":"Set mgh = U and solve.","calc":["h = U ÷ (m g)","h = 400 J ÷ (5.0 kg × 10 m/s²)","h = 400 ÷ 50 = **8 m**"]},{"text":"Dropping the ½ gives 16 m, and 80 m or 0.8 m come from a factor-of-10 slip in the powers of ten.","strike":["0.8 m","16 m","80 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 8 m."}],"trap":"16 m forgets the ½ in ½CV² (800 J). 80 m divides by m but forgets g."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** Two kinds of stored energy must be made equal.","marks":[{"t":"200 μF","note":"capacitance C"},{"t":"2.0 kV","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"5.0 kg","note":"mass m"}]},{"text":"**A capacitor** stores energy by holding + and − charge apart. Capacitance C (farads, F) = charge it holds per volt.","marks":[{"t":"capacitor","note":"an energy store"},{"t":"charged to","note":"voltage across it"}]},{"text":"**Convert to base units** before using any formula.","calc":["μ = 10⁻⁶: 200 μF = **2.0 × 10⁻⁴ F**","k = 10³: 2.0 kV = **2.0 × 10³ V**"],"marks":[{"t":"200 μF","note":"2.0 × 10⁻⁴ F"},{"t":"2.0 kV","note":"2.0 × 10³ V"}]},{"text":"**Energy in a capacitor: U = ½CV².** Square V first.","calc":["U = ½ × C × V²","V² = (2.0 × 10³)² = **4.0 × 10⁶**","U = ½ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 4.0 × 10⁶","U = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 4.0 × 10⁶ = **400 J**"]},{"text":"**Gravitational potential energy = mgh**: energy stored by lifting mass m to height h.","marks":[{"t":"gravitational potential energy","note":"PE = mgh"},{"t":"raised how high","note":"h: what we want"},{"t":"g ≈ 10 m/s²","note":"g"}]},{"text":"**Set them equal and solve for h.**","marks":[{"t":"same amount of energy","note":"mgh = 400 J"},{"t":"5.0 kg","note":"m"}],"calc":["mgh = 400 J","h = 400 ÷ (5.0 × 10)","h = 400 ÷ 50 = **8 m**"]},{"text":"**PE grows in a straight line with height.** 400 J is reached at 8 m.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"PE of a 5.0 kg mass vs height (g = 10 m/s²)","x":{"label":"Height h (m)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"PE (J)","min":0,"max":500,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"PE","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2,100],[4,200],[6,300],[8,400],[10,500]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"PE","x":8}],"labels":[{"series":"PE","x":8,"text":"400 J at h = 8 m","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong height is one slip.","calc":["forget the ½: U = 800 J → h = 16 m","power-of-ten slip in μ or k → 0.8 m or 80 m"],"marks":[{"t":"2.0 kV","note":"square: 4.0 × 10⁶"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** capacitor energy U = ½CV² (SI units: F, V); lifting energy PE = mgh. Set the energies equal and solve for the unknown."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A rechargeable battery is labeled 4.2 A·h and 3.7 V. Approximately how much charge can it deliver on a full discharge?",idStem:"A battery is rated at 4.2 A·h. About how much charge can it deliver?",choices:["4.2 C","250 C","1.5 × 10⁴ C","5.6 × 10⁴ C"],ans:2,exp:"**An amp-hour is a unit of charge: Q = It.** 4.2 A × 3600 s ≈ 1.5 × 10⁴ C. The voltage is extra data; multiplying by it would give energy in joules.",src:"pe4-cp:54",how:{"ask":"What does the A·h rating measure, and how do you express it in coulombs?","know":"**Current I = Q/t**, so **Q = It**. 1 A = 1 C/s; 1 h = 3600 s. So **1 A·h = 3600 C**. Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: an amp-hour is current × time, which is charge. The voltage is not needed for charge.","hl":"labeled 4.2 A·h and 3.7 V"},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds.","calc":["Q = I × t","Q = 4.2 A × 1 h","1 h = **3600 s**","Q = 4.2 × 3600 ≈ **1.5 × 10⁴ C**"],"strike":["4.2 C","250 C"]},{"text":"Multiplying by 3.7 V would give energy in joules, a different quantity.","calc":["E = QV = 1.5 × 10⁴ C × 3.7 V ≈ 5.6 × 10⁴ J (energy, not charge)"],"strike":["5.6 × 10⁴ C"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.5 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"5.6 × 10⁴ C also multiplies by 3.7 V, which gives energy (J), not charge. 250 C converts hours to minutes instead of seconds."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given and asked.** A battery label shows two numbers; we want charge.","marks":[{"t":"4.2 A·h","note":"amp-hours"},{"t":"3.7 V","note":"voltage"},{"t":"how much charge","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**Charge Q** = amount of electricity, measured in coulombs. **Current (I)** = charge flowing each second: 1 A = 1 C per second.","calc":["I = Q ÷ t","so **Q = I × t**"],"marks":[{"t":"deliver","note":"charge that flows out"}]},{"text":"**An amp-hour is current × time**, which is a charge. So the A·h number already measures charge, just in odd units.","marks":[{"t":"4.2 A·h","note":"4.2 A for 1 hour"}],"calc":["A·h = (C/s) × hours → **charge**"]},{"text":"**Convert hours to seconds** and multiply.","calc":["1 h = 60 × 60 = **3600 s**","Q = 4.2 C/s × 3600 s","Q = 15 120 C ≈ **1.5 × 10⁴ C**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Charge delivered at 4.2 A","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":3600,"step":600},"y":{"label":"Charge Q (C)","min":0,"max":16000,"step":4000},"series":[{"name":"Q","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1200,5040],[2400,10080],[3600,15120]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Q","x":3600}],"labels":[{"series":"Q","x":3600,"text":"1 h: ≈ 1.5 × 10⁴ C","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Voltage is not needed for charge.** Charge × voltage = energy in joules, a different quantity.","marks":[{"t":"3.7 V","note":"not needed for charge"}],"calc":["Q × V = energy (J), not charge","1.5 × 10⁴ × 3.7 ≈ 5.6 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 4.2 C forgets to turn hours into seconds; 250 C uses 60 (minutes) instead of 3600.","calc":["4.2 × 60 = 252 ≈ 250 (minutes: wrong)","4.2 × 3600 ≈ **1.5 × 10⁴** (seconds: right)"],"marks":[{"t":"full discharge","note":"all of it, 1 h at 4.2 A"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A·h × 3600 = coulombs (Q = It, t in seconds). Multiplying by voltage gives energy in joules, not charge."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"In a model of the aorta, a 20 g bolus of blood accelerates from 25 cm/s to 35 cm/s in 0.10 s. What average net force acts on the bolus?",idStem:"20 g of blood speeds up from 25 cm/s to 35 cm/s in 0.10 s. What average force acts on it?",choices:["2.0 N","0.020 N","20 N","0.070 N"],ans:1,exp:"a = Δv/Δt = (0.35 − 0.25 m/s) ÷ 0.10 s = 1.0 m/s². **F = ma** = 0.020 kg × 1.0 m/s² = **0.020 N**. Convert g → kg and cm → m first.",src:"pe4-cp:55",how:{"ask":"Find the acceleration from the change in speed, then use Newton's second law, all in SI units.","know":"**Newton's second law: F = ma** (F in N = kg·m/s², m in kg, a in m/s²). **Average acceleration a = Δv/Δt** = (final − initial velocity) ÷ time. 1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm = 10⁻² m.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: use the change in velocity over time to find acceleration, then F = ma.","hl":"accelerates from 25 cm/s to 35 cm/s in 0.10 s"},{"text":"Convert to SI units.","calc":["m = 20 g = **0.020 kg**","Δv = 35 − 25 = 10 cm/s = **0.10 m/s**"]},{"text":"Acceleration is the slope of the v–t graph.","calc":["a = Δv/Δt = 0.10 ÷ 0.10","a = **1.0 m/s²**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Bolus velocity vs time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.1,"step":0.05},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0.2,"max":0.4,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"v","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.25],[0.05,0.3],[0.1,0.35]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"v","x":0},{"series":"v","x":0.1}],"spans":[{"x":0.1,"from":0.25,"to":0.35,"text":"Δv = 0.10 m/s","side":"left"}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.1,"text":"Δt = 0.10 s"}]}},{"text":"Apply Newton's second law.","calc":["F = ma = 0.020 kg × 1.0 m/s²","F = **0.020 N**"],"strike":["2.0 N","20 N","0.070 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.020 N. 2.0 N keeps the mass in grams; 0.070 N uses the final speed instead of the change."}],"trap":"2.0 N leaves the speeds in cm/s (a = 100). 20 N leaves the mass in grams. 0.070 N uses the final speed instead of the change."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** A lump of blood speeds up; we want the force.","marks":[{"t":"20 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"25 cm/s to 35 cm/s","note":"start → end speed"},{"t":"0.10 s","note":"time taken"}]},{"text":"**Words first.** Bolus = a lump of blood. Accelerate = change speed. Net force = the total push on it.","marks":[{"t":"bolus","note":"a lump of blood"},{"t":"accelerates","note":"speed changes"},{"t":"average net force","note":"total push"}]},{"text":"**Newton's 2nd law: F = ma.** Force (N) = mass (kg) × acceleration (m/s²). Units must be kg and m.","calc":["F = m × a","1 N = 1 kg·m/s²"]},{"text":"**Convert to SI units first.**","marks":[{"t":"20 g","note":"= 0.020 kg"},{"t":"25 cm/s","note":"= 0.25 m/s"},{"t":"35 cm/s","note":"= 0.35 m/s"}],"calc":["20 g ÷ 1000 = **0.020 kg**","Δv = 0.35 − 0.25 = **0.10 m/s**"]},{"text":"**Acceleration** = change in speed ÷ time = the slope of the v–t line.","calc":["a = Δv ÷ Δt = 0.10 ÷ 0.10","a = **1.0 m/s²**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Bolus velocity vs time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.1,"step":0.05},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0.2,"max":0.4,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"v","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.25],[0.05,0.3],[0.1,0.35]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"v","x":0},{"series":"v","x":0.1}],"spans":[{"x":0.1,"from":0.25,"to":0.35,"text":"Δv = 0.10 m/s","side":"left"}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.1,"text":"Δt = 0.10 s"}]}},{"text":"**Now F = ma.**","marks":[{"t":"20 g","note":"m = 0.020 kg"},{"t":"0.10 s","note":"gave a = 1.0 m/s²"}],"calc":["F = 0.020 kg × 1.0 m/s²","F = **0.020 N**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong force skips one step.","calc":["2.0 N: speeds left in cm/s (a = 100)","20 N: mass left as 20 instead of 0.020","0.070 N: used 35 cm/s, not the change"],"marks":[{"t":"from 25 cm/s","note":"use the CHANGE: 10 cm/s"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a = (final − initial speed) ÷ time; F = ma. Convert g → kg and cm → m before multiplying."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A hollow metal box is placed in a uniform external electric field of 500 N/C and allowed to reach electrostatic equilibrium. What is the electric field at the center of the box, and why?",idStem:"Why is the electric field inside a conductor at equilibrium zero?",choices:["500 N/C, since electric fields pass through metal unchanged","Less than 500 N/C, since the metal absorbs part of the field","Zero, since induced surface charges cancel the field inside","Zero, since all of the metal's electrons are bound to atoms"],ans:2,exp:"A conductor's **free electrons move until the net field inside is zero**: charges collect on the surface and their field exactly cancels the external one inside. This is how a Faraday cage shields its interior.",src:"pe4-cp:56",how:{"ask":"What do the free charges in a metal do when an outside field pushes on them, and when do they stop moving?","know":"In a **conductor**, some electrons are free to move. An electric field exerts force on them (F = qE), so they move as long as any field is present inside. They stop (**electrostatic equilibrium**) only when **E inside = 0**. Excess or induced charge sits on the **surface**.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: a metal box is a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium; ask what its free charges do.","hl":"allowed to reach electrostatic equilibrium"},{"text":"The external field pushes free electrons to one face, leaving the opposite face positive. Metal does not 'absorb' field, and its electrons are not all bound.","strike":["Less than 500 N/C, since the metal absorbs part of the field","Zero, since all of the metal's electrons are bound to atoms"]},{"text":"The surface charges make a field opposite the external field. Charges keep moving until the two cancel inside.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Field magnitude across the box","x":{"label":"Position (cm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"E (N/C)","min":0,"max":600,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"E","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,500],[2,500],[2,0],[5,0],[8,0],[8,500],[10,500]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"E","x":5}],"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":8,"text":"inside the box"}],"labels":[{"series":"E","x":0,"text":"outside: 500 N/C","at":"right"},{"series":"E","x":5,"text":"center: E = 0","at":"above"}]},"strike":["500 N/C, since electric fields pass through metal unchanged"]},{"text":"Answer: zero, since induced surface charges cancel the field inside, the principle of a Faraday cage."}],"trap":"\"Zero, since electrons are bound\" has the right value but the wrong reason. Metals have free electrons, and that is why they can cancel the field."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given.** A metal box sits in an electric field. What is the field in the middle?","marks":[{"t":"hollow metal box","note":"conductor"},{"t":"500 N/C","note":"field outside"},{"t":"center of the box","note":"where we look"}]},{"text":"**Electric field** = the push on a charge at each spot. 500 N/C = 500 newtons on each coulomb of charge. Uniform = same everywhere.","marks":[{"t":"uniform external electric field","note":"same push everywhere"}]},{"text":"**Metal = conductor:** some of its electrons are free to move through it. Push them and they slide.","marks":[{"t":"metal","note":"free electrons"}]},{"text":"**The field pushes free electrons to one face;** the other face is left positive. These are **induced surface charges**.","calc":["electrons → one face (−)","opposite face left (+)","their own field points **against** the outside field"]},{"text":"**Equilibrium** = charges have stopped moving. Any field left inside would keep pushing electrons, so they stop only when the inside field is zero.","marks":[{"t":"electrostatic equilibrium","note":"charges stopped moving"}],"calc":["field inside ≠ 0 → electrons keep moving","stop only when **E inside = 0**"]},{"text":"**Picture it:** 500 N/C outside, zero everywhere inside the metal box.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Field strength across the box","x":{"label":"Position (cm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"E (N/C)","min":0,"max":600,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"E","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,500],[2,500],[2,0],[5,0],[8,0],[8,500],[10,500]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"E","x":5}],"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":8,"text":"inside the box"}],"labels":[{"series":"E","x":0,"text":"outside: 500 N/C","at":"right"},{"series":"E","x":5,"text":"center: E = 0","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Wrong reasons:** metal doesn't 'absorb' field; the surface charges cancel it. Metals have free electrons, not only bound ones. Fields don't pass through unchanged.","marks":[{"t":"What is the electric field","note":"E = 0: charges cancel it"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** inside a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, E = 0; charge sits on the surface. A closed metal shell shields its inside (Faraday cage)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biology",aamc:"4B",q:"A patient's blood pressure is 135/90 mmHg and heart rate is 72 beats/min. Mean arterial pressure is estimated as MAP = (2P_d + P_s)/3, where P_d is diastolic and P_s is systolic pressure. What is the patient's MAP?",idStem:"Blood pressure is 135/90 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure, MAP = (2 × diastolic + systolic) ÷ 3?",choices:["120 mmHg","112 mmHg","90 mmHg","105 mmHg"],ans:3,exp:"Systolic = 135 (top number), diastolic = 90 (bottom). MAP = (2 × 90 + 135) ÷ 3 = 315 ÷ 3 = **105 mmHg**. Diastole is weighted double because it lasts longer.",src:"pe4-cp:57",how:{"ask":"Which number is systolic and which is diastolic, and which one gets doubled in the formula?","know":"Blood pressure is written **systolic/diastolic** (peak pressure while the heart contracts / lowest pressure while it relaxes). **MAP = (2P_d + P_s)/3** weights diastole twice because the heart spends about ⅔ of each cycle in diastole. Heart rate is not needed.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: read systolic (top) and diastolic (bottom) pressures; heart rate is extra data.","hl":"135/90 mmHg"},{"text":"Plug into the given formula, weighting diastolic twice.","hl":"MAP = (2P_d + P_s)/3","calc":["P_s = **135**, P_d = **90** mmHg","MAP = (2 × 90 + 135) ÷ 3","MAP = 315 ÷ 3 = **105 mmHg**"]},{"text":"The MAP sits closer to diastolic because the heart spends about two-thirds of each cycle in diastole.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Simplified arterial pressure over one beat","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.8,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":80,"max":140,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Arterial pressure","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,90],[0.15,135],[0.3,112],[0.6,97],[0.8,90]]}]},"graph":{"yLine":105,"labels":[{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0.15,"text":"systolic 135","at":"right"},{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0,"text":"diastolic 90","at":"right"},{"x":0.5,"y":105,"text":"MAP = 105","at":"above"}],"shade":[{"x1":0.3,"x2":0.8,"text":"diastole: most of the cycle"}]}},{"text":"Swapping P_d and P_s gives 120; 90 is diastolic alone; 112 is the simple average, (135 + 90) ÷ 2.","strike":["120 mmHg","112 mmHg","90 mmHg"]},{"text":"Answer: 105 mmHg."}],"trap":"120 mmHg doubles the systolic value instead ((2 × 135 + 90)/3). 112 mmHg is the plain average of the two numbers."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what's given.** A blood-pressure reading, a heart rate, and a formula to plug into.","marks":[{"t":"135/90 mmHg","note":"top/bottom pressures"},{"t":"72 beats/min","note":"heart rate"},{"t":"MAP = (2P_d + P_s)/3","note":"formula given"}]},{"text":"**Systolic** = peak pressure while the heart squeezes (top number). **Diastolic** = lowest pressure while it relaxes (bottom number).","marks":[{"t":"systolic","note":"peak: top number"},{"t":"diastolic","note":"lowest: bottom number"}],"calc":["135/90 → P_s = **135**, P_d = **90**"]},{"text":"**Read the formula piece by piece.**","marks":[{"t":"2P_d","note":"diastolic counted twice"},{"t":"P_s","note":"systolic once"},{"t":"/3","note":"÷ 3 (3 parts)"}]},{"text":"**Plug in** the two pressures, diastolic twice.","marks":[{"t":"135","note":"P_s"},{"t":"90 mmHg","note":"P_d (×2)"}],"calc":["MAP = (2 × 90 + 135) ÷ 3","= (180 + 135) ÷ 3","= 315 ÷ 3 = **105 mmHg**"]},{"text":"**Why twice?** The heart spends about ⅔ of each beat relaxed (diastole), so the average sits nearer the low number.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Simplified arterial pressure over one beat","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":0.8,"step":0.2},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":80,"max":140,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Arterial pressure","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,90],[0.15,135],[0.3,112],[0.6,97],[0.8,90]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0.3,"x2":0.8,"text":"diastole: most of beat"}],"labels":[{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0.15,"text":"systolic 135","at":"right"},{"series":"Arterial pressure","x":0,"text":"diastolic 90","at":"right"},{"x":0.5,"y":105,"text":"MAP = 105","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Heart rate is extra data**: it isn't in the formula. Exams add numbers you don't need.","marks":[{"t":"72 beats/min","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong value is one slip.","calc":["simple average: (135 + 90) ÷ 2 = 112.5","swapped: (2 × 135 + 90) ÷ 3 = 120","diastolic alone = 90"],"marks":[{"t":"2P_d","note":"double the LOW number"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** BP = systolic/diastolic. MAP = (2 × diastolic + systolic) ÷ 3, closer to diastolic. Use only the numbers the formula needs."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"biology",aamc:"4C",q:"The trace shows the membrane potential at a neuron's axon hillock during a single action potential. Which event is mainly responsible for the change in potential at 2.0 ms?",idStem:"Which happens FIRST when an action potential is generated?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[0.5,-70],[1,-55],[1.5,30],[2,-20],[2.5,-75],[3,-80],[4,-72],[5,-70]]}]},choices:["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, and Na⁺ flows in","Voltage-gated K⁺ channels are open, and K⁺ flows out","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in","Voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, and K⁺ flows in"],ans:1,exp:"At 2.0 ms the potential is **falling from the +30 mV peak toward rest: repolarization**. By then voltage-gated Na⁺ channels have inactivated, and **voltage-gated K⁺ channels are open, letting K⁺ flow out** down its gradient, which makes the inside more negative.",src:"pe4-cp:58",how:{"ask":"Is the trace rising or falling at 2.0 ms, and which ion moving which way would make the inside of the cell more negative?","know":"• Phases of an action potential: **threshold** (about −55 mV) → **depolarization** (Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ in, rise to about +30 mV) → **repolarization** (Na⁺ channels inactivate, K⁺ channels open, K⁺ out, fall) → **hyperpolarization** (K⁺ channels slow to close, dip below rest) → return to rest.\n• K⁺ is concentrated inside the cell, so open K⁺ channels let it **leave**, never enter, under normal conditions.\n• The Na⁺/K⁺ pump maintains gradients slowly; it does not shape each spike.","steps":[{"text":"The question asks about one moment on the trace.","hl":"at 2.0 ms"},{"text":"Read the trace: the peak is +30 mV at 1.5 ms, and at 2.0 ms the potential has already fallen to −20 mV.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Vm","x":1.5},{"series":"Vm","x":2}],"xLine":2,"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":1.5,"text":"peak +30 mV","at":"right"},{"series":"Vm","x":2,"text":"2.0 ms: −20 mV, falling","at":"right"}]},"calc":["1.5 ms: **+30 mV** (peak)","2.0 ms: **−20 mV** → falling: repolarization"]},{"text":"A falling potential is not the Na⁺ upstroke, which happens between 1.0 and 1.5 ms.","strike":["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, and Na⁺ flows in"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":1.5,"text":"Na⁺ upstroke"}]}},{"text":"K⁺ is high inside, so open K⁺ channels let it out; the pump is too slow to shape a millisecond spike.","strike":["Voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, and K⁺ flows in","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1.5,"x2":2.5,"text":"K⁺ out: falling"}],"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":2,"text":"K⁺ flows out","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: voltage-gated K⁺ channels are open, and K⁺ flows out. Rule: on a spike trace, rising = Na⁺ in, falling = K⁺ out, dip below rest = K⁺ channels slow to close.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":1.5,"text":"rising: Na⁺ in"},{"x1":1.5,"x2":2.5,"text":"falling: K⁺ out"},{"x1":2.5,"x2":4,"text":"below rest"}]}}],"trap":"'K⁺ flows in' uses the right channel but the wrong direction. K⁺ is high inside the cell, so when its channels open it leaves, carrying positive charge out."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = inside of the cell more positive; down = more negative.** mV = millivolts.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"membrane potential","note":"y axis: inside voltage"}]},{"text":"**Right = time passing,** in milliseconds (ms). The whole spike lasts only a few ms.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"at 2.0 ms","note":"find this time"}]},{"text":"**An action potential** is a nerve cell's signal: a quick spike up and back down. **Axon hillock** = where the spike starts.","marks":[{"t":"axon hillock","note":"where the spike starts"},{"t":"action potential","note":"the spike"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":1.5,"text":"peak +30 mV","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Flat start = rest (−70 mV).** Once it climbs to threshold (about −55 mV), the spike fires on its own.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":0.5,"text":"rest −70 mV"}],"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":1,"text":"threshold −55 mV","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Ions** = charged atoms. Na⁺ is high OUTSIDE the cell; K⁺ is high INSIDE. An open **channel** lets an ion flow from high to low.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Typical ion concentrations (mM)","x":{"label":"Ion"},"y":{"label":"Concentration (mM)","min":0,"max":160,"step":40},"categories":["Na⁺","K⁺"],"series":[{"name":"Inside","values":[12,140]},{"name":"Outside","values":[145,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["K⁺","Inside"]]}},{"text":"**Rising = Na⁺ rushes in** through voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (they open when the voltage changes). + charge in → inside goes up.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":1.5,"text":"rising: Na⁺ in"}]}},{"text":"**Read 2.0 ms:** the trace has passed the peak and is at −20 mV, falling.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Vm","x":1.5},{"series":"Vm","x":2}],"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":2,"text":"2.0 ms: −20, falling","at":"right"}]},"marks":[{"t":"at 2.0 ms","note":"falling here"}]},{"text":"**Falling = K⁺ flows out.** Na⁺ channels have shut; voltage-gated K⁺ channels are open, + charge leaves, inside gets more negative.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1.5,"x2":2.5,"text":"falling: K⁺ out"}],"labels":[{"series":"Vm","x":2,"text":"K⁺ flows out","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** K⁺ can't flow IN through channels (it's already high inside). The Na⁺/K⁺ pump is far too slow to shape a 1 ms spike.","marks":[{"t":"mainly responsible","note":"the fast channel event"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2.5,"x2":4,"text":"K⁺ slow to close"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a spike trace: rising = Na⁺ in, falling = K⁺ out, dip below rest = K⁺ channels slow to close. The pump only keeps the gradients.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1,"x2":1.5,"text":"Na⁺ in"},{"x1":1.5,"x2":2.5,"text":"K⁺ out"}]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 4: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"A 2.0 kg water bath at 20 °C is heated to 70 °C by a 1.0 kW immersion heater. Assuming all of the heater's energy goes into the water, approximately how long does the heating take? (specific heat of water = 4.2 J/(g·°C))",idStem:"How much heat raises 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 70 °C? (c = 4.2 kJ/kg·°C)",choices:["0.42 s","2.8 min","7.0 min","9.8 min"],ans:2,exp:"**q = mcΔT** = 2000 g × 4.2 J/(g·°C) × 50 °C = 4.2 × 10⁵ J. **Power = energy/time**, so t = 4.2 × 10⁵ J ÷ 1.0 × 10³ W = 420 s = **7.0 min**.",src:"pe4-cp:59",how:{"ask":"Find the heat the water must absorb, then divide by the heater's power to get time.","know":"**q = mcΔT.** q = heat (J), m = mass (must match c's unit, here grams), c = specific heat (J/(g·°C)), **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. 1 kg = 1000 g; 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: heat needed q = mcΔT, then time = q ÷ power.","hl":"1.0 kW immersion heater"},{"text":"Match units: c is per gram, so convert kg to g; use the temperature change.","hl":"specific heat of water = 4.2 J/(g·°C)","calc":["m = 2.0 kg = **2000 g**","ΔT = 70 − 20 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["0.42 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat absorbed.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 2000 × 4.2 × 50","q = **4.2 × 10⁵ J**"],"strike":["9.8 min"]},{"text":"Divide by power: 1 kW = 1000 J each second.","calc":["t = q ÷ P = 4.2 × 10⁵ J ÷ 1.0 × 10³ J/s","t = **420 s** = **7.0 min**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Bath temperature while heating","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":480,"step":60},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":0,"max":80,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,20],[120,34.3],[240,48.6],[420,70]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Water","x":420}],"spans":[{"x":420,"from":20,"to":70,"text":"ΔT = 50 °C","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":420,"text":"70 °C at 420 s = 7.0 min","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"2.8 min uses ΔT = 20 °C; the bath heats at about 7 °C per minute, so 50 °C takes 7 min.","strike":["2.8 min"]},{"text":"Answer: about 7.0 min."}],"trap":"\"9.8 min\" uses the final temperature, 70 °C, instead of the change, 50 °C. \"0.42 s\" leaves the mass in kilograms while c is per gram."},teach:[{"text":"**Find every number.** We need the heat to warm the water, then how long the heater takes to deliver it.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"20 °C is heated to 70 °C","note":"start → end temp"},{"t":"1.0 kW","note":"heater power"}]},{"text":"**Specific heat** = energy needed to warm 1 g by 1 °C. Water needs 4.2 J per gram per degree.","marks":[{"t":"specific heat of water = 4.2 J/(g·°C)","note":"4.2 J warms 1 g by 1 °C"}]},{"text":"**Power** = energy delivered per second. 1 kW = 1000 W = 1000 J every second.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 kW","note":"1000 J each second"}],"calc":["power = energy ÷ time","so **time = energy ÷ power**"]},{"text":"**Match units:** c is per gram, so turn kg into g. Use the temperature CHANGE.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 kg","note":"→ 2000 g"},{"t":"20 °C is heated to 70 °C","note":"ΔT = 50 °C"}],"calc":["m = 2.0 kg = **2000 g**","ΔT = 70 − 20 = **50 °C**"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = m × c × ΔT","q = 2000 × 4.2 × 50","2000 × 50 = 100 000","100 000 × 4.2 = **4.2 × 10⁵ J**"],"marks":[{"t":"4.2 J/(g·°C)","note":"c"}]},{"text":"**Time = heat ÷ power.**","calc":["t = 4.2 × 10⁵ J ÷ 1.0 × 10³ J/s","t = **420 s**","420 ÷ 60 = **7.0 min**"],"marks":[{"t":"1.0 kW","note":"÷ 1000 J/s"},{"t":"how long","note":"t = q ÷ P"}]},{"text":"**On a graph:** the bath warms steadily, about 7 °C per minute, and reaches 70 °C at 420 s.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Bath temperature while heating","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":480,"step":60},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":0,"max":80,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,20],[120,34.3],[240,48.6],[420,70]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Water","x":420}],"spans":[{"x":420,"from":20,"to":70,"text":"ΔT = 50 °C","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Water","x":420,"text":"70 °C at 420 s","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** each wrong time is one slip.","calc":["ΔT = 20 (start temp) → 168 s = 2.8 min","ΔT = 70 (end temp) → 588 s = 9.8 min","kg not turned to g → 420 J → 0.42 s"],"marks":[{"t":"all of the heater's energy goes into the water","note":"no heat lost"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT (units of m must match c; ΔT = final − initial), then time = q ÷ power, with 1 W = 1 J/s."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 6 · Chem/Phys (built 2026-11-11) ──
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"A student treats 4-heptanol with an oxidizing agent and records the IR spectrum of the purified product, shown. Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?",idStem:"A reaction turns a ketone into an alcohol. Which new IR band proves the alcohol formed?",choiceFigs:["4-heptanone","4-heptanol","heptanoic-acid","e-3-heptene"],chart:{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectrum of the oxidation product","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[600,85],[800,78],[1000,80],[1200,72],[1380,60],[1460,55],[1600,88],[1715,10],[1800,90],[2000,94],[2250,94],[2500,93],[2800,88],[2870,45],[2960,30],[3100,90],[3300,93],[3600,94],[4000,95]]}]},choices:["Ketone","Unchanged alcohol","Carboxylic acid","Alkene"],ans:0,exp:"The spectrum has a strong, sharp band at **1715 cm⁻¹ (C=O)**, C–H dips near 2900 cm⁻¹, and **nothing broad at 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ (no O–H)**. Oxidizing a secondary alcohol gives a ketone: 4-heptanone.",src:"pe6-cp:1",how:{"ask":"Which two windows of the spectrum tell you whether the product has a C=O and whether it still has an O–H?","know":"• IR shows which bonds a molecule has. Check only the windows that matter: **O–H ≈ 3200–3600 cm⁻¹, broad and rounded**; **C=O ≈ 1700–1750 cm⁻¹, sharp and strong**; sp³ C–H ≈ 2850–3000 cm⁻¹ (almost every organic molecule has it); C=C ≈ 1650 cm⁻¹, weak.\n• A carboxylic acid shows a C=O **plus** a very broad O–H smeared across 2500–3300 cm⁻¹.\n• A secondary alcohol (C bearing OH and two carbons) oxidizes to a ketone and stops there.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the reaction: an oxidant acting on a secondary alcohol should remove the O–H and form a C=O.","hl":"treats 4-heptanol with an oxidizing agent","fig":["4-heptanol","4-heptanone"]},{"text":"Read the carbonyl window first: a deep, narrow dip at 1715 cm⁻¹ means a C=O is present.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Product","x":1715}],"xLine":1715,"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O window"}],"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":1715,"text":"1715 cm⁻¹: 10 % → C=O","at":"right"}]},"calc":["1715 cm⁻¹: transmittance **10 %** → strong C=O"]},{"text":"Now the O–H window: at 3300 cm⁻¹ the trace stays near the top, so there is no O–H. That removes the alcohol and the acid, whose O–H bands are broad and deep.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Product","x":3300}],"xLine":3300,"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3550,"text":"O–H window"}],"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":3300,"text":"93 %: no O–H dip","at":"below"}]},"strike":["Unchanged alcohol","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"An alkene has no C=O, so it could not make the 1715 dip; its weak C=C band would sit near 1650 cm⁻¹.","strike":["Alkene"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1620,"x2":1680,"text":"C=C would be ~1650"}],"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":1715,"text":"dip is C=O, not C=C","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: the ketone. Rule: look at 1700 for C=O, then at 3300 for O–H; C=O with no O–H means a ketone or aldehyde.","fig":"4-heptanone","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":1715,"text":"C=O present","at":"right"},{"series":"Product","x":3300,"text":"no O–H","at":"below"}]}}],"trap":"The carboxylic acid also has a C=O near 1715 cm⁻¹, but its O–H would add a very broad band from 2500 to 3300 cm⁻¹. This trace is flat there except for the narrow C–H dips."},teach:[{"text":"**Up and down = how much infrared light gets through** the sample. Near the top (≈ 90–100 %) = nothing absorbed. **A dip downward = the molecule absorbed light**: in IR, a “peak” is really a dip.","graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = wavenumber**, the kind of light. **Each type of bond absorbs at its own wavenumber**, so the position of a dip tells you which bond is in the molecule.","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**The C=O window is near 1700 cm⁻¹.** A carbonyl (ketone, aldehyde, acid) makes a deep, narrow dip here.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O window (~1700)"}]}},{"text":"**The O–H window is near 3300 cm⁻¹.** An alcohol makes a broad, rounded dip here; a carboxylic acid makes an even broader one that runs from about 2500 to 3300.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3550,"text":"O–H window (~3300)"}]}},{"text":"**Ignore the C–H dips just below 3000 cm⁻¹**: almost every organic molecule has them, so they do not tell compounds apart.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Product","x":2960,"text":"C–H dips (everyone has these)","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**How to read a dip: measure how far it falls from the flat top line.** Example with the C–H dip: the trace drops from about 90 % to 30 %, a deep dip, so that bond is clearly present.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":2960,"from":90,"to":30,"text":"deep dip","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Your job now:** look in each window. Is there a deep dip at ~1700? Is there a dip at ~3300? Then pick the structure that has exactly those bonds.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O?"},{"x1":3200,"x2":3550,"text":"O–H?"}]}}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"A reaction produces a mixture of two constitutional isomers that contain the same functional groups. Which technique would best allow a chemist to determine the relative amounts of the two isomers in the crude product?",idStem:"Which technique gives the RATIO of two isomeric products in a mixture?",choices:["IR spectroscopy","¹H NMR spectroscopy","UV–visible spectroscopy","Melting point analysis"],ans:1,exp:"**¹H NMR peak areas (integrals) are proportional to the number of H atoms producing them.** Compare a peak unique to each isomer and you get their mole ratio. Both isomers have the same functional groups, so their IR spectra look alike.",src:"pe6-cp:2",how:{"ask":"Which technique tells you HOW MUCH of each product there is (a ratio), not just WHAT is there?","know":"IR and UV identify groups or conjugation. In ¹H NMR, the **area (integral) of a peak is proportional to the number of H atoms** making it, so peak areas can be compared to get amounts.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the question asks for amounts, a ratio, not for identity.","hl":"determine the relative amounts"},{"text":"The isomers share functional groups, so their IR bands overlap, and a melting point only shows impurity.","hl":"contain the same functional groups","strike":["IR spectroscopy","Melting point analysis"]},{"text":"UV–vis needs conjugation differences and gives no H-by-H count; it is out too.","strike":["UV–visible spectroscopy"]},{"text":"Rule: a ¹H NMR integral is proportional to the number of H making the peak. Example: isomer A has a unique CH₃ peak, isomer B a unique CH₂ peak.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Integrals of the peaks unique to each isomer","x":{"label":"Peak"},"y":{"label":"Integral","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"categories":["A: CH₃ singlet","B: CH₂ quartet"],"series":[{"name":"Integral","values":[6,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["A: CH₃ singlet","Integral"],["B: CH₂ quartet","Integral"]]}},{"text":"Divide each integral by its number of H to get moles of each isomer.","calc":["A: 6 ÷ 3 H = **2**","B: 2 ÷ 2 H = **1**","A : B = **2 : 1**"]},{"text":"Answer: ¹H NMR, because peak areas count H atoms and so give the mole ratio of the two isomers."}],"trap":"IR identifies functional groups, and these isomers share theirs. A mixed melting point shows a sample is impure, but it cannot give a ratio."},teach:[{"text":"**Read what is asked.** Not *what* the molecules are, but **how much** of each one is in the mix.","marks":[{"t":"two constitutional isomers","note":"2 different molecules"},{"t":"same functional groups","note":"same reactive parts"},{"t":"relative amounts","note":"how much of each = ratio"}]},{"text":"**Constitutional isomers** = same atoms, joined in a different order. Example: 1-propanol and 2-propanol are both C₃H₈O with one –OH.","marks":[{"t":"constitutional isomers","note":"same formula, new order"}]},{"text":"**IR spectroscopy** detects functional groups (O–H, C=O). Both isomers have the **same groups**, so their IR spectra look almost alike and give no amounts.","marks":[{"t":"same functional groups","note":"IR sees these: looks alike"}]},{"text":"**UV–vis** only sees conjugated (alternating double bond) systems. A **melting point** of a mixture only says \"impure\", not the ratio.","marks":[{"t":"a mixture","note":"melting point: just impure"},{"t":"crude product","note":"unpurified mixture"}]},{"text":"**¹H NMR** gives one peak for each different kind of H. The **area (integral)** of a peak is proportional to the **number of H** making it.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Example: peak unique to each isomer","x":{"label":"Peak"},"y":{"label":"Integral (area)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"categories":["A: CH₃ peak (3 H)","B: CH₂ peak (2 H)"],"series":[{"name":"Integral","values":[6,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["A: CH₃ peak (3 H)","Integral"],["B: CH₂ peak (2 H)","Integral"]]}},{"text":"**Turn areas into amounts:** divide each area by the number of H in that peak.","marks":[{"t":"relative amounts","note":"area ÷ H per peak"}],"calc":["A: 6 ÷ 3 H = **2**","B: 2 ÷ 2 H = **1**","A : B = **2 : 1**"]},{"text":"**Why it works here:** a different atom order puts at least one H in a **different neighborhood**, so each isomer has a peak of its own.","marks":[{"t":"constitutional isomers","note":"each has its own H peak"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** \"how much of each\" in a mixture → ¹H NMR integrals (area ∝ number of H). IR/UV tell you *what* groups are there, not how much."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Which labeled carbon in the structure shown is a chirality center?",idStem:"A carbon carries H, OH, a benzyl group and a phenyl group. Is the molecule chiral?",fig:"ethylphenylhexanol-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:2,exp:"A chirality center is an sp³ carbon bonded to **four different groups**. Carbon III carries H, CH₃, the phenyl ring and the CH₂ chain. Carbon I has the OH but **two identical ethyl groups**; II has two H atoms; IV is a flat sp² ring carbon with only three neighbors.",src:"pe6-cp:4",how:{"ask":"For each labeled carbon, what four groups are attached, and are any two of them the same?","know":"• **Chirality center (stereocenter):** a carbon with four different groups attached; it makes the molecule non-superimposable on its mirror image.\n• Fast screens: a CH₂ or CH₃ carbon has two identical H atoms, so it is out. A carbon in a double bond or an aromatic ring has only three neighbors, so it is out.\n• Compare whole branches, not just the first atom: two ethyl groups are identical however they are drawn.","steps":[{"text":"You need a carbon with four different groups. Look at what each labeled carbon is bonded to.","hl":"chirality center","fig":"ethylphenylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"II is a CH₂, two identical H atoms. IV sits in the flat aromatic ring with only three neighbors.","strike":["II","IV"]},{"text":"I: list its groups. Two of them match.","calc":["I: OH, **CH₂CH₃**, **CH₂CH₃**, CH₂–chain","two identical groups → not a chirality center"],"strike":["I"]},{"text":"III: list its groups. All four differ.","calc":["III: H, CH₃, phenyl, CH₂–C(OH)Et₂","**four different** → chirality center"],"fig":"ethylphenylhexanol-c5"},{"text":"Answer: III. Rule: skip CH₂, CH₃ and sp² carbons, then check the rest for two matching branches."}],"trap":"I carries the OH, so it looks like the special carbon, but two of its branches are both ethyl groups. Two identical groups mean it is not a chirality center."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Corners and line ends are carbons; their H atoms are hidden. Letters are other atoms. I–IV mark the four carbons to judge.","fig":"t6-ephx-read"},{"text":"**Chirality center = a carbon bonded to four different groups.** Like your hands, its mirror image cannot be stacked on it.","fig":"t6-ephx-question"},{"text":"**A carbon always makes 4 bonds.** Count the drawn lines; the missing ones are hidden H atoms.","fig":"t6-ephx-count-h"},{"text":"**Carbon II carries two H atoms.** Two of its four groups are identical, so it is out.","fig":"t6-ephx-c2-out"},{"text":"**Carbon IV sits in the flat benzene ring.** It is bonded to only three atoms, so it cannot have four groups.","fig":"t6-ephx-c4-out"},{"text":"**Carbon I holds OH and two ethyl (CH₂CH₃) branches.** Compare whole branches: two are identical, so it is out.","fig":"t6-ephx-c1-out"},{"text":"**Carbon III: list its four groups.** CH₃, the ring, the CH₂ chain, plus one hidden H. No two match.","fig":"t6-ephx-c3-groups"},{"text":"**All four differ, so this carbon is a chirality center.**","fig":"t6-ephx-c3-yes"},{"text":"**Rule:** skip carbons with 2–3 H and carbons in double bonds or rings; for the rest, look for two identical branches. In the drawing, ask of each labeled carbon: are all four groups different?","fig":"ethylphenylhexanol-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"A cart starts from rest on a level track. Its position is plotted against time in the figure. During which interval is the net force on the cart zero?",idStem:"How do you get velocity from a displacement-versus-time graph?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Position of the cart versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":24,"step":4},"series":[{"name":"Cart","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[0.5,0.25],[1,1],[1.5,2.25],[2,4],[3,8],[4,12],[5,16],[5.5,17.75],[6,19],[6.5,19.75],[7,20]]}]},choices:["0 to 2 s","2 to 5 s","5 to 7 s","None of the intervals"],ans:1,exp:"Net force is zero when acceleration is zero, that is, when velocity is constant. On a position–time graph that is a **straight sloped line**: from 2 to 5 s the cart covers **4 m every second**. Before 2 s the curve steepens (speeding up) and after 5 s it flattens (slowing down), so a net force acts in both.",src:"pe6-cp:5",how:{"ask":"Which part of the position–time graph shows a velocity that does not change?","know":"• Newton's second law: F_net = ma. **Zero net force ⇔ zero acceleration ⇔ constant velocity**, which can be fast, not only zero.\n• On an x–t graph, velocity = slope. A straight line = constant velocity. A curve bending upward = speeding up; a curve leveling off = slowing down.\n• Check with differences: equal Δx in equal time steps means constant velocity.","steps":[{"text":"Zero net force means zero acceleration: look for the stretch where the slope, the velocity, stays the same.","hl":"During which interval is the net force on the cart zero","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"0–2 s"},{"x1":2,"x2":5,"text":"2–5 s"},{"x1":5,"x2":7,"text":"5–7 s"}]}},{"text":"Between 2 and 5 s the points lie on a straight line. Compute its slope.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":2},{"series":"Cart","x":5}],"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":5,"text":"run = 3 s"}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":4,"to":16,"text":"rise = 16 − 4 = 12 m","side":"left"}]},"calc":["slope = (16 − 4) m ÷ (5 − 2) s","= **4 m/s**, the same every second"]},{"text":"From 0 to 2 s the gaps grow, 1 m in the first second and 3 m in the next: the cart speeds up, so a net force acts.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":1},{"series":"Cart","x":2}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"speeding up"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":1,"text":"1 m","side":"left"},{"x":2,"from":1,"to":4,"text":"3 m","side":"left"}]},"strike":["0 to 2 s"]},{"text":"From 5 to 7 s the gaps shrink, 3 m then 1 m, and the curve levels off: the cart slows, so a net force acts again. One interval did have constant velocity, so \"none\" is out too.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Cart","x":6},{"series":"Cart","x":7}],"shade":[{"x1":5,"x2":7,"text":"slowing"}],"spans":[{"x":6,"from":16,"to":19,"text":"3 m","side":"left"},{"x":7,"from":19,"to":20,"text":"1 m","side":"left"}]},"strike":["5 to 7 s","None of the intervals"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 to 5 s. Rule: on an x–t graph, a straight sloped line means constant velocity and zero net force.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":5,"text":"straight: constant v, F = 0"}]}}],"trap":"From 2 to 5 s the cart moves fastest, so it can feel as if the force is largest there. A force changes velocity; steady motion, however fast, needs no net force."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = position**: how far along the track the cart is, in meters. Higher point = farther from the start.","marks":[{"t":"position","note":"y axis: meters along track"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = time passing**, in seconds.","marks":[{"t":"time","note":"x axis: seconds"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Net force** = all pushes and pulls added up. Newton's law F = m·a: zero net force means **zero acceleration**, so the speed is **not changing**.","marks":[{"t":"net force on the cart zero","note":"all pushes cancel"}],"calc":["F_net = m × a","F_net = 0 → a = 0 → **speed constant**"]},{"text":"**Steepness = speed.** Steep = many meters each second. Flat = barely moving. The cart starts at rest, so the curve starts flat.","marks":[{"t":"starts from rest","note":"speed 0 at t = 0"}],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":0.5,"text":"flat: slow","at":"above"},{"series":"Cart","x":4,"text":"steep: fast","at":"left"},{"series":"Cart","x":6.5,"text":"flattening: slowing","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**The choices split the graph into three stretches.** Test each one.","marks":[{"t":"During which interval","note":"test each stretch"}],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"0–2 s"},{"x1":2,"x2":5,"text":"2–5 s"},{"x1":5,"x2":7,"text":"5–7 s"}]}},{"text":"**0–2 s:** count meters gained each second: +1 m, then +3 m. The gain grows → speeding up → a net force acts.","calc":["1st s: 0 → 1 m = **+1 m**","2nd s: 1 → 4 m = **+3 m** (bigger)"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"speeding up"}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":1,"text":"+1 m","side":"left"},{"x":2,"from":1,"to":4,"text":"+3 m","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**2–5 s:** +4 m every second. Same gain each second = **constant speed** = zero acceleration.","calc":["speed = (16 − 4) m ÷ (5 − 2) s = **4 m/s**","same every second → **a = 0, F_net = 0**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":2,"x2":5,"text":"straight"}],"spans":[{"x":3,"from":4,"to":8,"text":"+4 m","side":"left"},{"x":4,"from":8,"to":12,"text":"+4 m","side":"left"},{"x":5,"from":12,"to":16,"text":"+4 m","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**5–7 s:** +3 m, then +1 m. The gain shrinks → slowing down → a net force acts (e.g. friction or brakes).","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":5,"x2":7,"text":"slowing"}],"spans":[{"x":6,"from":16,"to":19,"text":"+3 m","side":"left"},{"x":7,"from":19,"to":20,"text":"+1 m","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**\"None of the intervals\" is wrong** because one stretch is perfectly straight. Moving fast is fine: zero force only needs *steady* speed.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Cart","x":3,"text":"straight: F_net = 0","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a position–time graph, a straight slanted line = constant velocity = zero net force. A curve bending = speed changing = net force."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"A PCR product and a DNA ladder are run side by side on an agarose gel, with the wells at the negative electrode. The figure plots how far each ladder band traveled against its length. The PCR product traveled 3.1 cm. What is its approximate length?",idStem:"DNA moves through an electric field in gel electrophoresis. Which property makes it move?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Migration distance of DNA ladder bands","x":{"label":"Fragment length (bp)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"DNA ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,5],[1000,3.8],[1500,3.1],[2000,2.6],[3000,1.9],[4000,1.4],[5000,1]]}]},choices:["About 500 bp","About 1500 bp","About 2600 bp","About 4000 bp"],ans:1,exp:"DNA is negative (phosphate backbone), so all fragments run toward the positive electrode, and the gel's pores hold back **longer** fragments more. Read the ladder curve: **3.1 cm corresponds to 1500 bp**. Distance falls with the logarithm of length, so a straight-line estimate gives the wrong size.",src:"pe6-cp:6",how:{"ask":"On the ladder curve, which fragment length matches a travel distance of 3.1 cm?","know":"• **Gel electrophoresis:** every DNA fragment has the same charge per length (one negative phosphate per nucleotide), so the electric field pulls all of them toward the **anode (+)** equally hard per unit length.\n• The agarose mesh is the sorter: short fragments slip through and travel far; long ones are slowed.\n• A **ladder** is a set of fragments of known length. Find the unknown's distance on the y-axis, go across to the curve, then down to read the length. Distance is roughly linear in log(length), so the curve is not a straight line.","steps":[{"text":"DNA's phosphates are negative, so every fragment runs away from the wells toward the positive electrode; the gel slows long fragments the most.","hl":"with the wells at the negative electrode"},{"text":"Find 3.1 cm on the distance axis and go across to the ladder curve.","graph":{"yLine":3.1,"read":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500}],"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500,"text":"3.1 cm ↔ 1500 bp","at":"right"}]},"calc":["ladder band at **3.1 cm** = 1500 bp","PCR product at 3.1 cm → **≈ 1500 bp**"]},{"text":"The ends of the ladder are far from 3.1 cm: the 500-bp band ran 5.0 cm and the 4000-bp band only 1.4 cm.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":500},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":4000}],"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":500,"text":"500 bp: 5.0 cm","at":"right"},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":4000,"text":"4000 bp: 1.4 cm","at":"above"}]},"strike":["About 500 bp","About 4000 bp"]},{"text":"A straight-line shortcut between the end bands gives 2600 bp, but the curve is not straight.","calc":["straight line: 500 + (5.0 − 3.1) ÷ 4.0 × 4500 ≈ **2600 bp**","actual curve at 3.1 cm: **1500 bp**"],"strike":["About 2600 bp"],"graph":{"yLine":3.1,"labels":[{"x":2600,"y":3.1,"text":"straight-line guess ≈ 2600 bp","at":"above"},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500,"text":"real curve: 1500 bp","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: about 1500 bp. Rule: read size off the ladder curve itself; longer DNA travels less, and not in proportion.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500,"text":"≈ 1500 bp","at":"right"}]}}],"trap":"Drawing a straight line from 5.0 cm at 500 bp to 1.0 cm at 5000 bp predicts about 2600 bp. The real curve drops steeply for short fragments, so 3.1 cm sits at only 1500 bp."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = distance from the well**, in cm: how far each band ran through the gel.","marks":[{"t":"3.1 cm","note":"how far the PCR band ran"},{"t":"DNA ladder","note":"DNA pieces of known size"},{"t":"agarose gel","note":"a jelly that sieves DNA"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = longer DNA pieces**, measured in base pairs (bp).","marks":[{"t":"its length","note":"x axis: size in bp"}],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Why DNA moves:** its backbone phosphates are negative, so it is pushed away from the − end toward +. The gel mesh **holds back long pieces**.","marks":[{"t":"wells at the negative electrode","note":"DNA (−) pushed away from −"}]},{"text":"**Each dot is a ladder band of known size.** Short pieces slip through and run far; long ones barely move.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":500,"text":"500 bp ran 5 cm","at":"right"},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":5000,"text":"5000 bp ran 1 cm","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**The curve is not a straight line:** it drops fast for short pieces, then flattens for long ones.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":500,"x2":2000,"text":"drops fast"},{"x1":2000,"x2":5000,"text":"flattens"}]}},{"text":"**Read the unknown:** find 3.1 cm on the up axis, go across to the curve, then down to the length.","marks":[{"t":"3.1 cm","note":"go across at 3.1"}],"calc":["ladder band at **3.1 cm** = 1500 bp","PCR product → **≈ 1500 bp**"],"graph":{"yLine":3.1,"read":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500}],"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500,"text":"3.1 cm ↔ 1500 bp","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Trap:** a straight line between the end bands gives about 2600 bp. Wrong, because the real curve bends.","calc":["500 + (5.0 − 3.1) ÷ 4.0 × 4500 ≈ **2600 bp** (wrong)","read the curve itself: **1500 bp**"],"graph":{"yLine":3.1,"labels":[{"x":2600,"y":3.1,"text":"straight-line guess: wrong","at":"above"},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":1500,"text":"real curve: 1500 bp","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**500 bp and 4000 bp are far off:** those bands ran 5.0 cm and 1.4 cm, not 3.1 cm.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"DNA ladder","x":500,"text":"5.0 cm: too far","at":"right"},{"series":"DNA ladder","x":4000,"text":"1.4 cm: too short","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to size DNA, go across from its distance to the ladder curve and read down. Longer DNA runs less, and not in a straight-line way."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A borate buffer for an enzyme assay has pH 8.2 at 25 °C. How do its [H⁺] and [OH⁻] compare with those of pure water at 25 °C?",idStem:"How would you describe a buffer at pH 8.2?",choices:["[H⁺] is about 1.2 times lower and [OH⁻] is about 1.2 times higher","[H⁺] is about 16 times higher and [OH⁻] is about 16 times lower","[H⁺] is about 16 times lower and [OH⁻] is about 16 times higher","[H⁺] is about 16 times lower and [OH⁻] is the same as in water"],ans:2,exp:"pH is logarithmic: 8.2 − 7.0 = 1.2 units → a factor of **10¹·² ≈ 16**. [H⁺] = 6.3 × 10⁻⁹ M is 16× lower than 10⁻⁷ M, and since **[H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴**, [OH⁻] = 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M is 16× higher.",src:"pe6-cp:7",how:{"ask":"How many pH units separate the buffer from water, what factor is that in concentration, and how does Kw link [H⁺] to [OH⁻]?","know":"**pH = −log[H⁺]**, so each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺]; a difference of d units is a factor of 10ᵈ. At 25 °C, **[H⁺][OH⁻] = Kw = 10⁻¹⁴** and pH + pOH = 14, so when [H⁺] falls by a factor, [OH⁻] rises by the same factor.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: compare each concentration to water, where [H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ M.","hl":"pH 8.2 at 25 °C"},{"text":"Rule: pH = −log[H⁺] and pH + pOH = 14. Put both on a scale.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pH and pOH of water and of the buffer","x":{"label":"Solution"},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"categories":["Water","Buffer"],"series":[{"name":"pH","values":[7,8.2]},{"name":"pOH","values":[7,5.8]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Buffer","pH"],["Buffer","pOH"]]}},{"text":"Convert each log difference to a factor.","calc":["[H⁺] = 10⁻⁸·² = **6.3 × 10⁻⁹ M**","10⁻⁷ ÷ 6.3 × 10⁻⁹ = 10¹·² ≈ **16× lower**","[OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵·⁸ = **1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M**","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 10⁻⁷ ≈ **16× higher**"]},{"text":"A 1.2-unit pH change is a factor of 10¹·², not 1.2; higher pH means less H⁺; Kw fixes [OH⁻] once [H⁺] drops.","strike":["[H⁺] is about 1.2 times lower and [OH⁻] is about 1.2 times higher","[H⁺] is about 16 times higher and [OH⁻] is about 16 times lower","[H⁺] is about 16 times lower and [OH⁻] is the same as in water"]},{"text":"Answer: [H⁺] is about 16 times lower and [OH⁻] about 16 times higher than in water, a weakly basic solution."}],"trap":"\"1.2 times\" treats pH as linear; a 1.2-unit change is 10¹·² ≈ 16-fold. \"[OH⁻] is the same\" forgets that Kw ties [OH⁻] to [H⁺]."},teach:[{"text":"**Find what is compared:** the buffer's pH against pure water, both at 25 °C.","marks":[{"t":"pH 8.2","note":"the buffer's pH"},{"t":"pure water","note":"pH 7.0: neutral"}]},{"text":"**pH = −log[H⁺]**: a short tag for how much H⁺ (acid) is in water. **Higher pH = LESS H⁺.** Each 1 unit = ×10.","marks":[{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"acid particles (M)"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"[H⁺] falls 10× for each pH unit","x":{"label":"pH","min":7,"max":9,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"[H⁺] (× 10⁻⁹ M)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"[H⁺]","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[7,100],[7.5,31.6],[8,10],[8.2,6.3],[8.5,3.2],[9,1]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"[H⁺]","x":7,"text":"water: 100 (10⁻⁷ M)","at":"right"},{"series":"[H⁺]","x":8.2,"text":"buffer: 6.3","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**How far apart?** Subtract the two pH values.","marks":[{"t":"pH 8.2","note":"8.2 − 7.0 = 1.2 units"}],"calc":["ΔpH = 8.2 − 7.0 = **1.2**","factor = 10^1.2 (not 1.2!)"]},{"text":"**Turn 1.2 units into a factor.** 10^1.2 = 10 × 10^0.2, and 10^0.2 ≈ 1.6.","marks":[{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"16× lower"}],"calc":["10^1.2 = 10¹ × 10^0.2","= 10 × 1.6 = **≈ 16**","[H⁺] = 10⁻⁸·² = **6.3 × 10⁻⁹ M**","10⁻⁷ ÷ 6.3 × 10⁻⁹ = **16× lower**"]},{"text":"**Water's seesaw:** at 25 °C, [H⁺] × [OH⁻] is always 10⁻¹⁴. If H⁺ goes down 16×, OH⁻ must go **up** 16×.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ here"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"base particles: rises"}],"calc":["[H⁺] × [OH⁻] = **1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴**"]},{"text":"**Same idea with pOH:** pH + pOH = 14. The buffer's pOH is 5.8, lower than water's 7.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"pH and pOH of water and of the buffer","x":{"label":"Solution"},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"categories":["Water","Buffer"],"series":[{"name":"pH","values":[7,8.2]},{"name":"pOH","values":[7,5.8]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Buffer","pH"],["Buffer","pOH"]]}},{"text":"**Compute [OH⁻]** to check.","marks":[{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"16× higher"}],"calc":["[OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵·⁸ = **1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M**","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 10⁻⁷ = **16× higher**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** \"1.2 times\" forgets the log; \"[H⁺] higher\" forgets higher pH = less acid; \"[OH⁻] same\" forgets the seesaw.","marks":[{"t":"compare with those of pure water","note":"H⁺ down 16×, OH⁻ up 16×"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a pH difference of d units = a factor of 10ᵈ in [H⁺]. [OH⁻] changes by the same factor the other way (Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"The figure shows the titration of 50 mL of 0.10 M weak acid HA with 0.20 M NaOH. A buffer at pH 5.8 is to be prepared from HA and its conjugate base A⁻. What ratio of A⁻ to HA is needed?",idStem:"Boric acid has pKa 9.2. At pH 8.2, what is the ratio [base]/[acid]?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 50 mL of 0.10 M HA with 0.20 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":35,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"HA titration","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.9],[2.5,3.85],[5,4.2],[10,4.62],[12.5,4.8],[15,4.98],[20,5.4],[22.5,5.75],[24,6.18],[25,8.8],[26,11.4],[28,11.9],[30,12.1],[35,12.4]]}]},choices:["1:10","1:1","2:1","10:1"],ans:3,exp:"The equivalence point is at 25 mL, so the half-equivalence point is 12.5 mL, where [A⁻] = [HA] and **pH = pKa = 4.8**. Henderson–Hasselbalch: 5.8 = 4.8 + log([A⁻]/[HA]), so log ratio = 1 and **[A⁻]:[HA] = 10:1**.",src:"pe6-cp:8",how:{"ask":"What pKa can you read from the curve, and how far is the target pH above or below it?","know":"• **Equivalence point:** the steep vertical jump, where all the HA has been converted to A⁻.\n• **Half-equivalence point:** half that volume of base. There [A⁻] = [HA], so the pH on the curve equals the **pKa**; the curve is flattest here (the buffer region).\n• **Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Each pH unit above the pKa means 10 times more A⁻; each unit below means 10 times more HA.","steps":[{"text":"Find the equivalence point: the steep jump, at 25 mL.","graph":{"xLine":25,"read":[{"series":"HA titration","x":25}],"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":25,"text":"equivalence: 25 mL","at":"right"}]},"calc":["5.0 mmol HA ÷ 0.20 M NaOH = **25 mL**"]},{"text":"Half of 25 mL is 12.5 mL. The pH there is the pKa.","graph":{"xLine":12.5,"yLine":4.8,"read":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5}],"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5,"text":"12.5 mL: pKa = 4.8","at":"below"}]},"calc":["half-equivalence: 25 ÷ 2 = **12.5 mL**","pH at 12.5 mL = pKa = **4.8**"]},{"text":"Put the target pH into Henderson–Hasselbalch.","hl":"A buffer at pH 5.8","calc":["5.8 = 4.8 + log([A⁻]/[HA])","log([A⁻]/[HA]) = **1**","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10¹ = **10**"],"graph":{"yLine":5.8,"spans":[{"x":12.5,"from":4.8,"to":5.8,"text":"log ratio = 1","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"1:1 gives the pKa itself, 4.8; 2:1 gives 4.8 + 0.3 = 5.1; 1:10 gives 3.8, the flipped answer.","strike":["1:1","2:1","1:10"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5,"text":"1:1 → pH 4.8","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Answer: 10:1. Rule: read the pKa at half the equivalence volume; one pH unit above it means ten times more A⁻.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":12.5,"from":4.8,"to":5.8,"text":"+1 unit → 10:1","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5,"text":"pKa 4.8","at":"below"}]}}],"trap":"1:10 comes from flipping the log. A pH above the pKa means the base form, A⁻, is in excess, not HA."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = pH.** Higher = more basic, lower = more acidic.","marks":[{"t":"50 mL of 0.10 M weak acid HA","note":"acid in the flask"},{"t":"0.20 M NaOH","note":"base poured in"},{"t":"pH 5.8","note":"target buffer pH"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Left to right = mL of NaOH added.** Each bit of base turns some HA into A⁻.","calc":["HA + OH⁻ → **A⁻** + H₂O"],"graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Terms:** a **weak acid** HA only partly gives up its H⁺. Its **conjugate base** A⁻ is HA after losing the H⁺. A **buffer** = HA and A⁻ together.","marks":[{"t":"weak acid HA","note":"holds H⁺ loosely"},{"t":"conjugate base A⁻","note":"HA minus its H⁺"}]},{"text":"**The steep jump = equivalence point**: just enough base to use up all the acid.","marks":[{"t":"50 mL of 0.10 M","note":"5.0 mmol HA"},{"t":"0.20 M NaOH","note":"÷ this"}],"calc":["50 mL × 0.10 M = **5.0 mmol HA**","5.0 mmol ÷ 0.20 M = **25 mL** NaOH"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"HA titration","x":25}],"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":25,"text":"equivalence: 25 mL","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Halfway (12.5 mL), half the HA has become A⁻**, so [A⁻] = [HA]. The pH there **is the pKa**.","calc":["25 ÷ 2 = **12.5 mL**","pH at 12.5 mL = pKa = **4.8**"],"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":5,"x2":20,"text":"buffer region"}],"read":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5}],"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5,"text":"12.5 mL: pKa = 4.8","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch** links pH to the ratio. Put in the target pH.","marks":[{"t":"pH 5.8","note":"plug in here"},{"t":"ratio of A⁻ to HA","note":"A⁻ on top, HA below"}],"calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","5.8 = 4.8 + log([A⁻]/[HA])","log([A⁻]/[HA]) = **1**","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10¹ = **10**"]},{"text":"**See it on the curve:** 5.8 is exactly 1 pH unit above the pKa. One unit up = 10× more A⁻.","graph":{"yLine":5.8,"spans":[{"x":12.5,"from":4.8,"to":5.8,"text":"+1 unit → ×10","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** 1:1 gives pH = pKa = 4.8; 2:1 gives 4.8 + 0.3 = 5.1; 1:10 is flipped and gives 3.8.","calc":["1:1 → 4.8 + log 1 = **4.8**","2:1 → 4.8 + 0.3 = **5.1**","1:10 → 4.8 − 1 = **3.8**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"HA titration","x":12.5,"text":"1:1 → only 4.8","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKa = pH at half the equivalence volume. Each pH unit above the pKa = 10× more A⁻ than HA; each unit below = 10× more HA."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"A salt dissolves spontaneously in water at 20 °C, and the temperature of the solution drops as it dissolves. Which statement about the dissolution at 20 °C is correct?",idStem:"A reaction is spontaneous at 20 °C. What MUST be true at that temperature?",choices:["ΔH > 0 and TΔS < ΔH","ΔH < 0 and TΔS > ΔH","ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0","ΔH > 0 and TΔS > ΔH"],ans:3,exp:"Cooling means heat is absorbed: **ΔH > 0**. Spontaneous means **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0**, so **TΔS > ΔH**; the entropy gain drives the process.",src:"pe6-cp:9",how:{"ask":"What does the temperature drop say about the sign of ΔH, and what must be true of TΔS for ΔG to be negative?","know":"**ΔG = ΔH − TΔS; spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0.** If the solution gets colder, the process absorbs heat from it: **endothermic, ΔH > 0**. An endothermic process can be spontaneous only if ΔS > 0 and TΔS > ΔH.","steps":[{"text":"Translate the observation: the solution cools, so it absorbs heat from its surroundings. Dissolution is endothermic, ΔH > 0.","hl":"the temperature of the solution drops","strike":["ΔH < 0 and TΔS > ΔH","ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0"]},{"text":"Translate \"spontaneous\": ΔG < 0. Rearrange ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","hl":"dissolves spontaneously","calc":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0","→ **TΔS > ΔH**"]},{"text":"Example: with ΔH = +25 kJ/mol, ΔS must be large enough that TΔS beats ΔH at 293 K.","calc":["ΔS > ΔH/T = 25,000 J ÷ 293 K","ΔS > **85 J/mol·K**","with ΔS = 100: ΔG = 25 − 293 × 0.100 = **−4.3 kJ/mol**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS for ΔH = +25 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.10 kJ/mol·K","x":{"label":"T (K)","min":200,"max":350,"step":25},"y":{"label":"ΔG (kJ/mol)","min":-10,"max":6,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ΔG","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[200,5],[250,0],[293,-4.3],[350,-10]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"ΔG","x":293}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":250,"x2":350,"text":"ΔG < 0: spontaneous"}],"labels":[{"series":"ΔG","x":293,"text":"293 K: ΔG = −4.3","at":"right"},{"series":"ΔG","x":250,"text":"ΔG = 0","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"TΔS < ΔH would make ΔG positive, which is nonspontaneous.","strike":["ΔH > 0 and TΔS < ΔH"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH > 0 and TΔS > ΔH; an endothermic dissolution is spontaneous only because the entropy term outweighs it."}],"trap":"\"ΔH < 0\" is the reflex for spontaneous, but a solution that cools is absorbing heat, so ΔH > 0; the entropy term carries the reaction."},teach:[{"text":"**Two facts are given.** Each one translates into a sign.","marks":[{"t":"dissolves spontaneously","note":"happens on its own"},{"t":"temperature of the solution drops","note":"it gets colder"},{"t":"20 °C","note":"T = 293 K"}]},{"text":"**ΔH** = heat taken in (+) or given off (−). The water got colder because dissolving **pulled heat out of it**: endothermic, **ΔH > 0**.","marks":[{"t":"temperature of the solution drops","note":"heat pulled IN: ΔH > 0"}]},{"text":"**ΔS** = change in disorder (spread-out-ness). A neat solid crystal breaking into ions scattered in water → more disorder, **ΔS > 0**.","marks":[{"t":"A salt dissolves","note":"solid → scattered ions"}]},{"text":"**ΔG decides \"on its own\".** ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**.","marks":[{"t":"spontaneously","note":"ΔG < 0"}],"calc":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0","→ ΔH < TΔS","→ **TΔS > ΔH**"]},{"text":"**Picture it** with example numbers: ΔH = +25 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.10 kJ/mol·K. Above 250 K, TΔS wins and ΔG goes negative.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS (ΔH = +25, ΔS = +0.10)","x":{"label":"T (K)","min":200,"max":350,"step":25},"y":{"label":"ΔG (kJ/mol)","min":-10,"max":6,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"ΔG","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[200,5],[250,0],[293,-4.3],[350,-10]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":250,"x2":350,"text":"ΔG < 0: spontaneous"}],"labels":[{"series":"ΔG","x":293,"text":"20 °C: ΔG = −4.3","at":"right"},{"series":"ΔG","x":250,"text":"ΔG = 0","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Check the example at 20 °C.**","marks":[{"t":"20 °C","note":"293 K"}],"calc":["TΔS = 293 × 0.10 = **29.3 kJ/mol**","29.3 > 25 → **TΔS > ΔH**","ΔG = 25 − 29.3 = **−4.3 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Rule out ΔH < 0:** a negative ΔH would give heat off and warm the water, but it cooled.","marks":[{"t":"temperature of the solution drops","note":"rules out ΔH < 0"}]},{"text":"**Rule out TΔS < ΔH:** then ΔG = ΔH − TΔS would be positive, and the salt would not dissolve on its own.","marks":[{"t":"spontaneously","note":"rules out TΔS < ΔH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** gets colder = endothermic (ΔH > 0). Endothermic + spontaneous only works if entropy wins: ΔS > 0 and TΔS > ΔH."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A kinase is incubated with [γ-³²P]ATP and one of four peptides that are identical except at one position. The side chain at that position in each peptide is shown. Which peptide would incorporate ³²P?",idStem:"Which residue can NOT be phosphorylated?",choiceFigs:["valine-sc","threonine-sc","methionine-sc","alanine-sc"],choices:["Valine","Threonine","Methionine","Alanine"],ans:1,exp:"Kinases attach the γ-phosphate of ATP to a side-chain **–OH** (Ser, Thr or Tyr), forming a phosphoester. Only threonine's side chain has an OH. Valine has the same branched shape with CH₃ in place of OH; methionine's S carries no H; alanine is only CH₃.",src:"pe6-cp:10",how:{"ask":"Which side chain has an atom that can bond to the γ-phosphate and form a phosphoester?","know":"• A **kinase** moves the terminal (γ) phosphate of ATP onto an –OH: R–OH + ATP → R–O–PO₃²⁻ + ADP. The ³²P label goes with it.\n• Protein side chains with an OH: **serine (–CH₂OH), threonine (–CH(OH)CH₃), tyrosine (phenol –OH)**.\n• In a drawing, look for an O bonded to H at the end of the side chain; C–H, C–S–C and plain carbon chains cannot accept the phosphate.","steps":[{"text":"The label sits on ATP's terminal phosphate, and a kinase moves that phosphate onto a side-chain O–H.","hl":"[γ-³²P]ATP","calc":["R–OH + ATP → **R–O–PO₃²⁻** + ADP"]},{"text":"Scan each side chain for an O–H. Threonine ends in CH(OH)CH₃: it has one.","fig":"threonine-sc"},{"text":"Valine has the same branched shape, but a CH₃ sits where the OH would be: no acceptor.","fig":"valine-sc","strike":["Valine"]},{"text":"Methionine's S is bonded to two carbons and has no H; alanine is only a CH₃.","fig":["methionine-sc","alanine-sc"],"strike":["Methionine","Alanine"]},{"text":"Answer: threonine. Rule: a kinase needs an O–H on the side chain, so Ser, Thr or Tyr."}],"trap":"Valine looks almost identical to threonine: the same branched carbon and the same size. It has a CH₃ where threonine has an OH, so there is nothing for the phosphate to bond to."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read them.** Each drawing is a whole amino acid. Only the highlighted side chain differs between the four.","fig":"t6-thr-read"},{"text":"**In a peptide these ends are used to link amino acids together.** They are identical in all four, so look only at the side chain.","fig":"t6-thr-links"},{"text":"**A kinase is an enzyme that moves ATP's last phosphate onto a protein.** ³²P is radioactive phosphorus, a tag we can detect.","fig":"t6-atp-gamma-moves"},{"text":"**The phosphate lands on the O of an O–H.** The H leaves and the O bonds to P, as on this threonine.","fig":"t6-phosphothr"},{"text":"**So scan each side chain for an O–H.** Threonine has one.","fig":"t6-thr-oh"},{"text":"**Valine has the same branched shape, but CH₃ where threonine has OH.**","fig":"t6-val-no"},{"text":"**Methionine's S is bonded to two carbons and carries no H.** Nothing to attach to.","fig":"t6-met-no"},{"text":"**Alanine's side chain is only C and H.**","fig":"t6-ala-no"},{"text":"**Rule:** kinases phosphorylate a side-chain O–H: serine, threonine, tyrosine. In each drawing, look at the side chain and ask: is there an O with an H on it?","fig":["t6-ser-oh","t6-tyr-oh"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4A",q:"The power P dissipated in a resistor was measured at several currents I, as plotted in the figure. Which relationship between P and I is consistent with these data?",idStem:"When oxygen use rises 4×, energy use also rises about 4×. What is the relationship?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Power dissipated in a resistor versus current","x":{"label":"Current I (A)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Power P (W)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Resistor","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,5],[2,20],[3,45],[4,80]]}]},choices:["P ∝ I","P ∝ I²","P ∝ √I","P ∝ 1/I"],ans:1,exp:"Doubling I from 1 to 2 A raises P from 5 to 20 W, a factor of **4 = 2²**; tripling I to 3 A gives 45 W, a factor of **9 = 3²**. So P ∝ I², as P = I²R predicts with R = 5 Ω.",src:"pe6-cp:11",how:{"ask":"When the current doubles, by what factor does the power change?","know":"• Test a proportionality with ratios: multiply x by k and see what y is multiplied by.\n• y ∝ x: y × k. **y ∝ x²: y × k².** y ∝ √x: y × √k. y ∝ 1/x: y ÷ k.\n• Shapes: y ∝ x is a straight line through the origin; y ∝ x² curves upward; y ∝ √x rises and levels off; y ∝ 1/x falls.","steps":[{"text":"Look at the shape: the points curve upward instead of lying on a straight line, so P is not simply proportional to I.","graph":{"focus":["Resistor"],"labels":[{"series":"Resistor","x":3,"text":"curves upward, not straight","at":"left"}]},"hl":"Which relationship between P and I","strike":["P ∝ I"]},{"text":"Read P at 1 A and 2 A and compare.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Resistor","x":1},{"series":"Resistor","x":2}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":5,"text":"5 W","side":"left"},{"x":2,"from":0,"to":20,"text":"20 W","side":"left"}]},"calc":["I × **2** (1 → 2 A)","P: 5 → 20 W, × **4** = 2²"]},{"text":"Confirm with a second pair.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Resistor","x":3}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":5,"text":"5 W","side":"left"},{"x":3,"from":0,"to":45,"text":"45 W","side":"left"}]},"calc":["I × **3** (1 → 3 A)","P: 5 → 45 W, × **9** = 3²"]},{"text":"√I would make the curve level off, and 1/I would make P fall as I rises.","strike":["P ∝ √I","P ∝ 1/I"]},{"text":"Answer: P ∝ I². Rule: multiply x by k; if y goes up by k², it is a square law.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Resistor","x":4,"text":"P ∝ I²","at":"left"}]}}],"trap":"P rises steadily with I, which tempts P ∝ I. But the curve bends upward: equal steps in I give larger and larger steps in P, which a straight proportion cannot do."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = power P** in watts: energy the resistor turns into heat each second.","marks":[{"t":"power P","note":"y axis: watts"},{"t":"currents I","note":"x axis: amps"},{"t":"a resistor","note":"turns current into heat"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Right = more current I** (amps) flowing through the resistor.","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**∝ means \"is proportional to\".** P ∝ I² means: make I 2× bigger and P becomes 2² = 4× bigger.","marks":[{"t":"relationship between P and I","note":"how P grows with I"}]},{"text":"**Each law has its own shape.** Straight line = ∝ x. Bends upward = ∝ x². Rises then levels = ∝ √x. Falls = ∝ 1/x.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Shapes of four laws (all = 1 at x = 1)","x":{"label":"x","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"y","min":0,"max":16,"step":4},"series":[{"name":"y ∝ x","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,0.5],[1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[4,4]]},{"name":"y ∝ x²","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,0.25],[1,1],[2,4],[3,9],[4,16]]},{"name":"y ∝ √x","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,0.71],[1,1],[2,1.41],[3,1.73],[4,2]]},{"name":"y ∝ 1/x","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,2],[1,1],[2,0.5],[3,0.33],[4,0.25]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"y ∝ x²","x":3,"text":"x²: bends upward","at":"left"},{"series":"y ∝ x","x":4,"text":"x: straight","at":"above"},{"series":"y ∝ 1/x","x":0.5,"text":"1/x: falls","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Our data bend upward**, like the x² shape. So it is not a straight-line law.","graph":{"focus":["Resistor"],"labels":[{"series":"Resistor","x":3,"text":"bends upward","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Test it: double the current** from 1 A to 2 A and read P.","calc":["I × **2** (1 → 2 A)","P: 5 → 20 W = × **4** = 2²"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Resistor","x":1},{"series":"Resistor","x":2}],"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0,"to":5,"text":"5 W","side":"left"},{"x":2,"from":0,"to":20,"text":"20 W","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Confirm: triple the current** from 1 A to 3 A.","calc":["I × **3** (1 → 3 A)","P: 5 → 45 W = × **9** = 3²"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Resistor","x":3}],"spans":[{"x":3,"from":0,"to":45,"text":"45 W","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Physics agrees:** heat in a resistor is P = I²R. Every point gives the same R = 5 Ω.","marks":[{"t":"dissipated","note":"turned to heat: P = I²R"}],"calc":["5 ÷ 1² = 20 ÷ 2² = 45 ÷ 3² = **5 Ω**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Resistor","x":4,"text":"80 ÷ 4² = 5 Ω","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** multiply x by k. If y goes ×k → linear; ×k² → square law; ×√k → square root; ÷k → inverse."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Which arrangement of substituents is found in the most stable chair conformation of the compound shown, cis-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane?",idStem:"On a cyclohexane ring with a tert-butyl group, the methyl ends up axial in the favored chair. Why?",fig:"cis-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane",choices:["tert-Butyl equatorial, methyl axial","tert-Butyl axial, methyl equatorial","Both groups equatorial","Both groups axial"],ans:2,exp:"In a cis isomer both groups point to the same face. On a chair, carbons 1 and 3 have their same-face bonds of the **same type**, both axial or both equatorial. So cis-1,3 can put **both groups equatorial**, with no 1,3-diaxial strain.",src:"pe6-cp:12",how:{"ask":"For two groups on the same face at carbons 1 and 3, are they both axial, both equatorial, or one of each?","know":"• Each chair carbon has one **axial** bond (straight up or down) and one **equatorial** bond (out to the side). Axial directions alternate up, down, up around the ring.\n• So for two groups on the same face (cis): **1,2 and 1,4 → one axial + one equatorial; 1,3 → both axial or both equatorial.** Trans reverses each rule.\n• Bulky groups prefer equatorial; tert-butyl is so large it is almost always equatorial.","steps":[{"text":"Read the name and drawing: both groups on the same face (cis), on carbons 1 and 3.","hl":"cis-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","fig":"cis-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane"},{"text":"Walk around the chair: axial bonds alternate up and down, so C1 and C3 point their axial bonds the same way.","calc":["C1 axial = **up**, C2 axial = down, C3 axial = **up**","same face at C1 and C3 → **same type**","cis-1,3: both axial **or** both equatorial"]},{"text":"One axial and one equatorial would need the groups on opposite faces at C1 and C3, which is the trans isomer.","strike":["tert-Butyl equatorial, methyl axial","tert-Butyl axial, methyl equatorial"]},{"text":"Both axial is the ring-flipped chair, with a tert-butyl in severe 1,3-diaxial clashes.","strike":["Both groups axial"]},{"text":"Answer: both groups equatorial. Rule: cis-1,3 and trans-1,2 or trans-1,4 can be diequatorial; cis-1,2, cis-1,4 and trans-1,3 cannot."}],"trap":"tert-Butyl equatorial, methyl axial is the answer for the cis-1,4 isomer, where one group must be axial. In the cis-1,3 isomer both groups can be equatorial at once."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** The hexagon is a ring of six carbons, H hidden. A solid wedge bond points toward you, out of the page.","fig":"t6-cbmc-read"},{"text":"**Cis = both groups on the same face of the ring.** 1,3 = one ring carbon between them.","fig":"t6-cbmc-numbers"},{"text":"**The real ring is a puckered 'chair'.** Each carbon has one axial bond (straight up or down) and one equatorial bond (out sideways).","fig":"t6-cbmc-two-bonds"},{"text":"**Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring.** So C1 and C3 point their axial bonds the same way.","fig":"t6-cbmc-alternate"},{"text":"**Same face at C1 and C3 = same bond type.** Both axial, or both equatorial. One of each would be the trans compound.","fig":"t6-cbmc-same-type"},{"text":"**The chair can flip, swapping every axial bond with equatorial.** So the two options are both axial or both equatorial.","fig":"t6-cbmc-flip"},{"text":"**Axial groups bump into the axial H atoms two carbons away.** Equatorial groups point into open space. The bigger the group, the worse axial is.","fig":"t6-cbmc-big"},{"text":"**So the stable chair puts both groups equatorial**, with nothing crowding the tert-butyl.","fig":"t6-cbmc-both-eq"},{"text":"**Rule:** cis-1,3, trans-1,2, trans-1,4 can be both equatorial; the others need one axial (give it the smaller group). For each choice, ask: does it fit cis-1,3, and is tert-butyl equatorial?","fig":"cis-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"Which labeled atom in the structure shown has bond angles closest to 120°?",idStem:"A Schiff base (imine) links a carbon to nitrogen with C=N. How is that carbon hybridized?",fig:"aminoimine-nitrile-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:1,exp:"Count electron domains: bonded atoms plus lone pairs, with a multiple bond counting once. **II, the imine carbon, has three domains → sp², trigonal planar, 120°.** I, the nitrile carbon, has two (sp, 180°); III, the CH₂, has four (sp³, 109.5°); IV, the amine N, has three bonds plus a lone pair = four (sp³, about 107°).",src:"pe6-cp:13",how:{"ask":"How many electron domains surround each labeled atom, and which count gives a trigonal planar shape?","know":"• **Steric number** = atoms bonded + lone pairs. A double or triple bond counts as one.\n• 2 → sp, linear, 180°. **3 → sp², trigonal planar, 120°.** 4 → sp³, tetrahedral, 109.5° (a bit less when some domains are lone pairs).\n• Shortcut on a drawing: atom in a triple bond → sp; atom in one double bond → sp²; only single bonds → sp³. A neutral N with three single bonds still has a lone pair.","steps":[{"text":"120° belongs to a trigonal planar atom: three electron domains, sp².","hl":"bond angles closest to 120°"},{"text":"Count domains at each labeled atom.","fig":"aminoimine-nitrile-sites","calc":["I: C≡N and C–C → **2** → sp, 180°","II: C=N, C–CH₃, C–CH₂ → **3** → sp², 120°","III: 4 single bonds → **4** → sp³, 109.5°","IV: 3 bonds + 1 lone pair → **4** → sp³, ≈107°"]},{"text":"The nitrile carbon is linear and the CH₂ is tetrahedral.","strike":["I","III"]},{"text":"The amine N has only three bonds, but its lone pair makes four domains.","strike":["IV"]},{"text":"Answer: II, the imine carbon. Rule: one double bond on an atom → sp² → 120°.","fig":"aminoimine-nitrile-c2"}],"trap":"IV, the amine N, has three bonds like the imine carbon, so it can look trigonal. Its lone pair is a fourth domain, which makes it sp³ and pyramidal, near 107°."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H. Two lines = double bond, three = triple bond.","fig":"t6-ain-read"},{"text":"**Count 'electron groups' around an atom.** Each neighbor counts 1 (a double or triple bond still counts 1). Each lone pair (two unshared electrons) counts 1.","fig":"t6-ain-groups"},{"text":"**Groups push apart.** 2 groups → straight line, 180°. 3 → flat triangle, 120°. 4 → tetrahedron, about 109.5°. You want 3.","fig":"aminoimine-nitrile-sites"},{"text":"**I: triple bond to N + bond to CH₂ = 2 groups.** Straight line.","fig":"t6-ain-i"},{"text":"**II: double bond to N, plus CH₃ and CH₂ = 3 groups.** Flat triangle.","fig":"t6-ain-ii"},{"text":"**III is a CH₂: two carbons plus two hidden H = 4 groups.**","fig":"t6-ain-iii"},{"text":"**A neutral N with three bonds keeps one lone pair.** 3 bonds + 1 pair = 4 groups; the pair squeezes the angle to about 107°.","fig":"t6-ain-iv"},{"text":"**Rule:** count neighbors + lone pairs (hidden H included). One double bond → 120°, triple → 180°, all single → ~109.5°. For each labeled atom, ask: how many groups surround it?","fig":"aminoimine-nitrile-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Initial rates of an enzyme were measured with and without compound Z, and the data are shown as a double-reciprocal plot. Which type of inhibition does Z show?",idStem:"Adding a compound raises an enzyme's KM AND lowers its Vmax. What kind of inhibitor is it?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Double-reciprocal plot with and without Z","x":{"label":"1/[S] (mM⁻¹)","min":-0.5,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"1/v (min/μM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0.1875],[0,0.25],[1,0.5],[2,0.75]]},{"name":"With Z","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.25,0.1875],[0,0.5],[1,1.75],[2,3]]}]},choices:["Competitive","Uncompetitive","Pure noncompetitive","Mixed"],ans:3,exp:"The lines cross **left of the y-axis, above the x-axis**. The y-intercept doubles (Vmax 4 → 2 μM/min) and KM rises (1 → 2.5 mM). Vmax down with KM changed is **mixed** inhibition.",src:"pe6-cp:15",how:{"ask":"Where do the two lines cross, and what does each intercept say about Vmax and KM?","know":"• **Lineweaver–Burk:** 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax. y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Where the lines meet: **on the y-axis → competitive** (Vmax same, KM up); **on the x-axis → pure noncompetitive** (KM same, Vmax down); **parallel → uncompetitive** (both down); **anywhere else → mixed** (Vmax down and KM changed).\n• A higher line means slower rates.","steps":[{"text":"Start where the lines cross: left of the y-axis and above the x-axis.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.25},{"series":"With Z","x":-0.25}],"xLine":-0.25,"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.25,"text":"cross at (−0.25, 0.19)","at":"above"}]},"calc":["crossing at 1/[S] = **−0.25**, 1/v ≈ **0.19**","not on either axis"]},{"text":"Read the y-intercepts: they differ, so Vmax changed. Competitive inhibition would share the y-intercept.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0},{"series":"With Z","x":0}],"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.25","at":"right"},{"series":"With Z","x":0,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.50","at":"left"}]},"calc":["no Z: 1/Vmax = 0.25 → Vmax = **4 μM/min**","with Z: 1/Vmax = 0.50 → Vmax = **2 μM/min**"],"strike":["Competitive"]},{"text":"Get KM from slope × Vmax. KM went up, so the lines are not parallel and do not meet on the x-axis.","calc":["no Z: slope 0.25 × 4 = KM = **1 mM**","with Z: slope 1.25 × 2 = KM = **2.5 mM**"],"strike":["Uncompetitive","Pure noncompetitive"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0.25,"to":0.5,"text":"slope 0.25","side":"left"},{"x":1,"from":0.5,"to":1.75,"text":"slope 1.25","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"Answer: mixed. Rule: lines crossing off both axes mean Vmax fell and KM changed.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.25,"text":"off both axes → mixed","at":"above"}]}}],"trap":"The crossing point is close to the y-axis, which can look competitive. Competitive inhibition never changes the y-intercept, and here it doubles."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = 1/v**, one over the reaction speed. **Higher = slower enzyme.**","marks":[{"t":"Initial rates","note":"v: speed at the start"},{"t":"double-reciprocal plot","note":"plots 1/v vs 1/[S]"},{"t":"compound Z","note":"the possible inhibitor"}],"graph":{"axis":"y"}},{"text":"**Right = 1/[S]**, one over the substrate amount. Further right = **less** substrate. The vertical axis (1/[S] = 0) = endless substrate.","graph":{"axis":"x"}},{"text":"**Two key enzyme numbers.** Vmax = top speed with endless substrate. KM = substrate level that gives half of Vmax (low KM = grabs substrate well).","marks":[{"t":"type of inhibition","note":"how Z slows the enzyme"}],"calc":["Vmax = **top speed**","KM = [S] at **½ Vmax**"]},{"text":"**Normal view first:** Z lowers the top speed (4 → 2) and needs more substrate to reach half speed (KM 1 → 2.5).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Rate vs [S], same enzyme with and without Z","x":{"label":"[S] (mM)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"v (μM/min)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,2],[2,2.67],[4,3.2],[8,3.56],[10,3.64]]},{"name":"With Z","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.57],[2.5,1],[4,1.23],[8,1.52],[10,1.6]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":1,"text":"KM = 1: half of 4","at":"left"},{"series":"With Z","x":2.5,"text":"KM = 2.5: half of 2","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**On the question's plot: where a line hits the vertical axis = 1/Vmax.** The Z line hits higher, so Vmax got smaller.","calc":["no Z: Vmax = 1 ÷ 0.25 = **4 μM/min**","with Z: Vmax = 1 ÷ 0.50 = **2 μM/min**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0},{"series":"With Z","x":0}],"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":0,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.25","at":"right"},{"series":"With Z","x":0,"text":"1/Vmax = 0.50","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Slope = KM/Vmax.** Measure the rise from 1/[S] = 0 to 1, then KM = slope × Vmax.","calc":["no Z: KM = 0.25 × 4 = **1 mM**","with Z: KM = 1.25 × 2 = **2.5 mM**"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":1,"from":0.25,"to":0.5,"text":"slope 0.25","side":"left"},{"x":1,"from":0.5,"to":1.75,"text":"slope 1.25","side":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Where the lines cross** is the fingerprint: here left of the y-axis and above the x-axis, so on **neither** axis.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.25},{"series":"With Z","x":-0.25}],"labels":[{"series":"No inhibitor","x":-0.25,"text":"cross: off both axes","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Match the pattern** to the inhibition type.","marks":[{"t":"type of inhibition","note":"match the crossing point"}],"calc":["cross ON the y-axis → competitive","parallel lines → uncompetitive","cross ON the x-axis → pure noncompetitive","cross anywhere else → **mixed**"]},{"text":"**So Z is mixed:** Vmax went down and KM changed. Competitive would keep Vmax; noncompetitive would keep KM.","graph":{"focus":["No inhibitor","With Z"],"labels":[{"series":"With Z","x":2,"text":"Vmax ↓, KM ↑ → mixed","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. Find where the lines meet: y-axis, x-axis, parallel, or elsewhere."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"Glycogen phosphorylase catalyzes glycogen(n) + Pi → glycogen(n−1) + glucose-1-phosphate and is allosterically activated by AMP. Under which conditions would the reaction rate be greatest?",idStem:"AMP activates glycogen phosphorylase, and phosphate (Pi) is its substrate. Which conditions make product fastest?",choices:["High [AMP] and high [Pi]","High [AMP] and low [Pi]","Low [AMP] and high [Pi]","Low [AMP] and low [Pi]"],ans:0,exp:"Read the equation: **Pi is a substrate** (left side). More substrate → faster rate. **AMP is an activator.** Both high → fastest.",src:"pe6-cp:16",how:{"ask":"Is Pi a substrate, a product or an inhibitor here? Read which side of the arrow it is on.","know":"Rate rises with **substrate concentration** (until saturation). An **allosteric activator** makes the enzyme more active. Glycogen phosphorylase uses Pi as a substrate: glycogen + Pi → glucose-1-phosphate.","steps":[{"text":"Read the equation: Pi is on the left, so it is a substrate.","hl":"glycogen(n) + Pi"},{"text":"AMP is not in the equation; the stem says it is an allosteric activator.","hl":"allosterically activated by AMP"},{"text":"Low substrate or low activator each lowers the rate.","strike":["High [AMP] and low [Pi]","Low [AMP] and high [Pi]","Low [AMP] and low [Pi]"]},{"text":"Picture it: rate rises with [Pi] on each curve, and the high-AMP curve sits above the low-AMP one.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phosphorylase rate versus [Pi] at two AMP levels","x":{"label":"[Pi] (mM)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Rate (relative)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"High AMP","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,33.3],[2,50],[4,66.7],[8,80]]},{"name":"Low AMP","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,13.3],[2,20],[4,26.7],[8,32]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"High AMP","x":8},{"series":"Low AMP","x":8}],"labels":[{"series":"High AMP","x":8,"text":"high AMP: 80","at":"left"},{"series":"Low AMP","x":8,"text":"low AMP: 32","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":8,"from":32,"to":80,"text":"AMP boost","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: high [AMP] and high [Pi]; most substrate and the most active enzyme."}],"trap":"In many pathways Pi is a product or inhibitor, but here the equation shows it as a reactant. Always read the given equation."},teach:[{"text":"**Name the players** before reading the equation.","marks":[{"t":"Glycogen phosphorylase","note":"the enzyme"},{"t":"allosterically activated by AMP","note":"AMP = an on-switch"},{"t":"reaction rate be greatest","note":"fastest = ?"}]},{"text":"**Read the equation like a recipe.** Left of the arrow = used up. Right = made.","marks":[{"t":"glycogen(n) + Pi","note":"used up (substrates)"},{"t":"→","note":"turns into"},{"t":"glycogen(n−1) + glucose-1-phosphate","note":"made (products)"}]},{"text":"**What happens:** glycogen is a chain of n glucoses. Pi (phosphate) snips off one glucose, leaving a chain one shorter.","marks":[{"t":"glycogen(n)","note":"chain of n glucoses"},{"t":"glycogen(n−1)","note":"one glucose shorter"},{"t":"glucose-1-phosphate","note":"cut glucose + Pi"}]},{"text":"**Pi is a substrate** (on the left). More substrate = enzymes find it more often = **faster**, until they are all busy.","marks":[{"t":"Pi","note":"substrate: more = faster"}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Rate rises with substrate, then levels off","x":{"label":"[Pi] (mM)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Rate (relative)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Rate","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,33.3],[2,50],[4,66.7],[8,80]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Rate","x":1,"text":"low Pi: slow","at":"right"},{"series":"Rate","x":8,"text":"high Pi: fast","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Allosteric activator** = a molecule that binds the enzyme at a spot *other* than the active site and makes it work better. AMP is not in the equation.","marks":[{"t":"allosterically activated by AMP","note":"binds elsewhere: speeds up"}]},{"text":"**Both effects together:** at each [Pi], the high-AMP curve sits above the low-AMP curve.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phosphorylase rate vs [Pi] at two AMP levels","x":{"label":"[Pi] (mM)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Rate (relative)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"High AMP","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,33.3],[2,50],[4,66.7],[8,80]]},{"name":"Low AMP","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,13.3],[2,20],[4,26.7],[8,32]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"High AMP","x":8,"text":"high AMP + high Pi","at":"left"},{"series":"Low AMP","x":8,"text":"low AMP: 32","at":"left"}],"spans":[{"x":8,"from":32,"to":80,"text":"AMP boost","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Any \"low\" lowers the rate:** low Pi starves the enzyme of substrate; low AMP leaves the switch off.","marks":[{"t":"AMP","note":"low → switch off"},{"t":"Pi","note":"low → little substrate"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the substrates in the equation (left side) and the activators in the text. Highest rate = most substrate + most activator."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"In a photodetector, light ejects electrons from a metal surface. The electrons cross a vacuum gap to a collector and return through a copper wire to an ammeter that reads 1.0 μA. Which statement correctly describes the charge carriers in the copper wire?",idStem:"Photoelectrons freed in a metal detector travel through a wire circuit. What kind of conduction carries them?",choices:["Free electrons drift through the metal lattice, which is metallic conduction","Cu²⁺ ions drift through the wire, which is ionic conduction","Photons travel along the wire, which is photonic conduction","Electrons hop between dissolved ions, which is electrolytic conduction"],ans:0,exp:"In a metal wire, current is carried by **free (delocalized) electrons** moving through a fixed lattice of cations: **metallic conduction**. Ionic and electrolytic conduction need mobile ions in a solution or melt; the light only freed the electrons.",src:"pe6-cp:17",how:{"ask":"Once the electrons are in the copper wire, what is actually moving, and what is that kind of conduction called?","know":"**Metallic conduction** = free electrons moving through a metal. **Ionic/electrolytic conduction** = ions moving through a solution or melt.","steps":[{"text":"Separate the stages: light frees electrons at the surface, but the question is about the current in the wire.","hl":"return through a copper wire to an ammeter"},{"text":"Rule: in a solid metal the nuclei and core electrons stay fixed; valence electrons are free to drift. Ions move only in solutions or melts.","strike":["Cu²⁺ ions drift through the wire, which is ionic conduction","Electrons hop between dissolved ions, which is electrolytic conduction"]},{"text":"The photon is absorbed at the surface; it is not what flows through the wire.","strike":["Photons travel along the wire, which is photonic conduction"]},{"text":"Count the carriers the ammeter reading represents.","calc":["electrons per second = I ÷ e","= 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ C/s ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C","= **6.3 × 10¹² electrons/s**"],"hl":"1.0 μA"},{"text":"Answer: free electrons drift through the copper lattice, which is metallic conduction."}],"trap":"Ionic conduction is tempting because copper ions are present, but they are locked in the lattice; only the free electrons move."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the pieces of the setup** and what the question really asks about.","marks":[{"t":"light ejects electrons","note":"light knocks e⁻ out"},{"t":"copper wire","note":"a solid metal"},{"t":"1.0 μA","note":"current reading"}]},{"text":"**Three stages:** light frees electrons, they fly across empty space, then they go back through a wire. The question is **only about the wire**.","marks":[{"t":"metal surface","note":"stage 1: light frees e⁻"},{"t":"vacuum gap","note":"stage 2: e⁻ fly across"},{"t":"copper wire","note":"stage 3: asked about"}]},{"text":"**Current = moving charge.** The **charge carriers** are whatever actually moves to carry it.","marks":[{"t":"charge carriers","note":"what actually moves"}]},{"text":"**Inside a metal:** Cu atoms sit fixed in a grid (lattice) as Cu²⁺ ions. Their outer electrons are **free** to drift: a \"sea of electrons\".","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"fixed Cu ions + free e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Ions only move in liquids** (a solution or a melt). A solid copper wire has no dissolved ions, so ionic and electrolytic conduction are out.","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"solid: ions locked"}]},{"text":"**The photon is used up at the surface.** It gives its energy to one electron there; light does not travel down the wire.","marks":[{"t":"light ejects electrons","note":"photon absorbed here"}]},{"text":"**How many electrons is 1.0 μA?** Divide the charge per second by one electron's charge.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 μA","note":"= 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ C/s"}],"calc":["electrons/s = I ÷ e","= 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹","= **6.3 × 10¹² electrons/s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a solid metal wire the carriers are free electrons (metallic conduction). Ions carry current only in solutions or melts."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"5E",q:"How much heat must be added to 50 g of solid aluminum at its melting point to melt it completely? (Heat of fusion of Al = 4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg; specific heat of Al = 900 J/kg·K)",idStem:"Aluminum's heat of fusion is 4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg. How much heat melts 50 g already at its melting point?",choices:["1 × 10⁴ J","2 × 10⁴ J","1 × 10⁵ J","4 × 10⁵ J"],ans:1,exp:"At the melting point there is no ΔT, so **q = mL** = 0.050 kg × 4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg = **2 × 10⁴ J**. The specific heat is extra information you don't need.",src:"pe6-cp:18",how:{"ask":"How much heat melts a solid that is already at its melting point?","know":"During a phase change the temperature does NOT change, so use **q = m × L** (L = latent heat), not q = mcΔT. L is given per **kg**, so mass must be in kg.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: the solid is already at its melting point, so all heat goes into the phase change, not into raising T.","hl":"at its melting point to melt it completely","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 50 g of aluminum near its melting point","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":26,"step":2},"y":{"label":"T (°C)","min":580,"max":720,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Aluminum","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,600],[2.7,660],[22.7,660],[25,700]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Aluminum","x":2.7},{"series":"Aluminum","x":22.7}],"yLine":660,"shade":[{"x1":2.7,"x2":22.7,"text":"melting plateau: 660 °C"}],"labels":[{"series":"Aluminum","x":2.7,"text":"melting starts","at":"above"},{"series":"Aluminum","x":22.7,"text":"all liquid","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Use q = mL for a phase change; specific heat is for a temperature change and is not needed here.","hl":"specific heat of Al = 900 J/kg·K"},{"text":"Convert grams to kilograms, then multiply.","calc":["m = 50 g = **0.050 kg**","q = mL = 0.050 kg × 4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg","q = **2.0 × 10⁴ J**"]},{"text":"4 × 10⁵ J is L alone without the mass; 1 × 10⁵ and 1 × 10⁴ come from slips in the mass or the power of ten.","strike":["1 × 10⁴ J","1 × 10⁵ J","4 × 10⁵ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 × 10⁴ J, the length of the flat plateau on the heating curve."}],"trap":"The question gives the specific heat to tempt you into q = mcΔT, but ΔT = 0 during melting. Exams often include data you don't need."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives you and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"50 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg","note":"heat to melt 1 kg"},{"t":"900 J/kg·K","note":"heat to warm 1 kg by 1°"}]},{"text":"**What is happening?** The metal is already at its melting point. It is not getting hotter; it is turning from solid into liquid.","marks":[{"t":"at its melting point","note":"temperature stays the same"},{"t":"melt it completely","note":"solid → liquid"}]},{"text":"While something melts, **its temperature stays flat**. All the heat goes into breaking the solid apart.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating aluminum: temperature vs heat added","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":30,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":600,"max":700,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Aluminum","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,620],[4,660],[24,660],[28,690]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":4,"x2":24,"text":"melting"}],"labels":[{"series":"Aluminum","x":24,"text":"temperature flat","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"**Heat of fusion** = the heat needed to melt **1 kg**. This is the number you need.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg","note":"heat of fusion: per 1 kg"}]},{"text":"Specific heat is for **warming up**, not melting. No temperature change here, so you **don't need it**. Exams add extra numbers on purpose.","marks":[{"t":"900 J/kg·K","note":"not needed: no warming"}]},{"text":"The heat of fusion is **per kilogram**, but the mass is in **grams**. Convert first.","marks":[{"t":"50 g","note":"50 g = 0.050 kg"}],"calc":["1 kg = 1000 g","50 g ÷ 1000 = **0.050 kg**"]},{"text":"Now multiply: **mass × heat per kg**.","marks":[{"t":"50 g","note":"0.050 kg"},{"t":"4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg","note":"× this"}],"calc":["q = m × L","q = **0.050 kg** × 4.0 × 10⁵ J/kg","0.05 × 4 = **0.2**","q = 0.2 × 10⁵ = **2 × 10⁴ J**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** melting or boiling (temperature flat) → heat = mass × heat of fusion or vaporization. Warming (temperature changes) → heat = m·c·ΔT. Convert g → kg when the constant is per kg."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"The PET tracer [¹⁸F]fluorodeoxyglucose is glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) in which one hydroxyl group has been replaced by an ¹⁸F atom. What is the approximate molar mass of this tracer?",idStem:"A glucose molecule has the radioactive isotope ¹⁸F in place of one OH (C₆H₁₁FO₅). What is its molar mass?",choices:["179 g/mol","180 g/mol","181 g/mol","182 g/mol"],ans:2,exp:"Glucose = 180 g/mol. Remove one **OH (−17)**, add **¹⁸F (+18)** → 180 − 17 + 18 = **181 g/mol** (formula C₆H₁₁FO₅).",src:"pe6-cp:19",how:{"ask":"Build the tracer's formula from glucose, then add up the masses using 18 for this F.","know":"**Molar mass = sum of atomic masses.** C = 12, H = 1, O = 16. For a named isotope, use its mass number (¹⁸F → 18).","steps":[{"text":"Compare the structures: one –OH of glucose is replaced by ¹⁸F.","hl":"one hydroxyl group has been replaced by an ¹⁸F atom","fig":["beta-glucopyranose","fdg"]},{"text":"Start from glucose and do the swap.","calc":["glucose C₆H₁₂O₆ = 6(12) + 12(1) + 6(16) = **180 g/mol**","remove OH: 180 − 17 = **163**","add ¹⁸F: 163 + 18 = **181 g/mol**"]},{"text":"180 ignores the swap; 179 would mean the swap made it lighter, but ¹⁸F (18) outweighs OH (17).","strike":["179 g/mol","180 g/mol"]},{"text":"182 uses 19, the mass of ordinary fluorine, instead of the isotope named.","hl":"[¹⁸F]fluorodeoxyglucose","strike":["182 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 181 g/mol, glucose minus OH plus ¹⁸F, formula C₆H₁₁FO₅."}],"trap":"182 comes from using fluorine's periodic-table mass (19). 179 comes from removing only the O and forgetting the H. The isotope is named, so use 18."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the swap:** start from glucose, take out one –OH, put in one ¹⁸F.","marks":[{"t":"C₆H₁₂O₆","note":"glucose formula"},{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"–OH removed"},{"t":"¹⁸F atom","note":"F-18 added"}]},{"text":"**Molar mass** = the mass of one mole, in g/mol = add up the masses of all the atoms. C = 12, H = 1, O = 16.","marks":[{"t":"molar mass","note":"grams per mole"}]},{"text":"**Glucose first:** read the formula as 6 C, 12 H, 6 O.","marks":[{"t":"C₆H₁₂O₆","note":"6 C, 12 H, 6 O"}],"calc":["72 + 12 + 96 = **180 g/mol**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Mass from each element in glucose","x":{"label":"Element"},"y":{"label":"g/mol","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["C: 6 × 12","H: 12 × 1","O: 6 × 16"],"series":[{"name":"Mass","values":[72,12,96]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C: 6 × 12","Mass"],["H: 12 × 1","Mass"],["O: 6 × 16","Mass"]]}},{"text":"**A hydroxyl group is –OH**: one O and one H.","marks":[{"t":"hydroxyl group","note":"–OH = 16 + 1 = 17"}],"calc":["OH = 16 + 1 = **17 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**¹⁸F:** the small 18 is the **mass number**, so this fluorine weighs 18. Ordinary fluorine (¹⁹F) weighs 19.","marks":[{"t":"[¹⁸F]","note":"isotope: mass 18"}]},{"text":"**Do the swap.**","marks":[{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"− 17"},{"t":"¹⁸F atom","note":"+ 18"}],"calc":["180 − 17 = **163**","163 + 18 = **181 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**Check with the new formula** C₆H₁₁FO₅ (one H and one O fewer, one F more).","marks":[{"t":"this tracer","note":"C₆H₁₁FO₅"}],"calc":["6 × 12 + 11 × 1 + 18 + 5 × 16","72 + 11 + 18 + 80 = **181 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 180 ignores the swap; 179 makes it lighter, but 18 > 17; 182 uses 19 for normal F instead of ¹⁸F.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁸F atom","note":"18, not 19"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a swap, molar mass = old mass − group removed + group added. A named isotope (¹⁸F) uses its mass number."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Fluorine-18, used in PET imaging, decays by positron emission, whereas fluorine-19 is stable. Compared with a ¹⁹F nucleus, the ¹⁸F nucleus has a neutron-to-proton ratio that is:",idStem:"Compared with stable ¹⁹F, an atom of ¹⁸F has one fewer:",choices:["lower, and positron emission converts a neutron into a proton","higher, and positron emission converts a proton into a neutron","lower, and positron emission converts a proton into a neutron","higher, and positron emission converts a neutron into a proton"],ans:2,exp:"Both have **Z = 9**; ¹⁸F has **9 neutrons** vs 10 in ¹⁹F, so N/Z is lower (1.00 vs 1.11). Neutron-poor nuclei decay by **β⁺: p → n + e⁺ + ν**, giving ¹⁸O with 10 neutrons.",src:"pe6-cp:20",how:{"ask":"How many neutrons does each isotope have, and which way must positron emission change the proton and neutron counts?","know":"**Mass number A = protons Z + neutrons N**; isotopes share Z and differ in N. **β⁺ (positron) decay: p → n + e⁺ + ν**: Z drops by 1, N rises by 1, A unchanged. It happens in nuclei with too few neutrons; β⁻ decay (n → p) happens in neutron-rich nuclei.","steps":[{"text":"Same element means same Z = 9. Use mass number A = Z + N to get neutrons.","hl":"Compared with a ¹⁹F nucleus","calc":["¹⁹F: N = 19 − 9 = **10**, N/Z = 10/9 = **1.11**","¹⁸F: N = 18 − 9 = **9**, N/Z = 9/9 = **1.00**"]},{"text":"¹⁸F has one fewer neutron, so its N/Z is lower.","strike":["higher, and positron emission converts a proton into a neutron","higher, and positron emission converts a neutron into a proton"]},{"text":"Rule for β⁺ decay: a proton becomes a neutron plus a positron, which raises N/Z toward stability.","calc":["p → n + e⁺ + ν","¹⁸F (Z 9, N 9) → ¹⁸O (Z **8**, N **10**)"],"hl":"decays by positron emission"},{"text":"Compare the nuclides: decay moves ¹⁸F to the neutron-richer ¹⁸O.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Protons and neutrons in each nuclide","x":{"label":"Nuclide"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["¹⁸F","¹⁹F","¹⁸O"],"series":[{"name":"Protons Z","values":[9,9,8]},{"name":"Neutrons N","values":[9,10,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["¹⁸F","Neutrons N"],["¹⁸O","Neutrons N"]]},"strike":["lower, and positron emission converts a neutron into a proton"]},{"text":"Answer: lower, and positron emission converts a proton into a neutron, fixing the neutron deficit."}],"trap":"\"Converts a neutron into a proton\" is β⁻ decay. A positron carries positive charge away, so the nucleus loses a proton and gains a neutron."},teach:[{"text":"**Two kinds of fluorine:** the number after the name is the mass number.","marks":[{"t":"Fluorine-18","note":"mass number 18"},{"t":"positron emission","note":"gives off a + electron"},{"t":"fluorine-19 is stable","note":"does not decay"}]},{"text":"**A nucleus = protons (Z) + neutrons (N).** Mass number A = Z + N. Every fluorine has **Z = 9** protons.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁹F nucleus","note":"Z = 9, A = 19"},{"t":"¹⁸F nucleus","note":"Z = 9, A = 18"}],"calc":["A = Z + N → **N = A − Z**"]},{"text":"**Count the neutrons.**","calc":["¹⁹F: N = 19 − 9 = **10**","¹⁸F: N = 18 − 9 = **9**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Protons and neutrons in each fluorine","x":{"label":"Nuclide"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["¹⁸F","¹⁹F"],"series":[{"name":"Protons Z","values":[9,9]},{"name":"Neutrons N","values":[9,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["¹⁸F","Neutrons N"],["¹⁹F","Neutrons N"]]}},{"text":"**Neutron-to-proton ratio = N ÷ Z.** ¹⁸F has one fewer neutron, so its ratio is **lower**.","marks":[{"t":"neutron-to-proton ratio","note":"N ÷ Z"}],"calc":["¹⁹F: 10 ÷ 9 = **1.11**","¹⁸F: 9 ÷ 9 = **1.00** (lower)"]},{"text":"**Positron emission (β⁺)**: inside the nucleus a **proton turns into a neutron** and shoots out a positron (e⁺) and a neutrino.","marks":[{"t":"positron emission","note":"p → n + e⁺"}],"calc":["p → n + e⁺ + ν","Z goes down 1, N goes up 1, A same"]},{"text":"**Apply it:** ¹⁸F becomes ¹⁸O, with one more neutron. The ratio rises toward a stable value.","calc":["N/Z: 9/9 = 1.00 → 10/8 = **1.25**"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"¹⁸F before and ¹⁸O after positron emission","x":{"label":"Nuclide"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"categories":["¹⁸F (before)","¹⁸O (after)"],"series":[{"name":"Protons Z","values":[9,8]},{"name":"Neutrons N","values":[9,10]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["¹⁸F (before)","Protons Z"],["¹⁸O (after)","Neutrons N"]]}},{"text":"**Why it decays:** ¹⁸F is **neutron-poor**, so it fixes this by making a neutron. Neutron-rich nuclei do the reverse (β⁻: n → p).","marks":[{"t":"fluorine-19 is stable","note":"N/Z 1.11: stable"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** \"higher\" ratio is wrong (9 < 10 neutrons), and \"neutron into proton\" is β⁻, not positron emission.","marks":[{"t":"¹⁸F nucleus","note":"9 n: lower ratio"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** N = A − Z. Too few neutrons → β⁺ (p → n), Z drops by 1. Too many neutrons → β⁻ (n → p), Z rises by 1."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"In a proposed mechanism, a histidine side chain, modeled by the compound shown, first uses a lone pair to attack the phosphorus of a phosphoryl group and in a later step accepts a proton. Which labeled atom carries out both steps?",idStem:"A biotin nitrogen donates its lone pair to CO₂, then later pulls an H⁺ off a threonine. What are its two roles?",fig:"methylimidazole-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:2,exp:"Only an atom with a **lone pair that is not part of the aromatic sextet** can give it to phosphorus or to H⁺. **III, the =N– nitrogen, holds its lone pair in the ring plane**, so it acts first as a nucleophile and later as a base. I, the N–H nitrogen, uses its lone pair for aromaticity; II and IV are carbons with no lone pair.",src:"pe6-cp:22",how:{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a lone pair that is free to form a new bond?","know":"• **Nucleophile:** gives an electron pair to an electron-poor atom (C, P …) to form a bond. **Base:** gives an electron pair to H⁺. The same lone pair can do both.\n• Imidazole has two N atoms. The **N–H nitrogen** (pyrrole-like) puts its lone pair into the aromatic ring, so it is unavailable. The **=N– nitrogen** (pyridine-like) has its lone pair pointing out of the ring, so it is the reactive one; its conjugate acid has pKa ≈ 6–7.\n• Carbon atoms bonded only to atoms and H have no lone pair to give.","steps":[{"text":"Attacking phosphorus with a lone pair is acting as a nucleophile; accepting a proton is acting as a base. Both need a free lone pair.","hl":"uses a lone pair to attack the phosphorus"},{"text":"II and IV are carbons, bonded only to other atoms and H, with no lone pair.","fig":"methylimidazole-sites","strike":["II","IV"]},{"text":"Compare the two nitrogens.","calc":["I: N–H, lone pair **inside** the aromatic π sextet → not available","III: =N–, lone pair **in the ring plane** → available"],"strike":["I"]},{"text":"III, the pyridine-like nitrogen, attacks P and later picks up a proton.","fig":"methylimidazole-n3"},{"text":"Answer: III. Rule: in a histidine ring, the N without an H is the nucleophile and base."}],"trap":"I, the N–H nitrogen, looks like the obvious nitrogen with a lone pair, but that pair is part of the aromatic sextet. Using it would break aromaticity, so it neither attacks P nor takes H⁺."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** A five-atom ring of three carbons and two nitrogens. This ring is the working part of histidine.","fig":"t6-mim-read"},{"text":"**A lone pair is two electrons on one atom, not used in a bond.** Neutral N with three bonds has one; a carbon with four bonds has none.","fig":"t6-mim-pairs"},{"text":"**Nucleophile: uses a lone pair to bond to an electron-poor atom (here P). Base: uses a lone pair to grab H⁺** (H with no electron). Both need a free pair.","fig":"t6-phosphate-target"},{"text":"**II and IV are carbons with four bonds and no lone pair.**","fig":"t6-mim-carbons-out"},{"text":"**The ring is aromatic: six electrons shared around it make it extra stable.** The N–H nitrogen gives its pair to those six, so it can't use it.","fig":"t6-mim-n1-out"},{"text":"**The N without H already joins the ring by its double bond.** Its lone pair points outward, free to use.","fig":"t6-mim-n3-free"},{"text":"**Job 1: that pair bonds to P.** The N now has four bonds and a + charge.","fig":"t6-mim-n3-p"},{"text":"**Job 2: later the same nitrogen's pair grabs H⁺.**","fig":"t6-mim-n3-h"},{"text":"**Rule:** in a histidine ring, the N without H has the free lone pair (nucleophile and base). For each labeled atom, ask: is it N or O with a pair the ring isn't using?","fig":"methylimidazole-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"A ligand, shown, binds in a pocket where an arginine side chain donates a hydrogen bond to it. Which labeled atom of the ligand can accept this hydrogen bond but cannot donate one?",idStem:"Pyruvate's carboxylate and ketone oxygens are held by Arg and Gln side chains. How does pyruvate interact?",fig:"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites",choices:["I","II","III","IV"],ans:0,exp:"An H-bond **donor** needs an H on N, O or F; an **acceptor** needs a lone pair on N, O or F. **I, the carbonyl O, has lone pairs and no H**, so it can only accept. II, the O–H, can both donate and accept; III, the amide N–H, donates (its lone pair is tied up in resonance); IV is a CH₃ and does neither.",src:"pe6-cp:23",how:{"ask":"For each labeled atom: does it have an H on N or O, and does it have a free lone pair on N or O?","know":"• **Hydrogen bond:** X–H ··· Y, where X and Y are N, O or F.\n• **Donor:** the X–H (an H on N, O or F). **Acceptor:** a lone pair on N, O or F.\n• Carbonyl O: lone pairs, no H → acceptor only. O–H: donor and acceptor. Amide N–H: donor only, because the N lone pair is delocalized into C=O. C–H: neither.\n• Arg, Lys, Gln, Asn N–H groups are donors, so they pair with ligand acceptors.","steps":[{"text":"Two conditions: the atom must have a lone pair on N or O, and no H on it.","hl":"can accept this hydrogen bond but cannot donate one"},{"text":"Classify each labeled atom.","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites","calc":["I: C=O oxygen → lone pairs, **no H** → acceptor only","II: O–H → **donor and acceptor**","III: amide N–H → donor only","IV: CH₃ → neither"]},{"text":"IV has no N or O. III has an H to give, and its lone pair is used in amide resonance.","strike":["IV","III"]},{"text":"II can accept, but its H makes it a donor too.","strike":["II"]},{"text":"Answer: I, the carbonyl oxygen. Rule: an O or N with no H attached can only accept.","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-o1"}],"trap":"II, the O–H, accepts hydrogen bonds well, but it can also donate one, so it does not fit \"cannot donate\". Read both halves of the condition."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Corners and line ends are carbons with hidden H. Letters are O and N; 'OH' and 'NH' hold one H.","fig":"t6-hea-read"},{"text":"**Hydrogen bond: an H on N or O is drawn to a lone pair on another N or O.** The H side is the donor; the lone-pair side is the acceptor.","fig":"t6-arg-donor"},{"text":"**You need: a lone pair on N or O (accepts) and no H on that atom (can't donate).**","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites"},{"text":"**IV is a carbon: no lone pair, and C–H does not donate.**","fig":"t6-hea-iv"},{"text":"**II has lone pairs, but its H lets it donate too.**","fig":"t6-hea-ii"},{"text":"**III holds an H, so it donates.** Its lone pair is also shared with the C=O, so it accepts poorly.","fig":"t6-hea-iii"},{"text":"**I has two lone pairs and no H.** It can take arginine's H but has none to give.","fig":"t6-hea-i"},{"text":"**Rule:** donor = H on N or O; acceptor = lone pair on N or O. An N or O with no H (like C=O) only accepts. For each labeled atom, ask: lone pair? H attached?","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5E",q:"A proposed mechanism shows a threonine residue being deprotonated by a biotin intermediate and, in a subsequent step, removing a proton from the methyl group of pyruvate. This threonine most likely functions to:",idStem:"A Thr residue first loses its H⁺, then takes an H⁺ from the substrate. What is its role?",choices:["shuttle protons between reacting groups","coordinate a metal cofactor","covalently bind CO₂","transfer a methyl group"],ans:0,exp:"Thr gives up an H⁺ and later takes one from the substrate, so it moves protons from one place to another: a **proton shuttle** (acid–base catalysis).",src:"pe6-cp:24",how:{"ask":"What do you call a group that gives away a proton and then takes one back?","know":"Acid–base catalysis: a **general acid** donates H⁺; a **general base** removes H⁺. A residue that does both in turn moves protons around: a **proton shuttle**.","steps":[{"text":"Follow the proton in step 1: Thr loses its H⁺ to the biotin intermediate.","hl":"being deprotonated by a biotin intermediate","fig":"threonine-sc"},{"text":"Follow the proton in step 2: Thr–O⁻ takes an H⁺ from pyruvate methyl.","hl":"removing a proton from the methyl group of pyruvate","calc":["step 1: Thr–OH → Thr–O⁻ + **H⁺** (to biotin)","step 2: Thr–O⁻ + **H⁺** (from pyruvate) → Thr–OH","net: Thr unchanged, one H⁺ moved"]},{"text":"Thr –OH has no metal-binding role here and never bonds CO₂ or carries a methyl group in this mechanism.","strike":["coordinate a metal cofactor","covalently bind CO₂","transfer a methyl group"]},{"text":"Answer: it shuttles protons between reacting groups, acting first as acid then as base, and is regenerated."}],"trap":"\"Transfer a methyl group\" is tempting because pyruvate's methyl is mentioned. But only a proton (H⁺) is removed from it, and nothing moves the methyl group itself."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the two things threonine does.**","marks":[{"t":"threonine residue","note":"an amino acid in the enzyme"},{"t":"deprotonated","note":"loses an H⁺"},{"t":"removing a proton","note":"takes an H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Terms:** a **proton** = H⁺. A **mechanism** = the step-by-step story of a reaction. A **residue** = one amino acid in a protein chain.","marks":[{"t":"A proposed mechanism","note":"step-by-step story"}]},{"text":"**Threonine's side chain** has an –OH group. Its H can leave as H⁺, and the O⁻ left behind can grab an H⁺ back.","marks":[{"t":"threonine residue","note":"side chain: –OH"}]},{"text":"**Step 1:** biotin pulls the H⁺ off threonine's –OH.","marks":[{"t":"being deprotonated by a biotin intermediate","note":"Thr–OH gives H⁺ away"}],"calc":["Thr–OH → Thr–O⁻ + **H⁺** (to biotin)"]},{"text":"**Step 2:** the Thr–O⁻ grabs an H⁺ from pyruvate's CH₃.","marks":[{"t":"removing a proton from the methyl group of pyruvate","note":"Thr–O⁻ takes H⁺ back"}],"calc":["Thr–O⁻ + **H⁺** (from pyruvate) → Thr–OH"]},{"text":"**Track the H on threonine's O:** 1 → 0 → 1. It gives one away, then takes one, ending as it started.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"H atoms on threonine's oxygen during the mechanism","x":{"label":"Step","min":0,"max":2,"step":1},"y":{"label":"H on Thr oxygen","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Thr O–H","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[1,0],[2,1]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Thr O–H","x":0,"text":"start: Thr–OH","at":"right"},{"series":"Thr O–H","x":1,"text":"step 1: gave H⁺","at":"above"},{"series":"Thr O–H","x":2,"text":"step 2: took H⁺","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Net effect:** one H⁺ moved from pyruvate to biotin, carried by threonine. That is a **proton shuttle** (acid–base catalysis).","marks":[{"t":"This threonine","note":"proton shuttle"}],"calc":["net: H⁺ from pyruvate → biotin","Thr: **unchanged**, just carried H⁺"]},{"text":"**Traps:** no metal is mentioned; biotin (not Thr) carries CO₂; the methyl group only loses an H⁺, it is not moved.","marks":[{"t":"biotin intermediate","note":"biotin, not Thr, holds CO₂"},{"t":"methyl group","note":"loses H⁺, not transferred"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a residue that loses H⁺ in one step and gains H⁺ in another is a proton shuttle: general acid/base catalysis."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Partial acid hydrolysis of a tripeptide yields free Gly and Ala along with the dipeptides Ala–Gly and Gly–Ala. Complete hydrolysis yields Ala and Gly in a 2:1 ratio. What is the sequence of the tripeptide?",idStem:"Partial hydrolysis of a tripeptide gives Gly, Ala, Ala–Gly and Gly–Ala. Which tripeptide was it?",choices:["Gly–Ala–Ala","Ala–Ala–Gly","Ala–Gly–Ala","Gly–Ala–Gly"],ans:2,exp:"**2 Ala + 1 Gly.** To produce both **Ala–Gly** and **Gly–Ala**, Gly must sit between two Ala residues → **Ala–Gly–Ala**.",src:"pe6-cp:25",how:{"ask":"Which residues are in the tripeptide (the 2:1 ratio), and which order produces both dipeptides?","know":"Partial hydrolysis cuts some peptide bonds, leaving fragments that **keep the original order** (written N → C). Each dipeptide shows two neighbors in order.","steps":[{"text":"Composition first: a 2:1 ratio in a tripeptide means two Ala and one Gly.","hl":"Ala and Gly in a 2:1 ratio","strike":["Gly–Ala–Gly"]},{"text":"List each candidate with two Ala and one Gly, and the dipeptides it can release.","calc":["Gly–Ala–Ala → Gly–Ala, **Ala–Ala**","Ala–Ala–Gly → **Ala–Ala**, Ala–Gly","Ala–Gly–Ala → **Ala–Gly** and **Gly–Ala**"]},{"text":"The observed fragments include both Ala–Gly and Gly–Ala and no Ala–Ala.","hl":"the dipeptides Ala–Gly and Gly–Ala","strike":["Gly–Ala–Ala","Ala–Ala–Gly"]},{"text":"Check: Gly in the middle is flanked by Ala on both sides.","fig":"ala-gly-ala"},{"text":"Answer: Ala–Gly–Ala, the only sequence that can give both overlapping dipeptides."}],"trap":"Don't just match letters. List the two dipeptides each choice would produce and compare them with the data."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the clues:** a 3-unit chain, two kinds of cutting, and the pieces.","marks":[{"t":"Partial acid hydrolysis","note":"cut SOME bonds"},{"t":"tripeptide","note":"3 amino acids in a row"},{"t":"Complete hydrolysis","note":"cut ALL bonds"}]},{"text":"**Hydrolysis** = water breaks the peptide bonds between amino acids. Pieces **keep their order**, written from the N-end to the C-end.","marks":[{"t":"dipeptides","note":"2-unit pieces = neighbors"}]},{"text":"**Complete hydrolysis counts the units.** 2:1 in a chain of 3 means **2 Ala + 1 Gly**.","marks":[{"t":"2:1 ratio","note":"2 Ala : 1 Gly"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Amino acids after complete hydrolysis","x":{"label":"Amino acid"},"y":{"label":"Count in the tripeptide","min":0,"max":3,"step":1},"categories":["Ala","Gly"],"series":[{"name":"Count","values":[2,1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Ala","Count"],["Gly","Count"]]}},{"text":"**Each dipeptide shows two neighbors, in order.**","marks":[{"t":"Ala–Gly","note":"Ala right before Gly"},{"t":"Gly–Ala","note":"Gly right before Ala"}]},{"text":"**List every chain with 2 Ala + 1 Gly** and the 2-unit pieces each can give.","calc":["Gly–Ala–Ala → Gly–Ala, **Ala–Ala**","Ala–Ala–Gly → **Ala–Ala**, Ala–Gly","Ala–Gly–Ala → **Ala–Gly** and **Gly–Ala**"]},{"text":"**Only Gly in the middle** gives both pieces seen. The others would give Ala–Ala, which was not found.","marks":[{"t":"Ala–Gly","note":"cut after 2nd unit"},{"t":"Gly–Ala","note":"cut after 1st unit"}],"calc":["Ala–Gly │ Ala → **Ala–Gly** + Ala","Ala │ Gly–Ala → Ala + **Gly–Ala**"]},{"text":"**Gly–Ala–Gly is out** because it has 2 Gly and 1 Ala: the wrong count.","marks":[{"t":"2:1 ratio","note":"needs 2 Ala, not 2 Gly"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count units from complete hydrolysis, then overlap the partial pieces like puzzle pieces; the order must explain every fragment."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"Methane burns completely in oxygen. What volume of O₂, measured at STP, is consumed when 16 g of CH₄ burns?",idStem:"What volume of O₂ at STP burns 1 mol of CH₄ completely?",choices:["22.4 L","33.6 L","44.8 L","67.2 L"],ans:2,exp:"16 g ÷ 16 g/mol = **1 mol CH₄**. **CH₄ + 2 O₂ → CO₂ + 2 H₂O**, so 2 mol O₂ × 22.4 L/mol = **44.8 L**.",src:"pe6-cp:26",how:{"ask":"How many moles of O₂ does 1 mol CH₄ need, and what volume is that at STP?","know":"**Balance the equation first**; the coefficients are the mole ratios. At STP, **1 mol of any gas = 22.4 L**.","steps":[{"text":"Write and balance the combustion.","hl":"Methane burns completely in oxygen","calc":["CH₄ + **2** O₂ → CO₂ + **2** H₂O"]},{"text":"Convert mass to moles, then use the mole ratio.","hl":"16 g of CH₄","calc":["n(CH₄) = 16 g ÷ 16 g/mol = **1.0 mol**","n(O₂) = 1.0 × 2 = **2.0 mol**","V = 2.0 mol × 22.4 L/mol = **44.8 L**"]},{"text":"Compare the moles each step uses.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles in the reaction of 16 g CH₄","x":{"label":"Species"},"y":{"label":"Moles","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"categories":["CH₄","O₂","CO₂","H₂O"],"series":[{"name":"Moles","values":[1,2,1,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["O₂","Moles"]]}},{"text":"22.4 L assumes 1:1; 33.6 and 67.2 L use 1.5 or 3 mol of O₂.","strike":["22.4 L","33.6 L","67.2 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 44.8 L, two moles of O₂ per mole of CH₄ at 22.4 L/mol."}],"trap":"22.4 L is 1 mol of O₂. That comes from skipping the balancing step."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the given and the wanted.**","marks":[{"t":"16 g of CH₄","note":"mass of fuel"},{"t":"at STP","note":"0 °C and 1 atm"},{"t":"volume of O₂","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**Burning completely** (combustion) means reacting with O₂ to make only CO₂ and H₂O.","marks":[{"t":"burns completely in oxygen","note":"→ CO₂ + H₂O"}],"calc":["CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O (not balanced)"]},{"text":"**Balance it:** same number of each atom on both sides.","calc":["C: 1 left, 1 in CO₂ ✓","H: 4 left → **2** H₂O","O: 2 + 2 = 4 → **2** O₂","CH₄ + **2** O₂ → CO₂ + **2** H₂O"]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.** Molar mass of CH₄ = 12 + 4 × 1 = 16 g/mol.","marks":[{"t":"16 g","note":"÷ 16 g/mol = 1 mol"}],"calc":["n = 16 g ÷ 16 g/mol = **1.0 mol CH₄**"]},{"text":"**Coefficients = mole ratio.** 1 mol CH₄ needs **2 mol O₂**.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles when 16 g CH₄ burns","x":{"label":"Species"},"y":{"label":"Moles","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"categories":["CH₄","O₂","CO₂","H₂O"],"series":[{"name":"Moles","values":[1,2,1,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["CH₄","Moles"],["O₂","Moles"]]}},{"text":"**STP shortcut:** one mole of any gas at STP fills 22.4 L.","marks":[{"t":"at STP","note":"1 mol gas = 22.4 L"}],"calc":["V = 2.0 mol × 22.4 L/mol = **44.8 L**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 22.4 L forgets the 2 in front of O₂; 33.6 and 67.2 L use 1.5 or 3 mol of O₂.","marks":[{"t":"O₂","note":"2 mol, not 1"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** balance first, grams → moles, use the coefficients as the ratio, then moles × 22.4 L at STP."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"4D",q:"An unknown compound with formula C₄H₁₀O shows only two signals in its ¹H NMR spectrum: a singlet integrating to 9H and a singlet integrating to 1H. Which structure fits the spectrum?",idStem:"Which alcohol's ¹H NMR spectrum shows a doublet?",choiceFigs:["1-butanol","2-butanol-r","tert-butanol","diethyl-ether"],choices:["1-Butanol","2-Butanol","tert-Butanol","Diethyl ether"],ans:2,exp:"A 9H singlet means **three equivalent CH₃ groups with no H on the neighboring carbon**: a tert-butyl group on a carbon with no H. tert-Butanol, (CH₃)₃C–OH, gives exactly that plus the 1H O–H singlet. The others give more signals or split ones.",src:"pe6-cp:27",how:{"ask":"What group of hydrogens produces a 9H signal, and what must be true of its neighbor for that signal to be a singlet?","know":"• **Number of signals** = number of sets of equivalent H.\n• **Integration** = how many H are in each set. 9H = three identical CH₃; 6H = two identical CH₃; 3H = one CH₃.\n• **Splitting (n + 1 rule):** a signal is split into n + 1 peaks by n H on the neighboring carbons. A singlet means n = 0: the neighbor carbon has no H.\n• O–H protons usually appear as a singlet that integrates to 1H.","steps":[{"text":"The key signal: 9H, all equivalent, and not split.","hl":"a singlet integrating to 9H"},{"text":"Translate it into a group.","calc":["9H = **3 × CH₃**, all equivalent","singlet → neighbor carbon has **0 H**","→ (CH₃)₃C– on a carbon with no H"]},{"text":"tert-Butanol has exactly that: three CH₃ on one central carbon that carries only the OH.","fig":"tert-butanol-hl"},{"text":"1-Butanol and 2-butanol have five sets of H, with no 9H group. Diethyl ether gives a 6H triplet and a 4H quartet.","strike":["1-Butanol","2-Butanol","Diethyl ether"]},{"text":"Answer: tert-butanol. Rule: a 9H singlet means a tert-butyl group on a carbon with no H."}],"trap":"Diethyl ether also has a very simple spectrum, only two signals, but they are a 6H triplet and a 4H quartet: its CH₃ and CH₂ groups split each other."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Corners and line ends are carbons; a carbon makes 4 bonds, the rest are hidden H. All four choices are C₄H₁₀O.","fig":"t6-tbuoh-read"},{"text":"**¹H NMR gives one signal per set of H in identical surroundings.** Two signals = two kinds of H.","fig":"t6-tbuoh-kinds"},{"text":"**Integration = how many H in the set.** 9H means three identical CH₃ groups.","fig":"t6-tbuoh-9h"},{"text":"**Splitting: n H on the next-door carbon give n + 1 peaks.** A singlet (one peak) means that carbon has no H.","fig":"t6-tbuoh-neighbor"},{"text":"**tert-Butanol fits: one 9H singlet and one 1H singlet** (an O–H signal is usually unsplit).","fig":"t6-tbuoh-fits"},{"text":"**1-Butanol: four different carbon sets plus the OH = five signals.** No 9H set.","fig":"t6-1-butanol-sets"},{"text":"**2-Butanol: its two CH₃ have different neighbors, so they differ.** With CH₂, CH and OH: five signals.","fig":"t6-2-butanol-sets"},{"text":"**Diethyl ether is symmetric: two signals, but both split by H next door.** No singlets.","fig":"t6-ether-split"},{"text":"**Rule:** a 9H singlet = tert-butyl (three CH₃ on one carbon) on a carbon with no H. In each drawing, group identical H and ask: how many, and how many H next door?","fig":"tert-butanol"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4B",q:"A uniform wooden rod floats in water (density 1.0 g/cm³) with exactly half its volume submerged. The rod is then placed in a second liquid, where it floats with its top surface level with the liquid's surface. What is the density of the second liquid?",idStem:"A rod floats half-submerged in water. In which fluid would it float completely submerged (just barely)?",choices:["0.25 g/cm³","0.5 g/cm³","1.0 g/cm³","2.0 g/cm³"],ans:1,exp:"**Fraction submerged = ρ_object/ρ_fluid.** In water: ρ_rod = 0.5 × 1.0 = 0.5 g/cm³. \"Top level with the surface\" means fully submerged (fraction = 1), so **ρ_fluid = ρ_rod = 0.5 g/cm³**.",src:"pe6-cp:28",how:{"ask":"What fluid density makes the rod sink to exactly fully submerged?","know":"For a floating object: **fraction submerged = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid**. Water = 1.0 g/cm³.","steps":[{"text":"Rule for a floating object: fraction submerged = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid.","hl":"exactly half its volume submerged","calc":["½ = ρ_rod ÷ 1.0 g/cm³","ρ_rod = **0.5 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"Translate the second observation: top level with the surface means the whole rod is submerged yet still floating, fraction = 1.","hl":"its top surface level with the liquid"},{"text":"Apply the same rule in the new liquid.","calc":["1 = 0.5 g/cm³ ÷ ρ_liquid","ρ_liquid = **0.5 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"A denser liquid would let the rod ride higher, and 0.25 g/cm³ would sink it; water itself gives half submerged.","strike":["0.25 g/cm³","1.0 g/cm³","2.0 g/cm³"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.5 g/cm³, equal to the rod density, which is what fully submerged floating requires."}],"trap":"2.0 g/cm³ is the intuitive pick, but a denser fluid makes it float HIGHER. A less dense fluid lets it sink deeper."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the two floating clues.**","marks":[{"t":"density 1.0 g/cm³","note":"water's density"},{"t":"half its volume submerged","note":"50% under water"},{"t":"top surface level with the liquid's surface","note":"100% under, still floats"}]},{"text":"**Density** = mass per volume. An object **floats** when the liquid it pushes aside weighs as much as the object.","marks":[{"t":"floats in water","note":"weight = push-up force"}]},{"text":"**That gives a rule:** fraction under = object density ÷ liquid density.","calc":["weight = ρ_rod × V × g","push-up = ρ_liquid × V_under × g","→ **V_under/V = ρ_rod ÷ ρ_liquid**"]},{"text":"**In water:** half under.","marks":[{"t":"half its volume submerged","note":"½ = ρ_rod ÷ 1.0"}],"calc":["½ = ρ_rod ÷ 1.0 g/cm³","ρ_rod = **0.5 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"**See the rule** for this rod (0.5 g/cm³): denser liquid → it rides higher; liquid as light as the rod → all of it under.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction of the rod under the surface vs liquid density","x":{"label":"Liquid density (g/cm³)","min":0.5,"max":2,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"Fraction under","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.25},"series":[{"name":"Rod","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.5,1],[0.75,0.667],[1,0.5],[1.5,0.333],[2,0.25]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Rod","x":0.5,"text":"0.5: fully under","at":"right"},{"series":"Rod","x":1,"text":"water: half under","at":"above"},{"series":"Rod","x":2,"text":"2.0: only ¼ under","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**In the second liquid:** top level with the surface = whole rod under (fraction 1), but still floating.","marks":[{"t":"top surface level with the liquid's surface","note":"fraction under = 1"}],"calc":["1 = 0.5 ÷ ρ_liquid","ρ_liquid = **0.5 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** 1.0 is water itself (half under); 2.0 makes it ride high (¼ under); 0.25 is lighter than the rod, so it sinks.","marks":[{"t":"second liquid","note":"must equal ρ_rod"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fraction submerged = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid. Floating just fully submerged means the two densities are equal."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A protein with an isoelectric point of 6.0 is subjected to electrophoresis in three separate buffers, at pH 4.0, 6.0 and 8.0. Which prediction about its behavior is correct?",idStem:"A protein is studied at a pH equal to its isoelectric point. What is its net charge?",choices:["At pH 8.0 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move","At pH 4.0 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move","At pH 4.0 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 6.0 it moves toward the anode","At pH 8.0 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move"],ans:3,exp:"At **pH = pI the net charge is 0**, so no migration at pH 6.0. **Above the pI the protein is net negative** and moves to the **anode (+)**; below the pI it is net positive and moves to the cathode.",src:"pe6-cp:29",how:{"ask":"What sign of charge does the protein carry at each buffer pH, and which electrode attracts that charge?","know":"The **isoelectric point (pI)** is the pH where a molecule's net charge is zero. **pH < pI → net + (protonated)**; **pH > pI → net −**. In electrophoresis the **anode is +** and the **cathode is −**; a charged molecule moves toward the opposite charge.","steps":[{"text":"Rule: at pH = pI the net charge is zero; below the pI groups pick up H⁺ (net +), above it they lose H⁺ (net −).","hl":"isoelectric point of 6.0"},{"text":"Read the charge at each buffer pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge of a protein with pI 6.0 versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-12,"max":12,"step":4},"series":[{"name":"Net charge","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,12],[4,8],[5,4],[6,0],[7,-3],[8,-6],[9,-9]]}]},"hl":"at pH 4.0, 6.0 and 8.0","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Net charge","x":4},{"series":"Net charge","x":6},{"series":"Net charge","x":8}],"yLine":0,"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":6,"text":"net +"},{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"net −"}],"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":4,"text":"pH 4: +8","at":"right"},{"series":"Net charge","x":6,"text":"pH 6 = pI: 0","at":"above"},{"series":"Net charge","x":8,"text":"pH 8: −6","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Zero net charge means no net force at pH 6.0.","strike":["At pH 4.0 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 6.0 it moves toward the anode"]},{"text":"Opposite charges attract: the anode is +, the cathode is −. Net + at pH 4.0 goes to the cathode; net − at pH 8.0 goes to the anode.","calc":["pH 4.0 < pI → net **+** → **cathode**","pH 6.0 = pI → **0** → stays","pH 8.0 > pI → net **−** → **anode**"],"strike":["At pH 8.0 it moves toward the cathode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move","At pH 4.0 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move"]},{"text":"Answer: at pH 8.0 it moves toward the anode, and at pH 6.0 it does not move."}],"trap":"Mixing up the electrodes gives \"pH 8.0 toward the cathode\". A net-negative protein is pulled to the positive electrode, the anode."},teach:[{"text":"**Find the protein's key number and the three tests.**","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point of 6.0","note":"pI = 6.0"},{"t":"electrophoresis","note":"electric field pulls charges"},{"t":"pH 4.0, 6.0 and 8.0","note":"three test pHs"}]},{"text":"**Isoelectric point (pI)** = the pH where the protein's + and − charges cancel: **net charge 0**.","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point","note":"pH where net charge = 0"}]},{"text":"**pH sets the charge.** Below the pI (lots of H⁺) the protein picks up H⁺ → **net +**. Above the pI it loses H⁺ → **net −**.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge of a protein with pI 6.0 vs pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-12,"max":12,"step":4},"series":[{"name":"Net charge","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,12],[4,8],[5,4],[6,0],[7,-3],[8,-6],[9,-9]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":3,"x2":6,"text":"net +"},{"x1":6,"x2":9,"text":"net −"}],"labels":[{"series":"Net charge","x":4,"text":"pH 4: +","at":"right"},{"series":"Net charge","x":6,"text":"pH 6 = pI: 0","at":"above"},{"series":"Net charge","x":8,"text":"pH 8: −","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Electrophoresis** has two ends: the **anode is +**, the **cathode is −**. Opposites attract.","marks":[{"t":"electrophoresis","note":"anode +, cathode −"}],"calc":["net + → moves to **cathode (−)**","net − → moves to **anode (+)**"]},{"text":"**pH 4.0** is below the pI, so the protein is net + and moves toward the cathode.","marks":[{"t":"pH 4.0","note":"below pI: + → cathode"}]},{"text":"**pH 6.0** equals the pI: net charge 0, no pull, **it does not move**.","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point of 6.0","note":"at pH 6: charge 0, stays"}]},{"text":"**pH 8.0** is above the pI, so the protein is net − and moves toward the **anode**.","marks":[{"t":"8.0","note":"above pI: − → anode"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** it moves at pH 6 (no: charge 0); to the cathode at pH 8 (no: it is −); to the anode at pH 4 (no: it is +).","marks":[{"t":"Which prediction","note":"check each half"}],"calc":["pH 4 → **cathode**","pH 6 → **stays**","pH 8 → **anode**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH below pI → net + → cathode (−). pH = pI → no movement. pH above pI → net − → anode (+)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Hypochlorous acid released by neutrophils chlorinates a protein side chain at the aromatic ring carbon ortho to a hydroxyl group. Which side chain is modified?",idStem:"Oxidative damage links protein chains through a covalent bond between two phenol side chains. Which residue is involved?",choiceFigs:["phenylalanine-sc","serine-sc","tyrosine-sc","tryptophan-sc"],choices:["Phenylalanine","Serine","Tyrosine","Tryptophan"],ans:2,exp:"The target needs **both an aromatic ring and an OH on that ring** (ortho means the ring carbon next to the one bearing OH). Only tyrosine's side chain, a phenol, has both. Phenylalanine has the ring without OH, serine has OH without a ring, and tryptophan's ring has an N–H, not an OH.",src:"pe6-cp:30",how:{"ask":"Which side chain has both an aromatic ring and a hydroxyl group attached to that ring?","know":"• **Ortho** = the ring position next to a substituent; meta = two away; para = across the ring.\n• Aromatic side chains: **Phe** (benzene ring), **Tyr** (benzene ring with OH = phenol), **Trp** (indole: benzene fused to a five-membered ring with N–H), His (imidazole).\n• OH side chains: Ser, Thr, and Tyr. Only Tyr is in both lists.","steps":[{"text":"The description has two requirements: an aromatic ring and an OH attached to it.","hl":"aromatic ring carbon ortho to a hydroxyl group"},{"text":"Check each side chain for both features.","calc":["Phe: ring **yes**, OH **no**","Ser: ring **no**, OH **yes**","Trp: ring **yes**, OH **no** (N–H instead)","Tyr: ring **yes**, OH **yes**"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine and serine each have only one of the two features.","fig":["phenylalanine-sc","serine-sc"],"strike":["Phenylalanine","Serine"]},{"text":"Tryptophan's ring carries N–H, not O–H.","strike":["Tryptophan"]},{"text":"Answer: tyrosine, a phenol: the carbon beside the C–OH is the ortho position. Rule: ring + OH = Tyr.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"}],"trap":"Tryptophan is the largest aromatic side chain and has an H on a ring heteroatom, but that atom is N, not O. A hydroxyl means O–H."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read them.** Each drawing is an amino acid; only the highlighted side chain differs. Corners are carbons with hidden H.","fig":"t6-phe-read"},{"text":"**Hydroxyl = OH group, an O bonded to an H.**","fig":"t6-ser-hydroxyl"},{"text":"**'Ortho' = the ring carbons right next to the one holding a group.** Across the ring is 'para'.","fig":"t6-tyr-ortho"},{"text":"**So you need two things: an aromatic ring, and an OH bonded directly to it.**","fig":"t6-tyr-two-things"},{"text":"**Phenylalanine has the ring, but only H atoms on it.**","fig":"t6-phe-no-oh"},{"text":"**Serine has an OH, but on a plain CH₂, with no ring.**","fig":"t6-ser-no-ring"},{"text":"**Tryptophan's ring holds an N–H, not an O–H.**","fig":"t6-trp-nh-no"},{"text":"**Tyrosine has both.** HOCl puts Cl on the carbon next to the OH carbon (right).","fig":["t6-tyr-yes","t6-chlorotyrosine"]},{"text":"**Rule:** 'ortho to a hydroxyl' needs an OH directly on an aromatic ring; only tyrosine has it. In each drawing ask: is there a ring, and is an OH bonded right to it?","fig":"t6-tyr-ortho"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Technetium-99m, used in medical imaging, is an excited nuclear state that relaxes to technetium-99 by emitting a 140-keV gamma ray. How do the atomic number Z and mass number A change in this emission?",idStem:"An isotope emits gamma rays. What particles is it giving off?",choices:["Z decreases by 2 and A decreases by 4","Z increases by 1 and A is unchanged","Z is unchanged and A decreases by 1","Neither Z nor A changes"],ans:3,exp:"A **gamma ray is a photon: no mass number, no charge**. Tc-99m → Tc-99 + γ keeps **Z = 43 and A = 99**; only the nuclear energy falls by 140 keV.",src:"pe6-cp:32",how:{"ask":"What is a gamma ray made of, and what does losing one take away from a nucleus?","know":"Radiation types: **α = helium nucleus (⁴He)**; **β⁻ = electron**; **β⁺ = positron**; **γ = high-energy photon** (no mass, no charge).","steps":[{"text":"Translate: a gamma ray is a photon, with no charge and no nucleons.","hl":"emitting a 140-keV gamma ray"},{"text":"Match each change to the emission that causes it: Z −2, A −4 is alpha; Z +1 is beta-minus; A −1 is neutron loss.","strike":["Z decreases by 2 and A decreases by 4","Z increases by 1 and A is unchanged","Z is unchanged and A decreases by 1"]},{"text":"Check the mass carried by the photon: far less than one nucleon.","calc":["E = 140 keV × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ J/keV = **2.2 × 10⁻¹⁴ J**","Δm = E/c² = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ (3.0 × 10⁸)² = **2.5 × 10⁻³¹ kg**","one nucleon ≈ 1.7 × 10⁻²⁷ kg → **no nucleon lost**"]},{"text":"Compare the nuclide before and after.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Z and A before and after gamma emission","x":{"label":"Nuclide"},"y":{"label":"Value","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Tc-99m","Tc-99"],"series":[{"name":"Z","values":[43,43]},{"name":"A","values":[99,99]}]},"hl":"relaxes to technetium-99","graph":{"bars":[["Tc-99m","A"],["Tc-99","A"]]}},{"text":"Answer: neither Z nor A changes; only the nucleus drops from an excited state to a lower energy."}],"trap":"\"Z decreases by 2 and A by 4\" is alpha decay and \"Z increases by 1\" is beta-minus decay; gamma emission changes energy only."},teach:[{"text":"**Start and end of the change:** an excited nucleus calms down by giving off a gamma ray.","marks":[{"t":"Technetium-99m","note":"start: excited nucleus"},{"t":"technetium-99","note":"end: calm nucleus"},{"t":"140-keV gamma ray","note":"what is given off"}]},{"text":"**Define the two numbers.** Z = number of protons (decides the element). A = protons + neutrons.","marks":[{"t":"atomic number Z","note":"protons"},{"t":"mass number A","note":"protons + neutrons"}]},{"text":"**\"m\" = metastable:** the same protons and neutrons, just holding **extra energy**, like a stretched spring.","marks":[{"t":"excited nuclear state","note":"extra energy, same particles"}]},{"text":"**A gamma ray is light** (a high-energy photon): no charge, no protons, no neutrons.","marks":[{"t":"gamma ray","note":"light: no mass, no charge"}]},{"text":"**Each emission changes Z and A in its own way.** Gamma changes neither.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Change in Z and A for each type of emission","x":{"label":"Emission"},"y":{"label":"Change","min":-4,"max":2,"step":1},"categories":["alpha (⁴He)","beta-minus (e⁻)","positron (e⁺)","gamma (γ)"],"series":[{"name":"ΔZ","values":[-2,1,-1,0]},{"name":"ΔA","values":[-4,0,0,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["gamma (γ)","ΔZ"],["gamma (γ)","ΔA"]]}},{"text":"**Check the names:** both are technetium (same Z = 43) and both are 99 (same A).","marks":[{"t":"technetium-99","note":"Z 43, A 99: unchanged"}],"calc":["Tc-99m: Z = **43**, A = **99**","Tc-99: Z = **43**, A = **99**"]},{"text":"**Does the energy carry off a nucleon?** No: its mass equivalent is tiny.","marks":[{"t":"140-keV","note":"energy lost, not mass"}],"calc":["E = 140 keV = **2.2 × 10⁻¹⁴ J**","m = E/c² = **2.5 × 10⁻³¹ kg**","1 nucleon ≈ 1.7 × 10⁻²⁷ kg → **none lost**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** Z −2, A −4 is alpha; Z +1 is beta-minus; A −1 would be losing a neutron.","marks":[{"t":"this emission","note":"gamma: no Z or A change"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** α: Z −2, A −4. β⁻: Z +1. β⁺: Z −1. γ: nothing changes; only energy leaves."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"A maleimide dye reacts only with thiol (–SH) groups on proteins at pH 7. Which side chain shown would it label?",idStem:"A labeling reagent reacts with amines and thiols (–SH). Which two residues can it label?",choiceFigs:["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","serine-sc","lysine-sc"],choices:["Cysteine","Methionine","Serine","Lysine"],ans:0,exp:"A **thiol is an S bonded to H**. Cysteine's side chain, –CH₂–SH, is the only one with it. Methionine's S is bonded to two carbons (a thioether, no H); serine has –OH, the oxygen analog; lysine has –NH₂.",src:"pe6-cp:34",how:{"ask":"Which side chain ends in a sulfur that carries a hydrogen?","know":"• **Thiol:** R–S–H. **Thioether (sulfide):** R–S–R, no H on S. **Alcohol:** R–O–H. **Primary amine:** R–NH₂.\n• Amino acids with S: **cysteine (–CH₂SH, a thiol)** and **methionine (–CH₂CH₂SCH₃, a thioether)**.\n• Maleimides add to the S–H of cysteine; this is the standard way to attach dyes to cysteines.","steps":[{"text":"The reagent needs an S–H: a sulfur bonded to hydrogen.","hl":"thiol (–SH) groups"},{"text":"Find the atom at the end of each side chain and what it is bonded to.","calc":["Cys: –CH₂–**S–H**","Met: –CH₂CH₂–**S–CH₃**, no H on S","Ser: –CH₂–O–H","Lys: –(CH₂)₄–NH₂"]},{"text":"Methionine has sulfur, but it is a thioether with no H.","fig":"methionine-sc","strike":["Methionine"]},{"text":"Serine has an O–H and lysine an N–H₂, not S–H.","strike":["Serine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine. Rule: S with an H = thiol = cysteine; S between two carbons = methionine.","fig":"cysteine-sc"}],"trap":"Methionine also contains sulfur, so it is tempting. Its S is bonded to two carbons and has no H, so it is not a thiol."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read them.** Each is an amino acid; only the highlighted side chain differs. Letters: S sulfur, O oxygen, N nitrogen.","fig":"t6-cys-read"},{"text":"**A thiol is an S bonded to an H.** It is the sulfur twin of O–H (S sits under O in the periodic table).","fig":"t6-cys-thiol"},{"text":"**The dye's C=C is made electron-poor by the two C=O groups beside it.**","fig":"t6-maleimide"},{"text":"**The S gives up its H and bonds to that carbon.** The dye is now attached. No S–H, no reaction.","fig":"t6-cys-dye"},{"text":"**Methionine has S, but between two carbons, with no H.** That is a thioether, not a thiol.","fig":"t6-met-thioether"},{"text":"**Serine ends in O–H: oxygen, not sulfur.**","fig":"t6-ser-not-s"},{"text":"**Lysine ends in NH₂, with no sulfur at all.**","fig":"t6-lys-not-s"},{"text":"**Rule:** S with H = thiol = cysteine; S between two carbons = methionine. In each drawing, find the end of the side chain and ask: is it S, and does it hold an H?","fig":["t6-cys-thiol","t6-met-thioether"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"Normal saline is 0.15 M NaCl. Approximately how many grams of NaCl are needed to prepare 500 mL of normal saline? (Molar mass of NaCl = 58.5 g/mol)",idStem:"Physiological saline is 0.15 M NaCl (58.5 g/mol). What mass of NaCl is in 1.0 L?",choices:["0.08 g","2.2 g","4.4 g","8.8 g"],ans:2,exp:"moles = 0.15 mol/L × **0.500 L** = 0.075 mol; mass = 0.075 × 58.5 ≈ **4.4 g**.",src:"pe6-cp:35",how:{"ask":"Convert the volume to liters, find moles, then convert moles to grams.","know":"**moles = M × V (in L)**; **mass = moles × molar mass**.","steps":[{"text":"Collect the data and convert volume to liters.","hl":"0.15 M NaCl","calc":["V = 500 mL = **0.500 L**"]},{"text":"Moles from molarity, then grams from molar mass.","hl":"500 mL of normal saline","calc":["n = 0.15 mol/L × 0.500 L = **0.075 mol**","m = 0.075 mol × 58.5 g/mol","m = **4.4 g**"]},{"text":"8.8 g is the mass for a full liter; 0.08 is moles, not grams; 2.2 g halves again.","strike":["0.08 g","2.2 g","8.8 g"]},{"text":"Answer: about 4.4 g of NaCl."}],"trap":"8.8 g is the mass for a full liter. Did you convert 500 mL to 0.5 L? 0.08 g comes from dividing instead of multiplying."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.15 M","note":"concentration"},{"t":"500 mL","note":"volume to make"},{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"mass of 1 mole"}]},{"text":"**Molarity (M)** = moles of solute in **1 liter** of solution. A mole is just a counting unit for particles, like a dozen.","marks":[{"t":"0.15 M","note":"0.15 mol in every 1 L"}],"calc":["0.15 M = **0.15 mol per L**"]},{"text":"**Molar mass** = how many grams **one mole** weighs. It turns moles into grams.","marks":[{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"1 mol NaCl = 58.5 g"}]},{"text":"Molarity is per **liter**, but the volume is in **mL**. Convert first.","marks":[{"t":"500 mL","note":"= 0.500 L"}],"calc":["1 L = 1000 mL","500 mL ÷ 1000 = **0.500 L**"]},{"text":"**Moles = molarity × liters.**","marks":[{"t":"0.15 M","note":"× this"},{"t":"500 mL","note":"0.500 L"}],"calc":["n = M × V","n = 0.15 mol/L × 0.500 L","n = **0.075 mol**"]},{"text":"**Grams = moles × molar mass.**","marks":[{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"× this"},{"t":"grams of NaCl","note":"what we want"}],"calc":["m = 0.075 mol × 58.5 g/mol","m = **4.39 g ≈ 4.4 g**"]},{"text":"**Half a liter needs half the salt** of a full liter. 8.8 g is the full-liter amount; 0.075 (≈ 0.08) is moles, not grams.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Grams of NaCl for 0.15 M","x":{"label":"Volume made"},"y":{"label":"NaCl (g)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["1.000 L","0.500 L"],"series":[{"name":"NaCl","values":[8.8,4.4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["0.500 L","NaCl"]]},"marks":[{"t":"500 mL","note":"only half a liter"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** grams from a solution = M × V (in liters) × molar mass. Always turn mL into L first."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"A researcher loads 4.0 mL of a 20 mg/mL solution of a protein with a molecular weight of 80 kDa onto a column. How many moles of protein were loaded?",idStem:"4.0 mL of a 20 mg/mL solution of an 80 kDa protein is used. How many moles of protein is that?",choices:["2 mmol","300 μmol","1 μmol","40 nmol"],ans:2,exp:"Mass = 4.0 mL × 20 mg/mL = 80 mg. **80 kDa = 80,000 g/mol**, so 0.080 g ÷ 80,000 = 1 × 10⁻⁶ mol = **1 μmol**.",src:"pe6-cp:36",how:{"ask":"Turn volume × concentration into grams, then into moles.","know":"mass = volume × concentration (mg/mL). **Daltons = g/mol**, so 80 kDa = 80,000 g/mol. moles = grams ÷ (g/mol).","steps":[{"text":"Mass loaded = volume × concentration.","hl":"4.0 mL of a 20 mg/mL solution","calc":["m = 4.0 mL × 20 mg/mL = **80 mg** = **0.080 g**"]},{"text":"Translate the molecular weight: 1 Da = 1 g/mol.","hl":"80 kDa","calc":["80 kDa = **80,000 g/mol**"]},{"text":"Divide grams by g/mol.","calc":["n = 0.080 g ÷ 80,000 g/mol","n = **1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol**","= **1 μmol**"]},{"text":"The other values come from unit slips, such as treating 80 kDa as 80 g/mol or mg as g.","strike":["2 mmol","300 μmol","40 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 μmol of protein."}],"trap":"Forgetting mg → g gives 1 × 10⁻³ (1 mmol). Convert to grams before dividing by g/mol."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 mL","note":"volume loaded"},{"t":"20 mg/mL","note":"mg of protein per mL"},{"t":"80 kDa","note":"molecular weight"}]},{"text":"**A concentration in mg/mL** tells you how much protein sits in each milliliter.","marks":[{"t":"20 mg/mL","note":"each mL holds 20 mg"}]},{"text":"**Mass loaded = volume × concentration.** The mL cancels, leaving mg.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 mL","note":"× 20 mg/mL"},{"t":"20 mg/mL","note":"× this"}],"calc":["m = 4.0 mL × 20 mg/mL = **80 mg**","80 mg ÷ 1000 = **0.080 g**"]},{"text":"**Molecular weight** = mass of one mole. **1 dalton (Da) = 1 g/mol**, and **k = 1000**.","marks":[{"t":"80 kDa","note":"= 80,000 g/mol"}],"calc":["1 Da = 1 g/mol","80 kDa = 80 × 1000 = **80,000 g/mol**"]},{"text":"**Moles = grams ÷ (grams per mole).**","marks":[{"t":"How many moles","note":"grams ÷ g/mol"},{"t":"80 kDa","note":"÷ 80,000 g/mol"}],"calc":["n = 0.080 g ÷ 80,000 g/mol","n = 8 × 10⁻² ÷ 8 × 10⁴","n = **1 × 10⁻⁶ mol**"]},{"text":"**Read the prefix:** 10⁻⁶ mol is a **micromole** (μmol).","marks":[{"t":"moles of protein","note":"answer: 1 μmol"}],"calc":["milli (m) = 10⁻³","micro (μ) = **10⁻⁶**","nano (n) = 10⁻⁹","1 × 10⁻⁶ mol = **1 μmol**"]},{"text":"**Trap:** reading 80 kDa as 80 g/mol gives 1000× too many moles; forgetting mg → g does the same. Proteins are huge, so moles are tiny.","marks":[{"t":"80 kDa","note":"NOT 80 g/mol"},{"t":"20 mg/mL","note":"mg, not g"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass = volume × concentration; kDa × 1000 = g/mol; moles = grams ÷ g/mol. Keep every unit in grams."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A cysteine-reactive fluorescent probe is added to an enzyme in aqueous buffer. After labeling, the enzyme retains full catalytic activity. The labeled residue is most likely:",idStem:"A protein is labeled in water and keeps its full function. Where did the label most likely attach?",choices:["in the active site, beside the catalytic residues","buried in the hydrophobic core of the protein","on the surface, away from the active site","at the interface between two subunits"],ans:2,exp:"A water-soluble reagent reaches **surface residues** first. Labeling the active site would destroy its function, and buried residues are out of reach.",src:"pe6-cp:37",how:{"ask":"If labeling didn't hurt function, where did the label NOT go, and where could it reach?","know":"Proteins fold with hydrophobic residues **buried** inside and polar/charged residues on the **surface**. The **active site** is needed for function. A reagent in water reaches exposed residues first.","steps":[{"text":"Two clues: the probe is in water, and the enzyme still works fully afterward.","hl":"retains full catalytic activity"},{"text":"The probe attaches to a cysteine thiol; a bulky dye there would block anything nearby.","fig":"cysteine-sc","hl":"cysteine-reactive fluorescent probe"},{"text":"Full activity rules out a label beside the catalytic residues.","strike":["in the active site, beside the catalytic residues"]},{"text":"A water-soluble probe reaches exposed residues first; a buried core or a subunit interface is hidden from solvent.","hl":"aqueous buffer","strike":["buried in the hydrophobic core of the protein","at the interface between two subunits"]},{"text":"Answer: on the surface, away from the active site, where the probe can reach it without disturbing catalysis."}],"trap":"Buried hydrophobic residues are out of the reagent's reach unless the protein unfolds."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out the three clues** in the question.","marks":[{"t":"cysteine-reactive fluorescent probe","note":"glowing tag that grabs Cys"},{"t":"aqueous buffer","note":"in water"},{"t":"retains full catalytic activity","note":"still works 100%"}]},{"text":"**An enzyme** is a protein that speeds up a reaction. It does that job in one small pocket: the **active site**.","marks":[{"t":"an enzyme","note":"protein that speeds reaction"},{"t":"catalytic activity","note":"job done in active site"}]},{"text":"**A residue** = one amino acid in the protein chain. This probe sticks only to **cysteine**, through its –SH group.","marks":[{"t":"cysteine-reactive","note":"sticks to Cys –SH"},{"t":"labeled residue","note":"the Cys that got tagged"}]},{"text":"**Proteins fold like a ball:** oily (hydrophobic) parts hide in the core; water-loving parts sit on the surface; some patches are hidden where two subunits touch.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"How exposed to water each location is (typical)","x":{"label":"Location in the protein"},"y":{"label":"Water exposure (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Surface","Subunit interface","Hydrophobic core"],"series":[{"name":"Exposure","values":[80,10,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Surface","Exposure"]]}},{"text":"**The probe floats in water**, so it can only touch what water touches: the **surface**. The core and the subunit interface are hidden.","marks":[{"t":"aqueous buffer","note":"reaches exposed spots only"}]},{"text":"**A bulky dye in the active site would jam it.** The enzyme still works fully, so the tag is **not** near the active site.","marks":[{"t":"retains full catalytic activity","note":"active site untouched"}]},{"text":"**Put both clues together:** reachable from water **and** away from the working pocket = on the surface, away from the active site.","marks":[{"t":"aqueous buffer","note":"→ surface"},{"t":"retains full catalytic activity","note":"→ not active site"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reagent in water labels exposed surface residues; if activity is unchanged, the label is away from the active site."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A stock solution of CoCl₂ has a concentration of 1.0 mM. How many moles of Co²⁺ ions are present in a 5.0 mL aliquot?",idStem:"How many moles of CoCl₂ are in 5.0 mL of a 1.0 mM solution?",choices:["5 μmol","10 μmol","50 μmol","5 mmol"],ans:0,exp:"n = 1.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L × 5.0 × 10⁻³ L = **5 × 10⁻⁶ mol = 5 μmol**. One Co²⁺ per CoCl₂.",src:"pe6-cp:38",how:{"ask":"Moles from concentration and volume, with unit prefixes.","know":"**n = M × V**, where M is in mol/L and V is in L. milli (m) = 10⁻³; micro (μ) = 10⁻⁶.","steps":[{"text":"Convert both quantities to SI units.","hl":"1.0 mM","calc":["1.0 mM = **1.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L**","5.0 mL = **5.0 × 10⁻³ L**"]},{"text":"Multiply concentration by volume.","hl":"5.0 mL aliquot","calc":["n = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 5.0 × 10⁻³","n = **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol** = **5 μmol**"]},{"text":"Each CoCl₂ gives one Co²⁺, so the ion count equals the salt count; doubling applies only to Cl⁻.","strike":["10 μmol"]},{"text":"50 μmol and 5 mmol come from slipping a factor of 10 or 1000 in the units.","strike":["50 μmol","5 mmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 5 μmol of Co²⁺."}],"trap":"10 μmol is the number of Cl⁻ ions (2 per formula unit). Read which ion the question asks about. 5 mmol comes from skipping the mL → L conversion."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 mM","note":"concentration"},{"t":"5.0 mL aliquot","note":"small portion taken"},{"t":"Co²⁺ ions","note":"what we count"}]},{"text":"**mM = millimolar.** Milli means 10⁻³, so 1.0 mM = 0.001 mol in each liter.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 mM","note":"= 1.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L"}],"calc":["milli (m) = 10⁻³","1.0 mM = **1.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L**"]},{"text":"**Molarity is per liter**, so turn mL into L.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 mL","note":"= 5.0 × 10⁻³ L"}],"calc":["5.0 mL ÷ 1000 = **5.0 × 10⁻³ L**"]},{"text":"**Moles = molarity × liters.**","marks":[{"t":"1.0 mM","note":"10⁻³ mol/L ×"},{"t":"5.0 mL","note":"× 5.0 × 10⁻³ L"}],"calc":["n = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 5.0 × 10⁻³","add exponents: −3 + −3 = −6","n = **5.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol** of CoCl₂"]},{"text":"**How many Co²⁺ per CoCl₂?** The salt splits into **one** Co²⁺ and **two** Cl⁻. So Co²⁺ moles = CoCl₂ moles.","marks":[{"t":"CoCl₂","note":"→ 1 Co²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Ions released by one CoCl₂","x":{"label":"Ion"},"y":{"label":"Number of ions","min":0,"max":3,"step":1},"categories":["Co²⁺","Cl⁻"],"series":[{"name":"Ions","values":[1,2]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Co²⁺","Ions"]]}},{"text":"**Name the size:** 10⁻⁶ mol is a **micromole** (μmol).","marks":[{"t":"Co²⁺ ions","note":"5 μmol"}],"calc":["micro (μ) = 10⁻⁶","5.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol = **5 μmol**"]},{"text":"**Traps:** doubling counts Cl⁻, not Co²⁺. 50 μmol or 5 mmol come from slipping a factor of 10 or 1000 in the units.","marks":[{"t":"Co²⁺ ions","note":"×1, not ×2 (that's Cl⁻)"},{"t":"5.0 mL","note":"mL, not L"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles = M × V with M in mol/L and V in L; then multiply by how many of that ion one formula unit gives."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"A lithium-ion cell has a standard cell potential of 4.0 V. If the standard reduction potential of the cathode half-reaction is +1.0 V, what is the standard reduction potential of the anode half-reaction?",idStem:"A battery has E°cell = 4.0 V, and its cathode has E°red = +1.0 V. What is the anode's reduction potential?",choices:["+4.0 V","+3.0 V","−1.0 V","−3.0 V"],ans:3,exp:"**E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** → 4.0 = 1.0 − E°anode → **E°anode = −3.0 V**.",src:"pe6-cp:41",how:{"ask":"Solve E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode for the anode.","know":"**E°cell = E°red(cathode) − E°red(anode)**, where both values are REDUCTION potentials from the table. Don't flip any signs yourself.","steps":[{"text":"Write the rule: E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, both as reduction potentials.","hl":"standard cell potential of 4.0 V"},{"text":"Substitute and solve.","hl":"+1.0 V","calc":["4.0 = 1.0 − E°anode","E°anode = 1.0 − 4.0","E°anode = **−3.0 V**"]},{"text":"Picture it: the anode sits 4.0 V below the cathode.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials in the cell","x":{"label":"Half-cell"},"y":{"label":"E° (V)","min":-4,"max":2,"step":1},"categories":["Cathode","Anode"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[1,-3]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Anode","E°"]]}},{"text":"+4.0 V is the cell value; +3.0 V adds instead of subtracting; −1.0 V just flips the cathode sign.","strike":["+4.0 V","+3.0 V","−1.0 V"]},{"text":"Answer: −3.0 V; a very negative anode potential, as for Li⁺/Li, is what gives lithium cells their high voltage."}],"trap":"+3.0 V comes from simply subtracting 4.0 − 1.0. Write out the formula and solve for E°anode."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 V","note":"whole-cell voltage"},{"t":"+1.0 V","note":"cathode's value"},{"t":"anode half-reaction","note":"unknown"}]},{"text":"**A cell has two electrodes.** At the **cathode** a substance gains electrons (reduction); at the **anode** one loses electrons (oxidation).","marks":[{"t":"cathode half-reaction","note":"gains e⁻: reduction"},{"t":"anode half-reaction","note":"loses e⁻: oxidation"}]},{"text":"**Reduction potential** = how strongly a half-reaction pulls electrons in. Higher = pulls harder. Both electrodes are listed as reduction potentials.","marks":[{"t":"standard reduction potential","note":"pull for electrons"}]},{"text":"**Cell voltage = the gap** between the two: cathode minus anode.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 V","note":"E°cell"},{"t":"+1.0 V","note":"E°cathode"}],"calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode"]},{"text":"**Plug in and solve** for the anode.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 V","note":"= 4.0"},{"t":"+1.0 V","note":"= 1.0"}],"calc":["4.0 = 1.0 − E°anode","E°anode = 1.0 − 4.0","E°anode = **−3.0 V**"]},{"text":"**Picture it:** the anode sits 4.0 V **below** the cathode. That big gap is why lithium cells give high voltage.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Standard reduction potentials in the cell","x":{"label":"Half-cell"},"y":{"label":"E° (V)","min":-4,"max":2,"step":1},"categories":["Cathode","Anode"],"series":[{"name":"E°","values":[1,-3]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Anode","E°"]]}},{"text":"**Traps:** +4.0 V is the whole cell, +3.0 V comes from adding instead of subtracting, −1.0 V just flips the cathode's sign.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 V","note":"cell, not anode"},{"t":"+1.0 V","note":"don't just flip this"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, using reduction potentials for both. Never flip a sign yourself."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"organic_chem",aamc:"5D",q:"Glutamate side chains hold a Zn²⁺ ion in a protein at pH 7, and the metal is released when the pH is lowered to 3.0. Which structure shows free glutamate as it predominantly exists at pH 3.0? (pKa values: α-COOH 2.2, side-chain COOH 4.3, α-NH₃⁺ 9.7)",idStem:"A protein binds Co²⁺ tightly through glutamate side chains. Which group does the binding?",choiceFigs:["glutamate-net-minus1","glutamate-net0","glutamate-net-plus1","glutamate-net-minus2"],choices:["Both carboxylates ionized","Zwitterion with neutral side chain","Fully protonated cation","Neutral amine dianion"],ans:1,exp:"Compare pH 3.0 with each pKa. **α-COOH (2.2): pH is above, so COO⁻. Side-chain COOH (4.3): pH is below, so COOH. α-NH₃⁺ (9.7): pH is below, so NH₃⁺.** Net charge 0, and the neutral side-chain COOH no longer binds Zn²⁺.",src:"pe6-cp:42",how:{"ask":"For each ionizable group, is pH 3.0 above or below its pKa, and which form does that give?","know":"• Rule: **pH below pKa → the group keeps its H⁺** (protonated form); pH above pKa → it has lost the H⁺.\n• Carboxyl: protonated = –COOH (neutral), deprotonated = –COO⁻. Amino: protonated = –NH₃⁺, deprotonated = –NH₂ (neutral).\n• On structure choices, check each group against its own pKa, one at a time, then add up the charges.\n• Metal ions bind the negative –COO⁻; protonating it to –COOH weakens binding.","steps":[{"text":"Use the pKa list: compare 3.0 with each one.","hl":"pKa values: α-COOH 2.2, side-chain COOH 4.3, α-NH₃⁺ 9.7","calc":["α-COOH: 3.0 > 2.2 → **COO⁻**","side-chain COOH: 3.0 < 4.3 → **COOH**","α-NH₃⁺: 3.0 < 9.7 → **NH₃⁺**","net charge: −1 + 0 + 1 = **0**"]},{"text":"The matching drawing has one COO⁻, one COOH on the side chain, and NH₃⁺.","fig":"glutamate-net0"},{"text":"Two COO⁻ groups plus NH₃⁺ is the pH 7 form; both COOH protonated needs pH below 2.2.","strike":["Both carboxylates ionized","Fully protonated cation"]},{"text":"A neutral NH₂ needs pH above 9.7.","strike":["Neutral amine dianion"]},{"text":"Answer: the zwitterion with a neutral side chain. Rule: pH below pKa → protonated; check each group separately."}],"trap":"Both carboxylates ionized is glutamate at pH 7, the form that holds Zn²⁺. At pH 3.0 the side chain, pKa 4.3, has picked up its proton."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** COOH = carboxylic acid group; NH₃⁺ = nitrogen with three H and a + charge. Glutamate has three groups that can gain or lose H⁺.","fig":"t6-glu-read"},{"text":"**H⁺ is a hydrogen atom without its electron.** COOH ⇄ COO⁻ + H⁺; NH₃⁺ ⇄ NH₂ + H⁺. Left: all H⁺ on. Right: all off.","fig":["t6-glu-all-on","t6-glu-all-off"]},{"text":"**Low pH = lots of H⁺. Each group has its own pKa (tipping point).** pH below pKa → H⁺ stays on; above → it comes off.","fig":"t6-glu-pkas"},{"text":"**α-COOH: pH 3.0 is above 2.2, so its H⁺ comes off.** Charge −1.","fig":"t6-glu-alpha-off"},{"text":"**Side-chain COOH: pH 3.0 is below 4.3, so its H⁺ stays.** Charge 0.","fig":"t6-glu-side-on"},{"text":"**NH₃⁺: pH 3.0 is far below 9.7, so its H⁺ stays.** Charge +1.","fig":"t6-glu-amine-on"},{"text":"**Add up: −1 + 0 + 1 = net charge 0.** This is the form to look for.","fig":"t6-glu-net0"},{"text":"**Zn²⁺ is held by the negative side-chain COO⁻ (pH 7, left).** At pH 3 that group is neutral COOH (right), so the metal lets go.","fig":["t6-glu-ph7-zn","t6-glu-ph3-zn"]},{"text":"**Rule:** compare pH with each group's own pKa: below → H on, above → H off; then add charges. In each drawing, ask of all three groups: is the H on or off?","fig":"t6-glu-net0"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4C",q:"In a lithium-ion battery, LiPF₆ dissolved in an organic carbonate solvent fills the space between the graphite anode and the metal-oxide cathode. What is the function of the LiPF₆ solution?",idStem:"What does a dissolved LiPF₆ salt do inside a lithium battery?",choices:["It is oxidized at the anode","It is reduced at the cathode","It conducts Li⁺ ions between the electrodes","It conducts electrons between the electrodes"],ans:2,exp:"A dissolved salt = ions in solution = **electrolyte**. It carries **Li⁺ ions** between the electrodes. Electrons travel through the external circuit, not through the electrolyte.",src:"pe6-cp:43",how:{"ask":"Which battery part is a dissolved salt?","know":"Battery parts: **anode** (oxidation happens), **cathode** (reduction happens), **electrolyte** (an ion-conducting solution between them). A dissolved salt splits into ions.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: a salt dissolved in a solvent between the electrodes is an electrolyte, a medium of mobile ions.","hl":"LiPF₆ dissolved in an organic carbonate solvent"},{"text":"Electrons cannot travel through an ionic solution; they go through the external wire.","strike":["It conducts electrons between the electrodes"]},{"text":"Oxidation and reduction happen to the electrode materials, Li in graphite and the metal oxide, not to the salt.","hl":"graphite anode and the metal-oxide cathode","strike":["It is oxidized at the anode","It is reduced at the cathode"]},{"text":"Charge balance: every electron through the wire is matched by one Li⁺ through the electrolyte.","calc":["1 mol e⁻ = 96,485 C through the wire","→ **1 mol Li⁺** through the electrolyte","e.g. 1.0 A for 1 h = 3,600 C → **0.037 mol Li⁺**"]},{"text":"Answer: it conducts Li⁺ ions between the electrodes, completing the circuit inside the cell."}],"trap":"\"Conducts electrons\" is the trap. Electrons flow through the wire, and ions flow through the electrolyte."},teach:[{"text":"First, **name the parts** of the battery the question describes.","marks":[{"t":"LiPF₆ dissolved in an organic carbonate solvent","note":"salt in a liquid"},{"t":"graphite anode","note":"electrode 1"},{"t":"metal-oxide cathode","note":"electrode 2"}]},{"text":"**Electrodes** are where the chemistry happens. Anode: something **loses** electrons (oxidation). Cathode: something **gains** them (reduction).","marks":[{"t":"graphite anode","note":"oxidation: gives e⁻"},{"t":"metal-oxide cathode","note":"reduction: takes e⁻"}]},{"text":"**A salt dissolved in a liquid splits into ions** (charged particles) that can swim around.","marks":[{"t":"LiPF₆","note":"→ Li⁺ + PF₆⁻"}],"calc":["LiPF₆ → **Li⁺** + PF₆⁻"]},{"text":"**A liquid of mobile ions between the electrodes is an electrolyte.** Its job: carry ions from one electrode to the other.","marks":[{"t":"fills the space between","note":"= the electrolyte"},{"t":"the LiPF₆ solution","note":"ion highway"}]},{"text":"**Two paths in every battery:** electrons run through the outside **wire**; ions (here **Li⁺**) move through the **electrolyte**.","calc":["electrons: anode → **wire** → cathode","Li⁺ ions: anode → **electrolyte** → cathode"],"marks":[{"t":"graphite anode","note":"Li⁺ leaves here"},{"t":"metal-oxide cathode","note":"Li⁺ arrives here"}]},{"text":"**Electrons cannot travel through an ionic liquid**, so the solution does not conduct electrons.","marks":[{"t":"organic carbonate solvent","note":"blocks electrons"}]},{"text":"**The salt is not oxidized or reduced.** The electrode materials are: Li in the graphite gives up e⁻; the metal oxide takes them.","marks":[{"t":"graphite anode","note":"oxidized here"},{"t":"metal-oxide cathode","note":"reduced here"}]},{"text":"**Charge balance:** each electron through the wire is matched by one Li⁺ through the electrolyte, closing the circuit.","calc":["1 e⁻ through the wire ↔ **1 Li⁺** through the electrolyte"],"marks":[{"t":"the LiPF₆ solution","note":"carries Li⁺"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a dissolved salt between electrodes is the electrolyte; it carries ions, never electrons, and is not itself oxidized or reduced."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"Each lipid shown is mixed alone with water. Which one would assemble into a bilayer?",idStem:"Which lipid is NOT a membrane lipid?",choiceFigs:["triacylglycerol","stearic-acid","phosphatidylcholine","cholesterol"],choices:["Triacylglycerol","Fatty acid","Phosphatidylcholine","Cholesterol"],ans:2,exp:"A bilayer forms from molecules with a **polar head and two hydrophobic tails**, a roughly cylindrical shape. Phosphatidylcholine has a charged phosphocholine head and two acyl chains. Triacylglycerol has no polar head and forms oil droplets; a one-tailed fatty acid is cone-shaped and forms micelles; cholesterol's small OH cannot shield its rigid rings on its own.",src:"pe6-cp:44",how:{"ask":"Which molecule has a large polar head attached to two hydrophobic tails?","know":"• **Amphipathic:** part polar (faces water), part nonpolar (hides from water).\n• Shape decides the assembly: **one tail + head = cone → micelle**; **two tails + head = cylinder → bilayer**; no polar head = oil droplet.\n• In a drawing, look for charged or many-O groups (phosphate, choline N⁺) at one end and long CH₂ chains at the other.\n• Cholesterol sits inside membranes made by phospholipids but cannot form one by itself.","steps":[{"text":"A bilayer needs a molecule shaped like a cylinder: polar head plus two tails.","hl":"assemble into a bilayer"},{"text":"Phosphatidylcholine: a charged phosphate–choline head on glycerol, with two long acyl chains.","fig":"phosphatidylcholine","calc":["head: phosphate⁻ + choline N⁺ → **polar**","tails: **2** acyl chains → cylinder → bilayer"]},{"text":"Triacylglycerol has three tails and only ester oxygens, no polar head, so it clumps into oil droplets.","fig":"triacylglycerol","strike":["Triacylglycerol"]},{"text":"The fatty acid has one tail, a cone, so it forms micelles. Cholesterol's lone OH is too small to cover its fused rings.","strike":["Fatty acid","Cholesterol"]},{"text":"Answer: phosphatidylcholine. Rule: polar head + two tails = bilayer; one tail = micelle; no head = droplet."}],"trap":"The fatty acid is amphipathic too, with a polar COOH head and a long tail, but with only one tail it packs as a cone and forms micelles, not bilayers."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Long zigzags are chains of carbon with hidden H. Letters: O oxygen, P phosphorus, N nitrogen; + and − are charges.","fig":"t6-pc-read"},{"text":"**Water clings to charged and O/N groups, and shuns long C–H chains (hydrophobic = water-fearing).** Molecules hide their chains from water.","fig":"t6-pc-water"},{"text":"**Water-loving head + water-fearing tails = amphipathic.** Phosphatidylcholine has one big head and two tails.","fig":"t6-pc-parts"},{"text":"**A bilayer is two sheets tail-to-tail, heads facing water on both sides.** Flat sheets need cylinder-shaped molecules: head plus two tails.","fig":"t6-pc-cylinder"},{"text":"**A fatty acid has one tail and a small head: a cone.** Cones pack into a ball (micelle), not a sheet.","fig":"t6-fa-cone"},{"text":"**Triacylglycerol: three tails joined by ester O atoms, no charged head.** It clumps into oil droplets.","fig":"t6-tag-no-head"},{"text":"**Cholesterol: rigid fused rings with only a tiny OH head.** It can't form a sheet alone; it slips between phospholipids.","fig":"t6-chol-tiny"},{"text":"**Rule:** head + two tails = bilayer; head + one tail = micelle; no head = droplet. In each drawing ask: is there a charged or polar head, and how many tails?","fig":"t6-pc-cylinder"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"ATP, shown, is synthesized with every nitrogen atom replaced by ¹⁵N. Compared with unlabeled ATP, each labeled molecule is heavier by approximately:",idStem:"How many carbon atoms does deoxyadenylate (dAMP) contain?",fig:"atp",choices:["4 Da","5 Da","10 Da","15 Da"],ans:1,exp:"¹⁵N has one more neutron than ¹⁴N, so each N adds about 1 Da. All of ATP's nitrogens are in adenine: **four ring N atoms plus the NH₂ = 5 N → +5 Da**. Ribose and the phosphates contain no N.",src:"pe6-cp:45",how:{"ask":"How many nitrogen atoms does ATP contain, and how much does each one gain?","know":"• Isotopes differ only in neutrons: ¹⁵N − ¹⁴N = **1 neutron ≈ 1 Da**. So mass gain = number of atoms replaced × 1 Da (the same idea for ¹³C vs ¹²C, or ²H vs ¹H).\n• ATP = adenine (C₅H₅N₅ base: **5 N**) + ribose (C, H, O) + three phosphates (P, O). Formula C₁₀H₁₆N₅O₁₃P₃.\n• On a drawing, count each N, including the NH₂ outside the rings.","steps":[{"text":"Only nitrogen is labeled, and each ¹⁵N is one neutron heavier.","hl":"every nitrogen atom replaced by ¹⁵N","calc":["¹⁵N − ¹⁴N = **1 Da** per N"]},{"text":"Find every N in the drawing: all are in adenine, four in the rings and one in the NH₂.","fig":"atp-nitrogens","calc":["ring N: **4**","amino NH₂: **1**","total N = **5** → +5 Da"]},{"text":"Missing the NH₂ gives 4; counting the 10 carbons gives 10; 15 is the mass number, not the gain.","strike":["4 Da","10 Da","15 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 5 Da. Rule: mass gain = number of labeled atoms × 1 Da per extra neutron."}],"trap":"4 Da comes from counting only the ring nitrogens and missing the NH₂ on the outside of adenine. 10 Da counts carbons, which were not labeled."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read it.** Corners are carbons with hidden H. Letters: N nitrogen, O oxygen, P phosphorus. ATP has three parts.","fig":"t6-atp-read"},{"text":"**Isotopes differ only in neutrons, each ≈ 1 Da (dalton, the mass unit).** ¹⁴N has 7 neutrons, ¹⁵N has 8, so each N swapped adds 1 Da.","fig":"t6-atp-isotope"},{"text":"**So count every N.** Adenine's two rings hold four.","fig":"t6-atp-ring-n"},{"text":"**The NH₂ outside the rings is one more nitrogen.** Easy to miss.","fig":"t6-atp-nh2"},{"text":"**The sugar and phosphates hold only C, H, O and P.** They add nothing.","fig":"t6-atp-no-n"},{"text":"**Total: 5 nitrogens × 1 Da = 5 Da heavier.**","fig":"t6-atp-total"},{"text":"**Rule:** mass gain = number of labeled atoms × extra neutrons each. Traps: missing the NH₂, counting carbons, using 15 itself. Check which choice equals your N count.","fig":"t6-atp-total"}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biology",aamc:"4B",q:"The wall of the left ventricle is thicker than that of the right ventricle. This difference is best explained by the fact that the left ventricle:",idStem:"Why is the left ventricle's wall thicker than the right's?",choices:["ejects a larger volume of blood with each beat","receives blood directly from the venae cavae","must generate higher pressure to overcome systemic resistance","pumps deoxygenated blood"],ans:2,exp:"Both ventricles eject the **same stroke volume**. The left ventricle pushes it into the high-resistance **systemic** circuit (~120 mmHg vs ~25 mmHg), which needs more pressure and therefore more muscle.",src:"pe6-cp:46",how:{"ask":"Why does the left ventricle need more muscle?","know":"Right ventricle → lungs (short, low-resistance **pulmonary** circuit, ~25 mmHg). Left ventricle → whole body (long, high-resistance **systemic** circuit, ~120 mmHg). More pressure work → thicker muscle.","steps":[{"text":"Rule: both ventricles eject the same stroke volume each beat, or blood would pile up in one circuit.","strike":["ejects a larger volume of blood with each beat"]},{"text":"The venae cavae feed the right atrium, and the left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood.","strike":["receives blood directly from the venae cavae","pumps deoxygenated blood"]},{"text":"Compare the pressures each ventricle must reach.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Peak systolic pressure","x":{"label":"Ventricle"},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":0,"max":140,"step":20},"categories":["Left","Right"],"series":[{"name":"Pressure","values":[120,25]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Left","Pressure"]]},"calc":["120 ÷ 25 ≈ **5×** higher pressure","wall stress ∝ P·r/h → higher P needs thicker wall h"]},{"text":"Answer: the left ventricle must generate higher pressure to overcome systemic resistance, so it has more muscle.","hl":"thicker than that of the right ventricle"}],"trap":"\"Larger volume\" is the trap: the two sides must pump equal volumes, or blood would pile up in one circuit. The difference is pressure, not volume."},teach:[{"text":"First, **read what the question compares**: the muscle wall of the two lower heart chambers.","marks":[{"t":"wall of the left ventricle","note":"left pump's muscle"},{"t":"thicker","note":"more muscle"},{"t":"right ventricle","note":"right pump"}]},{"text":"**Two pumps, two loops.** The right ventricle sends blood only to the nearby **lungs**. The left ventricle sends it to the **whole body**.","marks":[{"t":"right ventricle","note":"pumps to lungs"},{"t":"left ventricle","note":"pumps to whole body"}]},{"text":"**Resistance** = how hard it is to push blood through the vessels. The long body loop resists far more, so the left side needs **higher pressure**.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Peak pressure each ventricle makes","x":{"label":"Ventricle"},"y":{"label":"Pressure (mmHg)","min":0,"max":140,"step":20},"categories":["Left","Right"],"series":[{"name":"Pressure","values":[120,25]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Left","Pressure"]]},"calc":["120 ÷ 25 ≈ **5×** more pressure"]},{"text":"**Same volume each beat** from both sides; otherwise blood would pile up in one loop. So volume can't explain the thicker wall.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Blood pushed out per beat (stroke volume)","x":{"label":"Ventricle"},"y":{"label":"Volume (mL)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Left","Right"],"series":[{"name":"Volume","values":[70,70]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Left","Volume"],["Right","Volume"]]}},{"text":"**Wrong facts to drop:** the venae cavae feed the **right** side, and the left ventricle pumps **oxygen-rich** blood fresh from the lungs.","marks":[{"t":"left ventricle","note":"gets O₂-rich blood"}],"calc":["body → venae cavae → **right** atrium","lungs → **left** atrium → left ventricle"]},{"text":"**Like a gym muscle:** more pressure work → more muscle → a thicker wall.","marks":[{"t":"thicker","note":"built for high pressure"}],"calc":["more pressure needed → **thicker wall**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heart wall thickness follows the pressure the chamber must generate, not the volume it pumps."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"A sample of ground-state helium atoms is placed in a strong, nonuniform magnetic field. What is the total electron spin of each atom, and how does the sample respond to the field?",idStem:"Helium has two electrons in its 1s orbital. What is their total spin?",choices:["0, and the sample is weakly repelled because it is diamagnetic","½, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic","1, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic","0, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic"],ans:0,exp:"He is **1s²**; by **Pauli exclusion** the two electrons have opposite spins, so **S = 0**. With no unpaired electrons the atom is **diamagnetic**: weakly repelled by a magnetic field.",src:"pe6-cp:47",how:{"ask":"How many electrons share the 1s orbital, what must their spins be, and what does that mean for magnetism?","know":"Each electron has spin +½ or −½. **Pauli exclusion:** two electrons in one orbital have opposite spins. **Paramagnetic** = has unpaired electrons, attracted into a field. **Diamagnetic** = all electrons paired, weakly repelled.","steps":[{"text":"Write the configuration: He has 2 electrons, 1s².","hl":"ground-state helium atoms","calc":["He: Z = 2 → **1s²**"]},{"text":"Rule: Pauli exclusion forces two electrons in one orbital to have opposite spins.","calc":["m_s = +½ and −½","S = +½ + (−½) = **0**"],"strike":["½, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic","1, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic"]},{"text":"No unpaired electrons means no net spin moment to align, so there is no attraction; paired electrons give only weak diamagnetic repulsion.","hl":"how does the sample respond to the field","strike":["0, and the sample is attracted because it is paramagnetic"]},{"text":"Answer: 0, and helium is diamagnetic, weakly repelled by the field."}],"trap":"\"0, and attracted\" gets the spin right but forgets that attraction needs unpaired electrons; ½ is the spin of a single electron."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out what the question gives and asks**.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state helium atoms","note":"lowest-energy He"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"sorts by magnetism"},{"t":"total electron spin","note":"add up spins"}]},{"text":"**Ground state** = every electron in the lowest spot it can take. Helium has **2 electrons**, both fit in the first orbital, 1s.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state","note":"electrons in lowest spots"},{"t":"helium atoms","note":"2 electrons each"}],"calc":["He: Z = 2 → **1s²**"]},{"text":"**Spin:** each electron acts like a tiny magnet pointing up (+½) or down (−½).","marks":[{"t":"electron spin","note":"each e⁻: +½ or −½"}]},{"text":"**Pauli exclusion:** two electrons in one orbital must spin **opposite** ways. Up + down cancels to zero.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Spins of helium's two electrons","x":{"label":"Electron"},"y":{"label":"Spin","min":-1,"max":1,"step":0.5},"categories":["e⁻ #1","e⁻ #2","Total"],"series":[{"name":"Spin","values":[0.5,-0.5,0]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["Total","Spin"]]},"calc":["S = +½ + (−½) = **0**"]},{"text":"**Two magnetic types.** Paramagnetic = has **unpaired** electrons, pulled **into** a field. Diamagnetic = all **paired**, weakly **pushed out**.","marks":[{"t":"respond to the field","note":"pulled in or pushed out?"}]},{"text":"**Helium's electrons are all paired**, so it is diamagnetic: the field weakly repels it.","marks":[{"t":"helium atoms","note":"all paired: diamagnetic"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"pushes He away weakly"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** S = ½ needs one unpaired electron (like H); S = 1 needs two (like O₂). Spin 0 with attraction contradicts itself.","calc":["H: 1s¹ → S = ½ → attracted","He: 1s² → S = **0** → weakly repelled"],"marks":[{"t":"total electron spin","note":"0, not ½ or 1"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pair the electrons in orbitals; any unpaired → paramagnetic (attracted); all paired → spin 0, diamagnetic (weakly repelled)."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"In a carbohydrate-binding site, a tyrosine ring stacks against a sugar ring, and the tyrosine side chain donates a hydrogen bond to a sugar hydroxyl. Which replacement for tyrosine, shown by its side chain, would most likely preserve both interactions?",idStem:"Three Trp residues bind cellulose by donating H-bonds from their ring N–H. Which substitution keeps that ability best?",choiceFigs:["phenylalanine-sc","serine-sc","leucine-sc","tryptophan-sc"],choices:["Phenylalanine","Serine","Leucine","Tryptophan"],ans:3,exp:"Two features must survive: a **flat aromatic ring** to stack and an **X–H donor** (H on N or O). Tryptophan's indole is a large flat aromatic ring and its **ring N–H** donates a hydrogen bond. Phenylalanine keeps the ring but has no donor; serine keeps an O–H but has no ring; leucine has neither.",src:"pe6-cp:48",how:{"ask":"Which side chain has both a flat aromatic ring and an H on N or O?","know":"• **Stacking (CH–π) with sugars** needs a flat aromatic ring: Phe, Tyr, Trp, His.\n• **H-bond donor** = an H on N, O or F. Tyr donates from its O–H; Trp from its indole N–H; Ser and Thr from O–H.\n• To choose a substitute, list the features the original residue uses, then find the side chain that keeps every one.","steps":[{"text":"List what tyrosine does: it stacks (needs a ring) and donates a hydrogen bond (needs an H on N or O).","hl":"stacks against a sugar ring","calc":["feature 1: flat **aromatic ring**","feature 2: **X–H** donor, X = N or O"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine has the ring but no X–H; serine has an O–H but no ring.","fig":["phenylalanine-sc","serine-sc"],"strike":["Phenylalanine","Serine"]},{"text":"Leucine is a plain branched hydrocarbon with neither feature.","strike":["Leucine"]},{"text":"Tryptophan's indole is a large flat aromatic ring, and its ring N–H is a donor.","fig":"tryptophan-sc"},{"text":"Answer: tryptophan. Rule: list the features the residue uses, then keep every one; Trp = ring + N–H donor."}],"trap":"Phenylalanine looks like the closest copy of tyrosine, the same ring minus the OH. Losing the OH loses the only donor, so the hydrogen bond is gone."},teach:[{"text":"**How to read them.** Each is an amino acid; the highlighted side chain is what changes. A hexagon with three double lines is a flat aromatic ring.","fig":"t6-tyr-read"},{"text":"**Job 1, stacking: a flat ring lies against the sugar's flat ring face, like stacked plates.**","fig":"t6-tyr-stack"},{"text":"**Job 2: an H on N or O is slightly + and is drawn to a lone pair on the sugar's O.** Giving that H = donating an H-bond.","fig":"t6-tyr-donor"},{"text":"**So the replacement must keep both: a flat aromatic ring and an H on N or O.**","fig":"t6-tyr-two-jobs"},{"text":"**Phenylalanine keeps the ring but has only C–H, which does not donate.**","fig":"t6-phe-ring-only"},{"text":"**Serine keeps an O–H donor but has no ring.**","fig":"t6-ser-no-ring"},{"text":"**Leucine is only carbon and hydrogen: neither job.**","fig":"t6-leu-none"},{"text":"**Tryptophan has a large flat ring system and an N–H in it that can donate.**","fig":"t6-trp-both"},{"text":"**Rule:** list every job the original side chain does, then keep all of them. In each drawing ask: is there a flat aromatic ring, and an H on N or O?","fig":["t6-tyr-two-jobs","t6-trp-both"]}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"biochemistry",aamc:"5D",q:"A secreted protein has a high proportion of serine and threonine residues on its surface. These residues most likely contribute to the protein's:",idStem:"What do the many Ser and Thr residues on a protein's surface most likely do?",choices:["catalytic activity","tendency to aggregate","net negative charge at pH 7.4","solubility in aqueous solution"],ans:3,exp:"Ser/Thr –OH groups are **polar and uncharged at pH 7.4**. They **hydrogen bond with water**, which keeps the protein dissolved.",src:"pe6-cp:49",how:{"ask":"What do –OH side chains on the surface do in water?","know":"Ser and Thr are **polar but uncharged**: their –OH groups **hydrogen bond with water**. Charge comes from ionizable side chains (Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His).","steps":[{"text":"Look at the side chains: Ser –CH₂OH and Thr –CH(OH)CH₃ are polar hydroxyls.","fig":["serine-sc","threonine-sc"],"hl":"serine and threonine residues on its surface"},{"text":"Their OH pKa is about 13, so they are uncharged at pH 7.4 and add no negative charge.","calc":["fraction ionized = 10^(7.4 − 13) ≈ **3 × 10⁻⁶**","→ essentially **neutral**"],"strike":["net negative charge at pH 7.4"]},{"text":"Polar OH groups hydrogen bond with water, which opposes aggregation; catalysis depends on a few active-site residues, not the whole surface.","strike":["catalytic activity","tendency to aggregate"]},{"text":"Answer: solubility in aqueous solution, because surface hydroxyls hydrogen bond with water.","hl":"most likely contribute"}],"trap":"The –OH of Ser/Thr isn't ionized at pH 7, so it adds no charge."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out the clues** in the question.","marks":[{"t":"secreted protein","note":"released into body fluid"},{"t":"serine and threonine","note":"two amino acids"},{"t":"on its surface","note":"touching water"}]},{"text":"**A residue** = one amino acid in the chain. What matters is its **side chain**. Ser and Thr both carry an **–OH** group.","marks":[{"t":"serine and threonine residues","note":"both carry an –OH"}],"calc":["Ser side chain: **–CH₂–OH**","Thr side chain: **–CH(OH)–CH₃**"]},{"text":"**Polar** = has a slightly + and slightly − end. An –OH is polar and forms **hydrogen bonds** with water.","marks":[{"t":"on its surface","note":"–OH faces water"}],"calc":["protein–O–H ··· OH₂ = **hydrogen bond**"]},{"text":"**Are they charged?** The –OH only loses H⁺ near pH 13. At body pH 7.4 essentially none is charged.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction of Ser –OH that is charged (pKa ≈ 13)","x":{"label":"pH","min":7,"max":14,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Charged (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Ser –OH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[7,0],[9,0],[11,1],[12,9],[13,50],[14,91]]}]},"graph":{"labels":[{"x":7.4,"y":0,"text":"pH 7.4: ~0% charged","at":"above"},{"series":"Ser –OH","x":13,"text":"pKa 13: half charged","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**So no added negative charge.** Charge comes from Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His, not Ser/Thr.","marks":[{"t":"serine and threonine","note":"neutral at pH 7.4"}]},{"text":"**A secreted protein lives dissolved in fluid.** Surface –OH groups grip water, which keeps it **dissolved**.","marks":[{"t":"secreted protein","note":"must stay dissolved"},{"t":"on its surface","note":"H-bonds to water"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** sticking together (aggregation) comes from oily surfaces, the opposite; catalysis needs a few active-site residues, not the whole surface.","marks":[{"t":"most likely contribute","note":"→ solubility"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** polar, uncharged surface residues (Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln) hydrogen bond with water and raise solubility."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5A",q:"A white solid dissolves in water to give NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ in a 1:1 ratio, and a 0.10 M solution has a pH of about 5. Which choice gives the correct name of the solid and the correct reason the solution is acidic?",idStem:"What is the name of NH₄Cl?",choices:["Ammonium chloride; NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water","Ammonia hydrochloride; Cl⁻ donates a proton to water","Ammonium chloride; Cl⁻ accepts a proton from water","Nitrogen tetrahydride chloride; NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water"],ans:0,exp:"**NH₄⁺ = ammonium**, Cl⁻ = chloride → **ammonium chloride** (ionic: cation then anion, no prefixes). NH₄⁺ is a weak acid, **Ka = Kw/Kb ≈ 5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰**, giving pH ≈ 5.1 at 0.10 M; Cl⁻ is a negligible base.",src:"pe6-cp:50",how:{"ask":"How are ionic compounds with a polyatomic cation named, and which ion in this salt can donate a proton?","know":"**Ionic naming:** cation name + anion name ending in \"-ide\"; NH₄⁺ = **ammonium**. Greek prefixes are only for molecular compounds of two nonmetals. **The conjugate acid of a weak base is a weak acid** (NH₄⁺, Ka = Kw/Kb), while the conjugate base of a strong acid (Cl⁻) has no effect on pH.","steps":[{"text":"Name the ions: NH₄⁺ is the polyatomic cation ammonium; Cl⁻ is chloride. Ionic names are cation then anion, with no Greek prefixes.","hl":"NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ in a 1:1 ratio","strike":["Ammonia hydrochloride; Cl⁻ donates a proton to water","Nitrogen tetrahydride chloride; NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water"]},{"text":"Find the acid: Cl⁻ is the conjugate base of a strong acid and is neutral; NH₄⁺ is the conjugate acid of the weak base NH₃.","calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺","Ka = Kw/Kb = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ = **5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰**"]},{"text":"Check the pH in the stem.","hl":"a 0.10 M solution has a pH of about 5","calc":["[H⁺] = √(Ka·C) = √(5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 0.10)","[H⁺] = **7.5 × 10⁻⁶ M**","pH = **5.1**"]},{"text":"Cl⁻ accepting a proton would make OH⁻ and a basic solution, the opposite of what is seen.","strike":["Ammonium chloride; Cl⁻ accepts a proton from water"]},{"text":"Answer: ammonium chloride; NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water."}],"trap":"\"Nitrogen tetrahydride chloride\" has the right acid but names NH₄⁺ as a molecular compound. Cl⁻ is too weak a base to change the pH."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out what the question gives**.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"ammonium ion"},{"t":"Cl⁻","note":"chloride ion"},{"t":"pH of about 5","note":"acidic (below 7)"}]},{"text":"**Naming an ionic solid:** say the + ion, then the − ion ending in \"-ide\". No Greek prefixes like tetra-.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ in a 1:1 ratio","note":"cation + anion = a salt"}],"calc":["NH₄⁺ = **ammonium**; Cl⁻ = **chloride**","→ **ammonium chloride**"]},{"text":"**pH** measures H⁺ in water: 7 = neutral, below 7 = acidic. pH 5 means extra H⁺ was released.","marks":[{"t":"pH of about 5","note":"more H⁺ than pure water"}]},{"text":"**An acid is a proton (H⁺) donor.** Something in the solution handed H⁺ to water.","marks":[{"t":"the solution is acidic","note":"someone gave H⁺ to water"}]},{"text":"**NH₄⁺ is the acid.** It is NH₃ with an extra H⁺, and it gives that H⁺ to water.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"H⁺ donor (weak acid)"}],"calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + **H₃O⁺**"]},{"text":"**Cl⁻ does nothing to pH.** It comes from HCl, a strong acid, so it never takes H⁺ back. If it did, it would make OH⁻: basic, not acidic.","marks":[{"t":"Cl⁻","note":"spectator: no pH effect"}]},{"text":"**Check the number** the question gives.","marks":[{"t":"0.10 M","note":"concentration C"},{"t":"pH of about 5","note":"matches ✓"}],"calc":["Ka = Kw/Kb = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ = **5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰**","[H⁺] = √(Ka·C) = √(5.6 × 10⁻¹¹)","[H⁺] = **7.5 × 10⁻⁶ M** → pH = **5.1**"]},{"text":"**Naming traps:** \"ammonia\" is neutral NH₃, not the ion; \"nitrogen tetrahydride\" uses prefixes, which are only for molecules of two nonmetals.","marks":[{"t":"white solid","note":"ionic salt: no prefixes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ionic name = cation + anion(-ide). The conjugate acid of a weak base (NH₄⁺) lowers pH; the conjugate base of a strong acid (Cl⁻) does nothing."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4D",q:"An NMR spectrometer operates at a frequency of 750 MHz. What is the energy of one photon at this frequency? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)",idStem:"What is the energy of a 750 MHz radio photon? (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)",choices:["3.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J","5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J","7.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J","9.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J"],ans:1,exp:"**E = hf** = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 7.5 × 10⁸ ≈ 5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J. Radio photons carry far less energy than visible light (about 10⁻¹⁹ J).",src:"pe6-cp:51",how:{"ask":"Photon energy from frequency.","know":"**E = h × f.** Mega (M) = 10⁶, so 750 MHz = 7.5 × 10⁸ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Translate: frequency is given; photon energy uses E = hf.","hl":"750 MHz"},{"text":"Convert MHz to Hz.","calc":["750 MHz = 750 × 10⁶ Hz = **7.5 × 10⁸ Hz**"]},{"text":"Multiply.","hl":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","calc":["E = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 7.5 × 10⁸ s⁻¹","E = 49.5 × 10⁻²⁶ J","E ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J**"]},{"text":"Estimate check: 6.6 × 7.5 ≈ 50, so the leading digits are 5, not 3, 7 or 9.","strike":["3.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J","7.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J","9.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J, a radio photon about a million times weaker than visible light."}],"trap":"Mishandling the MHz → Hz conversion, or the exponents, gives the other choices. Split the arithmetic into numbers and powers of ten."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"750 MHz","note":"frequency f"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"Planck's constant"},{"t":"energy of one photon","note":"unknown E"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in packets called photons.** Frequency = how many waves pass per second (Hz).","marks":[{"t":"one photon","note":"one packet of light"},{"t":"frequency of 750 MHz","note":"waves per second"}]},{"text":"**Photon energy = h × f.** Higher frequency → more energy per photon.","marks":[{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"E = h × f"}],"calc":["E = h × f"]},{"text":"**Convert the prefix:** mega (M) = 10⁶.","marks":[{"t":"750 MHz","note":"= 7.5 × 10⁸ Hz"}],"calc":["750 MHz = 750 × 10⁶ Hz","= **7.5 × 10⁸ Hz**"]},{"text":"**Multiply** the front numbers, then add the exponents.","marks":[{"t":"750 MHz","note":"7.5 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"× this"}],"calc":["E = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 7.5 × 10⁸","6.6 × 7.5 = **49.5**","10⁻³⁴ × 10⁸ = **10⁻²⁶**","E = 49.5 × 10⁻²⁶ ≈ **5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ J**"]},{"text":"**E rises in a straight line with f.** At 7.5 × 10⁸ Hz the line reads about 5 × 10⁻²⁵ J.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy E = hf","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10⁸ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (× 10⁻²⁵ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.65],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.95],[10,6.6]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"E = hf","x":7.5}],"labels":[{"series":"E = hf","x":7.5,"text":"750 MHz → 4.95","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"**Estimate check:** 6.6 × 7.5 is about 50, so the leading digit is 5, not 3, 7 or 9. Radio photons are tiny compared with visible light (~10⁻¹⁹ J).","marks":[{"t":"energy of one photon","note":"≈ 5 × 10⁻²⁵ J"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf. Convert MHz → Hz (× 10⁶) first, multiply the front numbers, add the exponents."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4C",q:"The gyromagnetic ratio of a nucleus is given by γ = 2πf/B, where f is the resonance frequency and B is the magnetic field strength. A spectrometer with an 18 T magnet detects protons at 750 MHz. What is γ for the proton?",idStem:"Using γ = 2πf/B with f = 750 MHz and B = 18 T, what is γ?",choices:["87 × 2π MHz/T","63 × 2π MHz/T","42 × 2π MHz/T","26 × 2π MHz/T"],ans:2,exp:"750 ÷ 18 ≈ 41.7, so **γ ≈ 42 × 2π MHz/T** (the proton's known value). Plug the given numbers into the given equation.",src:"pe6-cp:52",how:{"ask":"Just plug the given numbers into the given equation.","know":"The MCAT often gives an unfamiliar formula. You don't need to know what γ means, only how to do the algebra. γ = 2πf/B.","steps":[{"text":"Use the equation given: γ = 2πf/B; keep 2π as a factor since the choices do.","hl":"γ = 2πf/B"},{"text":"Plug in f and B.","hl":"18 T magnet detects protons at 750 MHz","calc":["γ = 2π × 750 MHz ÷ 18 T","750 ÷ 18 = **41.7**","γ ≈ **42 × 2π MHz/T**"]},{"text":"87, 63 and 26 do not equal 750/18; check by multiplying back: 42 × 18 = 756 ≈ 750.","calc":["42 × 18 = **756** ≈ 750 ✓"],"strike":["87 × 2π MHz/T","63 × 2π MHz/T","26 × 2π MHz/T"]},{"text":"Answer: 42 × 2π MHz/T, the known proton value."}],"trap":"Unfamiliar symbols trigger panic. Treat them as letters in an equation and substitute."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find the equation and the numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"γ = 2πf/B","note":"equation given"},{"t":"18 T magnet","note":"field strength B"},{"t":"750 MHz","note":"frequency f"}]},{"text":"**The question tells you what each letter means.** Match each to its number.","marks":[{"t":"f is the resonance frequency","note":"f = 750 MHz"},{"t":"B is the magnetic field strength","note":"B = 18 T (tesla)"}]},{"text":"**You do not need to know what γ is.** It is just a name; this is plug-in-and-divide algebra.","marks":[{"t":"gyromagnetic ratio","note":"just a name: plug in"}]},{"text":"**Keep 2π as a factor**, because every choice is written as \"× 2π\". Only f/B needs working out.","marks":[{"t":"γ = 2πf/B","note":"keep 2π, compute f/B"}]},{"text":"**Plug in and divide** frequency by field.","marks":[{"t":"18 T magnet","note":"÷ 18 T"},{"t":"750 MHz","note":"750 MHz on top"}],"calc":["γ = 2π × 750 MHz ÷ 18 T","750 ÷ 18 = **41.7**","γ ≈ **42 × 2π MHz/T**"]},{"text":"**Check by multiplying back:** 42 × 18 should give about 750.","marks":[{"t":"γ for the proton","note":"≈ 42 × 2π MHz/T"}],"calc":["42 × 18 = **756** ≈ 750 ✓","87 × 18 = 1566 ✗; 26 × 18 = 468 ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when the MCAT hands you an unfamiliar formula, match each symbol to a number, keep any factor the choices keep (like 2π), and do the algebra."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4B",q:"A reaction that releases a gas is run to completion twice at 1 atm: once at 25 °C and once at 5 °C. The volume of gas collected at 5 °C is smaller. Which explanation is most consistent with these results?",idStem:"The same reaction run colder produces a smaller VOLUME of gas. What best explains this?",choices:["Fewer moles of gas are produced at the lower temperature","The same amount of gas occupies a smaller volume at the lower temperature","The reaction does not occur at the lower temperature","The pressure in the collection vessel is higher at the lower temperature"],ans:1,exp:"The reaction runs **to completion** both times, so the moles are the same. At constant P and n, **V ∝ T (in K)**: 278 K/298 K ≈ 0.93, so about 7% less volume.",src:"pe6-cp:53",how:{"ask":"With the same amount of gas, why would the volume be smaller when colder?","know":"**PV = nRT.** With P and n fixed, **V ∝ T** (in kelvin): Charles's law.","steps":[{"text":"\"To completion\" means the same amount of reactant gives the same moles of gas both times.","hl":"run to completion twice","strike":["Fewer moles of gas are produced at the lower temperature","The reaction does not occur at the lower temperature"]},{"text":"Both runs are at 1 atm, so pressure did not change.","hl":"at 1 atm","strike":["The pressure in the collection vessel is higher at the lower temperature"]},{"text":"With n and P fixed, V ∝ T in kelvin.","calc":["T₁ = 25 + 273 = **298 K**","T₂ = 5 + 273 = **278 K**","V₂/V₁ = 278/298 = **0.93**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Volume of a fixed amount of gas at 1 atm versus temperature","x":{"label":"T (K)","min":260,"max":320,"step":10},"y":{"label":"V (relative to 298 K)","min":0.8,"max":1.1,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"Fixed n, P","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[268,0.899],[278,0.933],[288,0.966],[298,1],[308,1.034]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":298},{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":278}],"labels":[{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":298,"text":"298 K: 1.00","at":"left"},{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":278,"text":"278 K: 0.93","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":278,"from":0.933,"to":1,"text":"≈ 7 % smaller","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"Answer: the same amount of gas occupies about 7% less volume at the lower temperature."}],"trap":"\"Fewer moles\" contradicts \"run to completion\". \"Higher pressure\" contradicts \"at 1 atm\". Use every condition the question gives."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out what was kept the same and what changed**.","marks":[{"t":"run to completion twice","note":"reaction fully finishes"},{"t":"at 1 atm","note":"pressure"},{"t":"once at 5 °C","note":"colder run"}]},{"text":"**\"To completion\"** = all the reactant is used up. Same starting amount → the **same moles of gas** both times.","marks":[{"t":"run to completion twice","note":"same moles of gas"}]},{"text":"**Ideal gas law:** PV = nRT. With pressure P and moles n fixed, volume follows temperature: **V ∝ T**.","marks":[{"t":"at 1 atm","note":"P fixed both times"}],"calc":["PV = nRT","P, n fixed → **V ∝ T** (in kelvin)"]},{"text":"**Gas laws need kelvin.** Add 273.","marks":[{"t":"once at 25 °C","note":"= 298 K"},{"t":"once at 5 °C","note":"= 278 K"}],"calc":["25 + 273 = **298 K**","5 + 273 = **278 K**","V₂/V₁ = 278/298 = **0.93**"]},{"text":"**See it:** colder gas takes less room. Going from 298 K to 278 K shrinks the volume by about 7%.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Volume of a fixed amount of gas at 1 atm vs temperature","x":{"label":"T (K)","min":260,"max":320,"step":10},"y":{"label":"V (relative to 298 K)","min":0.8,"max":1.1,"step":0.05},"series":[{"name":"Fixed n, P","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[268,0.899],[278,0.933],[288,0.966],[298,1],[308,1.034]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":298},{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":278}],"labels":[{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":298,"text":"298 K: 1.00","at":"left"},{"series":"Fixed n, P","x":278,"text":"278 K: 0.93","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":278,"from":0.933,"to":1,"text":"≈ 7% smaller","side":"left"}]}},{"text":"**That explains the result:** same gas, just cooler, so smaller volume.","marks":[{"t":"is smaller","note":"same gas, cooler"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** the pressure was 1 atm both times, and the reaction finished both times, so it did occur and made the same moles.","marks":[{"t":"at 1 atm","note":"not higher pressure"},{"t":"run to completion twice","note":"not fewer moles"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list what is held fixed (P, n), then use PV = nRT: at fixed P and n, volume tracks kelvin temperature."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"The phase diagram of compound Q is shown. A sample of solid Q held at a constant 6 atm is warmed from −60 °C to 40 °C. Which sequence of phase changes occurs?",idStem:"On a phase diagram, a path crosses directly from the solid region to the vapor region. What is it?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Q","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-80,"max":60,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-80,0.5],[-60,1.2],[-40,2.3],[-20,4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-20,4],[-17.5,6],[-15,8],[-10,12]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-20,4],[0,5],[10,6],[20,7.2],[40,10.5]]}]},choices:["Sublimation only","Melting, then vaporization","Melting only","Vaporization, then melting"],ans:1,exp:"The triple point is at **4 atm and −20 °C**. At 6 atm, above the triple-point pressure, a horizontal line crosses the **solid–liquid line at about −17.5 °C (melting)** and then the **liquid–gas line at 10 °C (vaporization)**. Sublimation happens only below 4 atm.",src:"pe6-cp:54",how:{"ask":"Draw a horizontal line at 6 atm from −60 °C to 40 °C. Which boundary lines does it cross, and in what order?","know":"• A **phase diagram** maps pressure (y) against temperature (x). Each region is one phase: solid at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid between.\n• A boundary line = two phases in equilibrium. Crossing it = a phase change.\n• **Triple point:** where all three lines meet. Below its pressure no liquid exists, so solid goes straight to gas (sublimation). Above it, solid melts and then the liquid boils.\n• At constant pressure, the path is a horizontal line.","steps":[{"text":"Constant pressure means a horizontal path: draw it at 6 atm.","hl":"held at a constant 6 atm","graph":{"yLine":6,"shade":[{"x1":-60,"x2":40,"text":"warming path at 6 atm"}]}},{"text":"Compare with the triple point, where the three lines meet.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-20}],"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-20,"text":"triple point: 4 atm, −20 °C","at":"right"}],"spans":[{"x":-30,"from":4,"to":6,"text":"6 atm > 4 atm","side":"left"}]},"calc":["triple point: **4 atm**, −20 °C","6 atm > 4 atm → a liquid region exists on this path"]},{"text":"Going right, the path first hits the solid–liquid line: melting.","graph":{"yLine":6,"read":[{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-17.5}],"labels":[{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-17.5,"text":"melts ≈ −17.5 °C","at":"left"}]},"calc":["melts at ≈ **−17.5 °C**"]},{"text":"Then it hits the liquid–gas line: vaporization. Since it never touches the solid–gas line, there is no sublimation.","graph":{"yLine":6,"read":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":10,"text":"boils at 10 °C","at":"below"}]},"calc":["boils at **10 °C**; at 40 °C it is gas"],"strike":["Sublimation only","Melting only","Vaporization, then melting"]},{"text":"Answer: melting, then vaporization. Rule: draw the horizontal line at the given pressure; each boundary it crosses is a phase change, and sublimation needs a pressure below the triple point.","graph":{"yLine":6,"shade":[{"x1":-60,"x2":-17.5,"text":"solid"},{"x1":-17.5,"x2":10,"text":"liquid"},{"x1":10,"x2":40,"text":"gas"}]}}],"trap":"Sublimation is what dry ice does at 1 atm, and this diagram looks similar. At 6 atm the path passes above the triple point, so a liquid phase appears."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = higher pressure** pushing on the sample (atm).","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"held at a constant 6 atm","note":"pressure stays at 6"}]},{"text":"**Right = hotter** (°C). Warming means moving right.","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"warmed from −60 °C to 40 °C","note":"move right"}]},{"text":"**The three lines are borders between phases.** Crossing a line = a phase change.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-60,"text":"solid–gas border","at":"below"},{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-15,"text":"solid–liquid border","at":"right"},{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":20,"text":"liquid–gas border","at":"right"}]},"marks":[{"t":"phase diagram","note":"map of phases"}]},{"text":"**Each region is one phase:** cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; the wedge between = liquid.","graph":{"labels":[{"x":-60,"y":8,"text":"SOLID"},{"x":10,"y":10,"text":"LIQUID"},{"x":30,"y":2,"text":"GAS"}]}},{"text":"**Triple point** = where all three borders meet. Below its pressure no liquid can exist: solid turns straight to gas (sublimation).","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-20}],"labels":[{"series":"Solid–gas","x":-20,"text":"triple pt: 4 atm, −20 °C","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**Constant pressure = a horizontal path.** Draw it at 6 atm from −60 °C to 40 °C.","graph":{"yLine":6,"shade":[{"x1":-60,"x2":40,"text":"path at 6 atm"}]},"marks":[{"t":"constant 6 atm","note":"flat line at 6"}]},{"text":"**Compare with the triple point:** 6 atm is above 4 atm, so the path passes through a liquid region. No sublimation.","graph":{"yLine":6,"spans":[{"x":-30,"from":4,"to":6,"text":"6 atm > 4 atm","side":"left"}]},"calc":["6 atm > **4 atm** → liquid exists on this path"]},{"text":"**Moving right, the path first hits the solid–liquid border: melting.**","graph":{"yLine":6,"read":[{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-17.5}],"labels":[{"series":"Solid–liquid","x":-17.5,"text":"melts ≈ −17.5 °C","at":"left"}]},"marks":[{"t":"solid Q","note":"starts solid"}]},{"text":"**Next it hits the liquid–gas border: vaporization (boiling).**","graph":{"yLine":6,"read":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":10}],"labels":[{"series":"Liquid–gas","x":10,"text":"boils at 10 °C","at":"below"}]},"marks":[{"t":"sequence of phase changes","note":"melting, then vaporizing"}]},{"text":"**Read the whole path:** solid → liquid → gas. It never touches the solid–gas border.","graph":{"yLine":6,"shade":[{"x1":-60,"x2":-17.5,"text":"solid"},{"x1":-17.5,"x2":10,"text":"liquid"},{"x1":10,"x2":40,"text":"gas"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** draw a horizontal line at the given pressure; each border it crosses, in order, is a phase change. Sublimation only happens below the triple-point pressure."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5E",q:"The energy diagram for a reaction is shown. Which statement correctly describes the forward reaction?",idStem:"NH₄NO₃ decomposes with a negative ΔH, yet it is stable at room temperature. How do you describe its energetics?",chart:{"type":"line","caption":"Energy diagram for the reaction","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":180,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Reaction","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[1,40],[2,45],[3,80],[4,140],[5,160],[6,140],[7,100],[8,75],[9,70],[10,70]]}]},choices:["Endothermic, with an activation energy of 120 kJ/mol","Exothermic, with an activation energy of 120 kJ/mol","Endothermic, with an activation energy of 160 kJ/mol","Endothermic, with an activation energy of 90 kJ/mol"],ans:0,exp:"Products (70 kJ/mol) sit **above** reactants (40 kJ/mol), so ΔH = **+30 kJ/mol: endothermic**. The activation energy is measured from the reactants to the top of the peak: **160 − 40 = 120 kJ/mol**.",src:"pe6-cp:55",how:{"ask":"Where are the reactants, the products and the peak on the energy axis, and which differences give ΔH and Ea?","know":"• Left end = reactants; right end = products; highest point = **transition state**.\n• **ΔH** = products − reactants. Positive (products higher) = endothermic; negative = exothermic.\n• **Activation energy, Ea (forward)** = peak − reactants. The reverse reaction's Ea = peak − products.\n• ΔH tells you about thermodynamics; Ea tells you about speed. They are independent.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two ends: reactants on the left, products on the right.","hl":"the forward reaction","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0},{"series":"Reaction","x":10}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants: 40"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products: 70"}],"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":40,"to":70,"text":"ΔH = +30"}]},"calc":["ΔH = 70 − 40 = **+30 kJ/mol**","products higher → **endothermic**"]},{"text":"Products above reactants rules out exothermic.","strike":["Exothermic, with an activation energy of 120 kJ/mol"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"products above reactants","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Read the peak, the transition state, and measure up from the reactants.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":5}],"yLine":40,"spans":[{"x":5,"from":40,"to":160,"text":"Ea = 160 − 40 = 120","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea = 160 − 40 = **120 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"160 is the peak measured from zero, and 90 = 160 − 70 is the activation energy of the reverse reaction.","strike":["Endothermic, with an activation energy of 160 kJ/mol","Endothermic, with an activation energy of 90 kJ/mol"],"graph":{"spans":[{"x":4.5,"from":0,"to":160,"text":"160 (from zero)","side":"left"},{"x":6,"from":70,"to":160,"text":"reverse Ea = 90"}]}},{"text":"Answer: endothermic, Ea = 120 kJ/mol. Rule: ΔH = right end − left end; forward Ea = peak − left end.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":40,"to":160,"text":"Ea = 120","side":"left"},{"x":9.5,"from":40,"to":70,"text":"ΔH = +30"}]}}],"trap":"160 kJ/mol is the height of the peak measured from zero. Ea is the climb from where the reactants start, 40 kJ/mol, so it is 120 kJ/mol."},teach:[{"text":"**Up = energy** of the molecules (kJ/mol). Higher point = more energy at that moment.","graph":{"axis":"y"},"marks":[{"t":"energy diagram","note":"energy along the reaction"}]},{"text":"**Left to right = the reaction happening.** Left edge = before (reactants); right edge = after (products).","graph":{"axis":"x"},"marks":[{"t":"forward reaction","note":"left → right"}]},{"text":"**Flat left = reactants. Flat right = products. Top of the hill = transition state**, the hardest moment of the reaction.","graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":1,"text":"reactants: 40"},{"x1":9,"x2":10,"text":"products: 70"}],"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":5,"text":"transition state: 160","at":"right"}]}},{"text":"**ΔH = finish minus start.** It is the energy taken in or given out overall.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0},{"series":"Reaction","x":10}],"spans":[{"x":9.5,"from":40,"to":70,"text":"ΔH = +30"}]},"calc":["ΔH = 70 − 40 = **+30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Finish higher than start = energy absorbed = endothermic.** Finish lower would be exothermic.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"products above reactants","at":"above"}]},"marks":[{"t":"correctly describes","note":"endothermic"}]},{"text":"**Activation energy (Ea)** = the hill's height measured **from the reactant level**: the energy needed to get started.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Reaction","x":5}],"spans":[{"x":5,"from":40,"to":160,"text":"Ea = 160 − 40","side":"left"}]},"calc":["Ea = 160 − 40 = **120 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"**Trap 1:** 160 is the peak measured from zero, not from the reactants.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":4.5,"from":0,"to":160,"text":"160 from zero: wrong","side":"left"}],"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":0,"text":"measure from here","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Trap 2:** peak − products = 90 is the Ea of the **reverse** reaction.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":6,"from":70,"to":160,"text":"reverse Ea = 90"}],"labels":[{"series":"Reaction","x":10,"text":"not from here","at":"below"}]},"calc":["reverse Ea = 160 − 70 = 90"]},{"text":"**Put it together:** endothermic, Ea = 120 kJ/mol.","graph":{"spans":[{"x":5,"from":40,"to":160,"text":"Ea = 120","side":"left"},{"x":9.5,"from":40,"to":70,"text":"ΔH = +30"}]},"marks":[{"t":"forward reaction","note":"endo, Ea = 120"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔH = right end − left end (positive = endothermic); forward Ea = peak − left end; reverse Ea = peak − right end."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"4E",q:"Ammonium nitrate decomposes in a sealed vessel at 500 K: 2 NH₄NO₃(s) → 2 N₂(g) + O₂(g) + 4 H₂O(g). What is the mole fraction of water vapor in the gaseous products?",idStem:"A reaction forms 1 mol N₂, ½ mol O₂ and 2 mol H₂O, all as gases. What fraction of the gas is water vapor?",choices:["1/7","2/7","4/7","4/9"],ans:2,exp:"Gas moles: 2 N₂ + 1 O₂ + 4 H₂O = **7**. X(H₂O) = **4/7**. The solid reactant isn't part of the gas mixture.",src:"pe6-cp:56",how:{"ask":"Using the coefficients, how many moles of gas form, and how many of them are water?","know":"**Mole fraction X = moles of that gas ÷ total moles of gas.**","steps":[{"text":"Only gases count in a gas mole fraction; NH₄NO₃(s) is excluded.","hl":"mole fraction of water vapor in the gaseous products"},{"text":"Read the coefficients of the gaseous products.","hl":"2 N₂(g) + O₂(g) + 4 H₂O(g)","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles of each gaseous product","x":{"label":"Gas"},"y":{"label":"Moles","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["N₂","O₂","H₂O"],"series":[{"name":"Moles","values":[2,1,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["H₂O","Moles"]]}},{"text":"Divide water moles by total gas moles.","calc":["n_total = 2 + 1 + 4 = **7**","X(H₂O) = 4/7 ≈ **0.57**"]},{"text":"4/9 adds the solid reactant to the total; 1/7 and 2/7 use the O₂ or N₂ coefficient.","strike":["1/7","2/7","4/9"]},{"text":"Answer: 4/7."}],"trap":"4/9 comes from adding the 2 mol of solid NH₄NO₃ to the total. Mole fraction in a gas mixture counts only the gases."},teach:[{"text":"First, **read the equation** like a recipe: left side is used up, right side is made.","marks":[{"t":"2 NH₄NO₃(s)","note":"reactant"},{"t":"→","note":"turns into"},{"t":"mole fraction of water vapor","note":"what we want"}]},{"text":"**The letters in brackets give the phase:** (s) = solid, (g) = gas.","marks":[{"t":"(s)","note":"s = solid"},{"t":"(g)","note":"g = gas"}]},{"text":"**The big number in front = how many moles.** No number means 1.","marks":[{"t":"2 N₂(g)","note":"2 mol nitrogen gas"},{"t":"O₂(g)","note":"1 mol O₂ (no number = 1)"},{"t":"4 H₂O(g)","note":"4 mol water vapor"}]},{"text":"**Mole fraction** = moles of one gas ÷ total moles of **gas**. It is the share of the mix.","marks":[{"t":"gaseous products","note":"only these count"}],"calc":["X = moles of that gas ÷ **total gas moles**"]},{"text":"**Count the gas moles.** Water is 4 of them.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Moles of each gaseous product","x":{"label":"Gas"},"y":{"label":"Moles","min":0,"max":5,"step":1},"categories":["N₂","O₂","H₂O"],"series":[{"name":"Moles","values":[2,1,4]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["H₂O","Moles"]]},"calc":["total = 2 + 1 + 4 = **7**"]},{"text":"**Divide water by the total.**","marks":[{"t":"4 H₂O(g)","note":"4 → on top"},{"t":"2 N₂(g) + O₂(g)","note":"+ these = 7 below"}],"calc":["X(H₂O) = 4 ÷ 7 = **4/7** ≈ 0.57"]},{"text":"**Traps:** the solid reactant is not in the gas mix (that gives 4/9). The temperature is not needed.","marks":[{"t":"2 NH₄NO₃(s)","note":"solid: not counted"},{"t":"500 K","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mole fraction of a gas = its coefficient ÷ sum of the gas coefficients; leave out solids and liquids."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"A sealed radioactive source with a half-life of 2.0 years had an activity of 8.0 Ci when it was calibrated 6.0 years ago. What is its activity today?",idStem:"An isotope with a 2-year half-life had an activity of 8 Ci six years ago. What is its activity now?",choices:["0.8 Ci","1.0 Ci","1.3 Ci","2.7 Ci"],ans:1,exp:"6 years = 3 half-lives: 8 → 4 → 2 → **1 Ci**. Activity halves on the same schedule as the amount.",src:"pe6-cp:57",how:{"ask":"How many half-lives passed, and halve that many times.","know":"After each half-life, the amount AND the activity are cut in half. **Number of half-lives = elapsed time ÷ t½.** A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ.","steps":[{"text":"Count half-lives in the elapsed time.","hl":"half-life of 2.0 years","calc":["n = 6.0 years ÷ 2.0 years = **3** half-lives"]},{"text":"Halve once per half-life.","hl":"8.0 Ci","calc":["8.0 → **4.0** → **2.0** → **1.0 Ci**","A = 8.0 × (½)³ = **1.0 Ci**"]},{"text":"See the decay curve: it halves every 2 years, so at 6 years it reads 1 Ci.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the source versus time, t½ = 2.0 years","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (Ci)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,8],[1,5.66],[2,4],[3,2.83],[4,2],[5,1.41],[6,1]]}]},"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":2},{"series":"Activity","x":4},{"series":"Activity","x":6}],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"t½"},{"x1":2,"x2":4,"text":"t½"},{"x1":4,"x2":6,"text":"t½"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":2,"text":"4 Ci","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":4,"text":"2 Ci","at":"above"},{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"1 Ci","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Decay is exponential, not linear: 2.7 Ci is 8/3 and 1.3 Ci is 8/6; 0.8 Ci is one tenth.","strike":["0.8 Ci","1.3 Ci","2.7 Ci"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 Ci after three half-lives."}],"trap":"1.3 Ci comes from dividing 8 by 6 (the years). Count half-lives, not years."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find every number** the question gives and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 2.0 years","note":"halving time"},{"t":"8.0 Ci","note":"starting activity"},{"t":"6.0 years ago","note":"time passed"}]},{"text":"**A radioactive source** has atoms that break apart on their own. **Activity** = how many break apart per second, measured in curies (Ci).","marks":[{"t":"radioactive source","note":"atoms break apart"},{"t":"8.0 Ci","note":"decays per second (Ci)"}]},{"text":"**Half-life** = the time for the amount, and the activity, to drop to **half**.","marks":[{"t":"half-life","note":"time to drop by half"}]},{"text":"**Count the half-lives** in the time passed.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 years ago","note":"÷ 2.0"},{"t":"half-life of 2.0 years","note":"one halving"}],"calc":["n = 6.0 ÷ 2.0 = **3** half-lives"]},{"text":"**Halve once per half-life.**","marks":[{"t":"8.0 Ci","note":"start here"}],"calc":["8.0 → **4.0** → **2.0** → **1.0 Ci**","A = 8.0 × (½)³ = 8 ÷ 8 = **1.0 Ci**"]},{"text":"**See the curve:** it halves every 2 years, so it reads 1 Ci at 6 years.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the source vs time, t½ = 2.0 years","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (Ci)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,8],[1,5.66],[2,4],[3,2.83],[4,2],[5,1.41],[6,1]]}]},"graph":{"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":2,"text":"t½"},{"x1":2,"x2":4,"text":"t½"},{"x1":4,"x2":6,"text":"t½"}],"labels":[{"series":"Activity","x":2,"text":"4 Ci","at":"right"},{"series":"Activity","x":4,"text":"2 Ci","at":"above"},{"series":"Activity","x":6,"text":"1 Ci","at":"above"}],"read":[{"series":"Activity","x":6}]}},{"text":"**Traps:** decay halves, it does not divide by the years. 8/3 ≈ 2.7 and 8/6 ≈ 1.3 treat it like a straight line.","marks":[{"t":"activity today","note":"halve 3 times: 1 Ci"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** half-lives = time ÷ t½; then halve that many times: A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"physics",aamc:"4E",q:"Americium-241 (Z = 95), used in smoke detectors, decays by alpha emission. What is the daughter nucleus?",idStem:"²⁴¹Am (Z = 95) emits an alpha particle. What nucleus is left?",choices:["²³⁷Bk","²³⁷Np","²³⁹Cm","²³⁹Pu"],ans:1,exp:"**α = ⁴He: mass −4, Z −2.** 241 − 4 = 237; Z 95 − 2 = 93 = neptunium.",src:"pe6-cp:58",how:{"ask":"What's left after the nucleus loses an alpha particle?","know":"**α = ⁴₂He.** Alpha decay: **mass number − 4, atomic number − 2.** The atomic number tells you the element (93 = Np, 94 = Pu, 95 = Am, 96 = Cm, 97 = Bk).","steps":[{"text":"Write what an alpha particle removes: a ⁴He nucleus, 2 protons and 2 neutrons.","hl":"decays by alpha emission"},{"text":"Balance mass number and atomic number.","hl":"Americium-241 (Z = 95)","calc":["²⁴¹₉₅Am → ⁴₂He + ᴬ_Z X","A = 241 − 4 = **237**","Z = 95 − 2 = **93** → **Np**"]},{"text":"Mass number 239 subtracts only 2, and Bk (Z 97) adds protons instead of removing them.","strike":["²³⁷Bk","²³⁹Cm","²³⁹Pu"]},{"text":"Answer: ²³⁷Np."}],"trap":"²³⁷Bk goes UP in Z; ²³⁹Pu subtracts 2 from the mass and 1 from Z. Apply −4 and −2 together."},teach:[{"text":"First, **find what the question gives**.","marks":[{"t":"Americium-241","note":"mass number 241"},{"t":"Z = 95","note":"95 protons"},{"t":"alpha emission","note":"throws out ⁴He"}]},{"text":"**Mass number** = protons + neutrons (the 241). **Z** = protons only; Z decides which element it is.","marks":[{"t":"Americium-241","note":"A = 241"},{"t":"Z = 95","note":"Z = 95 → Am"}],"calc":["²⁴¹₉₅Am: top = mass number, bottom = Z"]},{"text":"**An alpha particle** is a helium nucleus: 2 protons + 2 neutrons.","marks":[{"t":"alpha emission","note":"loses ⁴₂He"}],"calc":["α = ⁴₂He: mass **4**, Z **2**"]},{"text":"**Daughter nucleus** = what is left after the decay.","marks":[{"t":"daughter nucleus","note":"what's left behind"}]},{"text":"**Balance top and bottom numbers:** they must add up the same on both sides.","calc":["²⁴¹₉₅Am → ⁴₂He + ᴬ_Z X","A = 241 − 4 = **237**","Z = 95 − 2 = **93**"],"marks":[{"t":"Americium-241","note":"241 − 4 = 237"},{"t":"Z = 95","note":"95 − 2 = 93"}]},{"text":"**Z names the element.** 93 is neptunium.","calc":["93 = **Np**, 94 = Pu, 95 = Am, 96 = Cm, 97 = Bk","daughter = **²³⁷Np**"],"marks":[{"t":"daughter nucleus","note":"²³⁷Np"}]},{"text":"**Traps:** mass 239 subtracts only 2; Cm (96) and Bk (97) add protons instead of removing them. Smoke detectors are just context.","marks":[{"t":"smoke detectors","note":"not needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** alpha decay → mass number − 4 and Z − 2; beta-minus → Z + 1 with mass unchanged. Then read the element from Z."}]},
  {s:"Practice Exam 6: Chem/Phys",disc:"gen_chem",aamc:"5B",q:"In the Lewis structure of carbon monoxide in which both atoms have complete octets, what are the bond order and the formal charge on carbon?",idStem:"In the best Lewis structure of carbon monoxide, how many bonds join C and O?",choices:["3 and −1","3 and 0","2 and 0","2 and −1"],ans:0,exp:"10 valence electrons → **C≡O with one lone pair on each atom**. Formal charge on C = 4 − 2 (nonbonding) − 3 (half of 6 bonding) = **−1**. On O: 6 − 2 − 3 = **+1**.",src:"pe6-cp:59",how:{"ask":"Draw CO with complete octets, then calculate carbon's formal charge.","know":"Lewis steps: count valence electrons, connect the atoms, complete octets, and convert lone pairs to multiple bonds if you run short. **Formal charge = valence e⁻ − nonbonding e⁻ − ½(bonding e⁻).**","steps":[{"text":"Count valence electrons.","hl":"carbon monoxide","calc":["C 4 + O 6 = **10** e⁻"]},{"text":"Give both atoms octets with 10 electrons: a triple bond plus one lone pair on each atom.","hl":"both atoms have complete octets","fig":"carbon-monoxide","calc":["triple bond = **6** e⁻, lone pairs 2 + 2 = **4** e⁻","total 10 ✓","bond order = **3**"],"strike":["2 and 0","2 and −1"]},{"text":"Formal charge = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding.","calc":["FC(C) = 4 − 2 − 6/2 = **−1**","FC(O) = 6 − 2 − 6/2 = **+1**","sum = 0 ✓"],"strike":["3 and 0"]},{"text":"Answer: bond order 3 and formal charge −1 on carbon, even though O is more electronegative."}],"trap":"\"3 and 0\" is the common error: carbon usually has FC 0 with 4 bonds, but here it has 3 bonds and a lone pair. Calculate the formal charge; don't assume it."},teach:[{"text":"First, **pick out what the question gives and asks**.","marks":[{"t":"Lewis structure","note":"dots-and-lines drawing"},{"t":"complete octets","note":"8 e⁻ around each"},{"t":"formal charge on carbon","note":"unknown 2"}]},{"text":"**Bond order** = how many bonds join the two atoms: single 1, double 2, triple 3.","marks":[{"t":"bond order","note":"number of C–O bonds"}]},{"text":"**Count outer (valence) electrons:** C has 4, O has 6.","marks":[{"t":"carbon monoxide","note":"C + O"}],"calc":["4 + 6 = **10** valence e⁻"]},{"text":"**Octet** = 8 electrons around each atom. Shared bond electrons count for both atoms, so more bonds save electrons.","marks":[{"t":"complete octets","note":"need 8 each"}],"calc":["single bond: needs 14 e⁻ ✗","double bond: needs 12 e⁻ ✗","triple bond: needs **10** e⁻ ✓"]},{"text":"**So CO is :C≡O:** a triple bond plus one lone pair on each atom.","marks":[{"t":"bond order","note":"= 3"}],"calc":["triple bond = 6 e⁻; lone pairs = 2 + 2 = 4 e⁻","total = **10** ✓; bond order = **3**"]},{"text":"**Formal charge** = bookkeeping of electrons an atom \"owns\": valence − lone-pair e⁻ − half the bond e⁻.","marks":[{"t":"formal charge on carbon","note":"valence − lone − ½ bond"}],"calc":["FC = valence − nonbonding − ½(bonding)"]},{"text":"**Compute both atoms.**","calc":["FC(C) = 4 − 2 − 6/2 = **−1**","FC(O) = 6 − 2 − 6/2 = **+1**","sum = 0 ✓ (neutral molecule)"],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Formal charges in :C≡O:","x":{"label":"Atom"},"y":{"label":"Formal charge","min":-2,"max":2,"step":1},"categories":["C","O"],"series":[{"name":"FC","values":[-1,1]}]},"graph":{"bars":[["C","FC"]]}},{"text":"**Trap:** a neutral molecule can still carry charges on its atoms. Surprisingly, the minus sits on C, the less electronegative atom.","marks":[{"t":"formal charge on carbon","note":"−1, not 0"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count valence e⁻, add bonds until every atom has an octet, then FC = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding for each atom."}]},
];
